export default [{"s":"concepts","t":"About the platform","h":["About the platform","Access levels","Modular architecture"],"x":"About the platform OneGPS is a monitoring system that collects data from sensors and displays information about the location and status of your assets in a convenient format. The system operates on secure servers, and only authorized users have access to the information. OneGPS works with various types of sensors and cameras, integrating them into a single network. The system analyzes incoming data, helps you make informed decisions, and alerts you to potential issues. With OneGPS, you can: track assets in real time view reports with different levels of detail review event history access photos and videos from your sites Access levels The system has two access levels: the user dashboard and the admin panel. The user account is a basic interface without admin rights. Here, users can monitor vehicles, equipment, people, and other assets. The admin panel provides full access to system settings. Administrators manage accounts, users, system assets, and the overall structure, configuring the platform to fit the company’s needs. Modular architecture OneGPS is built on a modular architecture. Modules are separate components, each responsible for specific functions. Thanks to this modular design, you can: customize the system for your company’s specific needs pay only for the features you actually use add new modules as your requirements grow You can enable the necessary modules through your contract administrator or by contacting technical support."},{"s":"what-s-new-in-pilot-7-10","t":"What’s new in OneGPS 7.10","h":["What’s new in OneGPS 7.10","August 2026","OneGPS web","New AI Assistant capabilities","Detecting hidden fuel drains","Tracking stop visits using RFID","Relative odometer","Managing downtime data","Video upload progress","Reports","Updated Report scheduler","Filter reports by driver","Selecting geofences for the report","Report builder","More sensor data in reports","Merging report rows","Filtering numerical data in reports","Configuring rows in geofence reports","Work shift reports","Integrations and API","Sending statuses and media via Telegram","Sending events to external systems","Access to event media files via API","Admin panel","New design","Comms Cloud SIM card management","Mobile app","Fleet analytics","AI Assistant","Additional sign-in protection","Viewing events and notifications","Balance top-ups via Stripe"],"x":"What’s new in OneGPS 7.10 August 2026 OneGPS 7.10 expands reporting, automation, and integration capabilities, introduces an updated admin panel, and adds new features to the mobile app. OneGPS web New AI Assistant capabilities We’re continuing to develop the OneGPS AI Assistant. In this release, we’ve updated its design and expanded its capabilities. It answers questions about the platform, helps users find relevant features and documentation, and suggests solutions to common tasks. If no ready answer is available, the assistant can help prepare a request for technical support. We’re continuing to develop the OneGPS AI Assistant. In this release, we’ve updated its design and expanded its capabilities. Developers can also get guidance on working with the API and creating extensions. The AI Assistant is still being tested and refined, so its capabilities and response quality will continue to improve. Detecting hidden fuel drains The fuel sensor settings include a new Reverse Flowdrain feature. It helps detect fuel theft involving fuel being drained through the return line. The system analyzes abnormal fuel consumption while the vehicle is moving, estimates the amount of fuel drained, and marks the event location on the map. Tracking stop visits using RFID Bus stop visits can be tracked using RFID tags. You can link one or more tags to each stop along a bus route. When a vehicle reads a linked tag, the system recognizes a visit to the corresponding stop. Relative odometer Added a new sensor that calculates mileage based on pulses from the speedometer or other connected equipment. It can be used when GPS/GLONASS data is unavailable or insufficiently accurate. You can configure the number of pulses per kilometer separately for each vehicle. Managing downtime data The Downtimes tab in the Service module includes options for filtering, importing, and exporting records. You can filter records by object, date, duration, and downtime reason, as well as export them to a file or import them back into the system. This makes it easier to find the required information and manage large numbers of records. Video upload progress Video uploads now display the amount of data transferred, completion percentage, current transfer speed, and estimated time remaining. This makes it easier to see when the video will be ready and identify slowdowns in data transfer. Reports Updated Report scheduler We redesigned the process for creating automated report schedules. Report settings, the report data period, and the generation schedule are organized into separate sections, allowing you to independently specify which data to include and when to generate and send the report. The scheduler supports reports for the entire previous day, custom start and end times, and periods that span midnight. Before saving, you can review the data period for the next report and select specific days of the week for report generation. Filter reports by driver Added the ability to generate reports for selected drivers only. The new On Driver option is available for the Excessive idling report, Mileage report (Driver), and Trips and parkings (with drivers) reports. When this option is enabled, the Driverspanel opens, where you can select one or more drivers or an entire driver group. The report includes only events associated with the selected drivers. Selecting geofences for the report Before building the Movement between zones report, you can select the required geofences. The report will include only movements between the selected geofences. Report builder More sensor data in reports Activity reports can include values from discrete, DIP, and selector sensors. To display the data, select the relevant sensor tag when configuring the column. Merging report rows The Report builder includes a new {\"merge\": true} setting. Use it when you need a single summary row for each split period instead of a detailed list of events. When enabled, records within the period are merged and intermediate rows are hidden. For example, in a report split by day, you can display one row per driver with the total mileage, driving and idle time, the start time of the first trip, and the end time of the last trip. This makes reports more compact and summary values easier to compare. Filtering numerical data in reports For columns containing numeric values, you can apply the >, <, or = conditions. For example, the > 25 filter in theEco Event Max Speed column includes only rows where the maximum speed exceeds 25 km/h. Configuring rows in geofence reports For reports of the Geofences type, you can select how rows are generated: Geofence visits — a separate row is created for each geofence visit Equipment work periods — a separate row is created for each operation period of the selected sensor within a geofence For example, Equipment work periodscan be used to determine how long equipment operated within a specified area. If the equipment was switched on before the object entered the geofence, the period starts upon entry. If the object leaves the geofence before the equipment is switched off, the period ends upon exit. The report includes only the intervals during which the object was inside the geofence and the equipment was operating. Work shift reports Added the ability to split report data by work shift. A separate interval with calculated metrics is created for each shift, allowing daytime and nighttime activity to be analyzed independently. In the report configuration, you can define shift boundaries, display one shift or all shifts, and choose whether to keep an overnight shift intact or split it at midnight. Integrations and API Sending statuses and media via Telegram Employees can send work statuses, photos, and videos to OneGPS through the Telegram bot. Available statuses are configured in advance for each account, so employees only need to select the appropriate option in the bot. Submitted photos and videos can be viewed and downloaded in the OneGPS web interface. This allows dispatchers to track work progress and keep supporting materials together with the related work data. Sending events to external systems The Webhook block has been added to Control room algorithms. It automatically sends event data to external services. Place the block in the required algorithm branch. When linking the algorithm to a notification, specify the recipient URL and the data to be sent. Access to event media files via API Added an API for retrieving photos and videos associated with ADAS and DMS events. You can request data for selected objects over a specified period and receive links to view the media files in a browser. The retrieved files can be used in external systems to analyze and review events. Admin panel New design The admin panel now features a new design and a higher-contrast color palette. The dark sidebar is visually separated from the light workspace. Selected rows, statuses, and data states are easier to distinguish, while large tables are easier to read. Search, filters, and record actions are located directly above the table, keeping the main tools close to the data. The user panel has moved from the upper-right corner to the bottom of the sidebar. It displays the user profile and provides quick access to: switching between light and dark themes selecting the interface language viewing news signing out collapsing the sidebar Along with the new design, the admin panel’s internal components have been updated. The interface now displays correctly in modern browsers, while tables, forms, and controls work consistently across all sections. Comms Cloud SIM card management OneGPS now supports Comms Cloud SIM cards. In one section, you can view SIM card details and status, open the linked vehicle, and block or unblock the SIM card when needed. Mobile app Fleet analytics The mobile app includes a new Dashboard tab for quick access to key fleet performance metrics. It displays an overview for the past 24 hours, including idle and operating time, mileage, and the number of vehicles. Vehicle and connectivity statuses, mileage, violations, geofences, and other metrics are presented in clear charts, making it easier to assess fleet performance and spot issues from your phone. AI Assistant The OneGPS AI Assistant is available in the mobile app. It helps users find features and explains how to complete common tasks, such as generating a report, configuring an object, creating a geofence, or connecting a device. Responses may include links to relevant documentation. Chats are saved in the history, so you can return to a previous conversation to review an answer or continue the discussion. Additional sign-in protection The OneGPS mobile app supports two-factor authentication. After entering your login and password, confirm the sign-in with a one-time code from an authenticator app or sent by email. This additional verification protects your account even if someone else obtains your password. Viewing events and notifications The Events section includes separate Notifications and Events tabs, making it easier to find the information you need. The first tab shows triggered notification rules, while the second displays changes in object and sensor states. For each tab, you can select objects, data types, and a viewing period separately. You can also view the location where an event or notification was recorded on the map. Balance top-ups via Stripe You can top up your organization’s balance through Stripe. In the Finance tab, enter the amount, proceed to payment, and select an available payment method on the Stripe page. This feature is available for accounts with Stripe payments enabled. After completing the payment, you can check the updated account balance directly in the app. OneGPS 7.10 introduces more tools for data analysis, process automation, and mobile access to the platform. These capabilities help users find the information they need faster and monitor fleet operations more effectively. We’ll continue to develop the platform and introduce new tools for fleet monitoring and management."},{"s":"release-notes","t":"Release notes","h":["Release notes"],"x":"Release notes In this section, you’ll find all updates and changes that have been released for the OneGPS system. Here you can see lists of new features, improvements, and bug fixes for the latest versions. Each version has a separate article describing its changes. Simply select the version you’re interested in to see what was added, changed, or fixed."},{"s":"pilot-7-9","t":"OneGPS 7.9","h":["OneGPS 7.9","Access management and security","Two-factor authentication via Email","Two types of tokens for accessing the monitoring service","User interface configuration","Integration enhancements","Telegram bot configuration","Links Field integration","Weather layer integration","Integration of equipment with Wirepas support","Admin panel","Account cloning","Report: Vehicle by sensor name","Module updates","Composite objects module — new interface","Service module enhancements","Quick downtime adding","Object analytics graph update","Event processing interval — Control room","BLE tag assignment in Routes module","Updated fuel analysis algorithm","Report builder","Service downtime tracking","Eco summary by event type","Speed at the moment of violation","Detailed fuel report","Connection loss","Driver performance control","Face ID for automatic driver assignment","Driver working hours","Driver working time tracking in the PTM module","Updated Eco driving logic and rankings","Notifications","Command templates in notifications","Account ID in Webhook notifications","New notification type: Alarm media","Notifications in object card","New API: Rental сontracts"],"x":"OneGPS 7.9 In version 7.9 we focused on improving usability, increasing configuration flexibility, and expanding analytics capabilities. In this section, we’ve gathered and described all key updates and improvements introduced in both releases. New tools for driver monitoring, access management, and notifications have been added. Reporting and integration capabilities have been expanded, and existing system modules have been further improved. Access management and security Two-factor authentication via Email The system now supports two-factor authentication (2FA) via email. When signing in, in addition to your login and password, you will need to enter a one-time code sent to your email address. This method adds an extra layer of security to your account and helps protect your data from unauthorized access. Two types of tokens for accessing the monitoring service The system now supports two types of tokens — secure links that provide access without sharing login credentials. You can choose the access format depending on your task: view-only or full login. Allows opening the Monitoring interface without exposing credentials — for example, to share the map with a client or provide temporary viewing access. Allows users to log in via a link without entering a password. The user is automatically authenticated with the same permissions as standard login. The token validity period can be limited or set as unlimited. User interface configuration You can now control the visibility of system sections and modules for each user in the Interface tab. Now you can: show or hide specific modules disable main menu tabs customize the workspace according to user roles Even if a module is enabled at the account level, it can be hidden for selected employees. Integration enhancements Telegram bot configuration Users can now quickly access the Telegram bot from their personal account. To configure: specify the bot configuration in the account settings add the bot address After saving, a dedicated tab with a link to the bot appears in the user’s personal cabinet. Links Field integration We integrated with the Links Field platform for SIM card management. When working with the Links Field provider, you can now: assign ICCID numbers to each vehicle configure API access receive SIM card data directly in the system use the provider to send SMS notifications Weather layer integration A Windy.com weather layer has been added to the map. Weather conditions are displayed directly in the system — over routes and monitored assets — helping with route planning and analysis. In the map settings, you can: enable the weather layer select a forecast model (depending on your subscription tier) choose display types: wind, precipitation, cloud cover, and other parameters A timeline panel with animation is available to review forecasts dynamically. Weather data synchronizes with the map and automatically updates during pan and zoom operations. Integration of equipment with Wirepas support The system now supports equipment using Wirepas technology for indoor navigation. This allows you to track the movement of objects inside buildings — where GPS signals are unavailable or unstable. The technology uses a network of wireless devices that form a self-organizing mesh network and transmit location data inside the building. This solution is suitable for monitoring vehicles, equipment, and personnel in warehouses, production facilities, and other indoor environments. It also helps control internal logistics and track movements within facilities. Admin panel Account cloning We expanded account cloning capabilities and simplified the creation of new accounts based on existing ones. When cloning in the Admin panel, you can choose which data to transfer: sensor templates custom object pricing payment email templates user assignments users with their permissions and interface settings reports from the Report builder geofences linked to the account Report: Vehicle by sensor name Added a new administrative report: Vehicle by sensor name. This report allows you to review sensors across accounts, verify naming consistency, and organize installed equipment records. It simplifies audits and improves data transparency. Module updates Composite objects module — new interface The Composite objects module is now available in the new interface. It allows you to combine the following into a single logical structure: stationary and mobile tanks input flow meters (fuel intake) output flow meters (fuel dispensing) related sensors Now you can: describe the complete fuel movement chain — from fuel intake to dispensing to the end consumer track storage, intake, and dispensing within a single structure analyze fuel inflow, outflow, balance, and possible losses across the entire system Create and configure composite objects, use them in monitoring and reports, and analyze system performance in one unified view. Service module enhancements We expanded the Service module to make maintenance planning and control more transparent. New fields in tables: current engine hours mileage until next service engine hours until next service You can now attach files to service records — photos, service reports, documents, or manuals. All files are stored with the record and are available for viewing and download. Access control has also been added. You can configure who can add, edit, or delete service records, and define access to module sections. Quick downtime adding You can now add downtime directly from the object’s context menu in the Onlinetab — without switching to the Servicemodule. Simply right-click the object and select Add downtime — the record will be created automatically. Object analytics graph update We’ve updated the analytics overview graph in the object card to make it easier to evaluate the total cost of ownership and make informed decisions based on both planned and actual data. In the Object analyticssection, you will now see a unified Analytics overview graph showing yearly data, including: planned and actual maintenance costs planned and actual fuel costs total cost of ownership changes in the object’s value over time cost per kilometer Event processing interval — Control room In the Control room module, we added a new setting: Event processing interval. It defines how long an event remains active and available for processing. Previously, unprocessed events were automatically deleted after 24 hours. Now the interval can be extended to 72 or 120 hours, allowing more time for review. BLE tag assignment in Routes module You can now assign BLE tags to route points and validate trip completion based on their signal. If the vehicle detects a BLE tag, the point is considered visited — even if GPS accuracy is low or the signal is jammed. As a result: routes are correctly validated even during satellite signal issues point visits are confirmed by physical vehicle presence arrival and departure are automatically recorded Updated fuel analysis algorithm The system now uses an improved algorithm for processing fuel sensor data. It allows more accurate detection of refueling and draining events, reduces the impact of sensor noise, and helps eliminate false events. The algorithm analyzes fuel level changes while considering several factors: volume thresholds, vehicle speed, fuel level change rate, and sensor data quality. This helps the system distinguish real fuel operations from normal fuel consumption or random fluctuations in sensor readings. Additional analysis parameters are now available in the fuel sensor settings, including: event volume thresholds, speed filter, speed evaluation mode, fuel level change rate filter, sensor data smoothing, and a mechanism for removing symmetric events. Report builder We expanded the Report builder to enable deeper fleet analysis and provide more actionable data in a familiar format. Service downtime tracking A new column has been added: Service downtime. You can now: see downtime per vehicle calculate total downtime for a selected period analyze maintenance downtime trends This helps evaluate the operational impact of maintenance and make data-driven decisions. Eco summary by event type For the Eco report type, summary analysis by event type is now available. New fields: Eco event type number of events average speed maximum speed You can now generate structured active safety summaries and analyze driving behavior more systematically. Speed at the moment of violation A new parameter has been added to reports: Speed at violation moment. You can now see the actual vehicle speed at the time the violation was triggered. Detailed fuel report Added a new report type: Fuel refills and drains. Each operation appears as a separate row with: time volume location fuel level You get a complete operation log and full transparency of fuel activity. Connection loss A new report type Connection Loss has been added. It helps you understand when and under what conditions a device loses connection to the system. The report shows the start and end time of the disconnection, its duration, the location where it occurred, the vehicle status at the moment the signal was lost, and the possible cause — for example, power loss or network issues. With this report, you can go beyond simply recording disconnection events. It helps you analyze situations more effectively: distinguish brief signal interruptions from real issues, identify suspicious cases, and respond to them faster. Driver performance control Face ID for automatic driver assignment The system now supports facial recognition via onboard cameras. Drivers can be assigned automatically based on Face ID results. Verification occurs: at ignition start periodically during the trip The system identifies who is driving, even when multiple drivers use the same vehicle, logs recognition results, and includes the data in reports. Driver working hours A new Work timetab has been added to the driver card. You can now configure: working days of the week time intervals for weekdays separate intervals for Saturday and Sunday If necessary, the system can restrict driver assignment outside the configured schedule. Driver working time tracking in the PTM module A working time check for drivers has been added to the OneGPS Task Manager (PTM) module when assigning tasks. Now, when creating a task, the system can analyze the driver’s work schedule. If the selected time interval falls within rest time or outside working hours, the system will display a warning to the user. To enable this check, activate the “Check working time” parameter — it is available for fields of type Datetime and Address. This feature helps dispatchers and logistics managers consider drivers’ working schedules when planning tasks and reduces the risk of assigning tasks during non-working hours. Updated Eco driving logic and rankings The Eco Driving scoring logic has been updated. Now the score is calculated per driver, considering all vehicles they operated. You can build rankings: by vehicles by drivers Violations are attributed to the specific driver rather than the vehicle, ensuring more accurate evaluation of driving behavior and better management decisions. Notifications Command templates in notifications Two new fields have been added to the notification creation and editing form: Command — manual text input Command template — selection from saved templates You can now either type a command manually or select a predefined template. Account ID in Webhook notifications Webhook notifications now include additional information — Account ID. Along with the event data, the system now sends the identifier of the account from which the notification was triggered. New notification type: Alarm media A new notification type has been added to the system. This notification is triggered when a device successfully uploads a media file (such as a photo or video) related to an event to the server. Unlike standard notifications, it is not triggered at the moment of the event, but only after the file has been successfully uploaded. This allows you to receive confirmation and immediately access media related to events, such as violations or ADAS/DMS alerts. Notifications in object card Toggle switches have been added to the asset card to manage notifications for individual vehicle limits. For the following parameters, notifications can be enabled or disabled separately: Speed limit Connection lost Idle For the Idle parameter, an ignition icon is available — it defines whether ignition status should be considered when calculating downtime. Limits continue to be recorded in the system even if notifications are disabled, ensuring monitoring without excessive alerts. New API: Rental сontracts A new API method has been added to retrieve a list of rental contracts from the Rent Car module. It allows you to access all types of contracts: active (open) reserved completed Each contract includes information about the renter, pickup and drop-off locations, scheduled pickup and return dates, as well as contract and vehicle numbers."},{"s":"pilot-7-8","t":"OneGPS 7.8","h":["OneGPS 7.8","Interface and data management improvements","New report access rights","Speed source selection","Temperature tracking on the route","Sensor values in the mobile app","Maintenance status display","Location sharing via Telegram","Downloading a video segment","New  module","Analytics update","New Features and integrations","AI Bot — your technical assistant","Toll roads: expense control and route planning","Jimi dashcam support","CMSV6 passenger counter integration","Wialon data migration wizard","Two-way communication via camera","Saving geofences with toll road settings","Send coordinates from the","Improved bus arrival prediction","Driving and rest time control in the  module","iButton Reuse Notification","Reports and automation","Custom intervals in the report scheduler","New waste container management report","Sensor templates for partner accounts","Fuel report update","Advanced sensor reports","Notification templates","Coordinates in the Speed violation report","OneGPS Mobile App Updates","Interface Updates","Dark Mode","Video Section","Admin Login","Vehicle Inspections"],"x":"OneGPS 7.8 In OneGPS 7.8, we’ve made the platform even more convenient and powerful. We’ve added useful tools, expanded reports, and improved data accuracy — so you can find the information you need faster and solve your daily tasks with ease. Interface and data management improvements New report access rights You can now manage report access more flexibly: set separate permissions for viewing, editing, and deleting reports. For example, you can allow an employee to view reports without being able to edit or delete them. This helps protect your reports from accidental changes or deletions. Speed source selection In object settings, you can now choose the speed source: use GPS by default or select another field, such as CAN bus data. This feature helps you calculate mileage and driving time more accurately, and better support different vehicle types. You can also set the desired units of measurement when choosing the speed source. Temperature tracking on the route When you click on the temperature graph in a report, you’ll instantly see where the vehicle was on the map at that moment. This makes it easy to track the relationship between temperature changes and the route. Sensor values in the mobile app You can now choose whether to display sensor values in the mobile app. If the option is enabled, the sensor value will be visible on your smartphone; if disabled, it won’t appear in the mobile version. Maintenance status display Vehicles with the Under maintenance status are now more visible in the system — they appear in the general list with a wrench icon. Clicking the icon shows you the current maintenance schedule and details. Location sharing via Telegram You can now use the updated location sharing feature via Telegram. It works with the latest version of the Telegram API and is even more stable and accurate. The updated Telegram bot can be used as a mobile tracker: send your location directly from the app, and the system will receive this data in real time. Downloading a video segment You can now export just the part you need from videos captured by connected devices. Open a recording, set the start and end times, and download a short clip—no extra minutes. Choose the exact interval to get a separate file, save time and data by skipping the full download, and prepare incident materials faster. New Points of interest module We’ve added Points of interest so you can mark important locations on the map: offices, warehouses, parking lots, client addresses, and more. For each POI, you can set coordinates, choose an icon, add a description, and group them by purpose. Analytics update The connection status chart for vehicles on the analytics dashboard has been updated. The chart now shows the percentage breakdown by connection status, so you can instantly see what portion of your fleet is currently online and which vehicles haven’t connected for a long time. Vehicle ratings can now be grouped by model, so you can compare data for individual vehicles or by model overall. New Features and integrations AI Bot — your technical assistant We’ve created a smart assistant that acts as a technical consultant. With the AI bot, you can quickly find answers to your questions and get help right inside the system. The bot is available in all languages and is always ready to help you: use the platform’s features find the information you need solve technical issues more easily Like any AI-based system, the bot may occasionally make mistakes. For important details, it’s best to double-check or contact support. Your feedback will help us make the bot even more useful. Toll roads: expense control and route planning Use the Toll roads feature in the Geofences module to track and manage toll road segments. With this feature, you can: monitor expenses and see when and how much your vehicles use toll roads take toll segments into account when planning routes analyze trips — toll road data is saved and available in reports For more details on how to work with toll roads and set up entry and exit points, see the user guide. Jimi dashcam support You can now connect and use the Jimi JC400 dashcam for video recording and transmission. CMSV6 passenger counter integration We’ve added support for CMSV6 passenger counting cameras. Based on this data, you now have two new reports: Number of boardings and alightings in each departure and arrival geofence Overall passenger flow statistics for all routes over a selected period These reports help you better understand route occupancy and plan transport operations more effectively. Wialon data migration wizard Transferring objects and events from Wialon to OneGPS is now easier than ever. We’ve created a dedicated migration wizard module — Import Wialon. With the migration wizard, you can: Create data transfer tasks Select the objects you want to migrate Track the status of each migration step Two-way communication via camera If a video device is connected, you can talk to the driver through the camera like a call. Open the camera and tap the Intercom icon — the driver will hear you through the device speaker and can reply. With Intercom, you connect quickly without extra calls: right in the camera view you can clarify the situation on site, give instructions, and get immediate voice confirmation. Saving geofences with toll road settings You can now save and transfer geofences along with toll road and scheduler settings. Use the export and import buttons — all parameters will be preserved. Send coordinates from the Control room The Control room module now has a button for quickly copying a link to a vehicle’s current location. You can copy the link in one click and send it to emergency services, colleagues, or other recipients — for example, in case of theft, an emergency, or to clarify a route. Improved bus arrival prediction In the Bus line module, bus arrival predictions are now more accurate: arrival times are calculated based on the actual route along the roads. The Online tab now includes three new columns: Bus line name Next stop Estimated arrival time at the next stop You can instantly see which bus is on which route, where it’s headed, and when it will arrive at the next stop. Driving and rest time control in the Eco-Driving module In the Eco-Driving module, you can set up rules to control drivers’ driving and rest times. You can define: how long a driver can be behind the wheel how much rest is required between trips daily and weekly rest periods The system automatically monitors these parameters and alerts you if any limits are exceeded. This feature is based on tachograph logic, helping you comply with work and rest regulations and ensuring driver safety. iButton Reuse Notification A new notification type has been added. It triggers when the same iButton is used on multiple vehicles within a set time period. This helps you quickly detect driver changes and avoid errors in linking “driver — vehicle.” For the notification to work, vehicles must have an iButton sensor installed, and the Drivers and Notifications modules must be enabled. Reports and automation Custom intervals in the report scheduler You can now set any interval for report delivery in the scheduler — every 5 minutes, every hour, or any other period. Reports are generated for completed intervals; for example, if a report is set to be sent every 15 minutes, at 10:00 you’ll receive data for 9:45–10:00. This lets you tailor the schedule to your needs and get exactly the data you want. New waste container management report A new report helps you analyze and optimize waste collection. It includes two sections: Shows how many times each container was serviced during the selected period. This helps you: Check if the collection schedule is being followed Identify containers with high or low usage Compare zones and container types Shows locations where containers were full. Each circle indicates how many times a container was full during the period. The map helps you: Identify areas with frequent overflows Find regions where collection frequency needs to be increased Adjust routes and send vehicles to the busiest zones Sensor templates for partner accounts You can now create a sensor template for an entire partner account and apply it to all relevant objects with a specific device type. In the interface, you can select and apply the template to all objects at once — no need to configure each object individually. Fuel report update The fuel report, which calculates consumption based on sensor readings, now includes tables for refueling and draining events. You can now see both consumption and all refueling/draining events with dates and volumes in a single report. Advanced sensor reports The report builder now lets you create sensor reports with new parameters. You can include: Geofence name Ignition status Door status Temperature Humidity You can see all vehicle and sensor parameters in one report, as well as the geofence where each event occurred. All necessary columns can be flexibly added via the report builder. Notification templates You can now create notification templates and transfer them between accounts. Save notification settings from one account and apply them to another. This feature helps you quickly set up notifications for new or similar accounts and saves time. Coordinates in the Speed violation report Every entry now includes precise coordinates. See exactly where each violation happened, discuss it with the driver using clear facts, and spot risky road sections to refine routes. OneGPS Mobile App Updates In the new version of the OneGPS mobile app, we refreshed the interface, brought back Dark Mode, and added useful features. We also fixed bugs and made the app more stable. Now using OneGPS is even easier and more convenient. Interface Updates We updated the color palette and synchronized it with the web version. Now the colors are consistent across all platforms. Contrast and shades were improved to make the interface easier to read and more comfortable on any screen, giving the system a cleaner and more consistent look. Markers and icons are now clearer and more noticeable, while some elements were rearranged so important data can be found faster in everyday scenarios. Dark Mode We brought back Dark Mode. You can switch between light and dark themes, while by default the app follows your system settings. Video Section In the Video tab, you can now see previews of all connected cameras at once, making it easier to assess the picture and switch to the one you need. We completely redesigned the player for both live streams and video history: Modern design and simple controls Switch between cameras with arrows Turn sound on or off Take screenshots Expand video to full screen Admin Login Administrators can now log into the app with their admin credentials and immediately see profiles of all users under the contract. It’s easy to switch between accounts, help colleagues, and manage settings. Vehicle Inspections The app now includes the “Inspections” tool from the Service module. You can check vehicle condition directly from your phone: Mark vehicle condition using ready-made forms Add comments and photos Confirm results with a signature Review inspection history to detect issues in advance and reduce downtime risks"},{"s":"pilot-7-7","t":"OneGPS 7.7","h":["OneGPS 7.7","Updated user interface for enhanced usability","Modernized interface design","Refined colour palette","Streamlined settings menu","Optimized map tools","Enhanced navigation with a new menu structure","Feature enhancements for improved operations","New Weighing module","Upgraded Service module with vehicle inspections","API enhancements for greater flexibility","Improved object management via API","Expanded API for vehicle addition","Revamped API for bus route management","Advanced API for Eco-driving data retrieval","The Eco-driving API now includes additional data points:","Enhanced reporting & Data analysis","ICCID field for SIM card identification","Time-stamped notifications","Map display improvements","Driver work data recalculation","Direct object deletion from admin panel","Enhanced data security for relayed configurations","Offline device report","Excel export for temperature reports","New monthly mileage report","Time interval selection in refrigeration reports","Paid toll report with geofence grouping","New Eco-driving reports","Enhanced crash detection report"],"x":"OneGPS 7.7 The OneGPS 7.7 release introduces significant upgrades, including a refreshed interface design, expanded API capabilities, improved reporting functionalities, and new operational tools. These enhancements are designed to streamline the management of assets, contracts, and routes, making the system more efficient and user-friendly. Updated user interface for enhanced usability Modernized interface design To provide a more intuitive and visually appealing user experience, we have redesigned the user interface. The new layout improves navigation, making essential functions easier to access while maintaining a clean, structured, and professional appearance. Refined colour palette A revamped colour scheme enhances readability and comfort, ensuring better visibility across different screens. The improved contrast and refreshed aesthetic make working with the system more seamless and engaging. Streamlined settings menu All infrequently used functions — such as language selection, theme switching, news updates, and support access—have been consolidated into a single, well-organized settings menu. This update declutters the interface, freeing up screen space for core functionalities. Optimized map tools All map-related tools have been moved to a dedicated panel in the bottom right corner, making them easier to locate and use. Users no longer need to search across different sections of the screen to access essential mapping features. Enhanced navigation with a new menu structure The primary sections remain at the top of the interface. Subsection tabs have been relocated to the left side for quicker navigation. This new layout simplifies workflows, ensuring users can find relevant information with fewer clicks and minimal effort. Feature enhancements for improved operations New Weighing module A dedicated Weighing module has been introduced to monitor and control vehicle weighing operations. Users can now: Add new weighing stations. Track historical weighing data. Export weight records for auditing and compliance. Access a step-by-step guide on module setup in the updated documentation. Upgraded Service module with vehicle inspections We have improved the module structure and added a new feature — a technical inspection for cars. Now, you can check your vehicle before driving or transferring it to someone else. With this new tool, you can: Create inspection reports for specific vehicles Answer security questions Attach photos and documents Note the condition of parts This new feature helps quickly assess the vehicle's condition and increases safety in the fleet. API enhancements for greater flexibility Improved object management via API A new API function enables the quick transfer of vehicles between accounts using a simple API request. Users only need to specify the vehicle ID and new account details, and the system will automatically move the asset. Expanded API for vehicle addition Users can now register new vehicles with a MAC address, allowing the system to automatically generate entries in the “Buses” section. Dual SIM support has been added for devices with two SIM card slots, improving network redundancy. Revamped API for bus route management The API endpoint for creating and assigning buses to routes has been updated. All related API queries must now be sent to the new designated address. Advanced API for Eco-driving data retrieval The Eco-driving API now includes additional data points: Account number & name Vehicle name & type Unique GPS tracker ID Users can retrieve data for all vehicles linked to an account in a single request, enhancing reporting efficiency. Enhanced reporting & Data analysis ICCID field for SIM card identification A new ICCID field has been added to each object card, enabling: Unique SIM card identification Simplified SIM data entry & management Automatic retrieval of missing ICCID data when integrated with a SIM provider. Time-stamped notifications Every notification now includes a precise timestamp. When returning after a network disconnection, users can see the exact sequence of events in SMS and push notifications. Map display improvements Users can now toggle between routes and stops, allowing for a clearer view of route paths without clutter. Driver work data recalculation Admins can now recalculate driver performance data for any specified period, ensuring report accuracy. Direct object deletion from admin panel The Delete button has been added directly to the Vehicles tab in the Admin panel, eliminating the need to switch interfaces. Enhanced data security for relayed configurations Admins now have greater control over sensitive relay configurations, including: Hiding tokens, passwords, and usernames from partners. Maintaining security compliance while allowing necessary access. Offline device report A new Offline devices report provides an overview of all objects that are currently disconnected. Users can schedule and automate report generation for better monitoring and response planning. Excel export for temperature reports Users can download temperature reports in Excel format, making it easier to: Analyse historical temperature data. Create custom charts and insights. New monthly mileage report Introduces a detailed breakdown of monthly and total mileage for each vehicle over a specified period. Time interval selection in refrigeration reports Users can now customize time intervals when generating refrigeration system reports, allowing for: More precise monitoring. Enhanced performance tracking. Paid toll report with geofence grouping The paid toll report now displays: The specific geofence where the toll is located. The group of geofences the toll belongs to for better categorization. New Eco-driving reports 5 New Eco-driving reports that will assist with driver behaviour management. These reports include data related to road speed violations and indicating lowest performing drivers/vehicles according to eco scores. Enhanced crash detection report The crash detection report will now display the direction of travel when impact was detected."},{"s":"pilot-7-6","t":"OneGPS 7.6","h":["OneGPS 7.6","Enhanced security","Improved Telegram bot","Additional account protection","Created a new API","New relay protocol","Integrated Hikvision video devices","Language selection in Report scheduler","Sound alerts for notifications","Access to user manual","Car vanufacturing year optional","Parking duration in history","Accurate display of connection absence time","Tachograph module in new interface","Tracking changes in driver history","New in working with maps","Infrared map with separate passenger traffic statistics","Report date in email","Saving service notifications","New fields and data synchronization feature in FOTA","Added last parking to report","Fixed error with service addition button","Updated interfaces for  and  sections","Added filtering by additional fields","New driver fields in the report"],"x":"OneGPS 7.6 In the version of OneGPS we have enhanced the security of our clients' data. We've expanded functionality and improved system stability by adding new features and fixing bugs. Enhanced security We have implemented additional protection for user data. Improved password security: they are now stored in encrypted form using a modern and reliable encryption algorithm. Improved Telegram bot We have updated our Telegram bot. Worked on stability — no more unexpected failures and re-authentication. Added the option to choose the language of communication with the bot. Made car search faster — just list the cars you're interested in, and the bot will immediately provide the information. Additional account protection Provided the option for partners to make two-factor authentication mandatory for all users when logging into the system. Now, in addition to login and password, the user must enter a one-time code from an authenticator app. Created a new API With one request, you can get the most important information about your vehicle. The new API provides a report that includes: distance traveled, cases of speeding, sudden braking, and seat belt violations. New relay protocol Added the Tripia relay protocol for transmitting vehicle data. Integrated Hikvision video devices Added support for several Hikvision mobile devices: a portable video recorder for recording videos, a car recorder for recording trips, and a camera that automatically recognizes license plates. These devices are now available for connection and management in our system. Language selection in Report scheduler Added the ability to choose a language for report generation in the scheduler settings. You can select the desired language from the dropdown list, which will be applied to reports regardless of the system interface language. Sound alerts for notifications Sound alerts for notifications are available again in the system. The feature can be disabled if necessary. Access to user manual Added a Manual tab in the interface. Account users can view documentation attached by administrators. Car vanufacturing year optional The Manufacturing year field has become optional. You can create new cars without specifying the manufacturing year if this information is unknown or not important. Save the car without specifying the year and edit these details later. Parking duration in history Information about the number and duration of parking has been added to the vehicle movement history. Now you can see how long each vehicle was parked. Accurate display of connection absence time Improved the display of the No connection status. The system shows the exact time of connection absence in hours, days, months. Tachograph module in new interface Added Tachograph to the updated interface. Now it's easy to manage module settings and view reports. Tracking changes in driver history Added a feature in Audit history that allows administrators to track changes in driver history, even if records were deleted. New in working with maps You can choose which map will open first by default. Also, the option to hide unused maps is available. Infrared map with separate passenger traffic statistics Added an infrared map to the Passenger traffic statistics report, which visually displays separate statistics for passengers boarding and alighting. Report date in email The email subject now includes the report formation date. Saving service notifications If you receive a notification from the Service module but miss it, information about it will automatically be saved in Events New fields and data synchronization feature in FOTA In the FOTA interface for machines with Teltonika devices, new fields have been added — ICCID, group identifier, and description, which help identify SIM cards, accounts, and vehicles. The synchronization button allows updating data between FOTA and OneGPS and automatically populating the vehicle card Added last parking to report Fixed an issue in the Trips and parking report where parking after trips was not displayed. Now each trip in the report ends with a parking entry. Fixed error with service addition button Clicking the Add new service button will open a popup window with a form to create a service. Updated interfaces for History and Reports sections Now there is the possibility to display additional fields. You can customize the display of information in these sections according to your tasks. Added filtering by additional fields Added the ability to filter by additional fields that you create for vehicles and drivers. When adding a new field in the field editor, the search and filtering function for this parameter becomes automatically available. New driver fields in the report Added new fields with driver information in the \"Report designer.\" Now the report can include the driver's name, group, driver's license expiration date, phone number, tags, and description."},{"s":"system-requirements","t":"Before you start","h":["Before you start","Computer requirements","Internet connection","Supported mobile operating systems","If the platform is running slowly","Additional recommendations"],"x":"Before you start Please review these system requirements to ensure stable operation with the OneGPS platform. Supported browsers OneGPS works in any modern browser: Google Chrome Mozilla Firefox Opera Microsoft Edge Always keep your browser updated to the latest version. Computer requirements RAM 1 GB 4–8 GB Processor 1.2 GHz 1.6 GHz or higher Screen resolution 1280 x 768 px 1920 x 1080 px or higher If you work with a large number of objects (more than 200), we recommend using a computer with at least 8 GB of RAM. Internet connection You’ll need an internet connection with a speed of at least 1 Mbps. A stable connection is important, especially if multiple users are working at the same time. Supported mobile operating systems Android: version 10 or higher iOS: version 14 or higher Always keep your mobile operating system up to date for best performance. If the platform is running slowly Close unnecessary tabs and programs Turn off extra map elements: objects, geofences, tracks Use clustering to group markers on the map when working with many objects for better readability Enable only the elements you need Additional recommendations Regularly clear your browser cache Check your antivirus settings If you experience slowdowns, restart your browser Use a wired internet connection if possible"},{"s":"glossary","t":"Glossary","h":["Glossary","User","Administrator","Account","Authorization","Authentication","AccountID","Object","AgentID","Device ID","Tracker","Sensor","Module","Dashboard","Object card","Object menu","Cache","Contract","Mapping contract","Geofence","Geocoder","Events","Alerts","Push notification","Report","History","Data transmission","API","Software version","Unix timestamp","Token","Tag","SMS tracker setup"],"x":"Glossary Before you start working with the system, we recommend getting familiar with the main concepts and terms: User a person working with the OneGPS monitoring system. Each user has a certain access level depending on their role. Some users can only see basic information, while others have access to system settings. Administrator a user of the admin panel who is responsible for configuring and managing the monitoring system. Account a personal user profile in the monitoring system. Through an account, a user gets access to their data and system features. The account stores personal information, login, password, contact details, and individual interface settings. Authorization defines a user’s access rights to system features and data after a successful login. Authentication confirming a user’s identity when logging in with a username and password. AccountID a unique identifier for a user’s account in the system, used to identify the user during login and operation. Object a vehicle, equipment, person, animal, or any other moving or stationary item being monitored. AgentID a unique number for a device or monitored object in the system, such as a GPS tracker on a vehicle. Device ID a unique number for a device installed on an object. Tracker a compact electronic device for tracking an object’s movement. Once activated, it automatically determines the current location and establishes an internet connection to transmit data. The tracker is configured via direct connection to a computer using special software. Sensor a device that collects and transmits data about a specific parameter or the state of an object. Система и интерфейс Module a separate part of the system with a specific set of features. Modules can be connected to the main system and work independently or together with other modules. Dashboard a control panel that displays key information in a visual format. The dashboard shows main indicators, charts, and quick access to important features. Object card a place where you can edit object information and configure its settings. Object menu a list of available actions you can perform with the selected object. Cache temporary data storage. Contract the main element in the system that brings together all monitored objects, users, and available features for a client or company. The contract specifies which devices are tracked and who can access them. Mapping contract a type of contract that combines data from multiple accounts or contracts into a single interface. It acts as a “mirror,” reflecting information from other accounts without directly changing them. Geofence a virtual restricted area on the map. Geocoder a tool that converts GPS coordinates into regular addresses. Events changes in object parameters, such as speeding, leaving a geofence, starting movement, or fuel drain. Alerts notifications about important events or deviations from set parameters, sent to the user via email, SMS, or messengers. Push notification an event notification sent to a mobile phone. Report collected information about monitored objects for a selected period. History the movement history of an object or the history of a sensor’s operation. Data transmission the process of collecting and sending information from devices installed on objects, via mobile networks, to the central server. Data on location, speed, and other parameters is processed, filtered, and stored in the database for further analysis and use. API a set of rules and tools for integrating different software systems. The API is used to exchange data between systems, services, applications, and the OneGPS platform. Software version the release number of the software, showing which version is currently in use. Each new version includes updates, fixes, or new features. Unix timestamp a numeric time format showing the number of seconds since January 1, 1970. Token a unique access code. Tag a special label for searching objects. SMS tracker setup a way to remotely configure a tracker using SMS commands. A text message with a specific instruction is sent to the SIM card in the device. Each SMS changes only one tracker parameter. After executing the command, the device sends a confirmation message."},{"s":"roles-and-access-rights","t":"Roles and access rights","h":["Roles and access rights","User account roles","Admin panel roles","Master status"],"x":"Roles and access rights OneGPS uses a hierarchical structure of roles and access rights. This hierarchy helps separate permissions for different types of users. For example, a company may have drivers, logistics staff, managers, and a director. All of them need access to information about vehicles, assets, and routes, but not to everything at once. A driver only needs to see their own vehicle and route. The director, on the other hand, needs to see all vehicles, assign drivers, and configure the system for the company. Each user only sees what they need for their job. Drivers aren’t distracted by unnecessary data, while the director has everything at their fingertips. That’s why OneGPS offers several user types, each with different access rights. User account roles 1. Administrator Has full access to all features in the user portal. 2. User A regular user of the system. They work through the user portal, but their permissions are limited. The administrator decides which actions the user can perform and which are restricted. Admin panel roles 1. Main administrator Usually technical support staff with unlimited access to all data and settings. They can perform any action in the system, including managing roles and permissions. 2. Administrator A OneGPS partner who sets up and integrates the monitoring system for different companies and fleets. They have limited rights but can view all fleets they work with, all contracts, vehicles, and all necessary information. 3. User An administrator (partner) can create additional users for their staff in the admin panel and assign them specific access rights within their own permissions. Master status In addition to roles, there is a special user status called Master. This status is assigned when logging in through the admin panel. A user with Master status has unlimited rights in the system."},{"s":"getting-started","t":"Getting started","h":["Getting started"],"x":"Getting started This section will help you quickly get familiar with your user account and start working with the system. Here you’ll learn how to: log in to your account use the main screen add an object add a sensor work with history generate a report You can find more detailed information about these features in the User accountsection"},{"s":"logging-in-1","t":"Logging in","h":["Logging in","How to get started with OneGPS:"],"x":"Logging in How to get started with OneGPS: 1. Open your web browser 2. In the address bar, enter the platform address: https://OneGPS-gps.com/ 3. Press Enter on your keyboard 4. You will see the login page with fields for your username and password 5. Enter the username and password you received from your contract administrator 6. Select your preferred interface language 7. Click the Log In button 1 — Enter your login 2 — Enter your password 3 — Choose your language 4 — Press Enter"},{"s":"interface-overview-1","t":"Interface оverview","h":["Interface оverview","Top panel","Workspace","Map"],"x":"Interface оverview After logging in, you’ll land on the main page. Here’s a quick introduction to the key interface elements to help you easily navigate the system. Top panel The top panel contains the main controls: Main menu with the primary sections of the system for navigation Object statuses showing the current state of your objects Username and balance — your personal information and available funds Notifications icon — click to see all your notifications Contract configuration settings AI Bot — technical assistant Additional settings menu, which includes: — theme color selection — language selection — access to news — contact technical support — log out button Workspace In the workspace, you can interact with the elements related to the current section and tab you’ve selected from the navigation menu. Map The map displays the real-time location of your objects with accurate GPS coordinates. You can monitor object movements and track their routes. The map also offers extra settings and tools for working with it. For a detailed description of the interface, please see the User account section."},{"s":"how-to-add-an-object-1","t":"How to add an object","h":["How to add an object","To add a new object:"],"x":"How to add an object An object can be a vehicle, equipment, person, animal, or any other moving or stationary item you want to monitor. To add a new object: 1. Click the Add object button 2. In the object card, fill in the required fields with information about the object 1 — Enter the vehicle number or object name 2 — Enter the unique device ID. The device ID can be the IMEI, which is usually printed on the device itself 3 — Select the type of device installed on the object The GPS device must be on the list of supported equipment. 3. Click Save — the object will appear in the list in your workspace. If you need to add a different type of object, select the appropriate type in the Object type field: special equipment, trailer, bus or another type. You can fill in the rest of the object’s information later, whenever it’s convenient or if you need to add more details. For more about adding extra information, see the Objects section."},{"s":"how-to-add-a-sensor-1","t":"How to add a sensor","h":["How to add a sensor","To add a sensor:"],"x":"How to add a sensor A sensor is a device that collects and transmits data about a specific parameter or the status of the monitored object. To add a sensor: 1. Open the object menu. To do this, right-click on the object in the list on the Online tab 1 — Object menu 2 — Sensors tab 2. Go to the Sensors tab in the object menu 3. In the Sensors tab, click the button to add a new sensor 4. Select the type of sensor you want to add, for example, an ignition sensor 5. Fill in the required fields: enter the sensor name and description select the field where the device sends the sensor’s data set threshold values, data update frequency, and other parameters as needed 6. Click Save Make sure the sensor appears in the list of active sensors and is transmitting data correctly."},{"s":"how-to-view-history-1","t":"How to view history","h":["How to view history","To view an object’s movement history:","List of selected tracks"],"x":"How to view history History shows information about an object’s past movements and statuses, including routes, stops, and sensor readings for a selected period. A track is a line on the map that shows the object’s movement path. It consists of points marking where the object was at different times. To view an object’s movement history: 1. Go to the Historysection in the top panel. Select the object from the list 2. Set the time range. Choose the desired time period by clicking the calendar icon 3. Click the Search button 4. In the search results, select the track records you want to view. Check the boxes next to the tracks you’re interested in. After you select the tracks, the following elements will appear on the screen: List of selected tracks 2. Player — a tool for playing and controlling track playback. 3. Events — a window showing important events or changes related to the object 4. Routes on the map 5. Chart shows the object’s sensor readings during the selected period."},{"s":"how-to-generate-a-report-1","t":"How to generate a report","h":["How to generate a report","To view a report:"],"x":"How to generate a report A report is a summary of information about monitored objects for a selected period. To view a report: 1. Go to the Reports section in the main menu. Select the object by checking the box next to it in the list 2. Set the time range for the report. Choose the desired time period by clicking the calendar icon 3. Select the report type from the dropdown list Set the report parameters: 4. Split. Choose whether to split the report by days, weeks, or not split it into intervals. 5. Report grouping. Choose to group the report by dates (to analyze changes over time) or by monitored objects (to compare different objects). 6. Sensor tags. Select one or more sensor groups to include in the report. 7. Click the Build report button After you click Build report, the report will appear in your chosen format. You can save the finished report as a CSV, Excel, or PDF file, or print it out."},{"s":"user-interface","t":"User account","h":["User account"],"x":"User account The User account in OneGPS is the main interface for tracking and managing vehicles, equipment, people, facilities, and other assets. Who uses the user account: Fleet managers Logistics department heads Business owners Private individuals tracking personal assets The OneGPS user account is a tool for monitoring your assets. You can see where your vehicles, equipment, or staff are located, track their movements, and get detailed reports."},{"s":"logging-in-2","t":"Logging in","h":["Logging in","Forgot your password","Signing in to your account with two-factor authentication","TOTP Authentication","How to Log In with TOTP:","Email Two-Factor Authentication","How to log in with Email 2FA"],"x":"Logging in To sign in to your account, follow these steps: Enter the username provided by your contract administrator. 2. Enter your password. To show the password you’ve typed, click the show/hide icon . 3. Choose the interface language. You can change it later in Settings 4. Check the Remember me box if you want the system to remember your login details. This feature will sign you in automatically the next time you open this page. It also helps quickly restore your session if the connection is unexpectedly interrupted, so you won’t have to re-enter your username and password each time. 5. Click Enter Forgot your password If you already have a OneGPS account and forgot your password, follow these steps: 1. On the sign-in page, click Forgot your password? 2. In the window that opens, enter your username and click Send password. If the information you entered matches our records, you’ll receive an email at the address you used during registration. 3. Open the email and follow the link provided 4. Create and enter a new password 5. Click Set password 6. After you change your password, you’ll be signed in to your account automatically. If you started the password recovery process by mistake, simply delete the email and continue using your existing credentials. Signing in to your account with two-factor authentication Two-Factor Authentication (2FA) is an additional layer of security for your account. In addition to your username and password, a one-time code is required to log in. This significantly reduces the risk of unauthorized access, even if your password is compromised. In the OneGPS system, two 2FA methods are available: TOTP Authentication — via an authenticator app Email Authentication — via a verification code sent to your email Two-factor authentication must be enabled by your contract administrator. TOTP Authentication TOTP authentication adds an extra layer of protection by requiring a temporary code from a mobile app. How to Enable TOTP: Before your first sign-in, install an authenticator app for iOS or Android: 2. In the Privacy tab, find the Two-factor authentication section 3. Click Enable 4. Scan the QR code using the authenticator app you installed 5. Enter the code from the app How to Log In with TOTP: Open your browser and go to https://OneGPS-gps.com/ 2. Enter your credentials: login and password 3. Click Enter 4. When prompted, enter the six-digit code from your authenticator app. Open the app on your smartphone, find the six-digit code, and enter it in the field on your computer. The code refreshes every 30 seconds. If it changes before you finish entering it, wait for the next one and enter that. 5. Click Log in Email Two-Factor Authentication with this method, after entering your username and password, the system will send a one-time code to your email. How to Enable Email 2FA 1. Go to the Privacy tab → Two-Factor authentication section 2. ClickEnable next to Email Authentication 3. Select a verified email address from the list 4. Click Send code. A confirmation code will be sent to the selected email 5. Enter the code from the email and confirm How to log in with Email 2FA 1. Open your browser and go to: https://OneGPS-gps.com/ 2. Enter your username and password 3. Click Log in 4. Enter the one-time code sent to your email 5. Click Log in"},{"s":"user-account-interface","t":"User account interface","h":["User account interface"],"x":"User account interface After you sign in, you arrive at the account home page. The interface includes the following main areas: Top panel Workspace Map 1 — Top panel 2 — Workspace 3 — Map In some parts of the interface, you may see an icon . Click it to view explanatory information about terms, buttons, and fields."},{"s":"top-panel","t":"Top panel","h":["Top panel","Navigation menu","The navigation menu is organized into two levels:","Main menu","Section menu","Managing the section menu view:","How to use the navigation menu:","Object statuses","Inactive objects status","Username and balance","Notifications","Account configuration settings","AI Assistant","How to use the AI Assistant:","Additional settings menu"],"x":"Top panel Top panel includes: Navigation menu Object statuses Username and balance Notifications icon Account configuration settings AI Assistant Additional settings menu Navigation menu The navigation menu is organized into two levels: Main menu Located on the top bar and contains the system’s main sections: Online,Reports,History, and others. The set of sections in the main menu depends on the modules enabled for your account. You can reorder sections on the top bar by dragging them. This lets you place frequently used sections first for quick access. Section menu Displayed on the left side of the interface and changes depending on the section selected in the main menu. Contains tabs for working with detailed information and tools for the current section. For example, when you select the Main section in the main menu, the left-side menu shows corresponding tabs with more in-depth information for that area. Managing the section menu view: Full view — shows icons and the names of all tabs. Compact view — collapses to icons only, freeing up more space for the main content. To switch between modes, click the arrow at the bottom of the menu. A left arrow collapses the menu to icons. A right arrow expands it to show all tab names. How to use the navigation menu: Select the desired section in the main menu on the top panel 2. Open the relevant tab in the section menu on the left 3. Work with the information or perform actions on the page that opens Object statuses The top panel also shows the number of objects and their statuses. By default, the status palette includes: total number of objects number of objects that are moving number of objects that are parked number of objects idling in a zone number of inactive objects You can customize the status palette on the top bar. Learn how to do this here. Clicking a status automatically filters the objects list by that status. For example, click the Moving status to show only moving objects. Inactive objects status When you click the inactive status icon, a window opens with a complete list of inactive objects and the duration of inactivity for each. In this window, you can review details for any object and, if needed, download the entire list to Excel. Username and balance The top panel displays the username you are registered under. Next to it, you’ll see the current account balance, which shows the funds available in your account. Notifications In OneGPS, you can set up notifications about events related to an object: speeding, idling, refueling, violations, entering or exiting a geofence, and more. Requirements: The Notifications module must be enabled in your contract 2. Notification parameters must be configured in advance To enable the Notificationsmodule, contact your contract administrator To view your received notifications, click the notifications icon on the top bar. For detailed setup instructions, see the article about the Notifications module. Account configuration settings When you click the contract settings icon , the Account settings section opens, where you can configure various system parameters yourself: Privacy and data access Interface appearance and units of measurement Display of addresses and events on the map Access to reports Additional geographic data (KML/KMZ) Object tags User account management Scheduled report distribution Access tokens You can find a detailed description of all available settings and how to change them in the next section,Account settings AI Assistant The AI bot is a smart helper that acts as a technical consultant. It helps you find answers faster and get assistance right inside the system. The bot is available in all languages and can help you with: How to use the platform’s features Where to find the information you need How to solve technical tasks more easily How to use the AI Assistant: Click the icon on the top panel 2. A chat window will open. On the left, you’ll see the list of chats and a New Chatbutton. On the right, there’s the message input field. 3. Type your question in the input field at the bottom right and press Enter To return to a previous conversation or delete a conversation, use the icons and next to the chat. In the top-right corner of the chat window, there is a three-dots button . Click it to open a menu with: Restart application — quickly restart the assistant if something goes wrong Settings — here you can: — enable wide mode for a more comfortable chat view — choose the app theme Print conversation — print your chat with the assistant Screen recording — record a video of your screen: — you can include audio — press Esc to stop recording — click Start recording to begin About — view the app version and developer information As with any AI-based system, the bot may sometimes make mistakes. It’s best to double-check important details or contact Support for clarification. Watch the video to learn how to activate and use the AI Bot. Additional settings menu The menu is located on the right side of the top panel and opens when you click the icon . Here you’ll find options to tailor your experience with the platform: Interface theme Choose the display mode that’s easiest on your eyes: use the light theme for daytime work, and the dark theme to reduce eye strain in the evening or at night. Language Select your preferred interface language. News center The News feature helps you stay up to date with platform and company updates. Click the tab to open a window with the latest news. Technical support Click theSupport button to contact our support team. Describe your situation, and OneGPS specialists will help resolve it. Log out Use the Log out button to exit your account."},{"s":"workspace","t":"Workspace","h":["Workspace"],"x":"Workspace In the workspace, you can work with the information for the current section and its tabs. To change the width of the workspace, hover your mouse over the divider and drag it left or right. Divider"},{"s":"map","t":"Map","h":["Map","Searching for objects on the map","Moving around the map"],"x":"Map The map is a tool that visually shows what’s happening with your objects. The map displays: Monitoring objects Their current location Movement routes Key geographic points and places Geofences Additional object details, such as status, speed, and direction The map is available across tabs and sections and usually takes up most of the screen. To get the widest map view, you can hide the workspace. Click the divider to collapse it. If you want to see only the map and hide all panels, headers, and browser controls, press F11 to enter full-screen mode. Searching for objects on the map When you select objects in the list, they automatically appear on the map. 1 — Selecting an object in the list. To select an object, check the box next to it 2 — Object on the map Moving around the map To move around the map, press and hold the left mouse button and drag in the desired direction. The map will move with the cursor. To view another area, just drag the map."},{"s":"map-tools","t":"Map tools","h":["Map tools","Zoom","Print map","Measure area","Measure distance between points","Choose map type","Measuring distance along roads","Distance to the nearest vehicle","Geocoder","Coordinates"],"x":"Map tools All tools for working with the map are grouped in one place — a dedicated panel in the bottom-right corner of the screen. Zoom You can change the map scale in several ways: 1. Zoom buttons и In the top-left corner of the map, use the quick zoom in and out buttons. When you click them, the map zooms while keeping the center point in place. 2. Mouse scroll wheel Scroll forward to zoom in and backward to zoom out. The cursor indicates which area you want to examine in detail. 3. Double-click Double-click the left mouse button anywhere on the map to zoom in. The current coordinates are always visible in the bottom-right corner and update as you move the cursor over the map. Print map This feature prints the current map view. When you click the Print button, you can choose one of three orientations: 1. Portrait — vertical orientation. Good for areas that are tall/narrow 2. Landscape — horizontal orientation. Useful for wide areas or horizontally arranged objects 3. Auto — the system automatically selects the best orientation based on the current map view and its proportions Measure area This tool helps you calculate the area of a selected region on the map. How to use: Click on the map to mark the vertices of the shape you want to measure 2. When you finish drawing the shape, the system will automatically calculate and display its area Measure distance between points This tool helps you calculate distances between any points on the map. How to use: Click on the map to set the first point 2. Click again to set the second point 3. A line will appear between the points showing the distance To measure multiple segments, keep clicking to add points. The length of each segment will be shown next to it. Choose map type In professional cartography this is called a basemap. For simplicity, we’ll use the term map type, which refers to the base layer used to display spatial data in the monitoring system. OneGPS offers several map types for the user interface. Choose the one that best fits your needs—some are better for navigation, others for exploring terrain or finding infrastructure. To change the map, click the Map selector button . In the menu that opens, select the desired map type — it will become your default and load automatically when you open the system. Measuring distance along roads This tool lets you determine the road distance between selected points on the map. How to use: Click the first point on the map 2. Mark the intermediate waypoints along the route 3. Finish the route with a final click at the endpoint 4. The system will automatically calculate the path along existing roads. The total distance and estimated travel time will be shown on the screen. Distance to the nearest vehicle This tool helps you quickly find the nearest vehicle and see the distance to it from a selected point on the map. How to use: Click any point on the map for which you want to find the nearest vehicle. 2. The system will automatically determine the nearest vehicle and display: the vehicle number or object name the distance from the selected point to the vehicle the estimated time it will take the vehicle to reach the point Geocoder The geocoder lets you find a point on the map by address. How to use: 1. Enter an address or coordinates in the geocoder field 2. A suggestion with a refined address will appear under the input 3. Click the exact address from the dropdown list 4. View the desired point on the map Coordinates сurrent coordinates that update as you move the mouse cursor over the map."},{"s":"map-type","t":"Map type","h":["Map type"],"x":"Map type In professional cartography this is called a basemap. For simplicity, we’ll use the term map type, which refers to the base layer used to display spatial data in the monitoring system. OneGPS offers several map types for the user interface. Choose the one that best fits your needs—some are better for navigation, others for exploring terrain or finding infrastructure. To change the map, click the Map selector button . In the menu that opens, select the desired map type — it will become your default and load automatically when you open the system. OpenStreetMap (OSM) An open online map regularly updated by contributors from around the world 2GIS A detailed city map with street and building names; highly detailed and convenient for finding places Yandex A map by the Russian company Yandex showing cities and regions with roads, places, transit schemes, and traffic Yandex Satellite High‑resolution satellite imagery from Yandex with detailed terrain visualization Google Map Google’s standard map with global coverage and regular updates Google Terrain A Google map view emphasizing terrain and elevation Google Satellite High‑resolution satellite imagery from Google with regular data updates WikiMapia A detailed map with object descriptions, created using data from OpenStreetMap and Wikipedia TomTom A navigation map from TomTom showing detailed road routes; widely used for in‑car navigation TomTom Dark A dark version of the TomTom navigation map TomTom Satellite High‑resolution satellite imagery from TomTom ArcGIS A professional geographic information system with multiple data layers Cosmo Sat High‑quality satellite imagery covering the entire Earth Dark A dark map style, convenient for evening or nighttime use; reduces eye strain Gray A gray‑scale map with a neutral design and improved readability"},{"s":"map-layers-and-additional-features","t":"Map layers and additional Features","h":["Map layers and additional Features","Object layers","Street view","Traffic","GPS Jam"],"x":"Map layers and additional Features Additional features are located in the upper-right part of the map. They help display more contextual information on the map: enable custom layers, view streets, check traffic conditions, and see GPS interference zones. In this article: Object layers — displaying additional geographic data from KML/KMZ files Street view — opening panoramic street images Traffic — displaying road congestion on the map GPS Jam — displaying zones with different levels of GPS interference Object layers The Object layersfeature turns on/off additional geographic data from uploaded KML/KMZ files on top of the basemap. How to use: Find the Object layers panel in the top-right corner of the map 2. Expand the list of available layers and check the ones you want to display You can enable multiple layers at once, or turn them all off to show only the base map. For instructions on uploading and working with KML/KMZ files, see the KML Settings section. Street view The Street view feature lets you see panoramic, street-level photos of an area. Available sources: GSV — Google Street View: high-quality imagery with broad coverage. YR — Yandex street panoramas: strong coverage in Russia and some neighboring countries. OSC — Community-sourced panoramas: may provide more recent imagery in some areas. To enable Street view: Check this option in the top-right of the map 2. Drag the yellow person (Pegman) onto the desired place on the map 3. Choose GSV, YR, or OSC 4. The corresponding panoramic service will open, where you can view interactive panoramas of the selected area. Traffic The Traffic layer shows current road congestion using color indicators: Green — free flow Yellow — moderate traffic Red — heavy congestion To turn on traffic, check this option in the top-right corner of the map. GPS Jam The GPS Jam layer shows areas where GPS signal interference was detected for the selected date. This data helps identify areas where object coordinates may be less accurate due to unstable GPS signal reception. The layer is available on the map as a separate GPS Jam checkbox in the upper-right part of the map. After it is enabled, colored cells showing the interference level are displayed on the map. A legend is shown in the lower-left corner under Level of GPS interference: Low — 0–2% Medium — 2–10% High — > 10% You can also select the date for which the data should be displayed. To do this, use the Date field in the layer panel."},{"s":"weather-on-the-map","t":"Weather on the map","h":["Weather on the map","How to enable the weather layer","How to enable weather","How to select a forecast model","How to select a weather overlay type","How to use the timeline panel"],"x":"Weather on the map The system includes weather visualization from Windy.com — an online platform with interactive weather maps and forecasts. Weather data is displayed as an overlay on the map and automatically synchronizes with map panning and zooming. How to enable the weather layer 1. Get an API Key 1) Register on the Windy API portal 2) Generate an API key 3) You will receive a key in the following format: 4epwZKT8erOIe6Boq2gfdhffs50ky By default, a trial key is issued. Trial limitations: Up to 500 sessions per day GFS model only Available layers: Wind, Temperature, Pressure 2. Configure the Key in OneGPS To make the weather layer available: 1) The Windyconfiguration must be enabled in the account. 2) In the Admin panel, open the account settings and add the parameter: windy_api_key = your_key After saving the settings, the weather functionality becomes available to users of the account. How to enable weather 1. Open the map 2. In the layer list, the Weather checkbox will appear 3. Check the box 4. The weather overlay will appear on the map, and the forecast timeline panel will be displayed at the bottom To disable weather, simply uncheck the box. How to select a forecast model Click the globe icon in the weather panel and select a model. Model availability depends on your subscription tier. Free GFS — Global model with basic resolution. Suitable for a general overview of worldwide weather conditions. Pro ICON — High resolution for Europe NAM — Accurate model for North America AROME — Very high resolution (especially for France and nearby regions) GEOS5 — NASA global analysis model CAMS — Air quality monitoring model HRRR — Hourly refresh model for the USA ECMWF (with separate subscription) ECMWF — One of the most accurate global forecast models, used for professional analysis The system automatically detects your tier and displays only the models available to you. How to select a weather overlay type Click the dropdown arrow and select the data type. Free: Wind Temperature Pressure Pro (additional): Humidity Precipitation Thunderstorms Cloud cover Visibility Satellite imagery Weather radar and other professional parameters After selection, the map updates automatically. How to use the timeline panel When the weather layer is enabled, a timeline panel appears at the bottom of the map. It allows you to view the forecast by hours and days. Play — Starts forecast animation. The map shows weather changes over time Pause — Stops the animation at the selected moment Time slider — Drag manually to jump to a specific date and time The timeline panel is visible only when the weather layer is enabled. If weather is disabled, the panel is automatically hidden."},{"s":"account-settings-1","t":"Account settings","h":["Account settings"],"x":"Account settings In Account settings, you can change certain system parameters yourself. To open Account settings, click the configuration icon on the top panel. Here you’ll find sections for configuring your account: Privacy Personalization Map and geocoding Reports list KML settings Object tags Staff and groups Report scheduler Tokens The set of tabs on the left may vary depending on which modules are enabled for your account."},{"s":"privacy","t":"Privacy","h":["Privacy","Email setup","How to add an email address:","Email statuses:","How to confirm your email:","Change password","How to change your account password:","IP-based protection (IP filtering)","How to enable IP filtering:","Two-factor authentication","TOTP Authentication","How to Log In with TOTP:","Email Two-Factor Authentication","How to log in with Email 2FA","How to disable 2FA","Notification settings","How to set up notifications:","Clear cache"],"x":"Privacy In the Privacy section, you can configure account security and notifications: Set up your email Change your password Restrict sign-in by IP Enable extra protection via a code Choose which notifications to receive and how you receive them Clear cache Email setup In OneGPS, email is used to receive reports and notifications. How to add an email address: In the Privacy tab, find the User email addresses section 2. Click Add email 3. Enter a valid email address 4. Click Save To receive reports and notifications, you must confirm your email address. Only after confirmation will the system send information to you. Email statuses: email confirmed email not confirmed How to confirm your email: Click Send confirmation email 2. Open your email inbox 3. Find the message from the OneGPS system 4. Follow the link in the email 5. Your email will be confirmed automatically The Refresh email listbutton updates the statuses of the added email addresses. Change password How to change your account password: In the Privacy tab, find the Change password section 2. Create a new strong password 3. Enter the new password 4. Re-enter the new password in the confirmation field 5. Click Save If you check Send to email, a message with the new password will be sent to the email address specified in your account. IP-based protection (IP filtering) IP filtering allows access to your account only from permitted IP addresses. How to enable IP filtering: In the Privacy tab, find the IP filtering section 2. Check the IP filteringbox 3. Enter the IP addresses from which you allow sign-ins (for example, your work and home computers) 4. Click Save When you enable the feature, your current IP address is added automatically so you don’t lose access. After enabling, you will only be able to sign in from the specified IP addresses. To disable this feature, simply uncheck the box and save the changes. Two-factor authentication Two-Factor Authentication (2FA) is an additional layer of security for your account. In addition to your username and password, a one-time code is required to log in. This significantly reduces the risk of unauthorized access, even if your password is compromised. In the OneGPS system, two 2FA methods are available: TOTP Authentication — via an authenticator app Email Authentication — via a verification code sent to your email Two-factor authentication must be enabled by your contract administrator. TOTP Authentication TOTP authentication adds an extra layer of protection by requiring a temporary code from a mobile app. How to enable TOTP: Before your first sign-in, install an authenticator app for iOS or Android: 2. In the Privacy tab, find the Two-factor authentication section 3. Click Enable 4. Scan the QR code using the authenticator app you installed 5. Enter the code from the app How to Log In with TOTP: Open your browser and go to https://OneGPS-gps.com/ 2. Enter your credentials: login and password 3. Click Enter 4. When prompted, enter the six-digit code from your authenticator app. Open the app on your smartphone, find the six-digit code, and enter it in the field on your computer. The code refreshes every 30 seconds. If it changes before you finish entering it, wait for the next one and enter that. 5. Click Log in Email Two-Factor Authentication with this method, after entering your username and password, the system will send a one-time code to your email. How to enable Email 2FA: 1. Go to the Privacy tab → Two-Factor authentication section 2. ClickEnable next to Email Authentication 3. Select a verified email address from the list 4. Click Send code. A confirmation code will be sent to the selected email 5. Enter the code from the email and confirm How to log in with Email 2FA 1. Open your browser and go to: https://OneGPS-gps.com/ 2. Enter your username and password 3. Click Log in 4. Enter the one-time code sent to your email 5. Click Log in How to disable 2FA Go to the Two-Factor Authentication section and clickDisable next to the active method. Notification settings You decide which notifications to receive. Choose only the ones that matter to you. To receive notifications, you must confirm your email address. How to set up notifications: In the Privacy tab, find the Notification settings section 2. Review the list of available notifications 3. Check the types of notifications you want to receive 4. For each selected type, choose the delivery method(s) 5. Click Save Clear cache Sometimes the system may not show the latest information because old data is stored in your browser. To fix this: 1. In the top-right corner of the Privacy tab, find the Clean local cache button 2. Click it — the system will clear old data and load the latest information After that, everything will display as it should."},{"s":"personalization","t":"Personalization","h":["Personalization","General display settings","Available display options:","How to configure:","Tooltips items","How to configure:","System of measurement","Here you can set:","How to configure:"],"x":"Personalization In the Personalization tab, you can customize how information is displayed in the system: General display settings Tooltips items Measurement system General display settings These settings help you personalize the interface. You can choose how information appears on the map and in other UI elements. Available display options: Show geofences labels — displays text labels with geofence names on the map Show global tags — display tags shared across all users for sorting object information Show tags labels — display additional tags on objects Show global service templates — display shared maintenance templates available to all users Show object tree tooltips — enable hover tooltips when pointing to objects in the list Show LRS Range — displays the accuracy radius when location is determined via LBS (cell towers). Use clusterer for markers — group nearby objects to make the map easier to read Marker animation — add motion to icons on the map Fullscreen map — display the map in full-screen mode How to configure: In the Personalization tab, find the Settings section 2. Review the list of options 3. Check the boxes for the options you want to enable 4. Click Savesettings in the Personalization tab Tooltips items In the Tooltips items section, you can choose what additional information appears when you hover over an object icon on the map. How to configure: In the Personalization tab, locate the Tooltip items and Tooltip extra items sections 2. Select the elements you need — check the corresponding boxes 3. Click Savesettings in the Personalization tab System of measurement Here you can set: Measurement system: Metric: kilograms, liters, kilometers Imperial: pounds, gallons, miles 2. Number format 3. The first day of the week for the calendar and reports How to configure: In the Personalization tab, find the System of measurement section 2. Choose the desired options from the dropdowns 3. Click Save settings in the Personalization tab Now all data will be shown in the format you prefer, and the calendar and reports will match your work schedule"},{"s":"map-and-geocoding","t":"Map and geocoding","h":["Map and geocoding","Address display","How to set up:","Events on the map","How to set up:","Speed color indicators","How to set up:","To change the default colors:","To add a new color band:"],"x":"Map and geocoding Here you can configure: Address display Event display on the map Speed color indicators Address display Choose which address components are shown in the system (for example, in reports or map tooltips). Hide address if geofence found: the system will show only the geofence name if the address is within it Reverse address values: display the address from country down to house number How to set up: In the Map & Geocoding tab, find the Address display settings section 2. Review the list of address components 3. Check the boxes for the components you want to show 4. Click Save settings in the Map & Geocoding tab Events on the map In the Map & History section, you can choose which event types will be shown in an object’s history. When you open the Events tab in the History section, you’ll see the event types you selected. Markers will also appear on the map at the locations where these events occurred. For example, if you select Parkingsand click a specific record, the map will highlight the exact place where it was recorded. How to set up: In the Map & Geocoding tab, find the Map & History section 2. Select the event types to display in the object’s history — check the boxes you need. 3. Click Save settings in the Map & Geocodingtab Speed color indicators Speed color indicators show speed changes using different colors. On the movement track in History, you’ll see how fast the object was moving at each moment. How to set up: In the Map & Geocoding tab, go to the Speed by color section 2. Check Speed by color to display the track in color in History 3. Default color legend : Gray — stopped Green — slow driving up to 60 km/h Orange — medium speed up to 90 km/h Red — fast driving over 90 km/h To change the default colors: Double-click the color field you want to change 2. Choose a new color 3. Click Update 4. Click Save in the Speed by color section To add a new color band: Click Add color 2. Choose the color and set the speed threshold 3. Click Save To delete a color band, click the delete icon next to it."},{"s":"reports-list","t":"Reports list","h":["Reports list","How to set up:"],"x":"Reports list In the Reports tab, choose which report types you want to use. The types you select here will be available when creating a new report. How to set up: 1. Open the Reports list tab 2. Select the report types — check the boxes next to the ones you need 3. Click Save settings The selected report types will appear the same in both the web version and the mobile app. For instructions on how to run and configure reports, see the Reportssection."},{"s":"kml-settings","t":"KML settings","h":["KML settings","Where to get a KML/KMZ file:","How to upload a file to OneGPS:","How to display layers on the map:"],"x":"KML settings KML/KMZ is a file format that lets you add extra geographic data to the map: points lines areas (polygons) images Think of it as a transparent overlay you place on top of the base map. It helps you show what you need: mark important objects, show boundaries, or draw routes. The base map stays visible, and the extra information is layered on top. You can upload a KML/KMZ file to OneGPS and see the additional data on the map. Where to get a KML/KMZ file: Create it yourself: In Google Earth By editing a text file manually 2. Download ready-made files: From specialized geodata websites From open mapping databases From professional communities and forums How to upload a file to OneGPS: Open the KML Settings tab 2. In the Loading KML/KMZ section, choose the source: URL — if the file is on the internet File — if the file is on your computer 3. Paste the link or select the file 4. Enter the file name that will be shown in OneGPS 5. Click Save KML/KMZ If you want all contract users to see the file, check Mutual. To delete an uploaded KML, click the delete icon next to it. After uploading a KML file, the Object Layers tool will appear on the map. How to display layers on the map: Find the Object Layers panel — a list with checkboxes — in the top-right corner of the map 2. Expand the list and check the layers you want to show. You can select one or multiple layers at the same time 3. The selected layers will appear on the map while the base map remains visible"},{"s":"object-tags-1","t":"Object tags","h":["Object tags","How to add object tags:","How tags look in the list"],"x":"Object tags An object tag is a short label that helps you quickly understand what an object is. Think of a tag like a name sticker for each object. A tag shows: Short name Color code Description In a tag, you can indicate, for example, the object type, its role, or status. Why tags are useful: — quickly find the object you need — tell one object from another — track and group objects — keep the list organized How to add object tags: Go to Account settings → Object tags 2. Click Add tag 3. In the dialog, fill in the tag details: Enter a name Choose a color Write a short description Select (check) the objects that should receive this tag 4. Click Save If you want a tag to be added automatically when you create a new object, set it as the Default tag. In this case, the tag will automatically appear in the Tags field for every new object, so you won’t have to add it manually. After you save the tag details, the tag will appear in the Object tags tab. How tags look in the list In the object list, the Tagscolumn shows colored markers that correspond to the object’s tags. You can assign multiple tags to a single object to describe it by different criteria."},{"s":"staff-and-groups","t":"Staff and groups","h":["Staff and groups"],"x":"Staff and groups On the Staff and groups tab, you can create employee accounts and configure their access to OneGPS. For each user, you can specify the objects, reports, modules, and system actions available to them. This section covers: Creating and editing users Configuring user profiles Assigning access rights Creating and applying access rights templates Searching for users Viewing login history Assigning available reports Configuring IP filtering Blocking and unblocking user accounts The available settings depend on the enabled modules and the current user’s access rights. Administrators can create users and manage their access."},{"s":"creating-users","t":"Creating users","h":["Creating users","How to create a user","How to edit a user","How to delete a user"],"x":"Creating users Administrators can create and configure users. When creating an account, enter the user’s login details, and then specify which objects, tags, modules, and actions are available to them. How to create a user 1. Open Account settings and select Staff and groups 2. Click the plus icon 3. Complete the user details. Required fields are outlined in red: Required fields are outlined in red: Login — the name the user enters when signing in to OneGPS Password — the password used to sign in. Click the edit icon in the field to set the password Name — the employee’s name or another descriptive name for the account Type — the user’s access level. OneGPS provides two user types: Administrator — can add users and objects, work with drivers, and manage access rights for users associated with the account User — can work only with the objects, data, and features assigned to them. Their available capabilities are determined by their access rights Email — the user’s email address. Click the send icon in the field to email the user a link for signing in to OneGPS Mobile — the employee’s contact phone number Information — additional information about the user. Complete this field if necessary 4. ClickSave The new account appears in the list on the Staff and groups tab. After creating the user, open their user card and configure access on the relevant tabs. How to edit a user 1. Open theStaff and groupstab 2. Find the user in the list 3. Double-click the user’s row 4. Make the required changes in the user card 5. Click Save How to delete a user Find the user on the Staff and groupstab and click the trash icon in their row. After deletion, the user can no longer access OneGPS. Before deleting the account, make sure it is no longer required. The account owner and the user currently signed in to OneGPS cannot be deleted through the OneGPS interface. This restriction prevents access to the account from being lost. The account owner is highlighted in green in the list. To delete the account owner, first assign another user as the contract owner in the admin panel. The original owner can be deleted after the owner has been changed. If you do not have access to the admin panel, contact your administrator or technical support."},{"s":"user-card","t":"User card","h":["User card"],"x":"User card The user card contains the employee’s account details and OneGPS access rights. Here you can specify which objects, drivers, tags, reports, and modules are available to the user, as well as which actions they can perform. The user card may contain the following tabs: Settings — login, password, contact details, user type, and additional account settings Climate rooms — access to the modes, objects, and data of the Climate module Vehicles — selection of the objects the user can view and use Drivers — selection of the drivers available to the user Object tags — assignment of tags that the user can use to find and filter objects Rights — permissions to view, create, edit, and delete data, as well as access to specific features and modules Interface — configuration of the available interface sections and elements Control Room event groups — access to event groups and Control room features The available tabs depend on the enabled modules, contract configuration, and access rights of the user configuring the account. Therefore, user cards may differ between accounts."},{"s":"settings-1","t":"Settings","h":["Settings","Main settings","Additional settings"],"x":"Settings The Settings tab contains the user’s account details, login settings, and the period of historical data available to them. Main settings Enter the user details. Required fields are outlined in red: Login — the name the user enters when signing in to OneGPS Password — the password used to sign in. To create or change it, click the edit icon in the field Name — the employee’s name or another descriptive name for the account Type — determines the user’s access level: Administrator — can add users and objects, work with drivers, and manage access rights for users associated with the account User — can work with the objects and features assigned to them. Their available capabilities are determined by their access rights Information — additional information about the employee. Complete this field if necessary Email — the user’s email address. Click the send icon in the field to email the user a link for signing in to OneGPS Mobile — the employee’s contact phone number Additional settings The number of possible login attempts — specify the permitted number of login attempts. This setting limits repeated attempts to sign in with incorrect credentials. A value of 0 disables attempt counting. The number of days available history — specify the period for which historical data is available to the user, including when generating reports: 0 — use the default period of 365 days A positive number — retain history for the specified number of days −1 — do not retain history The maximum period is determined by the Users default parameters account configuration. This configuration is added by a user with access to the admin panel. To increase the available period, contact your administrator or technical support. For example, setting max_days_available_history to 730 gives users associated with the account access to two years of historical data. After the configuration is applied, the The number of days available history field is populated with the configured number of days. After completing the fields, click Save. Then use the other tabs in the user card to assign objects, tags, and the required access rights."},{"s":"climate-rooms","t":"Climate rooms","h":["Climate rooms","To grant access:"],"x":"Climate rooms The Climate rooms tab controls the user’s access to data in the Climate module. You can grant access to an entire mode or only to specific objects and sites. The module provides two modes: NORMAL — the standard mode for working with climate sensors. It displays connected objects and sensors, their current readings, last update times, and charts. VELAVU — a mode for equipment, sites, and rooms imported from the Velavu platform. Users can view the structure of sites, buildings, and floors, as well as the locations of devices and RFID tags on a map or floor plan. To grant access: 1. Open the user card 2. Select the Climate roomstab 3. Select the checkbox next to the required mode: NORMAL — access to standard Climate module data VELAVU — access to Velavu integration data 4. To grant access only to specific data, expand the required mode and select the checkboxes next to the relevant items: In NORMAL mode — groups or objects with climate sensors. In VELAVU mode — sites, buildings, or individual floors 5. ClickSave"},{"s":"vehicles-1","t":"Vehicles","h":["Vehicles","To grant access:"],"x":"Vehicles The Vehiclestab controls the user’s access to monitored objects. The user can see only the objects selected here in OneGPS. To grant access: 1. Open the user card 2. Select the Vehicles tab 3. Expand the required folder 4. Select the checkboxes next to the objects that should be available to the user. To select all objects in a folder, select the checkbox next to the folder name 5. Click Save New objects are not assigned to users automatically. After creating or importing an object, open the user cards of the relevant users and grant access manually. This also applies to objects received from a mapping account. To revoke access to an object, clear the corresponding checkbox and save the changes."},{"s":"drivers-1","t":"Drivers","h":["Drivers","To grant access:"],"x":"Drivers The Drivers tab is available when the corresponding module is enabled for the account. This tab controls the user’s access to driver data. The user can view and use only the selected driver records. To grant access: 1. Open the user card 2. Select the Driverstab 3. Expand the required group 4. Select the checkboxes next to the drivers who should be available to the user. To select the entire group, select the checkbox next to the group name 5. Click Save New drivers are not assigned to users automatically. After adding a driver, grant the user access on the Drivers tab if required."},{"s":"object-tags-2","t":"Object tags","h":["Object tags","To grant access:"],"x":"Object tags The Object tags tab controls the user’s access to tags used to classify and quickly find objects. The user can view and use only the selected tags. To grant access: 1. Open the user card 2. Select the Object tags tab 3. Select the checkboxes next to the required tags 4. Click Save To revoke access to a tag, clear the corresponding checkbox and save the changes."},{"s":"rights","t":"Rights","h":["Rights","Configuring rights","Rights templates","How to create a template","How to apply a template"],"x":"Rights The Rights tab controls the actions the user can perform in OneGPS. For example, you can allow the user to view reports and edit objects while preventing them from creating or deleting data. Rights are organized into categories. The available categories depend on the enabled modules and account configuration. They may include permissions for: Working with vehicles Creating and editing objects Managing sensors and tags Viewing and generating reports Working with geofences Accessing financial data Changing account settings Using additional modules Configuring rights To grant rights to a user: 1. Open the user card 2. Select the Rights tab 3. Expand the required category 4. Select the checkboxes next to the permitted actions 5. Click Save Selecting the checkbox next to a category enables all rights within it. If the user needs only specific capabilities, expand the category and select the required rights individually. To find a specific right more quickly, enter its name in the search field. Grant users only the rights they need for their work. Access to objects, drivers, and tags is configured separately on the corresponding tabs of the user card. Rights templates How to create a template 1. Open the user card of a user with the required set of rights 2. Select the Rightstab 3. Click the save template icon 4. In the Saving template window, enter a descriptive name 5. Click Save The template appears in the list of saved templates. To open the list, click the template list icon on the Rights tab. The template list allows you to view existing templates and delete templates that are no longer required. How to apply a template To assign a saved set of rights to another user: 1. Create a user or open an existing user card 2. On the Settings tab, open the Type list 3. Select an option marked (template) 4. Complete the remaining user details 5. Click Save The selected set of rights is applied to the user."},{"s":"control-room-event-groups","t":"Control Room event groups","h":["Control Room event groups","To configure access:"],"x":"Control Room event groups The Control Room event groups tab controls which event groups are available to the user when working with the Control room module. Event groups are created and configured for the entire account under Settings on the GARM (Control Room) tab. In the user card, you can only grant or revoke access to existing groups. To configure access: 1. Open the user card 2. Select the Control Room event groups tab 3. Select the checkboxes next to the event groups that should be visible to the user 4. Click Save If you clear a checkbox, the corresponding event group is no longer displayed to the user."},{"s":"rfid-tags-1","t":"RFID tags","h":["RFID tags","How to assign RFID tags to a user"],"x":"RFID tags The RFID tags tab controls the user’s access to the account’s RFID tags. The user can view and use only the tags selected in their user card. This tab is displayed when the RFID module is enabled for the account. The list contains the following columns: ID — the RFID tag identifier Name — the tag name Description — additional information about the tag Account — the account to which the tag belongs How to assign RFID tags to a user 1. Open the user card 2. Select the RFID tags tab 3. Find the required tags using the search or filter field 4. Select the checkboxes next to the tags that should be available to the user 5. Click Save To assign all available RFID tags at once, click the checkmark icon in the upper-right corner. This selects all tags in the list. To revoke access to a specific tag, clear its checkbox and save the changes. If the tag list has changed, click the refresh icon to load the latest data."},{"s":"commands-1","t":"Commands","h":["Commands","To grant access:"],"x":"Commands The Commands tab is displayed in the user card when the Commands module is enabled for the account. This tab controls which commands the user can use to manage equipment. Commands are grouped by device type. The list displays the command Name and the compatible Device type. To grant access: 1. Open the user card 2. Select the Commands tab 3. Select the checkboxes next to the commands that should be available to the user 4. Click Save To select all commands, select the checkbox in the list header. The edit icon next to a command opens its settings. In the command editor, you can modify the command parameters, select objects, configure geofence and time restrictions, and set other available execution conditions."},{"s":"interface","t":"Interface","h":["Interface","Example: hiding the object tree"],"x":"Interface The Interface tab controls which sections and modules are displayed to the user. When a checkbox is selected, the corresponding item is available in the interface. Clearing the checkbox hides the item. Example: hiding the object tree The Online Tree setting controls whether the object tree is displayed on the Online tab. To hide the object tree: 1. Open the user card 2. Select the Interface tab 3. Expand the Main group 4. Clear theOnline Treecheckbox 5. Click Save 6. Refresh the page in the user’s account When Online Treeis selected, the tree containing folders and vehicles is displayed on the left side of the workspace. When Online Tree is cleared, the object tree is not displayed. Another available tab, such as Contragents, opens on the left side of the workspace instead. The map and other permitted modules remain available."},{"s":"searching-for-a-user","t":"Searching for a user","h":["Searching for a user","Account owner"],"x":"Searching for a user On theStaff and groupstab, you can search for a user by login, account name, or email address. 1. Enter part of the value in the search field in the upper-right corner 2. The list updates automatically to display only matching records Search is not case-sensitive, so you can use uppercase or lowercase letters To display all users again, click the clear icon in the search field. Account owner The account owner is highlighted in green in the user list, making them easy to distinguish from other users in the account. The account owner cannot be deleted through the OneGPS interface. To assign another owner, contact your administrator or technical support."},{"s":"authentication-history","t":"Authentication history","h":["Authentication history","To open the log:"],"x":"Authentication history Authentication history allows you to check when a user signed in to OneGPS and which device they used. To open the log: 1. Open the Staff and groupstab 2. Find the required user 3. Click the authentication history icon in the user’s row 4. In the Authentication History window, specify the start and end of the period in the From and To fields. 5. Click the search icon The following information is displayed for each login: User Id Login Role Login time Last activity time IP Browser OS To save the log to your computer, click the download icon . The data is exported as a CSV file. To reload the latest information, click the refresh icon ."},{"s":"assigning-reports","t":"Assigning reports","h":["Assigning reports"],"x":"Assigning reports You can select which reports are available to each user in OneGPS. 1. Open the Staff and groupstab 2. Find the required user 3. Click the report list icon in the user’s row 4. In the Access rights to reports window, select the checkboxes next to the required reports 5. Click Save To quickly find a report, enter part of its name in the search field . Select all assigns all available reports, while Deselect all removes all report assignments. If you clear the checkbox next to a report and save the changes, the report is no longer displayed to the user."},{"s":"ip-filtering-for-a-user","t":"IP filtering for a user","h":["IP filtering for a user","To configure IP filtering:"],"x":"IP filtering for a user IP filtering restricts access to a user account by IP address. Once enabled, the user can sign in to OneGPS only from permitted addresses. To configure IP filtering: 1. On the Staff and groupstab, find the required user 2. Click the filter icon next to the user’s name 3. In the IP Filter window, select Enable filtering 4. Enter the permitted IP addresses, one address per line 5. Click Save the list of IP When filtering is enabled, the current IP address is added to the list automatically. If necessary, add any other addresses from which the user will sign in to OneGPS. Incorrect IP filtering settings may prevent the user from accessing OneGPS. Before saving, make sure the list contains all required addresses. If the network IP address changes, add the new address to the list."},{"s":"blocking-and-unblocking-a-user","t":"Blocking and unblocking a user","h":["Blocking and unblocking a user","How to block a user","How to unblock a user"],"x":"Blocking and unblocking a user Blocking temporarily prevents a user from signing in to OneGPS while preserving their account and access settings. The user’s state is indicated by color in the Status column: Green icon — the user is active Red icon — the user is blocked How to block a user 1. On the Staff and groups tab, find the required user 2. Click the green icon in the Statuscolumn 3. In the confirmation window, click Yes The status icon changes to red . The user can no longer sign in to OneGPS. How to unblock a user 1. Find the blocked user in the list 2. Click the red icon in the Status column 3. In the confirmation window, click Yes The status icon changes to green , and the user can sign in again using their existing credentials."},{"s":"report-scheduler","t":"Report scheduler","h":["Report scheduler","Creating a schedule","Report parameters","Report data period","Generation schedule","Selecting objects or tags","Saving the schedule"],"x":"Report scheduler The Report scheduler automatically generates and sends reports according to a specified schedule. You can use it to: select the required report type configure the period for which data should be included in the report specify when and how often the report should be generated select objects or tags specify the email addresses to which the report should be sent To use the Report scheduler, confirm your email address on the Privacy tab. Creating a schedule 1. Open Account settingsand select Report scheduler 2. Click the button 3. In the window that opens, configure the report parameters, report data period, and generation schedule 4. Select the objects or tags for which the report should be generated 5. Click Save The schedule will appear in the Report scheduler. Reports will then be generated and sent at the specified frequency. Report parameters Complete the following fields in the Report parameters section: Name — enter a descriptive name for the schedule. This will help you distinguish it from other automatic report schedules Language — select the language in which the report should be generated Report type — select the required report from the list. Only report types that you have permission to access are displayed. You can view the available reports in Reports list Sensor tags — select the tags assigned to the sensors whose data should be included in the report. Sensor tags allow you to include data only from the required sensors. You can assign tags in the sensor settings . The availability of this field and its list of tags depend on the selected report type. Grouping — specify how to group the report data, for example, by dates or by objects Split — select how to divide the data within the report, for example, by days, by weeks, or without splitting Report timezone — select the time zone used to determine the report data period and the time of events in the report Format — select the report file format: PDF or Excel Emails — specify one or more email addresses to which the report should be sent Make sure that your email address is confirmed on the Privacy tab. You cannot use the Report scheduler without a confirmed email address. Report data period In the Report data periodsection, configure the time interval for which data should be included in the report: From — specify the report period start time Previous day — select this checkbox if the start time should refer to the previous day To — specify the report period end time Period — select the duration of the reporting period The Previous day option allows you to generate reports for intervals that start on one calendar day and end on the next. For example, if you specify: From — 20:00 Previous day — selected To — 08:00 the report will include data from 20:00 on the previous day to 08:00 on the current day. Generation schedule In the Generation schedule section, specify when and how often the report should be generated: Next generation — select the date and time when the report should next be generated and sent. Generate — select how often the report should be generated: Daily — the report is generated every day Weekly — the report is generated once a week Monthly — the report is generated once a month Selected days — the report is generated only on the selected days of the week The Data perioddisplayed below the schedule settings is the automatically calculated interval for which data will be included in the next report. Selecting objects or tags On the right side of the window, select what the report should be generated for: Objects — the report will be generated for the selected objects Tags — the report will be generated for objects associated with the selected tags You can select individual objects or an entire object group. Use the search field and filters to find the required objects. Saving the schedule Review the schedule settings and click Save. The schedule will appear in the Report scheduler. Reports will then be generated automatically and sent to the specified recipients. Watch the video to learn how to automatically schedule and send reports via email."},{"s":"tokens","t":"Tokens","h":["Tokens","Monitor token","Permissions for managing Monitor tokens","How to grant permissions to a user","How to create a monitor token","Access token","How to create an access token"],"x":"Tokens A token is a unique access code that allows other people to use specific OneGPS features without receiving the account credentials. You can create two types of tokens on the Tokens tab: Monitor token — provides access to the location of selected objects through a public link Access token — allows a user to sign in to OneGPS without entering a password Monitor token A Monitor token provides another person with access to a map showing selected objects and their movements. The recipient does not receive the account login, password, or full access to the account. Permissions for managing Monitor tokens The following permissions are used to manage Monitor tokens: Edit Tokens — allows users to create and edit Monitor tokens and share-location links Delete Tokens — allows users to delete Monitor tokens To allow a regular user to create or edit a share-location link in the OneGPS web application, an administrator or master must grant the Edit Tokens permission. To allow the user to delete these links, grant the Delete Tokens permission as well. How to grant permissions to a user 1. Open Contract Settings and go to the Staff and groups tab 2. Double-click a user in the list to open the user card 3. Go to the Rights tab 4. Expand the Tokens group 5. Select the required permissions: Edit Tokens — to create and edit tokens Delete Tokens — to delete tokens 6. Click Save How to create a monitor token 1. In the Monitor tokens section, click Add token 2. In the window that opens, fill in the token information: Set the validity period — select the start and end date and time for the token Choose which map to use for displaying the objects Specify whether to display object markers or sensors on the map Select the objects to monitor using this token 3. Click Save 4. The system will generate a unique link or iframe code for embedding 5. Copy the link or code and send it to the person who needs access to the information Access token An Access tokenis used to sign in to OneGPS without a password. When someone follows the generated link, OneGPS signs them in as the user associated with the token, as if they had entered that user’s login and password. An Access token can have a limited validity period or remain valid indefinitely. How to create an access token 1. In the Access tokens section, click Add token 2. In the new window, specify the start and end date and time for the token. If you do not set an end date, the token will work permanently 3. Click Save To edit a token, click the pencil icon . To delete a token, click the trash bin icon"},{"s":"display-settings-template","t":"Display settings template","h":["Display settings template","Exporting a template","Uploading a template"],"x":"Display settings template A template allows you to transfer display settings from one account to another. This is useful when creating accounts with similar settings or configuring multiple partner accounts: you can export the required settings to a file and reuse them later. Only users with theMaster role can export and upload templates. The buttons for exporting and uploading display settings templates are available on every tab under Settings account. A template can include settings from the Personalization and Map & Geocoding tabs, including: List of available reports (Reports list) Unit system (System of measurement) General display settings (Global) Address format (Geocoding) Display of events on the map in history (History) Tooltip contents (Tooltip items) Exporting a template 1. Open Settings account and select any tab, such as Staff and groups 2. Click the download settings icon 3. In the Download user settings window, select the settings to include in the template 4. Click Export The template file is saved to your computer. You can use it to transfer the selected settings to another account. Uploading a template Before uploading a template, make sure you have opened the account to which you want to apply the settings. 1. Open Settings account and select any tab, such as Staff and groups 2. Click the upload template icon 3. In the Upload user settings window, click Browser and select a previously saved file 4. Click Upload 5. When the upload is complete, refresh the page. The settings included in the template are applied to the selected account."},{"s":"objects-1","t":"Objects","h":["Objects"],"x":"Objects An object is a vehicle, piece of equipment, person, animal, or any other moving or stationary entity that is being monitored. To track an object, a special device is installed on it, which sends data to our system. To configure object tracking, go to the Online tab. Here you can add objects for monitoring and, if needed, group them together. After adding objects, you will see the list of objects in the same tab. In this section you will learn: how to add an object how to work with the object card how to work with the object menu how to work with the object list how to view additional information about an object"},{"s":"adding-an-object","t":"Adding an object","h":["Adding an object","Adding an object manually","Importing objects from a WLP file","How to import objects from Wialon:","How to import a group of wialon objects:","How to import object history:"],"x":"Adding an object To create objects, you must have the rights to create and edit objects. In OneGPS, an object can be added either manually or imported from a WLP file. Adding an object manually In the Online tab, click the Add object button 2. Complete the required fields in the object card. They are highlighted with a red border: Vehicle number — enter the vehicle registration number or a clear name for the object Device type — select the model of the GPS tracker or another terminal installed on the object The GPS device installed on the object must be in the list ofsupported equipment. Device ID/IMEI — enter the unique identifier of the equipment. It is usually printed on the device, its packaging, or in the manufacturer’s documentation 3. Click Save — and the object will appear in the object list You can complete the remaining fields immediately or add them later when editing the object. For details about all available tabs and settings, see Object card. Watch the video “How to add a vehicle” to quickly learn how to add a vehicle or object to an account in OneGPS. Importing objects from a WLP file The import from WLP file feature in OneGPS is designed to help clients who previously used the Wialon system. Before starting the import, let’s clarify the file types you will be working with: WLP files store the basic information about an object: its name, type, and characteristics WLN files contain the full history of movements and sensor readings for an object over a certain period How to import objects from Wialon: Download the list of objects with parameters from Wialon 2. Upload the objects into OneGPS: Upload the WLP file with the object via the button Tick the checkbox if you want to import sensor readings Click Load The objects will be automatically added to the system How to import a group of wialon objects: Export a group of objects from Wialon into WLP files Archive the WLP files into a single ZIP file. Upload the ZIP archive via the button How to import object history: In Wialon, select the object and parameters, then download a WLN file 2. In OneGPS, find the object in the list to which you want to transfer the history 3. Right-click the object — the object menu will open 4. In the object menu, select Import WLN 5. In the import window, upload the required WLN file via the Choose file button 6. Specify how to process the imported data: Tick Build tracksif you want to restore the object’s routes Tick Build sensors if you want to restore sensor readings 7. Click Load"},{"s":"object-card","t":"Object card","h":["Object card"],"x":"Object card The object card contains information about the vehicle and the settings of its associated equipment. Here you can edit the object’s main characteristics, configure the device and connection, add SIM cards, set fuel parameters, and enter other information. To open the object card, double-click an object in the list, or open its context menu and select Edit. The object card can include the following tabs: Main — vehicle details, its image, device, and connection settings SIM cards — slot numbers, MSISDNs, ICCIDs, and SIM providers for the main and additional devices Info — additional information about the object Settings — object operation and display settings Extra parameters — additional characteristics and settings Fuel — fuel monitoring parameters Files — documents and other files associated with the object The available tabs depend on the enabled modules and user permissions, so the object card may differ between accounts. After making changes, click Save. To discard any unsaved changes, click Reset."},{"s":"main-object-settings","t":"Main object settings","h":["Main object settings","Object details","Object photo","Device and connection","Setting availability"],"x":"Main object settings The Main tab contains vehicle details and settings for the connected equipment. To add an object, complete the required fields. You can enter the remaining information immediately or add it later by editing the object card. After the object is saved, the following information appears at the top of the tab: Object ID Account ID Odometer reading — if configured on the Info tab Machine hours — if configured on the Info tab Object image — a standard image based on the selected make, model, and modification, or a photo uploaded by you Object details Enter the vehicle registration number or a clear name for the object. It will appear in the object list, on the map, and in reports. Select the vehicle type, such as a car, truck, or special-purpose vehicle. OneGPS uses the corresponding icon in the object list and on the map. You can also select the icon color. Select the folder where the object will appear in the object tree. For details on creating and configuring groups, see Object list. Select the vehicle manufacturer. Select the vehicle model. The available models depend on the selected make. If necessary, select a specific version of the model, such as one with a particular body type, engine, or model year. After you select the make and model, OneGPS automatically adds a standard vehicle image. Select a predefined set of object settings, such as a sensor or eco-driving template. Use a template that matches the installed equipment and the vehicle’s intended use. Add any information that may be useful when working with the object, such as its department, purpose, operating conditions, or other notes. Assign tags to classify objects and find them more quickly. For details on creating tags, see Object tags. Object photo You can add a vehicle photo on the Main tab: 1. Click the camera icon next to the image 2. Select a file on your computer and upload it 3. Enable the toggle next to the photo if you want to display it instead of the standard vehicle icon The photo will appear in the object list and on the map. If the toggle is disabled, OneGPS will continue to use the standard icon for the selected vehicle type. To remove an uploaded photo, click the trash icon next to the image. Device and connection This section contains the settings for the object’s primary telematics equipment. Select the model of the GPS tracker or other terminal installed in the vehicle. The selected device type determines how incoming data is processed and which connection settings are used. If the required model is not available, contact technical support. Enter the unique identifier of the primary terminal. This may be a device ID, serial number, or IMEI. It is usually printed on the device, its packaging, or in the manufacturer’s documentation. OneGPS uses this identifier to determine which object the incoming data belongs to. A lock icon appears next to the Device ID/IMEI field to prevent accidental changes. Click the lock to enable editing You can link other installed equipment to the object, such as an additional tracker or video device: 1. Click the edit icon in the Additional devices field 2. In the window that opens, click Add 3. Enter the IMEI — the unique identifier of the additional device 4. Select the device type 5. Click Save The following service settings are available for additional devices: Ignore Coord — do not use coordinates from the additional device to determine the object’s location Ignore Retrans — do not send data from the additional device through the configured retransmission channels Enable these settings only if required by your connection configuration. If you are unsure, contact your administrator or technical support. After you select the device type, OneGPS automatically displays the server address and port to which the terminal must send data. Click the copy icon next to the field to copy the value and use it when configuring the equipment. This field is completed automatically and cannot be edited manually. Select the time zone in which the object operates. OneGPS uses it to display the correct time in history, events, and reports. Enter the time difference in hours if the device does not send timestamps in GMT. The default value is 0. Change this value only if the timestamps in the device messages differ from the actual time. Setting availability The available fields and editing permissions depend on the user’s access rights. For users with the User role: The Device type field is not displayed Odometer readings, machine hours, and the packet time shift may be available in read-only mode To change any unavailable settings, contact a user with Master or Administrator permissions."},{"s":"sim-sards","t":"SIM сards","h":["SIM сards","How to add a SIM card"],"x":"SIM сards The SIM cardstab stores information about the SIM cards installed in the object’s main and additional devices. The availability of this tab depends on the user’s role and permissions. Users with Master permissions can add and edit SIM card details. The information is grouped by device: Main device — the object’s primary terminal Additional device — other equipment added to the object card Complete the following fields for each SIM card: Slot — the number of the SIM card slot or position in the device. Enter 1 if the device supports one SIM card. For devices with multiple SIM cards, select the corresponding slot number. MSISDN — the phone number assigned to the SIM card by the mobile network operator. Its length may vary by country and operator, so enter it in the format provided by your operator. ICCID — the SIM card’s unique serial number. It usually contains 19 or 20 digits and can be found on the SIM card, its packaging, or in the operator’s online portal. SIM provider — the mobile network operator that provides service for the SIM card. How to add a SIM card 1. Open the SIM cards tab 2. Find the main or additional device in which the SIM card is installed 3. Enter the details of the first SIM card 4. If the device supports multiple SIM cards, click the plus icon next to the device name 5. For each additional row, select the slot number and enter the corresponding SIM card details 6. Click Save If an additional device is linked to the object, enter its SIM card details separately in the Additional device section. To remove an added row, click the trash icon . Click Reset to discard changes that have not yet been saved."},{"s":"info","t":"Info","h":["Info","Setting up document expiration reminders:"],"x":"Info On the Info tab, enter the vehicle registration data: Year of manufacture VIN code Engine number Engine capacity (liters) — select with arrows or enter manually Engine power (hp) Total number of seats and number of passenger seats Vehicle registration certificate data: expiration date — specify the exact expiration date issue date — specify the document creation date Registration certificate number and expiration date Insurance policy number and expiration date To specify the expiration dates, click the calendar icon and select the date. Setting up document expiration reminders: Click the icon next to the field to set up a reminder 2. Enter a name for the reminder 3. In the Datefield, select the expiration date 4. Specify the repeat interval (reminder sending frequency) 5. Specify how many days before the expiration date to send the reminder 6. Select the delivery method To receive email reminders, verify your email address in the Privacy section. 7. Compose your reminder: Subject (what the reminder is about) Headline (what will appear at the top of the message) Message text (you can use variables for auto-complete): %current_time% - current time %reminder_name% - reminder name %current_moto% - current engine hours %current_mil% - current mileage in miles %agent_id% - invoice number %vehicle_name% - vehicle name %driver_name% - driver name Example: Insurance for %vehicle_name% expires %current_time%. 8. Click Save — ​​the reminder will be created"},{"s":"settings-2","t":"Settings","h":["Settings","Valid flag in raw data"],"x":"Settings The Settings tab contains parameters that can be configured by the system administrator using preset configurations. These are ready-made templates that can be applied to objects. Contact the administrator to add the necessary settings to your contract. They will appear on this tab. The settings will be automatically applied to all objects in the contract. In the Settings tab, you define parameters that affect how the device works in the system. For example, here you can configure the trip and parking detector, trip segmentation, and data filtering. Select the speed source to ensure accurate vehicle movement data. By default, the system uses GPS speed, but you can select another parameter if needed, for example, CAN bus speed. If CAN speed is selected: The speed shown in the History chart will be taken from CAN Movement calculations in basic reports (Trips, Driving Time, Parking, etc.) will be based on CAN speed Driving time and stop detection will also be calculated using CAN speed If the selected parameter does not contain speed data, the system will automatically switch back to the default GPS speed. Make sure you select a parameter that actually represents speed. If you accidentally choose mileage or another value, the calculations may be incorrect. In the Speed sourceblock, you can use the Formulafield to convert the parameter value into the correct speed format. CAN bus speed is not always transmitted in the required unit. For example, it may be received in knots or meters per second. In this case, you can use a formula to convert the technical value into the correct unit for display and calculations in the system. For example, if speed is received in m/s, use the formula: * 3.6 (This converts meters per second to km/h.) With this function, you can configure: Minimum speed at which the system begins recording a trip Minimum time between trips — the system will separate trips if this interval passes between the end of one and the start of another Minimum parking time — how long the vehicle must remain stationary before it is registered as parking Maximum distance between tracking points — if points exceed this distance, the system will treat them as separate trips Consider ignition — a trip is only recorded when the vehicle ignition is on Fix speed — restores movement speed if data is missing or corrupted Mileage by CAN — trip history and reports will use CAN bus mileage instead of GPS. This is more accurate in cases where GPS signal is lost This function splits each trip into time intervals. For example, every 2 or 4 hours. Instead of a single record for the whole day, you’ll get shorter segments. This is useful for generating trip sheets for specific periods. Set parameters for validating received data: Minimum number of satellites required for accurate coordinates. Typically at least 3. Some devices work with 2, but accuracy is lower. Maximum HDOP value — HDOP (Horizontal Dilution of Precision) indicates location accuracy. The lower the value, the more precise the coordinates. If HDOP is higher than the threshold you set, the system will discard the data. The system also automatically filters out messages without coordinates or with zero latitude/longitude, even if the device did not mark them as invalid. Maximum speed value — points with a speed higher than this threshold will be filtered out in history tracks and speed/mileage/violation reports. The Formulafield allows you to filter data packets when building tracks. It works as follows: The formula returns either TRUE or FALSE for each packet. If the result is TRUE — the packet is excluded. If the result is FALSE — the packet is included in the track. Examples: =%S%<5 — excludes all packets with speed less than 5 km/h. =%S%<5 || %S%>200 — excludes packets where speed is below 5 km/h or above 200 km/h. You can also create more complex formulas using logical operators, brackets, and multiple conditions. Valid flag in raw data Validis a field in the raw packet data that shows whether the packet is considered reliable for further coordinate processing. Valid = 0 means that the packet is considered invalid. Such packets are stored in the system, but are excluded from processing when tracks, geofences, and other data that require reliable coordinates are recalculated. The Valid = 0 flag is assigned only in the following cases: the packet has no coordinates or contains zero latitude or longitude values the packet matches the condition specified in the filtering formula After such a packet is saved, its participation in recalculation can no longer be changed: it will not be used during repeated data processing. Filters by satellite count, speed, and other parameters from the Events filter block work differently. They are applied dynamically, only during data recalculation. If you change these filter parameters and run recalculation again, the result may change. For example, if the minimum satellite count is set to 4 in the object settings, packets with fewer satellites may be excluded when tracks and reports are recalculated. However, the packets themselves do not receive the Valid = 0 flag. If you need such packets to be marked as invalid immediately, use a filtering formula. For example: %satsinview% < 4 This formula marks packets where the satellite count is less than 4 as Valid = 0. These packets are stored in the system, but are not used when tracks, geofences, and other data are recalculated. Speed limit — the system notifies you when speed exceeds the set limit. Connection loss — the system notifies you if connection is lost and not restored within the set time. Idle time — the system registers idle periods when the object remains stationary beyond the set limit. Next to these fields, there are additional control icons: Bell icon — notification management for the limit: — notifications are enabled — notifications are disabled The limit itself continues to function regardless of the notification status: data is still recorded in the system and displayed in reports. To change the mode, click the icon. Ignition key icon (available for the “Idle” field) — determines whether the ignition status is considered when calculating idle time: — idle is recorded only when the ignition is ON — idle is calculated regardless of the ignition status Don’t forget to click Save to apply your changes."},{"s":"extra-params","t":"Extra params","h":["Extra params"],"x":"Extra params In this tab, you can enter additional information about the object. The exact fields available depend on your contract administrator, who manages them in the Admin panel. When the administrator configures additional fields, they will appear here. If more fields are needed in the future, the administrator can add them. Don’t forget to click Save to apply your changes."},{"s":"fuel-1","t":"Fuel","h":["Fuel"],"x":"Fuel In this tab, you can configure fuel consumption parameters. Summer and winter fuel consumption — specify separately to distinguish seasonal factors from vehicle usage Start dates — select the start of summer and winter periods via the calendar Fuel consumption per engine hour — useful for equipment that often works while stationary (construction machinery, agricultural equipment, generators) Idle fuel consumption Fuel type — Diesel, 80, 92, 95, 98, Gas Fuel tank capacity — for tracking consumption and fuel levels. Fuel cost Don’t forget to click Save to apply your changes."},{"s":"files","t":"Files","h":["Files","How to add a file:"],"x":"Files In this tab, you can attach documents and files to an object, keeping all necessary information in one place. Supported formats: DOC, PNG, TXT, Excel, PDF. This tab becomes available immediately after creating an object. How to add a file: 1. In the tab, click Add file 2. In the pop-up window, enter a description of the file 3. Upload the file from your computer via Select file 4. Click Save Uploaded files will appear in this tab."},{"s":"object-menu","t":"Object menu","h":["Object menu","Current track","Follow object","Map","Edit","Highlight with color","Sensors","Sensors tracing","Temporary blocking","Clone","Import WLN","History","Delete an object","Multiple object menu","Edit","Sensor templates","Export agents","Follow objects","History","Move to group"],"x":"Object menu The Object menu is a list of available actions you can perform with a selected object. To open the object menu, right-click on the object in the object list. The set of available actions depends on the modules included in your contract. Below are the main actions you can perform with an object via the menu. Current track The Current track shows where the object is now and how it has been moving recently. You will see its current location and route on the map. Current route of the object 2. Movement history player With the player, you can view how the object moved: its route, speed, and travel time. 3. Follow object on track Enable this option to watch the object move online. 4. Playback speed Increase or decrease the playback speed. To end playback, click the X. Follow object Click Follow object to view its live location on the map. To cancel following, click theX. Clicking the object icon shows a tooltip with its details. Map The Map tool lets you view objects in detail. A mini-map window will open, which you can resize and move anywhere on the screen. You can also view several objects one by one: select multiple objects in the list, and each will be shown in detail one after another. This way, all selected objects remain visible. Open mini maps are saved in the browser and automatically restored after the page is refreshed. Edit To edit object details, select Edit. The object card will open, allowing you to make changes. Double-click the object in the list to quickly open its card for editing. Highlight with color Use this feature to quickly mark important objects in the list for easy recognition. Sensors Clicking this menu option opens the Sensor settings window. Here, you can add and configure object sensors. Sensors tracing The Sensors tracing section shows raw data collected by the device sensors in real time. Raw data include: Exact time of data reception Location — latitude, longitude, altitude Speed Direction Number of satellites Readings from additional sensors (temperature, fuel level, battery charge, pressure, etc.) You can view object data in a table or on a chart. 1 — Select a date to generate a daily report for one object 2 — Build report 3 — Build chart. Select sensors, and their values will be displayed as a graph To download raw data, click . Temporary blocking Temporary blocking suspends an object in the system. When the equipment is not in use (for example, in winter), you can stop collecting data and paying for a subscription. This is especially useful for seasonal equipment that only works for part of the year. Clone If you have similar objects with minor differences, use cloning to quickly create a copy. The system will automatically duplicate all settings. You only need to: Enter a new object name Specify a new unique device identifier Import WLN This function transfers objects from the Wialon system to OneGPS. See the dedicated article for instructions. History Click Historyto open the Historysection, where you can view all previous moves and changes in the object's status. Delete an object Deleting an object deletes all its data and history. If you need to restore information, contact your system administrator. Multiple object menu In the list of objects, select multiple objects by holding down the Ctrl key on your keyboard. 2. Right-click on the selected objects to open the multi-object menu. Edit The Edit button is only active if all selected objects have the same device type. This allows you to configure settings for several objects at once. Select objects with the same device type for group editing. Clicking Edit will open a window with settings for the selected objects. In this window, you can simultaneously configure the parameters for all selected objects at once. Sensor templates The Sensor templates button opens preconfigured sensor settings. When you apply a template, the parameters will automatically appear in each selected object. Templates can only be applied to objects with devices of the same type. Export agents TheExport agentsfunction saves information about the selected objects into a JSON file. The file contains details about the vehicle, device, and sensors. Follow objects Click Follow objects to see the live location of all selected objects on the map. History Click History to open the Historysection and view past movements and statuses of the selected objects. Move to group This function moves selected objects into a group. In the Move to Group window, choose the group and click Move. For details on creating and managing object groups, see the next article."},{"s":"object-list","t":"Object list","h":["Object list","Working with the list","How to customize status colors:","Object list settings","How to customize the table:","Column data filters","Object groups","How to create a group:","Working with groups"],"x":"Object list In the Online tab, you will find the list of objects. Here, you can monitor the status of your objects and control their parameters. Working with the list Here we will consider the functions for working with the list. Add object To add a new object, click this button. Then fill in the information in the object card. After saving, the new object will appear in the list. Filtering objects in the list by status: Blocked objects Parking — objects that have been idle for longer than the specified time Idle in zone — objects that have been idle in a certain geozone for longer than the specified time Movement — objects that are in motion Speeding — objects that exceed the set speed limit No connection — objects that are without a connection You can also customize color indicators for each status. Color indicators in the list highlight each object in a specific color, which makes it easy to determine whether the object is moving, parked, or has problems. How to customize status colors: In the status filter, click Customize 2. In the window that opens, select your preferred colors for each object status 3. Click Save — ​​the status colors will be updated Use the Reset to default button to restore the standard palette. Turn on the display of color statuses in the object list by clicking the correspondingbutton. When you adjust colors, they are also displayed in the status bar on the top panel. Clicking on a status on the top panel filters the list, displaying only objects with that status. For example, if you click Moving, only moving objects will remain in the list. This feature helps visually identify object statuses in the list. Click Highlight object names in the list and choose one of two options: Default — object names are displayed without highlighting By status — object names are highlighted with colors according to their status palette Each object will then be displayed in a color that reflects its status. Click the view button to select how objects are displayed in the list: By groups By tags By geofences As a plain list To quickly find an object, start typing any letter or number. The system will automatically filter the list to show only objects whose name or parameters contain the entered value. Enter the name, number, or any other value of the object you’re looking for, and that object will be highlighted in the list. Download the object list in Excel format for printing, sharing, or archiving. See below for how to create and manage object groups. This function transfers objects from the Wialon system to OneGPS. See the related guide for details. Object data may change in real time (e.g., status updates). Click the refresh button to reload the list and see the current status of all objects. Object list settings Customize the object list table to your needs: choose which columns to display, and configure sorting and filters. How to customize the table: In the column header, click the arrow . The column settings menu will appear: Sort data ascending or descending Select columns to display Filter data by the chosen column 2. To choose which columns to display: Click Columns — a list of available parameters will appear Check the boxes for the parameters you want to see If you need new parameters, contact your administrator. Additional fields are created in the vehicle field editor in the Admin panel. Column data filters Each column has filters to quickly find objects by certain parameters. Types of filters: List selection — check one or more values from a dropdown. Example: filter objects by Type by selecting the needed value in the Type column. The table will instantly show matching objects. Search field — type a value, and the table will filter objects by your query. Example: filter by Geofence by typing the name in the search field. The table will show matching objects. Object groups You can group objects by any criteria for better organization. Groups are also used to assign access rights to multiple objects at once. How to create a group: Click Add group in the workspace 2. Enter the group name 3. Click Save If marked as private, the group will only be visible to the user who created it. Working with groups To add an object to a group, drag and drop it into the group. To manage a group: 1. Right-click the group To show or hide the list of objects within a group, click the expand/collapse icons. 2. Select an action: Rename Highlight in the list Import objects (upload a list of objects) Delete To delete a group, first remove its objects. To show or hide the list of objects within a group, click the expand /collapse icons."},{"s":"additional-object-information","t":"Additional object information","h":["Additional object information","Tooltip in the object list","Tooltip on the map","How to customize tooltip content:"],"x":"Additional object information Tooltip in the object list In the object list, you can view additional information about an object in a tooltip. The tooltip may include: mileage location presence in geofences speed object icon sensor values custom fields configured by the user To view the tooltip, hover your mouse over the object in the list. For tooltips to appear in the object list, go to Personalization and enable the option Show object tree tooltips. Tooltip on the map When you check objects in the list, they will automatically appear on the map. To open the tooltip, click the object icon on the map with the left mouse button. How to customize tooltip content: Go to Account settings 2. Open the Personalization tab 3. Find the section Tooltip items 4. Select the elements you want to display in the object tooltip — check the corresponding boxes 5. Click Save settings in the Personalization tab"},{"s":"sensors-1","t":"Sensors","h":["Sensors"],"x":"Sensors A sensor is a device that collects and transmits data about a specific parameter or the condition of an object. For example, sensors help monitor: fuel levels in tanks engine operation cargo temperature in refrigerated trucks air humidity and other parameters In this section, you will learn: how to add a new sensor and configure its parameters what types of sensors are available in the system how to use sensor templates how to work with handlers how to create sensor groups how to assign tags how to copy sensor settings"},{"s":"how-to-add-a-sensor-2","t":"How to add a sensor","h":["How to add a sensor","How to add a sensor","Sensor parameters window"],"x":"How to add a sensor Before adding a sensor, make sure that the device actually sends the specified parameter. If you add a sensor with a parameter that the device does not transmit, the sensor will not work. How to add a sensor Open the object menu. To do this, right-click the object in the list 2. In the object menu, select the Sensors tab 3. In the opened window, go to the Sensors tab and click the button to add a new sensor 4. In the pop-up window, select the sensor type by its purpose. For example, a battery charge sensor 5. Fill in the required fields — enter the sensor name, description, and select the field where the device sends the data values. Configure other necessary parameters 6. Click Save. Check that the sensor appears in the list of active sensors How to check if the sensor is working properly 1. Open the object menu 2. Go to the Sensors tracingtab 3. Here you will see the values transmitted by all sensors Watch the video “How to add a new sensor” to quickly learn how to add and configure a new sensor in the OneGPS system. Sensor parameters window When adding or editing a sensor, the sensor parameters window opens. To open this window for editing, double-click the sensor in the list. In the sensor parameters window, you fill in the following fields: Function Select the sensor’s purpose from the dropdown list Type The sensor type (e.g., binary, pulse, discrete). The system automatically sets the type when you choose the funcrion Name Required field. Enter the name to distinguish the sensor in the system. Use the copy-icon to fill in quickly. If you have multiple similar sensors, give them unique names Description Required field. Enter a description. Use the copy-icon for quick filling Field Required field. Select the field from the dropdown that contains the values for this sensor Unit of measurement Define the units for displaying sensor values (liters, kilometers, etc.) Group Select a sensor group. See how to create sensor groupshere Conversion formula Apply a formula to recalculate raw data into adjusted sensor readings Tags Assign a tag to the sensor. See how to create tagshere Min/Max value Set acceptable ranges of values. Helps filter out incorrect readings Min/Max threshold Define the working range for the sensor. If values go outside, the system logs a deviation On/Off names Define labels for the ON and OFF states You can also enable extra options: Sensor event filter Prevents false triggers caused by short-term noise. You can set how many data packets or seconds are required before the system confirms a state change. Example: set 3 → the system confirms ON/OFF only after 3 consecutive packets with the signal. Cenerate events Each sensor state change creates a record in the event log Filter null values Filters out invalid values. Filters out invalid values. Incoming data is transformed by formula and compared to the calibration table. Out-of-range values are ignored Show in tooltips Displays sensor values in object tooltips Fixed values Define specific numeric values for ON/OFF instead of ranges. Example: ON = 38, OFF = 37. Other values are ignored Save history If enabled, sensor values are saved to reports. Otherwise, only current values are available Count engine hours Tracks engine or equipment runtime based on sensor data Show tooltip in mobile app If enabled, the sensor values are visible on smartphones. If disabled — hidden in the mobile version Aggregation period The minimum time interval during which sensor data will be grouped (aggregated) into a single event. If disabled — the system processes every single message from the sensor separately. Because of this, reports may show many short triggers, false on/off events, and the sensor's working intervals may look broken and unstable. If enabled and set, for example, to 120 seconds — the system combines all sensor values received within this interval into one event. The sensor stops \"flickering\" due to frequent messages and works in a more stable way. Additional settings for some sensors: Frame for analysis of differences For temperature sensors, defines how many consecutive measurements must exceed the threshold before the system confirms it. Helps avoid false alerts. Smooth factor Improves accuracy by removing spikes. The system takes values before/after a measurement, discards extremes, averages the rest, and replaces the original reading. Example: n = 3 → takes 3 points on each side for averaging. The higher the n, the stronger the smoothing Temperature range Defines acceptable temperature limits Remove symmetric fillings/drain Filters out small fuel level changes (e.g., from slope or errors) Active Enables monitoring of refueling and draining events"},{"s":"how-to-set-up-the-calibration-table-of-the-sensor","t":"How to set up the calibration table of the sensor","h":["How to set up the calibration table of the sensor","Example: Fuel tank calibration","Graph calibration table","How to create a table","Text states calibration table"],"x":"How to set up the calibration table of the sensor OneGPS supports two types of calibration tables: 1. Graph calibration table — converts raw values to real ones using a graph. Commonly used for fuel level, weight, temperature, humidity sensors. 2. Text states calibration table — converts raw values into predefined statuses (e.g., “No Signal,” “Weak,” “Good”). Example: Fuel tank calibration Suppose the tank has an irregular shape, so volume is not directly proportional to fuel level. The sensor measures resistance (Ohm) instead of liters. You must perform calibration by emptying and filling the tank step by step, recording resistance at each fuel level. Example calibration table for a 100-liter tank: 0 180 10 160 20 140 30 120 40 100 50 80 60 60 70 50 80 40 90 20 100 10 When the sensor sends 100 Ohm, the system knows there are ~40 liters in the tank. Graph calibration table This type of calibration table converts sensor readings into real values using a dependency graph. On the X-axis, you see the sensor readings, and on the Y-axis, the actual measured values. A graph is built between the recorded points, which determines the real value for each sensor reading. This type of calibration table is often used for fuel level sensors, weight, temperature, and humidity sensors. How to create a table You can configure the table in the sensor parameters window or upload a ready-made table from a file. The configured table can be saved to a file and reused for other similar sensors. To open the calibration table, click Edit. How to add values to the table: Click Add 2. Enter the actual amount of fuel and the value displayed by the sensor 3. Click Save 4. Repeat these steps for all required reading Text states calibration table This type of calibration table is used for multi-position sensors and converts sensor readings into predefined statuses. Example of using this type of table Suppose you have a multi-position GPS antenna sensor that shows signal strength. The GPS receiver measures signal power in dBm, usually ranging from -160 dBm to -120 dBm. In the table, you translate the technical values from the sensor into understandable statuses: No signal Weak signal Medium signal Strong signal For example, when the sensor sends a value of -135 dBm, the system finds the corresponding value in the table and translates it into a text status — Weak signal. The text status will appear in the object tooltip and in reports. This type of calibration table can be used for various parameters. For example: Set value ranges for temperature: Cold — 0–10°C, Warm — 10–25°C, Hot — 25°C and above Show battery charge not in percentages but in statuses like Low, Medium, Full. How to add a calibration table for multi-position sensors: Open the sensor parameters window 2. In the Calibration table section, click Edit 3. In the calibration table window, click Add 4. Fill in the columns with the sensor’s minimum and maximum values 5. Set text statuses in the Semantics column 6. Select an icon and a background color for the status. The status color is used in charts and in the track history when displaying routes 7. ClickSave"},{"s":"how-to-work-with-a-list-of-sensors","t":"How to work with a list of sensors","h":["How to work with a list of sensors","How to customize the sensor list table:","How to delete a sensor"],"x":"How to work with a list of sensors Added sensors appear in the Sensors tab. The list contains columns with the same names as the sensor parameters. You can customize the sensor list table to suit your needs: choose which parameter columns to display and adjust their sorting. How to customize the sensor list table: In the header of any column, click the small arrow . A menu with column settings will appear: Sort data in ascending or descending order Select which columns to display 2. To adjust which columns are shown: Click Columns — a list of available parameters for display will open Check the boxes next to the parameters you want to see How to delete a sensor To delete a sensor, click in the sensor list."},{"s":"sensor-types","t":"Sensor types","h":["Sensor types","Sensor types","Types of sensors by operation principle","Text sensors","Discrete sensors","Pulse sensors","Two-position sensors","Discrete integral sensors","Pulse integral sensors","Inverse two-position sensors","Multi-position sensors","Composite sensors","Special sensors","Sensor table","How to configure a fuel sensor","General sensor settings","Refuel and drain detection","Reverse flowdrain","Event thresholds","Speed filter","Fuel change rate filter (L/h)","Sensor noise sensitivity","Removing symmetric events"],"x":"Sensor types This article explains the types of sensors available in the OneGPS system, their purposes, and the parameters that need to be configured for correct operation of certain sensors. You will learn: the different types of sensors and how they work how to select a sensor’s assignment and type how to configure a fuel sensor Sensor types The type of sensor determines which parameters it monitors in the system. Let’s take a closer look at the different types of sensors available in OneGPS and how they are used. When adding a sensor, first select its function. The type is assigned automatically, but you can change it if needed. 1 — Function 2 — Sensor type Types of sensors by operation principle Text sensors Display the device status in words. For example, instead of technical codes, a lock sensor will show messages like: “Lock open”, “Lock closed”. Discrete sensors Transmit raw data exactly as received from the device. To get precise values, this data can be transformed using calibration tables or formulas. For discrete sensors, you can enable alerts. When the sensor state changes, the system will send email or SMS notifications. Watch the video to learn how to add and calibrate a capacitive fuel sensor. Pulse sensors Count the number of pulses between readings. Example: An engine hour sensor counts pulses to calculate the total operating time of equipment. Two-position sensors Capture two states of an object: on/off or open/closed. The system determines the state based on a value range: if the reading falls between the defined minimum and maximum, the sensor is “on”; otherwise, it is “off.” To learn how to work with DIP sensors (such as door sensors), watch the video “Working with DIP sensors” on YouTube. Discrete integral sensors Work like discrete sensors but also accumulate values over time. Example: A sensor can count the total number of events — such as how many times the engine started or doors were opened during a shift. Pulse integral sensors Similar to pulse sensors, but with accumulation of the total pulse count. Example: These sensors can record the total engine runtime for a month or the vehicle’s cumulative mileage for a quarter. Inverse two-position sensors Work like two-position sensors but display states in reverse. Example: A sensor triggers an alarm if a door is opened without permission. A regular sensor might fail due to wiring specifics, but an inverse sensor “flips” the signal: door open → shows closed; door closed → shows open. This ensures the alarm is always triggered. Multi-position sensors Used to register values that can take multiple states. Values are interpreted using a calibration table. Examples: temperature, humidity, or pressure sensors. To see how temperature thresholds are configured in the system, watch the short video “Setting the temperature threshold for a sensor” on YouTube. Composite sensors Combine data from several sensors. Example: combining fuel sensor and engine hour sensor data to calculate fuel consumption per hour. Special sensors Designed for specialized data collection. Examples: RFID sensors to identify objects or people, GPS sensors to track location, CAN bus sensors to gather vehicle data, or people counters to monitor visitor numbers in a building. Sensor table Ignition sensor Two-position Used to monitor engine operation and working hours. Shows 1 when engine is on, 0 when off Discrete Monitors the fuel level in the tank. The sensor has configurable refuel threshold and drain threshold: Refuel threshold — the minimum amount of fuel that counts as a valid refuel. Drain threshold — the minimum amount of fuel that can be recorded as a drain. In the fuel sensor settings, you can also specify the maximum speed for filling detection (km/h). If the vehicle is moving faster than this value, changes in fuel level will not be considered a refuel or drain. This helps prevent false records caused by fuel sloshing or driving uphill/downhill. A value of 0 disables this filter. The sensor also includes the Remove symmetric fillings/drain option. It excludes events where refuel and drain volumes are nearly identical. For example, if the sensor briefly detects a change in fuel level due to road slope or a technical glitch, the system will discard these records to avoid distorting real fuel consumption statistics. Enable the Activeoption to turn on or off monitoring of fuel refuels and drains. When this option is active, the sensor records all changes in fuel level Equipment sensor Two-position Captures equipment start/stop. Shows 1 when on, 0 when off Battery charge sensor Discrete Tracks battery condition and charge level GPS antenna sensor Two-position Detects presence/absence of GPS signal. Shows 1 when signal is present, 0 when absent Temperature sensor Discrete Monitors cargo or ambient temperature. Requires min/max thresholds to be set SOS Button Two-position Marks messages as alarms when pressed. Requires threshold settings Engine block status Two-position Shows whether the engine lock mechanism is enabled or disabled CAN Mileage Discrete Tracks vehicle mileage Fluid volume sensor Discrete Monitors liquid level in a tank People counter Composite Detects number and direction of passengers entering/exiting Fuel consumption Pulse Measures the amount of fuel consumed using pulses. When a certain volume of fuel passes through the sensor, it sends an electrical signal. To correctly measure fuel consumption, you need to configure the Pulse per literparameter. This parameter defines how many pulses the sensor generates when one liter of fuel passes through it. iButton Special Used to identify drivers with an iButton, a compact electronic key with an embedded chip. When the key touches the reader, it transmits its unique number, which OneGPS uses to identify the driver. The iButton function can also be used toidentify drivers with a Mettax camera Accelerometer Discrete Detects speed changes and movement direction, records acceleration on X, Y, Z axes, and identifies collisions Fuel type Multi-position Used to monitor the consumption of multiple types of fuel, such as gas or gasoline. How this sensor detects refueling: 1. Refueling detection The sensor constantly monitors the fuel level and records readings. The system takes 5 consecutive fuel level measurements. It compares the difference between the first and last measurement. If the difference exceeds a specified value, the system considers it a refueling event. This value is set in the sensor settings. 2. Start of refueling When the system detects a refueling event, it analyzes the data from the past 20 minutes. It identifies the measurement point with the lowest fuel level during this period. This point is considered the start of refueling. 3. End of refueling The system determines that refueling has ended: If five consecutive measurements show the same fuel level, the vehicle has been refueled and is stationary. If the next measurement shows less fuel than the previous one, the vehicle refueled and started moving. If the signal is lost for an hour, the system assumes the vehicle’s power was turned off, for example, the ignition was switched off, which may also indicate the end of refueling. Fuel draining is determined similarly to refueling, but instead of an increase in fuel level, the system detects a sudden decrease. The system detects refueling or draining by comparing fuel levels at multiple points. If data is received too frequently, increase the number of points in the sensor settings to avoid false triggers. If data is received too infrequently, decrease the number of points Rotation speed Discrete Measures rotations of engines or mechanisms. Output in RPM Engine hour sensor Discrete Counts pulses between readings to track equipment operation time Loading/unloading sensor Two-position Tracks material loading/unloading in construction machinery RFID reader Special Used for reading RFID tags and identifying objects or users. An RFID tag is an electronic label or chip that stores a unique code to identify an object or person. It functions as a contactless electronic pass (RFID). Humidity sensor Discrete Used for monitoring humidity levels, for example, in refrigeration units, warehouses, and other locations where maintaining the proper humidity is important. It displays either a single value, e.g., 60%, or an allowable range, e.g., normal range 40–60%. Weight sensor Discrete Measures vehicle load Lock Text Sends unlock password. After triggering, a new menu item “Open Lock” appears in the object menu Sleep sensor Two-position Sends a signal when the device switches to sleep mode. After the last message, data transmission stops Security sensor Two-position Detects intrusion (e.g., door/window opening). States: on/off Battery connection Two-position Detects whether a battery is connected Pulse A sensor that calculates mileage using data received from the speedometer or other connected equipment. It records the distance traveled when GPS/GLONASS data is unavailable or insufficiently accurate. The terminal counts pulses between transmitted messages. OneGPS adds these values together and converts them into distance. To configure the sensor, specify the number of pulses per kilometer. This value must be determined separately for each vehicle because it depends on the installed equipment. For example, if 1,000 pulses correspond to one kilometer, enter 1000 in the Impulses per kilometer field How to configure a fuel sensor Fuel sensors in the system have multiple settings. This guide explains how to configure a sensor properly to receive accurate data and correctly track refueling and draining events. General sensor settings Function— the type of sensor in the system Type — the sensor’s operating principle, e.g., “Discrete” Name — internal sensor name displayed in the interface Field — the hardware input on the tracker the sensor is connected to Description — additional information about the sensor Units — usually liters (“L”) Conversion formula — if data adjustment is needed Tags — labels for easier search and filtering of sensors Refuel and drain detection The system can automatically detect refueling and draining events based on the fuel level graph. Active toggle — enables or disables refuel/drain detection for this sensor. For fuel sensors: all active sensors for an object are summed to calculate total fuel in the vehicle, and events are detected based on this sum. If disabled, the sensor works as a standard sensor without event detection. Reverse flowdrain The Reverse Flowdrain feature helps detect hidden fuel theft through the return line—the fuel line that carries unused fuel back to the tank. When enabled, the system analyzes abnormal fuel consumption while the vehicle is moving, estimates the amount of fuel drained, and marks the event location on the map. Event thresholds Refueling threshold — minimum volume (liters) for an event to be considered a refuel. Smaller changes are ignored to filter out minor fluctuations. Drain threshold — minimum volume for an event to be considered a drain. Speed filter This filter prevents normal fuel usage during movement from being detected as refuel or drain. Max speed for event detection — speed (km/h) below which the vehicle is considered stationary Speed mode — method for checking speed within the event Speed mode options: All points — all points in the event must be below the speed threshold (strictest mode) Start — only the first point is checked Any point — at least one point is below the threshold Avg — average speed of the event must be below the threshold Off — disables speed verification. In this mode, refuelings and drainings are recorded regardless of speed Ratio — checks the proportion of points below the threshold (recommended) Additional parameters for Ratio mode: Min speed ratio — minimum fraction of event points below the threshold (0–1, default 0.5). For example, 0.5 = 50% of points must be stationary. Min stop duration (sec) — minimum total stop time within the event. Helps ignore brief accidental stops. This mode ensures correct detection even if the start or end of a refuel overlaps a short movement period. Fuel change rate filter (L/h) This filter removes slow “drains” that are actually normal fuel consumption during operation. Min refuel speed (L/h) — minimum fuel level increase rate for an event to be considered a refuel Min drain speed (L/h) — minimum fuel level decrease rate for an event to be considered a drain If the rate is below the threshold, the event is treated as normal consumption, not refuel or drain. Sensor noise sensitivity Smoothing factor — degree of smoothing applied to the fuel graph before analysis. Higher values produce a smoother graph, reducing sharp jumps caused by sensor errors. The system takes several points before and after each measurement, removes extremes, and calculates an average. For example, n = 3 uses three points on either side for smoothing. Removing symmetric events This feature helps exclude false events where refueling and draining occur with nearly identical volumes (e.g., due to slope or technical glitches): Remove symmetric refuels/drains toggle — enables detection of “drain → refuel” or “refuel → drain” pairs with very similar volumes Max difference (%) — allowed relative difference between paired volumes Abs difference (L) — allowed absolute difference in liters Max distance (h) — maximum time interval for events to be considered a pair, e.g., 12 hours Watch a short YouTube video showing how to set up the fuel refill and drain detection."},{"s":"configuring-the-fuel-sensor","t":"Configuring the fuel sensor","h":["Configuring the fuel sensor","General sensor settings","Refuel and drain detection","Reverse flowdrain","Event thresholds","Speed filter","Fuel change rate filter (L/h)","Sensor noise sensitivity","Removing symmetric events"],"x":"Configuring the fuel sensor Fuel sensors in the system have multiple settings. This guide explains how to configure a sensor properly to receive accurate data and correctly track refueling and draining events. General sensor settings Function— the type of sensor in the system Type — the sensor’s operating principle, e.g., “Discrete” Name — internal sensor name displayed in the interface Field — the hardware input on the tracker the sensor is connected to Description — additional information about the sensor Units — usually liters (“L”) Conversion formula — if data adjustment is needed Tags — labels for easier search and filtering of sensors Refuel and drain detection The system can automatically detect refueling and draining events based on the fuel level graph. Active toggle — enables or disables refuel/drain detection for this sensor. For fuel sensors: all active sensors for an object are summed to calculate total fuel in the vehicle, and events are detected based on this sum. If disabled, the sensor works as a standard sensor without event detection. Reverse flowdrain The Reverse Flowdrain feature helps detect hidden fuel theft through the return line—the fuel line that carries unused fuel back to the tank. When enabled, the system analyzes abnormal fuel consumption while the vehicle is moving, estimates the amount of fuel drained, and marks the event location on the map. Event thresholds Refueling threshold — minimum volume (liters) for an event to be considered a refuel. Smaller changes are ignored to filter out minor fluctuations. Drain threshold — minimum volume for an event to be considered a drain. Speed filter This filter prevents normal fuel usage during movement from being detected as refuel or drain. Max speed for event detection — speed (km/h) below which the vehicle is considered stationary Speed mode — method for checking speed within the event Speed mode options: All points — all points in the event must be below the speed threshold (strictest mode) Start — only the first point is checked Any point — at least one point is below the threshold Avg — average speed of the event must be below the threshold Off — disables speed verification. In this mode, refuelings and drainings are recorded regardless of speed Ratio — checks the proportion of points below the threshold (recommended) Additional parameters for Ratio mode: Min speed ratio — minimum fraction of event points below the threshold (0–1, default 0.5). For example, 0.5 = 50% of points must be stationary. Min stop duration (sec) — minimum total stop time within the event. Helps ignore brief accidental stops. This mode ensures correct detection even if the start or end of a refuel overlaps a short movement period. Fuel change rate filter (L/h) This filter removes slow “drains” that are actually normal fuel consumption during operation. Min refuel speed (L/h) — minimum fuel level increase rate for an event to be considered a refuel Min drain speed (L/h) — minimum fuel level decrease rate for an event to be considered a drain If the rate is below the threshold, the event is treated as normal consumption, not refuel or drain. Sensor noise sensitivity Smoothing factor — degree of smoothing applied to the fuel graph before analysis. Higher values produce a smoother graph, reducing sharp jumps caused by sensor errors. The system takes several points before and after each measurement, removes extremes, and calculates an average. For example, n = 3 uses three points on either side for smoothing. Removing symmetric events This feature helps exclude false events where refueling and draining occur with nearly identical volumes (e.g., due to slope or technical glitches): Remove symmetric refuels/drains toggle — enables detection of “drain → refuel” or “refuel → drain” pairs with very similar volumes Max difference (%) — allowed relative difference between paired volumes Abs difference (L) — allowed absolute difference in liters Max distance (h) — maximum time interval for events to be considered a pair, e.g., 12 hours Watch a short YouTube video showing how to set up the fuel refill and drain detection."},{"s":"configuring-the-relative-odometer","t":"Configuring the relative odometer","h":["Configuring the relative odometer","Adding the sensor","Calculating mileage from pulses","Determining the number of pulses per kilometer","Verifying the calculation"],"x":"Configuring the relative odometer TheRelative odometer sensor calculates an object’s mileage from the number of pulses transmitted by its terminal. It is suitable for equipment that counts pulses until a data packet is sent and then starts a new count. OneGPS adds together the values received from the terminal and converts them into kilometers. The calculation uses the Pulses per kilometer coefficient. Configure the sensor separately for each object because the number of pulses per kilometer depends on the vehicle and installed equipment. Before configuring the sensor, make sure that: the terminal transmits the number of pulses in a separate field each value contains the pulses counted since the previous data packet was sent the number of pulses corresponding to one kilometer is known If the terminal transmits a continuously increasing total counter, the Relative odometer sensor with the Pulsetype is not suitable for processing these data. Adding the sensor 1. Add a new sensor to the object 2. In Function, select Relative odometer 3. Make sure that Type is set to Pulse 4. In Name, enter a clear sensor name, such as Relative odometer 5. In Field, select the parameter in which the terminal transmits the number of pulses 6. If necessary, complete Conversion formula. Leave this field empty if the terminal already transmits the number of pulses in the required format 7. In Pulses per kilometer, enter the number of pulses corresponding to one kilometer. If you do not know this coefficient, determine it using a test trip 8. Enable Filter values if required 9. ClickSave After you save the sensor, OneGPS starts adding together the values from the selected field and calculating the mileage using the specified number of pulses per kilometer. Calculating mileage from pulses A pulse is a signal that the terminal receives from connected equipment while the vehicle is moving. The farther the vehicle travels, the more pulses the terminal records. Each data packet contains the number of pulses counted since the previous packet was sent. OneGPS adds these values together for the selected period and calculates the mileage using the following formula: For example, the sensor is configured for 1,000 pulses per kilometer. During a trip, the terminal transmits three values: 200, 350, and 450. First, OneGPS adds the values together: 200 + 350 + 450 = 1,000 pulses It then calculates the mileage: 1,000 ÷ 1,000 = 1 km The value in Pulses per kilometer must be greater than zero. An accurate coefficient helps OneGPS calculate the mileage more precisely. Determining the number of pulses per kilometer If the coefficient is unknown, determine it using a test trip: 1. Record the initial mileage and pulse-counter readings 2. Drive a known distance. For a more accurate result, use a sufficiently long route, such as 10 kilometers 3. Determine the total number of pulses recorded during the trip 4. Divide the number of pulses by the distance traveled: Pulses per kilometer = Number of pulses ÷ Distance, km For example, the terminal records 12,500 pulses over 10 kilometers: 12,500 ÷ 10 = 1,250 pulses per kilometer Enter 1,250 inPulses per kilometer. After calibration, complete another test trip and compare the calculated mileage with the actual distance. If the values differ, adjust the coefficient. Verifying the calculation Compare the calculated mileage with the vehicle’s actual mileage over a test route. If the values differ: check that the correct pulse field is selected make sure the terminal starts a new count after sending each data packet adjust Pulses per Kilometer check that OneGPS received all data packets If OneGPS does not receive some of the packets, the calculated mileage may be lower than the actual mileage."},{"s":"sensor-templates","t":"Sensor templates","h":["Sensor templates","How to create a local template:","How to apply a template to a single object:","How to apply a template to multiple objects:","Where to find global templates:"],"x":"Sensor templates Templates are a ready-made set of parameters used for quickly configuring similar devices. A sensor template can only be applied to objects with the same type of device. For example, a template for the Teltonika FMB120 will only work for a Teltonika FMB120. There are two types of templates: Local templates — your own settings that you create on an object Global templates — ready-made settings that any user can use How to create a local template: Configure the sensors as needed 2. In the Sensors tab, click the Save template button 3. Enter the template name and description 4. Click Save as new How to apply a template to a single object: Go to the object to which you want to apply the template Sensor templates are only compatible with objects that have the same GPS device type. A template created for one object will only work on other objects with the same device. 2. Open the sensor window through the object menu 3. In the Sensors tab, select the saved template from the template dropdown list 4. Click Use template 5. If necessary, delete old sensors 6. Click Apply — the sensors from the template will appear in the Sensors tab To delete a template from the list, click the cross . How to apply a template to multiple objects: In the object list, select multiple objects while holding the Ctrl key 2. Right-click the objects — the multi-object menu will open 3. Select Sensor templates from the menu 4. In the window that opens, choose the saved template 5. Click Use template Where to find global templates: In the Sensors tab, in the template dropdown list 2. In the Sensor templates window when working with multiple objects"},{"s":"how-to-apply-formulas","t":"How to apply formulas","h":["How to apply formulas","Simple formulas","Examples of simple formulas","Variables in a formula","Examples of formulas with variables","Complex formulas","Examples of complex formulas","Conditional operators","Comparison operators","Example 1","Example 2","Example 3","PHP functions","Common PHP functions used in formulas","Examples of complex transformations","Division by zero","How to test a formula","Where else formulas are used"],"x":"How to apply formulas A conversion formula is a mathematical expression applied to the data received from a sensor. Why use a formula: To correct sensor readings — for example, a formula allows you to fix inaccuracies in sensor data To convert raw sensor data into real physical values. For example, if a sensor outputs numbers from 0 to 1000, the formula can transform them into actual liters of fuel in a tank To convert units of measurement, for example, kilometers to miles or liters to gallons To combine data — for example, a formula can use values from different sensors to calculate new indicators In the sensor settings window, the formula is applied in a special Conversion formula field. The formula is applied to data immediately after it is received from the sensor, before using the calibration table. The formula must start with one of the following signs: arithmetic signs: +, -, *, / equals sign: = You can use in a formula: mathematical operations: addition (+), subtraction (-), multiplication (*), division (/) parentheses ( ) to specify the order of operations Simple formulas Used for simple operations such as multiplication, division, addition, and subtraction. The raw sensor value is automatically substituted into the formula. Example of applying a formula Suppose we have a battery charge sensor that transmits a value in millivolts. In the raw points, we see: Vsource = 9337. To display the usual sensor value in volts, the raw value must be divided by 1000. In the sensor settings window, in the Conversion formula field, we write: /1000. To learn how to add a new sensor with a conversion formula, watch the video “New sensor with conversion formula to a sensor” on YouTube. The final sensor value will be calculated by the formula Vbattery/1000: 1. The system takes the current raw value from the sensor 2. Divides this value by 1000 3. The result becomes the new sensor value The final battery value in volts will be displayed in the object tooltip — 9.33V. Examples of simple formulas *10 Multiply the raw value by 10 /1000 Divide the raw value by 1000 /25.5+7 Divide by 25.5 and add 7 /100+(7*3-2)*4 More complex example using parentheses Decimal fractions in formulas must use a dot, not a comma. For example: 25.4, 1.75 Variables in a formula Formulas can use data from sensor fields and standard variables. All values used in a formula must be wrapped in %%. Standard variables that can be used in formulas: %S% — speed %A% — altitude %D% — direction %P% or %satsinview% — number of satellites %ts% — current packet timestamp %ts1% — previous packet timestamp %value% — current sensor value Timestamps are in Unix timestamp format (seconds since Jan 1, 1970). Other values can be taken directly from raw data packet field names. For example: %Vbattery% — battery charge value %GSM% — GSM signal strength %HDOP% — horizontal dilution of precision Simply wrap the parameter name in %%, and it will be replaced by the corresponding value during calculation. Examples of formulas with variables *%GSM% Multiply raw value by GSM field data /%HDOP% Divide raw value by HDOP field data %ts1%-%ts% Get time difference between current and previous packet %ts%/86400 Convert timestamp from seconds to days Complex formulas Complex formulas always begin with the = sign. In these formulas, the raw sensor value is not automatically used. If you need it, you must include it explicitly as %value%. This type of formula allows more advanced operations, including the use of variables, parentheses, and mathematical functions. Examples of complex formulas =%value%*2+10 Multiply raw value by 2 and add 10 =%S%*3.6+%value% Convert speed to km/h and add raw sensor value =(%DIN1%+%DIN2%)/%value% Divide sum of digital inputs by raw sensor value =7/%value% Divide 7 by raw sensor value Conditional operators Conditional operators are a way to set up decision logic in a formula. They allow the formula to return different values depending on whether a certain condition is true or false. This is similar to saying: “If the condition is true, do one thing, otherwise do another.” A conditional operator is written like this: condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false This expression can be explained as follows: condition — the expression being checked, for example, temperature greater than 30 value_if_true — the value returned if the condition is true value_if_false — the value returned if the condition is false Comparison operators You can use the following operators: == equal checks exact equality != != checks inequality > greater left value greater than right < less left value less than right >= greater or equal left ≥ right <= less or equal left ≤ right && and all conditions must be true || or at least one condition must be true Example 1 Let’s say we have a digital input sensor field DIN1, and we want to set a condition: If DIN1 equals 1, the result will be 22 If DIN1 does not equal 1, the result will be 33 The formula will look like this: =(%DIN1% == 1) ? 22 : 33 The system checks the condition: %DIN1% == 1 (whether the value of DIN1 equals 1). If the condition is true, it returns 22. If the condition is false, it returns 33. Example 2 Let’s say we have a temperature sensor field temp, and we want to set a condition: If the temperature is greater than 30, the result will be temperature * 10 If the temperature is less than or equal to 30, the result will be temperature * 20 The formula will look like this: =(%temp% > 30) ? %temp% * 10 : %temp% * 20 The system checks the condition: %temp% > 30 (whether the temperature is greater than 30). If the condition is true, it returns temperature × 10. If the condition is false, it returns temperature × 20. Example 3 Let’s say we have a temperature sensor field temp and a humidity sensor field humidity, and we want to set the following condition: If the temperature is greater than 25 AND the humidity is less than 60%, the result will be 100 Otherwise, the result will be 50 The formula will look like this: =(%temp% > 25 && %humidity% < 60) ? 100 : 50 Here: %temp% > 25— the first condition (temperature greater than 25) %humidity% < 60— the second condition (humidity less than 60%) The operator && means both conditions must be true. The system checks the conditions, and: If both conditions are true, it returns 100 If at least one condition is false, it returns 50 PHP functions PHP functions are built-in tools of the PHP programming language for processing, transforming, and formatting data. They can be used directly in formulas to perform advanced transformations. Usage steps: Write the function name in the formula 2. Put the value(s) you want to process in parentheses 3. The system automatically processes the value and returns the result Example: =round(45.78) → 46 Common PHP functions used in formulas substr Extracts part of a string =substr('%FIELD%', 1, 2) → returns 2 characters starting from position 1 round Rounds a number =round(%AIN1%/1000) hexdec Converts hex to decimal =hexdec('%Param%'), if Param = 0A, then the function will return the value 10 dechex Converts a decimal number to hexadecimal =dechex('%Param%'), if Param = 255, then the function will convert 255 into 'FF' decbin Converts a decimal number to binary =decbin('%Param%'), if Param = 10, the function will return 1010 hex2bin Converts a hexadecimal string into binary data =hex2bin ('%Param%'), if Param = 4142, then the function will return the binary representation 'AB' strrev Reverses a string =strrev('%value%'), if value = 0123d848010000e5, the function will convert it to 5e000001048d3210 bytesrev Reverses hexadecimal bytes =bytesrev('%value%'), if value = 0123d848010000e5, the function will convert it to e500000148d82301 explode Splits a string into parts using a specified delimiter and returns an array of strings =explode(',','%value%')[2] Suppose the sensor sends a string of values separated by commas: 90.5,3450,112,43,215 The formula =explode(',','%value%')[2] takes the sensor readings, splits them into an array: '90.5', '3450', '112', '43', '215', and then extracts the element with index 2 — '112'. A string array is a list of individual strings, numbered from zero. ','is the delimiter, in this case a comma. Splitting happens at this symbol. '%value%'is the string that needs to be split. [2]— square brackets are used to access an element of the array. [2] means “give me the third element”, since numbering starts at 0. max Compares several values and returns the largest one =max('%value%', 90), if value = 85, the function will return 90 min Returns the smallest value =min('%value%', 90), if value = 85, the function will return 85 abs Returns the absolute value of a number =abs('%value%'), if value = -15, the function will return 15 str_replace Replaces some characters or words with others search — what to look for replace — what to replace with subject — where to look (the string) Example: if you need to replace commas with dots in numbers: =str_replace(\",\", \".\",'%value%') If value = 1,234,567, the function will return 1.234.567 sqrt Calculates the square root =sqrt('%value%'), if value = 25, the function will return 5 pow Raises a number to a power =pow('%value%', 3), if value = 2, the function will return 8 (that is, 2³) sin Calculates the sine of an angle (in radians) =sin('%value%'), if value = 1.57, the result ≈ 1 cos Calculates the cosine of an angle (in radians) =cos('%value%'), if value = 0, the result will be 1 asin Calculates the arcsine (in radians) =asin('%value%'), if value = 1, the function will return 1.57 acos Calculates the arccosine (in radians) =acos('%value%'), if value = 0, the function will return 1.57 number_format Formats a number with separators =number_format(1234.56, 2, ',', ' ') , the function will return 1 234,56 time Returns the current time in UNIX format — the number of seconds since January 1, 1970 =time('2023-12-20 12:00:00') array_sum Adds up all numbers in an array =array_sum([1,2,3,4]) — the function returns 10 pack Packs data into binary format =pack('H*', '4142') — packs into binary data 'AB' unpack Unpacks binary data =unpack('H*', 'AB')[1] — returns '4142' Examples of complex transformations Unsigned number to signed number conversion =((%BLE_T1%>3000)?((%BLE_T1%-65535)/100):(%BLE_T1%/100)) If the number is greater than 3000 — subtract 65535 from it and divide by 100 If it is less than or equal to 3000 — divide by 100 This formula is often used for temperature sensors, where negative values are encoded as large positive numbers. Byte sequence conversion (little endian to big endian) =strtoupper(substr(unpack('H*',strrev(hex2bin(%value%)))[1],2,12)) Example: 1500000124286b01 → 6B2824010000 Step by step: hex2bin(%value%) — converts a hex string into binary data strrev(...) — reverses the bytes unpack('H*', ...) — converts back into hex substr(..., 2, 12) — extracts the required part of the string strtoupper(...) — converts the result to uppercase Division by zero When a formula involves division, be careful with the divisor — the number you are dividing by. If the divisor equals zero, an error will occur because dividing by zero is mathematically impossible. For example, the formula: =1000/%DIN1% will cause an error if the value of sensor DIN1 is 0 or undefined. In this case, use a conditional operator to check the divisor: =(%DIN1%==0) ? 0 : 1000/%DIN1% The system will check the condition: %DIN1% == 0 (whether the value of DIN1 equals zero). If the condition is true, the result will be 0. If the condition is false, the system will perform the division 1000 / DIN1 value. How to test a formula When you apply a formula, make sure it correctly transforms the data. You can test the formula using the Test button: Click the Test button in the Formula field 2. The system will display values that you can use to test the formula defined in the handler. You can select predefined values or enter your own. 3. After that, click Test again, and the system will show the result of applying the formula: whether it’s correct or not. Where else formulas are used In OneGPS, formulas can be used not only for processing sensor data. You can also apply formulas in the Object card or when working with the Notifications module. In all cases, the principle remains the same — the same formula writing rules and mathematical operations apply."},{"s":"sensor-handlers","t":"Sensor handlers","h":["Sensor handlers","How to create a handler","How to configure a calibration table","How to apply a handler","Examples of using handlers","Example 1. Converting sensor data with a handler","Example 2. Getting total and average values from two fuel level sensors"],"x":"Sensor handlers Handlers are tools that help convert and process raw sensor data into meaningful values. Instead of writing complex formulas for every single sensor, you can create a handler once and reuse it anywhere it’s needed. For example, let’s say you have a vehicle onboard voltage sensor that transmits values in millivolts. In raw data, the sensor value is: Vsource = 12270 To display the usual reading in volts, the value needs to be divided by 1000. We can create a handler for this operation and later reuse it with other sensors where values must be divided by 1000. How to create a handler In the Sensors window, open the Handlers tab In the tab, click Add 3. Enter a name for the handler. This name will later be used in the Formula field of the sensor settings. The name can contain only Latin letters, digits, and symbols 4. In the Conversion formula field, write the formula that will use this handler When referencing a sensor data field, wrap the field name with double percent signs (%%), for example: %fuel_1% or %value% 5. Add a description for the handler so you’ll know what it is used for 6. Click Save How to configure a calibration table A handler can include a calibration table that will be applied automatically. This table helps convert the sensor’s technical readings into understandable values. Steps: Click the calibration table icon 2. In the calibration table window, click Add 3. Enter the real value and the corresponding sensor reading 4. Click Save 5. Repeat the process for all required data points 6. Click Save again in the calibration table window In the calibration table, you can enable the options Select nearest value and Calibration first: Select nearest value — rounds to the closest calibration point. If the sensor returns a value not exactly listed in the table, the system can either take the nearest point (if enabled) or calculate an intermediate value using the surrounding points (if disabled). Calibration first — defines how the system processes data during calibration. The system always checks the calibration table first. If an exact value is found in the table — it is used. If not — the system applies the standard calculation algorithm. You can also use the buttons to upload a calibration table from a file or save it to your computer To delete a value from the table, click the delete icon . How to apply a handler Open the sensor where you want to apply the handler. 2. In the Formula field, enter the handler name: Start with = , then type the handler name. Then in parentheses add the sensor field wrapped in %...%. Example: =divide(%fuel_1%) 3. Test the handler using theTest button 4. Click Save in the sensor settings window to apply the handler. Examples of using handlers Example 1. Converting sensor data with a handler Let’s create a handler: Enter a name — divide 2. In the formula field, enter: %value%/1000 %value% is the raw sensor reading that must be divided by 1000. 3. Add a description — Division by 1000 4. Save the handler Apply the handler: Open the sensor settings where you want to apply it. 2. In the Formula field, write: =divide(%Vsource%) 3. Test the handler with theTest button 4. Save the sensor settings Check the display of sensor readings after applying the handler: Raw sensor value: Vsource = 12314 После обработки формулой в подсказке объекта показывается реальное значение датчика — 12,3 В. This example demonstrates how to perform a simple operation with a handler. However, handlers can also contain more complex formulas if necessary. Once created, a handler can be reused across multiple similar sensors, helping avoid formula errors. Example 2. Getting total and average values from two fuel level sensors Suppose you have two fuel level sensors and you want to see: their total fuel level, and their average fuel level. Both sensors must output values in the same units, and each must have its own calibration table configured. Create handlers for each sensor: Handler for Sensor 1 Name: fuel_nav_1 2. Formula: %t_53lls1% (copy the field name from the card of fuel sensor 1) 3. Save the handler Handler for Sensor 2 Name: fuel_nav_2 2. Formula: %lls2% (copy the field name from the card of fuel sensor 2) 3. Save the handler Now create a total fuel sensor Enter name and description — Total fuel sensor 2. Select sensor type — Discrete 3. In the field, specify the stream of one of the sensors (e.g., t_53lls1) — this stream will supply the data points for recalculation. 4. Formula: =fuel_nav_1()+fuel_nav_2() 5. Save the total fuel sensor Next, create an average fuel sensor: Enter name and description — Average fuel sensor 2. Select sensor type — Discrete 3. In the field, specify the stream of one of the sensors (e.g., t_53lls1) 4. Formula: =(fuel_nav_1()+fuel_nav_2())/2 5. Save the average fuel sensor Check the raw data to verify the new sensor values. As a result, the object will have four sensors: Fuel sensor 1 Fuel sensor 2 Total fuel sensor Average fuel sensor The same approach can be applied to process temperature sensors."},{"s":"sensor-groups","t":"Sensor groups","h":["Sensor groups","How to add a sensor group","How to assign a sensor to a group"],"x":"Sensor groups Sensor groups are a way to organize sensors in a way that is convenient for you. You can create groups based on any criteria important for your work. For example: by type of measurement: temperature, pressure, speed by location: sensors in the cabin, in the engine, on the external part of the object How to add a sensor group 1. Click the Add groupbutton 2. In the window that opens, enter a name for the new group 3. (Optional) Add a description for the group 4. Click Save How to assign a sensor to a group 1. Open the sensor’s edit window 2. In the Group field, select the desired group name"},{"s":"sensor-tags","t":"Sensor tags","h":["Sensor tags","How to create a sensor tag","How to assign a tag to a sensor"],"x":"Sensor tags Tags are a tool for grouping sensors by specific parameters. To use tags in the system, go to Personalization and enable the options Show global tags and Show labels tags. How to create a sensor tag 1. Click Add tag 2. Enter a name for the tag 3. Select the sensor type from the dropdown list 4. (Optional) Add a description 5. Choose an icon and color for the tag (Selecting a color is mandatory) 6. Click Save How to assign a tag to a sensor 1. Open the sensor edit window 2. In the Tags field, select the tag name You can use tags when creating reports, setting up notifications, and defining events."},{"s":"copying-sensor-settings","t":"Copying sensor settings","h":["Copying sensor settings"],"x":"Copying sensor settings If you have several similar monitoring objects, you can copy sensor settings from one object to another. Copying sensor settings from the current object. Open the sensor window of the object from which you want to copy settings 2. Click the Export button 3. Select the checkboxes for the sensor settings you want to copy 4. Click Export — the settings file will be saved to your computer Transferring sensor settings to a new object. Before importing sensors, make sure you are working with the correct object. To do this, check the object’s AgentID in the sensor window. This is the unique identification number of the object Open the sensor window for the object where you want to copy settings. Click the Import button Find and select the previously saved settings file 4. If needed, configure the import parameters — click Select import options. A settings window will open. 5. Click Import"},{"s":"history","t":"History","h":["History"],"x":"History History is information about an object's past movements and states, including its routes, stops, and sensor readings over a specific period of time. With History, you can see where the object was, analyze its route and speed, as well as review sensor data. In this section, you will learn: how to view the movement history of objects how to work with the track list how to use the history player how to display a track on the map how to view object events how to work with speed and sensor readings charts"},{"s":"viewing-history","t":"Viewing history","h":["Viewing history","How to view an object's movement history:"],"x":"Viewing history The Historysection helps track the movements of vehicles or other monitored objects. Here, you can view routes, analyze events, monitor sensor readings, and get detailed information about trips for a selected period. How to view an object's movement history: 1. Go to the History section in the top navigation panel Last report button opens the most recently generated history. 2. Select the object from the list by checking its box. 3. Specify the time period using one of the following methods: 1 — Use the calendar to select specific dates 2 — Use quick buttons — “Yesterday,” “Today,” “Last week” 3 — Check only working days, if needed 4 — To include parkings information in the history, check this box 4. Click the Search button 5. In the search results, check the track records of the object that you want to view A track is a line on the map showing the object's movement path. The track consists of points marking the locations where the object was at different times. After selecting the tracks you are interested in, the screen will display the following elements: 1 — Selected track list 2 — The player is a tool for playing back and controlling the track view 3 — The Events window displays significant events or changes related to the object 4 — Movement route on the map 5 — The chart displays the object's speed or sensor readings over the selected period"},{"s":"working-with-history","t":"Working with history","h":["Working with history","Track list","How to configure the track record list:","Buttons under the track list","Player","Track on the map","Track color by speed","Track сolor by sensor readings","Event markers","Events","Chart"],"x":"Working with history This article explains the history functions that help track an object’s movement and parameters and work with the collected data. Track list After selecting the time period and parameters for building the history, the search results display a track list. A track is a line on the map showing the object’s movement path. It consists of points marking the locations where the object was at different times. The list consists of track records with parameters such as track date, start, stop, duration, distance, and maximum speed of the object. To adjust the parameters that affect track construction, go to Object settings. Customize the track list as you like. Choose which columns with parameters to display, for example, parking or stop duration. How to configure the track record list: In the header of any column, click the arrow . A column settings menu will appear where you can: Sort the data ascending or descending Select which columns to show on the screen Group records by the selected parameter 2. To configure which columns to show: Click Columns — a list of available parameters for display will open Check the boxes next to the parameters you want to see Buttons under the track list Follow — tracks the object on the map in real time Return to Online — returns to the Online tab but keeps the player and track on the map Close — removes the selected tracks from the list Player The player allows you to view how the object moved in the past. Click the Start button to begin playback. Watch the object’s movement on the map and the speed chart at the bottom of the screen. Next to the player, you will see the date, time, and current speed of the object at a given moment. To speed up playback, click the speed increase button — this helps to review long routes faster. Track on the map When a track record is selected, the system displays the object’s movement route on the map. You can add multiple tracks to the map simultaneously, for example, to compare routes of one or several objects over different periods. Tracks can be displayed in different colors depending on the selected parameters. Track color by speed The speed color is configured in the Speed in Color section under the Map and Geocoding tab. Route segments are colored according to the object’s speed. Track сolor by sensor readings The track is colored according to the readings of a selected multi-position sensor along the entire route. To display a track with color-coded sensor readings: Make sure the sensor is configured and has colors set in the calibration table 2. Build the object’s movement history by selecting tracks in History 3. After the track appears, locate the sensor list panel at the bottom of the map 4. Select the desired sensor from the list 5. Click the palette button that appears 6. The track on the map will be colored according to the sensor readings at each point along the route To enlarge the map area, collapse the bottom chart panel by clicking the Collapse chart panel button. Event markers Special icons on the map indicate important events that occurred during movement, such as fuel thefts, speeding, or parking. Click on a marker to get detailed information about the event. Events Events in History are actions that occurred with the object. They are displayed in the Events tab. In the Map and geocoding tab, you can configure which types of events are displayed in History. To view events in History: Build the object’s history and select tracks 2. Go to the Eventstab. All actions of the object on the selected track will appear 3. Check the boxes next to the events you want to see on the map 4. Go to the map to view the selected events along the track Chart The chart displays the object’s speed and sensor readings. When a track is selected, only speed is displayed on the chart by default. To add sensor readings, select the desired sensors from the sensor panel. Their values will appear on the chart. To temporarily hide a sensor’s data, click its name to the left of the chart. The name will be crossed out, and its line will disappear. Clicking again will restore the display. Click Select all sensors to display all available readings. Click Clear to remove all selected readings from the chart. Watch the video “How to view fuel consumption chart in the History” to quickly learn how to monitor fuel usage and analyze refueling/draining events."},{"s":"reports-1","t":"Reports","h":["Reports"],"x":"Reports A report is collected information about monitored objects for a selected period of time. Reports help you track vehicle operations, control task completion, detect deviations, and make informed decisions. In this section, you will learn: —how to set up access to reports how to build a report how to configure report parameters how to download or print a finished report —which types of reports are available and what they are used for"},{"s":"setting-up-access-to-reports","t":"Setting up access to reports","h":["Setting up access to reports","Configuring reports via the Admin panel","Configuring reports in the user account","Displaying reports in the list"],"x":"Setting up access to reports To set up access to the required reports, you need to complete three main steps: Select available reports in the admin panel 2. Assign report permissions to specific users 3. Configure how reports are displayed in the general list Configuring reports via the Admin panel If you have administrator access: Log in to the Admin panel 2. Go to the Accounts tab 3. Select a contract 4. Right-click the chosen contract — a context menu will appear 5. In the context menu, select Report list 6. In the list that appears, check the boxes next to the reports you want to make available to the users of this contract If necessary, you can change a report name. To do this, click the field in the Alternative name column and enter a more user-friendly name. Configuring reports in the user account Next, you need to specify which reports will be available to specific users: In the contract configuration, go to the Staff and groups tab 2. Select a user from the list and click the icon 3. A window with the list of reports will open 4. Check the boxes next to the reports you want to assign access to for this user You can either grant access to all reports or limit access to only the necessary ones. Displaying reports in the list To configure which report types will be available when creating a new report: In the Contract setting , go to the Report list tab 2. Select report types — check the boxes next to the required report names 3. Click Save settings After configuring report display and access rights, you will be able to: View reports in the list Select the needed report for review Work only with the reports the user has access to"},{"s":"how-to-build-a-report","t":"How to build a report","h":["How to build a report","How to build a report:"],"x":"How to build a report How to build a report: In the main menu, go to the Reports section. Select an object by checking the box next to it in the object list 2. Set the time range for the report Choose the time interval for the report by clicking the calendar icon Or use quick buttons — report for Yesterday, Today, or Last week. 3. Select the report type from the dropdown list Specify parameters for the report 4. Choose whether the report should be split by days, weeks, or shown without interval breakdown 5. Select grouping by dates to analyze changes over time or by monitoring objects to compare data between different objects 6. Choose one or more sensor tags to include in the report. 7. Click Build report, and you will see the finished report with the chosen parameters"},{"s":"how-to-work-with-a-report","t":"How to work with a report","h":["How to work with a report","Interactive map","Additional parameters","Report settings","Example","Downloading a report","Report scheduler"],"x":"How to work with a report Once the report is created, you will immediately see the results online. 1 — Map 2 — Divider Interactive map Some reports include an interactive map to help you visualize report data. To expand the map area, simply drag the screen divider . For example: In trip reports, you can select a specific record, and the map will display the corresponding route with start and end markers. In Parkings reports, choose a record and a stop marker will appear on the map. Click it to view detailed information about the parking. Marker on map When you finish working with the map, click Clearin the upper-right corner of the map to remove all displayed data. Additional parameters Some reports support additional parameters. They help you display more data or filter results right when building the report. The set of parameters depends on the selected report type. Where to set parameters: Open the Reports tab and select the report type 2. A block with additional settings will appear below. Set the needed parameters and click Build report Types of parameters: Toggles (checkboxes) These enable or hide extra columns and report elements. To display information — check the box. To hide it — clear the box. For example, in the Trips and parkings report, additional parameters are available: columns with drivers, vehicle type, and a chart. If no parameters are selected, the report is built with default settings. Numeric parameters These are used for filtering by values and setting ranges. Enter numbers; the measurement units are shown next to the field (sec, min, temperature, etc.). If the upper threshold is not needed, in some reports you can leave it empty or set it to 0. For example, in the parking report, you can specify Minimum time (sec) and, if needed, Maximum time (sec) that will be counted as parking. Report settings Most reports can be customized: After building the report, click Report settings 2. Check the boxes for the parameters you need — for example, object, geofences, sensors, etc. 3. Click Build report again to refresh the report with your selected parameters Example In the Parkings report, all parameters are shown by default. But you can choose specific settings to get a report only for the parameters you need. This way, you only see the information that matters to you. Downloading a report You can save a finished report in a convenient format — CSV, Excel, or PDF — or send it directly to print for further use. Report scheduler Use the report scheduler to set up automatic delivery of reports to your email. The report scheduler is a tool that automatically sends reports according to a schedule. How the report scheduler works: Choose which reports you want Select the time when you want to receive them Reports will automatically arrive in your email inbox For more details on setting up the scheduler, see this article."},{"s":"report-types","t":"Report types","h":["Report types"],"x":"Report types In OneGPS, all reports are grouped by type depending on their purpose and the data they use. Each report is generated as a table. Tables are the main way data is displayed in reports. Each report type has its own set of columns, calculation logic, and display settings. The system includes basic reports used to analyze standard monitoring data such as trips, mileage, driving time, events, and other common indicators. These reports are available regardless of which modules are connected. The system also provides module-based reports. These are designed to work with specific system modules (for example, Drivers, Fuel, Geofences, etc.) and use additional data and settings that are available only when the corresponding module is active."},{"s":"admin","t":"Admin","h":["Admin"],"x":"Admin This section contains reports related to user management and monitoring user activity in the system. They help you track account changes, system logins, password resets, account lockouts, and other user actions. These reports allow you to: view the history of administrative actions monitor the creation, modification, deactivation, and locking of user accounts track successful and failed login attempts analyze the number and duration of user sessions identify which user performed an action, including their login or role"},{"s":"administrative-actions","t":"Administrative actions","h":["Administrative actions","What the report shows"],"x":"Administrative actions The Administrative Actions report shows the history of user actions in the system for the selected period. It allows you to identify who made changes to the data, when the changes were made, and what was changed. Each row represents one recorded user action. What the report shows Username — the name of the user who performed the action Role — the user’s role in the system, for example, Administrator User activity — the action performed and details of the changes Date — the exact date and time when the action was performed"},{"s":"blocked-users","t":"Blocked users","h":["Blocked users","What the report shows"],"x":"Blocked users The Blocked usersreport provides information about users whose access to the system was blocked during the selected period. It allows you to view the date and method of blocking, as well as the number of allowed and invalid login attempts. What the report shows Role — the blocked user’s role in the system Login — the user’s login name Lock date — the date and time when the user account was blocked Locking method — how the user was blocked, for example, by an administrator Allowed login attempts — the permitted number of login attempts Invalid attempts — the number of failed login attempts"},{"s":"created-users","t":"Created users","h":["Created users","What the report shows"],"x":"Created users The Created Users report provides information about user accounts created in the system. It allows you to check the account creation date, user role, current status, and account expiry date. What the report shows Login — the login name used to access the system Creation Time — the date and time when the user account was created User Role — the user’s role in the system, for example, Admin or User Display Name — the user name displayed in the system Password Last Set — the date and time when the password was last set Account Expiry — the account expiry date. If no expiry date is set, unlimited is displayed Status — the current account status, for example, active or block Created By — the user who created the account"},{"s":"disabled-user-account","t":"Disabled user account","h":["Disabled user account","What the report shows"],"x":"Disabled user account The Disabled user account report provides information about user accounts whose access was disabled during the selected period. After an account is blocked, the user can no longer log in. Access remains restricted until an administrator unblocks the account. What the report shows Login — the login name of the user whose access was disabled Changed date — the exact date and time when the account status was changed Current status — the current account status. The block value means that the user is blocked and cannot log in Changed by — the name of the user or administrator who changed the account status"},{"s":"disabled-users","t":"Disabled users","h":["Disabled users","What the report shows"],"x":"Disabled users The Disabled Users report provides information about user accounts that were disabled during the selected period. It allows you to check when an account was created and disabled, how or by whom it was disabled, and its current status. What the report shows Username — the user’s login name Display Name — the user name specified in the account settings Date User Created — the date and time when the user account was created Date User Disable — the date and time when the user account was disabled Disabled By — information about how or by whom the account was disabled, for example, by an administrator Account Status — the current status of the user account"},{"s":"failed-login-attempts","t":"Failed login attempts","h":["Failed login attempts","What the report shows"],"x":"Failed login attempts The Failed login attempts report provides information about unsuccessful login attempts during the selected period. What the report shows Username — the name or login of the user whose account was used for the login attempt No. of attempts — the number of recorded login attempts Failed Time — the date and time of the failed login attempt Possible Login Attempts — the number of login attempts allowed by the system If the system cannot identify the user or retrieve specific details about a login attempt, the corresponding fields may be empty."},{"s":"login-history","t":"Login history","h":["Login history","What the report shows"],"x":"Login history The Login historyreport provides information about user logins during the selected period. It allows you to identify who logged in, when the login occurred, which IP address was used, and how long the session lasted. What the report shows Name — the user’s display name Login — the login name used to access the system Email — the user’s email address Date — the date and time of the login Role — the user’s role in the system IP — the IP address from which the user logged in Duration — the duration of the recorded user session"},{"s":"login-summary","t":"Login summary","h":["Login summary","What the report shows"],"x":"Login summary The Login summary report provides summarized information about user logins during the selected period. It allows you to view the number of logins for each user, the time of their last login, and the total and average duration of their sessions. What the report shows Name — the user’s display name Email — the user’s email address Last Login — the date and time of the user’s last login during the specified period No Logins — the number of recorded user logins Tot Duration — the total duration of all user sessions Avg Duration — the average duration of one user session"},{"s":"modified-users","t":"Modified users","h":["Modified users","What the report shows"],"x":"Modified users The Modified users report provides information about changes made to user accounts during the selected period. It allows you to identify which account was modified, when the change occurred, which account details were changed, and who made the changes. What the report shows Username — the login name of the user whose account was modified Modified Time — the date and time when the changes were made Modified Attributes — the name of the account attribute that was changed, for example, lang indicates the interface language Modified By — the login name of the user who made the changes User Role — the user’s role after the changes Old Role — the user’s role before the changes"},{"s":"password-reset","t":"Password reset","h":["Password reset","What the report shows"],"x":"Password reset The Password resetreport provides information about user password changes during the selected period. It allows you to identify the account for which the password was reset, when the reset occurred, and who performed the action. What the report shows Username — the login name of the user whose password was reset Password change date — the date and time when the password was reset or a new password was set Changed by — the login name of the user or administrator who reset the password"},{"s":"user-activity-report","t":"User activity report","h":["User activity report","What the report shows"],"x":"User activity report The User activity report provides a history of actions performed by users in the system during the selected period. It allows you to identify which user performed a specific action, their role, when the action was performed, and which IP address was used. What the report shows Username — the display name of the user who performed the action Role — the user’s role in the system User Activity — the action performed and information about the related object Date — the exact date and time when the action was performed IP — the IP address from which the action was performed Location — the user’s location"},{"s":"user-usage-report","t":"User usage report","h":["User usage report","What the report shows"],"x":"User usage report The User usage report provides information about actions performed by users in the system during the selected period. It allows you to identify which user performed an action, which login was used, when the action was performed, and the user’s IP address and location. What the report shows Name — the display name of the user who performed the action Login — the login name used to access the system Description — the action performed and information about the related object Date — the exact date and time when the action was performed IP — the IP address from which the action was performed Location — the user’s location determined from the IP address"},{"s":"agriculture","t":"Agriculture","h":["Agriculture"],"x":"Agriculture This section contains reports related to the Agro module. With these reports, you can: monitor field operations analyze the duration and distance of completed field work calculate the cultivated area based on the implement working width track fuel and working fluid consumption compare operational results across different fields and geozones evaluate the efficiency of agricultural equipment"},{"s":"agriculture-report","t":"Agriculture report","h":["Agriculture report","What the report shows","Trailer selection","Report settings","Report prerequisites"],"x":"Agriculture report The Agriculture report provides information about the work performed by selected monitored objects and attached equipment in fields during the specified period. It allows you to determine the duration and distance of the work, calculate the cultivated area, and analyze fuel or working-fluid consumption. The data is grouped by objects, trailers, and zones. Each zone contains separate work periods, while the Zone total row shows the aggregated values. What the report shows Start — the date and time when work in the zone began Stop — the date and time when work ended Duration — the duration of the corresponding work period Distance — the distance traveled by the object during the work Width — the working width of the trailer or equipment for one pass Cultivated Area — the area calculated based on the distance traveled and the working width Consumed Fuel — the amount of fuel consumed during the corresponding period Consumed Fuel/ha — fuel consumption per hectare of cultivated area Start Location — the address where the work began Stop Location — the address where the work ended The Zone total row shows the totals for all work periods within the corresponding zone: total work duration total distance traveled total cultivated area total fuel or working-fluid consumption Trailer selection Before building the report, you can select the trailer used by the monitored object. After you click Build report, the Report parameters window opens and displays: Trailer — the selected attached equipment Width — the equipment’s working width used to calculate the cultivated area If necessary, you can change the width before generating the report. After confirmation, the system recalculates the cultivated area using the specified value. Report settings By default, the report displays fuel-consumption data. Use the Report settings button to select the consumption type: Fuel Consumption — displays the amount of fuel consumed and consumption per hectare Fluid Consumption — displays the amount of working fluid consumed and consumption per hectare Report prerequisites The Agriculture report requires two modules to be enabled and configured: Geofences — used to create and define the boundaries of cultivated fields Trailers — used to register agricultural equipment, link it to monitored objects, and specify its working width The report is generated when a vehicle is located inside a field geofence and has a trailer attached with the Agriculture option enabled. If the required modules are not enabled or are configured incorrectly, cultivated-area data is not displayed."},{"s":"base","t":"Base","h":["Base"],"x":"Base This section contains the main reports for analyzing the operation of vehicles and other monitoring objects. These reports help you monitor mileage, trips and parking, working time, engine hours, fuel consumption, equipment utilization, fleet status, and the completion of specialized tasks. With these reports, you can: analyze mileage using GPS, CAN bus, and odometer data review trips and parking periods, including their duration, addresses, and coordinates monitor driving time, engine operating time, and ignition-on time compare object activity during working and non-working hours track fuel consumption, refueling, and fuel drains analyze vehicle speed, equipment utilization, and axle load monitor driver performance and the completion of assigned routes view routes, parking locations, and events on maps and charts generate trip sheets and monitor document expiration dates analyze fleet composition and changes over the selected period The available metrics, filters, and additional parameters depend on the selected report, the equipment installed on the Object, and the system configuration."},{"s":"activity-report-1","t":"Activity report","h":["Activity report","What the report shows"],"x":"Activity report The Activity report provides information about the location and status of vehicles at each recorded route point. It allows you to track vehicle movement and view its speed, direction, coordinates, and status at a specific time. Before generating the report, select: a period of no more than one day no more than three vehicles A separate data section is generated for each selected object. Each row represents one point received from the GPS tracker. What the report shows # — the point sequence number from the beginning of the selected period Gps Date/Time — the date and time when the point was recorded by the GPS tracker Driver — the name of the driver assigned to the vehicle Speed — the vehicle speed when the point was recorded Direction — the vehicle’s direction of travel in degrees Distance — the distance traveled between recorded points Travel Time — the travel time for the corresponding interval Latitude — the latitude coordinate of the point Longitude — the longitude coordinate of the point Place — the address or description of the vehicle’s location Status — the vehicle status when the point was recorded, for example, Stopping Geozone — the name of the geofence where the vehicle was located GPRS Reveived Date/Time — the date and time when the point was received from the GPS tracker by the system"},{"s":"brag-report","t":"BRAG report","h":["BRAG report","General Information","Fleet Overview","BRAG Performance Distribution","Fleet Performance Summary","Individual Driver Performance","Preparing data for the report"],"x":"BRAG report The BRAG Reportis designed to assess driving safety and classify drivers using the BRAG categories. The report was developed with consideration for QatarEnergy requirements for in-vehicle monitoring systems (IVMS). The system analyzes recorded violations, including speeding, harsh acceleration, harsh braking, harsh cornering, and seat belt violations. Based on these events, each active driver receives a final score and a color category. Before generating the report, select a period and the required vehicles or vehicle group. The calculation includes drivers for whom data was received during the selected period. General Information The top section of the report displays: Contractor — the contractor’s name Project — the project name Department — the department for which the report was generated Report Period — the start and end of the analyzed period Generated — the date and time when the report was generated Report Type — the report type, for example, Weekly/Monthly Fleet Overview The Fleet Overview section provides general information about the fleet: Total Vehicles — the total number of selected vehicles Active Drivers — the number of drivers with recorded activity during the selected period Total Distance (km) — the total distance traveled by the vehicles Total Duration — the total vehicle operating time The number of active drivers may be lower than the number of vehicles. The BRAG distribution includes only drivers for whom the system received the required data. BRAG Performance Distribution The BRAG Performance Distribution section divides active drivers into four categories. Each category shows the number of drivers and their percentage of all active drivers: BLUE — Outstanding — outstanding driving performance GREEN — Acceptable — acceptable driving performance AMBER — Attention Required — driving performance requires attention RED — Action Required — corrective action is required The same colors are used to highlight driver names in the detailed table: blue — BLUE category green — GREEN category amber — AMBER category red — RED category A higher final score and a greater number of violations indicate that the driver’s performance requires more attention. Fleet Performance Summary TheFleet Performance Summary section provides summarized performance indicators for the selected fleet: Fleet Average Score — the average final score for the fleet’s drivers Total Events — the total number of recorded events and violations Events per 100 km — the average number of events per 100 km traveled The Events per 100 km indicator allows you to compare drivers and departments with different total distances. Individual Driver Performance TheIndividual Driver Performance section provides detailed results for each driver. The table displays: Driver ID — the driver’s identifier Driver Name — the driver’s name Duration HH:MM:SS — the driver’s total operating time Distance (km) — the total distance traveled Overspeed 120 kph — the number of speeding events in the 120 kph category Overspeed 100 kph — the number of speeding events in the 100–119 kph range Overspeed 80 kph — the number of speeding events in the 80–99 kph range Overspeed 60 kph — the number of speeding events in the 60–79 kph range Overspeed 40 kph — the number of speeding events in the 40–59 kph range Overspeed 30 kph — the number of speeding events in the 30–39 kph range Harsh Acceleration — the number of harsh acceleration events Harsh Braking — the number of harsh braking events Harsh Cornering — the number of harsh cornering events Seatbelt Violation — the number of seat belt violations Driver Score — the driver’s final score used to determine their BRAG category Preparing data for the report The report obtains violation data from notifications configured for the vehicles. Before using the report, create the required notifications and assign them to the appropriate vehicles. The algorithm identifies each event type by its notification name. Notification names must exactly match the approved naming convention: BRAG Harsh Braking BRAG Harsh Acceleration BRAG Harsh Cornering BRAG Overspeed BRAG Overspeed 30-39 BRAG Overspeed 40-59 BRAG Overspeed 60-79 BRAG Overspeed 80-99 BRAG Overspeed 100-119 BRAG Overspeed 120 BRAG Seatbelt Violation Assign the notifications to every vehicle whose data must be included in the report. If a notification is missing, assigned to the wrong vehicle, or its name does not exactly match the required convention, the algorithm may not recognize the event. As a result, the violation will not appear in the report, and the final performance indicators may be calculated incorrectly."},{"s":"connection-lost","t":"Connection lost","h":["Connection lost","What the report shows"],"x":"Connection lost The Connection lost report provides information about periods when the system did not receive data from monitoring objects. It allows you to identify which vehicle lost connection, when the connection was lost, how long the interruption lasted, and where the object was located according to the latest available data. Before building the report, select the reporting period and vehicles, and enter a value in the Min time (sec) field. The Min time (sec) field specifies the minimum duration of a connection loss required for the event to be included in the report. The value is entered in seconds. For example: 120— displays connection loss intervals lasting two minutes or longer 300— displays connection loss intervals lasting five minutes or longer Intervals shorter than the specified value are excluded from the report. This setting helps filter out brief connection interruptions and display only significant events. What the report shows Object — the name of the vehicle or other monitoring object Folder — the folder containing the object in the object list Start — the date and time when the system stopped receiving data from the object Stop — the date and time when the interval ended or data transmission resumed Duration — the total duration of the connection loss Address — the object’s address or location based on the latest available data"},{"s":"connection-prices","t":"Connection prices","h":["Connection prices","What the report shows"],"x":"Connection prices The Connection pricesreport provides information about tariff activation for monitoring objects in the account. It allows you to check the connection price for each object, the price activation date, the equipment identifier, and details of the installed SIM devices. What the report shows № — the row sequence number Vehicle name — the vehicle name or identification number specified in the system UniqID — the unique identifier of the installed equipment SIM device — the number of the first SIM device SIM device 2 — the number of the second SIM device, if installed Price activation date — the date when the price was activated for the object Price — the connection price and its currency"},{"s":"consolidated-report","t":"Consolidated report","h":["Consolidated report","What the report shows","Distance","Fillings","Drain Offs","Speed","Parkings"],"x":"Consolidated report The Consolidated report provides key information about the operation of selected vehicles during the specified period. It allows you to review mileage and travel time, fuel fillings and drain offs, speeding events, and parking periods. Before building the report, select the reporting period and vehicles, and use the checkboxes to choose the information to include: Fillings Drain Offs Speed Parkings Distance You can select all parameters or only those you need. A separate data section is generated for each selected vehicle. What the report shows At the beginning of each section, Object displays the name of the vehicle for which the values were calculated. Distance When the Distance checkbox is selected, the report displays: Mileage — the distance traveled by the vehicle during the selected period Travel Time — the total time spent traveling Max Speed — the maximum recorded speed Average Speed — the average driving speed Fillings When the Fillings checkbox is selected, the report displays recorded increases in the fuel level: the date and time of the filling the volume of fuel added in liters Drain Offs When the Drain Offs checkbox is selected, the report displays recorded decreases in the fuel level: the date and time of the drain off the volume of fuel drained in liters Filling and drain-off data is available when a fuel-level sensor is installed and configured correctly. Speed When the Speed checkbox is selected, the report displays speeding events: the date and time when the speeding event started the recorded speed the duration of the event in seconds Parkings When the Parkings checkbox is selected, the report displays: the parking start date and time Duration — the duration of the parking period"},{"s":"daily-vehicle-report","t":"Daily vehicle report","h":["Daily vehicle report","What the report shows"],"x":"Daily vehicle report The Daily vehicle reportprovides summarized vehicle operating data for the selected period. It allows you to review mileage and travel time, identify trips made outside duty hours, analyze speeding violations, monitor vehicle movement outside geozones, and check idling time. Report data can be divided into daily sections. Each row represents one vehicle for the specified time interval. The Totalrow shows the combined values for all vehicles. What the report shows # — the row sequence number Vehicle — the name or identification number of the monitoring object Total driven (km) — the total distance traveled during the reporting period Travel time (hh:mm) — the total time spent traveling Distance driven After Duty Time (km) — the distance traveled outside the configured duty hours Over Speed violations count — the number of recorded speeding violations Distance driven out of Geozone (km) — the distance traveled outside the monitored geozones Time spent Outside Geo Zone — the total time the vehicle remained outside the geozones Petrol Consumed (norma) (L) — the estimated fuel consumption calculated according to the configured fuel consumption rate Total Idling Time (working hours) — the total vehicle idling time during the configured working hours"},{"s":"data-quality","t":"Data quality","h":["Data quality","What is analyzed","What the report shows","Threshold values"],"x":"Data quality The Data qualityreport is designed to assess the quality of telematics data received from monitored objects during the selected period. It helps identify missing or delayed data, gaps between packets, GPS problems, sudden coordinate jumps, and other anomalies. The report helps determine whether the received data is reliable enough for building tracks, calculating mileage, processing sensor readings, and performing subsequent recalculations. It evaluates not only connection loss but also the quality of the data packets already received. Indicators are calculated separately for each object for every day within the selected period. What is analyzed When assessing data quality, the system checks: delays in packet delivery to the server intervals between consecutive packets sudden coordinate jumps GPS signal quality, including the number of satellites and HDOP value invalid packets and packets without coordinates packets with duplicate timestamps instances where the maximum speed configured for the object was exceeded What the report shows Object — name or number of the monitored object Start — beginning of the analyzed period Stop — end of the analyzed period Score — overall data quality score from 0 to 100. A higher score indicates better data quality Status — overall data quality status: ok— the data meets the configured requirements warning— issues requiring attention were detected bad— significant data quality problems were detected no_data— no data is available for the analyzed period Points — number of telematics packets received during the period Issues — total number of detected anomalies Delay max — maximum delay in packet delivery to the server Gaps — number of significant gaps between consecutive packets Longest gap — duration of the longest gap GPS problems — number of problems caused by an insufficient number of satellites or an HDOP value exceeding the permitted threshold Coord jumps — number of detected sudden coordinate jumps Threshold values The analysis uses configured thresholds for data transmission intervals, delivery delays, gaps, coordinate changes, and other parameters. These values can be configured individually for each object. If specific parameters are not configured, the system uses default values and the object’s existing settings, including the minimum number of satellites, maximum HDOP, maximum speed, and permitted connection-loss duration."},{"s":"detailed-trip-log","t":"Detailed trip log","h":["Detailed trip log","What the report shows"],"x":"Detailed trip log The Detailed trip report shows how a vehicle moved during the selected period. It includes trip times and routes, vehicle locations, driver details, and driving speeds. You can generate the report for one vehicle at a time. The selected period cannot exceed three days. What the report shows Object — the vehicle included in the report Date and time — the date and time of the event Location — the vehicle’s location at that time Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle during the trip Road speed — the vehicle’s speed at the specified route point Max. speed — the maximum speed on the corresponding route segment Status — the event type, such as the start of a trip or the end of parking The report also provides the following summary for each trip: trip number duration average speed maximum speed distance travelled"},{"s":"devices-offline-","t":"Devices offline","h":["Devices offline","What the report shows"],"x":"Devices offline The Devices offline report lists the selected objects that are not connected at the time the report is generated. It allows you to identify which device is not transmitting data, how long the connection has been unavailable, and when the last event was received from the device. The report shows the current status of the objects and can only be generated for the current day. If you select multiple vehicles, the report includes only those that are offline when the report is generated. What the report shows Group — the group to which the monitoring object belongs Object — the name or identification number of the vehicle Message — information about the connection status, such as how long the connection has been unavailable or a message indicating that the device has never established a connection Device type — the type of monitoring object, for example, a car or bus Last event — the date and time of the last event received from the device The Total row shows the total number of objects that are currently offline."},{"s":"equipment-work-on-days-1","t":"Equipment work on days","h":["Equipment work on days","What the report shows"],"x":"Equipment work on days The Equipment work on daysreport is designed to monitor the daily operation of monitoring objects. It allows you to review the duration of movement and parking, working time, ignition-on time, daily distance, and average speed. What the report shows Object — the name or identification number of the monitoring object Duration of moving — the total time the object was moving during the day. The minimum speed at which the object is considered to be moving is specified in the object settings Duration of parking — the total time the object was not moving. Parking detection parameters are configured on the Settings tab in the object properties Duration of work — the sum of the movement time and parking time during working hours Duration of parking in work time — the total duration of parking periods included in working time. The minimum duration used to detect a parking period is specified by the Min. parking time parameter in the object settings Time the ignition — the total time during which the ignition was on Vacation time — the part of the day not included in working time. It is calculated as the difference between 24 hours and the duration of work Distance — the distance traveled by the object during the day Avg. speed — the object’s average speed during the day"},{"s":"events-report-1","t":"Events report","h":["Events report","What the report shows"],"x":"Events report The Events reportprovides information about events recorded by the system for the selected monitoring objects during the specified period. It allows you to identify which event occurred, when and where it was recorded, the speed of the object, and the driver assigned to it. What the report shows Object — the name or identification number of the monitoring object Date & Time — the date and time when the event was recorded Event — the event type and additional details Speed — the object’s speed when the event was recorded. The field may be empty if speed does not apply to the event Drivers — the driver assigned to the object when the event occurred Geozones — the geozone where the event was recorded Place — the address or description of the object’s location when the event occurred The report contents depend on the events, sensors, and notifications configured for the selected objects."},{"s":"excessive-idle-events-report","t":"Excessive idle events report","h":["Excessive idle events report","How an idle event is detected","What the report shows"],"x":"Excessive idle events report The Excessive idle events reportprovides information about cases when a monitoring object remained stationary longer than the permitted idle time. It allows you to identify when and where excessive idling occurred, which driver was assigned to the object, and which geozone the object was in. How an idle event is detected The permitted idle time is configured in the object card on the Settings tab, in the Idle, sec. field. An idle event is detected as follows: 1. The vehicle stops 2. The configured idle-time countdown begins 3. If the vehicle does not start moving before this period expires, the system records an event 4. The idle period continues until the vehicle starts moving or the ignition is switched off, if ignition status is taken into account For example, if the configured idle time is 00:10:00, the event is recorded after the vehicle has remained stationary for more than 10 minutes. You can also enable ignition monitoring in the object settings. When this option is enabled, an idle event is recorded only if the vehicle remains stationary while the ignition is on. 1 — Configure the permitted idle time 2 — Enable ignition monitoring What the report shows Object — the name or identification number of the monitoring object Date & Time — the date and time when the idle event was recorded Event — information about the permitted idle time being exceeded, for example, Idle more than 00:10:00 Drivers — the driver assigned to the object when the event occurred Geozones — the geozone where the idle event was recorded Coordinates — the latitude and longitude of the object’s location during the idle event"},{"s":"excessive-idling-report","t":"Excessive idling report","h":["Excessive idling report","How an idle event is detected","What the report shows","Excessive fuel consumption","Filter by drivers"],"x":"Excessive idling report The Excessive idling report is designed to monitor vehicle idling periods. It allows you to determine when and where idling occurred, how long it lasted, which driver was assigned to the vehicle, and how much fuel was consumed during the period. How an idle event is detected An idling period is recorded when a vehicle remains stationary longer than the permitted idle time configured in the object settings. The permitted duration is specified in the Idle field in the object card. If necessary, you can enable ignition monitoring. In this case, idling is recorded only when the vehicle remains stationary while the ignition is on. 1 — Configure the permitted idle time 2 — Enable ignition monitoring What the report shows Vehicle — the name or identification number of the monitoring object Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle or identified by the system, for example, using an iButton Department — the group or folder to which the vehicle belongs Car type — the vehicle type, for example, a car or truck Start — the date and time when the idling period began Stop — the date and time when the idling period ended Duration (hh:mm:ss) — the idling duration, calculated as the difference between the start and stop times Address — the address where the vehicle was located during the idling period Coordinates — the latitude and longitude of the idling location Excessive fuel consumption Before generating the report, you can select the Excessive Fuel checkbox. If a fuel-level or fuel-consumption sensor is configured for the object, an additional column appears in the report: Excessive fuel consumption — the amount of fuel consumed by the vehicle during the idling period Filter by drivers When the On driveroption is enabled, an additionalDrivers panel opens next to the object list. 1 — Enable the driver filter 2 — Select a driver from the list In this panel, you can select one driver, multiple drivers, or an entire driver group. The report will display only events associated with the selected drivers."},{"s":"fleet-size","t":"Fleet size","h":["Fleet size","What the report shows"],"x":"Fleet size The Fleet Sizereport provides information about the number of vehicles in user groups and changes in fleet composition during the reporting period. It allows you to assess the initial fleet size, track new deliveries and vehicles removed from service, and determine the current number of vehicles. What the report shows Entity or User Dept — the name of the user group for which the values were calculated Existing Fleet — the number of vehicles assigned to the group at the beginning of the reporting period De-fleeted — the number of vehicles removed from the group or taken out of service during the reporting period New Deliveries — the number of vehicles added to the group during the reporting period Current Fleet — the number of vehicles assigned to the group at the end of the reporting period The bottom total row shows the total number of vehicles in the existing fleet, the total number of de-fleeted vehicles and new deliveries, and the current size of the entire fleet."},{"s":"garbage-collection-tracking-report","t":"Garbage collection tracking report","h":["Garbage collection tracking report","What the report shows"],"x":"Garbage collection tracking report The Garbage Collection Tracking Report provides information about visits made by waste collection vehicles to collection points during the specified period. It allows you to monitor assigned route completion, check vehicle arrival and departure times, and identify the collection points that were actually visited. The data is grouped by vehicle and date. For each vehicle, the report lists the collection points visited during the corresponding day. What the report shows Vehicle ID — the name, registration number, or fleet number of the vehicle Vehicle Type — the vehicle type Driver Name — the driver assigned to the vehicle Assigned Route — the route assigned to the vehicle Notes — additional notes Collection Point — the name or full address of the waste collection point Location — the actual location of the visited point, displayed as a link together with its geographic coordinates Arrival — the date and time when the vehicle arrived at the collection point Departure — the date and time when the vehicle left the collection point Notes — additional information about the visit to the collection point Links in the Location column allow you to open the corresponding point on the map. Comparing the Collection Point with the actual Location helps verify whether the required point was visited. The report helps you: monitor compliance with waste collection routes verify the sequence in which collection points were visited determine how long the vehicle remained at each collection point identify missed collection points or deviations from the assigned route analyze vehicle and driver activity by day"},{"s":"iman-group-refuels-report","t":"Iman Group refuels report","h":["Iman Group refuels report","What the report shows"],"x":"Iman Group refuels report The Iman Group refuels report provides information about fuel-level changes for the selected vehicles during the specified period. In addition to fuel fillings, the report also displays fuel drain-offs. It allows you to identify when and where a fuel-level change occurred, determine the amount of fuel added or drained, and compare the sensor readings at the beginning and end of the event. What the report shows Object — the name or identification number of the vehicle Date — the date and time when the fuel event was recorded Fuel start — the fuel level at the beginning of the event Refuel — the amount of the fuel-level change. For a filling, this is the amount of fuel added; for a drain-off, it is the amount of fuel drained Fuel end — the fuel level at the end of the event Type — the fuel event type: Filling — the fuel level increased Drain off — the fuel level decreased Geozone — the geofence where the event occurred Address — the address or description of the filling or drain-off location The report is generated using fuel-level sensor readings. To display fuel events correctly, a fuel-level sensor must be installed and properly configured for the corresponding vehicle."},{"s":"journey-detail-","t":"Journey detail","h":["Journey detail","What the report shows"],"x":"Journey detail The Journey detailreport provides detailed information about the movement and status of selected objects during the specified period. It allows you to track a route using individual GPS points and review the object’s speed, direction, coordinates, location, and connected sensor readings. Before generating the report, select a period of no more than three days and no more than three monitoring objects. A separate data section is generated for each selected object. Each row represents one point recorded by the GPS tracker. What the report shows # — the point sequence number Gps Date/Time — the date and time when the point was recorded by the GPS tracker Speed — the object’s speed when the point was recorded Direction — the object’s direction of travel in degrees Distance — the distance traveled between recorded points Travel Time — the travel time for the corresponding interval Latitude — the latitude coordinate of the point Longitude — the longitude coordinate of the point Place — the address or description of the object’s location when the point was recorded Status — the object’s status, for example, Stopping or Moving Geozone — the name of the geozone where the object was located GPRS Reveived Date/Time — the date and time when the data was received from the GPS tracker by the system Temperature — the temperature sensor reading Humidity sensor — the humidity sensor reading Battery — the battery charge level Door — the door status, for example, open or closed The temperature, humidity, battery, and door columns are populated only when the corresponding sensors are installed and configured correctly."},{"s":"mileage-monthly-report","t":"Mileage monthly report","h":["Mileage monthly report","What the report shows"],"x":"Mileage monthly report The Mileage monthly reportprovides information about the distance traveled by the selected vehicles during each month of the reporting period. It allows you to compare monthly mileage and view the total mileage of each vehicle for the entire selected period. Before generating the report, select the vehicles and a period covering one or more months. The system automatically divides the data by calendar month and creates a separate column for each month. What the report shows Vehicle — the name or identification number of the monitoring object Month — the vehicle’s mileage for the corresponding month Total — the vehicle’s total mileage for the entire selected period"},{"s":"mileage-report","t":"Mileage report","h":["Mileage report","What the report shows","Fuel data"],"x":"Mileage report The Mileage reportprovides information about trips made by the selected vehicles during the specified period. It allows you to view the start and end time and location of each trip, its duration, distance traveled, and average and maximum speed. TheTotalrow shows the total duration, total distance, and maximum speed for the corresponding object. What the report shows Object — the name or identification number of the monitoring object Beginning — the date and time when the trip began End — the date and time when the trip ended Start — the address or description of the trip’s starting location Stop — the address or description of the trip’s ending location The locations in the Start and Stop columns are displayed as links that open the corresponding location. Duration — the trip duration, calculated as the difference between the beginning and end times Distance — the distance traveled during the trip Avg speed — the average speed during the trip Max speed — the maximum speed recorded during the trip Fuel data Before generating the report, you can select the Fuelcheckbox. The following columns will then be added for each trip: Fuel at the beginning — the fuel sensor reading at the beginning of the trip Fuel at the end — the fuel sensor reading at the end of the trip Fuel consumed — the amount of fuel consumed during the trip, in liters Fuel consumption — the average fuel consumption during the trip, in liters per 100 km These values are available when a fuel-level or fuel-consumption sensor is installed and configured correctly. If the sensor is unavailable or no data is received from it, the corresponding columns may display zero values."},{"s":"mileage-report-2","t":"Mileage report #2","h":["Mileage report #2","What the report shows"],"x":"Mileage report #2 The Mileage report #2 provides information about trips made by the selected vehicle during the specified period. It allows you to view the start and end time and location of each trip, the assigned driver, odometer readings, duration, distance, and driving speed. What the report shows Beginning — the date, time, and location where the trip began End — the date, time, and location where the trip ended Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle during the trip Odometer at beginning, km — the odometer reading at the beginning of the trip Odometer at end, km — the odometer reading at the end of the trip Next service on, km — the remaining distance until the next scheduled service Duration — the duration of the trip Distance, km — the distance traveled during the trip Av.speed, km/h — the average speed during the trip Max Speed, km/h — the maximum speed recorded during the trip"},{"s":"moto-hours-report","t":"Moto hours report","h":["Moto hours report","What the report shows"],"x":"Moto hours report An moto hour is a unit used to measure the operating time of an engine or other equipment. In the simplest calculation, one engine hour corresponds to one hour of engine operation. Some systems may also take engine speed and load into account. Engine hours help determine the actual operating time of equipment, monitor its use, and schedule maintenance based on operating time rather than mileage alone. The Moto hours report provides information about the operating periods of selected objects during the specified period. Operating periods are determined using the sensor configured for engine-hour calculation. The Total row shows the total operating time and total fuel consumption for the corresponding object. What the report shows Sensor — the sensor used to calculate operating time, for example, an ignition sensor Start — the date and time when the sensor became active and engine-hour calculation began Stop — the date and time when the sensor became inactive and engine-hour calculation ended Duration — the operating time for the corresponding interval Fuel — the amount of fuel consumed during the operating interval For engine hours to be calculated correctly, the appropriate sensor must be configured for the object. Fuel data is displayed when a fuel-level or fuel-consumption sensor is installed and configured correctly."},{"s":"netstar-report","t":"Netstar report","h":["Netstar report","General information","What the report shows","Summary"],"x":"Netstar report The Netstar report is an electronic trip sheet (e-Trip Sheet) that provides detailed information about vehicle operation during the selected period. It shows movement and parking intervals, speed data, mileage, violations, and fuel consumption. Before building the report, select a period and a vehicle. In the Report parameters window, you can specify: Crew Member 1–4 — crew members Controller — the responsible controller The entered information is included in the electronic trip sheet. General information The top section of the report shows: Trip Sheet Number — the electronic trip sheet number generated by the system Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle Crew Members — the crew members specified before building the report Vehicle — the name or number of the monitored object Period — the dates covered by the trip sheet What the report shows Each row represents a separate movement or parking interval. Status — the vehicle status: movement or a numbered parking interval Start — the date and time when the interval started End — the date and time when the interval ended Duration — the duration of movement or parking Distance — the distance travelled during the movement interval Max — the maximum speed during the interval Avg — the average speed during the interval Driver ID — the driver identifier Over Speed — information about a recorded speeding event Harsh Brake — information about a harsh braking event Harsh Accel — information about a harsh acceleration event Over Idle — the amount of time exceeding the permitted idling limit Started at — the start time of the recorded activity or event Ended at — the end time of the recorded activity or event Activity — the type of activity during a parking interval, for example, Rest/Sleep/Eat/Other Work — the working-time category indicated by the NT/OT/DT codes If an event was not recorded, a dash is displayed in the corresponding column. Summary The bottom section of the report provides the following summary: Total Distance — the total distance travelled Move Time — the total movement duration Parking Time — the total parking duration Number of Stops — the number of recorded parking intervals Average Speed — the average driving speed Max Speed — the maximum recorded speed Fuel consumption (by norma) — estimated fuel consumption based on the configured rate Fuel consumption (by sensor) — fuel consumption calculated from sensor readings Average by norma — average standard fuel consumption per 100 km Average by sensor — average fuel consumption per 100 km based on sensor readings"},{"s":"no-movement","t":"No movement","h":["No movement","What the report shows"],"x":"No movement The No movement report is designed to identify vehicles that have remained stationary for a specified period or traveled less than a specified distance during the selected reporting period. It allows you to determine which vehicles have not moved for an extended time, when their data was last updated, and where they are currently located. Before generating the report, you can configure the following parameters: 1 — Vehicle not moving time (hours) — the minimum number of hours for which a vehicle must remain stationary. The report includes objects whose stationary time is equal to or greater than the specified value. 2 — Vehicle has traveled less than — the maximum distance traveled by a vehicle during the selected period. The report includes objects whose mileage is less than the specified value. For example, if you enter 2 in the first field and 5 in the second, the report will show vehicles that have not moved for at least two hours and have traveled less than five kilometers. What the report shows Group — the group or folder to which the vehicle belongs Registration — the vehicle’s registration or fleet number Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle Last Movement Date — the date and time when the vehicle was last moving Last Update time — the date and time when the latest data was received from the object Status — the object’s current status and parking duration Location — the vehicle’s last known location Difference — the interval between the last movement and the latest data update time"},{"s":"parkings-report","t":"Parkings report","h":["Parkings report","What the report shows"],"x":"Parkings report The Parkings reportprovides information about the parking intervals of the selected vehicles during the specified period. It allows you to find out when and where a vehicle was parked, how long the parking interval lasted, and which geozone the vehicle was located in. Before building the report, select a period and the required vehicles. You can also specify which parking intervals should be included: 1 — Min time (sec) — the minimum parking duration. Shorter parking intervals are not included. For example, if the value is set to 120, the report shows parking intervals lasting at least two minutes. 2.— Max time (sec) — the maximum parking duration. Parking intervals longer than the specified value are not included. A value of 0 means that no maximum duration is applied. These settings help exclude parking intervals that are too short or too long and retain only the intervals required for analysis. What the report shows Vehicle number — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Start — the date and time when the parking interval started Stop — the date and time when the parking interval ended Duration — the total parking duration Place — the address or description of the vehicle’s parking location Lat — the latitude of the parking location Lon — the longitude of the parking location Geozone — the geofence in which the parking interval was recorded The Total row shows the total duration of all parking intervals for the corresponding vehicle within the specified period."},{"s":"raw-speed-report","t":"Raw speed report","h":["Raw speed report","What the report shows"],"x":"Raw speed report The Raw speed reportprovides raw speed and coordinate data for the selected object during the specified period. It allows you to review the sequence of recorded measurements, check the vehicle’s speed, and determine its location at each point in time. What the report shows Date — the date when the measurement was recorded Time — the exact time when the measurement was recorded Speed — the vehicle’s speed when the data was received lat — the latitude of the object’s location Lon — the longitude of the object’s location"},{"s":"refrigerator-report","t":"Refrigerator report","h":["Refrigerator report","What the report shows"],"x":"Refrigerator report This report displays information about the sensors installed on the refrigerated unit, as well as its location and speed for the selected period. When generating the report, you can set the Minimum logging interval (in seconds). Min time (sec)minimum logging interval (in seconds) defines how often the system records the refrigerator’s status and adds new entries to the report. For example, if you set the interval to 900 seconds (15 minutes), data such as temperature, door status, ignition, and other parameters will be displayed in the report every 15 minutes within the selected period. To track specific sensors and display their readings in the report, they must be properly configured for the object. For example, if the refrigerated unit is equipped with temperature and humidity sensors, their readings will appear in the report. What the report shows GPS Date & Time — date and time within the selected reporting period Latitude and Longitude — geographic coordinates used to determine the object’s location Location — start location of movement Speed — object speed Ignition sensor — ignition status AC unit status — refrigeration unit status (on/off) Back door, Left door, Right door — door status (rear, left, right)"},{"s":"refrigerator-report-2","t":"Refrigerator report #2","h":["Refrigerator report #2","What the report shows"],"x":"Refrigerator report #2 This report is designed to monitor the operation of the refrigerated unit while the vehicle is moving and parked. It helps track temperature, equipment status, speed, and object location for the selected period. When generating the report, you can configure the Minimum logging interval (in seconds). Min time (sec)this parameter defines how often the system records data and adds new rows to the report. For example, if you set the interval to 900 seconds (15 minutes), information such as temperature, ignition status, and other parameters will be displayed in the report every 15 minutes within the selected period. What the report shows GPS Date & Time — date and time when the data was recorded Latitude / Longitude — object coordinates at the time of recording Location — address or detected location of the object Speed — vehicle speed at the time of recording Ignition sensor — ignition status (on/off) AC unit status — refrigeration unit status (on/off) Back door — rear door status (open/closed) Ground (Mass) — connection status to the vehicle’s electrical ground (power circuit monitoring) RFID — radio-frequency identification data (if tag-based authorization is used)"},{"s":"reports-of-loads-and-unloads","t":"Reports of loads and unloads","h":["Reports of loads and unloads","What the report shows","Summary"],"x":"Reports of loads and unloads The report provides information about loading and unloading operations performed by the selected vehicles during the specified period. It allows you to determine when and where the cargo weight changed, identify the operation type, and compare the weight sensor readings before and after the operation. A load is recorded when the detected weight increases, while an unload is recorded when it decreases. Each row represents one detected operation. What the report shows Vehicle number — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Date — the date and exact time when the operation was recorded Weight at the beginning (kg) — the weight sensor reading before the operation Difference of weights (kg) — the amount by which the weight changed during loading or unloading Weight at the end (kg) — the weight sensor reading after the operation Operation type — the type of recorded change: Load — the vehicle weight increased Unload — the vehicle weight decreased Address — the address or description of the location where the operation was performed The address is displayed as a link that opens the corresponding location. Summary The bottom section of the report shows: Total weight load — the total weight loaded Total weight unload — the total weight unloaded Max weight load — the maximum weight recorded for a single load Min weight load — the minimum weight recorded for a single load"},{"s":"rotation-sensors-report","t":"Rotation sensors report","h":["Rotation sensors report","What the report shows"],"x":"Rotation sensors report The Rotation sensor report provides graphical information about vehicle speed and the operation of rotating mechanisms during the selected period. It allows you to compare vehicle movement with the operating intensity of connected equipment, such as loading and unloading mechanisms. A separate chart is generated for each selected object. The horizontal axis shows the date and time, while the vertical axes show vehicle speed and mechanism rotation speed. What the report shows Speed, km/h — the vehicle’s speed Loading, rpm — the rotation speed of the loading mechanism Unloading, rpm — the rotation speed of the unloading mechanism Each value is shown in a different color. This allows you to identify periods of movement, determine when the mechanisms started and stopped operating, and compare their activity over time. Rotation data is displayed only when the corresponding sensors are installed and configured correctly for the object."},{"s":"runs","t":"Runs","h":["Runs","What the report shows","Fuel data","Viewing a trip on the map"],"x":"Runs The Runs report provides information about trips completed by the selected vehicles during the specified period. It allows you to determine when and where each trip started and ended, its duration, distance travelled, speed, and the coordinates of its start and end points. To view individual trips, click the icon to the left of the object name. The detailed trips will appear below the summary row. To collapse the list, click the icon. What the report shows Object — the vehicle name or number Beginning — the date and time when the trip started Start — the address or description of the trip’s starting location End — the date and time when the trip ended Stop — the address or description of the trip’s ending location Duration — the trip duration Distance (km) — the distance travelled during the trip Av. speed (km/h) — the average driving speed Max Speed (km/h) — the maximum speed recorded during the trip Start coordinates — the latitude and longitude of the trip’s starting point Stop coordinates — the latitude and longitude of the trip’s ending point Fuel data Before building the report, you can select the Fuel checkbox. The following columns will then be added to the table: Fuel at the beginning — the fuel sensor reading at the beginning of the trip Fuel at the end — the fuel sensor reading at the end of the trip Fuel consumption — the amount of fuel consumed during the trip Fuel consumption/100 km — fuel consumption based on sensor readings, calculated per 100 km Fuel norm consumption — estimated fuel consumption based on the configured fuel consumption rate Fuel norm consumption/100 km — the configured standard fuel consumption per 100 km Actual fuel level and consumption values are available when a fuel sensor is installed and configured correctly. Viewing a trip on the map You can expand the Mapsection above the report. To display a particular trip, first expand the list using the + icon, and then click the required trip row. The map shows: the vehicle’s route the trip’s start and end points the direction of travel the vehicle’s position the route sections corresponding to the selected trip"},{"s":"runs-plus","t":"Runs plus","h":["Runs plus","What the report shows","Fuel data"],"x":"Runs plus The Runs plusreport provides information about trips made by the selected vehicles during the specified period. It allows you to view when and where each trip started and ended, its duration, distance traveled, speed, and the coordinates of its start and end points. What the report shows Object / Event — the vehicle name or an associated event Beginning — the trip start date and time Start / Message — the address or description of the trip start location End — the trip end date and time Stop — the address or description of the trip end location Duration — the trip duration Distance (km) — the distance traveled during the trip Av.speed (km/h) — the average driving speed Max Speed (km/h) — the maximum speed recorded during the trip Start coordinates — the latitude and longitude of the trip start location Stop coordinates — the latitude and longitude of the trip end location Agent ID — the identifier of the monitored object The summary row shows the duration, distance, average speed, and maximum speed for the corresponding interval. Fuel data Before building the report, you can select the Fuel checkbox. The following columns will then be added to the table: Fuel at the beginning — the fuel sensor reading at the start of the trip Fuel at the end — the fuel sensor reading at the end of the trip Fuel consumption — the amount of fuel consumed during the trip Fuel consumption/100 km — the actual fuel consumption per 100 km Fuel norm consumption — the calculated fuel consumption based on the rate configured for the vehicle Fuel norm consumption/100 km — the configured fuel consumption rate per 100 km Actual fuel level and consumption values are displayed when a fuel sensor is installed and configured correctly."},{"s":"sec-by-sec","t":"Sec by sec","h":["Sec by sec","What the report shows","Downloading the report"],"x":"Sec by sec The Sec by secreport contains information about telematics points received by the system from the GPS tracker of the selected object. It allows you to check when each point was recorded and received, as well as its coordinates, speed, heading, ignition status, and other transmitted parameters. Before building the report, select: one vehicle a period of no more than one day What the report shows # — sequential number of the point Gps Date — date when the point was recorded by the GPS tracker Gps Time — time when the point was recorded by the GPS tracker Driver — driver assigned to the vehicle when the point was recorded Speed — object speed determined from GPS data Latitude — latitude of the object’s location Longitude — longitude of the object’s location GPRS Received Date — date when the point was received by the system GPRS Received Time — time when the point was received by the system Ignition — ignition status when the point was recorded Heading — direction of travel indicated by a compass point, such as N, SE, or W Acceleration/Deceleration — recorded acceleration or deceleration value Downloading the report A download button is located next to the Build report button. Use it to download the report in XLSX format for viewing and further processing in a spreadsheet application."},{"s":"shifts-dispatch","t":"Shifts dispatch","h":["Shifts dispatch","How a shift is determined","What the report shows","Preparing data for the report"],"x":"Shifts dispatch The Shifts dispatch report is designed to monitor vehicle operation according to a shift schedule in the Time control module. It allows you to compare actual dispatch and return times with the configured shift boundaries, identify schedule deviations, and review movement and parking intervals. One shift per vehicle is recorded for each day. Day boundaries are determined by the time zone configured for the vehicle. How a shift is determined A shift begins when the vehicle leaves the designated working geofence A shift ends when the vehicle returns to the working geofence The permitted shift start and end times are configured in the vehicle’s working schedule If the vehicle leaves after the configured start time, the system may register a late dispatch If the vehicle returns after the configured end time, the system may register a late return The report lists the vehicle’s movement and parking intervals throughout the shift in chronological order What the report shows Vehicle — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Dispatch/Return — the event marking the beginning or end of the shift: leaving or returning to the working geofence Shift Mismatch — a detected deviation from the configured schedule, such as a late dispatch or late return Status — the vehicle’s status during the corresponding interval, such as Movement or Parking Date — the date to which the shift relates Start Time — the actual start time of the displayed movement or parking interval End Time — the actual end time of the interval Shift Start — the configured shift start time Shift End — the configured shift end time Duration — the duration of the corresponding interval Distance — the distance travelled during the interval. Parking intervals normally show a zero value Start Place — the address or location description at the beginning of the interval Stop Place — the address or location description at the end of the interval Preparing data for the report To ensure that shift start and end times are determined correctly, configure the working geofence and the vehicle’s working schedule in advance: 1. In the geofence settings, assign the required vehicle and select Workingfor it. Leaving this geofence will be treated as the beginning of the shift, and returning to it as the end of the shift. 2. In the vehicle card, open the Working time tab, select the working days, and configure the schedules for weekdays and weekends. 3. Check the vehicle’s time zone. It determines the day boundaries and is used to compare actual events with the configured schedule 4. Check the parking-time setting in the vehicle card. This value determines the minimum stationary period that the system recognises as a parking interval If the working geofence, working schedule, time zone, or parking parameters are not configured, some shift details and schedule deviations may be missing or determined incorrectly."},{"s":"shifts-summary","t":"Shifts summary","h":["Shifts summary","What the report shows"],"x":"Shifts summary The Shifts summaryreport provides summarized information about vehicle activity in relation to a base working geofence, such as Office. It shows when a vehicle started operating and first arrived at the base geofence, how long it remained there, the number of departures, daily mileage, and odometer readings. The data is generated for each vehicle by day within the selected period. To generate the report correctly, assign one base geofence to the vehicle and enable the Workingoption for it. The system will use this geofence to register entries and exits. What the report shows Vehicle — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Start Date — the date for which the values were calculated Start Time — the time when the vehicle started operating Time of arrival in the base geofence “Office” — the time of the vehicle’s first arrival in the working geofence Duration of downtime in the base geofence during the day — the total time the vehicle remained stationary within the working geofence Number of trips outside the base geofence during the day — the number of registered departures from the working geofence Time of last entry into the base geofence during the day — the time of the last registered entry Time of last departure from the base geofence during the day — the time of the last registered departure Odometer reading at the beginning of the day — the odometer value at the beginning of the reporting period Odometer reading at the end of the day — the odometer value at the end of the reporting period Mileage — the distance traveled by the vehicle during the day Downtime duration is determined according to the parking settings configured for the corresponding vehicle. An example image of the report will be added to the user guide in a future version."},{"s":"shifts-summary-2","t":"Shifts summary 2","h":["Shifts summary 2","What the report shows"],"x":"Shifts summary 2 This summary report helps you analyze vehicle activity by shifts. It shows how mileage and operating time are distributed between the first shift, the second shift, and time outside shifts. The report uses the object’s Work time settings. For example, the object has two shifts configured: 1st shift — from 06:00 to 17:00 2nd shift — from 18:00 to 05:00 Friday — a non-working day The system uses this schedule to split the data into time intervals: driving from 06:00 to 17:00 is included in the 1st shift driving from 18:00 to 05:00 is included in the 2nd shift driving between shifts and on non-working days is included outside shifts What the report shows Vehicle — the object Start — the start of the report interval End — the end of the report interval 1st Shift (km) — mileage in the first shift 2nd Shift (km) — mileage in the second shift After Duty (km) — mileage outside shifts Total (km) — total mileage 1st Shift Hours — driving time in the first shift 2nd Shift Hours — driving time in the second shift After Duty Hours — driving time outside shifts Ignition On (hh:mm) — total ignition on time Running (hh:mm) — driving time Idling (hh:mm) — idle time Sensor (hh:mm) — operating time of the selected sensor Trips — number of trips Avg Speed — average trip speed Avg Km / Trip — average mileage per trip Avg Time (hh:mm) / Trip — average trip duration Fuel Consumption — fuel consumption"},{"s":"sky-favorit-report","t":"Sky Favorit report","h":["Sky Favorit report","What the report shows","Summary indicators"],"x":"Sky Favorit report The Sky Favorit reportprovides information about vehicle movements and parkings during the selected period. It helps you determine when and where a vehicle was moving or parked, review trip duration and distance, and monitor the operation of the door, air conditioner, and heater. What the report shows Status — the vehicle status: movement or a numbered parking Beginning — the date and time when the movement or parking began End — the date and time when the movement or parking ended Dur — the duration of the corresponding interval Distance (km) — the distance traveled during a movement interval Start — the address where the movement or parking began Stop — the address where the movement or parking ended Coordinates — the latitude and longitude of the interval’s start and end points Door Open (Count) — the number of registered door openings Air Condition (Dur) — the operating duration of the air conditioner Heater (Dur) — the operating duration of the heater Door, air conditioner, and heater data is displayed when the corresponding sensors are available and configured correctly. Summary indicators The following summary indicators are displayed at the bottom of each object section: Total mileage — the total distance traveled by the vehicle Movement time — the total duration of movement Parking time — the total duration of parkings Average speed — the average driving speed Max speed — the maximum recorded speed Fuel consumption (by norma) — the calculated fuel consumption based on the configured rate Fuel consumption (by sensor) — fuel consumption based on fuel sensor readings Average L/100 km (by norma) — the configured fuel consumption rate per 100 km Average L/100 km (by sensor) — the actual fuel consumption per 100 km based on sensor readings Actual fuel consumption data is available when a fuel sensor is installed and configured correctly."},{"s":"smart-waste-management","t":"Smart waste management","h":["Smart waste management","Collections Chart","Bins Statistics Map"],"x":"Smart waste management The Smart Waste Management report provides information about waste container servicing during the selected period. It helps you assess waste collection activity, identify areas where containers regularly become full, and adjust collection schedules and routes. The report consists of two sections. Collections Chart The chart shows the number of registered collections for each selected object. Object names or numbers are displayed on the left, while the values on the bars indicate the number of collections during the reporting period. The chart helps you: assess collection activity for each object compare the number of collections identify objects with high or low workloads check compliance with the waste collection schedule Bins Statistics Map The map shows the locations where the system registered full waste containers. The number on each marker indicates how many such events occurred at that location. Select a marker to open the Bins Statistics window, which contains: Coordinates — the latitude and longitude of the location Address — the address or description of the location Total full alerts — the total number of bin-full alerts Bin full alerts list — a list of objects for which alerts were registered and the number of events for each object The map helps identify areas where containers regularly become full or overflow, determine where more frequent collection may be required, and plan vehicle routes based on actual demand. The report is generated from registered collection events and bin-full alerts. To ensure that the data is displayed correctly, the relevant sensors and alerts must be configured for the selected objects."},{"s":"special-sensors-report","t":"Special sensors report","h":["Special sensors report","What the graph shows"],"x":"Special sensors report The Special sensors report contains charts showing readings from the sensors of the selected objects for the specified period. It allows you to track changes in parameters over time, identify sudden deviations and periods with missing data, and compare readings from multiple sensors. A separate set of charts is generated for each selected object. The report contents depend on the equipment and sensors installed on the object. What the graph shows the date and time when the data was received on the horizontal axis sensor readings in the corresponding units of measurement on the vertical axis different sensors or parameters as lines of different colors the exact time and recorded value when you hover over a line The sensor name displayed on a chart matches the name assigned to that sensor in the object settings. Sharp rises and drops in a line indicate changes in the corresponding parameter. Horizontal sections indicate that the value remained relatively stable. Special sensors must be added to the object and configured correctly before their readings can be displayed. If a sensor is missing, configured incorrectly, or did not transmit data during the selected period, its chart may not appear in the report."},{"s":"speed-report","t":"Speed report","h":["Speed report","What the graph shows"],"x":"Speed report The Speed report contains graphs showing changes in the speed of selected vehicles over the specified period. It allows you to track speed trends, identify periods of movement and stopping, and detect sudden changes or high-speed values. A separate graph is generated for each selected object and time interval. What the graph shows Horizontal axis — the date and time when the data was recorded Vertical axis — the vehicle speed in km/h Blue line — changes in the object’s speed over time Zero value — the object was not moving at that moment Gaps in the graph may indicate that no data was received during the corresponding period Hover over the line to display a tooltip with the date, time, and speed of the object at the selected point on the graph."},{"s":"summary-equipment-report-1","t":"Summary equipment report","h":["Summary equipment report","What the report shows"],"x":"Summary equipment report The Summary Equipment Report provides daily performance data for vehicles and their installed equipment over the selected period. It helps you assess operating time, idling, operation under load, and downtime, as well as review the number of recorded actions, fuel consumption, and distance travelled. What the report shows Object — the name of the monitored object Vehicle number — the registration number, fleet number, or another vehicle number specified in the system Date — the date for which the values were calculated Work time (Ignition sensor) — the operating time determined from the ignition sensor status Idling — the length of time the equipment operated without a load Under load — the length of time the equipment operated under load Downtime — the length of time the vehicle or equipment was not operating Actions number — the number of recorded operations or equipment activations Fuel consumption — the amount of fuel consumed during the corresponding day Distance — the distance travelled by the vehicle during the day Operating times are displayed in hh:mm:ss format. The availability and accuracy of the data depend on the correct configuration of the ignition, load, equipment operation, and fuel consumption sensors. If the required data was not received, some fields may be blank or display zero or invalid values."},{"s":"summary-mileage-report","t":"Summary mileage report","h":["Summary mileage report","What the report shows"],"x":"Summary mileage report The Summary mileage report provides information about the distance traveled by the selected vehicles during the specified period. It allows you to compare the daily mileage of vehicles based on GPS and CAN data and view the total mileage for the entire reporting period. The data is grouped by date: each row represents one day, and a separate column is created for each selected vehicle. The selected objects and reporting period are displayed at the top of the report. What the report shows # — row number Date — the date for which the mileage was calculated Overall — the total mileage of all selected vehicles for the corresponding date Vehicle — the name, registration number, or fleet number of the monitored object GPS — mileage calculated using GPS coordinates CAN — mileage calculated using CAN bus data The bottom total row shows the total GPS and CAN mileage for the entire selected period, both for all objects combined and for each vehicle separately. The availability of CAN data depends on the vehicle’s equipment and configuration. If no CAN bus data is received, the corresponding value may be blank or shown as zero."},{"s":"summary-mileage-report-2","t":"Summary mileage report #2","h":["Summary mileage report #2","What the report shows"],"x":"Summary mileage report #2 Summary mileage report #2 provides information about the distance traveled by the selected vehicles on a specified date. It allows you to compare the mileage of monitored objects based on GPS and CAN data and view the total mileage of all selected vehicles. What the report shows Object — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Date — the date for which the mileage was calculated GPS — mileage calculated using GPS coordinates CAN — mileage calculated using CAN bus data The Totalrow at the bottom displays the total mileage of all selected vehicles for the specified date, shown separately for GPS and CAN data. The availability of CAN data depends on the equipment installed and the object settings. If no CAN bus data is received, the corresponding value may be unavailable or equal to zero."},{"s":"summary-machine-hours","t":"Summary machine hours","h":["Summary machine hours","What the report shows"],"x":"Summary machine hours The Summary machine hours report provides information about the engine operating time and distance traveled by the selected vehicles during the specified period. Machine hours measure the amount of time an engine has been running. The report allows you to monitor vehicle operation and schedule maintenance based on engine operating time, rather than solely on the distance traveled. What the report shows Object — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Period — the start and end date and time of the period for which the data was calculated Machine Hours — the engine operating time during the specified period Distance — the distance traveled by the vehicle during the specified period The bottom Total row shows the combined engine operating time and total distance traveled by all selected vehicles during the specified period."},{"s":"trip-and-parkings-with-maps-","t":"Trip and parkings (with maps)","h":["Trip and parkings (with maps)","What the report shows","Report parameters","Show odometer","Show maps","Show parkings"],"x":"Trip and parkings (with maps) The Trip and parkings (with maps) report provides information about the trips and parking events of the selected vehicle during the specified period. It allows you to analyze the start and end times and locations, working and stop durations, distance traveled, and speed indicators. The report includes summary data for the selected period and detailed information about each trip. When maps are enabled, the vehicle’s route is displayed graphically. What the report shows Object — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Work begin time — the date and time when the vehicle started working Work begin location — the address where the vehicle started working Work end time — the date and time when the vehicle stopped working Work end location — the address where the vehicle stopped working Idling duration — the duration of the recorded idling event Working duration — the total vehicle working time Stop duration — the total duration of stops during the corresponding period Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle during the trip Mileage — the distance traveled by the vehicle Speed maximum — the maximum speed recorded during the trip Speed average — the vehicle’s average speed during the trip Report parameters Before generating the report, you can select the map type and configure additional display options: Show odometer — adds the odometer readings at the beginning and end of the trip to the table Show maps — adds a map showing the vehicle’s route to the report Show parkings — displays parking locations on the map; this option becomes available after maps are enabled Show odometer When the Show odometer option is enabled, additional columns appear in the summary and detailed sections of the report: 1 — odometer begin — the odometer reading at the beginning of the working period or trip 2 — odometer end — the odometer reading at the end of the working period or trip Show maps When the Show mapsoption is enabled, a map showing the vehicle’s route during the selected period appears below the report table. Before generating the report, you can use the Map Type field to select the map layer used to display the route. Show parkings When the Show maps and Show parkings options are enabled, parking markers are displayed on the map together with the route. Selecting a parking marker opens a tooltip containing the following information: Start — the parking start date and time End — the parking end date and time Duration — the total parking duration"},{"s":"trip-report-1","t":"Trip report","h":["Trip report","What the report shows"],"x":"Trip report The Trip report provides information about the trips made by the selected vehicle during the specified period. It allows you to determine the start and end time and location of each trip, check the odometer readings, and view the distance traveled. What the report shows Type — the data source used to calculate the trip data, such as CAN Object — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle during the trip Start time — the trip start date and time Start Odo — the odometer reading at the beginning of the trip Start Address — the address recorded at the beginning of the trip End Time — the trip end date and time End Odo — the odometer reading at the end of the trip End Address — the address recorded at the end of the trip Total Odo — the distance traveled during the trip, calculated as the difference between the end and start odometer readings The bottom Totalrow shows the total distance traveled by the vehicle during the selected period"},{"s":"trips-and-parkings","t":"Trips and parkings","h":["Trips and parkings","What the report shows","Summary indicators","How parking time is calculated","Filtering tracks and parkings","Report parameters"],"x":"Trips and parkings The Trips and parkings report provides information about the movement and parking periods of the selected monitored objects. It allows you to determine when and where a vehicle started and stopped moving, how long it was moving or parked, the distance traveled, and its speed. Events are displayed in chronological order. Each row represents a separate movement or parking period. The monitored object and reporting period are shown at the top of the report. What the report shows Status — the object status: Movement or Parking; each parking event is also assigned a sequence number Beginning — the date and time when the movement or parking period began End — the date and time when the movement or parking period ended Dur — the duration of the corresponding movement or parking period Distance, km — the distance traveled by the object during the movement period; no value is displayed for parking events Start — the address where the movement began or the parking location Stop — the address where the movement ended; this value may not be displayed for parking events Coordinates — the geographical coordinates of the beginning and end of the movement or the parking location The addresses in the Start and Stopcolumns are displayed as links and allow you to open the corresponding location. Summary indicators The bottom section of the report displays summary indicators for the current data block, such as one object for one day: Total odometer — the total distance traveled by the object within the current report block Track Time — the total duration of the recorded tracks, including movement time and parking time within the tracks Move Time — the total time during which the object was moving Track Parking Time — the total duration of parking events recorded within the tracks; together with Move Time, it forms Track Time Parking Time — the total time the object was parked, including parking periods before the first trip, between trips, and after the last trip within the reporting interval Average Speed — the object’s average speed while moving Max Speed — the highest speed recorded while the object was moving Below the separator, the report displays the same indicators calculated across all data blocks for the entire selected period: Total odometer for period — the total distance traveled by the object during the selected period Tracks Time for period — the total duration of all recorded tracks during the selected period, including movement time and parking time within the tracks Move Time for period — the total time the object was moving during the selected period Track Parking Time for period — the total duration of parking events recorded within the tracks during the selected period Parking Time for period — the total time the object was parked within the boundaries of the selected period Average Speed for period — the object’s average speed during the selected period Max Speed for period — the highest speed recorded during the selected period How parking time is calculated The report covers the entire selected time interval, including periods before the first movement and after the last movement: If the vehicle was not moving at the beginning of the reporting period, the system adds a parking event from the beginning of the period until the first recorded movement If the object remained stationary after its last movement, the parking event is displayed until the end of the reporting period If the selected reporting period ends in the future, the last parking event is displayed up to the current time This ensures that the report includes both trips and the intervals between them, providing a continuous sequence of events throughout the analyzed period. Filtering tracks and parkings The Parkings and Tracksoptions are available at the top of the generated report. They allow you to change which rows are displayed without selecting the reporting period again: Parkings and Tracks selected — all events are displayed Tracks selected only — only movement periods are displayed Parkings selected only — only parking periods are displayed Select or clear the corresponding checkbox to show or hide an event type. Report parameters Before building the report, you can configure the following additional parameters: Drivers — adds the Drivercolumn containing information about the driver assigned to the object during the event Vehicle type — adds the Vehicle type column containing the vehicle category Chart — adds a graphical representation of movement and parking periods to the report The chart appears above the report table and displays the sequence of events on a timeline: Parking — periods when the object was stationary Moving — periods when the object was moving The chart allows you to visually compare the duration of movement and parking periods and identify transitions between the two states. You can zoom in on a selected section of the timeline for a more detailed view. Click Reset zoomto restore the original chart scale."},{"s":"trips-and-parkings-charts-and-maps-","t":"Trips and parkings (charts and maps)","h":["Trips and parkings (charts and maps)","Report Summary","Trips details","Parkings details","Chart","Map"],"x":"Trips and parkings (charts and maps) The Trips and Parkings (charts & maps) report provides summary and detailed information about the trips and parkings of the selected monitored object during the specified period. It allows you to analyze the number and duration of trips, start and end locations, parking events, mileage, and speed indicators. A chart showing changes in speed and mileage and a map showing the route, parking locations, and recorded events are displayed below the report tables. Report Summary The Report Summary section displays the object’s overall indicators for the selected period: Object — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Start time — the date and time of the first recorded trip Start location — the address where the first trip began End time — the date and time when the last recorded trip ended End location — the address where the last trip ended Trips — the total number of trips Moving duration — the total time the object was moving Parkings — the number of recorded parking events Parking duration — the total duration of parking events recorded between trips Mileage — the total distance traveled by the object Idling duration — the total time during which the engine was running while the vehicle was not moving Speed maximum — the highest speed recorded during the selected period Speed average — the object’s average speed during the selected period The Totalrow shows the combined number of trips and parkings, moving and parking durations, mileage, and idling duration. Trips details The Trips detailssection contains a separate row for each trip: Start time — the trip start date and time Start location — the address where the trip began End time — the trip end date and time End location — the address where the trip ended Duration — the total trip duration Stop duration — the duration of stops associated with the trip Engine hours — the engine operating time during the trip Mileage — the distance traveled during the trip Speed maximum — the highest speed recorded during the trip Speed average — the average speed during the trip The start and end addresses are displayed as links and allow you to open the corresponding locations. Parkings details The Parkings detailssection contains information about recorded parking events: Start time — the parking start date and time End time — the parking end date and time Duration — the total parking duration Location — the address where the object was parked Chart The Chartbelow the tables shows changes in speed and mileage over time: Horizontal axis — time within the selected reporting period Left vertical axis — vehicle speed in km/h Right vertical axis — accumulated mileage in kilometers Speed — changes in the object’s speed over time Mileage — changes in accumulated mileage Sections where the speed is zero represent periods when the object was not moving. An increase in the Mileage line indicates that the distance traveled was increasing. Hover over a point on the chart to display a tooltip containing the corresponding date, time, and recorded speed. Map The map below the Chartdisplays the vehicle’s route during the selected period. The route line shows the recorded path, while the arrows indicate the direction of travel. The map may display: the beginning and end of the route parking locations recorded events speed-related event points route information points You can pan and zoom the map. The current pointer coordinates are displayed at the bottom of the map. Use the layer selector to change the map layer. Select a speed-related event marker on the map to open a card containing the following information: Speed — the vehicle speed recorded at the selected point Date — the event date and time Message — the event description, such as exceeding the configured speed threshold Coordinates — the geographical coordinates of the event Address — the address corresponding to the event coordinates Select a parking marker to open a card containing the following information: Date — the parking start date and time Duration — the parking duration Coordinates — the geographical coordinates of the parking location Address — the address where the vehicle was parked"},{"s":"trips-and-parkings-for-aramco","t":"Trips and parkings for Aramco","h":["Trips and parkings for Aramco","What the report shows","Filtering tracks and parkings","Summary indicators","Chart option"],"x":"Trips and parkings for Aramco The Trips and parkings for Aramco report provides information about vehicle movement and parking periods during the selected reporting period. It allows you to reconstruct the sequence of trips and parkings, determine their times and durations, view start and end addresses, calculate the distance traveled, and review summary operating indicators. What the report shows Status — the object status: Movement or Parking, together with the event sequence number Beginning — the date and time when the movement or parking period began End — the date and time when the movement or parking period ended Dur — the duration of the corresponding movement or parking period Distance (km) — the distance traveled during the movement period; no value is displayed for a parking event Start — the trip start address or parking location Stop — the trip end address; this value may not be displayed for a parking event Coordinates — the coordinates of the beginning and end of the trip or the parking location For a Movement event, the Coordinates column displays two coordinate pairs: the first corresponds to the beginning of the movement and the second to its end. For a Parking event, the coordinates of the parking location are displayed. The addresses in theStart and Stop columns are displayed as links and allow you to open the corresponding locations. Filtering tracks and parkings The Parkingsand Tracksoptions are available at the top of the generated report. They allow you to change which events are displayed: Parkings and Tracks selected — the complete sequence of events is displayed Tracks selected only — only movement periods are displayed Parkings selected only — only parking periods are displayed Select or clear the corresponding checkbox to show or hide an event type. Summary indicators Summary indicators are displayed below the table, first for the current object or report block and then for the entire selected period: Total trips — the total number of trips Total movements — the number of recorded movement periods Total parkings — the number of recorded parking periods Total odometer — the total distance traveled by the vehicle Move time — the total duration of all movement periods Parking time — the total time the object was parked Average speed — the vehicle’s average speed during the selected period Max speed — the highest speed recorded during the selected period Fuel consumption (by norma) — the calculated fuel consumption based on the configured consumption rate Fuel consumption (by sensor) — the fuel consumption calculated from sensor readings Average l/100km (by norma) — the calculated average fuel consumption per 100 km based on the configured rate Average l/100km (by sensor) — the average fuel consumption per 100 km calculated from sensor readings A separate summary section displays the same indicators calculated across all data included in the report: Total trips for period Total movements for period Total parkings for period Total odometer for period Move time for period Parking time for period Average speed for period Max speed for period Total fuel consumption (by norma) for period Total fuel consumption (by sensor) for period Chart option Before building the report, you can enable the Chart option. When enabled, a timeline showing the sequence of movement and parking periods appears above the report table. The Chart displays: Horizontal axis — the date and time within the reporting period Parking — periods when the vehicle was not moving Moving — periods when the vehicle was moving The Charthelps you determine when the object was moving or parked, compare event durations, and identify extended periods without movement."},{"s":"trips-and-parkings-mc","t":"Trips and parkings MC","h":["Trips and parkings MC","What the report shows","Summary indicators","Filtering tracks and parkings","Report parameters","Show debug","Auto reset [Beta]","Reset milage","Update milage","Show max/avg speed","Drivers","Vehicle type","Fuel","Chart"],"x":"Trips and parkings MC The Trips and Parkings MCreport provides information about the movement and parking periods of the selected monitored object. It allows you to reconstruct the sequence of events, determine trip and parking times and durations, view start and end addresses, and calculate mileage and other operating indicators. What the report shows Status — the object status: Movement or Parking Beginning — the date and time when the movement or parking period began End — the date and time when the movement or parking period ended Dur — the duration of the corresponding event Distance, km — the distance traveled by the object during the movement period; no value is displayed for parking events Start — the trip start address or parking location Stop — the trip end address; this value may not be displayed for parking events Summary indicators Summary indicators are displayed below the table, first for the current report block and then for the entire selected period: Total odometer — the total distance traveled by the object Track Time — the total duration of the generated tracks Move Time — the total time during which the object was moving Track Parking Time — the duration of parking events included within the tracks Parking Time — the total duration of all parking events within the reporting period Average Speed — the object’s average movement speed Max Speed — the highest recorded speed The section marked “for period” displays the same indicators calculated using all data included in the report. Filtering tracks and parkings The Parkings and Tracks options are available at the top of the generated report: Parkings and Tracks selected — all events are displayed Tracks selected only — only movement periods are displayed Parkings selected only — only parking periods are displayed These checkboxes allow you to change which rows are displayed without rebuilding the report. Report parameters The following additional parameters are available before building the report: Show debug Auto reset [Beta] Show max/avg speed Reset milage Update milage Drivers Vehicle type Fuel Chart Show debug The Show debugoption is intended for diagnosing trip generation and mileage calculation. When it is enabled, the Debug menuappears at the top of the generated report. The menu contains the following options: Type Odometer Show TS Show Lat/Lon These options allow you to view the internal data used to generate trips and parkings. When Type is enabled in the Debug menu, theType column is added to the table. It displays the internal event type or identifier used by the system to generate the corresponding row. Examples include: renderStart — an event generated at the beginning of the reporting period render_LP_tracks — a parking event generated between movement segments one_[…], m_start_[…], and m_last_[…] — internal identifiers or methods used to generate movement segments These values are primarily intended for diagnostics. They help determine which event or processing stage was used to generate a trip or parking period. When Odometer is enabled in the Debug menu, additional columns containing detailed odometer data are added: Odo type — the odometer data source or type, such as CAN Start — the odometer reading at the beginning of the trip Stop — the odometer reading at the end of the trip These columns allow you to verify which readings were used to calculate the value in the Distance, km column. Odometer readings may be unavailable for parking events. When Show TS is enabled, the Beginning and Endcolumns display numerical timestamps instead of formatted dates and times. The system uses timestamps to store the exact time of an event. This mode is intended for technical data verification. When Show TS is disabled, values are displayed in the standard date and time format. When Show Lat/Lon is enabled, the Start and Stop columns display geographical coordinates instead of addresses: lat — latitude lon — longitude For a movement period, the coordinates of the beginning and end of the trip are displayed. For a parking period, the coordinates of the object’s parking location are displayed. Auto reset [Beta] The Auto reset [Beta] option is used when calculating mileage from odometer readings. The system attempts to detect odometer resets or breaks in the sequence of readings, such as when a new odometer value is lower than the previous one, and account for them when calculating the distance. This option does not add a separate column. It affects how odometer readings and mileage are processed. Because the feature is marked Beta, the resulting values should be verified using Show debug → Odometer. Reset milage The Reset milage option enables the processing of events associated with odometer resets. It is used to prevent an incorrect difference between readings recorded before and after a detected reset. The option affects the Distance, km values and total mileage but does not add any columns. Use Show debug → Odometer to compare the starting and ending odometer readings. Update milage The Update milage option includes updated odometer readings when generating the report. It affects the odometer values used by the system and the calculated distance. The results can be seen in: the starting and ending odometer readings the distance calculated for individual trips the total mileage The exact effect of Auto reset [Beta], Reset milage, and Update milage depends on the sequence of incoming odometer readings and the mileage source settings. Show max/avg speed When Show max/avg speed is enabled, the following columns are added to the right side of the table: Max speed — the highest speed recorded during the trip Avg speed — the object’s average speed during the trip Drivers When Drivers is enabled, the Driver column is added to the table. It displays the driver assigned to the object during the corresponding trip or parking period. If no driver was assigned or the data was unavailable, the cell may remain blank. Vehicle type When Vehicle type is enabled, the Vehicle type column is added to the table. It displays the vehicle category or type specified in the monitored object settings. Fuel When Fuel is enabled, the following additional indicators are displayed in the summary section: Total consumption, l/100km — the calculated fuel consumption Balance at the beginning, L — the amount of fuel at the beginning of the reporting period Balance at the end, L — the amount of fuel at the end of the reporting period Total consumption for period — the total calculated fuel consumption for the entire selected period The availability and accuracy of these values depend on the presence and configuration of a fuel level sensor. Chart When Chartis enabled, a timeline showing the sequence of events appears above the table: Parking — periods when the object was not moving Moving — periods when the object was moving Horizontal axis — the date and time within the reporting period The Chartallows you to visually compare trip and parking durations, determine when each movement period began, and identify extended periods without movement."},{"s":"trips-and-parkings-report-working-hours-","t":"Trips and parkings report (working hours)","h":["Trips and parkings report (working hours)","What the report shows","Summary indicators","Working-hours options"],"x":"Trips and parkings report (working hours) The Trips and parkings report (working hours) provides information about the movement and parking periods of the selected vehicles based on their work schedules. It allows you to determine how an object was used during working or non-working hours: when it was moving, where it was parked, the distance traveled, and the time spent in each state. What the report shows Status — the object status: Movement or Parking Object — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle during the corresponding event Weekday — the day of the week on which the movement or parking occurred Beginning — the date and time when the movement or parking period began End — the date and time when the movement or parking period ended Dur — the duration of the corresponding trip or parking period Distance, km — the distance traveled by the object during the trip; no value is displayed for parking periods Start — the movement start address or parking location Stop — the movement end address; this value may not be displayed for parking periods Summary indicators Summary indicators for the current object or report block are displayed below the table: Total odometer — the total distance traveled by the object during the included hours Move Time — the total duration of all movement periods Parking Time — the total duration of all parking periods Average Speed — the object’s average speed while moving Max Speed — the highest speed recorded while the object was moving A separate summary section below displays the indicators for the entire selected period: Total odometer for period Move Time for period Parking Time for period Average Speed for period Max Speed for period Working-hours options Before building the report, you can select which data to include: Only work hours — includes events that occurred during working hours according to the configured work schedule Only non-work hours — includes events that occurred outside the configured working hours Working and non-working hours are determined by the object’s work schedule settings. When Only work hours is selected, the report allows you to analyze vehicle use during the hours specified in the work schedule. The report displays: trips made during working hours parking periods between trips the parking period from the beginning of the working interval until the first movement the parking period from the last movement until the end of the working interval When Only non-work hours is selected, the report displays movement and parking periods outside the configured work schedule. It can be used to identify: trips made after working hours vehicle use on weekends or non-working days movement before the beginning of a work shift extended parking periods outside working hours"},{"s":"trips-and-parkings-report-working-hours-2","t":"Trips and parkings report (working hours) #2","h":["Trips and parkings report (working hours) #2","What the report shows","Summary by date","Summary for the entire period","Working-hours options"],"x":"Trips and parkings report (working hours) #2 TheTrips and parkings report (working hours) #2 provides summary information about the movement of selected vehicles based on their work schedules. It allows you to determine when a vehicle was used, who drove it, where it traveled from and to, and review its movement duration, distance, and speed indicators. What the report shows Vehicle — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle during the movement period Day — the day of the week on which the trips occurred Start — the date and time of the first movement within the included interval End — the date and time of the last movement within the included interval Duration — the total movement duration shown in the report row Distance — the total distance traveled by the vehicle From — the movement start address To — the movement end address The addresses in the From and To columns are displayed as links and allow you to open the corresponding locations. Summary by date The following indicators, calculated using all rows for the corresponding date, are displayed below each daily section: Total odometer — the total distance traveled by the vehicle during the day Move Time — the total movement duration during the day Average Speed — the vehicle’s average speed during the day Max Speed — the highest speed recorded during the day Summary for the entire period The following indicators, calculated using all data included in the selected reporting period, are displayed at the bottom of the report: Total odometer for period — the total distance traveled by the vehicle Move Time for period — the total movement duration Average Speed for period — the average speed during the entire period Max Speed for period — the highest speed recorded during the entire period Working-hours options Before building the report, you can select which data to include: Only work hours — includes trips made within the object’s configured work schedule Only non-work hours — includes trips made outside the object’s configured work schedule Working and non-working hours are determined by the vehicle’s work schedule settings. When Only work hours is selected, the report shows vehicle use during the hours specified in the work schedule. This mode allows you to assess: the number and duration of work-related trips mileage during working hours movement start and end locations average and maximum speed the activity of the assigned driver When Only non-work hours is selected, the report shows trips made outside the configured work schedule. This mode allows you to identify: movement before or after a work shift trips made on weekends or non-working days vehicle use outside permitted hours mileage and speed indicators during non-working hours"},{"s":"trips-and-parkings-with-drivers-","t":"Trips and parkings (with drivers)","h":["Trips and parkings (with drivers)","What the report shows","Summary indicators","Filter by drivers"],"x":"Trips and parkings (with drivers) The Trips and parkings (with drivers)report provides information about vehicle movements and parkings, including the assigned drivers. It allows you to determine who operated the vehicle, when and where trips and parkings occurred, the distance traveled, and the calculated operational indicators. What the report shows Status — the object’s status: Movement or Parking; parkings may be assigned a sequence number Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle during the event Beginning — the date and time when the movement or parking began End — the date and time when the movement or parking ended Duration — the duration of the corresponding trip or parking Distance — the distance traveled during the trip; no value is displayed for a parking Start — the trip start address or parking location Stop — the trip end address; no value may be displayed for a parking Coordinates — the geographical coordinates of the trip’s start and end points or the parking location Max speed — the highest speed recorded during the trip Trip type — the trip category determined by the system or the object settings Addresses in the Start and Stopcolumns are displayed as links and allow you to open the corresponding locations. Summary indicators The following indicators are displayed below the table for each object and date: Total trips — the total number of trips Total movements — the number of recorded movement periods Total parkings — the number of recorded parkings Total odometer — the total distance traveled by the vehicle Move time — the total movement duration Parking time — the total parking duration Average speed — the average movement speed for the corresponding period Max speed — the highest recorded speed Fuel consumption (by norma) — estimated fuel consumption calculated using the configured fuel consumption rate Fuel consumption (by sensor) — fuel consumption calculated using sensor data Average l/100km (by norma) — estimated average fuel consumption per 100 km Average l/100km (by sensor) — average fuel consumption per 100 km calculated using sensor data Filter by drivers When the On driveroption is enabled, an additionalDrivers panel opens next to the object list. The panel allows you to select one driver, multiple drivers, or an entire driver group. The report will include only events associated with the selected drivers."},{"s":"trips-and-parkings-with-fuel","t":"Trips and parkings with fuel","h":["Trips and parkings with fuel","What the report shows","Filtering Trips and Parkings"],"x":"Trips and parkings with fuel TheTrips and parkings with fuel report provides information about trips and parkings for the selected vehicles during the specified period. It allows you to determine the start and end time and location of each event, its duration, mileage, and speed, as well as analyze refueling, fuel drains, and fuel consumption. What the report shows Object — the vehicle name, registration number, or fleet number Folder — the folder containing the object in the system Status — the object’s status: Movement or Parking Start — the date and time when the trip or parking began Start Address — the movement start address or parking location Stop — the date and time when the trip or parking ended Stop Address — the movement end address; no value may be displayed for a parking Duration — the duration of the corresponding trip or parking Mileage (km) — the distance traveled during the trip; zero is displayed for a parking Filled (L) — the amount of fuel added during the corresponding event Drained off (L) — the amount of fuel identified by the system as drained Fuel consumption on sensor (L) — the amount of fuel consumed, calculated using fuel-level sensor data Fuel consumed/100km (L) — the actual fuel consumption per 100 km calculated using sensor data Norm, l/100km — the configured standard fuel consumption rate per 100 km Max speed (km/h) — the highest speed recorded during the trip Average Speed (km/h) — the vehicle’s average speed during the trip Filtering Trips and Parkings The Parkingsand Trips options are available at the top of the generated report. They allow you to change which rows are displayed: Parkings and Trips selected — displays the complete sequence of events Trips selected only — displays movement periods only Parkings selected only — displays parking periods only Select or clear the corresponding checkbox to show or hide an event type."},{"s":"trips-and-parking-with-axle-load","t":"Trips and parking with axle load","h":["Trips and parking with axle load","What the report shows","Summary indicators","Filtering Trips and Parkings","Load data"],"x":"Trips and parking with axle load The Trips and parking with axle load report provides information about vehicle movements and parkings, supplemented with axle-load indicators. It is intended primarily for trucks and allows you to compare routes, mileage, and trip duration with the minimum, maximum, and average load values. Load data helps monitor vehicle operation and identify possible overloads. The permitted axle load depends on the vehicle’s type and design, the number of axles, and applicable regulations. What the report shows Status — the object’s status: Movement or Parking Beginning — the date and time when the movement or parking began End — the date and time when the movement or parking ended Dur — the duration of the corresponding trip or parking Distance (km) — the distance traveled during the trip; no value is displayed for a parking Start — the movement start address or parking location Stop — the movement end address; no value may be displayed for a parking Coordinates — the coordinates of the trip’s start and end points or the parking location Average weight — the average load recorded during the corresponding event Weight (min) — the minimum load recorded during the trip or parking Weight (max) — the maximum load recorded during the trip or parking Trip type — the trip category determined by the system or the object settings Addresses in the Start and Stopcolumns are displayed as links and allow you to open the corresponding locations. Summary indicators The following summary indicators are displayed below the table for the selected object and period: Total odometer — the total distance traveled by the vehicle Move Time — the total duration of all movement periods Parking Time — the total duration of all parkings Average Speed — the object’s average movement speed Max Speed — the highest speed recorded during the selected period Fuel consumption (by norma) — estimated fuel consumption calculated using the configured rate Fuel consumption (by sensor) — fuel consumption calculated using sensor data Average l/100km (by norma) — the standard average fuel consumption per 100 km Average l/100km (by sensor) — the actual average fuel consumption per 100 km calculated using sensor data Filtering Trips and Parkings The Parkings and Tracksoptions are available at the top of the report: Parkings and Tracks selected — displays the complete sequence of events Tracks selected only — displays movement periods only Parkings selected only — displays parking periods only Select or clear the corresponding checkbox to show or hide an event type. Load data Compatible equipment that transmits axle-load data must be installed and configured for the object to display the average, minimum, and maximum weight. If no data is received or the relevant parameters are not configured, a dash or no value is displayed in the weight columns."},{"s":"trips-and-stops-with-levels","t":"Trips and stops with levels","h":["Trips and stops with levels","What the report shows"],"x":"Trips and stops with levels The Trips and stops with levels report provides information about the movements and parkings of selected monitoring objects during the specified period. The data is grouped by object and date and displayed as summary rows that can be expanded to view the detailed sequence of events. Each summary row shows the start and end times and locations of the reporting interval, its total duration, and the total distance traveled. To view the movements and parkings included in it, click the button to the left of the object name. What the report shows Object — the name, registration number, or fleet number of the monitoring object Type — the event type: Movement or Parking Beginning — the date and time when the movement or parking began Start — the movement start address or parking location End — the date and time when the movement or parking ended Stop — the movement end address; for a parking, it usually matches its start location Duration (h) — the duration of the corresponding movement or parking Distance (km) — the distance traveled during the movement; zero is displayed for a parking"},{"s":"truck-load-report","t":"Truck load report","h":["Truck load report","Report structure","What the summary table shows","Equipment utilization chart","Object details"],"x":"Truck load report The Truck load reportprovides information about the utilization of the selected monitored objects during the specified period. It allows you to compare equipment idling time and work under load, as well as determine the total engine hours and distance traveled. The report helps evaluate equipment operating efficiency, identify prolonged operation without a productive load, reduce downtime, and allocate resources more effectively. Report structure The report contains several sections: Summary for the period — summary values for all selected objects Use of equipment for the period — a pie chart showing the proportion of parking, idling, and work under load Object details — daily values for each selected object What the summary table shows Object — the name, registration number, or fleet number of the monitored object Description — additional information about the object. If no description is specified, the field remains blank Number of nights — the number of calendar days included in the analyzed period Work at idle — the time during which the engine operated without a registered productive load. The value is shown in two columns: % — the proportion of idling calculated by the system hh:mm — the idling duration in hours and minutes Work under load — the time during which the equipment performed productive work. The value is shown in two columns: % — the proportion of work under load calculated by the system hh:mm — the duration of work under load in hours and minutes Total motohours — the total engine operating time for the corresponding period, shown in hh:mm format Distance, km — the distance traveled by the object during the specified period Equipment utilization chart Below the summary table, the Use of equipment for the period section contains a pie chart showing the proportion of the equipment’s main states: Parking — the time during which the equipment was not operating or moving Idling — the time during which the engine was running without a productive load Work under load — the time during which the equipment performed productive work The chart provides a visual comparison of these states and shows how much of the selected period was spent performing productive work, idling, or parked. Object details Below the chart, separate daily sections are displayed for each object. Each daily section shows: Description — additional information about the object Work at idle — the percentage and duration of idling Work under load — the percentage and duration of work under load Total motohours — the total engine operating time for the corresponding day Distance, km — the distance traveled by the object on the corresponding date This breakdown helps compare an object’s performance across different days and identify periods of high workload, prolonged idling, or inactivity."},{"s":"vehicle-license-expire","t":"Vehicle license expire","h":["Vehicle license expire","Checking period","What the report shows"],"x":"Vehicle license expire The Vehicle license expirereport is designed to monitor the expiration dates of vehicle registration documents. It helps identify vehicles whose registration certificate has already expired or will expire within the selected period. Checking period Before building the report, specify the checking period in months. The system calculates the period from the current date and selects vehicles whose registration certificate expiration dates fall within this range. For example, if a three-month period is selected, the report will include vehicles: whose registration certificates have already expired whose registration certificates will expire within the next three months The longer the selected period, the more upcoming expiration dates will be included in the report. To generate the report correctly, open the “Info” tab in each vehicle card and complete the following fields: Registration number — the series and number of the vehicle registration certificate Expire date — the expiration date of the vehicle registration certificate The system compares the specified expiration date with the checking period and includes the vehicle in the report if the document has already expired or will expire within the selected range. What the report shows Registration — the name, registration number, or fleet number of the monitored vehicle Group — the group containing the vehicle Description — additional information about the vehicle Year — the vehicle’s year of manufacture specified in the vehicle card Registration number — the document number specified in the vehicle card Expire date — the expiration date of the vehicle registration certificate"},{"s":"vehicle-usage-summary","t":"Vehicle usage summary","h":["Vehicle usage summary","What the report shows","Only work hours"],"x":"Vehicle usage summary The Vehicle usage summaryprovides time-based operating metrics for the selected monitored objects over the specified period. It allows you to compare moving and parking durations, ignition activity, maintenance periods, and determine the net working time. What the report shows Vehicle — the name, registration number, or fleet number of the monitored object Date — the start and end date and time of the interval for which the metrics were calculated Information — additional information about the monitored object; if no information is specified, the field remains blank Total Time — the total duration of the corresponding reporting interval Moving duration — the total time the object was moving Parking Time — the total duration of the object’s parking periods Engine On — the total time during which the ignition was registered as on Engine Off — the total time during which the ignition was off Break hours — the portion of the reporting interval classified as non-working time according to the object’s configured work schedule Breakdown hours — the total duration of periods during which the object was undergoing maintenance Nett hours — the time within the configured work schedule, excluding maintenance periods. It is calculated as follows: Total Time − Break hours − Breakdown hours Only work hours Before building the report, you can enable the Only work hours option. When enabled, the report metrics are calculated only within the working intervals defined in the object’s work schedule. This option excludes nighttime, weekends, and other non-working intervals from the analysis."},{"s":"working-hours-2","t":"Working hours #2","h":["Working hours #2","What the report shows","Total values"],"x":"Working hours #2 The Working Hours #2report provides information about the mileage and working time of the selected vehicle for the specified period. It allows you to compare odometer readings with the calculated mileage, determine the number of trips, and break down working time into normal, overtime, public holiday, and Sunday hours. What the report shows Date — the date for which the values were calculated Start Odometer — the odometer reading at the beginning of the first trip on the corresponding date End Odometer — the odometer reading at the end of the last trip on the corresponding date Total KM Driven — the total distance traveled by the vehicle during the day Number of Trips — the number of trips recorded on the corresponding date Normal/Working Hours — the working time within the configured work schedule Overtime Hours — the working time exceeding the configured normal working hours Public Holiday Hours — the working time recorded on public holidays Sunday Time Hours — the working time recorded on Sundays Total Mileage vs Hours — a graphical comparison of mileage and working-hour categories. The colored sections of the bar show the distribution of the values, while the calculated mileage is displayed below it Total values The bottom Total row displays the totals for the entire reporting period: total vehicle mileage total number of trips total normal working hours total overtime hours total working time on public holidays total working time on Sundays total value in the Total Mileage vs Hours column"},{"s":"bus-lines","t":"Bus lines","h":["Bus lines"],"x":"Bus lines This section contains reports related to the Bus lines module. They help you monitor vehicle movements along bus lines, stopping point visits, completed round trips, and schedule compliance. These reports allow you to: check which stopping points a vehicle visited monitor the sequence and time of stopping point visits analyze the number of completed and incomplete round trips check compliance with the established schedule view summary information about vehicle operation on routes"},{"s":"bus-stop-visits-report","t":"Bus stop visits report","h":["Bus stop visits report","What the report shows"],"x":"Bus stop visits report The Bus stop visits report is intended to monitor visits to stopping points by vehicles assigned to routes. It allows you to determine which stopping points a vehicle visited and view its actual arrival and departure times. Before generating the report, select the vehicles and the bus line for which you want to check stopping point visits. What the report shows Each row represents one recorded visit to a stopping point by a vehicle. The report displays: Order of visit — the stopping point’s position in the route sequence Trip number — the name or number of the route on which the stopping point was visited Bus stop— the name of the visited stopping point Address — the address of the stopping point Date — the date when the stopping point was visited Arrival time — the time when the vehicle arrived at the stopping point Departure time — the time when the vehicle departed from the stopping point Vehicle — the number of the monitored vehicle that visited the stopping point If an RFID tag is linked to a bus stop, OneGPS can detect a visit using data from the RFID reader installed on the vehicle. A visit recorded this way appears in the report in the same format as one detected using the vehicle’s coordinates."},{"s":"control-of-visiting-stopping-points","t":"Control of visiting stopping points","h":["Control of visiting stopping points","What the report shows"],"x":"Control of visiting stopping points The Control of visiting stopping pointsreport helps monitor whether vehicles actually follow their assigned routes. It shows which stopping points were visited, when a vehicle arrived and departed, and how long it remained at each stopping point. When generating the report, select the stopping points you want to monitor. If several stopping points are selected, the report will display all recorded visits to these stopping points by the selected vehicles during the specified period. What the report shows Each row represents one recorded visit to a stopping point by a vehicle. The report displays the following information: Order stop number — the position of the stopping point in the route sequence Route — the name or number of the route followed by the vehicle Stopping point name — the name of the visited stopping point Address — the location of the stopping point Date — the date on which the stopping point was visited Arrival time — the time when the vehicle arrived at the stopping point Departure time — the time when the vehicle left the stopping point Parking— the amount of time the vehicle remained at the stopping point Vehicle number — the vehicle’s identification number"},{"s":"report-on-timetable-adherence-","t":"Report on timetable adherence","h":["Report on timetable adherence","What the report shows","How the report is generated","Comparison logic","Color indication"],"x":"Report on timetable adherence This report is designed to monitor the actual execution of the schedule on a selected bus line. It compares the planned time of stop arrivals (according to the schedule matrix) with the actual time of stop visits and clearly shows how closely the real movement matched the plan. What the report shows The report is built for one selected bus line and is presented as a table where: The first column — the name of the schedule. Each next column — a stop on the route. Each cell — data for a specific stop on a specific trip. Each cell displays: Plan — the planned arrival time at the stop. Fact — the actual arrival time at the stop. Diff — the deviation of the actual time from the planned one. Vehicle — the vehicle (license plate) that actually performed the stop. If no actual stop visit is recorded, the cell is marked as “Missed” (not executed). How the report is generated 1. The system takes the selected bus line and the list of its stops 2. It loads the history of schedule versions that were active during the selected period 3. For each day within the reporting interval, it determines which schedule table should be applied: by a specific date by day of the week or daily 4. For each planned trip and each stop, the system searches for the closest actual visit to that stop 5. If a matching event is found within the allowed deviation window — the cell shows Plan / Fact / Diff / Vehicle 6. If no event is found — the cell is marked as “Missed” Comparison logic The report checks schedule compliance not by a specific bus, but by the actual execution of the stop on the line. If several buses are operating on the line at the same time, the report counts any bus that actually performed the stop at the required time. For each planned time, no more than one actual event is used. One actual event is not reused to confirm several planned arrivals. If the schedule changes within the reporting period, the system applies the version of the schedule that was active at the moment of the specific planned trip. Color indication Green — minimal deviation, the stop was performed very close to the plan. Yellow — acceptable deviation from the schedule. Orange — noticeable time deviation. Red — large deviation or “Missed” (no actual stop visit recorded)."},{"s":"contragents-1","t":"Contragents","h":["Contragents"],"x":"Contragents This section contains reports for the Contragents module (also called Sales Representative). These reports help you analyze sales representatives’ performance and monitor client visits. The reports show which outlets were visited, when the visits took place, and how much time the representative spent with each client. With these reports, you can: Monitor client visits See the actual visit time Analyze how long representatives work at each location Compare the performance of different sales representatives"},{"s":"clients-visits-by-tasks","t":"Clients visits by tasks","h":["Clients visits by tasks","What the report shows","Report fields"],"x":"Clients visits by tasks The Clients visits by tasks report helps you track how sales representatives visit clients. It shows which locations were visited, when visits took place, and how much time the representative spent with each client. With this report, you can: Check which clients were visited during the selected period View the agent’s arrival and departure time Evaluate visit duration Compare the performance of different sales representatives What the report shows At the top of the report, you can see: The period covered by the report The report generation date Below, there is a table. Each row represents a single client visit. Report fields No. — row number in the report Client ID — internal client ID in the system Client name — name of the retail location or client Start time — when the sales representative arrived at the client Departure time — when the representative left the client Duration — total time spent during the visit Agent — sales representative who performed the visit"},{"s":"report-on-contragents","t":"Report on contragents","h":["Report on contragents","What the report shows","Report fields"],"x":"Report on contragents The Report on contragents shows how a sales representative visited clients during a selected period. It helps you understand which counterparties were visited, when the agent arrived and left, and how much time was spent at each location. What the report shows At the top of the report, you can see: The selected reporting period The date and day of the week The object — the sales representative for whom the report is generated Below this information, there is a table. Each row in the table represents a separate visit to a counterparty. Report fields Countragent — the client or outlet name in the system Beginning — the time when the sales representative arrived at the client’s location End — the time when the representative left the client Duration — how long the visit lasted Address — the actual address of the counterparty Below the table, summary information is usually displayed: Total time — total duration of all visits during the selected period Unique visits — number of different counterparties visited by the sales representative"},{"s":"crash-detection-1","t":"Crash detection","h":["Crash detection","Main information","Accelerometer graph","Vehicle diagram","Trips and parkings"],"x":"Crash detection The report is available if the Crash detecion module is enabled. It helps to analyze in detail what happened to the vehicle: when and where the incident occurred, the vehicle’s speed, the direction of the impact, and which trips were made during this period. Main information At the top of the report, you’ll find the key details of the event: Report period — the date range for which the report is generated License plate (Garage number) — the vehicle identifier Crash (Event type) — description of the event, in this case an accident Time — the exact time of the incident Device type — the name of the device that provided the data Coordinates — the map location of the event (you can open it on the map) Accelerometer graph The central part of the report displays a graph showing the forces acting on the vehicle at the moment of the accident X axis (blue line) — forward/backward movement (acceleration, braking, frontal impact) Y axis (purple line) — left/right movement (turns, side impact) Z axis (green line) — up/down movement (bumps, hitting an obstacle) Red line — vehicle speed Sharp peaks or drops on the graph indicate the moment of collision. Vehicle diagram Next to the graph, the system shows a vehicle model with a red arrow indicating the direction of the impact during the accident. Trips and parkings At the bottom of the report, there is a list of trips and parkings for the selected period. The table includes: Status — moving or parked Begining / End — time when the trip or stop began and ended Max speed — the highest speed during the trip Duration — how long the trip or stop lasted Distance (km) — total distance covered during the trip Start / Stop address — the address where the trip started and ended Coordinates — precise GPS coordinates After an accident, the device may send multiple Crash tags. In the report, if the interval between such signals is more than one minute, it is considered a new accident event. Therefore, the report for a single vehicle may contain several accident entries. If you click on a specific accident in the report, the system will try to load the vehicle’s movement track for that time and display it on the map (top panel). If no track data is available, the map will remain unchanged."},{"s":"drivers-2","t":"Drivers","h":["Drivers"],"x":"Drivers This section contains reports related to the Drivers module. They help you monitor driver activity, driver license data, identifiers, and trips, as well as analyze vehicle usage. With these reports, you can: see who was driving a vehicle and when track driver assignments to vehicles monitor driver license expiration dates analyze trips, mileage, and geofence violations get summary information for all drivers in the fleet"},{"s":"daily-trip-report","t":"Daily trip report","h":["Daily trip report","What the report shows","Report fields"],"x":"Daily trip report This report shows how each vehicle and driver performed during the day. It allows you to see: when the first trip started and the last trip ended how much time the vehicle spent driving, parked, and idle total mileage and speed for the day how many trips were completed What the report shows Selected period — the date range for which the report is generated Specific date — the date currently displayed in the table Report fields The report is built by days: one table row = one working day of one vehicle Date — the date when the first trip of the day started Object — the vehicle Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle on that day (if no driver is assigned, no driver is shown) First trip start time — date and time when the vehicle first started moving during the day Time between first start and last end (hh:mm:ss) — total “working time” of the vehicle for the day, from the first trip to the last one, including driving and stops Driving time % — percentage of working time spent driving Driving time (hh:mm:ss) — total time in motion during the day Standing time (hh:mm:ss) — total time of stops and parking between trips Duration (hh:mm:ss) — total vehicle activity time for the day (driving + parking) Idle time (hh:mm:ss) — time when the vehicle was powered on but not performing work (for example, engine idling) Distance (km) — total distance traveled during the day Average speed (km/h) — average speed for the day (distance divided by driving time) Max speed (km/h) — maximum recorded speed for the day Trip count — number of individual trips completed during the day At the bottom of the table, the Total row automatically calculates: total working time total distance average values across all rows for the selected date"},{"s":"driver-license-expire","t":"Driver license expire","h":["Driver license expire","How to build the report","What the report shows"],"x":"Driver license expire This report shows which drivers have a driver’s license that is about to expire. For a driver to appear in the report, the following fields must be filled in the driver profile: 1. Driver’s license — license number 2. Driver’s license expiry date — expiration date 1 — Driver license 2 — License expiry How to build the report 1. Go to the Driversreports section 2. Select the Driver license expire report 1 — Months range 2 — Select the driver 3. Set the check period — the range of months during which the driver’s license may expire 4. Select the drivers 5. Build the report What the report shows Each row represents one driver whose license expiration date falls within the selected period. Driver — driver’s full name Group — the group the driver belongs to ID number — driver’s license number Contact number — contact phone number Registration — the object assigned to the driver Expiry date — driver’s license expiration date"},{"s":"driver-violation-report","t":"Driver violation report","h":["Driver violation report","Report fields"],"x":"Driver violation report This report shows violations committed by drivers within a selected geofence during a specified time period. Before generating the report, select: 1. Geofence — the zone where violations should be checked 2. Driver — the driver for whom the report will be generated 1 — Select the geofence 2 — Select the driver Report fields Object — the vehicle (monitoring object) where the violation occurred Group — the group or department the object belongs to Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle at the time of the violation Zone — the geofence where the violation was recorded Date — the exact date and time of the violation Violation — the type of violation"},{"s":"driver-tags","t":"Driver tags","h":["Driver tags","What the report shows"],"x":"Driver tags This report shows which identifiers are assigned to each driver — RFID cards, iButtons, access passes, and other authentication keys. What the report shows Group — the group the driver belongs to Driver — the driver’s name Identification number — the ID specified in the driver profile (for example, an RFID card or iButton number) ID — the actual ID received by the system from the device when the tag was used Date — the date when this ID was last used Difference — indicates discrepancies between the stored driver data and the ID received from the device"},{"s":"invalid-ibuttons","t":"Invalid iButtons","h":["Invalid iButtons","What the report shows"],"x":"Invalid iButtons This report shows cases where iButtons that are not registered in the system were used during the selected period. An iButton is an electronic driver identification key used to control access to monitored vehicles or objects. For the system to correctly identify a driver, the iButton must be registered in the system and compatible with the reader. What the report shows Object — the vehicle or monitoring object where the key was used Date — the date and time when the trip started iButton — the identifier of the key that is not registered in the system"},{"s":"driver-list","t":"Driver list","h":["Driver list","What the report shows"],"x":"Driver list This report provides summary information about all drivers registered in the system. What the report shows Group — the group to which the driver belongs Driver — name of the driver ID Number — driver’s identification number in the system Tag ID — identifier of the driver’s tag Contact Number — driver’s phone number Registration — vehicle or object assigned to the driver Date — date the driver was registered in the system License Expiry Date — date when the driver’s license expires"},{"s":"mileage-report-driver-","t":"Mileage report (Driver)","h":["Mileage report (Driver)","What the report shows","Driver filter"],"x":"Mileage report (Driver) TheMileage Report (Driver) provides information about trips made by the selected vehicles during the specified period, with the data assigned to drivers. It allows you to identify who was driving during each trip, check the trip duration and distance, and analyze speed indicators. What the report shows Period — the start and end date and time of the reporting period Object — the name, registration number, or fleet number of the vehicle Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle during the trip Beginning — the date and time when the trip started End — the date and time when the trip ended Duration — the total duration of the trip Distance — the distance traveled during the trip Av. speed — the vehicle’s average speed during the trip Max Speed — the highest speed recorded during the trip The total mileage for each vehicle over the selected period is displayed at the bottom of the corresponding object section. Driver filter Before building the report, you can enable the On Driver option. When enabled, an additional Drivers panel opens to the right of the object list. In this panel, you can select one driver, multiple drivers, or an entire driver group. The generated report will include only trips associated with the selected drivers. 1 — Enable the driver filter 2 — Select a driver from the list"},{"s":"report-on-drivers","t":"Report on drivers","h":["Report on drivers","What the report shows"],"x":"Report on drivers This report shows which drivers were assigned to vehicles during the selected period. What the report shows Object — the vehicle or monitored object Date — the period covered by the report Driver — name of the driver assigned to the vehicle Start — date and time when the driver was assigned to the vehicle End — date and time when the driver was unassigned Duration — total time the driver was behind the wheel during the selected period"},{"s":"summary-business-private-report","t":"Summary business private report","h":["Summary business private report","What the report shows"],"x":"Summary business private report This report provides information on drivers’ trips, separating them into business and personal trips. It helps analyze how much time and distance drivers spent on work-related and personal travel. Before generating the report select the driver for whom the report will be generated. What the report shows Group — driver group in the system Object — vehicle or monitored object Driver — who was driving the vehicle during the trip Total trips — total number of trips during the selected period Business trips — number of trips marked as business Personal trips — number of trips marked as personal Total distance — total distance traveled across all trips Business distance — distance covered during business trips Personal distance — distance covered during personal trips Total duration — total time spent on all trips Business duration — time spent on business trips Personal duration — time spent on personal trips"},{"s":"eco-driving","t":"Eco Driving","h":["Eco Driving"],"x":"Eco Driving This section deals with reports related to the Eco Driving module."},{"s":"eco-driving-report","t":"Eco Driving Report","h":["Eco Driving Report","What the report shows","Types of violations"],"x":"Eco Driving Report The Eco Driving Reporthelps evaluate how carefully and safely drivers operate vehicles. The system records events (harsh acceleration, harsh braking, speeding, etc.), converts them into penalty points, and generates a summary table. What the report shows Each row represents data for one vehicle for the selected date and the assigned driver. The report includes: Object — the vehicle for which violations were recorded. Group — the group name the vehicle belongs to. Date — the day for which the metrics are calculated. Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle at the time of the violation. Distance — the distance (in km) during which Eco Driving events were recorded. Duration — the total time of violations. Types of violations For each event type (acceleration, braking, turn, speed, seat belt), the report displays: Number of violations / Fine points Total fine — total number of points for all violations Fine / 100 km — average number of penalty points per 100 km The lower the value, the calmer and safer the driving style. At the bottom of the report, a Total row displays the aggregated values for all rows in the table. When generating the report, you can choose which data to include in the analysis: By objects — to see which vehicles have the most violations By groups — to compare performance between vehicle groups By drivers — to identify the safest drivers and those who violate rules more frequently If no objects are selected, the report will be generated for all vehicles operated by the selected drivers. If no drivers are selected, the report will include all drivers, as well as periods when no driver was assigned to the vehicle. When generating the report, you can also apply Eco Weight settings. These settings define which violations are considered more serious and which are less significant. The selected weights directly affect the penalty points and the final evaluation of drivers and vehicles. The Eco Weightsettings icon in the report is visible: — only to users with administrator rights — only if the Eco Driving module is enabled in the account"},{"s":"eco-driving-report-2","t":"Eco Driving Report 2","h":["Eco Driving Report 2","What the report shows"],"x":"Eco Driving Report 2 Eco Driving Report 2 is a detailed log of all detected violations. This report breaks down data to each specific event: when, where, and what type of violation occurred. What the report shows Each row in the table represents one recorded violation. The report includes the following columns: Object — the vehicle where the violation was detected Driver — the driver assigned to the asset at the time of the event Time — the exact date and time when the violation occurred Type — the category of the violation (for example, speeding, harsh braking, etc.) Message — a text description of the violation defined in the Eco-driving module rule settings Value — the actual measured value that triggered the rule (for example, speed, idle duration, acceleration level) Fine — the number of penalty points assigned for the event Place — the address where the violation occurred, with a clickable link to open the point on the map When you select a specific violation, its type and the location of the violation on the map will be displayed."},{"s":"fleet-eco-report","t":"Fleet ECO report","h":["Fleet ECO report"],"x":"Fleet ECO report The report is designed to display data on the current and previous Eco Driving Rating. Vehicle- is the object of monitoring in the system; Excess idle- the time of non-operation of the facility exceeds the established values; Over Speed - violation of established speed limits on roads; Harsh Brake- refers to a driving style that involves gentle acceleration and braking to reduce fuel consumption and reduce emissions. Harsh Acceleration - refers to a driving style that includes smooth acceleration and braking to reduce fuel consumption and reduce emissions of harmful substances into the atmosphere. Distance - mileage of the monitoring object during the specified trip in km; Duration- time of violation for the selected period. Current rating - an indicator that reflects achievements over a certain period based on recent driving activity. Previous Rating-is an indicator that reflects achievements over a certain period based on recent driving activity."},{"s":"rag-events-2-report","t":"RAG Events #2 Report","h":["RAG Events #2 Report"],"x":"RAG Events #2 Report Plate- the monitoring object on which the driving quality was recorded; Total distance (km) - the distance over which the violation was committed; Drive duration - the duration during which the violation was committed; Max. speed -is the maximum speed recorded during the trip. Over Speeding area - a section of the road on which a maximum speed limit for vehicles is set; Over Speeding normal - driving a vehicle at a speed exceeding the limits established by law or road signs; Over speeding duration area- duration of overspeeding; Over speeding duration normal- the time during which the vehicle is traveling in excess of the posted speed limit; Over speeding duration total- the time during which the vehicle is traveling in excess of the established speed limit; Speed score - speed estimate value; Total acceleration count - the value of the total acceleration for the specified period; Acceleration score - acceleration estimate value; Total decelerations - the value of the total deceleration for the specified period; Deceleration score - deceleration rating value; Time without a seat belt - the time spent traveling without a seat belt during the reporting period; Assessment of seat belt violations - value for violations without a seat belt; Excessive idling - the car engine idles longer than necessary; Downtime duration - value about the duration of downtime; Overall score- the final assessment of the trip. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"rag-events-report","t":"RAG Events Report","h":["RAG Events Report"],"x":"RAG Events Report The RAG Event Report is a report that provides an overview of key driving quality events. Driver- the driver assigned to this facility; Total distance (km) - the distance over which the violation was committed; Total acceleration- the overall value of the acceleration estimate; Acceleration score - acceleration estimate value; Total decelerations - the total value of the deceleration assessment; Deceleration score - deceleration assessment value; Highest speed (km/h) - the maximum speed recorded during the trip. Total overspeeding time - the total time during which the speed was exceeded; Speed score- speed estimate value; Time without a seat belt - the time during which the driver moved without a seat belt; Seat belt violation assessment- the value of assessing driving without a seat belt; •Score- is formed based on the sum of sharp turns, braking and acceleration. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"rag-report-summary","t":"RAG Report Summary","h":[],"x":"RAG Report Summary This type of report provides summary information on driving quality. Object - a monitoring object on which the quality of driving was recorded; Driver - the driver assigned to this object; Month - the month in which the violation was committed; Distance - the distance over which the violation was committed; Duration - the duration during which the violation was committed; Number of sharp accelerations - the number of sharp accelerations; The number of sharp accelerations per 100km - the number of sharp accelerations per 100km; Number of sharp turns - the number of sharp turns; Number of sharp turns per 100km - number of sharp turns per 100km; The number of sharp braking - the number of sharp braking; The number of sudden braking per 100km - the number of sudden braking per 100km; Maximum speed limit km/h - maximum speed limit; Duration of speedingD - the time during which the speed was exceeded; RAG scores - formed on the basis of the sum of sharp turns, braking and acceleration. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"vehicle-score-card","t":"Vehicle Score Card","h":["Vehicle Score Card"],"x":"Vehicle Score Card The report is designed to display a numeric or ordinal score of a driver's driving rating. Driver - the name of the driver in the system; Group - name of the group in the system; Rank - the driver’s place in the driving rating; Mileage - mileage value in km on the specified date; Score - account; Group- group. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"vehicle-score-card-2","t":"Vehicle Score Card #2","h":["Vehicle Score Card #2"],"x":"Vehicle Score Card #2 The report is designed to display your driving rating as a percentage. Driver- the driver assigned to this facility; No trips- number of trips; Distance - mileage value in km on the specified date; Number of speed violations - the number of speed violations for the specified date; Over Speeding - the time the driver exceeded the speed limit for the specified date; Percentage of speed - speeding by the driver as a percentage; Max. speed- maximum speed recorded during the trip; Avg. speed- average speed recorded during the trip; Excess idle- value indicating that the duration of downtime has been exceeded; Harsh Brake - the number of sharp accelerations; Harsh Acceleration - the number of sharp accelerations; Rating - rating based on the sum of sharp turns, braking and acceleration as a percentage; Group rating - rating based on the sum of sharp turns, braking and acceleration as a percentage;"},{"s":"vehicle-score-card-3","t":"Vehicle Score Card #3","h":["Vehicle Score Card #3"],"x":"Vehicle Score Card #3 Report score card No. 3 displays data by month. Driver - the driver assigned to this facility; Calendar month - estimate based on the sum of sharp turns, braking and acceleration as a percentage."},{"s":"equipment-1","t":"Equipment","h":["Equipment"],"x":"Equipment This section deals with reports related to the equipment of objects."},{"s":"dip-sensors-report","t":"Dip sensors report","h":["Dip sensors report"],"x":"Dip sensors report Dip sensor is a type of sensor that can be in one of two states: active or passive. Such sensors are usually used to change liquid level, pressure, temperature and other parameters. They can be mechanical, optical, magnetic, capacitive or inductive. The report is intended to display data on the status of the sensor.: Object- monitoring object; Date and time - date and time of the event; Event- description of the sensor event; Drivers- assigned driver to the object. Geozones- the place of the event. Place - the address of the sensor actuation event. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"equipment-report-table-","t":"Equipment Report ( table)","h":["Equipment Report ( table)"],"x":"Equipment Report ( table) The equipment operation report is designed to display data indicating the facility, equipment, equipment start and end times, and location. For the report to work correctly, all the necessary sensors of the connected equipment must be configured. When this type of report is selected, it is possible to configure the type of report display in the form: - Table - Graph Select the type of report view. Date- the period of time for which the report table is built. Object - the monitoring object for which the report table is being built. Equipment - the name of the equipment. Beginning- date and time when the equipment started to work. End - date and time when the equipment will end. Duration - equipment operation time, difference between end and start. Distance - the distance traveled by the object for the given period. Start- the place where the equipment starts to work. Stop - the place where the equipment stops working. Driver - driver assigned to the object."},{"s":"equipment-report-graph-","t":"Equipment Report (graph)","h":["Equipment Report (graph)"],"x":"Equipment Report (graph) The equipment operation report is designed to display data in the form of a graph indicating the object, equipment, start and end times."},{"s":"equipment-work-on-days-2","t":"Equipment Work on Days","h":["Equipment Work on Days"],"x":"Equipment Work on Days This report is designed to control the operation time of monitored objects. Allows you to display data in the form of a table containing the following parameters: Object- monitoring object. Duration of Moving (h) - the time during which the monitored object was in motion. The minimum speed at which the object is considered to be moving is set in the object's properties on the Settings tab. Duration of parking- total parking time of the object per day. The time from which the object is considered to have stopped is set in the object's properties on the Settings tab. Duration of work - equal to the sum of the time in motion and the time of stops during working hours. Duration of parking - the total time of stops of the object per day. The minimum time after which the object is considered to have stopped is set in the object's properties on the Settings tab (Min. parking time parameter). Time the Ignition - time during which the ignition was on. Vacation time - is equal to the difference between 24 hours and working time on the line. Distance - mileage of the monitored object for 24 hours. Avg. speed - average speed of the monitored object. For the report to work correctly, all the necessary sensors of the connected equipment must be configured. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"multi-position-sensors-events-report","t":"Multi-Position Sensors Events Report","h":["Multi-Position Sensors Events Report"],"x":"Multi-Position Sensors Events Report This type of report presents information in the form of a table, which displays changes in the readings of multi-position sensors and speed in a given period of time. Time - event time; Sensor - the sensor involved in the event; Message- sensor state; Speed - the speed of the object at the time of the event."},{"s":"multi-position-sensors-graph-report","t":"Multi-Position Sensors Graph Report","h":["Multi-Position Sensors Graph Report"],"x":"Multi-Position Sensors Graph Report This type of report presents information in the form of a graph, which displays changes in the readings of multi-position sensors and speed in a given period of time."},{"s":"multi-position-sensors-work-report","t":"Multi-Position Sensors Work Report","h":["Multi-Position Sensors Work Report"],"x":"Multi-Position Sensors Work Report The report displays changes in sensor operation in the form of a table indicating mileage for the selected period. •Object- vehicle entered into the system; Start- date and time of start of work; End- date and time of completion of work; Sensor- name of the sensor in the system; Value- status of the work sensor; Mileage - the speed of the object in km for the specified period. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"summary-equipment-report-2","t":"Summary Equipment Report","h":[],"x":"The equipment summary report is designed to display a table containing data on the quantitative indicators of the operation of special equipment with installed sensors for the bucket, boom, and other equipment. Object- monitoring object for which data is displayed in the report. Vehicle number - monitoring object for which the data is displayed in the report. Date- the date for which the data is displayed in the report. Work time (Ignition sensor)- the time of operation of the monitoring object (the time when the ignition sensor was in the \"on\" position). Idling- operation time of the monitored object when the special sensor was in the \"off\" position. •Under load- monitoring object operation time when the special sensor was in the \"on\" position. Downtime- time during the working day (for this report, the working day = 13 hours) when the monitoring object was not working (the time when the ignition sensor was in the \"off\" position). Actions Number- the number of actions that this sensor has performed (bucket flexion/extension, etc.). Fuel consumption - the amount of fuel consumed by the monitored object during the day. Distance - mileage of the monitored object passed during the day."},{"s":"fuel-2","t":"Fuel","h":["Fuel"],"x":"Fuel This section deals with reports related to fuel."},{"s":"can-fuel-consumption","t":"CAN fuel consumption","h":["CAN fuel consumption","Sensor configuration requirements","What the report shows"],"x":"CAN fuel consumption The CAN fuel consumption report shows fuel consumption based on CAN data for the selected period. It helps compare the amount of fuel consumed with the object mileage and calculate the average fuel consumption per 100 km. Sensor configuration requirements For data to be displayed in the report, a CAN fuel consumption sensor must be configured for the object. In the sensor settings, specify: Function — Fuel consumption Type — Discrete integral Save history — the checkbox must be enabled The sensor must transmit an accumulated fuel consumption value. To calculate the report, the system takes the initial and final value for each day and calculates consumption as the difference between them. If the sensor has a different function or type, or if history saving is disabled, the data may not be included in the report. What the report shows Object — the name of the monitoring object for which the report is generated Date — the date for which the data is displayed Consumption — the amount of fuel consumed on the specified date Distance — the object mileage on the specified date Fuel consumption — the average fuel consumption per 100 km Fuel consumption is calculated using the formula: (Consumption × 100 km) / Distance"},{"s":"consolidated-fuel-report-sensor-and-can-","t":"Consolidated Fuel Report (sensor and CAN)","h":["Consolidated Fuel Report (sensor and CAN)"],"x":"Consolidated Fuel Report (sensor and CAN) This report combines fuel data from the CAN bus and fuel sensor to compare readings. The report contains: No. - serial number of the object; Vehicle - object of monitoring; Distance at the beginning (By CAN) - the total mileage of the object before the trip; Distance ( CAN) - distance traveled per day; Distance at the end (By CAN) - the total mileage of the object after the trip; Fuel at the beginning - fuel level before the trip; Refuel - the amount of fuel filled in liters according to the sensor; Refuel (CAN) - the amount of fuel filled in liters via the CAN bus; Fuel at the end- fuel level at the end of the trip; Fuel consumption (CAN) - fuel consumed during the trip via the CAN bus; Fuel consumption - fuel consumed during the trip according to the sensor; Average fuel consumption (CAN) - average fuel consumption per 100 km via CAN bus; Average fuel consumption - average fuel consumption per 100 km according to the sensor; Total drain - the amount of fuel drained in liters."},{"s":"consolidated-report-2","t":"Consolidated Report #2","h":["Consolidated Report #2","Object -","Mileage","Fuel level at the beginning","Fuel level at the end,","Refills","Drains -","Fuel consumption","Consumption, l/100 km","Standard fuel consumption","Norm","Machine hours"],"x":"Consolidated Report #2 The report contains: •Object -monitoring object for which data is displayed. •Mileage - distance traveled in a given period of time. •Fuel level at the beginning, l - the amount of fuel at the beginning of the period. •Fuel level at the end,l - amount of fuel at the end of the period. •Refills - the amount of fuel filled for a given period. •Drains -the amount of fuel drained for a given period. • Fuel consumption- the amount of fuel consumed over a given period. •Consumption, l/100 km- average fuel consumption per 100 km in a given period. •Standard fuel consumption- fuel consumption at the established fuel consumption rate at the facility. •Norm- calculation of fuel consumption at the established norm of fuel consumption at the facility per 100 km. • Machine hours - the number of engine hours passed during a given period of time. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"consolidated-report-3","t":"Consolidated Report #3","h":["Consolidated Report #3"],"x":"Consolidated Report #3 Consolidated report No. 3 is designed to display information about refueling, draining, speeding and downtime for a specified period. The report contains: Vehicle number- the name of the monitoring object for which information is displayed. Fillings- the amount of filled fuel in liters. Drain Offs - the amount of drained fuel in liters. Overspeedings - the date and time when the speeding was recorded, the maximum recorded speed at the specified time. Downtime over 1 hour- duration, date and time of recorded downtime. Downtime addresses - downtime location."},{"s":"consolidated-report-4","t":"Consolidated Report #4","h":["Consolidated Report #4"],"x":"Consolidated Report #4 This report is designed to display information on gas stations and the distance traveled. The report contains: •Object- monitoring object; Distance (km) - distance traveled by the object per day; Filling - how many liters were filled; Fuel Average - average fuel consumption per 100km per day; Idling time - the duration of the object's downtime for the selected period. Parking time- the duration of the object's parking when refueling. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"diesel-generator-report","t":"Diesel generator report","h":["Diesel generator report"],"x":"Diesel generator report A special report shows fuel data. The report contains: Date- report start date; Period duration- duration of the period; Veh number - monitoring object; Machine hours- a unit of measurement of engine operating time; •Fuel consumption on sensor- an indicator that reflects the amount of fuel consumed by the vehicle, based on data received from the fuel level sensor; Refuels - the amount of fuel filled in liters according to the sensor; Drains off- amount of fuel drained in liters according to the sensor; Fuel consumption per hour - an indicator that indicates how much fuel is consumed by the engine in one hour of operation. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"diesel-generator-report-2","t":"Diesel Generator report #2","h":["Diesel Generator report #2"],"x":"Diesel Generator report #2 The report displays the operation of the generator, indicating the period of operation, the beginning of machine hours, the end of machine hours and duration. Date - report start date; Period duration - duration of the period; Site- website; Machine hours- a unit of measurement of engine operating time; Machine hours start- date and time of generator operation; Machine hours start stop - date and time of end of generator operation; Duration - duration of work; Fuel consumption on sensor - an indicator that reflects the amount of fuel consumed by the vehicle, based on data received from the fuel level sensor; Refuels- the amount of fuel filled in liters according to the sensor; Refueling time - refueling time ; Drainsoffs - the amount of fuel drained in liters according to the sensor; Time drain offs- time tracking in the off state; Fuel consumption per hour -is an indicator that indicates how much fuel is consumed by the engine in one hour of operation. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"drain-offs","t":"Drain Offs","h":["Drain Offs"],"x":"Drain Offs This type of report is intended to display information about fuel drains at a monitoring facility. Object - monitoring object, where the fuel drain was recorded. Date - date and time of the drain. Fuel at beginning- fuel level at the start of draining. Drained off (l) - amount of drained fuel. Address - the address where the fuel was drained. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"fluids-consumption-report","t":"Fluids Consumption Report","h":["Fluids Consumption Report"],"x":"Fluids Consumption Report The fluid consumption report presents information in the form of a chart that displays the amount of fluid consumed at a specified point in time. When you hover your mouse over a column, additional information about the expense pops up. Designed for pulse encoders only. Object- the monitoring object for which the diagram is being built. Date - the time period for which the chart is being built. Mileage, km- mileage of the monitored object for the specified date. •Total consumption, l- the amount of fuel consumed for the specified date. Consumption, l/100 km (sensor/norm) - the amount of fuel consumed per 100 km of run according to the sensor readings (calculated as (Consumption * 100 km)/Mileage) / the amount of fuel consumed per 100 km of run according to the established norm (the norm is set in fuel tab). Idling consumption- the amount of fuel consumed in the absence of speed at the monitored object. Consumption by engine hours, l/h - the amount of fuel consumed per engine hour, calculated as Total consumption/Motor hours."},{"s":"fluids-consumption-report-table-","t":"Fluids Consumption Report (table)","h":["Fluids Consumption Report (table)"],"x":"Fluids Consumption Report (table) Liquid consumption report (table) is designed to display information about the consumed liquid in a given period of time. This type of report is intended only for impulse sensors. Date - time period for which the report is generated •Object- the monitoring object for which the corresponding data is displayed in the report. Sensor - the name of the sensor. Start - date and time of the start of fluid movement. Stop - date and time when fluid movement ends. Duration - duration of fluid movement. Fuel - the amount of fuel used."},{"s":"fluids-report","t":"Fluids Report","h":["Fluids Report"],"x":"Fluids Report This type of report presents information in the form of a graph that displays changes in speed and fluid levels. Fillings and drains are marked with characteristic icons, by clicking on which additional information pops up. Object - the monitoring object for which the graph is being built. Date - the period of time for which the chart is built. Balance at the beginning, l - the amount of fuel in the tank at the beginning of the specified date. Balance at the end, l - the amount of fuel in the tank at the end of the specified date. Filled, l - the amount of fuel filled on the specified date. Seizure, l - the amount of fuel seized on the specified date."},{"s":"fuel-card-report-day-","t":"Fuel card report (day)","h":["Fuel card report (day)"],"x":"Fuel card report (day) The fuel card report is designed to display data in the form of a table with the number of the fuel card associated with the facility, the date of refueling, the number of the gas station, the type and quantity of fuel, as well as the price (with a discount), the amount (with a discount) and the card balance. When you select this type of report, the time breakdown and clarification parameters become unavailable for customization. Car/Card - object and number of the card used for refueling. Date - date and time of refueling. Gas station- a gas station where refueling was carried out. Fuel - the name of the fuel that was filled. Operation- an operation that was carried out using a fuel card. Quantity - amount of fuel filled. Sensor filling- the amount of fuel filled according to the readings of the fuel sensor (When the fuel sensor is installed Price/discount- fuel price per liter/fuel price per liter taking into account the discount (if any). Sum total - total price for refueling/including discount (if any)."},{"s":"fuel-cards-report","t":"Fuel Cards Report","h":["Fuel Cards Report"],"x":"Fuel Cards Report The fuel card report is designed to display data in the form of a table with the number of the fuel card associated with the facility, the date of refueling, the number of the gas station, the type and quantity of fuel, as well as the price (with a discount), the amount (with a discount) and the card balance. When you select this type of report, the time breakdown and clarification parameters become unavailable for customization. Car number/Card number - object and card number used for refueling. Date - date and time of refueling. Location- the location where the facility is refueled. Gas station - a gas station where refueling was carried out. Coincidence - status in case of correct display of the fuel being filled by the sensor and the coincidence of the fuel volume according to its transaction. Fuel - the name of the fuel that was filled. •Operation- an operation that was carried out using a fuel card. •Amount- amount of fuel filled. Sensor filling - the amount of fuel filled according to the readings of the fuel sensor (When the fuel sensor is installed) Report color code designation (by what percentage is one number greater than another). discrepancy up. less than or equal to 3% - green, less than or equal to 5% - blue. less than or equal to 7% - orange, more than 7% - red. Percentage - the percentage of discrepancy between the amount of fuel filled at the gas station and the amount of fuel indicated by the sensor. Price - fuel price per liter/fuel price per liter. Sum total- total price for refueling/including discount (if any). This report has an important column — 'match'. It shows whether the coordinates of the gas station and the time of refueling of the object match or not. If the object actually refueled at the gas station, the report will show the matching coordinates and the corresponding status. If the coordinates do not match, they can be corrected by clicking on Transactions, selecting the desired transaction, and then selecting the 'change' or 'add gas station' option under the map. Even if the report has already shown a discrepancy, you can return to the previous period, change the location and update the report. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"pumps-nozzles-summary-report","t":"Pumps Nozzles Summary Report","h":["Pumps Nozzles Summary Report"],"x":"Pumps Nozzles Summary Report This is report for fuel pumps At gas station One is for each transaction. For pumps and nozzles. Pump - pump . Nozzle- nozzle . Fuel- type fuel . Volume totals - volume totals . Volume totals - volume totals . Volume totals - volume totals . Total filled volume - total filled volume . Amount totals start- amount totals start . Amount totals end- amount totals end . Amount totals difference- amount totals difference . Total filled amount- total filled amount. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"fuel-cards-summary-report","t":"Fuel Cards Summary Report","h":["Fuel Cards Summary Report"],"x":"Fuel Cards Summary Report No. - serial number of the monitoring object in the report. Vehicle -is a monitoring object for which matching information is provided. Model - model of the monitoring object. Car number - license plate number of the monitoring object. Mileage - mileage of the monitoring object. Period- mileage for the period for which the report is being generated. Since the beginning of the year - mileage since the beginning of the year. Engine hours- the number of engine hours of the object under observation. Period - the number of engine hours for the period for which the report is being generated. Since the beginning of the year - the number of engine hours since the beginning of the year. Consumption rate, l - fuel consumption rate of the monitoring object (the rate is set in the fuel tab). Summer - fuel consumption norm in summer. Winter - fuel consumption rate in winter. Fuel map - fuel maps of monitoring objects. Consumption (l) - fuel consumption of various brands. Fuel supplier - fuel card suppliers. Mileage- mileage of the monitored object values at the beginning and end of the period for which the report is being generated. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"fuel-movement-report","t":"Fuel Movement Report","h":["Fuel Movement Report"],"x":"Fuel Movement Report This report is designed to account for fuel at the beginning of the day and at the end of the day. Vehicle- monitoring object; Start - time and date of the beginning of the movement of the vehicle. Fuel at start of refueling (l)- fuel level at the beginning of the day; •Refuel (l)- filling in liters; Fuel after refueling (l) - fuel level at the end of the day. •Stop- time and date of the end of the movement of the vehicle. •Address- the address where the refueling was made. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"fuel-report-at-norms-","t":"Fuel Report (at norms)","h":[],"x":"This type of report contains information about fuel consumption calculated according to the norm. Consumption is calculated as the rate multiplied by the mileage. The rate is set in the fuel tab. Object - monitoring object for which the corresponding information is displayed. Date - the period of time for which the corresponding information is displayed. Fuel type- fuel type of the monitored object. Norm Calculation type - mileage of the monitored object for the specified date. Normal - fuel consumption rate of the specified monitoring object. Fuel consumption - the amount of fuel consumed by the monitored object for the specified date. Mileage- mileage of the specified monitoring objects for the specified date. Machine hours- the number of engine hours for the specified date. If several types of fuel are used at the monitored object (for more details, see the type of fuel), each type of fuel consumed will be presented in the report."},{"s":"fuel-report-by-runs-","t":"Fuel Report (by Runs)","h":["Fuel Report (by Runs)"],"x":"Fuel Report (by Runs) The report on fuel (by trips) is designed to display fuel indicators during a trip. •Date- the date of the trip. Vehicle number - the number of the monitoring object. Distance km- mileage of the monitored object during the specified trip. Total time- the duration of the specified trip. Start - the starting point of the specified trip. Stop - the end point of the specified trip. Fuel consumption - the amount of fuel consumed by the monitored object during the specified trip. Filled - the amount of fuel filled during the trip. Drained - the amount of fuel withdrawn during the trip. Average km/l- the distance covered by the monitored object for one liter of fuel. Average l/100km - average fuel consumption per 100 km during the trip. Average l/h- average fuel consumption for one hour of travel. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"fuel-report-generators-","t":"Fuel Report (generators)","h":["Fuel Report (generators)"],"x":"Fuel Report (generators) Dedicated graphical report showing Fuel Report data (generators)."},{"s":"fuel-report-in-total-","t":"Fuel Report (in total)","h":["Fuel Report (in total)"],"x":"Fuel Report (in total) This type of report is designed to display summary information on fuel consumption for a specified period. Fuel data is generated based on fuel sensor readings. Object - the monitoring object for which the corresponding data is displayed. Date- the date for which the corresponding data is displayed. Distance, km - mileage of the monitored object for the specified date. Balance at the beginning, l- the amount of fuel in the tank at the beginning of the specified date. Balance at the end, l- the amount of fuel in the tank at the end of the specified date. Filled, l- the amount of fuel filled on the specified date. Drained, l- the amount of fuel drained on the specified date. Consumption, l- the amount of fuel consumed for the specified date. Average consumption, l/100 km - the amount of fuel consumed per 100 km of run, calculated as (Consumption * 100 km)/Mileage. Machine hours - the number of engine hours for the specified date. Average consumption, l/h- the amount of fuel consumed per engine hour, calculated as Consumption/Motor hours. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"fuel-report-sensors-","t":"Fuel Report (sensors)","h":["Fuel Report (sensors)"],"x":"Fuel Report (sensors) This type of report presents information in the form of a graph that displays changes in speed and fuel level. The blue area on the graph corresponds to the time when the ignition sensor was in the \"on\" position (if the sensor is present). Fillings and drains are marked with characteristic icons, by clicking on which additional information pops up. Object - the monitoring object for which the graph is being built. Date- the period of time for which the chart is built. Mileage, km- mileage of the monitored object for the specified date. Balance at the beginning, l- the amount of fuel in the tank at the beginning of the specified date. Balance at the end, l - the amount of fuel in the tank at the end of the specified date. Filled, l- the amount of fuel filled on the specified date. Drained, l- the amount of fuel drained on the specified date. Total consumption, l - the amount of fuel consumed for the specified date. Consumption, l/100 km (sensor/norm) - the amount of fuel consumed per 100 km of run according to the sensor readings (calculated as (Consumption * 100 km)/Mileage) / the amount of fuel consumed per 100 km of run according to the established norm (the norm is set in fuel tab). Consumption at Idling - the amount of fuel consumed in the absence of speed at the monitored object. Machine- hours consumption , l/h- the amount of fuel consumed per engine hour, calculated as Total consumption/Motor hours."},{"s":"refuels","t":"Refuels","h":["Refuels"],"x":"Refuels This type of report is designed to display information about the filling stations of the monitored object and includes the following data: •Object- monitoring object that was filled. Date - date and time of filling. Fuel at start (l)- fuel level at the start of filling. •Refuel (l)- the amount of filled fuel. Fuel at end (l) - fuel level after refuelling. Address - the address where the refueling was made."},{"s":"refuels-2","t":"Refuels #2","h":["Refuels #2"],"x":"Refuels #2 This report is designed to display gas stations in geofences. •Object- monitoring object; Date- time and date of filling; Refuel - how much was filled (in liters); Mileage- car mileage at the current moment; Geozone - geofence in which refueling was performed. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"sensor-and-norm-comparison","t":"Sensor and Norm comparison","h":["Sensor and Norm comparison"],"x":"Sensor and Norm comparison The fuel sensor/rate report is designed to display fuel indicators, which are set in the GPS monitoring system during the trip. This report contains data about the object, date, fuel type, average consumption and difference. Object- monitoring object for which the corresponding data is displayed. Folder - location of the object in the system; Date - the date for which the corresponding data is displayed. Fuel type - fuel type; Fuel consumption - the amount of fuel consumed for the specified date according to the sensor. Norm calculation type - type value; •Average consumption, l/100 km- the amount of fuel consumed per 100 km, calculated as (Consumption * 100 km)/Mileage. Difference - difference indicators in liters; Percentage difference"},{"s":"composite-objects-1","t":"Composite objects","h":["Composite objects"],"x":"Composite objects This section contains reports related to the Composite objects module. They help you monitor fuel movement and track fuel received, consumed, and remaining across the entire fuel scheme, which can include multiple tanks and sensors. Using these reports, you can: track daily fuel balances analyze consumption and identify discrepancies between tank level readings and flow meter measurements monitor fuel stocks and deliveries across the entire scheme"},{"s":"composite-objects-2","t":"Composite objects","h":["Composite objects","What the report shows"],"x":"Composite objects The Composite objects report shows the daily fuel balance for the entire fuel scheme configured in the Composite objectsmodule. It helps you see how much fuel was available at the start of the day, how much was received, how much was consumed, and how much remained overall for the composite object. What the report shows Each row in the table represents one day of operation and includes: Date — the day for which fuel movements are calculated Fuel at start — estimated fuel balance at the beginning of the day Fuel received — the volume of fuel added to the composite object during the day Fuel consumption — actual fuel used based on tank level readings Consumption by flow meters — total fuel recorded by output flow meters Consumption difference — the difference between consumption by level and consumption by flow meters Fuel at end — estimated fuel balance at the end of the day At the bottom of the report, there is a total row for the selected period, showing the overall received fuel, consumption, and remaining balance."},{"s":"diesel-logbook","t":"Diesel logbook","h":["Diesel logbook","What the report shows"],"x":"Diesel logbook The Diesel Logbook report shows the fuel balance of the selected composite object for the selected period. It helps monitor the initial and final fuel balance, fuel input, fuel consumption, output flow, and possible discrepancies. The report can be built for only one composite object. For correct calculation, tanks, flow meters, and links between the composite object elements must be configured for the object. What the report shows Initial balance — the fuel balance at the beginning of the selected period Input — the amount of fuel received by the composite object Consumption — the amount of fuel consumed during the period Output flow — the amount of fuel released through output elements or flow meters Discrepancy — the difference between calculated and actual values Final balance — the fuel balance at the end of the selected period A report screenshot will be added in the next version of the documentation."},{"s":"fuel-flow","t":"Fuel flow","h":["Fuel flow","What the report shows"],"x":"Fuel flow The Fuel flowreport shows fuel movement for a composite object over the selected period. It helps analyze fuel input, consumption, fuel balances, output readings, and fuel level changes based on sensor data. The report can be displayed as a table or a chart. To build the report, select a composite object and the required sensors. If needed, you can split the data by days and group it by dates or objects. What the report shows Initial balance — the fuel balance at the beginning of the selected period Input — the amount of fuel received by the composite object Consumption — the amount of fuel consumed during the period By outputs — the amount of fuel released through output elements or flow meters Discrepancy — the difference between calculated and actual values Final balance — the fuel balance at the end of the selected period Sensor readings — values from the selected fuel sensors in chart mode Fuel level change events — events related to fuel level changes A report screenshot will be added in the next version of the documentation."},{"s":"control-room-1","t":"Control room","h":["Control room"],"x":"Control room Reports in the Control room module help you review events that occurred in the system during a selected period. They allow you to understand what happened to the objects, when it happened, and whether the event was processed. The reports also show who processed the event, when it was processed, and what comments the operator added."},{"s":"events","t":"Events","h":["Events"],"x":"Events The Events report shows all events recorded by the system during the selected period. It helps you understand what happened, when it happened, and how the events were handled by operators. The main part of the report is a table that includes the following fields: Group — the object group to which the object belongs Object — the monitored object from which the event was received Time — the date and time when the event occurred Type — a description of the event (for example, speeding, Alarm → Lane deviation, etc.) Event Group (right column) — the event category (for example, Alarms) Status — the event processing status: Unprocessed or Processed Operator — the system user who processed the event Processing Time — the date and time when the operator completed the event processing Comments — additional notes added by the operator during processing"},{"s":"geofences","t":"Geofences","h":["Geofences"],"x":"Geofences This section deals with reports related to the Geozones module."},{"s":"equipment-by-geozones","t":"Equipment by geozones","h":["Equipment by geozones"],"x":"Equipment by geozones The report is intended to display data on the operation of equipment in the zone. Equipment - name of the sensor; Beginning - time and date when the sensor started operating; End - time and date when the sensor operation ends; Duration- operating time; Distance- mileage in km of the monitoring object for the specified date; Start- start of work in the zone; Stop- end of work in the zone; Driver - the name of the driver in the system."},{"s":"events-report-2","t":"Events report","h":["Events report"],"x":"Events report This report displays activities on an object for a certain period of time. Event- sensor event type; Event start - time and date when the sensor started operating; Event end - time and date of end of sensor operation; Duration- operating time; Distance- mileage in km of the monitoring object for the specified date; Event start address - event address; •Event end address- event address at the end;"},{"s":"geozone-idling-report","t":"Geozone Idling Report","h":["Geozone Idling Report"],"x":"Geozone Idling Report The report on downtime in a geofence is designed to control the downtime of monitored objects in geographic zones. Available when connecting an additional Geozone module. Vehicle (Object) — the monitored vehicle or asset Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle at the time of the idle event Department — the group the vehicle belongs to Object Type — type of vehicle (passenger car, truck, trailer, etc.) Start — date and time when the idle period began Stop — date and time when the idle period ended Duration — total idle time (hh:mm:ss) Address — the idle location address (based on geocoder data). Displayed as a hyperlink — clicking it opens the map with the idle location point Coordinates — exact latitude and longitude of the location point Geozone- the geographic area in which downtime was recorded. Beginning- date and time when the downtime recording started. End - date and time of the end of downtime fixing. Geozone Time - the time during which the monitored object was in the specified geofence. Inactivity setting - allowable downtime in a geofence (set in the object's settings on the \"Geofences\" tab in the downtime control field). Inactivity - downtime of the monitoring object in the geographical area. Equal to the difference between the start and end of downtime minus the allowable downtime. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"geozones-downtime","t":"Geozones Downtime","h":["Geozones Downtime","Object","Geofence","Beginning","End","Duration","Mileage","Downtime"],"x":"Geozones Downtime •Object - a monitoring object for which a table with downtime is generated. • Geofence - the geographic area in which the downtime was recorded. •Beginning- date and time when downtime recording began. •End- date and time when downtime recording ends. •Duration - the time during which the monitoring object was in the specified geofence. •Mileage - allowed downtime in a geofence (set in the object settings on the \"Geofences\" tab in the downtime control field). •Downtime- downtime of the monitoring object in a geographic area. Equal to the difference between the start and end of the downtime, minus the allowable downtime. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"geozones-report","t":"Geozones Report","h":["Geozones Report","The report contains:","Vehicle","Mileage","Start","Stop","Geofence","Duration","Driver"],"x":"Geozones Report The report contains: •Vehicle-is a monitoring object for which a table with idle times is generated. •Mileage- distance traveled at a specified point in time. •Start- the start time of the event in the geofence. •Stop- end time of the event in the geofence. •Geofence- the name of the geofence. •Duration- the period of time spent in the geofence. •Driver- the name of the driver to the associated object in the system. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"geozones-report-2","t":"Geozones Report #2","h":["Geozones Report #2","The report contains:"],"x":"Geozones Report #2 Geographical zones report 2 is designed to display data in the form of a table indicating the name of the object, time, message about arrival (entry) and departure (exit) from a certain zone, duration of stay in the specified zone, with the ability to specify a time filter (set in the object settings on the \"Geofences\" tab in the downtime control field). The report contains: Object- a monitoring object for which a table with downtime is formed. Zone - the geographical zone in which the downtime was recorded. Start- date and time of entry of the monitoring object into the geographical area. End- date and time when the monitored object enters the geographical area. Duration- the time the monitoring object stays in the geofence, equal to the difference between the time of entry and exit from the geofence. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"geozones-report-3","t":"Geozones Report #3","h":["Geozones Report #3","The report contains:"],"x":"Geozones Report #3 Report on geozones #3 is designed to take into account the initial geofence of the object and the final one. The report contains: Date - the date of the event. •Vehicle type- vehicle type, set in the main tab in the object's properties; Plate number - monitoring object; Start time- start time of the trip; Start zone - initial geofence; Trip duration- duration of the trip. Trip distance- the distance traveled during the selected time period. Arrival time- time of arrival to the zone; End zone - end geozone. End time- time of departure from the zone; Duration in zone - duration of stay in the zone; Drum sensor work - sensor work; Drum sensor duration - the duration of the sensor; Drivers- the driver assigned to this monitoring object. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"geozones-report-4","t":"Geozones Report #4","h":["Geozones Report #4","The report contains:"],"x":"Geozones Report #4 Geozones report #4 is designed to track the time spent in a geofence. The report contains: Object- monitoring object; Zone - the zone where the object arrived; Entry- time of entry into the geofence; Exit- time of departure from the geofence; Duration - duration of stay in the geofence. Mileage (km)- distance traveled. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"geozones-report-6","t":"Geozones Report №6","h":["Geozones Report №6"],"x":"Geozones Report №6 A brief report about the trip in geofences, indicating the name of the zone, start, end and mileage for the specified period. Plate number - monitoring object; Zone - the zone in which the object arrived; •Start- date and time of the start of movement; Stop- date and time of the end of movement; Mileage (km) - distance traveled. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"geozones-summary","t":"Geozones summary","h":["Geozones summary"],"x":"Geozones summary This report is designed to record visits to geofences by objects. Vehicle - monitoring object; Geozone- geofence in which visits were recorded; First enter- the first entry of the object into the geofence; Last exit- last exit of the object from the geofence; Visits Count - the number of visits for the specified period of time; Summary Duration - total duration of being in the geofence. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"geozones-summary-report-2","t":"Geozones summary report #2","h":["Geozones summary report #2","The report contains:","Object","Zone","Beginning","End","Duration","Start","Stop","Coordinates"],"x":"Geozones summary report #2 The report contains: •Object - vehicle of the monitoring object; •Zone- geofence in which visits were recorded; •Beginning - the first entry of an object into the geofence; •End- the last exit of the object from the geofence; •Duration - duration of stay in the geofence; Miliage -mileage in km for the specified period. Parking time - parking time for the reporting period; • Start - zone at the beginning; •Stop - zone at the end; • Coordinates- Coordinates are used to determine a location using latitude and longitude. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"idling-outside-geozones","t":"Idling outside geozones","h":["Idling outside geozones","What the report shows"],"x":"Idling outside geozones This report displays data for vehicles/objects that were located outside geofences during the selected period. What the report shows Vehicle — the monitored vehicle or asset Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle at the time of the idle event Department — the group the vehicle belongs to Object Type — type of vehicle (passenger car, truck, trailer, etc.) Start — date and time when the idle period began Stop — date and time when the idle period ended Duration — total idle time (hh:mm:ss) Address — the idle location address (based on geocoder data). Displayed as a hyperlink — clicking it opens the map with the idle location point Coordinates — exact latitude and longitude of the location point"},{"s":"mileage-in-geozones","t":"Mileage in Geozones","h":["Mileage in Geozones","Movement in Geozones","Outside Geozones","What the report shows"],"x":"Mileage in Geozones The Mileage in Geozonesreport shows how many kilometers a vehicle traveled inside each selected geofence during the specified period, as well as how long it stayed there — both in motion and parked. When generating the report, select one or several geofences — the system will calculate mileage, driving time, and parking time inside the selected zones for the specified period. You can also use additional checkboxes to refine the calculation. Movement in Geozones This checkbox enables tracking of movement inside geofences. If the checkbox is selected: — the report will include and display only the mileage recorded inside the selected geofences — data outside these zones will not be included Outside Geozones This checkbox controls the display of mileage outside the selected geofences. If the checkbox is selected: — the system will additionally calculate mileage and time when the asset was outside the selected zones — the report will include separate rows showing distance and time outside the zones What the report shows Each row represents one vehicle within one geofence for the selected period. The report includes the following data: Date — the period for which the metrics are calculated Object — the vehicle the report is generated for Zone — the name of the geofence where movement was recorded Mileage (km) — the number of kilometers the vehicle traveled inside the zone Time — total time spent in the zone Driving time — time the vehicle was moving inside the geofence Parking duration — time the vehicle was stationary inside the zone At the bottom of the report, a Total row displays the aggregated values: total mileage across all selected geofences total time spent total driving time total parking duration"},{"s":"mileage-out-of-geozones","t":"Mileage out of Geozones","h":["Mileage out of Geozones"],"x":"Mileage out of Geozones The report displays data on an object with mileage outside geofences. If the object did not leave the geofence and it does not have mileage outside the geofence, such mileage values ​​will be cut off and will not be displayed in the report. The report contains: • Object- monitoring object; Duration - duration of the run; •Mileage- duration of mileage in km for the reporting period. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"overlaps-in-geozones","t":"Overlaps in Geozones","h":["Overlaps in Geozones"],"x":"Overlaps in Geozones This report is designed to display information on the intersections of objects in geozones. The report contains: Geozone- the name of the geofence; Count - the number of objects that crossed in the geofence; Vehicles List- list of objects that crossed in the geofence; Start- start time of crossing in the geofence; Stop- time of the end of the intersection in the geofence If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"parkings-in-geozones","t":"Parkings in Geozones","h":["Parkings in Geozones","The report contains:","Object","Zone","Start","Stop","Duration","Address"],"x":"Parkings in Geozones The report contains: •Object- vehicle of the monitoring object; • Zone- parking zone; • Start - time and date of parking start; •Stop- time and date of parking end; •Duration- duration of parking; • Address- idle address; If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"trips-with-geozones","t":"Trips with geozones","h":["Trips with geozones","The report contains:","Direction","Number of loads (number of trips)","Trip duration"],"x":"Trips with geozones The report contains: •Direction- direction of movement between geofences. •Number of loads (number of trips)- number of trips between zones. •Trip duration- the duration of a trip from one zone to another."},{"s":"zone-2-zone","t":"Zone 2 Zone","h":["Zone 2 Zone","What the report shows","Preparing geofences"],"x":"Zone 2 Zone The Zone 2 Zone report shows vehicle movements between selected geofences during a specified period. It helps you determine which geofence a vehicle departed from, which geofence it arrived at, how long the movement took, what distance was travelled, and how many stops occurred along the way. Before building the report, select: a period using From Date and To Date one or more vehicles in the Objects list the geofences whose movements should be included The highlighted row at the bottom of each section displays the totals for the corresponding period. What the report shows Vehicle — the name or number of the monitored vehicle Driver — the driver assigned to the vehicle Start Time — the date and time when the movement between geofences started End Time — the date and time when the movement between geofences ended Start Zone — the geofence from which the vehicle started moving End Zone — the geofence in which the vehicle completed its movement Number Stops — the number of stops recorded during the movement Drive Time — the total time spent driving between geofences Idling Time — the total duration of idling periods Stop Time — the total duration of stops Distance — the distance travelled by the vehicle between geofences Time In Zone — the amount of time the vehicle spent in a geofence Preparing geofences To build the report correctly, create the required geofences in the Geofences module. Then assign them to the vehicle in the object settings and set their type to Working. A work geofence is an area where a vehicle is expected to remain or perform its tasks, such as a warehouse, construction site, production facility, or route section."},{"s":"zone-list-report","t":"Zone List Report","h":["Zone List Report"],"x":"Zone List Report List of account geozones indicating addresses. Zone name - the name of the zone in the system; Zone shape- shape into zones; Zone address - geographical location of the zone; Zone group - name of the zone group. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"toll-roads-summary-report","t":"Toll Roads Summary Report","h":["Toll Roads Summary Report"],"x":"Toll Roads Summary Report This report works provided that toll roads are configured in the Geofences module. Toll Road Name- the name of the toll road. Entrance Time - entry time. Entrance Location - entry location. Exit Time - departure date and time. Exit Location - departure location. Price- price per kilometer. Costs may vary depending on several factors, including the type of vehicle, time of day, day of the week, and the specific toll road. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"notification","t":"Notification","h":["Notification"],"x":"Notification This section deals with reports related to the Notification module."},{"s":"notification-messages-report","t":"Notification Messages Report","h":["Notification Messages Report","The report contains:","Object","Driver","Time","Notification type","Notificationname","Message","Lat","Longitude -","Place","Speed"],"x":"Notification Messages Report The report contains: •Object - monitoring vehicle; •Driver- the driver assigned to the vehicle; •Time- date and time of the notification received; •Notification type - name of the reminder; •Notificationname- the name of the reminder; •Message- reminder messages; •Lat- is a geographic coordinate that determines the position of a point on the map. •Longitude - is a geographic coordinate that indicates the position of a point on the map. •Place- place of reminder; •Speed - the speed is recorded. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"notifications-detailed-report","t":"Notifications Detailed Report","h":["Notifications Detailed Report"],"x":"Notifications Detailed Report The detailed report contains data on drivers, type, date, time and speed at the time the notification was triggered. Driver- the driver assigned to the vehicle; Object - monitoring vehicle; Notificationname - name of the reminder; •Notification type- the name of the reminder; Message - reminder messages; Date - date of the event; Start time - date and time of notification status; End time - date and time of notification status; Number of occurrences - number of notifications; Event value - meaning; Speed limit - speed value for notification; Max speed- the maximum speed is recorded in the notification; Duration - duration. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"ptm","t":"PTM","h":["PTM"],"x":"PTM This section deals with reports related to the PTM module."},{"s":"summary-mileage-and-tasks","t":"Summary Mileage and Tasks","h":["Summary Mileage and Tasks"],"x":"Summary Mileage and Tasks The summary report displays data on the total mileage for applications. Driver applications are located in the PTM Module. Date - date and time of the created application; Veh - monitoring object; Folder- location of the object in the system; Distance - the distance value for applications and outside applications. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"matrix","t":"Matrix","h":["Matrix"],"x":"Matrix"},{"s":"geozones-drivers-matrix-1","t":"Geozones Drivers Matrix#1","h":["Geozones Drivers Matrix#1"],"x":"Geozones Drivers Matrix#1"},{"s":"geozones-drivers-matrix-2","t":"Geozones Drivers Matrix#2","h":["Geozones Drivers Matrix#2"],"x":"Geozones Drivers Matrix#2"},{"s":"geozones-drivers-motohours-matrix","t":"Geozones Drivers Motohours Matrix","h":["Geozones Drivers Motohours Matrix"],"x":"Geozones Drivers Motohours Matrix"},{"s":"passenger-flow-1","t":"Passenger Flow","h":["Passenger Flow"],"x":"Passenger Flow This section contains reports on passenger traffic."},{"s":"passenger-statistic","t":"Passenger statistic","h":["Passenger statistic"],"x":"Passenger statistic This report contains statistics on the number of incoming/outgoing passengers and revenue. Flights - the number of flights; Entered - number of entered passengers; Departed - the number of exited passengers; Revenue- revenue received from passengers."},{"s":"report-on-passenger-flow","t":"Report on Passenger Flow","h":["Report on Passenger Flow"],"x":"Report on Passenger Flow Passenger traffic report allows you to keep track of passengers who have entered, exited and their number in the bus cabin while moving along the route. Time - door opening time. Door - name of the door (front, back). Came in - the number of entered passengers. Came out - the number of exited passengers. In the cabin - the number of passengers in the cabin. Bus Stop- the name of the stop. Place - the place of boarding, disembarkation of passengers."},{"s":"report-on-passenger-flow-city-bus-","t":"Report on Passenger Flow City Bus","h":["Report on Passenger Flow City Bus"],"x":"Report on Passenger Flow City Bus The report on passenger traffic (city bus) allows you to keep track of passenger traffic and fares on the bus line with a fixed fare (city transport). Time- door opening time. Door - the number of the door through which the passengers entered. Came in- the number of entered passengers. Place- the place where the passengers entered. Price- fare. Sum- the amount of money at the box office for boarding passengers in a given place (equal to the price multiplied by the number of passengers). Debug -ts: event time in timeStamp, id: video index in the system."},{"s":"report-on-passenger-flow-city-bus-2","t":"Report on Passenger Flow City Bus #2","h":["Report on Passenger Flow City Bus #2"],"x":"Report on Passenger Flow City Bus #2 Passenger traffic report (city bus) allows you to keep track of passenger traffic and accounting of scheduled and unscheduled stops. Circle - a circle that the object makes along the route; Object - the object of observation; Planned - indicates whether the stop was planned or not; On the route - whether the bus is on the route or not; Date and time - date and time of stop; Entered - the number of passengers entered at the stop; Left - the number of passengers who got off at the bus stop; Bus stop name - stop name; Coordinates - stop coordinates."},{"s":"report-on-passenger-transportation","t":"Report on passenger transportation","h":["Report on passenger transportation"],"x":"Report on passenger transportation The report on passenger traffic shows data on the object address and number of trips."},{"s":"report-on-trips","t":"Report on Trips","h":["Report on Trips"],"x":"Report on Trips Report for the accounting of the trip of the vehicle. Works in conjunction with the flight module. •Object- selected object; Trip number - the route associated with the object; Trip name- the name of the route attached to the object; Bus stop coordinates - object stop coordinates; Bus stop name - the name of the stop where the object stopped; Date- the date when the stop was made; Reference time - reference information on route time. Bus stop time- the time of arrival of the object; Door open sensor - indicates whether the door was open; Passenger in - number of entered passengers; Passenger out - the number of exited passengers. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"route-control","t":"Route Control","h":["Route Control"],"x":"Route Control This section deals with reports related to the routes module."},{"s":"bus-routes-summary","t":"Bus routes summary","h":["Bus routes summary"],"x":"Bus routes summary This report is designed to display information about visits to specified stops (geofences). Date - the period of time for compiling the report table. Object– monitoring of the object for which the report table has been compiled. Between geozones - a pair of geofences, the object of observation rotated between them. First exit - the first exit from the first pair of geofences. Last entry- the last entry to the second of the geofence pairs. Number -number of flights between geofences. Total- the total amount of flight time. Total for the day - departure and last entry in geozones, the total amount of flight time without taking into account traffic from / to the ATP."},{"s":"routes-1","t":"Routes","h":["Routes"],"x":"Routes This section deals with reports related to the Routes module."},{"s":"implementation-of-road-maintenance-work","t":"Implementation of road maintenance work","h":["Implementation of road maintenance work"],"x":"Implementation of road maintenance work This report belongs to the Routes module. After linking the object to the route, build a report on Execution of road maintenance work. A recalculation is provided for this report. If the route is configured and linked to an object and it is further indicated that the report is empty, it will need to be recalculated. If the recalculation through the interface does not recalculate and the recalculation resets, you should contact the support service for a recalculation. Vehicle name - object in the system; Vehicle type - vehicle type in the system; Time start - date and time of start of work; TimeEnd- date and time of completion of work; Route name - route name; Route length - route length; Mileage (working cycle), km - duty cycle is a parameter that shows how long the machine can work without a break in km. Area (Duty Cycle) ㎡- the total area that is processed during one working cycle of the machine. Mileage (Idle cycle) km - the distance in km that the car travels without a load. Mileage on other roads km - mileage on other roads."},{"s":"routes-2","t":"Routes","h":["Routes"],"x":"Routes The report is designed to view the path of the object, taking into account the direction of movement relative to geographic coordinates, indicating the start, end and intermediate points, if any. Special equipment includes: cranes, concrete mixers, tractors and combines, and other construction equipment."},{"s":"special-vehicles-routes","t":"Special vehicles routes","h":["Special vehicles routes"],"x":"Special vehicles routes This report belongs to the Routes module. The report is designed to display data about work on special equipment. Vehicle - is an object in the system; •Route- a specified route for equipment; Begin- date and time of start of work; Address begin - the address of the start of the equipment work; •End- date and time of completion of work; Address end- the address of the end of the equipment work; Actual duration - duration value; Reference duration- is a standard or normative time value that is used as a sample for comparison and calibration. Route length- the length of the route traveled; Distance traveled- distance traveled outside the route; Number of violations- the number of violations on the route."},{"s":"route-gps","t":"Route GPS","h":["Route GPS","What the report shows","Viewing the route on the map","Parking events"],"x":"Route GPS The Route GPS report helps you monitor planned trips, including visits to checkpoints, driving time, trip duration, distance travelled, and unplanned stops. The report includes only routes on which an object made a trip during the selected period. Routes without trips are not displayed. The report is designed for round-trip routes consisting of three checkpoints: 1. The starting point — the company’s premises. 2. The intermediate point — the customer’s location. 3. The final point — the same company premises. What the report shows Vehicle No — the object’s vehicle number Customer Name — the name of the second-to-last checkpoint on the route Actual Start Time — the date and time when the trip started Time arrived at customer location — the date and time when the object arrived at the second checkpoint End Time — the date and time when the trip ended and the object reached the final checkpoint. For a round-trip route, the final checkpoint is the same as the starting point Actual Route — the route followed during the trip Trip Status — indicates whether the driver visited all planned checkpoints: Finished — all checkpoints were visited Unfinished — one or more checkpoints were not visited, or the trip has not yet been completed Time In Transit — the time the object spent moving during the trip Trip Duration — the time from the beginning to the end of the trip Distance Travelled — the distance covered by the object during the trip Number of Stops Outside Planned Geofence — the number of stops made outside the planned checkpoint geofences Number of planned stoppage — the number of checkpoints included in the planned route Viewing the route on the map To examine the object’s movements in more detail, expand the Map panel and select the required report row. The map displays the data for the selected day: trips made by the object, shown as lines with arrows indicating the direction of travel; parking events, shown with P markers; the planned route, shown as a dashed line; route checkpoints, shown as numbered markers. If the object made several trips during the day, their lines may overlap. A collapsible Tripspanel appears on the left side of the map. It shows the start time, date, and distance travelled for each trip. Select a trip to display only its route on the map. Select a checkpoint marker to view: the checkpoint name its coordinates its address Parking events The map displays all parking events associated with the selected day. The last parking event is also included if it started on the selected day and ended on the following day. For example, if the object parked at 5:00 PM and resumed moving at 9:00 AM the following day, the parking time is included in Trip Duration. Therefore, the trip duration may sometimes exceed 24 hours."},{"s":"tachograph","t":"Tachograph","h":["Tachograph"],"x":"Tachograph This section deals with reports related to the Tachograph module."},{"s":"activity-report-2","t":"Activity report","h":["Activity report"],"x":"Activity report A tachograph report is a document that contains information about the driver’s work, speed, route, time and work and rest schedule. Install the tachograph on the car and connect it to the GPS tracker. Insert the driver card into the tachograph and select the operating mode. Monitor changes in operating hours and comply with driving and rest time standards. Veh number - an object in the system Activity type - type of driver activity Start- date and time of the start of the activity; End - date and time of the end of the activity; Position - place of activity; End position - the address of the final path; Duration - information about the duration of activities; Driver- information about drivers. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"tags","t":"Tags","h":[],"x":"Tags This section contains reports on the RFID module."},{"s":"rfid-group-report","t":"RFID Group report","h":[],"x":"The RFID group report is designed to view interactions with tags by group. UID - label identifier; Name - label name; Group - the group to which the label is attached; Count - the number of interactions of the object with the label; Date - time when there was an interaction with the tag; Address - the address where there was an interaction with the labe If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"rfid-tags-report","t":"RFID Tags report","h":["RFID Tags report"],"x":"RFID Tags report Tags report - report on selected tags (tags from 'drivers' section) Object - the object that interacted with the label; Driver - the driver of the object; Time - time of interaction with the tag; UID - label identifier; Tag Name - tag name; Group - the group to which the label is attached; Place - the place where the interaction with the tag took place."},{"s":"temperature-reports","t":"Temperature reports","h":["Temperature reports"],"x":"Temperature reports This section contains reports for analyzing readings from temperature and humidity sensors installed on vehicles and other monitoring objects. They allow you to track changes, detect readings outside the permitted temperature range, and compare sensor data with the object’s location. The reports allow you to: view temperature and humidity readings for the selected period analyze changes using tables and charts monitor whether the temperature remains within the permitted range configure the interval at which data is displayed compare readings from multiple sensors The available data and parameters depend on the selected report, installed sensors, and object settings. To learn where to find and how to use temperature reports, watch the video “Where to find temperature reports” on YouTube. The video shows how to view temperature data in different formats, including a chart with normal and acceptable ranges, a table for the selected period, and a report with all temperature sensor readings."},{"s":"temperature-discrete-report","t":"Temperature discrete report","h":["Temperature discrete report","What the report shows"],"x":"Temperature discrete report The Temperature discrete reportprovides temperature and humidity sensors readings for monitoring objects at specified time intervals. It allows you to track temperature changes over the selected period. Before building the report, select the reporting period and objects, and enter a value in the Min time (sec) field. TheMin time (sec) field specifies the interval between the temperature readings displayed in the report. The value is entered in seconds. For example: 900 — displays temperature readings at 15-minute intervals 1800 — displays temperature readings at 30-minute intervals This setting helps adjust the level of detail and avoid displaying excessive readings. What the report shows Date — the date and time when the temperature reading was recorded Temperature or humidity sensor — the value recorded by the sensor"},{"s":"temperature-report","t":"Temperature report","h":["Temperature report","How to configure a temperature sensor"],"x":"Temperature report The Temperature Report shows how temperature sensor readings changed over the selected period. A separate chart is generated for each day and monitoring object. The horizontal axis shows time, while the vertical axes show temperature and humidity. Sensor readings are represented by lines of different colors. Horizontal gray lines indicate the limits of the permitted temperature range. When you hover over the chart, a tooltip displays the date, time, and sensor readings at the selected point. If you open the map panel and select a point on the chart, the object’s location at the corresponding time appears on the map. This allows you to compare temperature changes with the object’s route. How to configure a temperature sensor Configuring the temperature sensor correctly helps ensure that the report contains accurate equipment or vehicle data. Working ranges and filters allow the system to detect deviations and exclude invalid readings. A temperature sensor is added in the same way as any other sensor. For more information about adding sensors, see this article. To configure a temperature sensor: 1. Add a sensor 2. Enter its name and description 3. Select the sensor type from the drop-down list 4. Specify the data field. Select the parameter from which the system should retrieve temperature readings, such as engine_temp. Make sure you select the field in which the device transmits the actual temperature 5. Set the minimum and maximum thresholds. These thresholds define the sensor’s working range—the limits within which its readings are considered normal. If a reading falls outside this range, the system records a deviation. The range limits are shown as gray lines on the chart 6. Configure the minimum and maximum value filters. Values outside the specified range are considered invalid and are automatically excluded from reports. This allows the system to filter out sensor errors and incorrect readings"},{"s":"temperature-report-2","t":"Temperature report #2","h":["Temperature report #2","What the report shows"],"x":"Temperature report #2 The Temperature Report #2 contains temperature sensor readings for the selected period. It allows you to track temperature changes, check whether the readings are within the permitted range, and determine the object’s location and speed when each reading was recorded. What the report shows Object — the name of the monitoring object for which the report was generated Date — the date and time when the temperature reading was recorded Event — the event associated with the recorded reading Temp — the temperature value recorded by the sensor Temp Name — the name of the temperature sensor Temp Status — the status of the recorded temperature value Temp High — the upper limit of the permitted temperature range Temp Low — the lower limit of the permitted temperature range Position — the object’s coordinates when the reading was recorded Speed — the object’s speed when the reading was recorded Place — the object’s address when the reading was recorded"},{"s":"temperature-report-3","t":"Temperature report #3","h":["Temperature report #3","What the report shows"],"x":"Temperature report #3 The Temperature Report #3 contains readings from temperature and humidity sensors configured for monitoring objects over the selected period. What the report shows Object — the name of the monitoring object Date — the date and time when the reading was recorded Temperature or humidity sensor — the value recorded by the sensor"},{"s":"tempreture-interval-report","t":"Tempreture interval report","h":["Tempreture interval report"],"x":"Tempreture interval report The Temperature Interval reportcontains temperature and humidity sensor readings for the selected period. It allows you to track changes in the readings and determine the object’s speed and location when they were recorded. The report can be generated for only one object, for a period of no more than one day, and with an interval of less than 900 seconds. What the report shows Object — the name of the monitoring object Date & Time — the date and time when the readings were recorded Speed — the object’s speed when the readings were recorded Location — the object’s address when the readings were recorded Temperature — the value received from the temperature sensor Humidity — the value received from the humidity sensor"},{"s":"maintenance","t":"Maintenance","h":["Maintenance"],"x":"Maintenance This section deals with reports related to the Maintenance module."},{"s":"availability-keport","t":"Availability кeport","h":["Availability кeport","What the report shows"],"x":"Availability кeport The Availability Reportshows vehicle availability for the selected period based on recorded breakdowns. It allows you to determine the fleet size, number of unavailable vehicles, total downtime, and availability percentage. The Service module must be configured to generate the report. If vehicle breakdowns and downtime periods were recorded, the fleet availability percentage decreases. What the report shows Entity or User Dept — the entity or user group for which the values were calculated Fleet Size — the total number of vehicles in the group Vehicles Down — the number of vehicles that were unavailable during the selected period Total Days Down — the total duration of vehicle downtime % Age Availability — the percentage of time the vehicles were available for operation, based on recorded breakdowns"},{"s":"fleet-condition","t":"Fleet condition","h":["Fleet condition"],"x":"Fleet condition The park status report displays general information about the facility. Mark - brand of the object in the system; Model- model of an object in the system; Prod year - the year of release of the object in the system; Mileage - total mileage of the object; Driver name- driver name in the system; Drivers history - the history of drivers during their work; Next maintenance - data on completion of maintenance; •Comment- additional information."},{"s":"inspections-report","t":"Inspections report","h":["Inspections report"],"x":"Inspections report The Inspection report helps you track all completed and scheduled maintenance operations for each vehicle over a selected period. It provides a convenient way to monitor both performed work and upcoming services. When generating this report, you can choose which services to display: All types — shows all service events: both completed and scheduled Completed — displays only the work that has already been finished Scheduled — shows services that have been planned but not yet carried out The report includes: Vehicle — which vehicle was serviced Service name — e.g., oil change, brake check, cleaning Service date — when the maintenance was performed Report generate date Days difference — how many days have passed since the service Distance — distance at the time of service Status — completed, finished, or pending Condition — one-time or recurring service Next event — when the upcoming service is due Costs — split into parts and labor Comments — additional notes"},{"s":"maintenance-schedule","t":"Maintenance schedule","h":["Maintenance schedule"],"x":"Maintenance schedule The Maintenance plan report is designed to generate information about the date of the last maintenance and the date of the next maintenance at the monitored objects. Mark - brand of the object in the system; Model- model of an object in the system; Prod year - the year of release of the object in the system; Mileage - total mileage of the object; Driver name- driver name in the system; Monthly average mileage - monthly average mileage ; Standard between maintenanc - standard between maintenanc; Date last maintenance - date last maintenance ; № last maintenance - last maintenance ; Date next maintenance - date next maintenance"},{"s":"operating-costs","t":"Operating costs","h":["Operating costs"],"x":"Operating costs Operating costs are a set of expenses associated with maintaining the operability and reliability of equipment and vehicles. Technical specifications, operating mode, operating conditions and other factors should be taken into account. Mark - brand of the object in the system; Model- model of an object in the system; Prod year - the year of release of the object in the system; Driver name - driver name in the system; Mileage - total mileage of the object in km; Current mileage - vehicle mileage for the reporting period; Maintenance and repair- data on maintenance and work; Cost of fuel - fuel costs; Sum of costs - the amount of expenses."},{"s":"reminders-report","t":"Reminders report","h":["Reminders report","The report contains:","Name","Object","Type","Next due from now","Repeat every","Notify before","Status","Current location location."],"x":"Reminders report The report contains: •Name- name of the object in the system; • Object- an object in the system; •Type- type of reminder; •Next due from now- deadline from now on is the reminder deadline; •Repeat every- specified repetition value; •Notify before- preliminary reminder before the start of the main reminder; • Status - status, completed, expired; •Current location -location. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"service-reminder-report","t":"Service reminder report","h":["Service reminder report"],"x":"Service reminder report A service reminder report is a report that informs a customer that maintenance or repairs are required on their equipment or vehicle. The service reminder report can be sent to the client by mail, email, SMS, messenger or any other convenient method. Registration - the name of the object in the system; Description - name of the reminder; Servicetype- types of services; Every - number of reminder days; Warning period- remind the number of days; SMS notification- the number to which the notification was received; Email Notification - the email to which the notification was received. Screen Notification - status. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"vehicle-registry","t":"Vehicle registry","h":["Vehicle registry"],"x":"Vehicle registry The vehicle register is a register of vehicles. Vehicle number - object in the monitoring system; Mark - brand of the object; Model- object model; Prod year - the year of manufacture of the object; Vehicle type - type of transport in the system; Engine - engine number; Engine capacity - engine volume; VIN - body vin code; Vehicle license - license information; •License- date of issue of the vehicle passport."},{"s":"services-due-and-overdue-report","t":"Services Due and Overdue Report","h":["Services Due and Overdue Report"],"x":"Services Due and Overdue Report The report on overdue and expired services requires a configured Service module (services). Account Name – account name. Vehicle Registration – vehicle registration number. Service KM Due – mileage before service. Service Date – service date. Next Service KM – mileage until the next service. Days Overdue – days overdue. KM Overdue – mileage in excess of the permissible limit. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"vehicle-utilization","t":"Vehicle Utilization","h":["Vehicle Utilization"],"x":"Vehicle Utilization Vehicle Usage Report is a report that provides data on how and how much a vehicle is used over a specified period of time. The report includes mileage information. Entity or User Dept – an entity or user group. 2500km to 4500km – from 2500 km to 4500 km. Above 4500km – more than 4500 km. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"violations","t":"Violations","h":["Violations"],"x":"Violations This section covers reports related to Violations at monitoring objects."},{"s":"speeding-report","t":"Speeding Report","h":["Speeding Report","The report contains:","Date","Object -","Date and Time","Address","Coordinates","Duration","Distance, km","Speed control, km/h","Excess","Max. speed","Fuel consumption"],"x":"Speeding Report The report contains: •Date- the time period for which data is displayed. •Object -monitoring object for which data is displayed. •Date and Time- date and time when the speeding was recorded. •Address- the address of the monitoring object where the speeding was recorded. •Coordinates - latitude and longitude on Google Maps gps coordinates. •Duration- the time during which the monitored object exceeded the speed limit. •Distance, km - the distance over which the monitored object exceeded the speed. •Speed control, km/h - maximum permissible speed (set in the object settings). •Excess- the value by how much the speed was exceeded. •Max. speed- maximum recorded speed. •Fuel consumption- average fuel consumption per kilometer. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"speeding-report-2","t":"Speeding Report #2","h":["Speeding Report #2","The report contains:","Object","Driver","Date and time","Address","Duration (s) -","Distance","Speed control","Exceedings","Max. Speed","Precent","Fuel consumption (standard)"],"x":"Speeding Report #2 The report contains: •Object - object of monitoring; •Driver- a driver attached to an object; •Date and time - date and time of the violation; •Address- address of the violation; •Duration (s) -duration of the violation in seconds; •Distance - distance traveled in violation of the speed limit; •Speed control- speed limit set for an object; •Exceedings - how much the limit was exceeded; •Max. Speed - the maximum speed of the object at the time of the violation. •Precent - expression of the value of exceeding the speed limit as a percentage. • Fuel consumption (standard)- the average value of fuel consumption per kilometer. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"speed-violations-report","t":"Speed Violations Report","h":["Speed Violations Report","The report contains:","Vehicle","Driver","Time","Duration","Distance","Speed control -","Max. speed","Speeding","Percentage -","Location","Road type"],"x":"Speed Violations Report The report contains: • Vehicle- object of monitoring; •Driver- the driver assigned to this object for the duration of the movement. •Time - time and date of the event; •Duration - duration of the event in seconds; •Distance - distance traveled while speeding in km; •Speed control -speed limit that has been exceeded; •Max. speed - the speed of the object at the moment of exceeding; •Speeding - the value by how much the speed limit was exceeded; •Percentage -value by how much the speed limit was exceeded in percent; •Location - the location of the speeding. •Road type- road type. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon, when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"speed-violations-report-3","t":"Speed Violations Report #3","h":["Speed Violations Report #3"],"x":"Speed Violations Report #3 The report is intended to display data about the violation. Object - monitoring object in the system; Time - time and date of the violation; Duration - duration of the violation; Distance (km)- distance traveled by the object in km; Speed limit (km/h)- speed limit that was exceeded; Speeding - how much the limit was exceeded; Max. speed - the maximum speed of the object; Place - the location of the violation. Road type - defines the characteristics and functions of the road surface that affect vehicle traffic conditions. Types of highways, expressways, non-expressways. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon , when pressed, a menu opens where you can disable / enable certain fields."},{"s":"summary-speed-violation-report","t":"Summary speed violation report","h":["Summary speed violation report","Speed limit"],"x":"Summary speed violation report The Summary speed violation report shows which vehicles exceeded the speed limit you set during the selected period. This report helps you monitor compliance with speed regulations. Before using this report, make sure to set a speed limit for each vehicle: 1. Open the object card for the desired vehicle 2. Go to the Settings tab 3. In the Misc section, enter a value in the Speed limit, km/h field. For example, if you set 90 km/h, any trip above this speed will be considered a violation. Speed limit If no threshold is set, speeding violations will not be recorded, and the report may be empty. Once you set the speed threshold, the system will automatically track all trips and flag any speeding incidents. The report will show: The total number of vehicles in the group How many vehicles exceeded the speed limit Speeding penalties — points for each speeding violation Total time spent driving over the speed limit Total time each vehicle was above the set threshold The highest recorded speed The average of the maximum speeds across all trips"},{"s":"car-rent-invoice","t":"Car Rent Invoice","h":["Car Rent Invoice"],"x":"Car Rent Invoice The report is designed to display information about the lease of an object, indicating the agreement, amount and type of lease. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon; when clicked, a menu will open where you can disable/enable certain fields."},{"s":"billing-report","t":"Billing Report","h":["Billing Report"],"x":"Billing Report A configured Rent Car module is required. The module must have additional fields configured for the report, which are entered in the Administrative part.  additional_fields = [{\"xtype\": \"combo\",\"name\":\"rent_type\",\"fieldLabel\": \"Rental Type\",\"displayField\": \"name\", \"valueField\": \"name\",\"store\": {\"data\": [{\"name\": \"FML\"}, {\"name\": \"MM\"}, {\"name\": \"Adhoc\"}], \"fields\": [\"name\"]}}] Entity or User Dept – User object or group. Contract – contract. MM – Maintenance Management. FML – Fleet Management Logistics. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon; when clicked, a menu will open where you can disable/enable certain fields."},{"s":"licenses-due","t":"Licenses Due","h":["Licenses Due"],"x":"Licenses Due A configured Rent Car module is required. The module must have additional fields configured for the report, which are entered in the Administrative part.  additional_fields = [{\"xtype\": \"combo\",\"name\":\"rent_type\",\"fieldLabel\": \"Rental Type\",\"displayField\": \"name\", \"valueField\": \"name\",\"store\": {\"data\": [{\"name\": \"FML\"}, {\"name\": \"MM\"}, {\"name\": \"Adhoc\"}], \"fields\": [\"name\"]}}] User object or group. License Due Date – license expiration date. Days Until Due Date – days left until expiration. Contract. MM – Maintenance Management. FML – Fleet Management Logistics. If necessary, you can disable certain parts of the report by clicking on the icon; when clicked, a menu will open where you can disable/enable certain fields."},{"s":"report-petroapp-monthly-","t":"Report PetroApp Monthly","h":["Report PetroApp Monthly"],"x":"Report PetroApp Monthly The main task of the report is to calculate the differences in fuel consumption compared to factory indicators. PetroApp Monthly- the report is built only by months. Mileage is the mileage at the time of the transaction. Transaction is the ID of the transaction. Fuel price - cost. Quantity is the volume in liters. Effective mileage (KM) is the total mileage for the period calculated by the formula \"Last transaction mileage\" minus \"First transaction mileage\". Effective consumption (Liters) - the sum of all liters from transactions. 1. 03/07/2021 - 20 liters 2. 03/08/2021 - 30 liters 3. 03/09/2021 - 25 liters 4. 03/10/2021 - 40 liters Only 1,2,3 are included in the report. Average factory consumption (L/100KM) - factory indicator. Taken from the object settings. Consumption result - percentage difference. Effective consumption Rate (L/100KM) - calculation of consumption per 100 km. An abbreviated PetroApp Monthly report with summary data. To work proper, need to make sure tank size is set for the vehicle and fuel consumption."},{"s":"report-builder","t":"Report builder","h":["Report builder"],"x":"Report builder The Report builder is a tool that lets you create your own reports in the monitoring system to match your specific needs. It helps you decide: what data to show how to display it and which sections the report should include You configure the report format once, save it, and then use it again whenever you need the same information in a convenient format."},{"s":"how-to-configure-access-to-reports","t":"How to configure access to reports","h":["How to configure access to reports"],"x":"How to configure access to reports Access to reports created in the Report builder is controlled through user permissions. 1. In the account configuration, go to the Staff and groups tab 2. Open the user card by double-clicking the user 3. In the Rights tab, check the box for the Report builder permission 4. Click Save You can also choose which specific reports the user can access: 1. In the Staff and groupstab, select the user from the list and click the icon 2. A window with a list of reports will open 3. Check the boxes next to the reports you want to grant this user access to"},{"s":"how-to-use-the-report-builder","t":"How to use the Report builder","h":["How to use the Report builder","How to create a report","Example report","How to build a report","Report grouping","Builder interface overview","Report configuration","Splitting data by hour","Splitting data by shifts","Displaying a single shift","Splitting a shift at midnight","Filtering data","Merging rows","Importing and exporting reports","How to export a report","How to import a report"],"x":"How to use the Report builder The Report builder lets you create your own report layout with the columns, filters, and calculation logic you need. In this article, you’ll learn: How to create a report — step-by-step setup of a report form How to build a report — launch a report from the Builder Report grouping — how to group data Interface overview — what the Builder includes Report configuration — how to additionally configure the report Import and export — how to move reports between accounts How to create a report 1. Go to the Reports section 2. Find the Builder button 3. Click it — the Report Builder window will open In the builder: 4. Click Add 5. Select the report type 6. In the Name field, enter the report name 7. In the Columns section, click and add the required fields 8. Set up the column data, formulas, filters, and other parameters 9. If needed, fill in the Configuration field 10. Click Save After saving, the report will be available in the Reports section. Example report For example, you can create an Idle – Summary Table report. Report type: Summary table Added columns: Vehicle — the object the report is based on Idle time — the duration of idling Total idle events — the number of events Fuel at beginning — fuel level at the beginning Fuel at end — fuel level at the end In the Configuration field, you can set a filter so the report includes only the data you need. For example, to show only objects with idle events: {\"filter\":[{\"key\":\"total_idle_events_number\",\"rule\":\">\",\"value\":0}]} Once the report is created and saved in the Builder, it becomes available in the Reports section. How to build a report 1. Go to the Reports section 2. Select the object by checking it in the list 3. Choose the report you created in the Builder 4. Specify the time period and any additional parameters 5. Click Build report The system will generate the report using the selected layout. You can then: view and analyze the report export it to CSV, Excel, or PDF — print it — Report grouping You can organize the data in a report created with the Report builder into separate sections. Depending on the report type, the following grouping options may be available: by drivers by dates by objects by object groups To configure grouping: 1. Go to Reports 2. Select a report created with the Report builder 3. Select the required option from the Groupinglist 4. Select the objects and, if necessary, other data for the report 5. Click Build report For example, when data is grouped by object groups, a separate section containing the corresponding data is created for each selected group. Builder interface overview On the left, there is the Reportspanel with the list of reports already created. It includes the list of saved reports and management buttons: create a new report import or export a report refresh the list On the right, there is the settings area for the selected report: Type — the report builder type Name — the report name Sorting — the order in which data is displayed Desc — descending sort order Columns — report structure settings Configuration — additional report settings in JSON format. This lets you control how data is displayed in the report. Report configuration The Configuration field is used for additional report settings. Parameters are specified in JSON format and determine how the report data is processed and displayed. Splitting data by hour To divide the report data into hourly intervals, enter: {\"split\": \"hour\"} When the report is generated, the data for each hour is displayed in a separate section. Splitting data by shifts Report data can be divided into work shifts. The system creates a separate interval for each shift and calculates metrics using only the data associated with that interval. To configure the split, specify the start and end times of each shift in the split parameter, for example: { \"split\": [\"08-20\", \"20-08\"] } This example defines two shifts: from 08:00 to 20:00 from 20:00 to 08:00 on the following day If the showparameter is not specified, the report displays all configured shifts. In this example, the Forklift Operation Timeline report is divided into day and night shifts. Each shift is displayed in a separate section containing the object, the interval start and end times, and the equipment operation metrics. The night shift is displayed as one continuous interval from 20:00 on the current day to 08:00 on the following day. Because the selected report period starts at 00:00, the beginning of the report also displays the remaining part of the night shift, from 00:00 to 08:00. Displaying a single shift To include only one of the configured shifts in the report, add the show parameter: { \"split\": [\"08-20\", \"20-08\"], \"show\": 1 } The parameter value corresponds to the shift’s position in the list: show: 1— display the first shift, from 08:00 to 20:00 show: 2— display the second shift, from 20:00 to 08:00 In this example, the show: 1 parameter is specified, so the report contains only day shifts from 08:00 to 20:00. A separate section is created for each day. Night shifts are not displayed in the report. Splitting a shift at midnight By default, a shift from 20:00 to 08:00 is treated as one continuous interval. To split it at the calendar-day boundary, add the split_days parameter: { \"split\": [\"08-20\", \"20-08\"], \"split_days\": 1 } The night shift is divided into two intervals: from 20:00 to 00:00 from 00:00 to 08:00 The day shift remains a single section from 08:00 to 20:00. The show parameter is not specified because the report includes both day and night shifts. All parameters can be used together: { \"split\": [\"08-20\", \"20-08\"], \"show\": 1, \"split_days\": 1 } In this example, the report displays only the first shift, while shifts that cross midnight are divided by calendar day. Filtering data You can use the configuration to specify which data should be included in the report. For example, the following columns can be added to the Idle – Summary Table report: Vehicle Idle time Total number of idle events Fuel at start Fuel at end To include only objects with at least one recorded idle event, enter: {\"filter\":[{\"key\":\"total_idle_events_number\",\"rule\":\">\",\"value\":0}]} As a result, rows in which Total number of idle events equals zero are not displayed. Merging rows To merge multiple rows from the same split period into a single summary row, enter: {\"merge\": true} The merged row displays the values of the configured columns, while the intermediate rows are hidden. Configure the columns to include in the report and save the changes. Row merging example In this example, the report is grouped by drivers and split by day. A separate section is created for each driver, and the driver’s data for each day is merged into a single row. The row displays the vehicle, date, first trip start time, last trip end time, total driving time, total idle time, and total mileage for the day. Importing and exporting reports Reports created in the Builder can be transferred between accounts. Use the Export and Import buttons for this. How to export a report 1. Click Export 2. Choose a location on your device 3. Save the file How to import a report 1. Click Import 2. In the window that opens, if needed, enable Remove existing reports if you want to delete existing reports in the Builder. 3. Select the report file 4. ClickImport The report will be uploaded to the account."},{"s":"report-types-in-the-report-builder","t":"Report types in the Report builder","h":["Report types in the Report builder","Available report types","How report data is generated","Example 1: Report type","Example 2: Report type","Report type examples","Summary table","Activity","Graph","Eco events","Trips table","Geofence table","Current state table","Sensor activity","Sensor events","Connection loss","Notifications","Cards","Driver behavior","Fleet summary","RFID tags","Filling and draining events","PTM"],"x":"Report types in the Report builder When you create a report, you first choose the report builder type. This type determines: which data will be used, which fields will be available in the Columns section, and what the final report will look like. Available report types The system supports the following report types: Summary table — summary data: totals, sums, event counts, and other aggregated values Activity — activity of an object or sensors over a selected period Eco events — events related to eco-friendly and non-eco-friendly driving, as well as fuel consumption Graph — data visualization in chart form Trips table — a list of trips with parameters such as time, mileage, start, and end Geofence table — geofence data: entries, exits, and time spent in a zone Current state — the object’s status at the time the report is generated Sensor activity — sensor activity data for a selected period Sensor events — sensor triggers, state changes, and threshold events Connection loss — information about communication interruptions Notifications — a list of system notifications Cards — fuel card and transaction data Driver behavior — events related to driving style Fleet summary — overall information about all objects RFID tags — RFID data Filling and draining events — fuel-related operations PTM — specialized reports for the PTM module How report data is generated 1. You select a report type 2. The system loads the available categories and fields for that type 3. In the Columns section, you choose the data you need 4. The report shows only the selected columns The set of available columns depends on the report type you choose. In the Builder, there are: universal (basic) fields — available in most report types specialized categories — available only for certain types Example 1: Report type Current state This type provides data about the current state of a vehicle. In addition to basic data such as make, model, and speed, the Services category becomes available, where you can add maintenance fields such as: Last TO motohours — last service motohours Next TO motohours — next service motohours Mh until next TO — motohours until the next service Last TO date — date of the last service Last TO number — number of the last service Next TO date — date of the next service Last TO mileage — mileage at the last service These fields help you track the current condition and maintenance schedule of the vehicle. Example 2: Report type Cards For the Cardsreport type, fuel card and transaction data is available. In the Columns section, the Card Transactionscategory appears, where you can select fields such as: Transaction Type — type of transaction Transaction Location — location Transaction Time — time Card Number — card number Distance Previous-Current — distance between values Current — current value Transaction Amount — amount This report type is used to analyze card transactions. Report type examples The Builder includes several report types, and each one is designed for a specific task. Below is a short description of each one to help you choose the right type. Summary table A Summary table helps you get summary data for objects over a selected period. You can configure: which columns to include in the report which metric to use for the summary how to sort the data When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — row order Summary — the main metric used for the summary Desc — descending order Columns — which data to show Configuration — additional settings Example of a report built in the Builder The Summary field defines the main metric used to build the report. The Sorting field defines the row order. For example, if you sort by Vehicle, the final report will be sorted by vehicles. If Desc is enabled, the data will be sorted in descending order. Activity The Activity report shows the activity of objects or drivers over a period. With this report, you can see: when an event occurred which object it belongs to where it was recorded parameter values The report can include values from discrete, DIP, and selector sensors. To select a sensor, choose its assigned tag when configuring the column. When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters In the example shown in the screenshot, the report Overspeed CAN general is created — a speeding report based on CAN data. The Configuration field includes a filter, so only events where the sensor value is greater than 85 are included: {\"filter\": [ { \"key\": \"sensor\", \"rule\": \">\", \"value\": 85 } ]} Graph A Graph report displays data visually in the form of charts. It is used to analyze how indicators change over time, such as speed, fuel consumption, temperature, mileage, equipment activity, and other parameters. You can add several data groups: Speed — vehicle speed chart ODO — mileage chart Fuel — fuel level and consumption Temperature — temperature from selected sensors Special sensors — special sensor data Equipment — data from connected equipment Eco events — environmental events and violations, such as hard braking, sharp turns, collisions, unfastened seat belt, etc. For each group, you can: turn it on or off, choose specific sensors or event types, enable or disable Show axis — display a separate Y-axis for that group, enable Single axis — use one shared Y-axis. For Eco events, you can also use: Show per hour — display the number of events per hour Show duration — display event duration After you save and generate the report in Reports, the system displays an interactive graph: the X-axis shows time the Y-axis shows the selected values, and there can be multiple Y-axes each parameter is shown in a different color hovering over the chart shows a tooltip with the exact time and all values at that point the legend at the bottom shows which line corresponds to which parameter the graph can be zoomed, moved, and analyzed in specific time ranges Eco events The Eco eventsreport type is used for reports about events related to: eco-friendly or non-eco-friendly driving style fuel consumption environmental driving behavior This report helps you analyze when and where eco events occurred, and what type of event was recorded. When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Summarize — summary mode Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters Trips table The Trips table report type is used to view vehicle trips for a selected period. When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters After the report is generated, the system shows a table of trips for the selected period. Geofence table Geofences table is a report type for analyzing geofence visits and equipment operation within geofences. The report can display entries and exits, time spent in a geofence, sensor operation periods, and other related data. Configure the following parameters when creating the report: Name — enter the report name Rows — select how report rows are generated: Geofence visits — each row represents one geofence visit Equipment work periods — each row represents a separate operation period of the selected sensor within a geofence Sort — select the field used to sort the data Desc — enable descending sort order Columns — configure the data to display in the report Configuration — specify additional parameters and filters if necessary The available data and columns may depend on the selected row generation mode. To build a report using a template from the Builder: 1. Go to the Reports section 2. Select the report type 3. Select the required objects 4. Choose the geofences you need 5. Set the report period and click Build report The system will then generate a table for the selected geofences. Current state table The Current statetable report type shows the current status of an object at the moment the report is generated. The example shown displays service data, but depending on the report configuration, the available data may be different. When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters Sensor activity The Sensor activity report type shows sensor activity data for a selected period. When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sensor groups — one or more sensor groups to include in the report Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters The Sensor groups field lets you limit the report to only the sensors you need. Sensor events The Sensor events report type shows events recorded by sensors: triggers, state changes, threshold events, and more. When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sensor groups — one or more sensor groups used to build the report Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters Connection loss This report type lets you build a custom connection loss report by choosing the columns you need and setting your own filter for outage duration. When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters Notifications The Notifications report type is used to view events created by the notification system. This report helps you analyze: which notifications were generated when they happened which object they belong to where the event was recorded which delivery channel was used When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Notifications Type — notification types to include in the report Filter by — filter by notification channel or delivery method Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Colors — color display Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters The Notifications Type field lets you choose which notification types to include in the report: Speed Ignition events Ignition activity Sensor events Sensor activity Sensor value change Sensor faults Device issues Temperature Humidity Connection loss Mileage Movement Parking Geofences Fuel level Scheduler RFID tags Composite notifications You can select multiple notification types. In that case, the report will show all selected types. The Filter by field is used to filter notifications by delivery method: SMS — text message to a phone Push (Mobile App) — notification in the mobile app Email — email message Alert — popup notification in the system interface Webhook — data sent to an external service Command to device — command sent to the tracker Telegram — notification via Telegram bot In the Columns section, you can choose what information is shown in the report, for example: Vehicle — the object the notification relates to Notification Type — type of notification Notification Time — when the event occurred Notification Name — notification name Notification Location — where the notification was recorded After the report is generated, the system displays a list of notifications for the selected period. Cards Cards is a report type used to view information about fuel cards and their transactions. When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters Driver behavior This report type is used to analyze driving style. It helps you track: harsh acceleration harsh braking speeding other violations safety and fuel-efficiency indicators When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters Fleet summary This report type is used to get summary information for the entire fleet. When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters RFID tags RFID tags is a report type used to view data related to RFID tags. This report helps you analyze, for example: which RFID tags were detected when the event happened where it happened which device or object the record belongs to When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters Filling and draining events Fuel fill-up and drain events is a report type used to view fuel-related operations. This report helps you control fuel operations and analyze: when refueling or draining happened how much fuel was added or removed where the event occurred what the fuel values were at the beginning and at the end When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters PTM This is a specialized report type for data from the PTM module. It is used to view and analyze PTM-related records, such as: task number client planned dates mileage When creating this type of report, you can set: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to order the data Desc — descending order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters"},{"s":"how-to-configure-report-columns","t":"How to configure report columns","h":["How to configure report columns","Column settings","Example of totals in a mileage report","How to delete a column","How to configure the columns table","How to use formulas in report columns","Formula for an existing field","Custom formula"],"x":"How to configure report columns To make sure a report shows the data you need, you must add and configure columns in the Report Builder. Each column defines: what information is displayed in the report, and how that information is processed. How to add a column: 1. In the Report builder, click the button in the Columns section 2. Fill in the settings for the new column Column settings 1. Name Enter the column name that will appear in the report header. 2. Data Select the data field that will be used in this column from the drop-down list. 3. Sensor groups Select the sensor group whose values you want to display in the column. When the report is generated, the system automatically displays the values of the sensors from the selected group. Reports support values from discrete, DIP, and selector sensors. 4. Total Functions This setting defines how the system calculates totals in the bottom row of the table. None No total is calculated for the column Sum Adds up all values in the column Average Calculates the arithmetic mean Max Shows the highest value in the column Min Shows the lowest value in the column Formula Lets you define a custom calculation formula You can select more than one function for a single column. Example of totals in a mileage report CAN mileage — Sum, Max GPS mileage — Min CAN mileage at start — Sum CAN mileage at end — Average In the generated report, total values will appear in the bottom row based on the selected functions. 5. Suffix Specify the text that will be displayed after the value, such as km, l, km/h, °C, or hours. 6. Formula Lets you use mathematical expressions, for example:(%odometer_end% - %odometer_begin%) / 100. 7. Filter You can filter report rows by any column containing numerical values. In the Filter field, enter one of the following conditions: > X — the value is greater than X < X — the value is less than X = X — the value is equal to X For example, if you enter > 25 for the Speed column, the report will display only events recorded at speeds above 25 km/h. 8. Render function Lets you apply special formatting to the value, such as rounding, date conversion, unit conversion, and more. 9. Hide Check this box if the column is needed only for calculations, for example in a formula, but should not be shown in the final report. How to delete a column Click the column and then click the Delete button . How to configure the columns table You can set up the columns table the way you prefer and choose which parameter columns should be displayed. 1. In the header of any column, click the arrow icon . A menu with column settings will appear: sort data in ascending or descending order choose which columns to display 2. To configure which columns are shown: click Columns — a list of available parameters will open check the boxes next to the parameters you want to see in the table column How to use formulas in report columns The Report builder supports two ways of adding formulas: 1. Formula for an existing field — apply a formula to an already existing data field 2. Custom formula — create a new column based on a formula that uses multiple fields Formula for an existing field Use this when you want to modify data in an existing field. How to set it up: 1. Add a column with an existing data field, for example Idle time 2. In the Formula field, enter the expression that will be applied to this field Example In the Fuel Consumption While Driving report, the Idle time column uses the formula: =%te%-%ts%. This expression calculates the difference between the end time (te) and the start time (ts) of the idle period. Custom formula Use this when you want to create a new column based on several data fields. How to set it up: 1. Add a new column 2. In the Datafield, select Formula 3. In the Formula field, enter an expression using the data field identifiers Example In the Fuel Consumption While Driving report, the Fuel consumption while driving column is configured like this: Data: Formula Formula: =%fuel%/((%te%-%ts%)/60/60) This formula calculates fuel consumption per hour: %fuel% — fuel consumption from the sensor (%te%-%ts%)/60/60 — time in hours, calculated from the difference between the end and start times of the event, divided by 3600 seconds After you save the report, the system automatically calculates the value for each row using the specified formula. For more information about formula syntax, see the separatearticle."},{"s":"data-columns-for-different-report-types","t":"Data columns for different report types","h":["Data columns for different report types","Example: report type RFID tags","List of available columns","Main parameters","Trips","Fuel","Filling and draining events","Equipment","Sensors","Temperature","Places","Speed","Violation monitoring","Geofences","Notifications","Eco driving","Services","Working hours","Driver fields","PTM","RFID tags","Fuel card transactions","Connection loss"],"x":"Data columns for different report types When creating a report, you first choose the report type, and then add the data columns. The available data depends on the selected report type: 1. You choose a report type 2. The system automatically loads the available data categories and fields 3. In the Columns section, you add the required data 4. When the report is generated, only the selected columns are displayed Example: report type RFID tags If you select the RFID tags report type, RFID-related data becomes available. This report helps you analyze: which RFID tags were detected when the event happened where it happened and which object or device the record belongs to When creating a report, you specify the main settings: Name — report name Sorting — the field used to sort the data Desc — descending sort order Columns — the data to display Configuration — additional parameters and filters After that, you can start adding columns: 1. Click the Add button in the Columns section 2. In the Data field, select the required parameter from the list The available fields depend on the selected report type. Example of available RFID data In addition to common data such as vehicle, device, ID, group, and so on, specialized fields will also be available: RFID Time — time when the tag was read RFID Name — tag name, for example an employee name or an object name RFID Location — reading location RFID UID — unique tag ID RFID Group — tag group Add the required columns to build the report structure. List of available columns Below is a structured list of all fields that can be selected in the Data field when creating a column. Main parameters Index number Unique object identifier Last speed Last recorded speed of the object Group Object group Company name Name of the company that owns the object Make Vehicle make Model Vehicle model Period duration Length of the selected report period Time zone Object time zone Created at Object creation time in the system Driver’s license expiry date License expiration date Max speed Maximum allowed speed of the object Engine number Vehicle engine number VIN Vehicle identification number Fuel type Fuel type used by the object Production year Year of manufacture Vehicle type Type of vehicle Device ID Monitoring device identifier Trailer Trailer information, if connected Cost km Cost per kilometer Empty string Blank row for report formatting Last event Last event recorded for the object Object tags Tags assigned to the object Connection lost count Information about connection loss for the object Connection loss duration Total duration of connection loss during the selected period Formula Formula used to calculate object data Geofence Geofence where the object is located Last movement time Time of the object’s last movement Speed control Speed control settings for the object Odometr current state Odometr current state Last location Last known location of the object Last update time Time when the object data was last updated in the system Start date Report period start date End date Report period end date Trips First trip start place Location where the object’s first trip in the selected report period started Total duration — of overtime trips summary Total duration of all overtime trips during the period Number of trips — of overtime trips summary Total number of overtime trips during the period Total idle events number Number of idle periods recorded during the period Trips parking time Total time spent parked during trips Trip count Total number of trips during the period Parking count Number of parking events during the period Max Parking Time Duration of the longest parking event during the period Max Parking Location Location where the longest parking event was recorded First trip start time Start time of the first trip in the selected period First trip end time End time of the first trip in the selected period First trip duration Duration of the first trip First trip mileage Distance covered in the first trip First trip end place Location where the first trip ended Last trip start time Start time of the last trip in the selected period Last trip end time End time of the last trip in the selected period Last trip duration Duration of the last trip Last trip mileage Distance covered in the last trip Last trip start place Location where the last trip started Active days Number of days when the object moved Trips start to end time Interval from the start of the first trip to the end of the last trip in the selected period Trips with tags count Number of trips with tags assigned Odometer CAN at beginning Odometer/mileage value from CAN at the beginning of the period Odometer CAN at end Odometer/mileage value from CAN at the end of the period CAN mileage Mileage for the period calculated from CAN data Odometer GPS at beginning GPS mileage at the beginning of the period Odometer GPS at end ЗGPS mileage at the end of the period Mileage GPS Mileage for the period calculated from GPS data Mileage Total mileage for the period Engine hours Number of engine hours for the period Odometer at beginning Odometer value at the start of the period Odometer at end Odometer value at the end of the period Duration Total trip duration for the period Trips moving time Total time the object was moving during trips Idle time Total idle time for the period Fuel Fuel cons on idle Amount of fuel consumed while the engine was running and the object was not moving CAN fuel consumption Fuel consumption calculated from the vehicle CAN bus data Fuel price Fuel price used to calculate consumption or refueling cost Fuel consumption on norm Fuel consumption calculated using the configured norm Fuel consumption on norm CAN Normative fuel consumption calculated using CAN data Fuel at beginning Amount of fuel at the beginning of the selected period Fuel at end Amount of fuel at the end of the selected period Refuels volume Total volume of all refuelings during the period Refuels count Number of refueling events during the period Drain Offs volume Total amount of fuel drained during the period Drain Offs count Number of fuel drain events during the period Fuel consumption on sensor Fuel consumption calculated from the fuel level sensor Fuel cards Fuel card data Filling and draining events Filling type Type of refueling Filling liters Amount of fuel added in one refueling event Filling location Address where refueling occurred Filling start fuel level Amount of fuel in the tank at the start of refueling Filling start time Date and time when the refueling event started Filling end time Date and time when the refueling event ended Fuel price Cost of fuel per unit Fuel at beginning Fuel amount at the beginning of the selected report period Fuel end fuel level Fuel amount at the end of the selected report period Sensor Fuel sensor Start place Location where the fuel event started Equipment Machine hours at beginning Engine hours at the beginning of the selected period Machine hours at end Engine hours at the end of the selected period Equipment work Total equipment operating time for the period Equipment count How many times the equipment sensor triggered or switched during the period Machine hours Total engine hours for the period Equipment last end Date and time when the equipment sensor was last switched off Event sensor count Number of event sensor triggers during the period Ignition operation Total time the ignition was on during the selected period Equipment first start Date and time when the equipment sensor was first activated in the period Sensors Sensor name Display name of the sensor in the system Sensor description Sensor description from the sensor settings Installed sensors List of sensors installed on the object Start Start time of the sensor-related interval End End time of the sensor-related interval Sensor start value First recorded sensor value for the selected period Sensor stop value Last recorded sensor value for the selected period Sensor difference Difference between the final and initial values for the period Sensor minimum Minimum sensor value for the selected period Sensor maximum Maximum sensor value for the selected period Sensor average Average sensor value for the selected period Sensor total Total sensor value for the period Sensor Problems Number of errors, failures, or invalid sensor readings recorded during the period Temperature Temperature sensor name Name of the temperature sensor Temperature sensor description Description of the sensor from its settings Temp sensor start value Temperature at the beginning of the selected period Temp sensor stop value Temperature at the end of the selected period Temp sensor minimum Lowest temperature during the period Temp sensor maximum Highest temperature during the period Temp sensor average Average temperature for the selected period Temp sensor difference Difference between temperature values during the period, usually between the initial and final values Places Start place Location where the vehicle was at the beginning of the period Stop place Location where the object was stopped Speed Max Speeding Time Duration of the longest speeding event during the period Over Speed > 30 Speeding by more than 30 Over Speed 21–30 Speeding by 21–30 Over Speed 11–20 Speeding by 11–20 Over Speed 1–10 Speeding by 1–10 Overspeed from the object settings Speeding events calculated according to the speed limit set in the object settings Speeding time Total time the object was moving while speeding Max Speeding Maximum speed Avg Speed Average speed Total speeding Summary metric for all speeding events during the period Mileage mileage Location Location where the speeding event was recorded Violation monitoring Max Speeding Time Duration of the longest speeding event during the period Over Speed Count 1-10 Number of speeding events by 1–10 Over Speed Count 11-20 Number of speeding events by 11–20 Over Speed Count 21-30 Number of speeding events by 21–30 Over Speed Count > 30 Number of speeding events over 30 Speeding Count Total number of speeding events Geofences Zone Speeding Time Duration of speeding inside a geofence. The calculation is based on the speed limit set in the specific geofence settings. If the Zone Speeding Time column is added to the report, the Zone speeding durationfilter appears when building the report. It can be used to limit speeding events by duration: • Min time (sec) — the minimum speeding duration in seconds • Max time (sec) — the maximum speeding duration in seconds Base First Enter Time Time of the first entry into the geofence Base Last Enter Time Time of the last entry into the geofence Base First Exit Time Time of the first exit from the geofence Base Last Exit Time Time of the last exit from the geofence Base Number of Exits Number of exits from the geofence Base Parking Time Parking time inside the geofence Total Parking Time Total parking time Uniq Geofences Count Number of different geofences visited during the period, excluding repeat visits to the same geofence Geofences History Timeline of geofence visits with entry and exit times Geofences List List of geofences visited Notifications Notifications Count Number of notifications during the selected period Eco driving Speeding Max Maximum speeding value Speeding count Number of speeding events Speeding value Speeding value Speeding fine Penalty for speeding Harsh Acceleration Count Number of harsh accelerations Harsh Acceleration Value Value of the harsh acceleration event Harsh Acceleration Fine Penalty for harsh acceleration Harsh Braking Count Number of harsh braking events Harsh Braking Value Value of the harsh braking event Harsh Braking Fine Penalty for harsh braking Harsh Turns Count Number of harsh turns Harsh Turns Value Value of the harsh turn event Harsh Turns Fine Penalty for harsh turns Forward collision Count Number of forward collision warnings Forward collision Value Value of the forward collision event Forward collision Fine Penalty for forward collision warnings Lane deviation Count Number of lane departure events Lane deviation Value Value of the lane departure event Lane deviation Fine Penalty for lane departure Car distance near Value Value of the event when the distance to the vehicle ahead was too small Car distance near Fine Penalty for unsafe following distance Pedestrian collision Count Number of risky pedestrian-related events Pedestrian collision Value Value of the pedestrian-related event Pedestrian collision Fine Penalty for pedestrian-related events Frequent lane change Violation related to frequent lane changes Seat Belt Count Number of seat belt violations Seat Belt Value Value of the seat belt violation Seat Belt Fine Penalty for seat belt violations Fatigue driving Count Number of fatigue or drowsiness events Fatigue driving Value Value of the fatigue driving event Fatigue driving Fine Penalty for fatigue driving Phone call Count Number of phone call events while driving Phone call Value Value of the phone call event Phone call Fine Penalty for using a phone while driving Smoking Count Number of smoking events while driving Smoking Value Value of the smoking event Smoking Fine Penalty for smoking while driving Eco Event Speed Vehicle speed at the time of the eco event Eco Event Avg Speed Average speed during the eco event Eco Event Max Speed Maximum speed recorded during the eco event Eco Event Location Location where the eco event occurred Eco Event Fine Penalty for the eco event Eco Event Value Value of the eco event Eco Event Duration The duration of the eco event Eco Event Count Number of eco events recorded during the period Eco Event Type Category of the eco event Services Service works price Total cost of the performed work Service date Service date Service name Name of the service performed Service supplier Company or contractor that provided the service Service type Service category Service status Service status: completed, scheduled, canceled Service amount Total service amount Service invoice number Invoice or document number related to the service Service comment Additional comment about the service Service due mileage Mileage at which the next service is due Service due motohours Engine hours at which the next service is due Service due date Planned date of the next service Service works & parts list List of completed work and used parts Service works list List of completed work Service parts list List of used parts Service parts price Cost of parts Service downtime How long the object was unavailable due to service Last TO motohours Engine hours at the last completed maintenance Next TO motohours Planned engine hours for the next maintenance Mh until next TO Engine hours remaining until the next maintenance Last TO date Date of the last maintenance Last TO number Number or sequence number of the last maintenance record Next TO date Planned date of the next maintenance Last TO mileage Mileage at the last maintenance Vehicles Down The number of vehicles that were unavailable during the selected period Working hours Shift start Time when the work shift starts according to the schedule Shift end Time when the work shift ends Night drive time Total trip time during the night interval Night drive start Time when the first night trip started Night drive end Time when the last night trip ended Night drive address Location where the night trip was recorded Exceeding time Total time the object worked or moved outside the scheduled working hours Exceeding drive time Part of the exceeding time that happened during trips Exceeding movement time Part of the exceeding time when the object was moving Exceeding parking time Part of the exceeding time spent parked Exceeding idling time Part of the exceeding time when the engine was running but the object was not moving Exceeding address Location where the working-hours violation was recorded Workday start Start time of the working day in the schedule Workday end End time of the working day in the schedule Break time Break duration in seconds, which is excluded or counted separately Saturday start Schedule start time for Saturday Saturday end Schedule end time for Saturday Sunday start Schedule start time for Sunday Sunday end Schedule end time for Sunday Driver fields Driver name Driver’s full name or name as stored in the system Driver group Group the driver belongs to Driver license Driver’s license number Driver license expire Date when the driver’s license expires Driver email Driver’s email address Driver phone Driver’s phone number Driver description Additional text description or note about the driver Driver iButton iButton/key identifier linked to the driver Driver tags Tags assigned to the driver for grouping PTM PTM fields Group of fields related to PTM tasks PTM tasks count Total number of PTM tasks for the selected period PTM tasks completed count Number of completed PTM tasks during the period PTM tasks unplanned stop duration Total duration of unplanned stops related to PTM tasks PTM tasks idle duration Total idle time related to PTM tasks RFID tags RFID Location Location where the RFID event was recorded RFID Time Date and time when the RFID tag was read RFID Name Name or description of the RFID tag RFID UID Unique RFID tag identifier RFID Group Group the RFID tag belongs to Fuel card transactions Transaction Gas Name of the fuel station or service point Transaction Type Type of transaction Transaction Location Address where the transaction was performed Transaction Time Date and time of the card transaction Номер карты Fuel card number Distance Previous-Current Distance between two consecutive operations Current Current reading at the time of the transaction Transaction Amount Monetary amount of the transaction Transaction Price Fuel price per unit in the transaction Transaction Fuel Fuel amount specified in the transaction Transaction Volume Fuel volume for the transaction Transaction Odo Odometer reading at the time of the transaction Transaction Gas Type Fuel type: diesel, petrol, gas Pump Amount Amount charged at the pump Pump Volume Amount of fuel dispensed by the pump Nozzle Number Number of the fuel nozzle Pump Number Number of the fuel pump Machine Hours Engine hours at the time of the transaction Previous-Current Change between the previous and current value Connection loss Total Disconnection Duration Total time without connection Disconnection Start Date & Time Moment when the connection to the device was lost Disconnection End Date & Time Moment when the connection was restored Ignition Status at Disconnection Whether ignition was on at the moment of disconnection Disconnection Address Address of the last known point before connection loss Last Known Latitude & Longitude Coordinates of the last known point before connection loss Reconnection Address Address where the connection was restored Reconnection Latitude & Longitude Coordinates where the connection was restored Reconnection Speed (km/h) Object speed at the time of reconnection Offline Reason Code Technical code indicating the reason for the connection loss"},{"s":"modules-1","t":"Modules","h":["Modules","Why modules are needed","How modules are connected","Reports by modules"],"x":"Modules Modules are separate components of the OneGPS system, each responsible for specific functions. Why modules are needed The modular structure allows you to: Customize the system for the specific needs of your company Pay only for the features you actually use Add new modules as your requirements grow How modules are connected Modules are connected by the account administrator in the Admin panel. Some modules have their own settings that need to be correctly activated. If you have any difficulties connecting a module, contact technical support. Reports by modules When a module is enabled, additional reports become available to help you analyze performance. You can find these reports in the Reports section."},{"s":"video-and-cms-","t":"Video and CMS","h":["Video and CMS"],"x":"Video and CMS The Video and CMS modules allow you to connect different types of video devices, including DVRs, vehicle cameras, and body-worn cameras. You can use them to view live streams and archived recordings and receive telematics data. Mettax cameras can also send driver face recognition results to OneGPS. The Video and CMS modules work together. Both modules must be activated for cameras and DVRs to connect to the system correctly."},{"s":"setting-up-dvrs-and-cameras-in-the-admin-panel","t":"Setting up DVRs and cameras in the admin panel","h":["Setting up DVRs and cameras in the admin panel","Enabling the modules","Configuring CMS in the Admin panel"],"x":"Setting up DVRs and cameras in the admin panel To enable DVRs and cameras in the system, you need to activate two modules — Video and CMS. They work together: CMS — connects devices and receives the data stream. Video — displays live streams, stores the archive, and provides access to the player. If only one of them is activated, camera and DVR connections will be incomplete. Enabling the modules If you have administrator access: 1. Log in to the Admin panel 2. Go to the Accounts tab 3. Select the required account 4. Right-click the account and choose Edit 5. Go to the Modules tab 6. Check the boxes next to Video and CMS After this, the modules will be activated for the selected account. Configuring CMS in the Admin panel 1. Go to the Module settings tab 2. Select the CMS module 3. Find the Configs section — by default, it will be empty 4. Enter the following key values: url: video.OneGPS-gps.com account: login password: password To receive your login and password, please contact technical support. For security reasons, these credentials are not published in the documentation. After completing the setup, the CMS module will be configured. Once everything is set up, the OneGPS system will: stream video live store recordings from DVRs and cameras collect telematics data together with video (speed, location, events)"},{"s":"supported-devices","t":"Supported devices","h":["Supported devices","Howen","CMS","Cipia","Teltonika DualCam","Hikvision"],"x":"Supported devices The OneGPS system supports different types of video devices. Each type has its own connection parameters. Howen Server 1 (Telematics): Server URL, port 20009 Server 2 (Video): video.OneGPS-gps.com, port 33000 For Howen devices, use the separate key url_howen in the Module settings. No password is required for connection. CMS Host: video.OneGPS-gps.com Port: 6608 Cipia Host: video.OneGPS-gps.com Port: 1883 Teltonika DualCam Host: video.OneGPS-gps.com Port: 20233 Hikvision Connecting Hikvision devices depends on the specific model. For some models, a standard connection to the server video.OneGPS-gps.com is used. For example, for the Hikvision G40: Host: video.OneGPS-gps.com Port: 6608 Other Hikvision models supported in OneGPS: DS-MCW407 (BWS) — wearable camera for video and audio with live streaming AE-MN5043 (DVR) — mobile DVR with GPS for vehicles AE-VC583I-IS/P(H) — high-resolution vehicle IP camera (license plate recognition) Different Hikvision models may require different connection settings. For exact parameters, please contact support."},{"s":"setting-up-tariffs","t":"Setting up tariffs","h":["Setting up tariffs"],"x":"Setting up tariffs The Prices tab allows you to set the cost of using connected equipment. A price defines how much it costs to connect and operate each device. You can choose a price plan for each manufacturer from the list and save the settings. How to set up tariffs for devices: 1. In the Admin panel, go to the Accounts tab 2. Select the required account 3. Right-click the account and selectEdit 4.Open the Prices tab 5. Select the appropriate price plan for the device 6. Click Save"},{"s":"connecting-a-video-device-in-the-user-account","t":"Connecting a video device in the user account","h":["Connecting a video device in the user account","Creating an object","Setting up cameras for an object","Connecting an additional video device","Adding an additional device"],"x":"Connecting a video device in the user account After the administrator has set upthe Video and CMS modules in the Admin Panel, the user needs to create an object and set up cameras. Creating an object 1. Log in to the User account 2. Go to the Main section 3. Click Create object 4. Fill in the object card: Vehicle number or object name Device ID number 5. In the Device type field, select: 1 — Device type CMS if you have a CMS device Howen if you have a Howen device or another supported device 2 — Template. For CMS, the system already includes global templates (default settings for quick setup) For another device, templates are not available — just select the device type. 6. Click Save — the settings will apply and the object will appear in the list. Setting up cameras for an object After the object is saved, specify how many cameras are connected and how they should work. 1. Open the object card — double-click the object in the list 2. Go to the Video tab 3. Click Add camera 4. Enter: Camera name Device type Channel number If you check Main stream, the camera will transmit video in high quality (clearer image, more details), but internet traffic usage will be higher. If you leave it unchecked, the sub-stream will be used — image quality will be lower, but bandwidth consumption is reduced. 5. Click Save 6. Repeat for all cameras you want to add Connecting an additional video device In the OneGPS system, you can link an additional video device to an object if you already have a main one (for example, Teltonika). This is useful in cases where: Teltonika collects telemetry (speed, tracks, sensors). CMS or Howen provides video. That way, a single object can simultaneously process GPS data and video streams. Adding an additional device 1. Open the object card 2. Go to the Additional IMEI field and click the button 3. In the new window, click Add 4. Select the device type from the list 5. When adding the device, you can configure parameters: Ignore coordinates — coordinates from the additional device will not be used (they’re already provided by the main device) Ignore retrans — prevents duplicate data from the additional device, so it won’t interfere with the main telemetry. 6. Click Save, and don’t forget to save changes in the object card as well."},{"s":"how-to-watch-live-video-","t":"How to watch live video","h":["How to watch live video","How to open live video","Streaming time limit","Video player controls","Two-way communication"],"x":"How to watch live video After you have saved the cameras in the object card, they become available in the system. In the object list, a camera icon appears next to the vehicle name: Green camera — device is active, video is available Gray camera — device is inactive How to open live video 1. In the object list, click on the camera icon 2. The video player will open with a real-time stream The player displays all available camera channels (for example: road, cabin, side or rear views — depending on the equipment). In the video you will see: Live duration Time and date of the recording Channel number GPS coordinates — latitude and longitude Vehicle speed in km/h Streaming time limit You can configure automatic stopping for online video after a specified time. This helps limit viewing duration and control how long live video remains active. To configure the limit, click the settings icon in the upper-right corner of the video player. The Streaming time limit window opens. In this window, you can enable automatic stopping and set the streaming duration. To enable automatic stopping, select Automatically stop streaming. Then specify: Duration — the streaming duration before stopping Unit — the unit of time, for example minutes or hours After configuring the settings, click Save. The current online streaming duration is displayed in the video player title in the following format: Live duration: current time / configured limit For example, Live duration: 00:00:11 / 00:30:00 means that the stream has been running for 0 minutes and the configured limit is 30 minutes. If automatic stopping is enabled, the system will stop the online video after the specified time. Video player controls In the player, you can use the following functions: Start / Stop — start or stop the stream Sound — adjust the volume or mute Screenshot — capture the current frame 🔲 Full screen — expand the player to full screen Two-way communication In the top-right corner of the player, there is an intercom icon . Using this feature, you can have two-way communication with the driver: hear them and speak to them."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-media-files","t":"How to work with media files","h":["How to work with media files","Media files in History","tab","How to view a recording:","Video player controls:","How to download video:","tab","How to work with media on the server:","Where violations in the Storage Server come from","Media files in the object menu"],"x":"How to work with media files All recordings and photos from devices are available for viewing in your user account — either in the History section or in the Media tab of the selected object. Media files in History In the History section, you can not only view the object’s tracks but also work with video: watch recordings, download clips, or check recorded violations. 1. Go to the History section in the top panel 2. Select the desired object from the list by checking the box next to it 3. Specify the desired time period 4. Click the Search button 5. In the search results, check the track recordings you want to view 6. The Media tab will appear in the bottom panel (next to Events) The Media tab is divided into two parts: Device — recordings stored on the device itself (dashcam or camera) Storage Server — recordings stored on the server (e.g., DMS/ADAS events: seatbelt unfastened, fatigue, smoking, etc.) Device tab This section shows everything still stored on the device. How to view a recording: 1. Click the icon next to the recording in the list 2. The player will open and display video for the selected time period On the video you will see: Channel number Date and time Vehicle speed and/or coordinates Video player controls: Play / Pause — start or stop playback Stop — end playback Rewind / Fast forward — move through the recording Screenshot — capture a frame from the video Sound — adjust volume (if the recording has audio) Playback speed — slow down or speed up the recording Full screen — expand the player to full screen How to download video: 1. Click the download button next to the recording 2. A download window will open 3. You can download the full video or only a selected segment: To select a segment, specify the start and end time in the calendar 4. Click Start 5. Wait while the system prepares the file. The device must be powered on, and the recording must still be available on its memory card. During file preparation, the Video Downloader window displays a progress indicator showing: the amount of data transferred and the total file size the completion percentage the current transfer speed the estimated time remaining For example, 7.3 MB / 136.3 MB (5.4%) • 249 KB/s • 8m means that 7.3 MB of 136.3 MB has been transferred, the download is 5.4% complete, the current transfer speed is 249 KB/s, and approximately 8 minutes remain. 6. Once prepared, you can save the file to your computer If the video has already been overwritten on the device, it cannot be downloaded. Storage Server tab This tab contains recordings stored on the server. The server stores safety-related events (DMS and ADAS), for example: Driver not wearing a seatbelt Smoking while driving Lane departure Signs of fatigue Each event has a description, e.g., “Seatbelt unfastened,” “Fatigue while driving.” How to work with media on the server: To view a photo of an event, click the icon . For some events, video is also available — click the play icon . To save a media file (photo or video) to your computer, use the download button . Where violations in the Storage Server come from All violations you see in the Storage Server tab are recorded by the device itself. When you create an object and apply a global settings template (e.g., for CMS devices), the object gets a multi-position Alarm sensor. The device sends violation codes, which OneGPS automatically maps to the sensor’s calibration table. The table translates technical codes into understandable statuses, such as: Using a phone Unsafe distance Smoking while driving Fatigue Lane departure Other violations When a driver commits one of these violations, the device sends a code along with a photo or video to the system. The basic set of violation codes is already built into the calibration table, so most events are recorded immediately without additional setup. Media files in the object menu The object menu has a separate Media tab. How to open it: 1. Find the desired object in the list 2. Right-click on it 3. Select Media from the menu The Mediatab is divided into two parts: Device — recordings stored on the device itself (dashcam or camera) Storage Server — recordings stored on the server (e.g., violation recordings) For more details on what is contained in each section and how to work with video, see the descriptions above."},{"s":"viewing-cameras-in-the-dashcam-panel","t":"Viewing cameras in the Dashcam panel","h":["Viewing cameras in the Dashcam panel","Panel interface","Selecting cameras","Building the dashboard","Adjusting the scale","Full-screen camera view","Video player controls","Hiding an individual camera","Restoring hidden cameras","Closing an object block","Streaming time limit"],"x":"Viewing cameras in the Dashcam panel The Dashcamsection in the main menu allows you to monitor cameras from several objects at the same time. For example, you can display views from the cabs of different vehicles on one screen and quickly switch to the required camera. The panel supports up to 64 video streams. The cameras belonging to each object are grouped into a separate block. In this article Panel interface — the main areas and Dashcam controls Selecting cameras and building the dashboard — selecting objects and starting the required video streams Adjusting the scale — changing the size of all video blocks Viewing a camera in full-screen mode — enlarging the image and returning to the camera grid Video player controls — starting and stopping streams, controlling sound, and taking screenshots Hiding and restoring cameras — temporarily stopping unnecessary video streams and reopening them Closing an object’s video block — stopping all streams for the selected object Streaming time limit — automatically stopping streams after a specified period Panel interface The Camera managementarea on the left contains the list of objects and their connected cameras. The Dashcam workspace on the right displays the video blocks. The following controls are available at the top of the panel: Refresh — refreshes the list of objects and cameras — opens the selected cameras in the panel — changes the size of all open video blocks; Camera counter — shows the number of open video streams — shows the duration of the current viewing session Streaming time limit — configures automatic stopping of video streams Selecting cameras The Camera management list contains all objects with connected cameras. Expand an object to view its cameras. The connected cameras and their names appear under the object name. Building the dashboard To open cameras: 1. Go to the Dashcam tab 2. In the Camera management area, expand the required folder and find the object 3. Expand the object and select the cameras you want to open You can select individual cameras or all available cameras belonging to the object. 4. Check the number of selected cameras at the top of the panel 5. Click Build dashboard Separate blocks for the selected objects appear in the workspace. Each block displays the selected cameras. No more than 64 cameras can be open at the same time. If the dashboard has already been built, you do not need to rebuild it to add another object. Double-click the required object in the Camera management area. Its cameras are added to the workspace, while the existing streams continue without restarting. Adjusting the scale Use the Zoom slider to change the size of all open video blocks at the same time: zoom out to fit more objects on the screen zoom in to view camera images in greater detail Full-screen camera view Double-click a camera to open it in full-screen mode. To return to the camera grid: double-click the image again or press Esc Video player controls The following controls are available at the bottom of each video player: Start / Stop — starts or stops the video stream Sound — adjusts or mutes the sound Screenshot — captures the current frame Full screen — expands the player to full screen Hiding an individual camera If you temporarily do not need a particular camera, you can hide it. Click the × icon in the upper-right corner of its video stream. After the camera is hidden: it is no longer displayed in the block its active video stream stops the other cameras belonging to the object continue streaming the size and layout of the block remain unchanged, and a static area replaces the hidden camera Stopping unnecessary video streams helps reduce network traffic and computer resource usage. Restoring hidden cameras To restore a hidden camera: 1. Click the settings icon in the object block header 2. Open the list of hidden cameras 3. Select the camera you want to restore To restore all hidden cameras belonging to the object at once, select Show All. If the block does not contain any hidden cameras, the menu displays No hidden cameras. Closing an object block To close an entire video block, click the × icon in its header. The block is removed from the workspace, and all its active video streams stop. Cameras belonging to other objects continue streaming. Streaming time limit You can configure streams to stop automatically after a specified period so that they do not remain active longer than necessary. 1. Click the settings icon next to Live duration 2. In the Streaming time limitwindow, enable Automatically stop streaming 3. Specify: Duration — how long the streams remain active before stopping Unit — the unit of time, such as minutes or hours 4. Click Save After saving, Live duration displays the current viewing time and the configured limit: Live duration: current time / configured limit When the configured time expires, OneGPS automatically stops the streams. If automatic stopping is no longer required, disable Automatically stop streaming and save the settings."},{"s":"drivers-","t":"Drivers","h":["Drivers"],"x":"Drivers The Drivers module is designed to manage drivers in the system. With this module, you can: create and edit driver profiles assign drivers to vehicles configure drivers’ access to vehicles view which vehicles a driver used and during which periods"},{"s":"how-to-create-a-driver","t":"How to create a driver","h":["How to create a driver","Main tab","Vehicles tab","Assigning a driver to a vehicle","Show history","Access to vehicle","Notifications","Finishing work with the Driver profile","Files tab","How to add a file"],"x":"How to create a driver To add a new driver, go to the Drivers tab in the Mainsection. 1. Click Add driver 2. The driver profile will open. Fill in the driver information The driver profile consists of two tabs: Main and Vehicles. Main tab This tab contains the driver’s personal information: Driver name — full name or display name shown in the system Date of birth Gender Driving start date Driver’s license — license number and expiration date iButton — an authentication key used for driver identification. For example, the iButton field can be used to identify a driver automatically by the Face ID received from a Mettax camera Email — email address Phone — contact phone number Group — the group the driver belongs to Description — any additional information For all users — if the checkbox is selected, the driver becomes available to all users in the system Tags — tags for searching and grouping drivers Photo — driver photo You can upload a photo by clicking the icon under the image. The icon becomes available after the first time you save the profile. After filling in the details, click Save. Vehicles tab On this tab you can: assign a driver to a vehicle configure access to objects enable or disable notifications Assigning a driver to a vehicle 1. Enable Link driver to vehicle After that, the Object and Datefields will become active. 2. In the Object field, select the vehicle to assign the driver to 3. Set the date and time when the assignment should start 1 — Date and time when the assignment should start 2 — Show history If a driver is already assigned to a vehicle, they are not unassigned automatically. The system allows multiple drivers to be assigned to the same vehicle at the same time. This supports real-life scenarios, such as two drivers in one vehicle or shift changes during a trip. If you need a vehicle to always have only one active driver, enable the Single_Driver configuration in the contract settings in the admin panel. In this mode, when a new driver is assigned, the previous one is automatically unassigned. The system will not allow two active driver sessions for one vehicle. If the driver uses an iButton, assigning a new driver will automatically unassign all previous drivers. Show history The Show history button opens a window with the driver’s work history: which objects the driver worked with during which periods vehicle changes occurred Access to vehicle The Access to vehiclefield allows you to choose which objects the driver can access in the system. If specific objects are selected, access will be limited to those objects only. If the field is left empty, the driver will have access to all objects. Notifications If Send alerts is enabled, the system will send notifications related to this driver. If the option is disabled, no such notifications will be sent. Finishing work with the Driver profile After configuring all settings, clickSave to apply the changes. Deletecompletely removes the driver profile from the system. Files tab After the driver profile is saved for the first time, the Files tab becomes available. Here you can store driver-related documents, for example: driver’s license medical certificates contracts other files How to add a file 1. Click the Add button 2. In the window that opens: enter a file description click Select file and upload a file from your device 3. Click Save The file will then appear in the list on the Files tab. To download a file, click it in the File column. To delete a file, click the trash icon next to it."},{"s":"working-with-drivers","t":"Working with drivers","h":["Working with drivers","Working with the Driver list","Configuring table columns","How to open a driver profile","Driver groups","Assigning drivers to vehicles"],"x":"Working with drivers The Drivers tab in the Main section is used to manage drivers in the system. Here you can: work with the list of drivers manage driver-to-vehicle assignments Working with the Driver list All drivers are displayed as a list in the Drivers tab. The table shows: Driver name Tags Vehicle the driver is currently assigned to iButton identification key (if used) Other data — depending on the selected table columns Configuring table columns You can customize the driver list by selecting columns, enabling sorting, and applying filters. 1. Click the arrow in the header of any column — the settings menu will open. 2. In the menu, you can: sort data in ascending or descending order apply filters for the selected column choose which columns are displayed 3. To select visible columns: click Columns check the required fields — the table will update automatically How to open a driver profile To open a driver profile for viewing or editing: 1. Find the required driver in the list 2. Double-click the row with the driver’s name The driver profile will open in a separate window. Driver groups When adding a driver, you assign them to a group. Groups are created in the Online tab (object groups), but they can also be used to organize drivers. The main actions are available at the top of the list: Driver assignment — bulk assignment of drivers to vehicles Export drivers — download the driver list to a file Import drivers — upload drivers from a file Add driver — create a new driver Refresh list — update the data Assigning drivers to vehicles To link drivers with vehicles, open the DriversLink window. In the Drivers tab, click Assign driver to objectat the top of the list. In this window, you can: assign drivers to vehicles configure vehicle access using ID / iButton DriversLink does more than just link a driver and a vehicle — it allows you to create assignment groups. Each group stores: a list of vehicles a list of drivers the selected access mode 1. Select an Access mode At the top of the window, choose one of the following modes: Driver ID control is turned off. The vehicle can be used without driver identification. Only assigned drivers are allowed to use the vehicle. Any ID can start driving. The system only records who is driving. 2. Select vehicles In the Objects section, select the vehicles to which the settings should be applied. 3. Select drivers In the Drivers section, select the drivers who are allowed to access the selected vehicles. If mode [0] Disabled is selected, drivers do not need to be chosen. If mode [1] or [2] is selected, drivers must be assigned. 4. Create an Assignment group After selecting vehicles and drivers: 1. Click Save 2. In the popup window, enter a group name and click OK The group will appear in the list on the left and will contain: the selected access mode the list of vehicles the list of drivers The group name will also appear in the Group column for the selected vehicles. 5. Apply the settings Click Save at the bottom of the window. The selected settings will be applied to all vehicles in the group."},{"s":"how-to-view-driver-history","t":"How to view driver history","h":["How to view driver history","Driver history in the Driver profile","Driver history via the Object menu","Assignment history","Allowed drivers"],"x":"How to view driver history You can view a driver’s work history in two ways: through the driver profile through the object (vehicle) menu Driver history in the Driver profile The driver profile shows which vehicles the driver worked with and when. How to open the history: 1. Open the driver profile 2. Go to the Vehicle tab 3. Click Show history In the history window, you will see: Period — select a date range using the calendar and click Refresh to view the required period Object — the vehicle the driver was assigned to Driver — the driver’s name From / To — start and end date and time of the assignment. If the assignment is still active, the To field will be empty iButton — the identification key used by the driver (if driver identification is enabled) Available actions: Export the history to a file Refresh the data Delete a specific assignment record Driver history via the Object menu This method allows you to view driver history for a specific vehicle and manage which drivers are allowed to use it. How to open the history: 1. Go to the Online tab 2. Select the required object 3. Right-click the object 4. Select Drivers from the menu The DriversLink window will open. It is divided into two parts: Left side — driver assignment history Right side — drivers allowed to use the vehicle Assignment history Here you can see the history of driver assignments for the selected vehicle: Period — select a date range and click Refresh Object — the vehicle Driver — driver name From / To — start and end date and time of the assignment. If the assignment is still active, the To field will be empty iButton — identification key used by the driver (if enabled) Available actions: Export history to a file Refresh data Delete a specific assignment record Allowed drivers The right side shows a list of drivers who are allowed to work with this vehicle. Select drivers using the checkboxes — they will be considered authorized for this vehicle. After making changes, click Save to apply them."},{"s":"driver-identification-by-face-id","t":"Driver identification by Face ID","h":["Driver identification by Face ID","Preparing drivers on the device","Recognition data","Recognition data in raw data points","Configuring the Face ID sensor","Testing the formula","Linking Face ID to a driver"],"x":"Driver identification by Face ID Mettax cameras can recognize a driver’s face and send the driver’s identifier to OneGPS. After successful recognition, OneGPS automatically assigns the driver to the object in the same way as with iButton and RFID tags. This feature makes it possible to identify who is driving a vehicle when several drivers use the same object. Preparing drivers on the device Drivers must first be registered on the Mettax device. After registration, the device creates a faceId for each driver. The procedure for adding drivers depends on the camera model and its settings. If you do not know how to register drivers on the device, contact the specialist who installed or configured the camera. After a driver is registered, the device must: 1. Recognize the driver’s face 2. Send the generated faceId to OneGPS 3. Send the recognition result in the faceRecogn parameter The faceId is generated by the Mettax device. The value received from the device is linked to the corresponding driver card in OneGPS. Recognition data When checking a face, the device sends the following parameters to OneGPS: faceId — the driver identifier generated on the device faceRecogn — the recognition result alarm — the type of verification performed ThefaceRecogn parameter can have the following values: 1 — the face was successfully recognized 0 — the verification failed 2 — the face could not be identified 3 — the comparison failed For automatic driver assignment, the device must send faceRecogn=1. The camera can send the following verification events: alarm=647 — periodic driver verification alarm=648 — driver verification when the ignition is switched on Recognition data in raw data points When checking a face, the Mettax camera sends the alarm, faceId, and faceRecogn parameters in the object’s raw data. The highlighted row in the example contains the following values: alarm=647; faceId=1; faceRecogn=1 Here: alarm=647 — the camera performed a periodic driver verification faceId=1 — the device sent driver identifier 1 faceRecogn=1 — the face was successfully recognized In the rows above, the camera sent: faceId=20210301001; faceRecogn=0; The faceRecogn=0 value indicates that the face could not be recognized. In the row containing alarm=648, verification was initiated when the ignition was switched on. The faceRecogn=0 value also means that the face was not recognized. For automatic driver assignment, thefaceId and the successful faceRecogn=1result must be received in the same data point. The received faceId must match the value in the iButton field of the driver profile. Configuring the Face ID sensor To allow OneGPS to use the received faceId to identify the driver, create a special sensor with the iButton function on the object. 1. Add a new sensor to the object 2. Specify the following parameters Function — iButton Type — Special Name and Description Field — for example, faceId Conversion formula — *%faceRecogn% . It multiplies the faceId value by the recognition result. When recognition is successful (faceRecogn=1), the faceId remains unchanged, allowing OneGPS to match it with the identifier in the driver profile. For other values, the formula result does not match the original faceId; Maximum value — a value large enough to accommodate the faceId identifiers in use 3. If necessary, enable Close driver’s session on ignition off. After the ignition is switched off, the assigned driver will no longer be considered active for the object. 4. Save the sensor Testing the formula Test the formula using sample data before saving the sensor. 1. Click Testnext to the conversion formula 2. In the window that opens, find the faceid and faceRecogn parameters 3. For faceid, enter a test driver identifier, for example: faceid=20210301001 4. Replace the automatically generated faceRecogn value with 1: faceRecogn=1 5. Click Test If the test is successful, the result must match the faceid value: Success: 20210301001 The *%faceRecogn% formula multiplies faceid by the recognition result. When faceRecogn=1, it returns the original driver identifier. For other values, the formula result does not match the original faceId. The automatically generated test values are not data received from the device. Before running the test, replace faceRecogn with one of the values supported by the Mettax camera: 0, 1, 2, or 3. Linking Face ID to a driver When a driver is registered, the Mettax device generates a unique faceId for that driver. To allow OneGPS to identify the driver automatically, specify this identifier in the driver profile. 1. Find the faceId assigned to the driver by the Mettax device 2. Copy the faceId value 3. Open the corresponding driver profile in OneGPS 4. Go to the Main tab 5. Paste the faceId value into the iButton field 6. Save the changes The value in theiButton field must exactly match the faceId sent by the camera. A separate identifier is used for each driver. After successful recognition, the camera sends faceId and faceRecogn=1. OneGPS matches the received faceId with the value in the iButton field and assigns the corresponding driver to the object."},{"s":"logbook-","t":"LogBook","h":["LogBook"],"x":"LogBook LogBook is a portal that provides drivers with all the data about their trips, events, fuel expenses, as well as information about their driving style. Through this portal, drivers can track both work-related and personal trips, receive reports on fuel expenses, and toll road costs."},{"s":"how-to-sonnect-logbook","t":"How to сonnect LogBook","h":["How to сonnect LogBook","Connecting the LogBook module in the Admin panel","Connecting LogBook in the User account interface"],"x":"How to сonnect LogBook To set up LogBook, you need to complete two steps: 1. Activate the LogBook module in the admin panel 2. Connect LogBook in the user interface Connecting the LogBook module in the Admin panel 1.Log into the admin panel using your username and password 2. Select the contract to which you want to add the LogBook module 3. Right-click on the contract — a context menu will open 4. In the menu, choose the Edit option 5. In the contract settings window, go to the Module settings tab 6. Select the Drivers module and set the key for it: logbook=1 7. After adding the key, click Update and save the contract settings Connecting LogBook in the User account interface 1. Go to the user’s account 2. In the Main section, navigate to theDrivers tab 3. Choose the driver you want to enable LogBook for, and open their profile — double-click on their name in the list 4. Enable the LogBook module for the selected driver by checking the LogBook Access checkbox 5. Set the driver’s login for access to the portal 6. Click the Send to Driver button. An email will be sent to the address listed in the driver’s profile with a link to access the LogBook portal Please ensure that the driver’s email is valid; otherwise, they will not receive the access email. For proper password and portal access configuration, we recommend contacting technical support."},{"s":"how-to-log-in-to-logbook","t":"How to log in to LogBook","h":["How to log in to LogBook","If you need to change your password"],"x":"How to log in to LogBook To log in to LogBook: 1. Go to the link: logbook.OneGPS-gps.com 2. Enter your login and password 3. Click Continue. After that, you will be logged into the system and can start using all its features If you need to change your password If you’ve forgotten your password or want to change it, follow these steps: 1. Click Reset password 2. Enter your email address and click Reset password again 3. You will receive an email with a password reset link 4. Click the link, enter a new password, confirm it, and click Continue"},{"s":"portal-interface-overview","t":"Portal interface overview","h":["Portal interface overview","Left Panel: filters and summary","Top panel"],"x":"Portal interface overview After logging into LogBook, you will land on the homepage. Let’s define the main interface elements: Left panel with summary and filters Top panel with section menus Left Panel: filters and summary At the top of the screen, you can select the period for which you want to view trips. To do this: Set the date and time using the calendar icon Click the Refresh button In the Main section, you’ll see the following details: Timezone – the time zone in which the trip times are displayed Driver – the name of the driver whose data is shown Current vehicle – the vehicle number or ID Vehicle – a dropdown menu to select the relevant vehicle if multiple vehicles are assigned to you In the Summary section, the total mileage for the selected period is shown: Total km – The total mileage Business km – The distance marked as business trips Private km – The distance marked as private trips This section displays information on toll road expenses: Total Toll – The total amount spent on toll roads SANRAL / NON-SANRAL – Categorization of toll roads by operator type (if applicable) Business Toll – The amount spent on toll roads for business trips Private Toll – The amount spent on toll roads for private trips In the Driver statisticssection, data on driving style and safety is displayed: Harsh Acceleration – the number of hard accelerations Harsh Brakes – the number of harsh braking events Excess Idling – long stops with the engine running Over Speeding – instances of exceeding the speed limit Max Speed – the highest recorded speed Driver Score – the overall score of the driver’s style: the higher the score, the more careful and economical the driving Top panel At the top of the screen, you’ll find the main portal sections: Trips – a list of all trips for the selected period Events – individual driving events (harsh braking, speeding, etc.) Fuel – data on fuel fill-ups and consumption Tolls – detailed information on each toll road Media – photos or videos related to trips On the top-right of the screen, you will find: Email – the current logged-in user Settings – access to your account settings Logout – Log out of the system."},{"s":"using-the-settings-on-the-logbook-portal","t":"Using the settings on the LogBook portal","h":["Using the settings on the LogBook portal","Setting up weekends and holidays","Uploading the work calendar","Manually adding a holiday","User guide","Changing your password","Viewing activity history","How to view activity history"],"x":"Using the settings on the LogBook portal In this article, we will explain how to use the settings on the LogBook portal. You will learn how to: set up the calendar with weekends and holidays to track work hours access and view the user guide for the portal change your password view the user activity history on the portal Setting up weekends and holidays To ensure that the LogBook system correctly interprets trips on weekends and holidays (e.g., marking them as private), you can configure the calendar. Uploading the work calendar 1. Go to the Settings section and select Holidays 2. In the opened window, click Import to automatically upload the calendar for the desired year 3. Enter the following details: Year – specify the year for which you want to upload the holiday list Country code (2 letters) – for example, ZA for South Africa 4. Click Saveto save the data Manually adding a holiday 1. Open Settings and select Add 2. Enter the holiday details: Date – select the holiday date Name – specify the official name of the holiday Local Name – the holiday name in your language or company Type – the type of holiday (e.g., national, regional, corporate, etc.) Launch Year – specify the year the holiday is recognized. 3. Click Saveto save the holiday User guide If you need more information on how to use the LogBook portal, you can refer to the Help manual. Changing your password To change your password for logging into the LogBook portal: 1. In Settings, select Change password 2. Enter the new password and confirm it by typing it again 3. Click Save to save your new password Viewing activity history With the Audit feature, you can review the history of actions on the portal. How to view activity history 1. In the Auditwindow, select: Start Date – choose the start date for the events you want to display End Date – choose the end date for the events you want to display Driver – select the driver or user whose activity you want to track 2. Click Show After that, you will see a list of all actions for the selected period: Date – when the event occurred Driver – who performed the action Login – under which account the action was performed Operation – what exactly was done IP – from which IP address the action was performed Browser – which browser was used for the operation"},{"s":"how-to-work-with-sections","t":"How to work with sections","h":["How to work with sections","Trips","Auto processing of trips","Current position of the object","Zooming In and Out","Printing the map","Measuring distance between points","Street view","Viewing traffic","Choosing the map type","Object tooltip","Editing locations","Reports","Selecting trip types","Changing the trip type","Editing business trips","Events","How to view events","How to view events on the map","Fuel","How to view fuel transactions","Tolls","Media"],"x":"How to work with sections At the top of the screen, you’ll find the main sections of the portal: Trips – a list of all trips for the selected period Events – individual driving events Fuel – data on fuel fill-ups and consumption Tolls – detailed information about each toll road Media – photos or videos related to trips Trips The Trips section displays all the trips made by the driver. Functions in the Trips section: Auto process – automatically sorts trips into business and private categories Show current position – displays the current location of the object on the map Edit locations – helps correct or specify the start and end addresses of trips Reports – creates reports for trips and expenses for the selected period Business and Private flags – you can filter trips using these flags: show only business, only private, or all trips Auto processing of trips The Auto Process function helps automatically classify trips as business or private. Here’s how to set it up: 1. Click Auto Process in the Trips section 2. In the window that appears, select the period for which you want to apply auto-processing: Current Month – the system will automatically process trips for the current calendar month Select Date Range – if you want to process trips for a specific period, select the dates using the calendar Enter: Start Date – From which date to start auto-processing. End Date – Until which date the processing should apply. 3. Specify the standard working hours: Start Work – The time after which trips will be considered business trips (e.g., 08:00). End Work – The time after which trips will be considered private (e.g., 19:00). All trips occurring between Start Work and End Work will be automatically classified as Business. Trips before Start Work, after End Work, and on weekends (if configured) will be automatically classified as Private. 4. Once all settings are specified, click Save to apply auto-processing. Current position of the object The Show current position function displays the real-time location of the object on the map. You can use the following tools to adjust the map: Zooming In and Out 1. Use and buttons at the top left of the map. The point in the center will stay in place as you zoom. 2. Use the mouse scroll wheel: scroll away from you to zoom in, and towards you to zoom out. The cursor shows what area you're focusing on. 3. Double-Click: double-click on any point on the map to zoom in. Printing the map You can print the current map view. When you click the Print button , you’ll be presented with four printing orientation options: Landscape – For horizontal maps, suitable for displaying extended areas or objects arranged horizontally. Portrait – For vertical maps, best for areas stretched vertically. Auto – The system will automatically select the optimal print orientation based on the current map view. Custom area – Choose this option to print a specific part of the map. Select the desired area with your mouse, and it will be printed. Measuring distance between points This tool helps calculate distances between any two points on the map. How to use: 1. Click on the map to mark the first point 2. Click again to mark the second point 3. A line will appear between the points, showing the distance If you need to measure multiple segments, continue clicking on the map to add new points. The length of each segment will be displayed next to it. Street view The Street viewfunction allows you to see panoramic photos of the area. Available view options: GSV – High-quality panoramic images from Google with wide coverage OSC – Community-generated panoramas, which may contain fresher images in some areas To enable street view: 1. Check the box in the upper-right corner of the map for this option 2. Drag the yellow person icon on the map to the desired location 3. Choose the preferred view option – GSV or OSC 4. After selecting an option, the corresponding panoramic service will open, where you can explore the interactive panoramas of the selected area. Viewing traffic The Traffic function shows the current traffic levels using color indicators: Green – free flow of traffic Yellow – moderate traffic Red – heavy traffic To enable traffic view, check the box in the upper-right corner of the map. Choosing the map type You can choose from several map types to display spatial data in the LogBook interface. To change the map type, click the Map type selection button . Object tooltip A tooltip appears when you click on an object on the map after enabling the Show current position function. It provides real-time information about the vehicle’s status. Editing locations The Edit locations function allows you to refine the start and end addresses of trips and specify the type of these points. How to edit a location: 1. Select the trip from the list in the Trips section 2. Click Edit locations 3. Check the box for eitherStart location or End location Start Location – the starting address of the trip End Location – the ending address of the trip 4. Edit the address 5. Choose the Type of location – for example: Home, Office, Client 6. Optionally, set a Radius (in meters) to define the area around the location 7. Click Save to apply the changes Reports With the Reports function, you can save trip information for the selected period in PDF or Excel format. How to Create a Report: 1. In the Trips section, select Reports 2. Click the dropdown arrow to open the file format menu 3. Choose either PDF or Excel as the file format Selecting trip types In the Tripssection, you can manage which trips are displayed in the list. Use the checkboxes to filter by trip type: Business – trips related to work tasks Private – personal trips Changing the trip type You can change the type of any trip, whether it's business or private. How to change the trip type: 1. Find the desired trip in the list 2. Click the Change type button 3. The trip type will be updated, e.g., a private trip will be marked as business Editing business trips For business trips, detailed editing options are available: 1. Click the edit icon next to the desired trip to open the details 2. At the top of the window, you will see the date and time of the trip This form allows you to describe the business trip in detail: Reason for Trip – Select the reason, e.g., work, delivery, repair Reason Description – Provide a brief description of the purpose of the trip Contact Name – The person you visited Contact Details – Phone number, email, or other contact information Company Name – The client or partner’s company name Reference Number – The task number Assign same reason to all end locations – If checked, the selected reason and details will automatically apply to all trips ending at the same location 3. Click Save to apply the changes Events In the Events section, you can track events related to trips, such as: when a vehicle starts or finishes a trip when a refueling happens when speeding occurs, a sensor is triggered, etc. You can not only view events in a list but also display them directly on the map, helping you better understand where and under what conditions they occurred. How to view events 1. Go to the Events section On the left, you’ll see a list of event groups (e.g., Battery, Eco Driving, Fillings, Speed Violations, etc.). These are the available types of events 2. Check the boxes next to the events you want to view, such as Fillings (fueling), Speed Violations (speeding), or Ignition (engine on/off) 1 — Show on map 2 — Clear map 3. Click theShow button How to view events on the map 1. Select one or more events from the list 2. Click Show on map The selected events will appear on the map with icons marking where they occurred. At the bottom of the map, there is a player that allows you to review the route and events during the trips. To start the player, click In the bottom-right corner of the map, there is a checkbox that you can check to follow the object on the map. You can also adjust the player’s speed to speed up or slow down the display of the route and events. If you want to remove the markers from the map, click Clear map. Fuel In the Fuel section, you can view all transactions related to fuel for the selected vehicle and period. Here, you can: See the total fuel expenses for the chosen period Review transactions from fuel cards, including: Date and time of refueling Fuel volume Amount charged for each transaction Fuel station (gas station) location How to view fuel transactions 1. In the left panel of LogBook, select the period using From / Toand choose the Vehicle 2. Go to the Fuelsection 3. Click Refresh to load all fuel transactions for the selected period. All transactions will then be displayed in the list. Tolls In the Tolls section, you can view all transactions related to toll roads for the selected vehicle and period. Here, you can see: when and where the vehicle used toll roads the amount charged for each toll transaction Media The Media section is designed for working with video and photo files captured by in-vehicle cameras. To find media files, you can use the filters at the top of the screen: Evidence Type — All – displays all media files without restrictions — Investigate – materials us=ed for incident reviews and investigations (e.g., accidents) — Trainable – recordings suitable for driver training — Vault – important materials stored in the archive for long-term retention — Custom – user-defined materials with a custom category Sub сategory – select a subcategory for filtering (e.g., NTF, FTI, etc.) Description – enter part of the text that appears in the Description column of media files After selecting the necessary filter options, click Refresh to load the media files that match your criteria. For each media file, the following information is displayed: Date – when the recording was made Group – the group assigned to the file Vehicle – the vehicle number or ID from which the file was captured Driver – the driver operating the vehicle at the moment of recording Channel – which camera the file came from (front, cabin, etc.) Alarm – which event triggered the recording (e.g., harsh braking, speeding, SOS button) Type – the file format Category – media category Description – a short explanation of what is shown or why the recording was made Location – where the recording occurred: an address or map coordinates"},{"s":"poi","t":"POI","h":["POI","How to use the module:","Access rights","How to add a point:","How to edit a point:","Saving and loading data","Working with the map"],"x":"POI The Points of Interest module allows you to mark important locations on the map. These can include offices, warehouses, service points, parking areas — anything you need to keep track of. How to use the module: Mark important locations on the map with precise GPS coordinates Use different icons to quickly identify the type of location Add descriptions for each point Group points by purpose Save and load data in Excel format Access rights You can flexibly configure access rights for each user when working with the module. This allows you to separate employee roles and protect Points of Interest (PoI) data from unauthorized changes. How to set access rights: 1. Go to Account settings 2. Open the Staff and groups tab 3. Select the desired user and double-click on them 4. In the user card, go to the Rights tab 5. Find the Points of Interest (PoI) module and check the necessary permissions: Add own POI — create personal points of interest, visible only to this user. Read non-own POI — view points created by other users. Edit non-own POI — modify points created by other users. Delete non-own POI — delete points created by other users. Now each user can create private PoIs while also working with shared points according to their assigned rights, ensuring safe and organized map management. How to add a point: Click the Add point button 2. In the pop-up window, fill in the information: enter the name of the point write a description choose an appropriate icon and color select the group to which the point will belong specify the latitude and longitude, or click on the map to indicate the location 3. Click Save How to edit a point: Double-click on the point entry in the list 2. In the pop-up window, edit the necessary information 3. Click Save Saving and loading data To save all points to an Excel file, click the download button To upload points from an Excel file, click the upload button To refresh the list of points, click the refresh button Working with the map Select the points from the list, and they will be displayed on the map with the icons you have chosen. Click on a point to view its description. Use the map tools to work with the points. For example, measure distances between them or calculate travel distances to plan routes."},{"s":"contragents-2","t":"Contragents","h":["Contragents","How to upload a contragent list","How to work with the Contragent list","How to add a sales representative","Universal sales representative","How to view a sales representative’s contragents","How to recalculate"],"x":"Contragents The Contragentsmodule, also known asSales representative, is used to track and analyze visits made by sales representatives to contragents. Contragents are clients, companies, or retail locations that your company works with. In this article, you will learn: How to upload a contragent list How to work with the contragent list How to add a sales representative What a universal sales representative is How to view a sales representative’s contragents How to recalculate visit statistics How to upload a contragent list The contragent list is uploaded to the system from a file. This is usually done by technical support upon a client’s request. To upload the list, use the page: https://<server_address>/ax/mod_tp_upload_utf8.php where <server_address> is your server address. 1. Open the upload page Enter the login and password of the account where the contragents should be uploaded. 2. Prepare the file The file must be in CSV format and should include: Contragent ID Link to a sales representative or vehicle (if used) Contragent group Location address (required) Coordinates 3. Upload the file Click Select file Choose the prepared CSV file Click Submit The system will upload the data, and the contragents will appear in the Main→ Contragents tab. How to work with the Contragent list In the Contragents tab, you can view clients, search for locations, and display them on the map. Tools available at the top of the window: Search — helps find a contragent by name or address. Start typing, and the list will be filtered automatically. Additional actions are available in the top-right corner: Export — downloads the current contragent list as a file Clear map — removes contragent markers from the map Show on map — displays contragents on the map Refresh — updates the list with current data How to add a sales representative To add a sales representative to the system: 1. Go to the Main section. Open the Online tab 2. Click Add object 3. Fill in the main object parameters Enter all required information: 1 — Vehicle number 2 — Device ID 3 — Device type 4. Assign a group Select the group the object belongs to, for example Sales representatives. If the group does not exist, create it in advance. This group typically contains sales representative objects. 5. Specify the Dealer ID Enter the Dealer ID in the object card. 1 — Dealer ID 2 — Universal sales Universal sales representative By default, reports include only contragents assigned to a sales representative. If a sales representative can visit any location (not only assigned ones), enable theUniversal salesoption. In this case, reports will include visits to all contragents, regardless of assignment. 6. Save the object After saving, the sales representative will appear in the list. How to view a sales representative’s contragents To see the list of contragents assigned to a specific sales representative: 1. Right-click on the desired sales representative and open the context menu 2. Select Contragents A window will open showing all locations assigned to the representative. Select the desired contragent in the window and it will be displayed on the map. If you need to clear the card, click the Clear map button . The Recalculate for button updates contragent visit statistics for a selected period. Use it if data has changed or if routes and visits need to be recalculated. How to recalculate 1. Enter the number of days for which data should be updated (for example, 2 or 7 days) 2. Click the ▶ button 3. The system will analyze the sales representative’s trips during the selected period and update visit and route information. After completion, a summary statistics window will appear: From date — Period used for recalculation Points Total — total number of contragents processed Optimal — number of points included in the optimal route Visits — number of recorded visits Execution time — time taken to complete the calculation Click OK to close the window"},{"s":"control-room-2","t":"Control room","h":["Control room","Key features","Event Monitoring","Event processing algorithms","Notification management"],"x":"Control room The Control room, is a module designed to monitor and manage object parameters within the system. It helps you track events related to objects and simplifies working with notifications and event-processing logic. With this module, you can not only receive alerts about events, but also define how the system should react when those events occur. Previously, this module was called GARM. You may still see this name in some parts of the interface. The current and official name is Control room, and it will fully replace the old name over time. Key features Event Monitoring The system continuously tracks events related to objects and notifies you when they occur — for example, when parameters deviate from normal values or an object’s status changes. Event processing algorithms You can configure automated scenarios that the system will execute when an event occurs. For example, the system can: send a notification log or record data create a command or task trigger other predefined actions Notification management You control: which events trigger notifications who receives those notifications which event-processing algorithm is applied in each case"},{"s":"how-to-sreate-an-algorithm","t":"How to сreate an algorithm","h":["How to сreate an algorithm","Creating an algorithm","Example of a simple algorithm"],"x":"How to сreate an algorithm The Control room module allows the system to automatically respond to events. This is done using event-processing algorithms — predefined sequences of actions that are triggered when specific conditions are met. For example, you can configure the system to: notify an operator immediately and mark an event as critical when speed or temperature exceeds a limit create a task for a dispatcher and send a reminder to a driver during long idle periods record the arrival time and send an SMS to the customer when a vehicle enters a client geofence Creating an algorithm Go to Algorithms in the top menu of the interface 2. Create a new algorithm In the Listtab, clickAdd. The algorithm builder workspace will open 3. Build the algorithm using blocks In the workspace, you can drag and drop blocks to create your scenario. Each block represents a single step that the system performs when an event is triggered. Available blocks include: Start — the entry point of the algorithm Text — displays or records a message or comment Command — a system action (for example, sending a notification) Command Template — a predefined action for reuse Condition — a logical check (for example, if temperature exceeds a threshold) Choice — a branching block with multiple possible paths End — completes the algorithm Additional blocks may be available depending on your system configuration. 4. Arrange and connect the blocks An algorithm usually starts with a Start block, then includes condition checks and actions, and ends with an End block. Connect the blocks by dragging links between them. For example, from a Condition block to a Text block, you can set up two branches: Yes — if the condition is met No — if the condition is not met Place the Webhook block in the branch from which data should be sent to the external system. If the request must be sent regardless of the condition result, add a separate Webhook block to each branch. At this stage, you are defining only the structure of the algorithm. Parameter values (thresholds, notification texts, and so on) are configured later, when the algorithm is linked to a notification. 5. Save the algorithm When the diagram is ready, click Accept. In the dialog that appears, enter the algorithm name and click OK. The algorithm will be saved and become available in the list. Example of a simple algorithm Task: record an event if the cargo temperature exceeds the allowed limit. How to build the algorithm: 1. Add a Startblock 2. Add a Condition block — it will check whether the temperature is above the limit The Temperature parameter and threshold are set later when linking the algorithm to a notification. 3. From the Condition block, add Text blocks and connect them using the Yes and No branches 4. Finish the algorithm with an End block and make sure all blocks are connected This is a basic algorithm that can be used as a foundation for automatic temperature event processing. The created algorithm does not run by itself. To activate it, you must link the algorithm to a notification. How to do this is described in the next article. For a visual walkthrough of setting up the Control Room module, watch the video “How to configure Control room”"},{"s":"how-to-link-an-algorithm-to-a-notification","t":"How to link an algorithm to a notification","h":["How to link an algorithm to a notification","Linking an algorithm to an event","How to create a tag","Configuring the Webhook block","Configure the event processing form","Finalizing notification setup","Algorithm for manual event generation","How to generate an event manually"],"x":"How to link an algorithm to a notification In this article, we explain how to configure algorithms to work through notifications. You will learn: —how to link an algorithm to a notification so it triggers automatically when an event occurs how to create a notification with an algorithm for manual event generation For an algorithm to start working and automatically respond to events, it must be linked to a notification. To use this feature, theNotifications module must be enabled in the account. Linking an algorithm to an event When linked, the algorithm will trigger automatically when the conditions are met and the event will appear in the Control room. 1. In the top panel, click the Settings icon and open Account settings 2. Go to the Notifications tab 3. Click to create a new notification 4. Link an algorithm 1) In the notification settings window, click Algorithm binding 2) Select the required algorithm from the list 3) Click the algorithm — its block diagram will open 5. Configure algorithm blocks 1) Double-click the required block 2) Enter the text and select event tags 3) Click OK How to create a tag Tags help quickly understand the nature of an event and visually highlight it in the list. If the required tag does not exist: 1. Click the Tag field 2. In the tags window, click 3. Enter the tag name and choose a color 4. Save — the tag will appear in the list and become available for selection Configuring the Webhook block The Webhookblock sends event data from OneGPS to an external system, such as a CRM, dispatch system, or corporate application. The request is sent when the algorithm reaches this block. For more information about how webhook notifications work and how to configure them, see this article. If the algorithm contains a Webhook block: 1. Select the Webhookblock in the diagram 2. Double-click the block to open its settings 3. Click the edit icon next to the block name 4. Complete the fields in theWebhook Details window Enter the address of the external system to which OneGPS should send the data. Obtain this address from the administrator or developer of the receiving system. Specify the request headers. By default, the data is sent in JSON format: [ \"ContentType: application/json\" ] Change the headers only if the receiving system requires a different data format or additional parameters. Specify the data to be sent. The following template is used by default: { \"agent_id\": \"%AGENTID%\", \"vehiclenumber\": \"%VEHNUM%\", \"ts\": \"%TS%\", \"lat\": \"%LAT%\", \"lon\": \"%LON%\", \"speed\": \"%SPEED%\", \"data\": \"%DATA%\", \"zone\": \"%ZONE%\", \"driver\": \"%DRIVER%\" } The system automatically replaces the variables enclosed in % signs with data from the event. For example, %SPEED% is replaced with the object speed, while %VEHNUM% is replaced with the vehicle number. You can modify the request body by removing unnecessary fields, adding custom fields, or using other available variables. Keep the JSON syntax valid: \"field_name\": \"%VARIABLE%\" If the JSON structure or variable syntax is invalid, the external system may not receive or process the request. %ACCOUNTID% Account ID %AGENTID% Object ID %VEHNUM% Vehicle number %TS% Timestamp %DATETIME% Date and time %LAT% Latitude %LON% Longitude %SPEED% Speed %ODO% Mileage (from sensor) %DATA% Notification data %ZONE% Geofence %ZONE_ID% First geofence ID %ZONE_NAME% First geofence name %DRIVER% Driver %MAXSPEED% Maximum speed (for overspeed notifications) %DUR% Overspeed duration %SPEEDLIMIT% Configured speed limit (Speed Control) The available variables may depend on the notification type. For example, %MAXSPEED%, %DUR%, and %SPEEDLIMIT% are intended for speeding notifications. After completing the fields, click Save, and then click OK to confirm the block settings. If different branches of the algorithm contain separate Webhook blocks, configure each block individually. Configure the event processing form You can customize how the event processing form looks for the operator. 1. In the algorithm binding window, click Settings 2. Click to insert elements Available settings: Element type — the type of block (for example, plaintext for regular text) Heading — block title Text — information shown to the operator Example: 1. Add a plaintext element 2. In Heading , enter:Event information 3. In Text, enter: Check the cargo temperature and contact the driver To add a vehicle information block, click the vehicle icon. A new block with type vehicle will appear, where you can set the title and text. 1 — To add a vehicle information. To add a driver information block, click the driver license icon. A new block with type driver will appear, where you can also define the title and text. 2 — To add a driver information After configuration, click OK — the operator will see the event form exactly in this layout. Finalizing notification setup 1. In the Notification settings window, specify: Notification name Object Notification type — Control room (GARM) Group Notification conditions (for example, the Temperature parameter and its threshold) 2. Click Save 3. The notification with the linked algorithm will appear in the list and will be sent to the Control roomwhen the conditions are met. Algorithm for manual event generation Manual event generation is used when an event does not occur automatically — for example, in emergency situations, for testing, or when an operator needs to record an event manually. How to create a notification for manual generation 1. Open Account settings and go to the Notifications tab 2. Click 3. In the notification settings, specify: 1 — Notification type Control room (GARM) 2 — Only for manual generation Object Notification name Notification type — Control room (GARM) Group Enable Only for manual generation 4. Click Algorithm binding 5. Select the required algorithm and click OK 6. Save the notification How to generate an event manually 1. Select an object in the main list 2. Right-click it and choose Generate event 3. Select the notification type for manual generation and enter the event text 4. The event will appear in the paneland will be processed according to the linked algorithm"},{"s":"how-to-view-event-history","t":"How to view event history","h":["How to view event history","Algorithm diagram","Example of an algorithm diagram"],"x":"How to view event history Event history helps you understand what happened, for which object, and how the system responded. To view the event history: 1. Go to Algorithms in the top menu and open the Historytab 2. Select a date or date range and click Search — a list of events will appear below If you need to save the history to files, use the buttons: 1 — Save history to PDF 2 — Save history to XLSX 3 — If you want to display only unprocessed events, enable the Unprocessed checkbox. 3. Click the arrow next to an event to view its details. Each event shows: Event date and time Object the event occurred for Group the event belongs to Event text Operator — the user who processed or confirmed the event Processing date — when the operator actually processed the event Algorithm diagram On the right side of the event list, you will see the diagram of the algorithm that was executed for the selected event. The diagram shows: Algorithm blocks — the steps performed by the system Algorithm branches — possible scenario paths The diagram helps you understand which steps the system executed and which path the algorithm followed while processing the event. Example of an algorithm diagram This algorithm includes the following blocks: Start — the beginning of the algorithm, the moment the event occurs Condition — a condition check (for example: “speed > 90 km/h for more than 10 seconds”) From the condition, two branches are possible: Yes — the condition is met No — the condition is not met Text / Command blocks — system actions End — completion of event processing The blue line shows the path the algorithm actually followed (for example: Start → Condition → Yes → Text → End). The gray lines show branches that were not triggered for this event."},{"s":"how-to-process-an-event","t":"How to process an event","h":["How to process an event","Module panel","Top section","Event table","How to start processing an event","Event processing window","Completing event processing","Working with the event processing window"],"x":"How to process an event Module panel When a notification with a linked algorithm is triggered, the event immediately appears in the interface — in the operator’s working panel for event processing. The Control room panelis located at the bottom of the Main section interface. To expand the panel, click the arrow . The Control roompanel shows a list of all events received from notifications that are waiting for the operator’s action. Top section The top section of the panel helps you quickly find the events you need. Available options: Event Groups — filter by event type (speed, temperature, geofence, etc.) Search — search by object name or event text Refresh — manually refreshes the list Additional actions : Event processing interval — the time during which an event is considered active and available for processing. Select the desired interval from the list to determine how long the event will appear in the list before it is automatically moved to the “unprocessed” status Page size — the number of events displayed on a single page Grouping — combines identical events for the same object into a single row for easier viewing —Processed — check this box to display processed events. By default, only unprocessed events are shown Event table Each row represents an object with a current event or a group of events. Main columns: Date — time of the latest event Object — object name (address or zone may be shown below) Stage — current step of the algorithm Operator — who has taken the event into work Event: Group — the group the event belongs to A red circle with a number shows how many events are still unprocessed Text — standard event description Alternative text — custom description from notification settings (if configured) There is an arrow on the left side of each row — click it to expand and see all events inside the group. How to start processing an event 1. When a notification is triggered, the event appears in the list on the panel 2. Click the row — the event will be shown on the map 3. Expand the row and select the required event. 4. Click: Start — to process the event Skip — if the event does not require processing Events are automatically removed from the interface after 24 hours. Event processing window After clicking Start, a step-by-step event processing window opens. It follows the logic of your algorithm. At the top: Event ID — internal event number Address — event location (can be copied by clicking the icon) Event text — event description Below: Comment — what the operator did in response to the event (for example: “Called the driver”, “Informed the dispatcher”). If the algorithm includes additional steps, the following may appear: Drop-down lists to select a reason or status Next step button — moves to the next algorithm block Completing event processing At the final step of the algorithm, there may be two buttons: Yes — confirm the event (the violation occurred, action was taken) No — alternative outcome (false alarm, no action required) After selection: The algorithm is completed The event is marked as processed The result is saved in the history If the algorithm has no branching, a Finish button is shown instead of Yes / No. Working with the event processing window In the processing window, click the map icon — a built-in map will open at the bottom. On the map you will see: The object’s position at the moment of the event The movement track before and after the event Address and coordinates Click the details icon in the processing window to open an additional panel with detailed information about the object and the event. This panel contains three sections: 1. Sensors Sensor values at the moment of the event Current sensor values 2. Processed events Dates of previous events for this object Which operators processed them Processing time, group, and event text 3. Task information Task number and status Status change times Route and actual timings This panel allows the operator to see the full context of the event without leaving the processing window."},{"s":"how-to-configure-event-parameters","t":"How to configure event parameters","h":["How to configure event parameters","Event group settings","Event stage color settings"],"x":"How to configure event parameters You can customize how events are displayed in the Control room: set event groups, colors, and processing statuses. 1. Go to Account settings 2. Open the Control room tab In the interface, this tab may still be called GARM — this is the previous name of the module. Event group settings On the left side, you will see a list of event groups coming from the ontrol room (for example: Observation, Arming/Disarming, RFID Antenna, and others). Each row represents a separate event group. For each group, you can configure: Group color — select a color from the list Colors help quickly identify the event type (for example, blue for monitoring, red for alarms) Alarm type — enable this option if events in this group should be treated as alarms Trash icon — deletes the group if it is no longer needed How to add a new event group: 1. Click Add at the bottom of the event group list 2. Select a color for the new group 3. If needed, enable the Alarm type 4. Save the changes After saving, the new group will immediately appear in the list and will be used in the Control room. Event stage color settings On the right side, you can configure the colors used to display the event status. These colors help operators understand the current state of an event at a glance. Available statuses: In progressing — the event is being processed Unprocessed — a new event Processed — the event has been fully handled by the algorithm and the operator Unprocessed (timer expired) — the event was not processed within the required time To set a color, simply select it from the dropdown list."},{"s":"climate","t":"Climate","h":["Climate"],"x":"Climate The Climate module is designed to monitor temperature and humidity at monitored sites where climate control is important, such as residential premises, warehouses, offices, and cold storage rooms. In the standard Climate mode, you can: view current sensor readings see the date and time of the last update track changes in readings display sensors on a room layout When the Velavu integration is connected, theClimate module can also be used for indoor positioning — tracking objects inside buildings. In this mode, the module displays Velavu objects, their location on the floor plan, their status, and their movement history."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-the-climate-module","t":"How to work with the Climate module","h":["How to work with the Climate module","Before you start","Example","Module interface","Room and sensor List","Room layout","How to work with the module","1. Create a room","2. Add nested elements","3. Add a sensor","Working with sensors in the list","Sensor tooltip","Mini-graph","How to delete a sensor","How to upload a room layout","How to upload","How to place sensors on the layout","Room actions menu"],"x":"How to work with the Climate module The Climate module helps you monitor temperature and humidity at monitoring objects. It allows you to organize sensors into a room structure, view their current values, track updates, and display sensors on the object layout. In this article, we'll cover: What to do before you start How the module interface is structured How to create a room How to add nested elements How to add a sensor How to work with sensors in the list How to upload a room layout How to place sensors on the layout What actions are available for a room Before you start For the Climate module to work correctly, temperature or humidity sensors must be pre-configured at the monitoring objects. The module only works with sensors that have already been added to the system and linked to monitoring objects. Therefore, before you begin, ensure that you have: 1. Created a monitoring object 2. Configured a temperature or humidity sensor on that object Example Suppose you want to monitor the temperature in a living space. Here is the workflow: 1. First, create a Home object in the monitoring system. Read how to create a monitoring objecthere 2. Then, add a temperature sensor to it. Read how to configure a sensor on an objecthere 3. After that, open the Climate module and link the sensor to a room. Module interface The module interface is divided into two main parts: Room and sensor List The left side contains the main workspace with a list of: Names of buildings, floors, and rooms Sensors linked to each room The current value of each sensor Last update time A mini-graph showing value changes At the top of the list, you will find tools: Search — a bar to quickly find a specific object or sensor Filter — to filter elements based on conditions Add button — to add a new room Refresh button — to update data Room layout If an SVG layout has been uploaded for a room, a plan with sensor markers will be displayed on the right. How to work with the module 1. Create a room To start monitoring the climate, you need to create a structure: building, floor, or room. 1. Go to the Climate section 2. Click the Add button at the top of the list 3. In the pop-up window, enter the name of the room 4. Click OK The new room will appear in the list. You can now work with it via the context menu. To edit a created room: 1. Right-click on the room 2. Select Edit from the menu 3. In the opened window, change the name 4. If necessary, select the room type: Building, Floor, or Room 5. Click Save 2. Add nested elements If your space consists of several zones, you can make the structure more detailed. For example, add floors to a building, and rooms to a floor. 1. Right-click on the desired room in the list 2. Select Add apartment from the menu 3. Specify the name of the new element 4. Select the type of room: Building, Floor, or Room 5. Click Save The new element will appear inside the selected room. You can repeat this process to build the structure you need 3. Add a sensor After creating a room, you need to link a sensor to it: 1. Select the desired room in the list 2. Right-click and select Add sensor 3. A settings window will open where you need to select: Monitoring object — the object to which the sensor is linked Sensor — the specific temperature or humidity sensor from the list 4. Click Add After saving, the sensor will appear in the Climatemodule list. The following will be displayed for it: Name — the sensor's name in the system. Current value — the temperature or humidity right now. Update time — when the data was last received. Mini-graph — how the value has changed over time. If a layout is uploaded, the sensor will also be displayed on it. Working with sensors in the list Sensor tooltip If you hover your cursor over a sensor in the list, a tooltip will appear. It shows: Sensor name Sensor ID Sensor type Current value Current status Sensors can have the following statuses: In Range — the value is within the acceptable range. Out of Range — the value has exceeded the acceptable limits. Offline — no current data is being received from the sensor. Mini-graph A mini-graph of the sensor's operation and value changes may be displayed next to the sensor in the list. It helps you quickly see how values have changed over time. If you hover over the graph, you can see a tooltip with additional information—for example, the value at a specific point in time or the time of the reading change. How to delete a sensor 1. Right-click on the sensor 2. Select Remove sensor 3. Confirm the deletion How to upload a room layout A room layout is an SVG file containing the plan of your site. After uploading the layout and adding sensors, you can: see sensors directly on the plan compare values between rooms — see the temperature difference in different zones monitor the situation visually How to upload 1. Select the desired room in the list. 2. Right-click and select Load SVG 3. Select the SVG file with the room layout 4. Specify the width and height of the layout — these are the dimensions in which the layout will be displayed in the interface 5. Click Save After this, the layout will appear in the right part of the module window. How to place sensors on the layout After uploading the SVG layout, added sensors will automatically appear on the plan. You can drag and drop sensors with your mouse to place them according to their real-world location. When you hover over a sensor on the layout, a tooltip appears with its name and current value. Room actions menu To open the actions menu, right-click on a room. Through this menu, you can: Edit element Add apartment — create a nested element in the structure Upload layout — add an SVG file so the layout appears on the right Add sensor — connect a temperature or humidity sensor Delete — remove the room"},{"s":"how-to-work-with-the-velavu-integration","t":"How to work with the Velavu integration","h":["How to work with the Velavu integration"],"x":"How to work with the Velavu integration Velavu is an indoor positioning system that lets you track the location of objects inside buildings. It uses fixed positioning points — anchors — and movable tags to determine the location of equipment, vehicles, assets, or personnel on a building or floor plan. The Velavu integration with OneGPS allows you to work with this data directly in the system interface. With the Velavu integration in OneGPS, you can: load the structure of sites, buildings, and floors from Velavu view the location of objects inside buildings open detailed device information track movement history"},{"s":"how-to-set-up-the-velavu-integration","t":"How to set up the Velavu integration","h":["How to set up the Velavu integration"],"x":"How to set up the Velavu integration The Velavu integration is configured at the partner account level through configuration parameters. Only users with main administrator privileges can set up this integration. If you don't have the required access level, please reach out to our support team for assistance. 1. In the admin panel, go to the Partners section 2. Double-click on the selected partner — the partner card will open 3. Open the Config tab 4. Click the Add button 5. From the dropdown list, select the Velavu key. In the parameter value field, paste your Velavu configuration in JSON format. Example Velavu JSON configuration [ { \"broker\": \"iot.velavu.com\", \"port\": \"8883\", \"customer_id\": \"YOUR_CUSTOMER_ID\", \"api_token\": \"YOUR_API_TOKEN\", \"cert\": \"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\\nYOUR_CERTIFICATE\\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----\", \"key\": \"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nYOUR_PRIVATE_KEY\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\" } ] broker Velavu MQTT broker address port Connection port customer_id Your customer ID in the Velavu system api_token Velavu API access token cert SSL certificate for the connection key SSL private key You can get all of these details (customer_id, api_token, certificate, and key) from your Velavu account. 6. Save the configuration. Once saved, the integration will be available for all accounts associated with this partner."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-velavu-objects","t":"How to work with Velavu objects","h":["How to work with Velavu objects","How to switch to Velavu mode","How to get started with Velavu","Synchronizing the Velavu structure","How to interact with Velavu objects","What objects are displayed in Velavu","Color coding of Velavu objects","Tooltip on hover","Viewing device details","Overwiew","Location","Device status","Environment","Assignment","Technical data","Velavu object menu","Velavu device menu","Velavu geofences"],"x":"How to work with Velavu objects With the Velavu integration in OneGPS, you can track the location of objects inside buildings in real time — see which floor, room, or zone your equipment and personnel are in. In this article, we'll cover: How to switch to Velavu mode How to get started with Velavu How to perform synchronization How to interact with devices How to view device details Object and device menus Velavu geofences How to switch to Velavu mode Once the Velavu integration is connected, the Climate module lets you switch between two modes: Standard — the regular Climate module mode for working with temperature and humidity sensors Velavu — the mode for displaying Velavu objects 1. Open the Climate module 2. On the Climate tab, use the dropdown list to switch the mode from Normal to Velavu 3. The interface will switch to Velavu mode In Velavu mode, instead of the usual temperature and humidity sensors, you'll see a tree of Velavu objects. For each item in the tree, the following is typically displayed: Name — for example, Velavu-00Q6N Type — for example: ANCHOR — anchor TAG — tag Device — device At the top of the window in Velavu mode, you have access to: Filter — to narrow down the items shown Search — to quickly find a device, floor, or site Sync button — to load or update the structure from Velavu Add button — lets you manually add a room if you need to extend the structure How to get started with Velavu To make Velavu objects appear in the Climate module, you first need to set up the objects in the system. 1. Create a monitoring object. For details on how to create an object, see the separate article 2. In the object's card, specify the object identifier from the Velavu system 3. After this, you can configure additional object settings, such as: choosing an icon setting a color filling in other parameters 4. Run synchronization Once your objects are ready, go to the Climate module and synchronize the structure. Synchronizing the Velavu structure Synchronization loads the Velavu object structure and displays it in the Climate module: 1. Open the Climate module 2. Switch to Velavu mode 3. Click the Sync button 4. The system will load buildings, floors, and devices from the Velavu API. After synchronization, the entire structure will appear in the object tree, grouped by accounts, buildings, and floors. How to interact with Velavu objects If you click an object in the tree on the left, the corresponding object on the floor plan will be highlighted. If you click an object on the floor plan, it will be automatically selected in the object list. What objects are displayed in Velavu The module can show several types of objects: Anchors — fixed reference points used to calculate the position of other devices Tags — markers usually attached to equipment, people, or other objects. Once anchors are set up, the position of tags is calculated automatically Proximity tags — tags that are detected within the range of another object Color coding of Velavu objects Velavu objects appear on the plan as colored circles. The color indicates the object's type and status: Blue Anchor A fixed positioning point installed in the room Green Tag A movable object — equipment, vehicle, or personnel Yellow Proximity Tag A tag located within the range of another object Red Offline The object isn't transmitting data or is out of coverage Tooltip on hover If you hover over an object in the list or on the plan, a tooltip with brief information about the object will appear. The tooltip shows: Name — the object's name in the system Agent ID — the object's internal identifier Device ID — the Velavu device identifier Location type — the type of object placement Coordinates — the object's current coordinates Site — the name of the site the object belongs to Floor — the floor the object is on Status — the device's current state: online or offline This tooltip helps you get key information about an object without opening its full device card. Viewing device details A detailed card with full information is available for every Velavu device. To open it, click the view icon next to the object in the tree. The device card opens in a separate window with several tabs: Overwiew This tab shows general details about the device: where it's located, what it's linked to, and its current status. Device name — the device's name in the Velavu system, e.g., Velavu-Sp8mv Device ID — the unique device identifier, e.g., Sp8mv Site — the name of the site where the device is installed, e.g., GARGASH Floor — the floor where the device is located, e.g., Level 1 - GR001 Connection status — the device's current state: online or offline Below is the Assignment block, which may show information about the asset: Asset name — which asset the device is linked to Asset group — the group the asset belongs to Asset category — the asset's category Operation — the asset's current action or state At the bottom of the tab, you'll see: Last update — the date and time when data was last received from the device Location TheLocation tab shows information about where the device is located. Here you can see: Floor name — which floor the device is on Site name — which site the device is at Location type — how the device is placed Latitude — the current latitude value Longitude — the current longitude value Proximity device ID — the identifier of the nearest device Proximity distance — the distance to the nearest device Device status The Device status tab shows the device's current technical condition. This block may show: Battery level — the battery charge percentage, e.g., 90% Battery charging — whether the device is plugged in to charge Here you'll see: Connection status — online or offline RSSI — the signal strength Gateway ID — the identifier of the gateway the device is connected through Signal level — the connection quality This block shows: Motion detection — whether the object is moving or stationary Last activity — the date and time of the device's last activity Environment This tab shows data from environmental sensors, if the device supports temperature, humidity, or pressure measurement. Here you can see: Temperature — current value in °C Humidity — current value in % Pressure — current value in kPa Below is the History charts block. If data is available, it shows charts of how temperature, humidity, and pressure have changed over time. Above the charts, you can choose the time period: 1h — 1 hour, 6h — 6 hours, 1d — 1 day, 7d — 7 days, 30d — 30 days, All — the full available period. Assignment The Assignment tab shows information about which asset the device is linked to. Here you'll typically see: Asset name — which asset the device is linked to Asset group — the group the asset belongs to Asset category — the asset's category Operation — the asset's current action or state If asset data isn't filled in, the fields may show N/A. Technical data The Technical data tab shows additional service information about the device. Depending on the device, the following may be available: Firmware / model Beacon interval Heartbeat frequency Status update frequency Temperature and humidity update frequency Beacon transmission power Site ID Floor ID Velavu object menu A context menu with additional actions is available for Velavu floors and sites. To open the menu of object actions, right-click on a Velavu object. Through this menu you can: Edit — change the room's name and type Add apartment — create a nested item in the structure Show anchors — display fixed positioning points on the object plan. By default, anchors are hidden. To show anchors, choose Show anchors in the menu. They will appear on the plan and in the list. To hide them, choose Hide anchors in the menu. Add object — link a Velavu monitoring object to a room Delete — remove a room from the structure. Velavu device menu For an individual Velavu device, the context menu offers: Remove object This action removes the link between the device and theVelavu room. Velavu geofences Velavu geofences are loaded into the system automatically from Velavu. You don't need to create them manually in OneGPS. Just set up your sites and buildings in Velavu, then click the Sync button in Velavu mode within the Climate module — and the system will automatically import the geofences, the site and floor structure, and the linked devices. After that, Velavu geozones will appear in the Geofences section in a dedicated folder."},{"s":"how-to-view-velavu-object-history","t":"How to view Velavu object history","h":["How to view Velavu object history","How to open Velavu history","Customize your column display","What you can do in history","View the object on the map","Play back movements","View the device card"],"x":"How to view Velavu object history The History tab allows you to view the history of Velavu objects for a selected period. You can: see where an object was located at a specific moment check which room it was in track the object's movement on the map How to open Velavu history 1. Go to the History tab 2. In the Viewfield, select Velavu mode 3. Specify: Period from Period to 4. In the Objects field, select one or more Velavu objects for which you want to view the history After this, records for the selected objects will appear in the table below. Typically, the following columns are displayed for each record: Name — the name of the Velavu object RFID — if RFID data is available Type — the object type, e.g., Anchor (ANCHOR) or Tag (TAG) Date — the date and time of the record Building — the building associated with the record Floor — the floor where the object was located Duration — how long the object remained in that state or location Sensor — related sensor data, if available Actions — buttons for viewing and playback Customize your column display Click the arrow icon in any column header. A settings menu will appear where you can: Sort data in ascending or descending order Choose which columns to display on the screen To configure which columns to show: Click Columns — a list of available display parameters will open Check the boxes next to the parameters you want to see What you can do in history View records by object History is grouped by each selected object. You can expand the list for any object to view its history chronologically. View the object on the map When you select a record in the list, a map will appear on the right. The map will show the object's position at the time of that specific record. Play back movements Next to each device, there is a player control — a play button and a timeline slider. Use the player to see how the device moved across the site over time. View the device card Next to each record, there is an icon . Click it to open the Velavu device card. The card opens with the exact data that was recorded at the moment that specific history entry was created."},{"s":"rfid-tags-2","t":"RFID Tags","h":["RFID Tags","How RFID Tags work","RFID use cases","Automatic trailer attachment","Route verification","Container service tracking","Vehicle Identification","Driver identification"],"x":"RFID Tags The RFID Tags module is used to create and manage RFID tags for automatic object identification within the monitoring system. RFID tags help the system identify which object a vehicle has interacted with. For example, they can be used to determine which container was serviced by a garbage truck or which checkpoint a vehicle has visited. In this module, you can: add RFID tags and organize them into groups check when a tag was last read view a tag's location on the map review the tag's read history How RFID Tags work RFID requires two main components: RFID reader — a device installed on a vehicle that detects nearby RFID tags RFID tag — a device with a unique identifier attached to a container, trailer, piece of equipment, or another object When an RFID tag enters the reader's detection range: 1. The RFID reader installed on the vehicle detects the tag 2. The reader reads the tag's unique identifier and sends it to OneGPS 3. OneGPS searches for an RFID tag with the same identifier in the RFID tag directory 4. If a matching tag is found, the system updates its latest read information 5. If any automated actions are configured for the tag or its associated object, OneGPS performs them automatically RFID use cases Automatic trailer attachment An RFID tag is installed on the trailer, while the RFID reader is installed on the towing vehicle. When the trailer enters the reader's range, OneGPS identifies the tag and can automatically attach the trailer to the vehicle. Route verification RFID or BLE tags are installed at checkpoints such as bus stops, warehouses, company facilities, or other locations. When a vehicle passes within range of a tag, OneGPS records the checkpoint visit. Container service tracking RFID tags are attached to containers, and RFID readers are installed on garbage trucks or other service vehicles. When a tag is read, OneGPS records information about the serviced container, including the time, location, and the vehicle that performed the service. Vehicle Identification RFID tags can be used to automatically identify vehicles when they enter company premises, pass through checkpoints, or perform operational tasks. Driver identification An RFID tag can be assigned to a driver. After the tag is read, OneGPS identifies the driver and can associate trips, events, or completed tasks with that driver."},{"s":"how-to-set-up-rfid","t":"How to set up RFID","h":["How to set up RFID","1. Install the hardware","2. Configure the RFID Reader in OneGPS","3. Add RFID Tags to the directory","RFID field in the object card"],"x":"How to set up RFID Before using RFID, install an RFID reader, connect it to a tracking device, and configure it to send tag identifiers to OneGPS. 1. Install the hardware 1. Install an RFID reader on the vehicle or other equipment 2. Connect the RFID reader to the tracking device that sends data to OneGPS 3. Attach RFID tags to the objects you want to identify. For example, tags can be installed on containers, trailers, equipment, checkpoints, and other stationary or movable assets 2. Configure the RFID Reader in OneGPS 1. Add an RFID sensor to the object's sensor list 2. Select the device field that receives the RFID tag identifier from the reader 3. Save the settings After the configuration is complete, OneGPS will begin receiving tag identifiers from the tracking device and matching them with RFID tags stored in the RFID tag directory. If the RFID reader sends an identifier that does not exist in the RFID tag directory, OneGPS cannot match it to an existing tag. In this case, the tag may be displayed as Unknown. Once an RFID tag with the corresponding identifier is added to the system, OneGPS will recognize it correctly during subsequent reads. 3. Add RFID Tags to the directory Add the RFID tags used by your assets to the RFID tag directory. For each tag, specify its unique ID and any other required information. Once the tag is saved, OneGPS will be able to match identifiers received from the RFID reader with the corresponding RFID tags. For detailed instructions, see Managing RFID Tags. RFID field in the object card The object card includes an RFID field where you can specify the identifier of the RFID tag associated with the object. For example, if the monitored object is a container equipped with an RFID tag, enter the tag's identifier in this field. OneGPS uses this information in the RFID module and when generating RFID-related reports."},{"s":"how-to-manage-rfid","t":"How to manage RFID","h":["How to manage RFID","How to add an RFID Tag","How to create an RFID Tag group","How to edit an RFID Tag","How to find an RFID Tag","Toolbar commands","RFID Tag actions"],"x":"How to manage RFID The RFID Tags module allows you to add and edit RFID tags, organize them into groups, search for tags, and import data from Excel. How to add an RFID Tag To create a new RFID tag: 1. Open the RFID Tags tab 2. Click Add The new RFID Tagform opens. Fill in the required fields: ID — the unique identifier of the RFID tag. It is usually entered manually using the identifier transmitted by the RFID reader when the tag is read. In some cases, the ID may be assigned automatically after the tag is read for the first time. Name — the name displayed for the RFID tag in the system. Group — the group to which the RFID tag belongs. Description — additional information about the RFID tag. Latitude and Longitude — the approximate location of the RFID tag. For example, if the tag is installed at a container site, checkpoint, or another stationary location, you can store its coordinates in the system. Fixed locarion— enable this option if the RFID tag is installed in a permanent location and is not expected to move. A fixed-location RFID tag can be linked to a geofence , route point , or bus stop . OneGPS then takes tag detections into account when determining visits to the linked location 3. Click Save. The RFID tag will be added to the list How to create an RFID Tag group If the required group does not already exist, you can create it while adding or editing an RFID tag. 1. Enter a new group name in the Groupfield 2. Complete the remaining RFID tag fields 3. Click Save The new group will be created automatically and will be available when adding future RFID tags. How to edit an RFID Tag 1. Double-click the RFID tag in the list 2. Modify the required fields 3. Click Save How to find an RFID Tag You can search for RFID tags using either the Group filter or the search box. 1 — Search by Group Select a group above the list. The table will display only the RFID tags that belong to the selected group. 2 — Search by Name or ID Enter the RFID tag name or ID in the search box. Only matching RFID tags will remain in the list. To display all RFID tags again, clear the search field. Toolbar commands The toolbar provides commands for creating, updating, importing, exporting, and displaying RFID tags. Add — create a new RFID tag Export to Excel — export the RFID tag list to an XLSX file Import from Excel — import RFID tags from an XLSX file to add new tags or update existing ones. Before importing, make sure the file matches the required Excel template Download Excel template — download the Excel template for importing RFID tags. Fill in the required fields, save the file, and then use Import from Excel to upload it Refresh — refresh the RFID tag list without reloading the page Icon color settings — configure how RFID tag icons are colored on the map based on how recently data was received Show on map — display the selected RFID tags on the map. Select one or more tags before using this command Clear map — remove all displayed RFID tags from the map RFID Tag actions Additional actions are available for each RFID tag in the list. Show on map — display the last known location of the RFID tag on the map Tag history — view the RFID tag's read history Delete tag — remove the RFID tag from the system"},{"s":"how-to-check-an-rfid-tag-s-location-and-history","t":"How to check an RFID Tag's location and history","h":["How to check an RFID Tag's location and history","How to check the latest read","How to show a single RFID Tag on the map","How to show multiple RFID Tags on the map","How to view RFID Tag history","How to configure marker colors"],"x":"How to check an RFID Tag's location and history The RFID Tags module allows you to check when a tag was last read, display its location on the map, and view its read history. How to check the latest read Whenever a vehicle detects an RFID tag, OneGPS automatically updates the tag information. The list displays the following information: 1. Updated — the date and time when the tag information was last updated. 2. Last update — the time elapsed since the tag was last read. 3. Vehicle — the monitored vehicle that most recently read the RFID tag. The vehicle is displayed after the tag has been successfully read. Physical contact between the RFID reader and the RFID tag is not required. The tag only needs to be within the reader's detection range. How to show a single RFID Tag on the map 1. Open the action menu for the RFID tag 2. SelectShow on map The map will automatically center on the tag's last known location and display a marker. Click the marker to open an information pop-up showing: the date and time of the last update the tag name the tag ID coordinates that can be copied the address To close the pop-up, click the × in the upper-right corner or select another marker. How to show multiple RFID Tags on the map 1. Select one or more RFID tags in the list 2. Click Show on map on the toolbar Markers for the selected RFID tags will appear on the map. To remove all displayed markers, click Clear map. How to view RFID Tag history 1. Open the action menu for the RFID tag 2. Select Tag history Each record includes: the vehicle that read the RFID tag the date and time of the read the location where the tag was read How to configure marker colors Marker colors indicate how much time has passed since the latest data was received from an RFID tag. By default, the following rules are applied: Green — data was received within the last four hours Red — more than four hours have passed since the last data was received Marker colors help you quickly identify RFID tags whose information has not been updated recently. To configure marker colors: 1. Click Icon color settings on the toolbar 2. In the RFID Tag icon colors window, click Add 3. Create a rule and specify: the marker color the comparison condition the time threshold in hours 4. Click Save to apply the new rules"},{"s":"analytics-","t":"Analytics","h":["Analytics"],"x":"Analytics This may take a while to load the first time. But this page will load quickly in the future. Open the Panel tab. Select an object from the list. Select a date. View the vehicle info block. Module operation reports or data from the Analytics module are not provided. You can work with reports by highlighting them on the chart, as shown below.  Important! Depending on the modules connected, for example Eco Driving, the PTM module or violations in Geofences, these values ​​will also be displayed in graphs on the corresponding topics. If these modules are not enabled, there will be no graph. From the geozones block, a transition to the \"Online\" tab has been implemented with the selection of these vehicles in the tree of objects located in the specified geozones. Click on the column indicating the geozone. Right-click on the menu. \"Show vehicles on the main map\". Only those objects that are in this zone will be selected in the object tree. Return to the module in the usual way. This example shows an object that the system has allocated for this geozone. Data refresh button. Select Template A user can create their own profile and set it as default. A system administrator can create a profile and set it as default for a partner account. In this case, all users will see this profile as the default profile. Create template Once you have selected additional options, click \"Save as new\". Right click on the chart and the system will offer several reports to build.  Generate reports on relevant topics, for example, reports related to mileage from the mileage chart, fuel consumption from the fuel chart, equipment operation, and others. Trip and Parkings Trip and Parkings (with driver) The reports will be generated in the regular \"Reports\" tab with all the details about duration, distance, address and other fields.  To return to analytics from the Reports section, go to the Dashboard tab in the usual way."},{"s":"engine-lock-","t":"Engine lock","h":["Engine lock","How to enable the module","Command configuration","How to lock or unlock the engine"],"x":"Engine lock The Engine lock module allows you to remotely lock and unlock a vehicle through the system. To use this feature, a special engine lock relay must be installed in the vehicle. How to enable the module The setup is done in the Admin panel at the account level. 1. Open the account card 2. Right-click the account to open the context menu and select Edit 3. Go to the Modules tab 4. Enable the Engine lock module 5. Save the changes Command configuration The module can be linked to your command templates. 1. In the Admin panel, open the account card. 2. Go to the Module settings tab 3. Select the Engine blocking module 4. In the Configs section, add the configuration: arm_template_id — ID of the command template used to lock the engine disarm_template_id — ID of the command template used to unlock the engine In the Valuefield, enter the ID of the created command template 5. Save the account settings After that: when clicking Lock, the system will send the command from arm_template_id when clicking Unlock, the system will send the command from disarm_template_id How to lock or unlock the engine 1. In the user panel, select the required vehicle 2. Right-click the vehicle to open the context menu 3. Select Block or Unblock 4. Enter the account password to confirm and click the appropriate button After confirmation, the command will be sent to the device. The account password specified in the account card is used for confirmation."},{"s":"weighing","t":"Weighing","h":["Weighing","How it works","How to add weighing sensors","Weighing history"],"x":"Weighing The Weighingmodule automatically determines the weight of cargo using sensors installed on vehicles. With this module, you can: monitor the weight of vehicles add and configure weighing checkpoints view the history of all weighings keep track of transported materials export weighing data for reports How it works 1. Weighing sensors are installed on the vehicle, which transmit data to OneGPS 2. A weighing checkpoint is created on the map 3. When an empty vehicle crosses the checkpoint, OneGPS records the current weight 4. After loading the material, such as gravel, sand, or grain, the vehicle crosses the checkpoint again 5. The system records the new weight and calculates the cargo mass How to add weighing sensors 1. Go to the Main section and select the Weighingtab 2. Click the Add button 3. Fill in the fields in the pop-up window: ID — unique identifier for the weighing sensor Name — name of the weighing sensor Description — additional information about the weighing sensor Latitude and Longitude — coordinates of the weighing sensor's location 4. Click Save New weighing sensors will be added to the list. If the new sensors do not appear in the list, click Refresh . To change the parameters of a weighing sensor, double-click on the desired sensor in the list — a window with parameters will open. Edit the necessary fields and save the changes. Weighing history To view the weighing history for a specific sensor, click the History icon in the list of weighing sensors. Here you will see data about all weighings, including information about the vehicles and cargo: Vehicle Number Vehicle Type First time and Second time — entry and exit times First weight and second Weight — weight before and after weighing Cargo Type, e.g., \"Gravel 5-20\" Client name To save the weighing information, click the Exportbutton — the data will be saved in CSV format."},{"s":"geofences-","t":"Geofences","h":["Geofences","What is a geofence?"],"x":"Geofences The Geofences module is designed for territorial control of monitored objects. With this module, you can: Track when an object enters or leaves a specific area Monitor idle time within a zone and receive alerts if the allowed time is exceeded Get reports on the movements and activities of objects within defined areas What is a geofence? A geofence is a designated area on the map that you create to monitor the movement of your objects. A geofence can have any shape: Polygon — for example, the territory of a facility or an entire city Circle — a zone with any radius you choose Line — for example, a specific street or road segment You can set up automatic notifications for entering or leaving a geofence, and view detailed reports on all geofence-related events in the Reports section. You need the appropriate access rights to create and manage geofences."},{"s":"how-to-create-a-ceofence","t":"How to create a ceofence","h":["How to create a ceofence"],"x":"How to create a ceofence To create a geofence: 1. In the Main section, select theGeofencestab 1 — Create a group of geofences 2 — Types of geozones 2. Create a group of geofences For better organization, you can group geofences by region or type: 1) Click the group icon to create a new geofence group 2) Enter a group name and click Save 3. Choose the type of geofence and add it: Polygon — mark the corners of the area on the map to create a polygon Circle — click and hold the left mouse button, then drag to set the radius. You can adjust the radius by dragging the markers on the circle, or move the entire zone by dragging its center Line — mark two or more points on the map to create a segment or route 4. Enter a name for the geofence and choose a color for easy identification 5. Click Save — your geofence will appear in the list"},{"s":"how-to-link-a-geofence-to-an-object","t":"How to link a geofence to an object","h":["How to link a geofence to an object","How to view geofence history"],"x":"How to link a geofence to an object Linking a geofence to an object allows you to automatically track its movements, monitor idle time and speed, and receive notifications about any violations. How to set it up: 1. Open the settings for the desired geofence 2. Link objects to the geofence: Check the boxes next to the objects you want to associate with this geofence. The system will track their movements in relation to the selected zone and apply all the configured parameters. 3. Configure additional parameters: Idle control: Set the number of minutes after which staying in the zone is considered idling. For example, if you set 15 minutes, the system will record an idle event if the object stays in the zone longer than that. If you set 0, idle time will not be tracked for this zone (this is the default). Idle data can be viewed in special idle reports and summary reports. Speed limit: Set a speed limit for objects within this geofence. Zone type: Choose whether the zone is a working or restricted area: — Working geofence: an area where the object should be and perform its tasks (e.g., warehouse, construction site, route) — Restricted geofence: an area where the object is not allowed to enter (e.g., private property, hazardous or closed zones, military sites) Alert type: Set up the type of notification for this zone: — Idle in zone: the system will send a notification if the object stays in the zone longer than the set time — Border crossing: the system will send a notification when the object enters or leaves the geofence — Idle and border crossing: the system will send a notification for either event Geofence recalculation: If you have changed the geofence settings or just created a new one, you can recalculate events for this zone for a previous period (for example, the last 7 days). To do this, check the box for Recalculate geofence forand select the number of days. The system will recalculate all events (entries, exits, idling, etc.) for the selected period using the new settings. 4. Click Save to apply the geofence settings. How to view geofence history In the geofence settings, there is aShow historybutton Click it to see: The full history of object movements in this zone Dates and times of each object's entry and exit Duration of time spent in the zone If you want to save the movement or event history for the geofence, click the Save as CSVbutton."},{"s":"geofences-in-the-object-card","t":"Geofences in the object card","h":["Geofences in the object card"],"x":"Geofences in the object card When the Geofences module is enabled, a separate Geofences tab appears in the object’s card. In this tab, you can: link the object to specific geofences — check the boxes next to the geofences you want to associate with this object set individual parameters for the object: Idle control — specify the number of minutes after which staying in the zone is considered idling. For example, if you set 15 minutes, the system will record an idle event if the object stays in the zone longer than that. If you set 0, idle time will not be tracked for this zone (this is the default) Speed imit — set a speed limit for the object within this geofence Zone type — choose whether the zone is a working or restricted area for this object: — Working geofence: An area where the object should be and perform its tasks (e.g., warehouse, construction site, route) — Restricted geofence: An area where the object is not allowed to enter (e.g., private property, hazardous or closed zones, military sites) Alert type — set up the type of notification for this zone: — Idle in zone: The system will send a notification if the object stays in the zone longer than the set time — Border crossing: The system will send a notification when the object enters or leaves the geofence — Idle and border crossing: The system will send a notification for either event After you have filled in all the parameters, don’t forget to click Save to apply the changes."},{"s":"linking-an-rfid-tag-to-a-geofence","t":"Linking an RFID tag to a geofence","h":["Linking an RFID tag to a geofence","How to prepare an RFID tag","How to link an RFID tag","How a visit is detected","How to view tag details on the map"],"x":"Linking an RFID tag to a geofence A fixed-location RFID tag can be linked to a geofence. OneGPS takes tag detections into account when identifying visits to the geofence. This helps record visits correctly even when the object’s GPS coordinates are temporarily inaccurate. RFID tag management is available if: you have administrator permissions the RFID Tags module is enabled for the account the geofence belongs to the account and can be edited How to prepare an RFID tag Before linking an RFID tag, add it to the system: 1. Open Main and select RFID Tags 2. Create a new tag or edit an existing one 3. Enter the tag ID. It must match the identifier transmitted by the RFID reader 4. Enter the Latitude and Longitude. The tag’s coordinates must be within the geofence to which you want to link it 5. Select Fixed Location 6. Click Save If the tag’s coordinates are outside the geofence, OneGPS will not allow the tag to be linked. Check the tag’s coordinates or the geofence boundaries. How to link an RFID tag 1. Open Main and select Geofences 2. Select the checkbox next to the required geofence to display it on the map 3. Make sure the RFID tag is located within the geofence 4. Right-click the geofence and select Manage RFID Tags 5. Find the required tag and select its checkbox in the Link column To find a tag by its name or ID, use the Search field. To display only the tags already linked to the selected geofence, select Linked. 6. Click Save The RFID tag is now linked to the geofence. If the tag’s coordinates are outside the geofence, OneGPS will not allow the tag to be linked. How a visit is detected When an object detects an RFID tag linked to a geofence, OneGPS processes the detection regardless of the object’s current GPS coordinates. As a result, a visit can be recorded even if GPS data places the object outside the geofence. A geofence visit is the period between entry and exit, rather than each individual detection of the RFID tag. An exit is recorded when both of the following conditions are met: the object no longer detects the linked RFID tag the object’s GPS coordinates are outside the geofence Therefore, multiple RFID tag detections do not always create separate visits. For example, if an object moves away from the tag and then detects it again while its GPS coordinates remain within the geofence, the current visit does not end. How to view tag details on the map Click the RFID tag marker on the map to open its tooltip. The tooltip displays: the date and time of the latest detection the tag name the tag UID the coordinates the address The tooltip header shows the object that most recently detected the RFID tag. If the tag has not been detected yet, no object information is available."},{"s":"importing-and-exporting-geofences-in-kml-format","t":"Importing and exporting geofences in KML format","h":["Importing and exporting geofences in KML format","Importing geofences","Exporting geofences"],"x":"Importing and exporting geofences in KML format To make working with geofences easier, you can upload them into the monitoring system from files, or save created geofences to a file for copying or transferring to other devices. Importing geofences Import allows you to add geofences to the system that were previously created or obtained from other sources. 1. Go to the Geofences tab 2. Click theImport button 3. Select a file in KML or KMZ format from your computer KML or KMZ is a special file format for storing geographic data such as points, lines, and areas on a map. 4. Specify the group where you want to upload the geofences 5. Confirm the import — the selected geofences will appear in the list and be displayed on the map Exporting geofences Export allows you to save geofences to a file for use in other systems. 1. In the Geofences tab, check the geofences you want to save 2. Click the Export button 3. Choose the file format — KML or KMZ. 4. Specify the group from which the geofences will be exported 5. Save the resulting file to your device"},{"s":"how-to-delete-a-geofence","t":"How to delete a geofence","h":["How to delete a geofence","Deleting a single geofence","Deleting multiple geofences at once"],"x":"How to delete a geofence Deleting a single geofence To remove an unwanted geofence, find it in the list and click the trash bin icon next to its name. Confirm the deletion, and the geofence will be removed from the list and will no longer appear on the map. Deleting multiple geofences at once If you want to delete several geofences at the same time: 1. Hold down the Ctrlkey and select all the geofences you want to delete in the list 2. Right-click on the selected geofences and chooseDelete 3. Confirm the deletion"},{"s":"toll-roads","t":"Toll roads","h":["Toll roads"],"x":"Toll roads Toll roads is a feature of the Geofences module that allows you to track road segments where a toll is charged. Use Toll roads to: Control expenses — always know when and how often your vehicles travel on toll roads Plan routes — take toll segments into account when building routes in advance Analyze trips — all toll road data is saved and available in reports To better understand how Toll roads work, watch the short video tutorial on YouTube"},{"s":"how-to-sreate-a-toll-road","t":"How to сreate a toll road","h":["How to сreate a toll road"],"x":"How to сreate a toll road If your monitored object’s route includes segments with tolls, you can account for them in the system using the Toll roads feature. To create a toll road: 1. Create a geofence of the Polyline type 2. Draw the required road segment on the map Mark two or more points on the map to create the desired section or route that will be considered a toll road. 3. Check the Toll road box When creating the zone, select the Toll roadoption so the system recognizes this segment as a toll road. 4. Set the color and width of the line for easy identification 5. Click Save— the toll road will appear in your list of zones"},{"s":"how-to-work-with-a-toll-road","t":"How to work with a toll road","h":["How to work with a toll road","How to add toll gates","Simple gates","Complex gates","How to use the scheduler","How to delete a toll road"],"x":"How to work with a toll road A special menu is provided for configuring toll roads. Click on the desired toll road to open the menu, where you’ll find the following options: Gates — set up entry and exit points Scheduler — define conditions and rates for the toll road Delete — remove the toll road How to add toll gates Gates are points on the toll road that record when an object enters or exits the toll section. With gates, the system can accurately determine when a vehicle starts and finishes traveling on the toll road. To ensure precise tracking, add entry and exit points (gates): 1. Open the toll road menu and select Gates 2. Click to add a new gate 3. Enter a name for the gate 4. Choose the gate type: Entrance — marks the entry point to the toll road Exit — marks the exit point from the toll road Complex — can serve as both entry and exit 5. A gate marker will appear on the map. You can move it to the desired location 6. After adding the gates, click Save Depending on the structure of the toll road and traffic patterns, you can use different gate configurations. This allows you to accurately track all trips and correctly calculate tolls for any route. There are two main scenarios: simple gates and complex gates. Simple gates Simple gates are when entry and exit points are on the same line (the same toll road segment). You simply mark the start and end of the toll section, and the system records all trips between these two points. Example: A vehicle enters the toll road at \"Gate 1\" and exits at \"Gate 2\" on the same segment. Even if GPS is temporarily lost (for example, in a tunnel), the system will still remember the entry and exit points and correctly calculate the toll. Complex gates Complex gates are when entry and exit can be on different lines (different toll road segments). You can link one line as the entry and another as the exit, creating as many combinations as needed. These gates are useful for complex traffic patterns, such as roads with multiple routes, interchanges, entries, and exits. They allow you to track trips between different toll road segments. Example: A vehicle enters the toll road at \"Gate A\" (on one segment) and exits at \"Gate B\" (on another segment). You can create multiple such links, for example: \"Entry 1\" — \"Exit 1\", \"Entry 2\" — \"Exit 2\", etc. How to create complex gates: 1. Create toll roads on the map: Draw the first toll road segment as a polyline, set its width, and give it a clear name, e.g., Polyline_North. Draw the second toll road segment and name it, e.g., Polyline_South. 2. Go to the settings of one of the toll roads, e.g., Polyline_North 3. In the toll road menu, select Gates 4. Click to add a gate 5. Enter a name for the gate, e.g., North_to_South 6. Select the gate type — Complex 7. In the Link zonefield, select the second toll road you want to link to, e.g., Polyline_South. 8. Click Save 9. Set up rates and schedules: For this composite gate pair, open the Scheduler and set individual rates and schedules for the route between, for example, Polyline_North — Polyline_South. 10. If needed, create the reverse direction for the second toll road. This way, if a vehicle enters the toll road through a gate on Polyline_North and exits through a gate on Polyline_South, the system recognizes this as the North_to_South route and applies the corresponding rate and schedule. How to use the scheduler The Scheduler is a tool for setting up toll road usage conditions. How to configure the scheduler: 1. Open the toll road menu 2. Select Scheduler 3. In the Scheduler window, set the parameters: Traffic interims: For each day of the week (weekdays, Saturday, Sunday), set the required time intervals: — normal — high — heavy For each period, specify your own time frames, e.g., 08:00–10:00 — high traffic. Rates for different vehicle types: For each time period, specify the toll for: — motorcycles and cars — buses and trucks — trucks with trailers Tolerance: Click the tolerance button and specify the allowed delay or deviation time so the system does not treat minor delays as violations. 4. After filling in all parameters, click Save at the bottom of the window. The toll road conditions will now be applied automatically according to your settings. How to delete a toll road To delete a toll road, open the toll road menu, selectDelete— the toll road will be removed from the list of zones and will no longer appear on the map."},{"s":"command-","t":"Command","h":["Command"],"x":"Command Go to commands Click add. Select one or more devices. Select a template. Specify the command. The master password is in the main contract settings (administrator panel). Select a ready-made template or create a new one, click \"Download\". Creating templates for sending commands Click + Click on the create button Specify a name Template Permissions Use global templates for each language in commands. One of the conditions for commands with specified parameters is to enclose the parameters in percentage brackets, where n is a number, s is a string. Commands for an object Go to the user interface. Go to the context menu of the object. Select commands. Select a team. Send. View history - select a period and click the search icon. 1. Add module to account 2. Configure sms gateway. Currently smsgateway24 is supported. https://<address>/ax/commands/smsstatus.php?cmd=incoming https://<address>/ax/commands/smsstatus.php?cmd=status Fill this parameters in module configuration section: To configure the templates: 1. Click on the configuration button, located on the right side 2.Open the \"commands\" tab 3.Click on the \"+\" symbol, then this window will open, allowing us to edit or add a new command: In this window, we can add a new command, edit the text of the message, select for which vehicles the message will be sent in the left panel. 1. By clicking on the \"send\" button, the system will send the message that we have previously written to the vehicles selected in the left panel 2. The \"save template\" option will allow us to save the template, which will make it easier for us to send this command in the future without re-entering the data. When selecting this option, the system will ask us to assign a name to our new template. Onlyadministratorcan generate templates and assign rights to them to a user and that each template is created for only one type of device. Templates should be created for each type of device. In the tree, devices are grouped by type: 1. You need to select the devices 2. The desired template is chosen. 3. lick on \"load template\" In the list on the left we can find the list of templates created previously, it is enough to click on the icon to delete an unwanted command. How to delete atemplate? (available only to the administrator). After the devices are selected, in \"templates\" it will show us the existing templates, there we can delete by clicking on the icon: To configure user rights on the templates it is necessary: 1. In settings, select the button \"staff and groups\" and select the user to whom we will configure the rights. 2. The \"user editing\" window will open and we will go to the \"commands\" option, there we can find the templates and select which templates this user will have the right to: With a left click on the vehicle we can find the option \"send command\" which will allow us to see the history of sent messages, and besides that, forward messages. To see how to use the Commands module and send GPRS commands, watch the video “Using the Commands module: how to send GPRS commands” on YouTube. The video demonstrates how to send commands to devices and track the history of sent commands in the system. Additionally, watch the video “How to create command templates” to quickly learn how to set up and save command templates for faster usage, making it easier to send commands without manually entering them each time."},{"s":"time-control-","t":"Time control","h":["Time control","How to сonfigure an object’s working hours","Days of the week","Schedule","3. Save the changes","Night Time","Alerts settings","How to configure a driver’s working hours","Default working time settings at account","How to set contract configuration","Available configuration keys"],"x":"Time control The Time сontrol module helps you monitor the working hours of vehicles and drivers according to a defined schedule. With this module, you can configure: which days and hours an object is allowed to operate how long it may drive without a break required rest time during the day, at night, and within 24 hours maximum time on a route, inside a geofence, or between geofences Based on these settings, the system generates violation notifications — for example, overtime driving, insufficient rest, or excessive time on a route. How to сonfigure an object’s working hours 1. Go to the Online tab 2. Select the required object from the list 3. Double-click it to open the object card 4. In the left panel, open the Working time tab The tab includes several sections: Days of the week Select the days when the object is allowed to operate. Schedule The Schedule block defines working time intervals for: Weekdays (Mon–Fri) Saturday Sunday To set time intervals: 1. Click the icon next to the required row 2. In the window that opens: Click Specify the working time interval Add multiple intervals if necessary 3. Save the changes Night Time The Night time block defines the time period considered as night. Specify: night start time night end time These settings are used to calculate night rest and generate related notifications. Alerts settings Here you define threshold values. If they are exceeded, the system generates notifications. Available parameters: Continues driving (min.) — allowed continuous driving time without a break Rest time-day (min.) — required daytime rest Rest time-night (min.) — required night rest (within the night period) Rest time within 24 hours (min.) — minimum total rest within 24 hours Max driving time between geofences (min.) — maximum trip time between geofences Max parking time in geofence (min.) — maximum allowed parking time inside a geofence Max time on route (min.) — maximum allowed time on a route from start to trip completion The system compares actual data with these values. If a violation is detected, a notification can be created. How to configure a driver’s working hours To configure drivers, the Drivers module must be enabled. 1. Go to the Drivers tab 2. Double-click the required driver 3. Open the Work time tab 4. Configure the schedule: Select the days when the driver is allowed to work In the Schedule block, define working intervals for: — Weekdays (Mon–Fri) — Saturday — Sunday To set time intervals: 1. Click the icon next to the required row 2. In the window that opens: Click Specify the working interval 3. Save the changes If you try to assign a driver to a vehicle outside of working hours, the system will display a warning and block the operation. Default working time settings at account In the administrative panel, you can configure default working time and limitation settings for the Time control module at the contract level. These settings are used as a template: when creating new objects for objects that have never saved their own Working Time settings This means you configure the rules once in the contract, and when a new object is created, these values are automatically applied. They can still be edited later at the object level if needed. You can specify only the parameters you want to use as defaults. Other fields will use system defaults or be filled manually. How to set contract configuration 1. Go to the Admin panel 2. Open the required contract card 3. Open the Module settings tab 4. Select Time control module 5. In the Configs section, add a key and its value, for example: works_day = 1,2,3,4,5 (working from Monday to Friday) max_driving_time = 1000 (no more than 1000 minutes of movement between geofences) max_parking_time = 500 (no more than 500 minutes of parking in the geofence) 6. Save the contract settings Available configuration keys Configuration key Description Example continues_driving Max continuous driving (min.) continues_driving = 250 max_driving_time Max driving time between geofences (min.) max_driving_time = 10 max_parking_time Max parking time in geofence (min.) max_parking_time = 60 max_stay_on_route Max time on route (min.) max_stay_on_route = 600 rest_time_day Minimum daytime rest (min.) rest_time_day = 15 rest_time_night Minimum night rest (min.) rest_time_night = 15 rest_time_full Minimum rest within 24 hours (min.) rest_time_full = 30 works_day Specific weekdays (1–7 Monday–Sunday) works_day = 1,2,3,4,5 work_schedule Work schedule in JSON format Intervals are defined in the format HH:MM-HH:MM. schedule — general schedule template days_of_week — specific weekdays (1–5 = Monday–Friday) wknd1, wknd2 — weekend settings (Saturday, Sunday) { \"wknd1\": \"04:00-19:00, 19:00-04:00\", \"wknd2\": \"04:00-19:00, 19:00-04:00\", \"schedule\": \"04:00-19:00, 19:00-04:00\", \"days_of_week\": { \"1\": \"04:00-19:00, 19:00-04:00\", \"2\": \"04:00-19:00, 19:00-04:00\", \"3\": \"04:00-19:00, 19:00-04:00\", \"4\": \"04:00-19:00, 19:00-04:00\", \"5\": \"04:00-19:00, 19:00-04:00\" } } By configuring settings at the account level, you centrally define the main working rules and save time when creating new objects."},{"s":"violations-","t":"Violations","h":["Violations","How to configure violation monitoring","Settings","Speeding violation","Speed limit exceed threshold","Send notifications","Speed limits","How to change a speed limit"],"x":"Violations The Violations module automatically detects vehicle speeding violations. While the vehicle is moving, the system compares its current speed with the speed limit for the road segment it is traveling on. If the vehicle exceeds the speed limit by more than the configured threshold, the system records a speeding violation. How to configure violation monitoring 1. Open the object card by double-clicking the object in the object list 2. Go to the Violations tab 3. Select the Speed violations checkbox to enable speeding detection 4. Enter a value in the Speed violation dataexceed threshold field 5. If you want to receive notifications, select the Send alertscheckbox 6. Click Save After saving, the system will automatically monitor speeding violations using the configured settings. Settings Speeding violation Enables or disables speeding detection for the selected object. When enabled, the system monitors the vehicle's speed and records violations based on the configured settings. Speed limit exceed threshold Specifies how much the vehicle's speed must exceed the posted speed limit before a violation is recorded. For example: Road speed limit: 80 km/h Configured threshold: 20 km/h In this case, a violation will only be recorded when the vehicle's speed exceeds 100 km/h. Send notifications When enabled, the system sends a notification each time a speeding violation is recorded. All recorded speeding violations are also available in the Events module. Speed limits To determine whether a vehicle is speeding, the system uses speed limits assigned to each road segment. By default, these values are based on OpenStreetMap (OSM) road classifications. The system stores speed limits for each road type and uses them when calculating speeding violations. If necessary, you can modify these values by clicking Edit global speed limits. A table containing speed limits for different road types and vehicle categories will open. The first column lists road types defined by OpenStreetMap. Road types are displayed using OSM's internal identifiers. For example: AT — rural roads in Austria DE — urban roads in Germany bus_guideway — roads reserved for buses You can configure separate speed limits for each vehicle category: Limit default— default value Limit truck — for trucks Limit bus — for buses Limit car — for passenger vehicles If a custom speed limit is specified for a vehicle category, the system uses that value when determining speeding violations. How to change a speed limit 1. Click Edit global speed limits 2. Find the required road type 3. Double-click the cell for the appropriate vehicle category 4. Enter the new speed limit 5. Click Save 6. Close the window, then click Save in the object card"},{"s":"logistics-","t":"Logistics","h":["Logistics"],"x":"Logistics The logistics module consists of several tabs: Flights Clients Warehouse Cargo type Cargo skills Settings Show trip on map If you need to see geofences, enable them on the map. Use maps and settings as in the regular geozone module."},{"s":"trips","t":"Trips","h":["Trips"],"x":"Trips The Flights tab consists of several elements to fill in: Add a new order +. Select transport. Add new task List of trips When you click on a flight line, the flight will be displayed on the map."},{"s":"customers","t":"Customers","h":["Customers"],"x":"Customers Add Customers Click + Fill in the fields. Save. Show on map"},{"s":"warehouse","t":"Warehouse","h":["Warehouse"],"x":"Warehouse Add warehouse • Click + Fill in the fields. Save."},{"s":"cargo-type","t":"Cargo type","h":["Cargo type"],"x":"Cargo type Add cargo • Click add + Enter parameters Save."},{"s":"cargo-skills","t":"Cargo skills","h":["Cargo skills"],"x":"Cargo skills Add skills Press + Enter data Save."},{"s":"settings-3","t":"Settings","h":["Settings"],"x":"Settings Default value - these parameters set the parameters for downloading and unloading. Geocoder settings - this setting allows you to select a geocoder. Task type settings - in this parameter, select the default task type from the drop-down list. Speed ​​factors - the field is used to specify the speed factor by day of the week and time."},{"s":"data-recalculation","t":"Data recalculation","h":["Data recalculation","How to start a recalculation","How to view the recalculation results"],"x":"Data recalculation The Data recalculation module allows you to reprocess an object's data for a selected period and update trip and geofence visit information if the object's settings have changed or the data was not processed completely. Two types of recalculation are available: Trips — the system recalculates the object's trips Geofences — the system reprocesses the object's geofence visit data How to start a recalculation 1. Go to General → Online 2. Find the required object in the list 3. Right-click the object 4. Select Data recalculation 5. In the Recalculation type field, select the data you want to recalculate: Trips — the system recalculates the object's trips Geofences — the system reprocesses the object's geofence visit data 6. In the Days count field, specify the period to recalculate. 7. Click Start After you start the recalculation, the request is placed in the processing queue. Recalculation is not performed immediately. Processing time depends on the selected period and the amount of data to be processed. Larger periods may take longer to complete. Once the recalculation is finished, the results will be available in the recalculation history. How to view the recalculation results To check the recalculation status, click Historyin the Data recalculation window. A list of all recalculation requests for the selected object will be displayed. Here you can review the recalculation parameters, its current status, and the processing results. The table contains the following information: Object — the object for which the recalculation was requested Recalculation type — whether trips or geofences were recalculated Days — the number of days selected for recalculation Queued — the date and time when the request was submitted Processed — the date and time when processing finished or the status was last updated Result — the current status and details of the recalculation If the Processed and Result fields are still empty, the recalculation may still be waiting in the queue or currently being processed. Check the history again later to see the updated status. After a Trip recalculation, the result may include: Execution Time — the processing time in seconds Agents — the number of processed messages received from the object Points — the number of processed location points Tracks — the number of generated trips Parkings — the number of generated parking events After a Geofence recalculation, the result may include service information, execution time, and the number of processed geofences. This information is primarily intended for diagnostics. For normal operation, it is sufficient to verify that the recalculation has completed successfully. If the recalculation does not start, finishes with an error, or the data is still not updated after processing, contact your system administrator or technical support."},{"s":"trailers","t":"Trailers","h":["Trailers","How to create a trailer","Trailer settings","Additional settings","Object access","How to assign a trailer","From the trailer card","From the object card","Working with trailers","Trailer information on the map","Trailer information in the object list","Trailer history","How to delete a trailer"],"x":"Trailers The Trailers module allows you to create, configure, and manage trailers used together with monitored objects. A trailer can be used in two ways: As an informational object — stores trailer information and can be assigned to a vehicle. As a monitored object — has its own GPS tracker and sensors and sends data to the system. When a trailer is assigned to a vehicle, the system displays them as a single unit, allowing you to monitor both the towing vehicle and the trailer. In this article you'll learn how to: create a trailer configure trailer settings assign a trailer to a vehicle view trailer information on the map and in the object list view the trailer assignment history delete a trailer How to create a trailer 1. Go to the Trailers tab 2. Click Add The Create trailer window opens. Fill in the required fields and click Save. Trailer settings Trailer name — the name under which the trailer will be displayed in the system Related object — select the monitored object associated with this trailer. The trailer will receive location, sensor, and other data from this object. A related object is not the vehicle to which the trailer is attached. It is the monitored object whose data belongs to this trailer. Type — select the trailer type If the required type is not available, contact technical support to have it added. Width — enter the trailer width and select the unit of measurement: millimeters, centimeters, meters Group — select the group to which the trailer belongs RFID — the RFID code transmitted by the vehicle's RFID sensor when the trailer is attached. The system uses this identifier to automatically determine which trailer is connected to the vehicle Description — additional information or comments about the trailer Additional settings Exclusive — controls how trailers are assigned to vehicles. When enabled, assigning this trailer automatically unassigns any other trailers currently attached to the same vehicle. Use this option if only one trailer can be connected to the vehicle at a time. Agriculture — used together with the Agro module. Enable this option only for objects that use agricultural functionality. Send alerts — enables notifications for events related to this trailer Object access Select the objects that should have access to this trailer. If no objects are selected, the trailer will be available to all objects. How to assign a trailer A trailer can be assigned to a vehicle in two ways. From the trailer card At the bottom of the trailer card, open the Objectssection. Select one or more vehicles to which you want to assign the trailer or grant access. To find a specific object, you can use: the filter search with highlighted matches — matching characters are highlighted in yellow After selecting the required objects, click Save. From the object card 1. Select an object in the object list 2. Right-click the object 3. Select Trailers from the context menu 4. Choose one of the following actions: Assign — attach a trailer to the vehicle Unassign — detach the currently assigned trailer Working with trailers After a trailer is assigned: the vehicle icon on the map changes to indicate that a trailer is attached the Trailercolumn in the object list displays the name of the assigned trailer Trailer information on the map You can view trailer information in the object's map tooltip. To open the tooltip, click the object's icon on the map. If trailer display is enabled, the tooltip shows information about the assigned trailer. If an associated object is configured, you can also view data received from the trailer's GPS tracker. To display trailer information in the map tooltip: 1. Go to Account settings 2. Open the Personalization tab 3. Under Tooltip iteams, enable the Trailer option 4. Click Save Trailer information in the object list To view trailer information, hover over an object in the object list. To enable tooltips in the object list: 1. Go to Account settings → Personalization 2. Enable Show object tree tooltips Trailer history A history of trailer assignments is available for each trailer. To open the history: 1. Right-click the trailer on the Trailerstab 2. Select Show history The history includes: the object to which the trailer was assigned the trailer name the assignment date and time the unassignment date and time the RFID code To delete a history entry, click the Delete icon . How to delete a trailer 1. Right-click the trailer on the Trailerstab 2. Select Delete"},{"s":"relay-","t":"Relay","h":["Relay"],"x":"Relay The module is designed to send data via retransmission for a certain period. The maximum possible period is no more than 14 days. Module settings in the admin panel. max_days_resend_data - means how many days back the data can be sent.  This module does not have Reports. Go to the context menu of the object. Select the required field from the list of retransmissions. Editing and resending Retransmission window. Resend data. Select a period and click add to queue. Additional menu Edit entry. Delete. Resend data - resend data."},{"s":"service-","t":"Service","h":["Service","Module structure","Access rights","How to configure access rights"],"x":"Service The Service module is designed for managing vehicle maintenance. With this module, you can: monitor the condition of your vehicles plan scheduled maintenance keep records of tires log breakdowns and issues set reminders for upcoming service tasks Module structure The module includes the following tabs: Dashboard Services Inspections Dictionary Downtimes Tires The Services,Inspections, and Dictionary tabs also contain submenus to help you fine-tune the module’s functionality. Check the dedicated articles on each tab to learn more about how to use them in detail. Access rights You can flexibly configure access rights for each user working with the module. This allows you to define employee roles and protect service data from unwanted changes. How to configure access rights 1. Go to Account settings 2. Open the Staff and groups tab 3. Select the required user and double-click on their name 4. In the user profile, open the Rights tab 5. Find the Service module and select the required permissions The Administrator can configure access separately for the following sections: Services Inspections Dictionary (Suppliers, Service еypes, Works, Parts) Downtime Tires For each section, you can define the allowed actions: Add — can create new records Edit — can modify existing records Delete — can remove records You can configure access according to the employee’s role. For example, a manager may have full access, while other users may have view-only permissions."},{"s":"dashboard","t":"Dashboard","h":["Dashboard","Objects list","How to configure the Objects list:","Using filters","Dashboard overview","How to use the dashboard:","Object analytics","Analytics graph","Lifecycle estimation","Depreciation methods","Service cost estimation","Fuel cost estimation"],"x":"Dashboard In the Dashboard tab of the Service module, you can: View the list of objects Use a dashboard with charts and diagrams for a visual overview Track service reminders and maintenance costs Analyze data for each individual vehicle Objects list The Objects list shows data for each vehicle: Number of completed services Date of the last service Whether the service is currently active Next service reminder Current mileage Mileage left until the next service Total service cost Quick access to the object’s analytics You can customize the visible columns in the list and use filters for convenient searching. How to configure the Objects list: 1. Click the arrow in the header of any column — a menu will appear 2. In the menu, you can: Sort the data in ascending or descending order Select which columns to display To configure the displayed columns: Click Columns — a list of available parameters will open Check the boxes next to the parameters you want to see Using filters Filters allow you to display only the objects you need. 1. Configure a filter by object type — for example, view only passenger cars or special equipment 2. For more detailed filtering, click Show filters 3. Filter fields will appear in the table header 4. You can filter by conditions: Contains Equals Not equal Empty / Not empty Greater than / Greater than or equal Less than / Less than or equal Examples In the Objects column, choose the Contains filter and enter part of a name — all matching objects will be shown. In theLast service column, choose the Equalsfilter and specify a date (manually or from the calendar) — all objects serviced on that day will be displayed. To refresh the objects list, click the Refreshbutton . Dashboard overview The dashboard is a screen with visual data in the form of charts and diagrams. How to use the dashboard: 1. Expand the dashboard to full screen for focused analysis Find the Expand dashboard button Click it — the dashboard will open in full-screen mode To exit full screen, click the button again 2. To refresh the data in the charts, click the Refresh button The dashboard includes: A pie chart showing the distribution of service reminder statuses: Active Overdue Closed Due soon A chart with service costs, showing monthly maintenance expenses. Object analytics To view analytics for a specific object: 1. Find the Analytics column in the objects list 2. Click the graph icon You will be taken to detailed analytics, where you can calculate: Lifecycle cost estimation Service cost estimation Fuel cost estimation In object analytics, you control the data and adjust parameters as needed. Changes are reflected instantly in the calculations. Analytics graph The graph shows how the total cost of ownership changes over time by year. All data in the graph is calculated based on the values you enter in the following sections: Lifecycle estimation Service cost estimation Fuel cost estimation When these parameters are updated, the graph recalculates automatically. What the graph shows: Bars represent yearly expenses: — maintenance costs — fuel costs — planned and actual values TCO line shows the total cost of ownership for each year Depreciation curve helps you see how the asset’s value decreases over time Cost per km shows the average cost per kilometer, both planned and actual Lifecycle estimation Fill in the following fields: Estimated vihicle life (in months): e.g., 60 months for a car Purchase price: the amount you planned to spend Estimated disposal cost: expected cost of disposal or decommissioning at the end of service life Estimated annual mileage (miles): for example, how many miles a car drives per year Actual purchase price: the real amount paid Estimated salvage value: the expected value at the end of its service life Estimated fuel efficiency (L/km): how many liters of fuel are consumed per kilometer. Depreciation methods Choose the depreciation method suitable for the object: Double-declining balance method: Writes off value faster in early years. Applies double the straight-line depreciation rate to the remaining book value. Example: Car price: $10,000 Service life: 5 years Straight-line rate = 1/5 = 20% Double-declining rate = 40% Year 1: 10,000 × 40% = $4,000 Year 2: (10,000 – 4,000) × 40% = $2,400 This method is used for assets that lose value quickly or provide more utility early in their lifecycle. Sum-of-years-digits method: A smoother depreciation method compared to accelerated. First, sum all years of useful life, then calculate yearly fractions. Example: Service life: 5 years Sum of years: 1+2+3+4+5 = 15 Year 1: 5/15 of cost Year 2: 4/15 Year 3: 3/15, etc. This method fits assets with uneven wear but without a sharp value drop. Service cost estimation Specify planned vs. actual service costs by year to track whether expenses meet expectations. Fuel cost estimation Similarly, specify planned vs. actual fuel costs for each year."},{"s":"services-1","t":"Services","h":["Services","All Services","Working with the service list:","Creating a service","Service objects","Jobs","Parts","Files","Creating reminders","Completing a service record","Additional functions","Templates","How to create a template:","How to edit a template:","How to delete a template:","How to apply a template:","Reminders","How to edit a reminder:"],"x":"Services In the Servicestab, you can manage all service records, create templates, and set reminders. Here’s how it works: This section shows all created service records. You can: work with the list of service records create a new service record add service objects add completed jobs and spare parts add files set service reminders use additional functions in each service record Templates help quickly create standard service records. Here you will learn: Where saved service templates are stored How to create and apply a template Keep track of upcoming services and never miss important dates. You can: Edit reminders Change their status All Services This section shows all service records added to the system. Working with the service list: Customize the list: select which columns and parameters to display Delete unnecessary records by clicking the icon next to the row Use the buttons in the top-right corner: Show filters — opens fields for data filtering Refresh — updates the service list Create service — click to add a new service record Creating a service 1. Click Create service 2. In the window that opens, fill in the service information: General information: Template — select a template to auto-fill fields Occurrence — indicate if it’s a one-time or recurring service Service name — enter the service title Supplier — select from the dropdown (see below on how to add a supplier) Service status — mark as completed or not Invoice number — e.g., invoice or internal document number Service type — choose from the list Date and time — specify when the service was or will be performed Comment — add a note if necessary Service objects Specify which objects are involved in this service: In the Add to objects section, select one or more objects from the dropdown list To remove an object, click the Delete button next to it. Jobs How to add a job: 1. Go to the Jobs section 2. Click Add job 3. In the window that opens, fill in: Job name Service type — select from the list To add a new type: Сlick Enter the name, and select a category: service, job, or part 3. Сlick Save— the type will appear in the list Cost and quantity Comment — if needed 4. Mark Completedif the job is finished 5. Click Save Added jobs will appear in the list. The total cost of all jobs will be calculated at the bottom. Parts How to add a spare part: 1. Go to the Parts section 2. Click Add part 3. In the window that opens, fill in: Part name Part type — select from the list Supplier or manufacturer — select from the list To add a new supplier/manufacturer: Click Enter the name, phone, email, and address 3. Click Save Part code, price, count Comment — if needed 4. MarkCompleted if the part has been installed 5. Click Save Added parts will appear in the spare parts list, and their total cost will be calculated automatically. Files You can attach documents and files to a service record to keep all maintenance information in one place. How to add files: 1. Open the service record and go to the Files section. 2. Click the Add button and select a file from your device. 3. The file will be attached to the record. Managing files in a service record: Download a file — click the download icon next to the desired file. Delete a file — click the delete icon to remove the file from the record. Creating reminders In the Reminderssection, you can set automatic notifications for services. How to create a reminder: 1. Go to Reminders 2. Click Add reminder 3. In the new window, fill in: Reminder name Service date Optional fields: Repeat interval — how often the reminder should repeat Days before — how many days before the service to send the notification Mileage or engine hours Mileage/engine hours interval Notification method — email or others To receive email notifications, confirm your email in Privacy Reminder text: — Subject — what the reminder is about — Header — what will appear at the top of the message — Message text — you can use variables for automatic data substitution: %current_time%— current time %reminder_name%— reminder name %current_moto%— current mileage %current_mil%— current mileage in miles %agent_id%— account ID %vehicle_name%— vehicle name %driver_name%— driver name Example: Planned maintenance for %vehicle_name% scheduled on %current_time% 4. Click Save — the reminder will appear in the list In a service record, you can manage tables for objects, jobs, spare parts, and reminders. Select which columns to display. Completing a service record After filling in the information, click Save in the top-right corner. The service will appear in the All services list. Additional functions Click the three dots in the top-right corner: Download — download a file with service data Print — print a maintenance report Create template — save the current service as a template Delete — delete the service record Templates The Templates tab stores all saved service templates. Templates help quickly create new service records without re-entering all information. How to create a template: 1. Create a new service record or open an existing one 2. Click the three-dot menu → Create template 3. Enter the template name 4. Optionally, select the vehicle make, model, and modification To add a make, click , enter the name, and click OK 5. ClickSave— the template will appear in the Templates tab How to edit a template: 1. Go to Templates 2. Double-click the template 3. Edit the name, brand, or model 4. Click Save How to delete a template: Click theDelete icon next to the template in the list. How to apply a template: 1. In All services, click Create service 2. Select the desired template 3. All saved details (parts, jobs, reminders) will auto-fill 4. Select objects, date, and time 5. Click Save — the new service record will appear in the list Reminders The Reminders tab shows all created reminders. How to edit a reminder: 1. Double-click the reminder in the list 2. Edit the data as needed 3. Change the status if necessary: Active Overdue Closed 4. Click Save To delete a reminder from the list, click on the icon ."},{"s":"inspections","t":"Inspections","h":["Inspections","Forms","How to create an inspection form:","Completed","How to view details","How to conduct an inspection:"],"x":"Inspections Inspections are a tool for convenient and systematic vehicle condition checks. Here’s what you can do: Create inspection templates with the questions you need Schedule and conduct inspections Record what was checked and its condition Detect problems early to prevent downtime The tab consists of two sections: Forms — create inspection templates Completed — perform inspections and review results Forms In this section, you create inspection templates — sets of questions used during checks. You can make forms for: Regular maintenance inspections Pre-trip checks Acceptance inspections Unscheduled checks You can create as many forms as you need, one for each case. How to create an inspection form: 1. Click Create form 2. You’ll see the form creation window with two blocks: General information Questions 3. When everything is ready, click the Save icon in the upper right corner. To edit a form: double-click it in the list. To delete a form: click the delete icon . This is the header of the form: 1. Inspection name — e.g., “Engine check” 2. Comment — describe when and how the form should be used. Example: “A detailed inspection focusing on engine performance, fluids, and components.” 3. Signature — check the box if you want the inspector to sign the form for validity. Now add the questions for the inspection. How to add a question: 1. Click Create question 2. Fill in the fields: Question title Comment — what exactly needs to be checked 3. Configure extra options: Allow additional comment Allow skip Add photo Require photo Button labels: — Positive answer (e.g., “Yes” / “OK”) — Negative answer (e.g., “No” / “Not working”) — Skip (if allowed, e.g., “Skip”) 4. Click Save The question will appear in your form. To edit: double-click it. To delete: click the delete icon . When all questions are added, click Save in the upper right corner to save the form. Now you can use this form when conducting inspections. For a visual example of the process, watch the video “How to create an inspection form”, which shows how to configure inspection fields, organize the form structure, and save the form for further use. Completed Here you see all finished inspections. You can check: Which object was inspected Which form was used Who conducted the inspection When it was done Number of answered questions Which questions were skipped Icons in the list let you: delete an inspection record print the inspection form download the form How to view details Double-click an inspection — the filled form will open. Here you can: Review all answers Correct mistakes if necessary How to conduct an inspection: 1. Go to the Completed tab and click Create inspection 2. In the new window, select: An inspection form (created earlier) An object (vehicle) 3. Click Continue — the form with questions will open 4. Answer the questions: Choose the answer Add photos or comments if needed 5. Sign the inspection: Use your mouse or stylus to sign If you make a mistake, click Clear and try again 6. Click Complete — the inspection will appear in the Completedlist See the video “How to fill an inspection form” for a visual example of filling in an inspection form in the Service module."},{"s":"dictionary-1","t":"Dictionary","h":["Dictionary","Suppliers","Service types","Works","Parts"],"x":"Dictionary Dictionary like a database that you can use when creating service records. Instead of entering the same details every time, you can save them here once and simply pick them from the list. You can create and store information about: Suppliers and manufacturers Service types Works Parts Suppliers Here you keep information about spare parts suppliers or manufacturers. How you use this info is up to you — for record keeping, for contacts, or just for convenience. How to add a supplier: 1. Go to the Suppliers tab 2. Click Add 3. In the window, enter: Company name Phone Email Address 4. Click Save— the supplier will appear in the list How to edit or delete a supplier: Edit: double-click the record, update it, and save Delete: click the delete icon next to the record Service types Here you define types of service operations. How to add a service type: 1. Go to the Types tab 2. Click Add 3. In the window: Enter the service type name (e.g., “Maintenance-2”) Select a category: Service — e.g., scheduled maintenance Work — e.g., replacement, repair Material — consumables, spare parts 4. Click Save— the type will appear in the list How to edit or delete: Edit: double-click the record Delete: click the delete icon Works In the Works tab you create and store all jobs that can later be added to service records with one click. How to add a work item: 1. Go to the Works tab 2. Click Add 3. Fill in the fields: Work name Service type — choose from your list Cost Comment — optional 4. CheckCompletedif the work is already done 5. Click Save Parts Here you store all spare parts used during maintenance. How to add a spare part: 1. Go to the Parts tab 2. Click Add 3. Fill in the fields: Part name Service type — choose from the list Supplier or manufacturer — select from your saved suppliers Part code — if available Cost Comment — optional 4. CheckCompleted if the part is already installed 5. Click Save"},{"s":"downtimes","t":"Downtimes","h":["Downtimes","Working with the downtime list","Filtering records","How to create a downtime record:","How to edit or delete a downtime record","How downtime is displayed","Object behavior during downtime","How to add downtime via the object menu","How to remove downtime"],"x":"Downtimes Downtime is the period when a vehicle is temporarily not in use. For example, when it is undergoing maintenance or repair. In the Downtimes tab, you will see all records of vehicle downtime periods. Working with the downtime list The following list management buttons are available in the upper-right corner of the Downtimes tab: Export — export downtime records to a file Import — import downtime records from a file Filter — filter records by column values Refresh — reload the data and update the list Filtering records Click Filter. Filter fields appear below the table headers: Object — enter the vehicle name or number Start and End — select the date and time Duration — specify the downtime duration Duration current year — specify the total downtime duration for the current year Reason for downtime — enter text from the downtime reason You can complete one or several fields. The table will display only records that match all the specified conditions. For example, to find downtime records for a particular vehicle, enter its name or number in the field below the Objectcolumn. To clear the filters, remove the entered values. How to create a downtime record: 1. In the Downtime tab, clickAdd 2. In the new window: Select the object (vehicle) currently in downtime Specify the reason for downtime Choose start and end date and time using the calendar 3. Click Save — the downtime record will appear in the list How to edit or delete a downtime record Edit: double-click the record in the list, update the details, and save Delete: click the delete icon next to the record How downtime is displayed When you create a downtime record for a vehicle due to maintenance, a special icon appears next to the object in the Online tab, and the object status changes to Downtime, highlighted in yellow. Clicking the icon will open a window with details about the maintenance period — you’ll immediately see how long the downtime is planned for. Object behavior during downtime If the downtime has already started The object status automatically changes to “Downtime”, and it is excluded from the issues and alerts panel. This helps keep real-time monitoring clear and focused. If the downtime is scheduled for the future Only the icon is displayed, while the object status remains unchanged until the downtime start time. How to add downtime via the object menu 1. Go to the Online tab 2. Select the required object from the list 3. Right-click on it to open the context menu 4. Select Add downtime After that, a downtime record will be created for the object, and a wrench icon will appear next to it. How to remove downtime 1. Right-click on the object 2. In the context menu, select Remove downtime 3. Confirm the action"},{"s":"tires","t":"Tires","h":["Tires","Tab overview","Object list","Axle and tire layout","Parameter graph"],"x":"Tires In this tab, you can track the condition of vehicle tires — monitor pressure and temperature indicators, as well as manage information about axles and tires. In this article: What you see on the screen How to add an axle How to add a tire Tab overview When you open the Tires tab, you will see three main sections: Object list Here you’ll find all vehicles you are monitoring. You can quickly find the needed object using: 1 — Search in the list. Enter a name or number, and the search will highlight results 2 — Filter by object type 3 — Filters in the table header – conditions include: Contains Equals Not equals Empty / Not empty Greater than / Greater or equal Less than / Less or equal Axle and tire layout When you click an object in the list, you will see its axle and tire layout — a visual diagram where you can: Add axles and tires See where each tire is installed Track the condition of every tire Parameter graph This graph shows the history of indicators for the selected object: Vehicle speed Tire temperature Tire pressure You can select a date to see how values changed over time — this helps spot overheating or pressure drops early."},{"s":"axles-and-tires","t":"Axles and tires","h":["Axles and tires","How to add an axle","How to add a tire"],"x":"Axles and tires In this article, we will explain how to configure the axle and tire layout for a selected object. First, add the axles, then install the tires on them. After configuration, the system will be able to display the number of axles and tires, their positions, mileage, and pressure and temperature readings if sensors are connected to the tires. How to add an axle An axle can be added in two ways: through the axle and tire layout or through the object menu. To add an axle through the layout: 1. Select an object from the list (click it) 2. Go to the axle and tire layout 3. Click Add axleon the layout 4. In the window, enter: Axle name — for example, front axle or rear axle Position number — the axle order on the layout 5. Click Save — the new axle will appear in the layout You can also add an axle from the object menu. Right-click the object in the list and select Add axle. The same axle configuration window will open. How to add a tire Once axles are added, you can install tires: 1. Click Add tire on the layout 2. In the window, fill in tire details: Tire name Installation date and time (calendar selection) Mileage at installation Mileage threshold (for wear control) Side of installation (left or right) Position number Serial number Pressure and temperature sensors (select from the list) 3. Click Save — the tire will appear in the layout Once axles and tires are added, the object’s list entry will show: Number of axles configured Number of installed tires Tire locations (by side and position) Pressure values (from sensors) Temperature of each tire Mileage — both vehicle mileage and mileage of each installed tire This data helps identify issues like pressure drops or overheating, extend tire lifespan, and avoid unplanned repairs."},{"s":"tire-templates","t":"Tire templates","h":["Tire templates","To create a new template:","To apply a template to an object:"],"x":"Tire templates Tire templates allow you to save the axle and tire layout of one object and apply it to other objects. Use templates when several vehicles have the same axle and wheel configuration. You can open templates from the object menu on the Tires tab. To do this, right-click the object and select Template. Before creating a template, axles and tires must already be added to the object. If there is no tire data for the object, the system will show an error message. To create a new template: 1. Open the object menu and click Templates 2. In the Tires template window, click Create new 3. Enter the template name 4. If needed, specify the make, model, and modification 5. Click Save The Name field is required. The Make,Model, and Modification fields can be left empty. To apply a template to an object: 1. Open the menu of the object to which you want to apply the template 2. Select the required template from the list 3. Click Use After the template is applied, the system replaces the current axle and tire layout of the object with the data from the selected template. If axles and tires were already configured for the object, they will be deleted and replaced with the template data. If the template is no longer needed, select it from the list and click Delete."},{"s":"tire-history","t":"Tire history","h":["Tire history"],"x":"Tire history Tire history shows when and where a tire was installed on or removed from an object. To open the history, right-click the object on the Tires tab and select History. The history displays: Install Time — the date and time when the tire was installed Uninstall Time — the date and time when the tire was removed Axle Position — the axle position Tire Position — the tire position Mileage — the mileage at the time of installation or removal"},{"s":"events-","t":"Events","h":["Events","How to find events","How to view the event list","How to change the list view","Viewing events on the map","How to change event colors"],"x":"Events TheEvents module allows you to find events related to vehicles and other monitored objects and view where they occurred on the map. With this module, you can: find events for a selected time period view speeding, idling, refueling, fuel theft, maintenance reminders, and other events filter events by specific objects and event types view where an event occurred on the map view notifications In this article, you will learn how to: find events for a selected time period open and review the search results change how the event list is displayed view event locations on the map change the display color of event types How to find events 1. Open the Events module from the top menu 2. In the From and Tofields, specify the time period you want to search 3. In the left pane, select the monitored objects 4. In the right pane, select the event types you want to display You can select: an entire event group, such as Notifications or Events individual event types within a group, such as Speed, Idle, or Inspections To expand or collapse an event group, click the arrow next to its name . 5. Click Search After the search is complete, the matching events will appear in the list. How to view the event list By default, the search results are grouped first by object and then by event type. The number in parentheses indicates how many events were found. To view the events, expand: 1. the monitored object 2. the required event type Each event displays: the event name the date and time it was recorded a color indicator How to change the list view By default, events are grouped by object and event type. To change the list view, click the View button in the upper-right corner of the panel. The events will be displayed as a single list without grouping. Each entry shows: the monitored object the date and time the event name or description Events are sorted by time, with the most recent events displayed first. To return to the grouped view, click the same button again. Viewing events on the map To view where an event occurred: 1. Select the event in the list 2. The location where the event was recorded will be highlighted on the map with a colored marker. The marker color matches the color indicator shown next to the event in the list. To view event details, click the marker on the map. The information pop-up displays: the event date and time the monitored object the event name and description additional information, if available To close the pop-up, click the × in its upper-right corner or select another marker. If several events are located close together, they are grouped into a single marker displaying a number. The number indicates how many events are located in that area. Zoom in on the map to display the individual event markers. Some events do not contain location coordinates. In this case, no marker is displayed on the map. For example, this may apply to certain maintenance reminders. However, the event remains available in the list and can still be viewed. To display all found events on the map, click in the upper-right corner of the Events panel. To remove all displayed events from the map, click . How to change event colors Each event type has its own display color. The same color is used both in the event list and on the map. You can change the color if needed. To do this: 1. In the Event types panel, find the required event type 2. Click the color indicator next to its name 3. Select a new color from the color palette The selected color is applied immediately to that event type in both the event list and on the map. To see how the Eventmodule works in practice, watch the video “How to use the Event module” on YouTube."},{"s":"fuel-cards-","t":"Fuel cards","h":["Fuel cards"],"x":"Fuel cards The Fuel cards module helps you track fuel expenses and monitor fuel card transactions. You can use the module to: add fuel cards and link them to objects view transactions and verify operations configure transaction notifications import transaction data automatically or manually The module contains three tabs: Cards — add and configure fuel cards and link them to objects Transactions — view refuelling transactions, charges, and other data received for the cards Services — connect and configure fuel provider services for automatic transaction imports"},{"s":"cards-","t":"Cards","h":["Cards","How to add a card"],"x":"Cards In this section, you can add and view fuel cards. How to add a card 1. Click Add card to add a fuel card 2. In the new window: Enter the card number Select the gas station type or fuel provider In Object , select one or more objects that use the card. For example, if the same card is used to refuel a tractor and its trailer, select both objects. At least one object is required. After you save the card, it will be available in the details of every linked object. Check the date. It is entered automatically, but you can change it if necessary 3. Click Save After saving, you will be able to view the details of the fuel card. To do this, right-click and double-click on the card in the list. The card details will appear on the right side of the screen. You can also save the card’s information in a PDF format by clicking the Save to file button."},{"s":"transactions","t":"Transactions","h":["Transactions","How to find transactions","How to view operation details","Checking coordinate matching","Configuring the table"],"x":"Transactions The Transactions tab displays operations performed with fuel cards. Here, you can find a specific transaction, view its refuelling details, and check whether the object’s location corresponds to the gas station coordinates. How to find transactions 1. Specify the required period in From Date andTo Date 2. Select a fuel card in Card 1 — Transaction period 2 — Fuel card selection The table displays all transactions for the selected card and period. How to view operation details Select a transaction in the table. TheOperation details panel opens on the right side of the screen. It displays: the company that provided the data the fuel card number the transaction date and time the gas station the object’s location the odometer reading the fuel type the amount of fuel the price the total transaction amount the refuelling amount recorded by the fuel sensor the coordinate-matching result a map showing the object’s location Checking coordinate matching OneGPS compares the object’s location at the time of the transaction with the saved coordinates of the gas station. The coordinates are considered to match if the object is within 100 metres of the gas station. The result is displayed inCoincidence: the green Correspond status means that the object was within 100 metres of the specified gas station the red Not correspond status means that the object was more than 100 metres away from the saved gas station coordinates. A mismatch does not always mean that the transaction is incorrect. It may be caused by inaccurate gas station coordinates, positioning accuracy, or missing data. If the fuel card is not linked to an object, OneGPS cannot determine the object’s location for the check. In this case, the system displays a message stating that the card is not linked to an object. If the gas station coordinates are missing, contact technical support. Provide the gas station name or ID, address, latitude, and longitude. Configuring the table To change the transaction table display, select the arrow in the required column header. The menu allows you to: sort records in ascending or descending order select which columns are displayed group records by the selected parameter display the data in groups To select the displayed columns: 1. Open the menu in any column header 2. Select Columns 3. Select the columns you want to display The Gas station column identifies the station specified in the transaction. You can view the coordinate-matching result in the operation details."},{"s":"services-2","t":"Services","h":["Services","How to manage notifications","How to upload transactions manually"],"x":"Services In this section, you can enable or disable transaction notifications and manually upload fuel card transaction data. How to manage notifications 1. In the Transaction notifications section, check or uncheck the box for the desired type of notification. Available notification options include Email and SMS If you plan to receive SMS notifications, make sure your SMS package is activated and paid. 2. ClickSave Notifications will only be sent for transactions that are automatically uploaded. No notifications are sent for manually uploaded transactions. How to upload transactions manually 1. In the Transaction upload section, select the required file format 2. Click Select file If the transactions are exported from the operator in Excel format, first save the file as CSV. When preparing the file, make sure: The format is CSV The delimiter is a comma The file structure matches the chosen upload format 3. To view a sample file, click the icon next to the desired format 4. Click Upload to import the data"},{"s":"displaying-gas-stations-on-the-map","t":"Displaying gas stations on the map","h":["Displaying gas stations on the map","How to display gas stations","Checking coordinates for a transaction"],"x":"Displaying gas stations on the map The main OneGPS map can display gas stations belonging to connected fuel networks. This allows you to view their locations and check which stations are close to your objects. The saved gas station coordinates are also used to verify fuel card transactions. OneGPS compares the object’s location at the time of the transaction with the location of the specified gas station. How to display gas stations 1. Open Fuel station types on the map 2. Select the required fuel networks The gas stations belonging to the selected networks appear on the map . To hide the stations belonging to a particular network, clear the corresponding checkbox. The list includes only the fuel networks connected to the account. If the required network or gas station is unavailable, contact technical support. To request a new gas station, provide: the fuel network name the gas station address its latitude and longitude Technical support will check the information and add the gas station to the system. Checking coordinates for a transaction After a gas station has been added, its coordinates are used to verify transactions. To view the result: 1. Open the Fuel cards module 2. Select the Transactions tab 3. Select the required transaction 4. Check Coincidence in the operation details: the green Correspond status means that the object was within 100 metres of the saved gas station coordinates the red Not correspond status means that the object was more than 100 metres away from the saved gas station coordinates If the gas station coordinates have not yet been added, OneGPS cannot complete the check correctly. Contact technical support and provide the station’s address, latitude, and longitude."},{"s":"connecting-a-gas-station-directory","t":"Connecting a gas station directory","h":["Connecting a gas station directory","How to connect a directory"],"x":"Connecting a gas station directory This setting is available to technical support specialists and administrators who have access to the OneGPS admin panel. If you do not have the required access, contact technical support. They will help you connect the required fuel networks. The connected directories determine which fuel networks users can see in Fuel station types on the main map. Before adding a gas station, prepare: the fuel network name the station address and coordinates, if the station has not yet been added to the system You will need the directory IDs from the database. Add only the gas stations required for the account so that Fuel station types displays relevant networks. How to connect a directory 1. Open the account details in the admin panel 2. Open the Modules settings tab 3. Select Fuel cards module 4. Click Add 5. In Key, enter: types 6. In Value, enter the required gas station directory IDs as a list, for example: [1,2,3] Use the actual directory IDs from the database. 7. Click Save The connected fuel networks will then appear in Fuel station types. Networks that are not included in the types setting will not be displayed."},{"s":"composite-objects-3","t":"Composite objects","h":["Composite objects","How to create a composite object","How to add elements to a composite object","Configuring the scheme","Working with the composite objects list"],"x":"Composite objects The Composite objects module is designed to combine multiple fuel-related objects and sensors into one logical object and track them as a single fuel accounting system. It helps you build a clear fuel movement scheme — from source and storage to dispensing — and accurately calculate fuel inflow, outflow, and balance. The module is useful in the following scenarios: when using a tanker truck with multiple compartments when operating a stationary fuel station or oil depot when fuel intake, storage, and dispensing must be tracked within one unified structure In this module, you can: create and configure composite objects add elements (objects) and sensors to them use them in reports and monitoring for fuel calculations How to create a composite object 1. Before creating a composite object, make sure that the following are already configured in the system: the required objects (vehicles, tanks, and other elements that will be included in the scheme) the corresponding sensors All objects and sensors must be added and properly configured in advance — only then can they be combined into a composite object. 2. Go to Main → Composite objects 3. Click the add button 4. Enter the name of the composite object 5. It will appear in the list under the Composite objects tab How to add elements to a composite object 1. Open the created сomposite object 2. Click the Add button 3. Specify the following: Object — the element of the composite object (select from the list) Sensor — select from the asset’s available sensors Date — the date from which the element is active Element type — its role in the fuel scheme: Tank — a fuel storage container, such as a tanker compartment, stationary reservoir, or generator tank Input flow meter — measures the volume of fuel entering the system, for example fuel intake at an oil depot, filling a stationary tank, or recording fuel received before storage Flow meter — measures the volume of fuel leaving a tank, for example vehicle refueling, dispensing fuel to clients, or supplying fuel to equipment Input tank — an external fuel source, such as a large supplier reservoir 4. Click Save The element will become part of the composite object and appear in the list. Click on it to display it in the diagram. Add all required elements. Configuring the scheme At the bottom of the window, you will see the scheme section. 1. Drag elements with the mouse to arrange them as needed. 2. Connect them with arrows — from input to output. 3. Click Save below the diagram After that, the system will use this scheme in fuel calculations and reports. Working with the composite objects list If the list is large, you can: 1 — use the filter 2 — use the Search field To refresh the list, click the Refresh button . To create a new composite object, click . To delete an element or an entire composite object, click the Delete button next to it in the list. To edit an object, double-click it in the list — the editing window will open."},{"s":"notifications-","t":"Notifications","h":["Notifications"],"x":"Notifications A notification is an automatic message sent by the system when a specific event occurs, as defined in your settings. Notifications keep you informed about key events, such as: engine start or stop (ignition on/off) speeding or exceeding speed limits changes in fuel level fuel refuelings or drainings entering or exiting a geofence loss of connection with the device maintenance alerts Notifications enable you to respond quickly to critical events and monitor the status of your objects in real-time. The Notifications module allows you to configure: the conditions that trigger the notification the objects the notification applies to the delivery method, such as email, SMS, Telegram, in-system messages, or mobile app push notifications."},{"s":"how-to-create-a-notification","t":"How to create a notification","h":["How to create a notification","Main notification parameters"],"x":"How to create a notification Once the Notifications module is activated, a new Notifications tab will appear in the Account settings. Use this tab to configure the events that trigger automatic notifications for users. 1. Go to Accountsettingsand open the Notifications tab 2. Click 3. The notification configuration window will open Main notification parameters 1. Template Select a Template if you wish to use a pre-configured set of settings. A template is created after you have configured a notification — simply fill in all the parameters and click Save Template. You can then reuse this configuration for other notifications 2. Selection by object or tag Specify which objects the notification should apply to: By objects — select specific vehicles or pieces of equipment By tags — apply the notification to a group of objects sharing the same tag 3. Type and name Enter a Name for the notification to help you quickly identify it in the list and understand which event it monitors. Select the Delivery Method you wish to use: SMS — text message to a phone Push — notification in the mobile app Email — email message Alert — a pop-up notification within the system interface Control Room — the event will be displayed in the Control room Webhook — data transmission to an external service Command — sends a specific command to the tracker. Command template — sends a pre-configured command template to the device. Telegram — notification via a Telegram bot To receive Email notifications, please ensure your email address is verified in your personal settings. If needed, you can enter a Alternate message textthat will be sent when the notification is triggered. If the Control Room module is active, the Algorithm binding button will be available. This allows you to set up automated processing scenarios — for example, a sequence of actions that the system should perform when a specific event occurs. 4. Notification time In the Notification time block, you can define when the notification should be active: Select a time zone Specify days of the week Set specific time Intervals 5. Additional information Check the parameters you wish to include in the notification text: Current speed Object driver Geofence name (if the Geofences module is active) Google maps link Live link to the event Event address The live link to the event opens the object's location directly on the map from the notification. After clicking, you will see the object's current position and its main parameters. This link is temporary and will expire after a certain period. 6. Notification types and conditions Select the event that should trigger the notification. Examples include: Speeding Sensor triggers Idling or movement Loss of connection Other system events Each notification type has its own set of trigger conditions (e.g., speed threshold, event duration, or sensor values). For more details, please refer to the \"Notification types and trigger conditions\" article. 7. Geofence parameters This setting is available if the Geofences module is active. You can specify which geofences should trigger the notification. Select one or more geozones from the list or use the button . Then, choose where the notification should be active: Anywhere — the notification is sent regardless of the object's location. In selected zones — the notification is sent only if the object is inside the marked geofences Outside selected zones — the notification is sent only if the object is outside the marked geofences After configuring all parameters, click Save. If you wish to save this configuration for future use, click Save Template. f you enable the For all users parameter, the notification will be sent to every user within the account. All created notifications are listed in the Notifications tab under Account settings. To learn how to manage them, please read the \"Working with the notifications list\" article."},{"s":"notification-types-and-trigger-conditions","t":"Notification types and trigger conditions","h":["Notification types and trigger conditions","How to configure conditions","Detailed notification types","Speed","Ignition events","Ignition operation","Sensor events","Sensor work","Change of sensor value","Multisensor events","Sensor complex event","Sensor promblems","Device problems","Pressure","Temperature","Humidity","Connection lost","Odometer","Moving","Parking","Geofences","Fuel level","Fuel Refuelings & Drainings","Approaching to object","Formula","Blocked for limits violation","Scheduler","Alarm media","iButton duplicate detector","Routes"],"x":"Notification types and trigger conditions When creating a notification, you select the event type that the system will monitor and configure its trigger conditions. These conditions determine exactly when a notification is sent—for example, when a speed limit is exceeded, during a prolonged stop, or when a sensor value changes. Available notification types: Speed Moving Parking Odometer Routes Scheduler Connection lost Device problems Sensor events Ignition operation Ignition events Sensor work Change of sensor value Multisensor events Complex sensor event Sensor problems Temperature Humidity Pressure Fuel Level Fuel Refuelings & Drainings Geofences Approaching to object Formula Alarm media iButton duplicate detector Blocked for limits violation How to configure conditions 1. Select a notification type The notification type determines which specific event the system monitors—for example, speeding, idling, or a sensor trigger. 2. Set parameters Define the criteria, such as a speed threshold or the duration of an event. 3. Add additional conditions (if needed) To add another condition, click the button To remove a condition, use the button You can change the order of conditions using the arrows If multiple conditions are set, you can choose a logical rule between them: AND — the notification triggers only if ALL conditions are met simultaneously OR — the notification triggers if AT LEAST ONE of the conditions is met Detailed notification types Speed The notification triggers if the object's speed matches the specified condition. Conditions: Speed is: Greater than Less than Equal to the specified value You can specify how long the condition must be met: seconds minutes hours kilometers You can set a repeat interval to avoid receiving too many identical notifications. Example: If the speed exceeds 60 km/h for more than 60 seconds, the system sends a notification. The next notification will appear only after the set interval (e.g., 5 minutes) if the speeding continues. To learn how to set up overspeeding notifications in the system, watch the video “How to create an overspeeding notification” on YouTube. Ignition events The Ignition events notification type is used to receive messages when the ignition state of an object changes. Available conditions: On — the notification is sent when the ignition is turned on Off — the notification is sent when the ignition is turned off On and Off — the notification is sent for both events Moving without sensor on — the notification is sent if the object starts moving, but the ignition sensor is not turned on If necessary, you can enable the Failure trigger option. This helps track repeated errors or unstable sensor performance. If this option is enabled, the following additional fields become available: Error count — how many times the error must repeat for the notification to trigger Reset time — after how many minutes the error counter will be reset Ignition operation This notification type allows you to monitor how long an object remains with the ignition turned on or off. After selecting this type, you must define the trigger condition: Longer than — the notification is sent if the ignition is on or off for longer than the specified value. Enter the numerical value for the duration and select the unit of measurement: seconds, minutes, hours, or kilometers of travel Break duration — a setting that allows the system to consider only those events separated by a break of a specified length. You must also specify a value and a unit of measurement for the break Sensor events The Sensor events notification type allows you to receive notifications when the state of a selected sensor changes. Available conditions: On — the notification is sent when the sensor switches to the \"on\" state Off — the notification is sent when the sensor switches to the \"off\" state On and Off — the notification is sent for any change in the sensor's state Moving without sensor on — the notification is sent if the object starts moving, but the selected sensor is not turned on If necessary, you can enable the \"Failure trigger\" option. This helps track repeated errors or unstable sensor performance. If enabled, the following additional fields become available: Error count — how many times the error must repeat for the notification to trigger Reset time — after how many minutes the error counter will be reset Sensor tags — select the group of sensors to which this condition applies Watch the video “How to link sensor tags to notifications” to quickly learn how to configure sensor tags and set up notifications for alerts via various channels. Sensor work With this notification type, you can monitor how a sensor value changes and how long it remains in a specific state. After selecting the type, configure the conditions: Have no changes — the notification is triggered if the sensor value does not change for longer than the specified time. Specify a numeric value and a unit of measurement. Longer than — the notification is triggered if the sensor remains in a specific state or its value meets the condition for longer than the specified time. Specify a value and a unit of measurement. Break duration — defines which break in sensor operation is considered long enough to end the current event. Sensor tags — select the sensor tag group to which the condition will be applied. If a sensor with the selected tag is in the active state, the system starts counting how long it has been active. When the duration reaches the Longer than value, an event is created and a notification is sent. After the notification is sent, repeated notifications for the same event are not sent while the sensor continues working without interruption. If the sensor turns off, the system starts counting the break duration. If the sensor turns on again before the Break duration time has passed, the event is considered ongoing, and a new notification is not created. If the sensor remains off for at least the time specified in Break duration, the current event ends. The next sensor activation period is considered a new event. For example, if Longer than = 10 minutes and Break duration = 2 hours, the notification will be sent after 10 minutes of sensor operation. A repeated notification will appear only after the sensor has been off for at least 2 hours, then turns on again and works for longer than 10 minutes. Change of sensor value The Change of sensor value notification type allows you to track how much a selected sensor's value has changed over a specific period of time. After selecting this type, you must define the trigger condition: Difference more than — enter the numerical value of the change required to trigger the notification. Example: \"5\" means the notification triggers if the value changes by more than 5 units Absolute value (Abs) — if enabled, the system tracks changes in both directions (increase and decrease) In period — the time interval during which the change is analyzed (seconds, minutes) Sensor tags — select the group of sensors to which this condition applies Multisensor events The Multisensor events notification type is used for sensors that operate based on a state table (e.g., equipment status, device operating mode, switch positions). Such a sensor can have several possible values, and the notification is triggered when its state changes. Next, configure the conditions: Changes only — if this option is enabled, the notification is sent only when the sensor value changes. Ignore values out of table — if this setting is enabled, the system will only consider values specified in the sensor's table. All other values (not described in the table) will be ignored, and no notifications will be sent for them. Sensor tags — select the group of sensors to which this condition applies. Sensor complex event The Sensor complex event notification type allows you to configure a single notification based on multiple sensor events simultaneously. Select the sensor tags to which the condition applies Reset time — the period after which the system resets the event state Sensor promblems The Sensor promblemsnotification type is designed to detect potential problems in sensor operation and ensure their correct functioning. This notification also acts as a fault indicator: A yellow indicator is displayed near the object status when a malfunction is detected A red indicator is displayed when the sensor enters a \"failure\" state You can enable one or more conditions simultaneously: Out of calibration table — the notification is sent if the sensor value remains outside the calibration table for a specified interval of time. No changes — the notification is sent if the sensor value does not change for the specified time. Changes count — the notification is sent if the number of sensor value changes is above or below a set threshold over a specified period of time. Additionally, you can take into account: Geofence enter — sensor changes will only be tracked when entering a geofence. Geofence exit — sensor changes will only be tracked when exiting a geofence. Additional Rule — allows setting a custom condition for fault detection. You must specify: Sensor field name Condition: >, <, = Value The use of handlers and formulas is supported. Failure trigger settings — if a malfunction repeats several times, the sensor may enter a \"failure\" state. In the trigger settings, you define: Error count — how many malfunctions must occur for the sensor to enter a failure state. Reset time (min) — the period without errors after which the failure status is automatically cleared. Trigger reset rules — specifies how long an object must stay at a parking spot or travel in motion without errors for the malfunction status to be automatically cleared. You can configure the conditions under which the trigger resets: Parking time (in minutes) Moving time (in minutes) Formula (if necessary) Sensor tags — select the group of sensors to which this condition applies. Device problems The Device problemsnotification type is designed to detect incorrect tracker operation or errors in data transmission. One or more conditions can be enabled simultaneously: Coords change without speed — the notification is triggered if the object's coordinates change, but the speed remains zero. You must specify a threshold and a unit of measurement. Speed without changing coords — the notification is triggered if the device transmits speed, but the coordinates remain the same. Coord change — the notification is sent upon a sudden or abnormal change in coordinates. You can set parameters to define the threshold beyond which the event is considered a problem. Additional rule — allows setting a custom condition for fault detection. You must specify: Sensor field name Condition: >, <, = Value Failure trigger settings — if a problem occurs several times, the device may enter a \"failure\" state. In the trigger settings, you define: Error count — how many times the problem must repeat. Reset time (min) — after how many minutes without errors the failure status will be cleared. Trigger reset rules — you can configure the conditions under which the trigger resets: Parking time (in minutes) Moving time (in minutes) Formula (if necessary) Pressure The Pressure notification type is used to monitor the readings of pressure sensors. The notification is triggered if the pressure value matches the specified condition. After selecting this type, you must define the trigger condition: Condition — select how the system should compare the pressure value: Greater than — the notification triggers if the pressure becomes greater than the specified value Equal to — the notification triggers if the pressure equals the specified value Less than — the notification triggers if the pressure becomes less than the specified value Value — specify the numerical pressure value for comparison Longer than — specify how long the condition must be met for the notification to trigger. You must enter a number and select a unit of measurement: seconds minutes hours kilometers of travel Repeat interval — sets a pause between identical notifications if the condition continues to be met Example: If the pressure is below the specified value for more than 10 seconds, the notification is sent once. The next notification will appear only after the specified interval if the condition still persists. Sensor tags — select the group of sensors to which this condition applies Temperature This notification type is used to monitor temperature via a selected sensor (e.g., in a refrigerator, engine, or other equipment). Next, configure the conditions: If temperature — defines the main rule by which the system compares sensor readings with a specified value in degrees Celsius (°C). Available options: Greater than — the notification triggers if the temperature exceeds the specified value Equal to — the notification triggers if the temperature is exactly equal to the specified value Less than — the notification triggers if the temperature falls below the specified value Out of range — the notification triggers if the temperature goes outside the specified range (e.g., below +2°C or above +8°C) Longer than — allows you to set the minimum duration the condition must be met for the notification to trigger. This helps filter out brief and insignificant fluctuations (e.g., when opening a refrigerator door). In the \"Units\" field, you can choose seconds, minutes, or hours Have no changes — a separate condition that helps detect possible sensor malfunctions when readings \"freeze\" at one value. The notification is triggered if the temperature does not change for longer than the specified period Sensor tags — select the specific temperature sensors to which this rule applies To learn how to receive alerts about critical temperature changes, watch the video “How to set up temperature notifications” on YouTube. The video shows where to find notification settings and how to create a temperature notification to monitor conditions and respond in time. Humidity The Humidity notification type is used to monitor humidity via a selected sensor (e.g., in a cargo hold or refrigerator). After selecting the type, define the conditions: If humidity: Greater than — the notification triggers if the humidity is above the specified value Less than — the notification triggers if the humidity is below the specified value Equal to — the notification triggers if the humidity equals the specified value Out of range by — the notification triggers if the humidity deviates from the specified range by the indicated value Value — specify the numerical humidity value for comparison. The value is set in percent (%) Longer than — specify how long the condition must be met for the notification to trigger: Seconds — the condition is met for longer than the specified number of seconds Minutes — the condition is met for longer than the specified number of minutes Hours — the condition is met for longer than the specified number of hours Have no changes — this setting allows you to monitor situations where humidity does not change for a specified time. The notification is triggered if the humidity value remains unchanged for longer than the specified period. Connection lost The Connection lostnotification type is used to monitor the connection status of an object. The notification is triggered if no data is received from the device for a specified time or distance. After selecting this type, you must define the trigger condition: Longer than — specify how long the connection loss must last for the notification to trigger: Seconds — triggers if connection loss lasts longer than the specified number of seconds Minutes — triggers if connection loss lasts longer than the specified number of minutes Hours — triggers if connection loss lasts longer than the specified number of hours Kilometers — triggers if the object has traveled the specified distance without connection Odometer The Odometer notification type is used to monitor the object's mileage via the odometer. It allows you to receive notifications if the mileage is greater or less than a specified value over a selected period. After selecting the type, define the conditions: If Odometer: Greater than — the notification triggers if the object's mileage is greater than the specified value. Less than — the notification triggers if the object's mileage is less than the specified value. Monthly limit exceeded — the notification triggers if the mileage for the month has exceeded the established value. Units — specify the numerical mileage value for comparison. The value is set in kilometers. Period — select the period over which the condition is checked: Absolute — the condition is checked for the entire period after the notification was created. Per month — the condition is checked within the current month. Per day — the condition is checked within the current day. Moving The Moving notification type is used to monitor the movement of an object. The notification is triggered if the object is in motion for longer than a specified time or covers a specified distance. Configure the conditions: Longer than — specify how long the object must be in motion for the notification to trigger. Enter a number and select the unit of measurement: Seconds — triggers if the movement continues for longer than the specified number of seconds. Minutes — triggers if the movement continues for longer than the specified number of minutes. Hours — triggers if the movement continues for longer than the specified number of hours. Kilometers — triggers if the object has traveled more than the specified distance in motion. Break duration — this setting allows the system to account only for movement events separated by a break of a specified length. If the break between two segments of movement is less than the specified value, the system will consider them one continuous movement. Example: If a break of 10 seconds is specified, a stop of less than 10 seconds will not interrupt the movement event. Parking The Parking notification type is used to monitor object stops. The notification is triggered if the object remains stationary for longer than the specified time. After selecting this type, you must define the trigger condition: Longer than — specify how long the object must be stationary for the notification to trigger: Seconds — triggers if the stop lasts longer than the specified number of seconds Minutes — triggers if the stop lasts longer than the specified number of minutes Hours — triggers if the stop lasts longer than the specified number of hours Geofences The Geofences notification type is used to monitor events related to geofences. It allows you to receive notifications when an object enters a geofence, leaves it, stops inside it, or passes through it without stopping. Available conditions: In — the notification triggers when the object enters the selected geofence. Out — the notification triggers when the object leaves the selected geofence. In+Out — the notification is sent for both entering and leaving the geofence. Stop in geofence — the notification triggers if the object stops inside the geofence. No stop in geofence — the notification triggers if the object enters a geofence but leaves it without stopping. Min. speed — for stop or no-stop conditions, you can specify a minimum speed. This parameter helps determine whether the object is considered stationary or moving within the geofence. Fuel level The Fuel level notification type is used to monitor the fuel level via a selected sensor on an object. The notification is triggered if the fuel level matches the specified condition. After selecting this type, you must define the trigger condition: If fuel level: Greater than — the notification triggers if the fuel level is higher than the specified value Less than — the notification triggers if the fuel level is lower than the specified value Equal to — the notification triggers if the fuel level equals the specified value Units — specify the numerical fuel level value for comparison with the sensor readings Fuel Refuelings & Drainings The Fuel Refuelings & Drainingsnotification type is used to monitor events related to changes in fuel level. It allows you to receive notifications about fuel refuelings and drainings on an object. After selecting this type, you must specify which events should trigger the notification: Refuelings — the notification is triggered when the system records a fuel refueling Drainings — the notification is triggered when the system records a fuel draining Refueling start — the notification is triggered at the moment the refueling begins Draining start — the notification is triggered at the moment the draining begins Approaching to object The Approaching to object notification type is used to monitor situations where one object approaches another within a specified distance. In this notification type, one object is treated as a moving geozone, and the system tracks when another object enters or leaves this zone. After selecting this type, you must define the trigger condition. If the distance to the object is: Less than — specify the distance at which an object is considered to be near another object. The value is set in meters Longer than — specify how long the object must remain within the action zone of another object for the notification to trigger. The value is set in seconds Enter — if this option is enabled, the notification is triggered when the object enters the action zone of another object Exit — if this option is enabled, the notification is triggered when the object leaves the action zone of another object Objects — select the objects from the list for which proximity tracking will be enabled The system compares the objects from the list and determines when one object comes within the specified distance of another. In this process, the object being approached is treated as a moving geozone. For example, if a vehicle from the selected list comes closer than 100 meters to another object and remains nearby for more than 30 seconds, the system will send a notification. Formula The Formula notification type is used to verify a user-defined condition specified as a formula. The notification is triggered if the result of the formula corresponds to the value true or false. In the Formulafield, you must enter the expression for verification. You can use fields from raw data, sensor values, handlers, and formulas in this expression. The notification is triggered if the result of the verification is: true — the condition is met false — the condition is not met Blocked for limits violation The Blocked for limits violationnotification type is used to send a notification if an object has been blocked due to a violation of established limits. If the system records such a lock, a notification will be sent via the selected channel. After selecting this type, you must specify the delivery method, for example: Email SMS Webhook Telegram Push If Email is selected, notifications will be sent to the specified email address. This notification type is automatically applied to all existing objects, as well as to all new objects created after its configuration. Scheduler The Schedulernotification type is used to monitor object movement during working and non-working hours in accordance with its work schedule settings. After selecting this type, define the trigger conditions for the notification: In scheduled time — the notification is triggered if the object is moving within the established working hours Out of scheduled time — the notification is triggered if the object is moving outside the established working hour Longer than — specify how long the object must be in motion for the notification to trigger Break duration — this setting allows the system to account for breaks between movement events. If the break is shorter than the specified value, the system will consider the movement as continuous. For example: if a break of 10 seconds is specified, a short stop of less than 10 seconds will not interrupt the movement event Alarm media The Alarm media notification type is used to send a notification when a device successfully uploads a media file associated with a recorded event to the storage server. This notification is triggered not at the moment of the event itself, but only after the file has been successfully transmitted and saved on the server. For example, a notification can be sent when the device uploads a photo of a violation, a video fragment of an event, a snapshot from the driver's cabin, a road situation image, or any other file created after the event was recorded. Such notifications can be used for events related to ADAS / DMS violations and warnings, such as driver distraction, phone use, smoking while driving, fatigue, collision risk, lane departure, or harsh braking. The Repeat interval parameter allows you to set a pause between identical notifications if the device uploads several media files consecutively for the same event. iButton duplicate detector The iButton duplicate detector notification type is used to detect situations where the same iButton is recorded on more than one vehicle within a specified period of time. Such a notification helps detect incorrect driver identification. For example, if a driver used the same iButton on one vehicle and then on another, the system might assume the driver switched to another object and update the link. As a result, trip data, driver information, and reports may become incorrect. After selecting this type, you must define the condition: For the period — specify the time during which the system will check if the same iButton was used on more than one object. The notification is triggered if the same iButton is detected on multiple objects within the specified time. For this notification type to work correctly, the following must be true: an iButton sensor must be configured on the objects the drivers module must be in use the notification must be linked to the objects that need to be monitored Routes The Routesnotification type is used to monitor events on routes that have already been assigned to an object. In the settings of this notification, you choose which route events should trigger notifications. After a vehicle is linked to a route, the system begins comparing the object's trips with the assigned route: it records route entry and exit. it tracks the passing of control points. it includes trips in route reports. In the settings for this notification type, you specify which route events should trigger notifications: In the stop area — the notification is triggered when the object is within the zone of a control point or stop. The radius of such a zone is defined during route configuration on the map. Outside the stop area — the notification is triggered when the object leaves the zone of a control point or stop. The zone boundary is also determined by the radius specified when editing the route. Entry in route — the notification is triggered when the object enters the route boundaries. Route boundaries are determined by the route width configured on the map. Out the route — the notification is triggered when the object leaves the route boundaries."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-the-notifications-list","t":"How to work with the notifications List","h":["How to work with the notifications List","Notifications list","Notification templates","Export notifications","Import notifications","Automatic notification assignment"],"x":"How to work with the notifications List All created notifications are displayed in the Notifications tab in Account settings. Here you can: view the list of all created notifications create new notifications edit existing notifications and delete unnecessary ones work with notification templates automatically apply notifications to new objects Notifications list The table displays all notifications available in the current account. For each notification, the following information is shown: ID — the internal notification identifier User — the user who created the notification Name — the notification name Type — the event type the notification is based on Date — the date and time when the notification was created Transport — the objects assigned to the notification The following actions are available on the right side of each row: Edit — open the notification for editing Delete — delete the notification Notification templates The tab includes notification export and import functions. You can use already configured notifications as templates and transfer them to another account. 1 — Export notifications 2 — Import notifications Export notifications When exporting, the current list of notifications is saved as a JSON file. 1. Click Export notifications 2. The system will create a file in JSON format This file can be used as a template — for example, to quickly configure notifications in another account. The following data is automatically excluded from the file during export: vehicles, geofences, phone numbers, and email addresses. This is done because another account may contain different objects and contacts. After import, this data must be specified manually. Import notifications Import allows you to upload previously saved notifications from a JSON file. Before importing, choose the appropriate option: Do not remove old notifications — new notifications will be added to the existing ones Remove old notifications — current settings will be replaced with the imported ones How to import: 1. Select the file to upload 2. Click Import Automatic notification assignment The Enable auto add notificationsoption determines whether notifications will be automatically applied to new objects. If this option is enabled, all created notifications are automatically assigned to new objects that you add to the account. If this option is disabled, new objects will need to be added to notifications manually."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-webhook-notifications","t":"How to work with Webhook notifications","h":["How to work with Webhook notifications","How webhook notifications work","How to set up a webhook","How to insert a sensor value","How to get raw data","Request example"],"x":"How to work with Webhook notifications A webhook is a way to automatically send event data from the system to an external service or your server. For example, if a vehicle exceeds the speed limit, the system can automatically send a request to the URL you specify and pass information about the event. How webhook notifications work 1. You specify the URL address — where the data should be sent 2. When the notification is triggered, the system sends a POST request with JSON 3. Your server receives the data and processes it as needed Before you start, make sure that: the URL is correct and accessible the server responds with code 200 (OK) if the server does not respond or returns an error, the address will be temporarily added to a blacklist\\ How to set up a webhook 1. Go to the notification creation or editing page 2. In the Type and name section, select Webhook as the delivery method 3. To configure webhook notifications, click the edit field — the Webhook details window will open, where you can set the data sending parameters In the Webhook details window, you will see three fields: Enter the address to which the data will be sent. This field specifies the format of the transmitted data. By default, the required value is already set: [\"ContentType: application/json\"] This is the data that will be sent. By default, the following template is used: { \"agent_id\": \"%AGENTID%\", \"vehiclenumber\": \"%VEHNUM%\", \"ts\": \"%TS%\", \"lat\": \"%LAT%\", \"lon\": \"%LON%\", \"speed\": \"%SPEED%\", \"data\": \"%DATA%\", \"zone\": \"%ZONE%\", \"driver\": \"%DRIVER%\" } You can modify or extend the request body using the allowed placeholders listed on the right. Make sure to follow the required format: \"value\": \"%VALUE%\". If the syntax is incorrect, the webhook will not work. %ACCOUNTID% Account ID %AGENTID% Object ID %VEHNUM% Vehicle number %TS% Timestamp %DATETIME% Date and time %LAT% Latitude %LON% Longitude %SPEED% Speed %ODO% Mileage (from sensor) %DATA% Notification data %ZONE% Geofence %ZONE_ID% First geofence ID %ZONE_NAME% First geofence name %DRIVER% Driver %MAXSPEED% Maximum speed (for overspeed notifications) %DUR% Overspeed duration %SPEEDLIMIT% Configured speed limit (Speed Control) How to insert a sensor value If you need to send data from a specific sensor: 1. Open the Sensors tab 2. Copy the exact sensor name 3. Return to the webhook settings window and insert the variable into the Body field in the following format: %Sensor Name% For example, if the sensor is named Fuel sensor status, enter: %Fuel sensor status%. How to get raw data You can also send raw data directly from the device: 1. Go to the Sensors tracing tab 2. Find the parameter in the Raw data column 3. Insert it into the Body field in the webhook window in the following format: %Parameter Name% For example, if the parameter is named Valid, enter: %Valid% Request example When the notification is triggered, your server will receive data in approximately the following format: { \"agent_id\": 4328, \"ts\": 1545254055, \"data\": [\"У286НМ 67\", \"Parkingdur\", \"2\", \"1\", \"мм\"] } The data set depends on which variables you specified in the request body and which notification type is configured."},{"s":"how-to-set-up-notification-delivery","t":"How to set up notification delivery","h":["How to set up notification delivery"],"x":"How to set up notification delivery To start receiving notifications: 1. Go to Account settings and open the Privacy tab 2. Find the Notification settings section 3. Check the types of notifications you want to receive: Speed Refuelings Connection lost Idle Violation control Drivers 4. For each selected notification type, choose how you want to receive notifications: Email — notifications to your email address To receive email notifications, you need to verify your email address. Alert — pop-up notification in the system SMS — text message to your phone Telegram — message in Telegram Push — notification in the mobile app 5. Click Save"},{"s":"how-to-view-notifications","t":"How to view notifications","h":["How to view notifications","What is shown in the Alerts window"],"x":"How to view notifications When an event configured in the system occurs, you receive a notification. It appears in the Alerts window. When you log in to the system for the first time, this window may open automatically. If you closed or minimized it, open it again by clicking the notification icon on the top panel. The icon shows the number of unread notifications, so you can immediately see whether there are any new events. What is shown in the Alerts window In the window, you will see a list of all notifications. For each event, the following is displayed: date and time when the notification was triggered object notification text with an event description Click a notification to expand it and view the details. In the expanded view, the following may be displayed: a more detailed event description additional information, such as: address, geofence, direct link to the event The following options are available at the bottom of the window: Clear — remove the list of notifications from the window Mute — turn off the sound for new notifications OK — close the Alerts window"},{"s":"how-to-view-notifications-in-history","t":"How to view notifications in History","h":["How to view notifications in History","How to enable notification display","How to view notifications"],"x":"How to view notifications in History You can see where and when notifications were triggered in the History section. How to enable notification display To make notifications appear in History: 1. Go to Contract Settings and open the Map & Geocodingtab 2. Find the Map & Historysection 3. Enable the Notifications option 4. Save the settings How to view notifications 1. Open the History section 2. Build a track for the required period 3. Open the Eventstab The list of events will show notifications that were triggered during the selected time period. Click the required notification in the list — a marker will appear on the map showing the location of the event and information about it."},{"s":"bus-lines-","t":"Bus lines","h":["Bus lines"],"x":"Bus lines The Bus lines module helps automate and simplify working with bus routes, schedules, and stops. With this module, you can: create and edit bus lines add and manage stops assign buses to lines set up schedules monitor bus movement in real time generate reports on schedule adherence"},{"s":"how-to-add-stops","t":"How to add stops","h":["How to add stops","How to link an RFID tag to a stop"],"x":"How to add stops A stop is a geographic area that the bus must visit during a bus line route. 1. Go to the bottom part of the workspace — here you will see the list of all stops 2. Click the Add stop button to create a new stop 3. In the window that opens, fill in the following: Name and description Coordinates: enter manually using arrows or by moving the marker on the map Stop radius (m) — the zone within which the bus arrival is registered Optionally, you can check: Technical stop — not included in passenger boarding or alighting, but counted in the route calculation. A technical stop is a point on the route that: Does not appear in passenger schedule Does not record passengers boarding or alighting Is used in route construction — helps define the correct route trajectory Can be used for bus movement monitoring — e.g., as a control point Does not affect passenger flow reports — its data is not included in boarding/alighting statistics Final stop — the point where the route ends, after which the bus starts the return trip. If you mark a stop as Final, the system will treat it as the last stop on the route: The route will be trimmed — all stops after this one will not be used The bus will start moving in the opposite direction — switching to the return schedule (if the line is bidirectional) Only stops up to and including this final stop will be included in schedules and reports 4. Click Save — the stop will appear in the list. In the list, all added stops are displayed with ID, radius, type, and description. To edit a stop, double-click it in the list and make changes in the window that opens. To delete a stop from the list, use the delete icon . Once you add bus lines, the list will show all created lines and the number of stops in each. Click the arrow next to a bus line to expand its stops list. In each stop entry, there is an arrow — click it to open a menu to manage the route: Forward / Back — change the stop order on the route Turnaround — indicate that the bus turns at this stop and starts moving in the opposite direction To remove a stop from a line, right-click the stop in the list and select Delete. How to link an RFID tag to a stop A stop visit can be detected using an RFID tag. When a vehicle reads a tag linked to a stop, the system processes the data as a visit to that stop within the assigned bus line. Before linking a tag, add and configure it in the RFID Tagsmodule. The tag must be located within the stop radius. To link RFID tags: 1. Create a stop or select an existing one 2. Click the tag icon next to the stop 3. In the RFID Tags for Stop window, find the required tag by its name or ID 4. Select the checkbox next to the tag 5. Select additional tags if necessary 6. Click Save You can link multiple RFID tags to one stop. In this case, the system can process the visit when any of the linked tags is read. To display only the tags already linked to the stop, select the Linked checkbox. If the required tag is not listed, check that it has been added and configured in the RFID Tagsmodule. Also make sure that the tag is located within the stop radius. After linking the tag, add the stop to a bus line and then assign that bus line to the vehicle. Once all these steps are completed, the system can match the RFID tag read by the vehicle with the corresponding stop and process the visit."},{"s":"how-to-create-a-bus-line","t":"How to create a bus line","h":["How to create a bus line"],"x":"How to create a bus line A bus line is the movement of a vehicle from the starting point to the destination along a specified route and schedule. 1. In the workspace, locate the Bus lines section 2. On the toolbar, click the Add line icon 3. In the window that opens, fill in the following: Name and number of the line Description Check the box if the line is bidirectional (the vehicle travels back and forth along the route) Interval between trips (in minutes) Ticket price (if applicable) 4. Add the required stops to the line — select them from the list of available stops and click the arrow to include them 5. Click Save — the bus line will appear in the main list To edit a bus line: right-click the line and select Edit from the menu or double-click the line in the list — a window will open where you can make changes and save them To delete a bus line: Choose Delete from the same menu."},{"s":"menu-for-managing-bus-lines","t":"Menu for managing bus lines","h":["Menu for managing bus lines","Edit","Timetable","Buses","Show all stops","Live monitor","Upload stops or route line from KML","Build line and duration for the route","Delete"],"x":"Menu for managing bus lines The bus line menu contains several tools. To open it: right-click on the desired bus line in the list or click the arrow next to the line Edit Click this option to open the Edit line window. Here you can: Change the name and number of the line Update the description Adjust parameters: direction, interval, ticket price Modify the list of stops After making changes, click Save. Timetable A timetable is the schedule that specifies when a bus should arrive at each stop along the line. From here, you can add a new timetable or view existing ones. See the related article on how to create a timetable. Buses This section shows all buses assigned to the selected line. Read here how to link a bus to a route. Show all stops Click this option to display all stops of the route on the map. Click on any stop to view its name. Live monitor Select Live monitor to view the entire route with stops and the schedule in real time. The live map shows: Buses on the route — icons with bus numbers represent vehicles currently moving along the line Arrival prediction — click a bus icon to see when it will reach the next stop Stops — marked with triangles Arrows — indicate the direction of travel Green and yellow lines — show the current positions of buses along the route Timeline — at the top and bottom, you can see when each stop is scheduled to be reached Upload stops or route line from KML KML is a special file format for geographic data such as points, lines, and areas on a map. To import stops or a route line from a KML file: 1. Select the corresponding menu option 2. Choose the required KML file from your device 3. Click Save The file will be added to the line, displaying stops or the route line on the map. Build line and duration for the route After adding all stops to a line, click Build line and duration for the route. The system will calculate travel time between stops and display it in the Live monitor view. Stops with estimated arrival times will only appear after calculation — they won’t show otherwise. The calculation usually takes up to 7 minutes. This feature helps visualize the stop order, route path, and arrival predictions. The data is used for building schedules, monitoring adherence, and generating reports. Delete To remove a bus line, select this option from the menu."},{"s":"tools-for-working-with-bus-lines","t":"Tools for working with bus lines","h":["Tools for working with bus lines"],"x":"Tools for working with bus lines You can use the following tools to manage your bus lines: live monitor Use this tool to view all routes of selected bus lines in real-time. Check the box next to the desired line in the list and click this tool to display it. add bus line group Create a new group, enter its name, and clickSave. The group will appear in the bus lines list. To organize lines, simply drag and drop them into the desired groups. add bus line Use this tool to create a new bus line. See the related article on how to add a bus line. clear map Removes all routes, stops, and objects from the map. refresh bus lines list Updates the data in the bus lines list so you always see the latest changes and up-to-date information. Use this button if you’ve added or edited lines and the updates didn’t appear right away."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-timetables","t":"How to work with timetables","h":["How to work with timetables","How to add a timetable:","Timetable tools"],"x":"How to work with timetables A timetable is a schedule that specifies when a bus should arrive at each stop along a specific bus line. How to add a timetable: 1. Right-click on the desired bus line in the list and select Timetable from the menu 2. In the Timetable window, click Add timetable and enter a name for it 3. Click the button on the right to add a timetable table 4. In the pop-up window, select the type of timetable: Daily By days of the week On a specific date 5. Click Create 6. In the Timetable window, a timetable chart will appear for Bus Line 1 with all stops. Assign the bus arrival time for each stop. Here, you can also delete an existing trip or add a new one. When adding a new trip, specify its start time. If you check Continue , the system will automatically create several trips in a row with the given interval (step) and departure time. If you leave it unchecked, you can manually add and configure each trip with its own departure time. 7. Fill in the timetable for each trip Use the Import andExport buttons to upload timetables from files or export them. Timetable tools The Timetable window provides several tools: view timetable history The timetable history shows all periods when a specific timetable was active for the bus line: when it was assigned and until when it was used. How to activate a timetable: 1. In the timetable list, find the desired timetable. 2. Click the light gray checkmark next to it — it will turn dark gray. This indicates the timetable is currently active for the bus line. 3. The timetable becomes active and is now applied to the bus line 4. The system will recalculate the route and schedule based on the new timetable. This may take a few minutes 5. After recalculation, you can generate a Report on timetable adherence. export the current timetable to a file or import a timetable from a file create a new timetable remove the selected timetable update timetable data"},{"s":"how-to-assign-a-bus-to-a-line","t":"How to assign a bus to a line","h":["How to assign a bus to a line"],"x":"How to assign a bus to a line To enable a bus to operate on a specific route and appear in the timetable, you need to assign it to a particular line: 1. Go to the Online tab 2. Find the required vehicle, right-click on it, and select Bus linesfrom the menu 3. In the opened window: Check the boxes for the desired bus line(s) Specify the start date and time for the trip 4. Click Save In the same window, you can also view the assignment history — click the History button to see active lines and assignment dates."},{"s":"timetable-","t":"Timetable","h":["Timetable","How to create a timetable"],"x":"Timetable The Timetable module works together with the Bus lines module. It defines when a vehicle should pass the trip checkpoints or begin another run along the route. Timetable data is used in the Round Trips report. The report compares the planned time with the vehicle’s actual movement and shows any deviations from the timetable. The Bus linesmodule defines the route, while the Timetable module defines the planned times for travelling along that route. How to create a timetable 1. Open the Timetable tab 2. Click Add timetable 3. Complete the timetable settings: Name — enter a name for the timetable Bus line — select the trip for which you want to create the timetable. The trip must first be created in the Trips module. The timetable will be linked to the selected route Time — enter the planned time at which the vehicle should pass each stop on the selected trip, and then click +. Add each time separately. The added times appear in the list at the bottom of the form For example, if the trip has three consecutive stops, you can add: 07:15 — planned time at the first stop 07:30 — planned time at the second stop 07:45 — planned time at the third stop Is circle start time — select this option if the added times should indicate when new route cycles begin For example, if you add 07:12, 08:12, and 09:12, the vehicle should: begin the first route cycle at 07:12 begin the second route cycle at 08:12 begin the third route cycle at 09:12 A new route cycle means that the vehicle starts another run along the selected route from its starting point. The added values appear in theTime section at the bottom of the form. To remove a value, click the trash can icon next to it. 4. Click Save"},{"s":"routes-","t":"Routes","h":["Routes"],"x":"Routes The Routesmodule is designed for creating, editing, and monitoring vehicle route execution. With this module, you can: create routes of any complexity configure checkpoints assign routes to assets analyze route completion in reports"},{"s":"how-to-create-a-route","t":"How to create a route","h":["How to create a route","How to create a route","Main settings","Control time","Route completion percentage","How to add route points","How to assign an RFID / BLE tag to a route point","How to view or edit a route"],"x":"How to create a route To create a route in the system, you need to fill in the main parameters in the route builder and add route points that will be displayed on the map. In this article, we explain how to create a route and configure its parameters so it is ready for use in the system. How to create a route 1. Go to the Routestab 2. Click — the Build route form will open 3. Fill in the route parameters Main settings 1. Name Enter a clear and descriptive route name, for example: “Route No. 5”, “Downtown Delivery”. 2. Detection range (km) This parameter defines how far from the route line a vehicle can be and still be considered as following the route. In simple terms, the system creates a “corridor” around the route. For example, if you set 0.3 km, it means 300 meters on each side of the route line. As long as the vehicle moves within this corridor, the system considers it to be on the route. If it goes beyond the corridor, a route deviation is recorded. You can enter a decimal value (for example, 0.3). A value that is too small (for example, 0.05 km) may cause false deviations due to GPS inaccuracies. A value that is too large (for example, 5 km) may treat significant detours as part of the route. 3. Geocoder Select the service that automatically determines the address based on the coordinates of route points. Control time This is the planned time required to complete the entire route. For example, if the route usually takes 2 hours and 30 minutes, enter 2 hours and 30 minutes. This value is used in reports: to compare planned versus actual time to check whether the driver meets the expected schedule Route completion percentage This parameter defines what percentage of route points must be visited for the route to be considered completed. For example, if there are 10 points and the percentage is set to 90%, the driver may skip one point and the route will still be considered completed. This allows flexibility in case of unforeseen circumstances, such as accidents or traffic jams, when the driver is forced to bypass part of the route. Active checkbox To apply the completion percentage rule: 1. Enter the required percentage (for example, 80) 2. Enable the Active checkbox 3. Save the route builder How to add route points A route point is a checkpoint where the system records the fact that a vehicle has passed a specific location. 1. Go to thePoints section below the route settings 2. Click 3. Select the required location on the map or enter the address manually. The point will appear on the map 4. If necessary, drag the marker to the exact position On the map, a route point is displayed as a numbered icon surrounded by a circle — this circle represents the detection radius. On the map, it appears as a red dashed line. The radius determines when a vehicle enters or exits the checkpoint area. When a vehicle enters the radius, the system records the point as visited. To change the radius, left-click on it and drag it to the desired size. The radius affects detection accuracy: the larger the radius, the greater the allowed margin for visit confirmation. A radius that is too large may register arrival before the actual stop, while a radius that is too small may result in a missed point. If the vehicle does not cross the checkpoint radius, the system will not record the visit — this will be considered a route deviation. 5. Repeat the process for other route points and click Save If you add two or more points, the system will automatically build a route between them. How to assign an RFID / BLE tag to a route point A BLE tag is a small physical device (Bluetooth Low Energy) installed at a specific location, for example at a terminal stop or on a client’s premises. When a vehicle approaches this location, the tracker detects the tag and sends its unique identifier (IButton parameter) to the system. Why this is useful: when GPS signal is unstable or jammed when precise confirmation of arrival at a specific location is required when accurate route completion tracking is important Tags can only be assigned to route points if the RFID Tags module is enabled. Before assigning tags, they must be configured in that module. To learn how to create a tag, refer to the corresponding article. If you use BLE tags: 1. Open the route 2. Select the required route point 3. Click the tag icon next to the selected point 4. In the pop-up window, select the required tag and assign it to the point The tag must be located within the radius of the point. 5. Click Save You can assign multiple tags to a single route point. After assigning the tag, save the route. When a vehicle follows a route, the system applies the following logic: 1. Checks whether a BLE tag is assigned to the route point. 2. Searches for an IButton event within a ±5-minute interval from the time of passing. 3. If the tag is detected, the point visit is recorded based on BLE data. 4. If the tag is not detected, the system uses the standard GPS geofence validation. As a result, even if the GPS signal is unstable, the route will still be validated correctly — based on the actual physical arrival at the point. How to view or edit a route 1. Go to the Routes tab 2. Find the required route in the list 3. Right-click on the route 4. Select Edit 5. The route builder will open, where you can make changes or simply review the current settings"},{"s":"how-to-create-a-route-from-a-track-in-history","t":"How to create a route from a track in History","h":["How to create a route from a track in History","How to create a route in History"],"x":"How to create a route from a track in History You can create a route not only through the Routesmodule (as described in the previous article), but also directly from a track in theHistory section. How to create a route in History 1. Go to the History section and select an asset from the list by checking the box next to it 2. Set the required time interval to search for tracks and click Search 3. The system will display a list of track records for the selected asset 4. Find the track for the required date. Right-click on it to open the track menu 5. Select Create route from the menu 6. The route will open on the map and will be displayed in grey 7. If you want to create a route based on only part of the track, select the start and end points of the required segment by clicking on the track line. 8. After selecting the segment, click Go to route builder to configure the route parameters 9. In the route builder, set the required parameters and click Save Your route will now be saved and will appear in the routes list."},{"s":"how-to-assign-a-route-to-an-object","t":"How to assign a route to an object","h":["How to assign a route to an object"],"x":"How to assign a route to an object To allow the system to track route-related events and generate reports for a specific object, you need to assign the route to a vehicle. 1. Go to Main → Online 2. Find the object you want to assign the route to 3. Right-click on the object and select Route 4. In the window that opens, select the routes you want to assign to the selected object 5. Click Save to confirm the assignment After saving, the system will prompt you to recalculate reports because routes have been changed or reassigned for this object. Recalculation ensures that all data is up to date and that reports correctly reflect the updated route configuration. If you want to recalculate reports, click Yes, specify the recalculation period (start and end dates), and click Start calculate Report recalculation runs in the background. Once completed, the data will be updated automatically."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-a-route","t":"How to work with a route","h":["How to work with a route","Route menu","Actions with a route","Center map on route","Edit route","Recalculate route","Delete","Viewing a route and its points","Show points on map","Rename a point"],"x":"How to work with a route This article explains how to work with routes in the system, what actions you can perform, and which tools are available. Route menu On the Routes tab, you can see all created routes. To open the menu and perform actions on a route, right-click on the desired route. Actions with a route Here are the main actions you can perform from the route menu: Center map on route Select this option to focus the map on the route. The map will automatically center on the route. Edit route Click Editin the menu to open the route builder. In this mode, you can: change route parameters add or remove points adjust other settings Recalculate route To recalculate a route and analyze its data, open the Recalculationsmenu. All objects assigned to the route will be displayed here. For each asset, you can: start a recalculation, or view the recalculation history Right-click an asset to open the actions menu. Start recalculation — select the start and end dates; the system will recalculate route completion for this period List calculates — shows when and which recalculations were performed for this route 1 — Start recalculate 2 — Focus vehicle track To recalculate again, select a previous recalculation and click Start recalculate. Focus vehicle trackthis button shows the vehicle’s movement track for the recalculated period. It is active when at least one recalculation has been completed. Delete If a route is no longer needed, it can be deleted. Confirm deletion, and the route along with all its points and assigned assets will be removed. Viewing a route and its points On the Routes tab, all created routes are displayed in a list. To expand a route and see its points, click Selecting a route will display it on the map Clicking a specific point in the list will automatically focus the map on that point Show points on map Check the Show points box to display individual route points on the map. Uncheck it to view the route without points. Rename a point Right-click a point in the list, select Rename, enter the new name, and clickOK."},{"s":"pilot-task-manager","t":"OneGPS Task manager","h":["OneGPS Task manager"],"x":"OneGPS Task manager OneGPS Task Manager (PTM) is a module within the OneGPS system designed for task management. It acts as an electronic dispatcher and task manager: here you create tasks, assign performers, and track their completion. With PTM, you can: Create and assign tasks Plan work by dates and routes Monitor task status and deadlines Work with reports and the calendar Who uses PTM: Dispatchers and logisticians: Plan and assign tasks Drivers and performers: Receive tasks and mark them as completed Managers: View reports and results Access to PTM functions depends on your role and permissions. If you have administrator rights, you will have access to all functions. For users with limited permissions, only the necessary set of features for their work will be visible. If you need access to additional functions, please contact support. Watch the video to learn how to activate PTM and log in."},{"s":"how-to-log-in-to-ptm","t":"How to log in to PTM","h":["How to log in to PTM"],"x":"How to log in to PTM To log into OneGPS Task Manager: 1. Go to the following link: https://tasks.OneGPS-gps.com 2. Enter your username and password for your account 3. If you'd like the system to remember your login details, check the box Remember me — next time, you will be logged in automatically 4. If needed, you can change the language of the interface. To do this, click the language selection button in the top right corner and choose your preferred language 5. Click Log in— you will be taken to the electronic dispatcher panel"},{"s":"interface-overview-2","t":"Interface overview","h":["Interface overview","Left menu","Left menu","Top panel","Top panel","Profile","Workspace"],"x":"Interface overview After logging into the OneGPS Task Manager (PTM) dispatcher panel, you’ll land on the main page. Here, you will find tools for managing tasks, users, and settings. Below is a brief overview of the main interface elements. Left menu The left menu contains the main sections of OneGPS Task Manager Left menu Click on any item to navigate to that section. Main sections in the menu: Dashboard This section displays the tasks you are working on. You can view, open, and manage them. Driver reports A list of acts related to tasks, such as work completion reports or acceptance and transfer acts. Users Manage system users: roles, permissions, and settings. Queue Tasks that are not yet assigned or require processing. Dictionary Auxiliary data and settings used when creating tasks. Settings General settings for working with the PTM module. Calendar / Calendar v2 View tasks in a calendar format. Privatw office Access your personal settings and information. The left menu can be collapsed or expanded to create more screen space for working. To do this, click the arrow at the bottom of the menu. The left arrow collapses the menu to icons. The right arrow expands the menu and shows the section names. Top panel At the top of the screen, in the top-right corner, you’ll find the account and notification controls: Top panel Reset table settings – removes saved column order and filters in the browser. Use this to restore tables to their default view if they appear incorrectly Sound – turns sound notifications on or off Bell – displays system notifications, such as new tasks Envelope – important messages, like SMS balance reminders Account Name – displays the current user’s name Language – choose the interface language Log out – logs you out of the system Profile Icon – takes you to your personal account Profile To go to your personal account, click on the profile icon in the top-right corner. In your personal account, you can view and edit your profile details: Name Email Password Date of Birth Phone numbers Additional info: Address, Position, Department, Description To edit your data, click Edit, make changes, and then click Save. Workspace In the center of the screen, you’ll find the workspace. This is where the main content of the selected section is displayed: task lists, calendar, users, etc. The content of the workspace changes based on which section you select in the left menu."},{"s":"dashboard-","t":"Dashboard","h":["Dashboard","Task table","Task management panel","Task management panel","Task templates","Download task template","Upload tasks from template","Export to Excel","Task menu","Workspace selection on the dashboard","Resources","Tasks","Signature","Tenders"],"x":"Dashboard The Task management dashboard is a section in PTM where you can see all the created tasks and manage them. This dashboard serves as the window to view and handle tasks that have been assigned or are in progress. In this guide, we will cover: How the task table is structured and what information its columns display Buttons and features for quick task management How to create, upload, and export tasks using templates What actions are available when right-clicking on a task row How to switch between workspaces Task table In the center of the panel, you'll see a table with the created tasks. Each row in the table represents an individual task. The columns in the table show task-related data, such as: Task ID – the unique task number in the system Time of arrival – when the task was created Task type – scheduled time or estimated completion time for the task Order type – the category of the task (e.g., \"Delivery,\" \"Booking,\" etc.) Status – the current status of the task Scheduled date and time – planned arrival/departure time Actual date and time – the actual arrival and departure times (from the tracker or driver) Vehicle – the vehicle assigned to the task Driver – the name of the driver assigned to the task Driver accepted – when the driver confirmed receiving the task Started moving – the actual time the vehicle started moving Task started – the time the task was initiated Task completed – the time the task was completed Task management panel At the top of the task table, there is a task management panel with buttons that allow quick task management. Task management panel Add – create a new task Edit – open a selected task for editing Delete – delete selected tasks Filter settings – configure filters to display only the tasks you need Start vehicle rebinding – initiate automatic reallocation and replacement of vehicles in tasks. Use this button when you need to update vehicle assignments (for example, when the fleet changes). Once the process is completed, a window with results will appear: Tasks found – how many tasks were processed Virtual vehicles – how many tasks were linked to virtual vehicles Real vehicles – how many tasks were assigned to real vehicles Substitutions made – how many tasks were assigned a new vehicle Tasks recalculated – how many tasks were recalculated (routes, times, mileage, etc., after vehicle reassignment) Task templates On the right side of the task list panel, you will find three buttons for working with task templates: Download task template this button allows you to download an Excel template for mass task creation. Click on this button to open a window: 1. Select the desired task type (e.g., \"Organization,\" \"Private Person,\" \"Trip Sheet,\" \"Booking,\" etc.). The choice will determine the columns in the Excel file 2. Click Download to get the template file 3. Open the downloaded file and fill it with your data (addresses, dates, drivers, loads, etc.). Upload tasks from template this button is used to upload tasks from a previously filled template Click this button to open a window: 1. Click on the Excel icon and choose the template file from your device. 2. Click Upload. The system will read the rows from the file and create or update tasks based on the data in the template Export to Excel this button downloads the task list in Excel format. Click this button to open a window: 1. In the Template field, select the template. The template choice determines the columns that will appear in the Excel file 2. In the Fromand To fields, specify the period for which you want to export tasks 3. If you want to include only tasks shown in the table with your applied filters (status, vehicle, etc.), check the boxTake into account applied filters 4. Click Download and the system will generate an Excel file with tasks based on the selected template and filters Template downloading will only work if it has been pre-configured in the system. If no template is set up, the system will show an error. Templates can be set in the Templates section during template editing, where parameters visible in the task table and exported to Excel are defined. Task menu When you right-click on a task row in the log, a task menu will appear with various actions for the selected task: 1. Create by template Creates a new task by copying the main fields from the current task (client, route, load, parameters, etc.) so you don't have to fill them in again. The task will be copied with the status \"Not Assigned.\" 2. Pin task The dispatcher assigns themselves as responsible for the task. 3. Export for customer Exports task data to a file, which can be sent to the requester (client). This export works through a configured template. 4. Assign a logistican Opens a submenu where you can choose the logistician responsible for completing this task. 5. Edit Opens the task card to modify its parameters. 6. Delete Deletes the task from the system. 7. Audit statuses Displays the status change history of the task. 8. Task audit Displays the full history of changes for the task (creation, editing, dispatcher assignment, etc.). 9. Set status Opens a submenu to choose a new status for the task, such as \"Closed,\" \"Client Refused,\" \"Visited,\" \"In Progress,\" and others. 10. Signatories Allows you to view or assign responsible signatories for the task or related documents. 11. Print Prints the task document. 12. Work Completed Act Generates and prints a work completion act for the task, available after the task is completed and if there is data for the act. Some items in the menu may be grayed out and unavailable until the task reaches the required status or necessary data is filled in. Workspace selection on the dashboard Above the task management panel and the task table, you will find buttons to open additional workspaces: Resources Tasks Signature Tenders Resources After clicking, the panel opens, showing resources (vehicles and drivers). In this section, you can see: The status of resources (online/offline) Current or next task Schedule and base address Use this area to track who is available, who is busy, and who can be assigned to the next task. Tasks The main workspace where all tasks are displayed. Here you can: Create, edit, and filter tasks Assign resources Track task status Signature A section for working with electronic signatures for tasks and related documents. Tenders The section for working with transport tenders. With this panel, you can: Create tenders based on tasks Send tenders to contractors and carriers Track received proposals"},{"s":"how-to-create-a-task","t":"How to create a task","h":["How to create a task","Task creation form","Top section (Left side)","Top section (Right side)","Act of accomplished works","Map","Bottom section","Control buttons at the bottom of the form","How to create a task from a template"],"x":"How to create a task To create a new task in PTM, click theAddbutton above the task table on the Dashboard — a task creation form will open. Task creation form The Task form is where you define all the important parameters for the task. It is divided into several sections: Top section (Left side) Template — select the task type, which will determine the structure of fields for the task Vehicle — choose the vehicle that will be used for the task Driver and Driver’s Phone — enter the driver's full name and contact number Trailer Plate — enter the trailer number, if one is being used for transport Route Name — internal route identification Cargo Name — specify the cargo being transported Minimum / Maximum Temperature — if temperature control is required, set the minimum and maximum temperatures Top section (Right side) Task Information — here, you will see the key route details: Address List — the primary address/route with options to edit, delete, or add new points to the route Details: Coordinates of Route Points — the geographical coordinates for each route stop. Redirect this point — use this point as a redirect address. Visit Time — the scheduled arrival time. Warehouse — the warehouse or location name. Time at Stop — how long the vehicle should stay at the stop. Radius — the allowable deviation from the route point (in meters). Add Address — button to add another route point. Start Time and End Time — specify the start and end time for completing the task. Description — a field for any additional comments about the task. Act of accomplished works If a Act of accomplished works template has been created for the task template in advance, it can be assigned to the task. In this case, the driver will be able not only to complete the task in the OneGPS Driver mobile application but also to fill out a Act of accomplished works as a task report. Two configuration options are available: Act of accomplished works for a route point— assign a separate act of accomplished works template to a specific route point. Select the required template from the list of previously created report templates. Main act of accomplished works — assign an act of accomplished work template to the entire task. This template is selected in the lower section of the task card. If no separate report template is assigned to a route point, the Main act of accomplished workst will be used. After the task is created, the selected reports will become available to the driver in the OneGPS Driver application. Depending on the configuration, the driver can complete reports either for individual route points or for the task as a whole. Map On the map, the route will be displayed with all the task's route points marked. Bottom section Counterparties — in this section, you can select the company that will be associated with the task. Template for act — choose a report template for completed tasks, if configured in the system. Control buttons at the bottom of the form Backwards — if you decide not to save the task, you can go back to the task table without saving any changes Save — after entering all the details, click this button to save the task and start processing it Forecast — use this button to calculate a forecast for the route Watch the video to learn how to create a task and send it to a vehicle. And also watch the video how to create a task using templates. How to create a task from a template To quickly create a new task based on an existing one, use the Create from templatefeature: 1. Right-click on a task row in the task table 2. In the task menu that appears, select Create from template 3. The system will automatically generate a new task with all the basic data copied from the original task (such as client, route, cargo, parameters) 4. You can then edit the task by selecting \"Edit\" in the menu. This will open the task form, where you can make any necessary changes"},{"s":"actions-with-tasks","t":"Actions with tasks","h":["Actions with tasks","Task progress visualization","Route progress","Map tools","Zooming In/Out","Measuring distance between points","Measuring area","Printing the map","Viewing traffic","Choosing map type","Route player","Route player","Recalculate task"],"x":"Actions with tasks In this article, we’ll cover additional actions you can take with tasks to help you manage and track their execution. We'll explain: How to track the progress of a task being executed by the system and the driver How to use the trip sheet How to initiate task recalculation Within the task table, there is a column Actions that contains icons for performing various operations on a task. Task progress visualization Click on the icon , and a task execution chart will open. The chart helps you track how the task was planned, how the system is executing it, and how the driver is actually performing it. Route progress Click on the icon to open the waybill with detailed route information. The waybill is a detailed report that shows how the vehicle traveled the route. It allows you to track the actual execution of the task by comparing the planned and actual data. Key elements of the waybill At the top, you’ll see the task parameters such as: Task ID — the unique number of the task in the system. Date and Time — when the task was created. Execution Status — the current status of the task. In the center of the screen is a table of route points. Each row represents a specific route point (address). Key columns in the waybill table: Actions — this column contains icons for working with route points, for example: Mark entry into a geofence Mark entry into a geofence (without location check) Mark exit from a geofence Change the working radius of the geofence — click the point to specify a new radius Change location and radius of geofence on the map marker — move the marker on the map and save the changes Save changes — fix the changes to the marker Address — full address of each route point Type — type of point (e.g., loading, unloading, intermediate point) Actual distance and time — the actual distance the vehicle traveled and the real travel time Planned values — the scheduled time and distance for the route point You can customize the columns of the waybill table as needed. To adjust the columns: 1. Click on the arrow in the column header to open the column settings menu, where you can: Sort the data in ascending or descending order Select which columns to display in the table 2. To choose which columns to display: Click Columns — a list of available parameters for display will open Check the boxes next to the parameters you want to see On the right side of the waybill, a map of the route will be displayed: Route points are numbered The red dashed line shows the planned route on the map The green line shows the actual route The current or last recorded location of the vehicle is marked Map tools You can use the following tools to interact with the map: Zooming In/Out To zoom in or out on the map, you have several options: 1. Use the buttons on the map tool panel: and 2. Scroll the mouse wheel: scrolling away from you zooms in, toward you zooms out. The cursor will indicate which area you're focusing on 3. Double-click anywhere on the map to zoom in directly Measuring distance between points This tool helps calculate the distance between two points on the map. To use it: 1. Click on the map to mark the first point 2. Click again to mark the second point 3. A line will appear between the points, showing the distance To measure multiple segments, keep clicking to add new points. The length of each segment will be displayed next to it. Measuring area This tool helps calculate the area of a selected region on the map. To use it: 1. Click on the map to mark the corners of the shape you want to measure 2. When you finish drawing the shape, the system will automatically calculate and display the area Printing the map You can print the current view of the map. When you click the print button , you’ll be given four orientation options: 1. Landscape — Prints the map in horizontal orientation, suitable for wide areas 2. Portrait — Prints the map in vertical orientation, suitable for vertically stretched areas 3. Auto — The system automatically selects the optimal print orientation based on the current map view and proportions 4. Custom — Choose this option if you want to print only a specific area of the map. Select the area using your mouse, and it will be sent to print Viewing traffic The traffic feature shows real-time road congestion with color coding: Green — free-flowing traffic Yellow — moderate congestion Red — heavy traffic To enable traffic display, check the option in the top-right corner of the map. Choosing map type You can choose from several types of maps to display the area in the PTM interface. The map type is the base layer used for visualizing spatial data. To change the map, click the map selection button . At the bottom of the map, you’ll see the route parameters to help you track its progress: Distance — Total distance traveled along the route (e.g., 36.59 km) Travel Time — The actual time spent traveling, excluding stops Total Time — The total time spent on the route, including both travel time and stops Route player At the bottom of the Trip Sheet window, there is a Route Player that shows the vehicle's movement along the route in real-time. Route player Click the Play button ▶ to start playing the route. The vehicle icon will move along the track from start to finish. The timeline below the player shows the entire route interval. You can drag the slider to manually jump to any moment on the route and see the exact vehicle location on the map. Below the timeline, you’ll see: Date and Time (e.g., 14.08.2025, 15:55) — the moment corresponding to the current position of the slider Speed (e.g., 6 km/h) — the vehicle’s speed at that point in the route Additional Player Features: Tracking — if you check the icon next to the eye, you will follow the vehicle on the map while the route is being played Speed control — use the slider on the right to speed up or slow down the playback of the route Recalculate task Click on the icon to start the recalculation of all task parameters based on the latest data. You can use task recalculation if you’ve received new data from the tracker or if the task settings have changed and need to be updated without manually editing them. When recalculating: The system will adjust the route points based on updated data New settings are applied: auto statuses, auto forecasts, changes to templates, and other parameters The system will recalculate the task status and points, planned and actual times, distances, and stop durations"},{"s":"driver-reports","t":"Driver reports","h":["Driver reports","Filtering reports","Actions with reports"],"x":"Driver reports In the Driver reports section, you can view all the reports that have been created for completed tasks. A Driver report is an official document confirming the completion of work for a specific task. Filtering reports To quickly find the required report, use the Search bar — enter the task ID, report ID, title, address, or any other text related to the report. You can also filter the reports by date. To do this, select the date range in the From and To fields. After that, click Set filter for period. To reset the filter, click Clear filter. Actions with reports For each report, the main details are displayed: Unique report number and title The address associated with the report Task name and type. Driver and vehicle that completed the task Start time, acceptance time, and completion time On the right side of each report row, you’ll find buttons for working with it: View report — open the report for viewing Download report — save the report to your computer Print report — print the report for physical documentation Delete report record — remove the report if it is no longer needed"},{"s":"users-1","t":"Users","h":["Users","User actions","How to add a user","How to edit a user"],"x":"Users In the Users section, you can manage all the users of the system: dispatchers, logisticians, drivers, administrators, and other employees. Here, you can see a list of all users and set their access to the system. Each row in the Users table represents a separate user. You'll see the following details: Username — the login name used to access the system Email — work email address Full Name — the user's full name Phone — contact number Role — for example, Logistician, Dispatcher, Administrator Driver — a marker indicating if the account is associated with a driver User actions Above the table, there are several buttons for managing users: Add — create a new user by setting their username, password, contact information, and access rights Edit — edit the details of a selected user Delete — delete a user's account Download — export user information to an Excel file Upload — upload user information from a pre-prepared file Update drivers — update data for driver accounts Show drivers — check this box to see driver accounts in the list as well How to add a user 1. Click on the Addbutton at the top of the screen to open the new user card 2. Fill in the main information: Login — the name under which the user will log in to the system Email — the work email for notifications and access Password — create a password and confirm it Full name — the user's full name Phone — contact number Address — work address, if needed Signatories — select the signatory template if this user will sign documents Permission templates — choose a predefined set of permissions for the user (e.g., Administrator, Logistician, Driver) Position — specify the position (logistician, driver, dispatcher, etc.) Passport — enter passport details if required User Color — choose a color to highlight this user in the system Description — add comments or notes 3. Set permissions On the right side of the user card, there is a block with checkboxes where you can set which actions are allowed for this user. Check the relevant boxes to activate permissions for this account. 4. Save the user card Once all the data is filled in and permissions are set, click Sand. The new user will appear in the list and will be able to log in using the provided username and password. If you change your mind, click Cancel, and the changes will not be saved. How to edit a user To edit a user's details, select them from the list and click theEdit button at the top of the table. A user card will open where you can make the necessary changes. You can also double-click the row with the user, and the user card will open automatically. After editing the user card, click Save to save the changes. Watch the video to learn how to manage users and signatories."},{"s":"queue","t":"Queue","h":["Queue"],"x":"Queue The Queue section shows tasks that are waiting to be sent to a driver for execution. In the table, you’ll see the following information for each task: Task ID — the unique identifier of the task Task name — a short description of the task Driver — the person assigned to perform the task Starting time — when the task was placed in the queue to be sent for execution While tasks are in the queue, you can select a task and click Removeto remove it from the queue. If the queue is empty, it means there are currently no tasks waiting to be sent to drivers."},{"s":"dictionary-2","t":"Dictionary","h":["Dictionary","How to enable the Dictionary section","How to create a dictionary","Dictionary examples","Templates dictionary","Signatories","Task types","Task priorities","Task statuses","Address classifier","Drivers","Notification settings","Permission templates"],"x":"Dictionary The Dictionarysection allows you to create and manage structured data lists that are used across tasks, vehicles, and drivers. For example, dictionaries can store: task statuses cargo types drivers Using dictionaries simplifies daily work: instead of entering the same data manually each time, you select values from predefined lists. How to enable the Dictionary section 1. The Dictionary section is not always available in the PTM menu and is enabled on request. To activate it, please contact technical support 2. After activation, the section will be empty. The system does not include predefined dictionaries by default. You can create and configure dictionaries based on your business needs. How to create a dictionary 1. Open the Dictionary section 2. In the Handbooks list panel, click Add 3. The Add new dictionary window will open On the left, define general dictionary information: Name — the display name of the dictionary (for example: Cargo type, Cancellation reasons) Alias — the technical identifier of the dictionary Primary key, Foreign key, Display key, Parent alias — these fields define the dictionary structure and relationships with other system tables If you’re unsure how to configure these fields, we recommend contacting technical support. On the right, define the dictionary row structure: 4. Click Add above the table on the right 5. Specify: Column alias — internal field name Type — select the data type Optional settings: Is null — allows the field to be left blank Unique — ensures all values in the field are unique Formula — the field value is calculated automatically 6. Repeat steps 4–5 for all fields required in the dictionary. When finished: 7. Click Save 8. The new dictionary will appear in the list on the left 9. Select the dictionary from the Handbook list On the right, the Handbook datatable will open, where you can: add new entries edit existing records delete rows If creating the dictionary structure is difficult, contact technical support. Once the structure is ready, you can manage the data independently. Dictionary examples The Dictionary section supports a wide range of data lists. Since the section starts empty, you fully control which dictionaries exist in your system. Below are common examples. Templates dictionary The Templates dictionary stores all task and document templates. Template defines: which fields appear in the task card which columns are shown in the task list which Driver reports can be generated for tasks How to add a template: 1. Go to Dictionary → Templates 2. Click Add Template window opens. Fill in: 1. A clear template name (for example, City Delivery, Service Visit) 2. A short description of the template’s purpose 3. Optional settings: Default template — automatically selected when creating a new task Send SMS to driver — sends an SMS when a task is created Send SMS to initiator — sends an SMS to the task creator 4. Header template — technical configuration for table headers and Excel exports (contact support if you’re unsure) 5. Click Send.The template will appear in the list After creating a template, configure its fields. Click Parameters to define: which fields appear in the task card the order of fields which columns appear in the task list required and system fields How to add a field to a template: 1. In Templates, select the required template 2. Click Parameters 3. Will open the field table 4. Click Add 4. Fill in: Type — data type Alias — internal field name Name — field label in the interface Group name Default value Weight — controls field order Settings: — Calculated by formula — Field is allow blank — Field is active — Field can be deleted — Visible only in edit mode — Show warehouse name — Show Ticket ID — Is the index of the key to search Permission templates — roles that can access the field Read-only for selected roles 5. Click Save.The new field will be added to the template How to create an act of accomplished works for a template: 1. Go to Dictionary → Templates 2. Select a template 3. Click Act of accomplished works If the button is not visible, check permission templates or contact support. 4. The list of acts for the template opens 5. Click Add 6. Fill in: Name — report name Existing file — use an existing file template if needed File — upload a custom template if required 7. Write the act text in the Editor tab Auto-inserting task data: On the right, in Parameters for act , select fields from: Basic — task ID, dates, addresses, totals Executor — data filled in by the driver in the mobile app (these fields must appear after the basic fields) Template — fields specific to the selected template 2. Copy the selected fields into the acr text 3. Mark the report as Default for the template 3. Click Save Now, tasks created using this template can generate and print the configured driver report. Watch the video to learn how to create and use task templates. Signatories Defines who signs documents and in what order. Watch the video to learn how to manage users and signatories. Task types Defines task categories such as Delivery, Repair, Logistics. Watch the video to learn how to create task types. Task priorities Defines urgency levels: Not urgent, Urgent, Critical, etc. Task statuses Defines all possible task states: New, In Progress, Completed, etc. Address classifier Stores previously used addresses for quick reuse. Watch the video to learn how to add a new address using the Address classifier. Drivers Stores contract driver information used for task assignment. Notification settings This dictionary defines notification rules and delivery channels. How to add a notification — click Addand complete the form: 1. Notification Information Name — descriptive rule name Event — the trigger event Resources — specific vehicles or All 2. Options Event color — highlight color in the UI Delivery channels: Email, SMS, Telegram, Push, Browser alert, Mobile push For all users Geofence address 3. Conditions Task template Task types Device statuses Task statuses Time in status 4. Click Save The notification rule becomes active immediately. Permission templates Permission templates define access levels for users, ensuring everyone can see and edit only what they are allowed to."},{"s":"settings-4","t":"Settings","h":["Settings","Geographic information system (GIS) integration","Calendar settings","Task settings","Integrations","Webhook settings","Additional integration parameters","Account settings"],"x":"Settings In the Settingssection, administrators can configure PTM system parameters, set up integrations with external services, and define the system's default behavior. The following sections describe all available settings. Below is an overview of the available settings. Geographic information system (GIS) integration This section is used to configure mapping services that are responsible for address handling, geocoding, and travel time calculations. 1. Connecting mapping services You can use services such as OSM, DaData, Google, HERE, Yandex, City Guide, and others. For each service, specify: Host, API key, or Token Checkboxes: Use for Geocoding — the service will be used to search for addresses and coordinates. Use for Forecasting — the service will be used to calculate travel time and route forecasts. 2. Geocoding priority Configure the order in which services are queried. The service with the highest priority will be used first. 3. Map layers Select which map providers (OSM, Yandex, Google, etc.) will be available in the system. 4. Forecast parameters Configure the parameters used to calculate estimated travel time: Average speed Average loading/unloading time Maximum driving time Calendar settings This section controls how tasks are displayed in the calendar. 1. Color grouping By task type — tasks are displayed in different colors depending on their type (for example, delivery, maintenance, etc.). By vehicle — tasks are displayed in different colors depending on the assigned vehicle. 2. Enable the display of only active tasks When enabled, the calendar displays only tasks with the following statuses: New In queue Unassigned Sent to driver Accepted En route In progress Pending approval Task settings These settings define how your dispatching system behaves. 1. Requests You can specify a request repository and a request template: Request repository — a request directory obtained through PARUS API integration. It is used to match requests with tasks in PTM. Request template — a predefined template used for grouping and processing related tasks. 2. Color highlighting Configure how tasks are highlighted in tables and calendars: By task type — each task is assigned a color based on its type (for example, delivery, maintenance, etc.). By user — the color depends on the user responsible for the task. User colors are configured in the user profile. 3. Closure reasons You can require users to select a cancellation reason from a predefined directory when canceling a task. 4. Geofence-based auto statuses Auto statuses allow tasks to change status automatically based on visits to route geofences. Geofence radius, m — radius of the virtual geofence automatically created around a route point Geofence accuracy, m — additional value added to the geofence radius during task processing Start delta time, min, min — defines how many minutes before the planned start time a task can automatically enter processing End delta time, min , min — defines how many minutes after the planned completion time a task may be automatically completed Check-in time: Parking time, min; Time to mark arrival, sec; Time to mark departure, sec — minimum time a vehicle must remain inside a geofence for the visit to be recorded. This check applies to both entering and leaving the geofence Enable сircular кoutes — allows proper processing of routes where vehicles repeatedly travel between the same points before returning to the base Completion function — defines the condition used to automatically complete a task. Available completion functions Return to Base — the task is completed when the vehicle is detected again within the base geofence The base can be: The first route point The last route point if it matches the first point A route point with the Base subtype An alternative geofence created in the OneGPS system Complete by shift —the task is completed after the calculated processing time expires: Planned completion time + Finish After value Last route address (Geofence entry) — the task is completed when the vehicle enters the geofence of the last route point. Last route address (Geofence exit) — the task is completed when the vehicle leaves the geofence of the last route point. These settings provide additional automation and task handling rules. Enable for task auto-completion — forcibly completes tasks after a specified number of minutes following the planned completion time. It is not recommended to use this option together with auto statuses. Task execution time — used when calculating the planned completion time if the duration field is not available for manual editing. Enable sending unassigned tasks to all drivers — allows drivers to independently select tasks from the mobile application's shared task queue. To enable task editing in the table only for the current day — restricts direct task editing in the task table to the current day only. Works together with user permissions and applies only to dynamically generated task tables. Enable the autocomplete task by key fields — automatically fills task data using information from previously created tasks based on entered values. Disable selection of system task types — hides service task types from users when creating tasks. Turn on task status highlighting — highlights status cells according to the current task status. By default the initiator in the current user — automatically assigns the current user as the initiator when creating a task. Enable tooltips for table rows — displays brief task information when hovering over table rows. Close task after signing — automatically closes a task after the entire approval and signing workflow is completed. Turn waypoints on bypassing autostatuses — creates route points based on the planned route regardless of auto-status processing. Enable the use of geofence priority for route points — if a route point is linked to a OneGPS geofence, that geofence will be used as the primary geofence for visit detection. Do not use the first point as the last point — disables automatic route closure and affects route sheet generation. Enable the selection of the vehicle for replacement — allows assigning a backup vehicle without changing the primary vehicle assigned to the task. Enable synchronization of address editing with the Address classifier — automatically updates the address directory when an address is modified within a task. Integrations Integration with external services allows the system to exchange data with other platforms. You can: 1. Connect external services 2. Configure automatic data import and export via API, email, or webhooks 3. Define which events and data should be exchanged between systems Integrations are used for: Creating notifications Automatically importing tasks from email using predefined templates (for example, the Magnit integration). To configure an integration: 1. Select an integration group 2. Select an integration system 3. Fill in the required connection settings If only notifications are used, the Magnit integration requires only an email address without a password. Webhook settings Each integration supports the following parameters: Webhook URL — destination URL for webhook requests Webhook headers — additional HTTP headers, such as authorization tokens Webhook parameters — payload data sent in the webhook request To automatically insert values, special functions can be used: fn[getApiKey]— get the API key from the configuration (ERP Sarmix only) fn[getAgentId]— get the vehicle ID from the OneGPS system fn[getCurrentAgentLocation]— get the vehicle's current location fn[getCurrentUTC]— get the current vehicle packet timestamp in seconds fn[getCurrentZoneId]— get the ID of the geofence where the vehicle is located fn[getTaskId]— get the ID of the task currently being executed fn[getErpOrderId]— get the request ID (ERP Sarmix only) fn[getCurrentSpeed]— get the vehicle's current speed fn[getVehicleNumber]— get the vehicle registration number In the Webhook parameters field, specify a JSON object containing the data to be sent to the external system. Special functions are used to automatically substitute values. { \"access_token\": \"fn[getApiKey]\", \"agent_id\": \"fn[getAgentId]\", \"geolocation\": \"fn[getCurrentAgentLocation]\", \"time_event\": \"fn[getCurrentUTC]\", \"zone_id\": \"fn[getCurrentZoneId]\", \"pilot_task_id\": \"fn[getTaskId]\", \"erp_order_id\": \"fn[getErpOrderId]\", \"speed\": \"fn[getCurrentSpeed]\", \"vehiclenumber\": \"fn[getVehicleNumber]\" } The system will automatically replace the functions with actual values, for example: { \"access_token\": \"123456789abcdef\", \"agent_id\": \"4521\", \"geolocation\": \"55.751244,37.618423\", \"time_event\": \"1750054800\", \"zone_id\": \"125\", \"pilot_task_id\": \"98765\", \"erp_order_id\": \"ORD-4567\", \"speed\": \"68\", \"vehiclenumber\": \"A123BC777\" } Additional integration parameters Algorithm ID — the identifier of an algorithm created in the OneGPS system for operator notification processing. Algorithm data — a payload passed to a OneGPS algorithm when a notification is triggered. This configuration is typically performed by technical support when required. The structure depends on the specific algorithm and processing scenario. { \"-6\": { \"text\": \"Perform the required actions\", \"options\": [ \"Action 1\", \"Action 2\", \"Action 3\" ] } } Additional parameters: Algorithm timer — timeout value for the algorithm in seconds. Sensor ID — identifier of a sensor in the OneGPS system. Email addresses — list of email addresses that will receive notifications. Account settings Configure system-wide settings for your account. 1. Mobile app task queue Enable the task queue for drivers and choose whether: Drivers select tasks themselves Tasks are assigned automatically 2. Profile and integration settings Set login and password Define default system time zone and language Enable ExactFarming integration and configure the token 3. “Logistician – Dispatcher” workflow Enable the workflow chain and select permission templates for logisticians and dispatchers. 4. Mobile interface Enable the new mobile interface and configure automatic generation of driver reports. 5. Message sending Configure notifications sent to task initiators and drivers when needed. 6. System event notifications Set up alerts for system issues such as: Geocoding errors Missing vehicles or tracks Invalid time ranges Geofence overlaps Track gaps"},{"s":"calendar","t":"Calendar","h":["Calendar","Calendar layout","How to create a task from the calendar","Calendar V2"],"x":"Calendar The Calendar displays all tasks in a schedule format and helps you plan work by date. Calendar layout At the center of the screen, you’ll see the calendar grid. You can switch between different view modes: Day — a detailed view of tasks for a specific day Week — an overview of all tasks for one week Month — a high-level overview of tasks for the entire month Each calendar cell shows tasks with their time and task ID. On the left, you’ll find filters that let you choose which task types are displayed in the calendar. For example, you can show only logistics, delivery, loading, repair, or other task types. Each task type is displayed in its own color for easier visual navigation. Main buttons and actions: Create — create a new task directly from the calendar Today — return to the current day Arrow buttons — move to the previous or next period Day / Week / Month toggles — switch the calendar view scale Search by Task ID — enter the task number in the search field to find it in the calendar and click Settings icon — opens additional calendar display options How to create a task from the calendar 1. Click on the desired day in the calendar grid 2. A new task creation window will appear 3. Fill in the required task details 4. Click Sendto save and start the task Calendar V2 Calendar V2 is a scheduling view designed for managing tasks assigned to vehicles. In this version, you can clearly see which task is assigned to each vehicle on a specific day. Calendar structure: The left column displays a list of all vehicles used to perform tasks The top row shows days of the week with dates This allows you to see tasks assigned to each vehicle for each day in a single row Calendar cells display tasks assigned to a specific vehicle on the selected day Main actions and controls: Refresh — reload the calendar and fetch the latest task updates Settings — open additional calendar display settings Arrow buttons — scroll forward or backward to view tasks on future or past days To view or edit a task, double-click the corresponding cell. The task card will open, allowing you to modify all required parameters."},{"s":"private-office","t":"Private office","h":["Private office"],"x":"Private office When you select Private office from the menu, you are taken to your personal workspace. In the Private office, you can view and edit your profile information: Name Email Password Date of birth Phone numbers Additional information — address, job title, department, description To update your information: 1. Click Edit 2. Make the necessary changes 3. Click Saveto apply the updates You can also open thePrivate officeby clicking the profile icon in the top-right corner of the top panel."},{"s":"eco-driving-driving-quality","t":"Eco driving: driving quality","h":["Eco driving: driving quality"],"x":"Eco driving: driving quality The Driving quality module helps evaluate driver behavior and identify unsafe or inefficient driving maneuvers. The system automatically analyzes data received from the device installed in the vehicle and detects driving events such as: harsh acceleration harsh braking harsh cornering speeding excessive idling other events defined by custom rules Each detected violation can be assigned fine points. These data are used to generate Eco driving reports, allowing you to evaluate the driving quality of individual drivers or vehicles. Driving events are generated based on data received from the device installed in the vehicle. The available event types depend on the capabilities of the installed device and the parameters it supports. The overall driving quality score is calculated based on the detected driving events, accumulated fine points, the selected penalty calculation method, and the configured Eco driving weights."},{"s":"configuring-driving-quality-rules","t":"Configuring driving quality rules","h":["Configuring driving quality rules","Opening the driving quality settings","Driving quality rules","Rule parameters","Main event types","Fine types","Creating a rule","Additional fines"],"x":"Configuring driving quality rules Driving quality rules define which driver actions are considered violations and how they affect the overall driving quality score. Each rule monitors a specific parameter received from the device installed in the vehicle, such as speed, acceleration, or idle time. When the configured conditions are met, the system records a driving event. For each rule, you can configure: the parameter to monitor the conditions for recording an event the event type the message displayed in reports the number of fine points and the fine calculation method You can create as many rules as needed and configure them according to your vehicles, installed hardware, and business requirements. Opening the driving quality settings 1. In your account, double-click the required vehicle in the object list 2. Open the Eco Drivingtab in the vehicle card On this tab, you can create and manage the rules used to detect driving violations. Driving quality rules The center of the window contains the list of driving quality rules. Each row represents a separate rule used to analyze data received from the installed device. When the configured conditions are met, the system records a driving event. You can create any number of rules and configure them to match your vehicles and installed hardware. Rule parameters Field The sensor or parameter to monitor, such as speed, acceleration, ignition status, or any other value received from the device Formula An optional mathematical or logical expression used to process the value before it is evaluated Type The driving event type that is recorded when the rule conditions are met. Event types are used to classify violations and calculate Eco Driving metrics. You can use the built-in eventtypesor create your own Message The text displayed in reports when the event is recorded, for example Harsh Braking or Speeding Min The minimum value at which the rule is triggered Max The maximum value that is still considered a violation. For example, if the range is set to 132–250 km/h, the event is recorded when the vehicle speed falls within that range Period The time interval, in seconds, during which repeated occurrences are treated as a single event. For example, if 600 seconds is specified for idling, another idle event will not be recorded until 10 minutes have passed Fine The number of penalty points assigned when the event occurs. A value of 0 records the event without assigning fine points The method used to calculate penalty points Main event types The following event types are commonly used in Eco Driving reports: Acceleration — harsh acceleration Braking — harsh braking Cornering — harsh cornering Speed — speeding You can also create custom event types if required. Fine types Each rule uses one of the following fine calculation methods. Assigns the fixed number of Finepoints specified in the Fine field each time the rule is triggered. For example, if fine is set to 5, each recorded violation adds 5 Fine points. Use this method when every violation should result in the same number of Fine points. Calculates Finepoints based on the severity of the violation. The greater the deviation from the configured threshold, the more Fine points are assigned. Use this method when more severe violations should have a greater impact on the driving quality score. Calculates fine points using the following formula:Violation value × Fine Example: Violation value: 5 Fine: 2 Result: 10 fine points Use this method when the number of fine points should increase proportionally to the severity of the violation. Creating a rule You can create as many driving quality rules as required. To create a rule: 1. Open the Eco-Drivingtab and click Add rule 2. A new row appears in the rule list 3. Configure the rule parameters Specify: 1. the Field to monitor 2. a Formula, if required 3. the Event type. If the required type does not exist, you can create a new one 4. the Messagedisplayed in reports 5. the Minimum and Maximum values that define when the event is recorded 6. the Period for merging repeated events 7. the Finevalue and Fine type Click Save to apply the changes. After saving, the rule is used to process all new data received from the device. To edit a rule, modify the required values directly in the corresponding fields and save the changes. To delete a rule, click the Delete icon in the corresponding row and save the changes. Additional fines The lower part of the window contains additional Fine settings that are applied independently of individual driving quality rules. The following options are available: Restricted geofence fine — assigns fine points when a vehicle enters a restricted geofence Restricted driver fine — assigns fine points when the vehicle is operated by a driver who is not authorized to use the selected vehicle Speed violations fine per/minute — assigns additional аine points for exceeding the configured speed limit After entering the required values, click Save to apply the changes."},{"s":"rule-templates","t":"Rule templates","h":["Rule templates","Ready-made templates","How to apply a template","How to create your own template","How to configure template applicability","Template applicability options","To configure template applicability:","Importing and exporting rules","Exporting rules","Importing rules"],"x":"Rule templates A rule template is a saved set of driving quality settings. It allows you to apply ready-made rules to a vehicle without configuring each rule manually. You can select a template based on the device and vehicle types, apply it to an object, or create your own template. In this article: Ready-made templates How to apply a template How to create a template How to configure template applicability Importing and exporting rules Ready-made templates To open the list of templates: 1. Open the vehicle card 2. Go to theEco Driving tab 3. Expand the Template list The list contains suitable templates and a separate Universal templates group Universal templates are not linked to a particular device or vehicle type, so they can be applied to different vehicles The system also provides ready-made templates for specific telematics devices: CIPIA FLEX — for devices using the Flex protocol (for example, Smart 2333) GB100 ECO (Queclink GB100) GV65 ECO (Queclink GV65) Ruptela Teltonika Select the template that matches the device installed in the vehicle. How to apply a template 1. Open the Eco Driving tab 2. Select the required template in the Templatefield 3. Click Apply 4. The template rules are loaded into the table. Review them and click Save at the bottom of the vehicle card. When you create a vehicle, OneGPS selects templates based on the device and vehicle types. If the vehicle type is not specified, OneGPS uses Car to select templates, but does not automatically fill in the vehicle type field. How to create your own template First, configure the driving quality rules. To add a rule: 1. Click the plus icon 2. Configure the rule parameters 3. Click Save in the rule row To save the configured rules as a template: 1. Click the save icon next to the Templatefield 2. In the Save as template window, specify: Template name Device type — optional Vehicle type — optional 3. Click Save If you leave the device type or vehicle type blank, the template can be used with any value for that parameter. The new template appears in the list of available templates. How to configure template applicability Template applicability determines the devices and vehicles for which the template is available. Template applicability options A template can be linked to: both a device type and a vehicle type only a device type only a vehicle type neither type — in this case, the template is universal If no device type is specified, the template can be used with any device. If no vehicle type is specified, the template is available for all vehicle types. To configure template applicability: 1. In the upper-right corner of the Eco Driving window, locate the Eco Driving tools 2. Click the link icon 3. In the Template applicability window, select: Device type Vehicle type Template 4. ClickLink In the Device type field, you can select a specific equipment model. In the Vehicle typefield, you can select a specific vehicle type or leave All vehicle types selected. In the Template field, select the template whose applicability you want to configure. For example, you can link a template to the FleetGuide device and the Car vehicle type. To clear a selected value, click the cross icon in the corresponding field. After the template is linked, OneGPS considers the device and vehicle types when selecting available templates. The template is not applied automatically: you select it and confirm its application. When a template is applied, its rules are copied to the vehicle settings. If you edit the rules manually, the settings no longer match the original template. Subsequent changes to the template also do not affect vehicles to which it was previously applied. Importing and exporting rules Import and export allow you to transfer driving quality rules between vehicles or create a backup copy of the current configuration. In the upper part of the Driving Quality window, locate the Import and Exporttools. Exporting rules Export saves the current rule configuration to a JSON file. To export the rules: 1. Click the Export button 2. Save the JSON file to your computer The exported file contains all rule settings. Importing rules Import loads a previously exported JSON file. To import rules: 1. Click the Import button 2. Select the JSON file 3. The rules are automatically loaded into the rule table Imported rules replace the current rule set in the table. If you want to keep the existing configuration, export it before importing another JSON file."},{"s":"driving-event-types","t":"Driving event types","h":["Driving event types","Opening the driving event type directory","Creating a driving event type"],"x":"Driving event types Driving event types are categories used by the system to classify driving violations and generate Eco Driving reports. Each driving quality rule is associated with a specific event type. When a rule is triggered, the system records an event of the selected type. The following event types are available by default: Acceleration — harsh acceleration Braking — harsh braking Turn — harsh cornering Speed — speeding Seat Belt — seat belt violation Driving event types are stored in a dedicated event type directory. When creating or editing a driving quality rule, you select the required event type from the Type field. If the predefined event types do not meet your requirements, you can create custom event types. Once created, they become available for use when configuring driving quality rules. Opening the driving event type directory 1. Open the Eco Driving tab 2. In the upper-right corner of the window, locate the Eco Driving tools 3. Click Driving event types The driving event type directory opens. The event types listed in the directory are available in the Type field when creating or editing driving quality rules. Creating a driving event type 1. In the driving event type directory, click Add 2. Enter the event type name 3. Click Save After the event type is saved, the system automatically assigns it a unique identifier (ID). The ID is used internally by the system to identify the event type and does not require manual configuration. The new event type is immediately available for selection in the Type field when creating or editing driving quality rules. To rename an event type, double-click the corresponding row, make the required changes, and save them. To delete an event type, click Delete . Delete an event type only if it is no longer used by any driving quality rule. If the event type is currently assigned to one or more rules, replace it with another event type before deleting it."},{"s":"eco-driving-weights","t":"Eco driving weights","h":["Eco driving weights","Indicators","Example","Configuring Eco Driving weights"],"x":"Eco driving weights Eco Driving weights(weighting factors) determine how much each type of driving violation affects the overall driving quality score. When the system records a driving event, it assigns fine points. The overall driving quality score is then calculated by applying the configured Eco Driving weights to those Fine points. The higher the weight, the greater the impact of that violation type on the final driving quality score. Changing the Eco Driving weights does not affect how events are detected or how many fine points are assigned. It only changes how those fine points influence the overall score in Eco Driving reports. Eco Driving weight settings apply to the entire account and are used when generating Eco Driving reports. Indicators The following indicators are available: Speeding Speed limit violations Excess Idle Excessive engine idling Harsh Brake Harsh braking Harsh Accel Harsh acceleration Harsh Corner Harsh cornering For each indicator, the following values are displayed: Weighting factor — the numeric value used when calculating the overall driving quality score. Slider — allows you to adjust the importance of the corresponding violation type. Percentage — shows the contribution of the violation type to the overall driving quality score. The total of all Eco Driving weights must always equal 100%. Example If you increase the weight for Speeding, speeding violations will have a greater impact on the overall driving quality score than the other violation types. The recorded events and assigned Fine points remain unchanged. Configuring Eco Driving weights 1. Open the Eco Driving weights window 2. Adjust the indicator values using the sliders 3. Make sure the total of all Eco Driving weights equals 100%. 4. Click Save The Harsh Brake and Harsh Accel weights are the most commonly adjusted when you want to increase or decrease the impact of harsh driving maneuvers on the overall driving quality score. The following commands are available at the bottom of the Eco Driving weights window: Update Defaults — saves the current values as the default settings Load Defaults — loads the previously saved default settings Reset Defaults — restores the system default settings The default Eco Driving weights are: Speeding — 58.3% Excess Idle — 10.4% Harsh Brake — 6.9% Harsh Accel — 13.9% Harsh Corner — 10.4%"},{"s":"tachograph-","t":"Tachograph","h":["Tachograph","How to сonfigure data for the module to work","Required sensors","How to view tachograph data","How the system works behind the scenes","How to interpret tachograph data","Table 1","Table 2","Examples of calibration tables","Where to view tachograph reports"],"x":"Tachograph The Tachograph module lets you track and analyze driver activity using data from onboard equipment. All information comes directly from the tachograph — it cannot be edited manually. With this module, you can see when drivers are driving, resting, working, or waiting. Based on these activity records, you can: Understand each driver's workload Verify that drivers follow work and rest regulations Analyze actual work schedules How to сonfigure data for the module to work Go to the Administrator panel and open the Vehicles tab 2. Locate the vehicle linked to the tachograph 3. Right-click the vehicle and choose Edit 4. In the settings window, go to the Configuration tab 5. Click the button to add the module configuration and select Save Setup won’t work unless this configuration is in place. Required sensors To display accurate information, the vehicle must have these sensors added: Driver status Tracks the mode switch (driving, rest, work, waiting) Card status Shows if the driver’s card is inserted, in use, or not Tachograph mode Logs changes in the device’s operating modes For instructions on adding sensors, visit the Sensors section. How to view tachograph data 1. In your user account, go to the Tachograph section 2. Select the vehicle you want to review 3. Use the calendar to pick a date or time range 4. Click Search You’ll see a clear, interactive report showing daily and weekly breakdowns of when drivers were driving, resting, waiting, or working. How the system works behind the scenes All data comes directly from the tachograph as raw input, which the system automatically processes into clear activity statuses: driving, rest, work, and waiting. How to interpret tachograph data Let’s take a closer look at the data tables received directly from the digital tachograph interface — for example, via the CAN bus or another data transmission channel. These raw data are processed by the monitoring system and help determine: Which driver card was inserted (Table 1) The current status of each driver — whether they are resting, working, or driving — and the card status in each slot (Table 2) These parameters are encoded as codes and bit values. To interpret them correctly, the data must be processed by the system or analyzed by a specialist during equipment setup. Below is a decoding of these values according to the tachograph specification. Table 1 This table describes the structure of the packet that transmits the driver card ID number installed in the tachograph: 0x02 – indicates Driver Card 1 or Driver Card 2. The specific card is determined by the type of event that generated the data packet. Event codes associated with these packets help precisely identify which card was inserted and under what condition. 3 Driver card number 16 U8[16] Identification number of the driver card for the tachograph Table 2 This table describes a data byte from the tachograph interface with the identifier ID: 98. This byte contains compressed information about the following parameters: Driver activity — such as rest, availability for work, driving, etc. Card slot status — whether a driver card is inserted, and whether it is authorized or not. The byte is divided into bit fields — a total of 8 bits, split into 4 groups of 2 bits each, where each group represents a specific parameter. Driver 1 Activity 0..1 0 – Rest 1 – Availability for work 2 – Work not related to vehicle driving 3 – Driving Driver 1 Card slot 2..3 0 – No card 1 – Not authorized 2 – Authorized 3 – Card removal failed Driver 2 Activity 4..5 0 – Rest 1 – Availability for work 2 – Work not related to vehicle driving 3 – Driving Driver 2 Card slot 6..7 0 – No card 1 – Not authorized 2 – Authorized 3 – Card removal failed The information from this byte helps to: Connect external equipment to the tachograph Obtain accurate data on driver activity Configure the system to correctly interpret what the driver is doing For example: if bits 0–1 = 3, it means Driver 1 is driving the vehicle. These data are also often used to build calibration tables. Calibration tables are required to convert the raw byte values from the tachograph into readable statuses. Instead of seeing raw values like 2 or 3, you will see statuses such as Restor Card authorized. One byte (8 bits) is divided into four pairs of bits — each pair (2 bits) represents one specific status: 0–1 Driver 1 Activity 0, 1, 2, 3 2–3 Driver 1 Card slot 0, 1, 2, 3 4–5 Driver 2 Activity 0, 1, 2, 3 6–7 Driver 2 Card slot 0, 1, 2, 3 Therefore, you need to configure calibration tables to convert these values into readable driver activity statuses and card states. Examples of calibration tables Calibration Table Driver status. This table is used to decode bit positions 0–1 and 4–5, which represent the activity status of the drivers. Calibration Table Card status. Use this table to interpret the values of bits 2–3 and 6–7, which indicate the status of driver cards. To learn how to configure a calibration table, click here. Where to view tachograph reports The module generates driver activity reports that help you monitor: Periods when the driver was behind the wheel Time spent resting, waiting, or working Driving without a card or with an unauthorized card Time intervals for each individual driver"},{"s":"module-wasl-","t":"Module WASL","h":["Module WASL"],"x":"Module WASL The WASL module is an electronic platform for registering and monitoring land transportation. It helps improve transport safety and service quality. With WASL, you can: issue and renew transport licenses issue and renew driver cards register companies and individual drivers"},{"s":"how-to-set-up-the-wasl-module","t":"How to set up the WASL module","h":["How to set up the WASL module","Adding an API key","Setup for a warehouse company"],"x":"How to set up the WASL module Adding an API key 1. Open the Admin paneland go to the Accounts tab 2. Select the required account 3. Right-click the contract to open the context menu 4. Choose Edit 5. In the contract settings window, open the Modules tab 6. Enable WASL integration by checking the box 7. Open Module settings — click the row with WASL Integration 8. Paste the API key into the corresponding field 9. Click Save The module will not work without an API key. Setup for a warehouse company If your company operates as a warehouse, you need to add an additional configuration: 1. In the contract settings window, open the Configs tab 2. Add or specify the following parameter: SFDA = 1 3. Click Save After this, the WASL module will be fully configured for your company."},{"s":"registering-a-company-or-an-individual","t":"Registering a company or an individual","h":["Registering a company or an individual","Company registration","Delete a company","Individual registration","Delete an individual","Form actions"],"x":"Registering a company or an individual This guide explains how to register a company or an individual in the WASL system. Registration is required to comply with WASL regulations, allowing your company or drivers to be officially recognized and enabling access to services such as transportation licensing, driver cards, and reporting. Following these steps ensures that all necessary information is correctly submitted and verified in WASL. 1. Go to the User account 2. Open the Account section 3. Switch to the WASL tab Company registration 1. Select the type In the Types section, select Company The company registration form will open on the right. 2. Fill in the company form Identity Number Commercial Record Number — the company’s Commercial Registration (CR) number (10 digits) Commercial Record Issue Date — the issue date of the Commercial Registration according to the Hijri calendar Manager Name —full name of the company’s responsible manager Manager Phone Number — the manager’s landline (office) phone number Manager Mobile Number — the manager’s mobile phone number 3. Register the company 1. After filling in all required fields, click Save 2. Then clickRegister company 3. Wait for the response from WASL If registration is successful, the company status and WASL ID will appear. Delete a company To cancel the registration: 1. Click Delete company 2. Confirm the action Individual registration 1. Select the type In the Types section, chooseIndividual The individual registration form will open on the right. 2. Fill in the individual form Identity Number Date of birth Phone number — mobile phone number. Email — email address 3. Click Save 4. Click Register individual 5. Wait for confirmation and a status update Delete an individual To remove the registration: 1. Click Delete individual 2. Confirm the action Form actions In the upper-right corner of the company or individual registration form, you’ll find the available actions: Save — saves all data entered in the form Register company / individual — sends the form data to the WASL system. Use this button only after saving the form and completing all required fields Inquiry сompany — sends a request to WASL for the current company’s certificate Update company contacts — sends updated company contact information to WASL Delete company / individual — removes the registration of the company or individual"},{"s":"tow-truck-in-wasl","t":"Tow truck in WASL","h":["Tow truck in WASL","How to enable Tow truck support:"],"x":"Tow truck in WASL This setting enables a separate activity type Towing сar for your company and vehicles when working with WASL. How to enable Tow truck support: 1. In the Admin panel, open the desired Account 2. Go to the Modules settings tab — click the row with WASL Integration 3. In theConfigs block, click Add 4. Enter the following: Key: TOW Value: 1 5. Click Save Once saved, users in their account (under the WASL tab) will be able to select Towing Car in the Company Activity field. This allows the company to register tow trucks or recovery vehicles in WASL."},{"s":"registration-of-vehicles","t":"Registration of vehicles","h":["Registration of vehicles","Save button","Object registration","Wasl Log","Car service request","Update IMEI","Delete"],"x":"Registration of vehicles 1.Right click on the vehicle, then select the WASL menu item: 2. A form will open for filling out, then you need to fill it out and save it. Save button Save button after filling out the form. Object registration After filling out and saving the form, click \"Register\" to register the car in WASL. Wasl Log After a successful registration, we can see the updated WASL information using the \"Wals Log\" option. Car service request Car service request button, click \"Request\". Update IMEI To change (update) the IMEI of the device, change the IMEI in the form and click \"Update IMEI\". Delete To delete a vehicle from WASL, use the Remove option."},{"s":"warehouse-registration","t":"Warehouse registration","h":["Warehouse registration"],"x":"Warehouse registration 1. Create a folder (the folder will be the warehouse). 2. Add coordinates to the registration form. When adding coordinates, it is necessary to correct the location of the warehouse in the \"Ground coordinates\" field. You must complete the warehouse registration form and save the form by clicking \"Save Form\". After completing and saving the form, click \"Register\" to register the object. 3.The facility is an area in the warehouse. 4. Sensors for this object must be created Temperature and Humidity. The names of these sensors should be the same as in the picture. After successful registration, click \"Wasl Log\" to see Wasl updates."},{"s":"driver-registration","t":"Driver registration","h":["Driver registration","Save button","Driver registration","Delete driver"],"x":"Driver registration 1.Right-click on the driver and select the WASL context menu item: 2. Save button Fill out the form and click \"Save Form\" to save the data. Driver registration After completing and saving the form, click \"Register Driver\" to register the driver. Delete driver To remove the driver, click Uninstall Driver."},{"s":"associate-the-driver-with-the-vehicle","t":"Associate the driver with the vehicle","h":["Associate the driver with the vehicle"],"x":"Associate the driver with the vehicle After registering the vehicle and driver, Link the driver to the vehicle using the \"Link driver to vehicle\" option in driver settings. If each step is successful, we can see the WASL connection status in the main window and the Wasl logs in the Wasl object settings."},{"s":"sensors-2","t":"Sensors","h":["Sensors"],"x":"Sensors Sensors that are added to objects with WASL must be added to sensors with exactly the following names: “Weight” or “weight” •“Temperature” or “temperature” •“Humidity” or “humidity” •“Tamper with weight sensor wasl” •“Accident” An ignition sensor and external power must be added for the other WASL statuses to work properly. Other gauges are optional and will only be displayed in the OneGPS system. Weight sensor example Temperature sensor example Humidity sensor example"},{"s":"crash-detection-2","t":"Crash detection","h":["Crash detection","What the module includes","How to enable crash notifications","How notifications work","Crash history","Crash graph","Graph axes","Example interpretation"],"x":"Crash detection The Crash detection module tracks and processes crash events sent by a vehicle. What the module includes Notification settings — enable the Crash detection notification type in the Account settings Event display in history — crash events are saved in history and shown on the map and charts How to enable crash notifications 1. Go to Account settings 2. Open the Notifications tab 3. Click Add notification 4. In the list of notification types, enable Crash detection 5. Click Save Once enabled, the system will monitor incoming Crash tags from the vehicle and send you notifications. How notifications work When the system receives a crash signal (Crash tag) from a vehicle, it processes it as follows: If the crash data can be decoded (Crash_id), the notification will include detailed crash information. If the data cannot be decoded, you’ll receive a standard message: Crash detected! To avoid a flood of repeated messages (since a device may send the same crash tag multiple times): 1. The system reacts only to the first crash signal and sends a notification. 2. Any duplicate crash tags are ignored until the system receives a “clean” point (without a crash tag). 3. If another crash tag is received after that clean point, you’ll get a new crash notification. This way, you receive only one notification per crash, not multiple repeats. Crash history To find and review a crash event: 1. Go to the Historysection Make sure crash events are enabled in Account settings → Map & geocoding → Map & history → Crash detection. 2. Select the date range when the crash occurred 3. Choose the history tracks 4. Open the Events tab in the history panel 5. Locate the crash event on the map (marked with an icon) 6. Click the marker to view the crash graph Crash graph The graph shows how forces affected the vehicle at the time of the crash. Data comes from the accelerometer — a sensor that records acceleration and sudden changes in movement. The graph includes three main axes: Blue (X) — forward/backward movement (acceleration, hard braking, frontal impact) Purple (Y) — left/right movement (sharp turns, side impacts) Green (Z) — up/down movement (bumps, obstacles, bottom impact) Sharp spikes or drops indicate the moment of impact. They help identify the type of crash: frontal, side, or combined. Graph axes Y-axis (vertical) — acceleration/deceleration force (in accelerometer units) X-axis (horizontal) — time (milliseconds) Example interpretation Before the crash, the lines oscillate around 0 — the car was moving steadily. At the center, there is a sharp drop (blue X and purple Y) — the impact moment, with strong braking and a sideways shift. After the crash, the lines return closer to zero but show small ripples — vibrations after the collision."},{"s":"rent-car-","t":"Rent car","h":["Rent car"],"x":"Rent car To work with the module, you need to enable three modules. Violation control module. Rent Car. Eco driving module. To work with the module, you need to fill in the data on the contract, date, cost and driver. Configuration in the admin panel •max_fee, if the trip scores more points, this driver will receive a notification. trips_warning- if the driver has more than this number of unsuccessful trips, a Garm notification to the control room will be activated. link_host- the host name of the link with trip information that will be sent to the driver. garm_notification_id - the identifier of the notification configuration from the notification module, which will be triggered in the event of a trip warning. Speed_delta is the default delta value that can be changed for each car in the account. Reports for this module are in theReports section."},{"s":"user","t":"User","h":["User"],"x":"User The user interface consists of: Contracts Trips Drivers Contracts Add + Fill out the contract. Trips The window is designed to display rentals. By clicking on a flight, the route of the object will be displayed on the map. Driver Go to the tab Add+. Add a setting. Add a setting. Garm settings Add a setting. Next step. Settings details."},{"s":"rent-in-geozones","t":"Rent in geozones","h":["Rent in geozones"],"x":"Rent in geozones Go to the Geofences module Select a geofence Specify the type. Name - display name of the type. Type - (GREEN, BLUE, RED) - to be displayed in the rental mobile app. Penalty - penalty for visiting this geozone or staying in this geozone longer than the idle limit. •Idle limit- limit in minutes for this geozone (e.g. drop-off zone at the airport) Office rental - office parking rental where a vehicle can be located without a contract Activity zone - geozone where a contract vehicle can operate (e.g. city, region or country)."},{"s":"configuration-for-report","t":"Configuration for report","h":["Configuration for report"],"x":"Configuration for report  {\"top\":{\"Company name\":\"AFRIRENT (PTY) LTD \",\"VAT Number\":\"4340251455\",\"Address\":\"522 Jacqueline Drive Garsfontein Pretoria, 0081\",\"Telephone\":\"(012) 880 - 0108\"},\"bottom\":{\"Bank Name\":\"STANDARD BANK\",\"Account Number\":\"061 016 527\",\"Swift\":\"SWFT\",\"Bank code\":\"CODE\",\"Bank address\":\"bank address here\"}} Configuration keybill_values​​, value in json format, for example: { \"top\": { \"Company Name\": \"AFRIRENT (PTI) LTD\", \"VAT Number\": \"4340251455\", \"Address\": \"522 Jacqueline Drive Garsfontein Pretoria, 0081\", \"Phone\": \"(012) 880 - 0108\" }, \"bottom\": { \"Bank Name\": \"STANDARD BANK\", \"Account Number\": \"061 016 527\", \"Swift\": \"SVFT\", \"Bank Code\": \"CODE\", \"Bank Address\": \"Bank Address Here\" } }"},{"s":"import-from-wialon","t":"Import from Wialon","h":["Import from Wialon","How to import data:","Migration steps","Migration status values"],"x":"Import from Wialon To transfer data from the Wialon system, use theImport from Wialon module. Data migration is performed through a migration wizard — simply select the objects you need and import them into OneGPS. Before transferring data, make sure your Wialon account has export permissions — specifically, access to the Import/Export feature. If you don’t have these permissions, the Import from Wialon won’t be able to export any objects. Watch the video to learn how to use the migration wizard to import data from Wialon. How to import data: 1. Enable the Import from Wialonmodule in the admin panel. After this, the Import from Wialon button will appear in the Main tab 2. Click the button 3. The migration wizard window will open, divided into two parts: On the left: a list of migration tasks On the right: a list of Wialon objects available for transfer 4. Create a new migration task: 1) Click Add task 2) In the pop-up window, fill in the fields: Wialon login — your Wialon login Wialon password — your Wialon password 3) Click Load object list from Wialon 4) At the bottom, you’ll see a list of objects available for import. Check the boxes next to the objects you want to import Objects marked as processed are already selected in other tasks and cannot be chosen again. 5) Click Save 5. The new task will appear in the list on the left in the migration wizard. All statuses will be empty, meaning the task has not yet started. 6. To start the data transfer, click on the task in the list and then click Run task Once started, the task cannot be edited. 7. After starting the task, data transfer will begin automatically. The status of each step will update in the migration wizard window — this usually takes less than a minute. Wait for the process to finish: you’ll see the statuses change and new data appear. To monitor the migration process and see the latest statuses, click the Refresh list button. After running task ID: 322, the only Wialon object was fully and successfully transferred to OneGPS. Migration steps For each object, the following migration steps are displayed: Step Description start Task started. Migration has begun and the system is starting the process auth Authorization in Wialon API. The system connects to Wialon to access your data wlp Exporting object parameters. The main parameters of objects are exported from Wialon (shows the number of successfully processed parameter files / total number of Wialon objects in this task) wln Exporting object events. Events related to objects are exported from Wialon (shows the number of successfully processed event files / total number of Wialon objects in this task). veh Creating objects in OneGPS. Objects are created in the OneGPS system based on the Wialon data (shows the number of successfully created OneGPS objects / total number of Wialon objects in this task). sns Importing sensors. Transfers data about sensors installed on the objects. evt Importing events. All events related to the objects are imported (for example, speeding, refueling, alarms). rc Data recalculation: rc1 — tracks: recalculating routes and movement tracks rc2 — binary sensors: recalculating data from sensors with two states (e.g., on/off) rc3 — sensors: recalculating data from all sensors rc4 — odometer: recalculating mileage rc5 — eco: recalculating eco-driving data rc6 — multi-sensors: recalculating data from complex (multi-channel) sensors Migration status values Value Description empty Step not performed Step completed successfully Step completed with errors 1001 Wialon API error code no data Wialon API error message (no data)"},{"s":"garbage-collection-module","t":"Garbage collection module","h":["Garbage collection module"],"x":"Garbage collection module Sensors are installed in the containers, signaling to the OneGPS platform that the container is full to the sensor level. The sensor is installed at a height of about 80% of the full tank, so that there is still time for the garbage collector to arrive. The operator of a waste removal company sees on the OneGPS platform in real time information that the occupancy of several garbage containers is approaching the upper mark and creates a task to build an optimal route to collect these overflowing containers earlier. The garbage truck driver receives a task in the application about the need to pick up certain containers and the optimal route to them. The operator sees the task being completed online in real time. In the event of an emergency, such as a fire in a dumpster, the operator also receives a notification on the OneGPS platform (as well as via SMS, push or email, depending on the method convenient for him) about the incident and the need to respond. Moreover, on the OneGPS platform, you can pre-set a scenario for responding to emergency situations (a fire in a garbage can, an accident with a garbage truck, a car leaving the route) and then any operator of a garbage collection service company will know what to do if a particular situation occurs . This module does not have a separate icon and its operation consists of two connected modules, these are the Logistics Module and the PTM Module. The module works with one of these Modules, but it is recommended to use two of the above modules simultaneously. The module settings are displayed in the context menu only with the Dumpster type. In order to create a task, you must select Create a task in PTM. You only need to create garbage collection jobs from the context menu of the garbage can object. When the Logistics module is connected, the Client's name will be available. By default, the current time is set, which can be changed, for example, to a future period. The specified planned time will be taken into account when completing the task. If you want to select a larger number of tanks per car, you should select the first and last container and hold down shift. Further data on the completion of the garbage collection task can be tracked in the PTM module. The PTM module in this case is intended for viewing, not creating tasks. To create tasks, you must select the second menu Create a task in Logistic. It is important to note that the Logistics module takes into account the settings from the facility’s work schedule and takes into account the warehouse operation settings. Setting the scheduled time for garbage collection. For objects, you should also fill in the parameters for Logistics. There is no provision for creating a task in Logistics; a garbage collection task is created only from the context menu, as shown above. Created tasks can be found through the date filter. Window for creating a landfill address in the Logistics module. Application processes are displayed in the Status column. 1. Fire 2. Temperature sensor 3. Full Tank 4. Tilt Alarm"},{"s":"agro-","t":"Agro","h":["Agro"],"x":"Agro The agricultural module is a combination of two modules. Geofences Module and Trailers Module. Add new trailer +. Fill in the fields. Save. Add trailer Trailer Name – The name of the trailer. • TypeList of trailer types. •Width– When we use farming equipment like a forklift we can determine its width and from this dimension we can calculate how much area has been processed in the field. The formula for calculation is: Distance * Trailer Width / 10,000 = Ha. Group – Fleet group structure to group trailers. • RFID – If we are using RFID tags we can automate the binding and auto detaching of trailers by installing sensors on the objects and similar to Ibuttons for drivers we can create a sensor to detect the RFID tag of the trailer and bind or detach. Description – Any additional details about the trailer. Exclusivity – if we check exclusivity, then each trailer can only be linked to one object at a time, which means that if we link the same trailer to another object, it will be disconnected from the previous one, or if this object is used to link to another trailer, it will be disconnected from the first one. Agriculture– as explained earlier, this should enable the trailer for the agricultural module. Send alerts– if we check this box, we will receive alerts on the OneGPS platform when the trailer is linked or not linked to any object. Access to vehicles– we can restrict access to the vehicles to which the trailer can be attached. Object tree – all objects that the user has the right to manage and when selecting a link to the current object. Show history – we can check the history of this trailer, and also change it if necessary. Data recalculation – available from the admin panel of the OneGPS part with the type Agriculture. Trailer History Select Trailer. Select the history menu. If everything is set up correctly, the agricultural module will aggregate data when the object is inside a working field with an agricultural trailer attached. When the trips are completed, we can use the history module to play back the agricultural history data. Tick the checkbox for agriculture. If there is data, we can see for each field the history of trips with track width (trailer width settings) and the calculated distance and area traveled: The report for this module is in theReports section."},{"s":"avis-integration-","t":"Avis Integration","h":["Avis Integration"],"x":"Avis Integration After activating the module, shortcuts with panel elements will appear. These settings are uploaded to an xls file with data on objects, then this file can be loaded back into the contract, for example after a mass replacement of data on objects. Mass update window Import"},{"s":"admin-panel-","t":"Admin panel","h":["Admin panel"],"x":"Admin panel Here you will find information on creating contracts, working with modules, using and working with the configuration, as well as making other settings for effective use of the system. Please read the contents of each section for detailed information and successful completion of tasks."},{"s":"panel","t":"Panel","h":["Panel"],"x":"Panel To launch the admin panel, you need to enter the platform address in the address bar. As a result, the start page with the authorization form will be displayed on the screen. The login and password are issued by the company manager. Number of objects in the system. Object statuses. Name of organizations. Balance. Tariff. Exit button. Tab bar - designed for working with contracts, objects, reports, recalculations and other actions. Work area - displays a graphical interface with objects. Personal user settings - includes a number of steps that allow you to personalize your data. • Add a photo • Change name • Change password"},{"s":"account","t":"Account","h":["Account"],"x":"Account The contract is the account that is intended for work in the user interface. The process of creating an account usually includes entering personal data, the name of the contract, choosing the type of organization, the type of account, the tariff and other parameters. •Add contract + button. Contract export button - a button for downloading contracts in excel format. Search filters - a tool that allows users to search by specified criteria. ​​The main working window consists of: a unique contract ID number, name, organization type, account type, contract creation date, number of objects and other fields). For ease of working with contracts in the system, it is possible to display or hide columns in the settings header.  The report download for the Report Builder will apply to the selected contracts and to all users associated with those contracts. Add report and save. Watch the video to learn how to create a client account."},{"s":"account-menu","t":"Account menu","h":["Account menu"],"x":"Account menu After creating a contract, you can right-click on it to open the context menu with available actions. Main account actions: Users – opens a window with the list of all users linked to the account Edit – allows you to modify or add contract details Vehicles – opens the tab with vehicles assigned to the account Video devices – displays the list of video devices connected to the account Audit & Audit History – lets you review detailed user actions related to the contract and its assets Reports list – tab for enabling reports in the account; alternative names can be applied Vehicles Excel Report – download the vehicle list in CSV format Email templates – configure notifications for clients (e.g., 7 or 14 days before balance expiration) Email Template Editor – add and save emails for topics like account lock, password change, email registration, etc. Payment reminder – set up email templates for monthly overdue payments Postpaid (Light) message – configure messages about the contract’s block period Finance – view payment and deduction history, add new transactions Bloking – manage object locks Delete – remove the account from the system Clone – create a copy of the contract without vehicles or notifications"},{"s":"users-2","t":"Users","h":["Users"],"x":"Users The administrator can see all users of the agreement. It is possible to navigate to user interfaces from under any user using the OneGPS button or by double-clicking on the line with the user. When a new contract is entered into the system, one new user of the system is automatically created with administrator rights. Link to video new user of the system"},{"s":"edit-1","t":"Edit","h":["Edit"],"x":"Edit The contract editing window contains tabs to navigate to various sections of the contract for additional settings."},{"s":"audit-and-audit-history","t":"Audit and Audit history","h":["Audit and Audit history"],"x":"Audit and Audit history Allows you to view or find any information about changes in settings on the object. Audit history for a more detailed view."},{"s":"template","t":"Template","h":["Template"],"x":"Template This section works for legal contracts that work on an advance payment basis. By default, two types of letter templates have been added about the end of funds: after 7 or 14 days. In the template types, you can edit and change the text as you wish, or leave the default OneGPS templates."},{"s":"delete-1","t":"Delete","h":["Delete"],"x":"Delete You can delete a contract from the system only without objects.  If there are still objects on the contract, first remove the objects from the contract through the user interface, and then delete the contract through the OneGPS system administrator panel. In the admin panel, objects are not deleted."},{"s":"clone","t":"Clone","h":["Clone"],"x":"Clone You can create a new account based on an existing one by copying the necessary settings and data. How to clone an account: 1. Select the account you want to copy 2. Right-click on the account to open the context menu 3. Click Clone 4. Enter the settings for the new account 5. Click Save — a window will open to choose what to copy to the new account What can be copied: Sensor templates – all sensor settings and templates (fuel, temperature, doors, etc.) Individual account prices – personalized tariffs assigned to specific assets of the account Payment email templates – texts and settings of email notifications (invoices, reminders, etc.) Mapping users – which users are linked to which assets or clients Additional fields – all custom fields Users – rights and display settings of account users Reports from the report builder – custom reports created using the report builder Geofences – all zones associated with the account (base, warehouses, routes, etc.) 6. Select what to copy into the new account and click Save."},{"s":"edit-2","t":"Edit","h":["Edit"],"x":"Edit Click the Add + button on the Contracts tab. Fill in the contract windows (basic settings, modules). Save. Name - contract name. Email - contract email address (will be used when automatically creating a new user). Phone - contract phone number. Balance - current balance. Deposit - payment that can be made by the client as an advance payment for services rendered. IIN - individual identification number. KPP - registration code. Partner - the partner with whom the contract will be linked (select from the list). Currency - select a list of currencies. Tariff - the tariff plan for the contract (select from the list). Organization type - the type of organization concluding the contract (select from the list). Account type - select from the drop-down list*. Postpayment - payment is made after the service is provided. If the balance is limited, the client will receive notifications by email about the payment amount. Prepayment - payment made before the service is provided. If the balance is negative, the contract is blocked. Postpayment (light) - when selecting the account type, the \"Blocking limit\" field is added in which the payment amount is set when the automatic account blocking is triggered. 1C code - code for synchronization with the 1C program. Active - a checkbox for creating a contract in an active state. Warehouse account - this is an account in which blocked objects are stored without a subscription fee. A warehouse account has the same rights as a regular account. Such an account can be used for objects that are entered into the database in test mode and have not yet been put into operation on a paid basis. Only the administrator can transfer objects through the admin panel. Link to video Stock account You can only create one stock account with a Partner. User passwords - create a complex or simple password. Display the password in the user settings. Show user passwords - this action allows you to view passwords in the user's personal card. User passwords can be hidden or remain accessible. • Master password - the ability to send commands to devices using the master password  This tab is intended for prices for video devices.  The section with prices for video devices is available only if the Video and CMS modules are activated. A list of prices for the service is provided for each type of video device registered in the OneGPS system. Paid services allow administrators to provide modules on a paid basis. In order for a module to be paid, it must be activated in the settings, and then selected in the Services + tab. You can see some of the ways to configure a contract by following thelink. The description of modules and their purpose are described in theModules section. The module settings window is designed to make additional settings for the module's operation. This is a tool that allows users to add additional fields to work with vehicles, description of how to create fields is at thelink. The Driver Fields Editor is designed to add additional fields about the driver. The field types used are the same as for theVehicle."},{"s":"configuration","t":"Configuration","h":["Configuration"],"x":"Configuration Configuration is the process of adjusting system parameters to achieve optimal performance or to meet specific customer requirements and objectives. In order for new authorized vehicles to be automatically added to notifications, you must set this configuration in your personal account. This function is intended for mapping objects (i.e. for objects from other contracts).  ConfigurationConfirm adding vehicles to notifications=1  Configuration hide_device =1 Intended for the test period of the system.  ConfigurationPartner Grace Period (days)= 60 To display the address in the online tab, you should add the following configuration to the contract settings.  ConfigurationAddress in Online Tree=1 Provided that the partner has an account type - prepayment and the balance is less than or equal to zero (including the deposit). If the partner is in the system, then for 5 days, every 3 hours, a dialog box with a warning will appear in the admin panel. On the 5th day, if the partner is logged in, the session will be automatically terminated and the partner will be transferred to the login page. The partner will not be able to log in to the admin panel. Add notification window. After replenishing the balance, the partner logs into the system. Assign only one driver to the object when creating a new driver or updating an old one.  Configuration single_driver = true (for the account). When we create a driver or update an old record and associate a driver with a vehicle or if that particular vehicle already has an associated driver, it will automatically unassociate the previous driver first and then associate the new driver. In order to display video broadcast in a new tab, you need to enter the config in the contract with the value true, in the module settings. In order for the configuration to become inactive, you need to write false. Activate the CMS module. Go to the module settings tab. Add the entry open_in_new_tab When you click on the camera icon in the user interface, the broadcast opens in a new tab via the link. By clicking on the camera stash and selecting a date period, the history of recordings from surveillance cameras will appear.  ConfigurationTOTP Label = name Designed to change the company name in the icon when the client receives codes to log into the account.  ConfigurationOnly private prices = 1 Tariffs can be hidden using the configuration in the Partner settings. Configuration for this window, visibility in the contract settings. The configuration is designed to display the Analytics Panel when the interface is loaded.  ConfigurationMain Tab = dashboard The tab in the User Interface is displayed as the start tab.  Configuration = additional_fields for the fuel cards module in the account. By default, the columns are hidden and the user can display them in the transaction tab. Depending on the type of your fuel account, the fields may be different for configuring.  ConfigurationLow Ballance Emails = s@OneGPS-telematics.com(multiple addresses can be specified) This configuration is designed to select accounts for which you want to receive low balance notifications. Specify the required email addresses for notifications in the settings.  ConfigurationMaps = {\"default\": \"Google Sat\", \"hidden\": [\"OSM\"]} The configuration for a contract or partner allows you to manage the visibility of maps and set the default map (Yandex, Google, OSM).  Configuration Link to manual =https://doc.OneGPS-gps.ru The configuration allows users to upload their own training materials and user guides to the system. Uploaded documents will be available for viewing at any time, providing convenient access to instructions on OneGPS functions. Users can easily find the materials they need and use them to obtain information and assistance on various aspects of working with the system. Telegram Bot Configuration To add a quick access link to the Telegram bot in the user account, use the Telegram botconfiguration setting. In the Value field, enter the bot’s username, for example: @PilotGpsBot. After saving, a separate tab with a link to the Telegram Bot will appear in theAdditional settings menuof the user account."},{"s":"modules-2","t":"Modules","h":["Modules"],"x":"Modules System modules are individual components that together form a complex system, ensuring its functionality and interconnected operation. Each module performs specific tasks or functions, and they can be developed and updated independently of each other. Only the system administrator can manage activation via the admin panel. The list of modules in the agreement may change depending on the Partner's settings. CMS and Video- a component of the system that allows you to monitor the condition and behavior of the driver and passengers inside the car. The Driver Module-is a system component that is used to manage and track the work of drivers in the system. Contragents-is designed to manage information about counterparties, including clients, suppliers, partners and sales representatives. This module helps organize interaction with counterparties and improve the quality of service. Garmis a component of the system for tracking the location of a car and scanning its condition. Climate control-is a system of automatic regulation of climate conditions in a closed space, such as a car, office or living space. The main goal is to maintain comfortable conditions for people, including optimal levels of temperature, humidity and air quality. RFID tag module- the module is designed to control the reading of RFID tags, a narrowly focused module, created to control garbage collection (there is a tag on the tank, and a reader on the car). The analytics module (Panel)-is a system component that provides the ability to collect, process, and analyze data from monitoring objects in order to obtain valuable information about them. The engine immobilizer module-is a system component that is designed to prevent unauthorized access and starting of the engine. Geozone module- designed for territorial control of monitoring objects. Allows you to record the facts of the object's presence in a given zone, exceeding the permissible downtime in the zone and leaving the zone. Command module- a system component that includes a set of commands that can be sent by users to devices. Time control module- a system component designed to track, record and manage working hours. The Violation Monitoring- module is a system component that is used to monitor and detect violations with a set of pre-defined rules. The logistics module-is a system component that is designed to manage and optimize logistics operations within the framework of deliveries, warehousing and transportation of goods or services. The recalculation module- is a system component that is used to perform recalculations and data transformations in the system. Recalculation is possible for two types of Zones and Trips. Trailer module- is designed to monitor the status of trailers and semi-trailers attached to main vehicles. This module allows you to track the location, status and movement of trailers, as well as view the history of connections and disconnections from the vehicle. A relay module-is a system component that is used to transmit data from one source to another or to several other sources at once. It acts as an intermediary between the sender and the receiver of data. The service moduleis a system component that is designed to facilitate planning or reflecting the fact of a technical inspection of a vehicle. The event module-is a system component that is designed to track and record various events configured in the system. It plays a key role in monitoring objects and in analyzing user actions. Fuel card module- the choice of fuel depends on specific requirements, operating conditions and environmental factors. The notification moduleis a system component designed to send notifications to users about various events, changes or conditions. It provides prompt information to the user and allows for timely response to important events. The Flights moduleis a system component designed to automate and optimize the operation of bus routes, improve the quality of passenger service and ensure the efficiency of the transport fleet. The routes module-is a system component designed to control passenger flow. OneGPS Task Manager- is a module designed to monitor, manage and coordinate the execution of tasks or processes in the system. It provides users with the ability to monitor and manage current processes. Schedule- module, used to create, manage and optimize schedules for vehicles and drivers. Eco Driving-is a component of the transport management system designed to monitor, analyze and improve driving style. Tachograph module- designed to monitor and record data on the work and rest mode of drivers, speed and other important parameters related to the operation of vehicles. Tachographs play a key role in ensuring road safety and compliance with regulatory requirements. Avis Integration- additional elements for working with objects. Crash detected-a component of the transport management system designed to automatically detect road accidents involving vehicles. The module uses various sensors to monitor traffic parameters. Rent Сar- module designed to manage and automate vehicle rental processes. Garbage collection module- designed for planning, optimization and monitoring of garbage collection and removal operations. The Agro module (Trailers module)is designed to automate and optimize the management processes of agricultural machinery and resources in the agro-industrial complex. The equipment performance monitoring moduleis a system component designed to monitor the condition of various components and systems of vehicles. This module helps to identify faults, prevent breakdowns and optimize maintenance. The LogBook module-is a portal for drivers where they can track their trip statistics, economical driving data, fuel costs, toll costs and their ratings. Each driver can access the portal with only their own statistics, which are analyzed based on all trips of all cars they have used."},{"s":"vehicle-field-editor","t":"Vehicle Field Editor","h":["Vehicle Field Editor"],"x":"Vehicle Field Editor A field editor is a tool that allows users to modify or manage data fields. For example, in a text or table type, the field editor can be used to change the properties of a field, such as its size, the type of data it contains, or how it is displayed. View by settings in the user interface. When editing a field, you can change its type by selecting the desired one from the list provided.  Please note that when editing a field, the text for the name property must be entered only in lowercase Latin letters. For example, if the field name (fieldLabel) has the value \"fieldLabel\":\"MVA Number\", then in name you can write this: \"name\":\"mvanumber\".  If you need to make the field mandatory, then copy and paste \"allowBlank\":false: {\"fieldLabel\":\"MVA Number\",\"name\":\"mvanumber\",\"xtype\":\"numberfield\",\"allowBlank\":false}  Text field {\"fieldLabel\":\"AS400 Status\",\"name\":\"as400_status\",\"xtype\":\"textfield\"} Number field {\"fieldLabel\":\"MVA Number\",\"name \":\"mvanumber\",\"xtype\":\"numberfield\",\"allowBlank\":false} Combobox {\"fieldLabel\":\"Vehicle Colour\",\"name\":\"colour\",\"xtype\":\"combobox \",\"store\":[[\"0\",\"Unknown\"],[\"1\",\"White\"],[\"2\",\"Black\"]]} Date field {\"fieldLabel\":\" Vehicle Installation date\",\"name\":\"vehinsdate\",\"xtype\":\"datefield\",\"format\":\"d.m.Y\"} Checkbox{\"fieldLabel\":\"Example text\",\"name\":\"exampletext\",\"xtype\":\"checkbox\"} fieldLabel - field name; name - form element identifier; xtype - field type; allowBlank - required field; format - date format; store - list elements, where \"1\" is the element identifier in the list, \"White\" is the element name in the list;"},{"s":"vehicles-2","t":"Vehicles","h":["Vehicles"],"x":"Vehicles Agent ID - the identifier of the monitoring object. Name - the name of the monitoring object in the system. Folder - the folder of the monitoring object in the system. Data volume - the volume of data. Creation date - the date of creation of the monitoring object. Type - the type of the installed unit. Tariff - the tariff plan and the price per month. Partner tariff - the tariff plan for the partner. Unique - the IMEI unit. Msisdn - the SIM card number on the device. Agreement - the number of the agreement in the system to which the monitoring object belongs. Owner - the name of the agreement to which the monitoring object belongs. Partner - the partner to whom the monitoring object belongs. Himself - blocking the monitoring object by the user. Administrator - blocking the monitoring object by the administrator. Last update - the date of the last object of the monitoring point. Online - the current state of the monitoring object."},{"s":"edit-3","t":"Edit","h":["Edit"],"x":"Edit The tab is used to enter various parameters for the vehicle. From this editor, you can transfer cars from one contract to another with all the entered parameters by selecting the Account field. Object Configuration Window"},{"s":"service-history","t":"Service history","h":["Service history"],"x":"Service history This section is intended to provide information about the completed service task."},{"s":"commands-2","t":"Commands","h":["Commands"],"x":"Commands SMS command sending function, you can view the history of sending commands."},{"s":"relaying-1","t":"Relaying","h":["Relaying"],"x":"Relaying 1. Select the monitoring object. 2. Select \"Relay\" from the context menu. Host - host address for data retransmission via the specified protocol (as an IP address or domain name). Port - port to which data is retransmitted via the specified protocol. Format - retransmission format (select from the drop-down list). External id - retransmission identifier of the monitoring object (usually the imei block is used, the last digits of the imei). Path - When using a domain name, this field contains additional parameters. USPW - If identification is required, this field will contain the username and password. Time zone - time zone in GMT format from the drop-down list. Speed ​​limit - field for specifying the maximum data transfer rate. Enabled - button for enabling retransmission. Json config - additional configuration for setting up retransmission in json format (for example, to display parameters for different sensors). Comment - additional information about retransmission.  { \"tags_rewrite\": { \"temperature\": \"galileo_temp_0\", //replace the name of the \"temperature\" field from the raw data, \"digital\": \"galileo_in_2\" //with the value \"galileo_temp_0\", }, //and send to retransmission \"save_data_for_report\": \"1\", //save the retransmission logs for the report \"temperature_data_generator\": { //by the state of the door sensor \"DOS\" =0 \"DOS\": \"galileo_in_2\", //generate a random value from 4 to 8 for the \"temperature_sensors\" field. \"closed_value\": 0, //implemented for a specific request \"temperature_sensors\": [ \"galileo_temp_0\" ] } } { \"params\": { \"Vsourse\": { //replace the name of the \"Vsourse\" field from the raw data \"name\": \"pwr_ext\", //with the value \"pwr_ext\", and send via relay \"type\": \"int\" } } } GPS API service that allows receiving coordinates and telemetry data from the physical devices in the GPS hub of CamionGO. Port: 80 Add the authorization token to the Json_config field auth_key: { \"auth_key\": \"AAA-BBB-CCC-DDD\" } Date and time of the event in UTC format. Latitude. Longitude. Vehicle license plate. Vehicle speed. Direction of movement. If sensors with the following names are present, they are also included in the data transfer packet: Battery - battery level, if available. Temperature - temperature, if available. Humidity - humidity, if available. Select multiple objects in the Machines tab. Click the \"Relay\" button. Add parameters. Enter one IMEI and after adding in the user interface change this parameter in each object to the valid one."},{"s":"users-3","t":"Users","h":["Users"],"x":"Users Window with a list of available users for this monitoring object. Double click to go to User Interface or click the OneGPS button."},{"s":"video-devices-1","t":"Video devices","h":["Video devices"],"x":"Video devices View the list and add a new device to the object."},{"s":"raw-data","t":"Raw data","h":["Raw data"],"x":"Raw data This is non-processed data that comes from the server on the platform at a given frequency. Data can be selected by the agent's vehicle ID at the top of the screen. Data can be selected for up to two days. Points are allowed to be recalculated and maintained. Use the graph to display fields from sensors."},{"s":"audit-1","t":"Audit","h":["Audit"],"x":"Audit Function for specific viewing of audit for a given object."},{"s":"audit-history-1","t":"Audit history","h":["Audit history"],"x":"Audit history Tab for more detailed viewing."},{"s":"sensors-3","t":"Sensors","h":["Sensors"],"x":"Sensors Options to apply templates, as well as import and export are available. Save template"},{"s":"data-volume","t":"Data volume","h":["Data volume"],"x":"Data volume The window contains data on Internet traffic consumption. Available as a table or graph."},{"s":"teltonika-fota-","t":"Teltonika FOTA","h":["Teltonika FOTA"],"x":"Teltonika FOTA  Name Device model Series Configuration Last connection and update Product code The application tab allows you to upload files. Upload files for this service include firmware updates and configuration files that can be downloaded and applied to devices remotely.  To access the download files and use the Teltonika FOTA service, you will need to register and set up an account on the official Teltonika website. Watch the video to learn how to activate and integrate Teltonika FOTA WEB in OneGPS."},{"s":"teltonika-fota-connection-to-the-system","t":"Teltonika FOTA. Connection to the system","h":["Teltonika FOTA. Connection to the system"],"x":"Teltonika FOTA. Connection to the system The system has implemented integration with Teltonika's FOTA. This integration allows you to configure Teltonika devices from the OneGPS system interface. This function can be activated both for a partner and for a separate account (contract). To remotely manage the configuration of Teltonika devices from the OneGPS platform, you must activate the integration. To do this, you must complete the following steps.  1. Log in to your Teltonika FOTA WEB account (https://fm.teltonika.lt) 2. Generate and copy the API Key. 3. Enter any name for the API Token and click the Create button. 4. Enter a custom name for the API Token and click the Create button. 5. Copy the generated token value. Open your account settings in the admin panel and add a new entry there Open the \"Configuration\" section and add the fotaTokens key. The fotaTokens key format and value: {\"<Name>\":\"<Key>\",\"<Name1>\":\"<Key1>\",\"<NameN>\":\"<KeyN>\"} <Name> - any name <Key> - API Key received in the Teltonika FOTA interface You can specify more than one key separated by commas."},{"s":"finance","t":"Finance","h":["Finance"],"x":"Finance The tab is used to monitor existing payments and add new ones. ID - single payment number. External ID - external identification of the payment. Agreement - agreement number under which the payment was made. Amount - payment amount. Currency - currency in which the payment was made. Type - payment type. Description - additional description for the payment. Date - payment creation date. Receipt date - date of receipt of funds for payment. Organization - name of the organization that made the payment. Partner - the person to whom the client belongs. Subpartner - the subpartner to whom the client belongs. (may be empty). Click add + for a new payment. Window for issuing a new payment to the Client. You can check the daily write-off for services by selecting the date filter. Click + add, for a new manual write-off. Window for entering manual write-off. Click + add, new tariff for services. Specify a partner. You can add your price list Add new price"},{"s":"partners","t":"Partners","h":["Partners"],"x":"Partners A Partner is an integrator or company that uses the OneGPS platform to work with its own clients. The partner manages its customer base within the system and is responsible for the connected objects. In this section of the Admin panel, you can: create and edit partner agreements assign pricing plans monitor the balance view the number of contracts and connected objects"},{"s":"how-to-add-a-partner","t":"How to add a partner","h":["How to add a partner","General Information","Access and assignment settings","Financial and pricing parameters","Login credentials"],"x":"How to add a partner In the Partners section, you can create a new partner account. 1. Click the Add button in the Partners section 2. In the window that opens, go to the Main settings tab 3. Fill in the required partner agreement details: General Information Name — the agreement or company name (as it will appear in the system) Email — partner’s email address Phone — contact phone number Deposit — advance payment amount (if applicable) IIN — Individual Identification Number KPP — company registration code VAT — specify the percentage 1C Code — code for synchronization with the 1C accounting system (if integration is used) Region — select from the list Access and assignment settings Block Partner — completely blocks the partner’s access to the system Block users of accounts partner— blocks login for all users created under this partner account Partner — the main partner to which the agreement will be assigned (used in sub-partner structures) Financial and pricing parameters Currency — main billing currency Additional currencies — select additional currencies if needed Price — pricing plan applied to the agreement. Choose the price list that will be used for objects. The price can be assigned to a specific account or to a partner (in this case, it applies to all their accounts). The tariff applies only to new objects. Existing vehicles keep their previously assigned pricing plans. Partner price— additional tariff within the partner program Organization type — select the legal form from the list Account type — choose the billing model from the dropdown list Login credentials Password o for Users — select password complexity level: simple or complex Show user password — choose whether passwords are visible or hidden Login — enter the login used to access the partner’s Admin panel (required field) Password — administrator account password 4. After completing all required fields, click Save. Once saved, you will be able to assign contracts to the partner, connect objects, and create users."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-the-partners-list","t":"How to work with the partners list","h":["How to work with the partners list","Partners table","Customizing the table"],"x":"How to work with the partners list In the Partners section, you can see all created partner agreements. Here, you can quickly find a partner, check their status, and manage their data. Available actions (Top of the page) Add — create a new partner agreement Export — download the partners list as a file Search — quickly find a partner by name, login, phone number, or other details Show deleted — display archived records Clear filters — reset search and filter settings Partners table Each row represents a separate partner agreement. The table includes the following columns: ID — unique agreement number in the system Name — internal agreement name Tariff — pricing plan used for billing Balance — current financial status (positive, zero, or outstanding debt) Email and Phone — contact details Login — partner’s login credentials Number of accounts — total number of agreements under the partner Number of vehicles — number of connected vehicles/assets Partner — shows the main partner if this is a sub-partner Comment — internal notes or cooperation terms Customizing the table You can adjust the table view to match your workflow. Choose which columns to display, and configure sorting and filters. 1. Click the arrow in the header of any column. A settings menu will appear with the following options: Sort ascending or descending Filter by the selected column Select which columns to display 2. To choose visible columns: Click Columns Select the required parameters — the table will update automatically"},{"s":"partner-menu","t":"Partner menu","h":["Partner menu","How to open the menu","Main menu actions","Edit","Accounts","Video devices","Payments","Write-offs","Add payment","Add write-off","Payment reminder","Postpaid (light) message","Prepaid message","Delete"],"x":"Partner menu How to open the menu 1. Go to the Partners tab 2. Find the required partner in the list 3. Right-click on the partner’s row — the action menu will open The available options depend on the user’s permissions. Administrators see the full set of functions. Partners only see the actions available to them. Unavailable options are displayed in gray. Main menu actions Edit Opens the partner card to modify key information: name, contact details, tariff, currency, administrator account, and other settings. Available to administrators only. Accounts Opens the list of this partner’s Accounts. Here you can review active and completed contracts, see connected vehicles, and check applied tariffs. Video devices Opens the list of connected cameras and DVRs assigned to the partner. Payments Opens the payment history in a new tab. You can review payment dates, amounts, and descriptions. Write-offs Displays the history of deductions from the partner’s balance (subscription fees, services, and other charges). Add payment Manually credit funds to the partner’s balance. 1. Fill in the form: Contract — select the contract the payment applies to External Payment ID — payment reference from your accounting system Invoice Number (optional) — specify if the payment relates to a specific invoice Payment Type — select from the list Amount — enter the amount to be credited Payment Date — actual date funds were received Description — short comment 2. Click Save — the amount will be added to the partner’s balance and recorded in the payment history. Add write-off Manually deduct funds from the partner’s balance. 1. Fill in the form: Contract — select the contract External Write-off ID — transaction or document number from your accounting system Quantity — number of service units Write-off Type — select from the list Amount — amount to deduct from the partner’s balance Write-off Date — service date or adjustment date Description — short comment 2. After verifying the data, click Save — the amount will be deducted and reflected in the transaction history. Login in Log in as the partner (using their account), for example, to check settings or provide assistance. Payment reminder You can create and send a notification to the partner about an upcoming or overdue payment. The message includes: outstanding amount contract number After applying the template, the partner will see a notification such as: “You need to pay the outstanding subscription fee in the amount of … Otherwise, all objects will be disconnected from monitoring starting from the 1st day of the next month.” Postpaid (light) message Postpaid settings are configured at the account level. You can: select the contract specify the amount due set the payment deadline define the possible suspension date/time enter the notification text The message reminds the partner about payment and warns about possible service restrictions. Prepaid message You can send a notification to a prepaid partner, for example, reminding them to top up their balance. If the balance becomes negative: 5 days before the estimated zero balance date, a warning will appear upon login to the admin panel the calculation is based on the average daily write-offs after 5 days, access to the admin panel is blocked until the balance becomes positive again If a blocking calculation error appears This means: 1. A vehicle has a tariff in a different currency. The system cannot correctly calculate the blocking date if write-offs are in different currencies. Solution: Log in as an administrator, open the corresponding AgentID, and assign the correct pricing plan. 2. The contract contains incorrect tariffs. To fix this: 1. Open Accounts and right-click to open the context menu 2. Select Finance → Incorrect price 3. Select the required objects and update pricing in bulk Delete Remove the partner from the system (available to administrators only)."},{"s":"email-sending-configuration-smtp-","t":"Email sending configuration (SMTP)","h":["Email sending configuration (SMTP)","How to configure SMTP","Configuration parameters","SMTP server test","Error handling"],"x":"Email sending configuration (SMTP) How to configure SMTP 1. In the Admin panel, go to the Partners section 2. Double-click the selected partner — the settings window will open 3. Select the SMTPtab The SMTP tab is available for partners only. This tab is used to configure email sending through an external SMTP server. Here you specify the mail server parameters that the system will use to send notifications, invoices, and other emails. Configuration parameters SMTP Host — SMTP server address SMTP Port — SMTP server port SMTP Login — username for mail server authentication SMTP Password — password for authentication SMTP Email — email address from which messages will be sent SMTP Security — connection type (No encryption, SSL, or TLS — depending on the server configuration) Please carefully verify all entered data — email delivery depends on the correctness of these settings. SMTP server test After entering the parameters, you can test the connection. The system uses the SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) to test connectivity and authentication. The test process works as follows: 1. The system retrieves the SMTP server address from the request and verifies that it is specified. 2. It retrieves the connection port. If the port is not specified, the default port 25 is used. 3. An SMTP client instance is created, and the connection timeout and debug level are configured. 4. The system attempts to connect to the server: if SSL is selected, the ssl:// prefix is automatically added to the server address when connecting via SSL, certificate verification is temporarily disabled 5. After a successful connection, the system sends the EHLO command (extended SMTP greeting). 6. The system retrieves the list of supported ESMTP extensions from the server. 7. If TLS mode is selected and the server supports STARTTLS, the system: initiates a secure connection sends the EHLO command again 8. If the server supports authentication, the system attempts to log in using the specified username and password. Error handling If an error occurs at any stage: the information is recorded in the error log HTTP status code 400 is returned an error message is returned in JSON format Regardless of the test result, the connection is properly closed using the QUIT command. At the end of the process, the function returns the connection parameters and the test result. This procedure ensures that the server is reachable, supports the selected encryption type, and accepts the provided credentials. If the test fails, we recommend rechecking the server address, port, security type, and login credentials."},{"s":"how-to-add-a-configuration-to-a-partner","t":"How to add a configuration to a partner","h":["How to add a configuration to a partner","Steps to add a configuration","Configuration list","Partner configurations","Available partner configurations"],"x":"How to add a configuration to a partner A configuration is a set of system parameters and settings that customize platform behavior for specific business needs. Steps to add a configuration 1. In theAdmin panel, go to thePartners section 2. Double-click the selected partner — the settings window will open 3. Open the Configuration tab and click Add 4. In the dropdown list, select the required configuration and specify its value 5. ClickUpdate, then save the changes in the Configuration tab 1 — Exporting and importing a configuration template 2 — Configuration list You can use the action icons to edit or delete an existing configuration. Use the Export button to save the current configuration settings to a JSON file. To apply previously saved settings to another agreement, click Import and select the saved JSON file. Configuration list To view the full list of available configurations, click Configuration list. The list contains all configurations that can be added in the system. Partner configurations The system provides a predefined list of available configurations that can be assigned to a partner. Each configuration includes: Name — the display name of the setting Key (Value) — the technical parameter name used internally by the system If a partner requires specific business logic (for example, SMS provider integration, custom balance display, integration with Helpdesk, Twilio, HERE Technologies, etc.), the corresponding configuration must be added with the appropriate value. Examples To display balance in days instead of currency: balance_in_days = 1 To connect a Telegram bot, specify the bot address (for example): @PilotGpsBot To configure SMTP or an SMS provider, enter authentication parameters as an object (login, password, API key, etc.). Available partner configurations Name Value Description Support and Helpdesk Support Url support_url URL used to access technical support from the user account. When clicking the “Support” icon, the user is redirected here. Can be: • a regular support website link • or mailto:support@domain.com (opens an email draft immediately) support_link support_link Direct link to a support/helpdesk portal, for example:https://example-support.com/login okdesk okdesk When clicking the “? (Help)” button in OneGPS, the user is redirected to the Okdesk support portal (https://help.iglit.ru/) okdesk_link okdesk_link Direct link to the Okdesk support portal okdesk_key okdesk_key Okdesk integration key helpdeskeddy helpdeskeddy Enables integration with the Helpdeskeddy support system helpdeskeddy_link helpdeskeddy_link Link to the Helpdeskeddy portal helpdeskeddy_key helpdeskeddy_key Helpdeskeddy API key Interface Main Tab main_tab Set the “Main” tab as default Address in Online Tree adress_in_tree Adds an “Address” column in the object tree on the “Online” tab. May slightly reduce performance with many objects edit_car_model edit_car_model Value 1 allows the partner to edit vehicle brands and models Subpartner price partner_price Price paid by the partner to Sky Electronics for services model_and_mark_in_reports model_and_mark_in_reports Adds vehicle make and model to selected reports (for Koltsovo) Language OneGPS edit languages_pilot_edit Enables interface language management in OneGPS. Editable languages are listed comma-separated (e.g., ru,en) Root column in accounts root Adds a “Root” column in the contracts section Additional languages languages List of additional interface languages, separated by commas Font settings font_settings Setting the font for the partner. This is an object where the key is the language and the value is the font parameters. Example: { \"ru\": { \"url\": \"https://fonts.gstatic.com/s/lobster/v22/neILzCirqoswsqX9zo-mM5Ez.woff2\", \"font-family\": \"Lobster\" } } SMS and communications smsgetway24 smsgetway24 Integration settings forhttps://smsgateway24.com Example: { \"login\": \"...\", \"password\": \"...\", \"device_id\": \"...\" } twilio twilio Integration with Twilio SMS service: { \"sid\": \"sid\", \"token\": \"token\" } TNF SMS Provider tnf_sms_provider Enables TNF provider and “TNF SMS SIM Cards” menu Sms provider smsprovider Select SMS provider for the partner Etisalat SMS Provider etisalat_sms_provider SMS settings for Etisalat: { \"username\": \"demo\", \"api_key\": \"generated in api site\", \"account_id\": \"copy from profile in api site\" } Emnify SIM Provider emmnify_sms_provider SIM card settings for Emnify (https://portal.emnify.com): {\"application_token\": \"token\"} Endless sms endless_sms Enables unlimited SMS mode. If value = 1: • SMS balance may drop to -1 • After reaching -1, no further deductions are made email_send_invoices_settings email_send_invoices_settings Email invoice sending settings unsubscribe_url unsubscribe_url Link for unsubscribing from email notifications sim_mts sim_mts MTS SIM account settings Opsos list Example: [ {\"name\": \"Beeline\"}, {\"name\": \"t2\"} ] Configures the list of SIM operators available for selection in the Operator (SIM provider) field in the vehicle card. The list is defined at the server level and displayed in the interface as a dropdown menu Links Field (links_field_sms_provider) Example: { \"access_key\": \"demo_access_key_1234567890abcdef\", \"secret_key\": \"demo_secret_key_abcdef1234567890\" } Integration with a SIM card provider (SMS provider) via Links Field. In this configuration, you must specify the provider’s API credentials: access_key — public access key for the service secret_key — secret key used to authorize API requests For the integration to work correctly, each vehicle must have the ICCID field заполнено. ICCID is the unique identifier of the SIM card. Telegram bot Example: @PilotGpsBot Adds quick access to a Telegram bot from the user account LBS Config { \"lbs_api_keys\": { \"yandex\": \"74c80fe0-176e-485f-8b3e-3b7...\" } } Selects the LBS provider used to determine the device location. LBS (Location-Based Service) is a technology for determining a device's location without GPS, using data from nearby cellular base stations (GSM/LTE). It is useful for beacon devices and trackers without GPS, as well as a backup option when the GPS signal is unavailable. In the key value, specify: lbs_api_keys — a general block for the API keys of all supported LBS providers. yandex — the API key for Yandex (location detection by base stations via the Yandex service). Memory and performance Low Memory lmem Enables optimization mode for handling large fleets queue_max_items queue_max_items Limits the number of items processed from the queue at one time Security and authorization Auth Token auth_token Token used for password reset 2FA_Email 2FA_email Enables two-factor authentication via email password_policy password_policy Password complexity policy: • true (1) — enabled • false (0) — disabled TOTP Label totp_label The setting specifies the name (signature) that will be displayed in the two-factor authentication application when adding an account. The user will see this line in their application (for example, in Google Authenticator or Microsoft Authenticator) next to the verification codes. Single Driver single_driver Allows only one active driver per vehicle Block partner timestamp_block_partner Timestamp from which the partner is considered blocked is_block_vehicles_stock_account is_block_vehicles_stock_account Block vehicles Maps and geocoding HERE API Key here_api_key API key for HERE Technologies maps StadiaMaps stadiamaps Enables StadiaMaps integration Geocoder geocoder Geocoding service configuration Web Socket ws Enables WebSocket connections pilot_map_url pilot_map_url If set, coordinate links from emails open on the OneGPS map yandex_route_apikey yandex_route_apikey API key yandex_route_mode yandex_route_mode Route transportation mode yandex_route_avoid_tolls yandex_route_avoid_tolls Avoid toll roads yandex_route_avg_weight yandex_route_avg_weight Average vehicle weight (tons) yandex_route_max_weight yandex_route_max_weight Maximum vehicle weight (tons) windy_api_key Example value: 4epwZKT8fdOIe6Boq2gbhdfs50ky API key for integration with Windy.com. It is used to connect the weather service and display weather data in the system. After adding a valid API key: — the Weather checkbox will appear in themap layersettings — weather model selection will become available (depending on the subscription tier) — weather overlays (wind, temperature, pressure, etc.) can be enabled on the map Pricing hide_subpartner_price hide_subpartner_price Hides subpartner price columns Hide main partner prices hide_main_partner_prices Hides main partner prices from subpartners Only private prices only_private_prices Enables private pricing mode Hide / Show elements hide_device hide_device Hides technical device data in the object card (IMEI, SIM, etc.) Hide_fields Hide_fields Hides selected object card fields (comma-separated list, e.g., vin,engine,year,color) List of fields that can be hidden: name, uniqid, additional_imei, car_mark, car_model, car_mod, file, msisdn, iccid, opsos1, msisdn2, opsos2, color, type, ico, tags[], conf, parent, template, time_zone, initial_mileage, initial_motor_hours, time_shift, info, rfid, card_num, terminal_id, model, request_fines, gibdd_car_owner, gibdd_car_number, gibdd_region, auto_gibdd_id, lat, lon, un, garmin, id, agent_id, year, vin, engine, engine_cap, engine_horse, full_capacity, seating_capacity, choose_lic_type, license, lic_exp, ins_sert, ins_exp, driver_name, driver_phone, speed_parameter, speed_formula, speed_type, minspeed, mintime, minparking, maxflylength, cons_ignition, fix_speed, can_mileage, split, split_buslines, minsat, maxhdop, maxspeed, formula, speed_limit, lost_connection_time, minimum_idle, minimum_idle_cons_ignition, zones, zones_idle, zones_control, zones_sos, zones_speedlimit, zones_photo, recalc_zones, summer_fuel, winter_fuel, idle_fuel, mh_fuel, summer_start, winter_start, fuel_type, fuel_norm_type, fuel_price, fuel_tank_capacity, summer_gas, winter_gas, idle_gas, eco_zone_fine, eco_driver_fine, eco_speeding_fine, eco hide_finances hide_finances Hides financial data HideReportsFromPartner Example value: 2,77 This configuration is used to hide reports from a partner. It limits the list of reports that the partner can view and assign to accounts. The configuration value contains the report IDs that should be hidden. To hide multiple reports, list their IDs separated by commas. After the configuration is applied, these reports are not displayed to the partner in the reports list and cannot be added by this partner to new accounts. The restriction applies to the partner and their direct child accounts. It does not apply to the super administrator. If a hidden report has already been enabled in an existing account, it must be disabled manually. If a report ID is removed from the HideReportsFromPartner configuration, the previously disabled report will not be enabled automatically. It must be enabled manually. Object settings Add object idle send alert setting add_vehicle_send_alert_idle Add idle alert Add object idle setting add_vehicle_idle Add idle tracking Add object speed limit setting add_vehicle_speed_limit Add speed limit setting Add object speed limit send alert setting add_vehicle_send_alert_speed Add speed alert Add object connection lost send alert setting add_vehicle_send_alert_lost Add connection loss alert Add object connection lost setting add_vehicle_connection_lost Add connection loss feature Event max speed event_max_speed Add max speed event filter Add object consider ignition setting add_vehicle_consider_ign_1 Add ignition tracking Add object idle consider ignition setting add_vehicle_consider_ign_idle Add ignition idle alert Add object fix speed setting add_vehicle_fix_speed Speed correction for tracks Add object max points dist setting add_vehicle_max_points Max distance between track points Add object mileage by can setting add_vehicle_can_mileage Mileage via CAN bus Add object min parking time setting add_vehicle_min_parking_time Minimum parking time Add object min speed setting add_vehicle_min_speed Minimum trip registration speed Add object min tracks split time setting add_vehicle_min_tracks_time Minimum time between trips Minimum number of satellites add_vehicle_minsat Minimum satellites Max HDOP add_vehicle_maxhdop Maximum HDOP obj_types_for_task obj_types_for_task Allowed object type IDs for tasks veh_types_for_task veh_types_for_task Allowed vehicle type IDs (comma-separated) edit_car_models edit_car_models Edit vehicle models Accounts and users Contract API auto create new contract contract_api_auto_create_new If = 1, active contract auto-closes when creating a new one new_user_settings new_user_settings New user settings map_new_accounts map_new_accounts Automatically link new contracts to selected user IDs Default user parameters {\"max_days_available_history\": 730} Sets the maximum history period available to a user for viewing data and generating reports. After adding the configuration, specify the required number of days on theSettingstab of the user card. For example, a value of 730 provides access to two years of historical data. Other settings Time control module Activates time control module Tires tires Temporary tires configuration Fota Tokens fotaTokens Tokens for Teltonika FOTA fine_api fine_api Integration parameters for fines service SFDA SFDA Enables additional logic for warehouse companies in WASL module Wasl Price wasl_price Pricing for WASL module Max Day Recalc max_day_recalc Maximum allowed recalculation period (days) Confirm adding vehicles to notifications confirm_adding_vehicles_to_notifs Shows confirmation popup when adding vehicles to notifications Currencies GHS Currency Default language eng Default language Partner Grace Period (days) grace_period Grace period in days min_speed_limit_check min_speed_limit_check Minimum speed threshold check Modules list mod_list List of enabled module IDs auto_notifications auto_notifications Automatic notifications configuration only_tags_model only_tags_model Restricts work to models linked to tags Velavu [ { \"broker\": \"iot.velavu.com\", \"port\": \"8883\", \"customer_id\": \"YOUR_CUSTOMER_ID\", \"api_token\": \"YOUR_API_TOKEN\", \"cert\": \"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\\nYOUR_CERTIFICATE\\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----\", \"key\": \"-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\nYOUR_PRIVATE_KEY\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\" } ] Configuration of the integration with the Velavu indoor positioning platform. It allows loading Velavu sites, buildings, floors, devices, and geofences into the Climate module. The value field contains the JSON connection configuration. It can be obtained from Velavu or generated via the OneGPS endpoint: <server_address>/backend/ax/generate_velavu_config.php?api_key=<velavu_api_key> Where <server_address> is the address of the OneGPS server where the configuration is generated, and <velavu_api_key> is a valid Velavu API key. After opening the link, the system downloads a JSON file. Copy the contents of the file and paste them into the Velavu configuration in the admin panel. bi_analytics 1 Enables access to the BI Analytics API for the partner account. To activate this setting, set the value to 1"},{"s":"users-4","t":"Users","h":["Users"],"x":"Users The tab is designed to create new administrators and edit existing ones. The partner can create two types: admin user and mechanic (for access in the admin panel). ID - a unique user number in the system. Name - user name. Full name - full user name in the system. Last login - time and date of visit. Updated - update status in the system. Created - time and date of creation. Add new users."},{"s":"reports-2","t":"Reports","h":["Reports"],"x":"Reports The tab is used to build different types of reports. Select a report Set a period. Select an organization. Save a report."},{"s":"video-devices-2","t":"Video devices","h":["Video devices"],"x":"Video devices CCTV cameras are installed to provide security and monitoring. They include both wired and wireless models. Add new. Tariffing is configured from the list in the Main tab. Configuration"},{"s":"mechanic","t":"Mechanic","h":["Mechanic"],"x":"Mechanic The tab is used to monitor existing settings and add new machines. Rights with the Mechanic type have limited actions in the system. The mechanic can only create an object and make settings. ID - a unique number in the system. Device ID - a unique id number in the system. SIM - the SIM number linked to the object. Machine number - the name of the machine in the system. Contract - the contract number in the system and the window for viewing the machine settings. Client - the name of the contract."},{"s":"qr-code","t":"QR code","h":["QR code"],"x":"QR code The QR codes tab is used to register equipment sets in the monitoring system. id - unique number in the system. Agent id- unique equipment number. •IMEI- have a set of equipment. Created- time and date of entry into the database. Account name - the name of the contract in the system. Account ID - unique number of the contract. Activated - time and date of activation. Active - equipment operation status. Download A zip archive is loaded in which pictures (QR codes) of cars are loaded. The link to OneGPS is encrypted in the QR code. Add Search field Clear all filters Page navigation bar The number of objects displayed on the page, and the total number"},{"s":"add-qr","t":"Add QR","h":["Add QR"],"x":"Add QR Go to the QR codes section and create cars, select the parent account. QR code generation window. After we have created one or more machines, we return to the previous screen. Sort by id DESC. Select the machines (highlighted in gray), and click the download button. Follow the link. After entering the necessary data, wait until the SMS code arrives on the phone. Enter SMS code. And we get right into the OneGPS. In which we see our car. The machine is already under an account that was cloned on the parent. The parent account was selected at the stage of creating a QR code In the admin panel, we can also see these machines and the generated account. If the user already has an account in the system, and he needs to add an object to the existing account, then the user only needs to log in to the system after scanning the QR code. After you have created one or more machines, return to the previous screen. Sort by id DESC. We select our machines (highlighted in gray), and click the download button. A zip archive is loaded in which pictures (QR codes) of our cars are loaded. The link to the OneGPS is encrypted in the QR code."},{"s":"configuration-qr","t":"Configuration QR","h":["Configuration QR"],"x":"Configuration QR In order to use the trial or test period of the object on the OneGPS platform, when creating a QR code, i.e. opening an object, add the number of free days, where: - grace_period is the number of days, - grace_end_timestamp is the date format in unixstamp when the free period ends. The trial number of days must be specified in the field when creating the object and after setting it up on the platform, specify the period until which moment the billing for the car will not be calculated."},{"s":"fuel-3","t":"Fuel","h":["Fuel"],"x":"Fuel Click Add +. Fill in the fields. Add configuration."},{"s":"sim-cards","t":"SIM cards","h":["SIM cards"],"x":"SIM cards SIM cards in monitoring systems are used to provide communication between monitoring devices and the server. They play a key role in systems that require remote tracking and data transfer. SIM cards are used in GPS vehicle trackers to transmit real-time location data. This allows you to control routes, monitor schedule compliance, and respond to deviations from the route. SIM cards allow monitoring devices (e.g. GPS trackers, sensors) to connect to mobile networks to transmit location, status, and other parameters to the monitoring server. This is especially important for devices that are on the move or in remote locations without access to Wi-Fi or wired networks."},{"s":"etisalat","t":"Etisalat","h":["Etisalat"],"x":"Etisalat Etisalat SIM is SIM cards and related services provided by Etisalat, one of the largest telecommunications operators in the Middle East, Africa and Asia. Jasper Platform, M2M SIM card control and management platform. Allows you to use various settings and get a full history of connections between equipment in real time. Data is provided both digitally and graphically. This is a single control center, accessible remotely and at any convenient time. Recall that M2M (machine-to-machine) is a solution designed to ensure wireless data exchange between various devices (ATMs, terminals, security systems, sensors, etc.) and a single control center. In order to set up the integration, you need to enter the setting in the partner form in the configuration.  Configuration Go to the SIM cards section to manage SIM cards. SIM card management window. All information that can be obtained from a SIM card. Go to the car. Managing SIM card status. You can double-click to change the SIM card status. username - login to etisalat admin api_key - generated here https://etisalat.jasperwireless.ae/provision/ui/help/help.html account_id - this is the account id in etisalat see here https://etisalat.jasperwireless.ae/provision/ui/admin/accts/acctDetails.html?acctId=100031607 You can send SMS of size < 320 characters. DEFAULT. The device cannot exceed the data limit. TEMPORARY_OVERRIDE. The device can use any amount of data until the end of the current billing cycle, after which the Control Center begins to enforce the data limit set in the rate plan. PERMANENT_OVERRIDE. The device can use any amount of data, regardless of the data limit defined in the rate plan. For additional information on these settings, please contact technical support."},{"s":"recalculate","t":"Recalculate","h":["Recalculate"],"x":"Recalculate The tab is used to manually recalculate data. Specify the agent for recalculation Select the Recalculation Type Specify the recalculation period Run Check the report or history"},{"s":"relaying-2","t":"Relaying","h":["Relaying","Relaying section tabs","Endpoints","Statistics","Objects","Key relay concepts","Endpoint","Object","Partner","Contract","Auto add (Automatic object assignment)","External ID","Uspw","Access rights","Administrator","User (Partner)"],"x":"Relaying The Relaying section is designed for transmitting monitoring object data to external systems. For example, data can be sent to: city platforms integration services monitoring systems such as Wialon servers using the EGTS protocol REST APIs other external systems that accept telematics data The relay process is based on the following principle: 1. Create a data transmission destination (endpoint) 2. Configure connection parameters for the external system 3. Connect one or more objects to this endpoint After that, the system will automatically start sending data from the selected objects to the external system. This approach allows you to configure the connection once and reuse it for any number of objects without re-entering server addresses, ports, authentication details, or other settings. Relaying section tabs The Relayingsection consists of three tabs: Endpoints Data transmission destinations to external systems. This is where connection settings are configured, such as server address, port, protocol, authentication data, and other parameters. Statistics Transmission statistics, error information, and delivery status. Here you can check for errors, view the number of sent and received packets, monitor queue status, and ensure that data is successfully delivered to the external system. Objects Objects connected to the relay. Any number of objects can be linked to a single endpoint. An object can be disconnected or reassigned to another endpoint if needed. Key relay concepts Below are the main terms used in this section. Endpoint A data transmission destination to an external system. An endpoint includes connection parameters such as: server address port data transfer protocol path (if required) authentication parameters additional settings One endpoint can be used by multiple objects. For example, if all objects must send data to a single Wialon account, you only need to create one endpoint and connect all required objects to it. Object A vehicle, device, or any other monitored entity whose data is transmitted via an endpoint. Each object can have its own relay settings, such as an external identifier or transmission schedule. Partner A partner agreement that owns the endpoint. Depending on access rights, a user may work only with their own endpoints or also view endpoints belonging to subordinate partners. Contract An end-user agreement that owns the monitoring object. Auto add (Automatic object assignment) This feature allows new objects from a selected contract to be automatically connected to an endpoint. For example, if all client objects must send data to the same external system, a rule can be configured so that every newly created object in the selected contract is automatically attached to the endpoint without additional manual setup. External ID An identifier of the object in the external system. By default, the IMEI of the device is typically used as the External ID, but it can be changed if needed. The external system uses this ID to recognize the object and match incoming data. Uspw Authentication data used for sending information to an external system. Depending on the protocol, this may include a login, password, token, or other access credentials. If additional authentication parameters are required, they are specified in the Json config field. Access rights Available data and actions depend on the user role in the admin panel. Administrator An administrator has full access to the section and can: view all endpoints manage all relay objects create, edit, and delete endpoints view overall relay statistics work with test endpoints User (Partner) A partner user can only see data related to their partner structure. Available actions: their own endpoints endpoints of subordinate partners objects within accessible contracts statistics for available endpoints A partner cannot view or modify data belonging to other partner structures."},{"s":"endpoints","t":"Endpoints","h":["Endpoints","What is an endpoint","Endpoints list","Top panel","Statistics period selection","Endpoints table columns","Endpoint actions","How to create an endpoint","How to edit an endpoint","Enable or disable an endpoint","How to delete an endpoint","Endpoint objects","Object list columns","How to add objects","How to edit an object","Automatic object assignment (Auto add)","How to configure Auto add"],"x":"Endpoints This article describes how to work with data transmission endpoints: from creating and configuring connections to attaching objects and automating the relay process. Here you will learn: What an endpoint is How to work with the endpoints list How to create endpoints How to connect objects to an endpoint How to configure automatic connection of new objects What is an endpoint An endpoint is a data transmission destination to an external system. It defines connection parameters such as server address, port, data transfer protocol, and authentication settings. After an endpoint is created, one or more objects can be connected to it. Endpoints list To open the endpoints list, go to Relaying→ Endpoints. This tab displays all available data transmission destinations and their current status. Here you can: create new endpoints edit existing endpoint settings connect objects configure automatic object assignment view transmission statistics enable, disable, or delete endpoints Top panel The following actions are available in the top panel: 1. Add — create a new endpoint 2. Statistics — select a period for statistics display 3. Search — search for endpoints in the table 4. Clear filters — reset all filters Statistics period selection The Statistics parameter affects the mini-graphs displayed in the table columns: Err — errors Send — data transmission attempts Ack — confirmed packets These mini-graphs help you quickly evaluate relay performance over a selected period. The graphs show: indicator dynamics total number of events for the selected period When hovering over a point, detailed information is displayed. Available periods: 12 hours — last 12 hours Day — last 24 hours Week — last week 2 weeks — last two weeks Month — last month Endpoints table columns Actions — available endpoint actions ID — unique endpoint identifier Partner ID — owner partner identifier Partner name — name of the owner partner Host — external server IP address or domain name Port — connection port Format — transmission protocol Statistics / Objects — number of objects connected to the endpoint Statistics / Active — number of active objects currently transmitting data Statistics / Auto — number of contracts with auto-add enabled Statistics / Queue — number of packets in the sending queue Statistics / Err — error mini-graph for the selected period Statistics / Send — send attempt mini-graph for the selected period Statistics / Ack — received packet mini-graph for the selected period On/Off — endpoint status (enabled or disabled) Status — endpoint state: active, disabled, or no active objects Uspw — authentication data (hidden by default) Testing — test endpoint flag (admin-only) TimeZone — time zone Comment — additional information Speed Limit — maximum speed transmitted to the external system Schedule — relay operating schedule Mapping fields — field mapping settings for specific formats You can configure which columns are displayed in the table: 1. Click the arrow in any column header 2. Select Columns 3. Check the columns you want to display 4. Uncheck the ones you want to hide The same menu also includes: sorting ascending sorting descending filtering data Endpoint actions In the Actions column, the following options are available: Enable/Disable — turn endpoint on or off Edit — open endpoint settings (you can also double-click the row) Auto add — configure automatic object assignment Statistics — open endpoint statistics Objects — open connected objects list Delete — remove endpoint How to create an endpoint 1. Click Add in the top panel 2. Fill in connection parameters: Name — endpoint name Host — external server address Port — external system port Format — transmission protocol Path — additional URL path if required Uspw — authentication data Time zone — default time zone for objects Speed limit — maximum speed sent to external system Enabled — enable/disable endpoint Testing — test endpoint mode (admin only) JSON config — additional settings in JSON format Comment — optional description Mapping fields — field mapping for special formats Schedule — allowed transmission schedule 3. ClickSave After saving, the endpoint becomes available for object assignment. How to edit an endpoint 1. Click Editin the Actionscolumn or double-click the endpoint row 2. Modify required parameters 3. Click Save Changes are automatically applied to all objects using this endpoint. If the system detects a duplicate endpoint configuration, it may suggest merging them. If you do not confirm the merge, changes will not be saved. Enable or disable an endpoint To temporarily stop or resume data transmission: Click the enable /disable button in the Actions column If an endpoint is disabled, all connected objects stop transmitting data to the external system. How to delete an endpoint 1. Click the Deletebutton in the Actions column 2. Confirm deletion The system will show how many objects are using this endpoint. After deletion, data transmission through this endpoint stops completely. Endpoint objects To view objects connected to an endpoint: 1. Find the required endpoint 2. Click Objectsin the Actions column This opens a list of all objects sending data through the endpoint. From here you can: add new objects edit object relay settings temporarily disable transmission remove objects from the endpoint Object list columns Agent ID — object identifier Vehicle — object name IMEI — device identifier Contract — object contract External ID — identifier in external system On/Off — relay status Err — error mini-graph Send — send attempt mini-graph Ack — received packet mini-graph Object actions: enable /disable object in endpoint remove object from endpoint How to add objects 1. Click Add in the Objectswindow 2. Select one or more objects 3. Optionally edit the External ID (by default IMEI is used) 4. Click Add If the object list is large, use the Search field at the top. How to edit an object 1. Double-click the object in the list 2. Edit parameters in the form Editable parameters: Endpoint External ID Enabled switch Speed limit Time zone Uspw JSON config Schedule The following parameters cannot be changed here: Host, Port, Format, Path (they are inherited from the endpoint) 3. Click Save Automatic object assignment (Auto add) The Auto add feature automatically connects new objects to an endpoint. It is useful when all objects of a contract must send data to the same external system. After configuration, new objects are automatically assigned without manual setup. How to configure Auto add 1. Click Auto add in the Actions column 2. In the window, click Add in the list of contracts 3. Select one or more contracts 4. Click Add again to confirm After saving, all new objects from selected contracts will automatically be connected to this endpoint"},{"s":"statistics","t":"Statistics","h":["Statistics","Statistics view modes","Statistics filters","Scope","Protocol","Metric","Endpoint","Loop threshold/hour","From / To","Statistics charts","Traffic over time","Outcome","By protocol","By endpoint","Metrics","Accepted / Ack","Send","Errors","Spooled","Dropped","Queue","Statistics tables","Top endpoints","Recent endpoint errors","Top vehicles for endpoint","Loop suspicion"],"x":"Statistics The Statisticstab displays data transmission statistics and information about possible relay errors. To open the statistics page, go to Relaying → Statistics. This page allows you to monitor relay performance, track data delivery success, analyze errors, and identify problematic objects or transmission destinations. Statistics are generated from relay service data and are displayed according to the current user's permissions. An administrator can view global statistics for the entire system or statistics for individual endpoints. A partner user can view statistics only for their own endpoints and the endpoints of partners within their hierarchy. Statistics view modes For administrators, the Scope filter is available: Global — statistics for the entire relay system Endpoints — statistics for individual data transmission endpoints Users with the Partner role can only work in Endpoints mode. Statistics filters The top section of the page contains filters used to select data for analysis. Scope Defines the source of statistics: Global — statistics for the entire system Endpoints — statistics for individual endpoints Available to administrators only. Protocol Filters statistics by data transmission protocol. Allows you to display statistics only for the selected protocol. Metric Determines which events are displayed in charts and tables. Available values: All metrics — all metrics OK only — successfully accepted packets only Errors only — errors only Spooled only — queued packets only Dropped only — dropped packets only Endpoint Allows you to select a single endpoint or a group of endpoints for analysis. Endpoints are grouped by partner. Loop threshold/hour Used to identify objects transmitting an unusually high number of packets. If the number of packets exceeds the specified threshold, the object may appear in the Loop suspicion table. From / To Defines the beginning and end of the analysis period. All chart timestamps are displayed in the user's local time zone using a 24-hour format. Statistics charts The upper section of the page contains charts that provide a quick overview of relay performance. Traffic over time Shows how the number of transmitted packets changes over time. This chart helps identify: periods of high activity sudden increases in traffic load moments when errors occur periods with no data transmission Outcome Shows the distribution of transmission results. The chart allows you to quickly see: how many packets were successfully accepted how many packets resulted in errors how many packets were placed in the queue whether there are transmission issues By protocol Compares statistics across different transmission protocols. This chart helps determine: which protocol is used most frequently which protocol generates the most errors which protocols create the highest traffic load By endpoint Compares statistics between endpoints. This chart helps identify: the most active endpoints endpoints with a high number of errors transmission destinations generating the highest load When you click an endpoint in the By endpoint chart, the system automatically: selects that endpoint in the filters updates all other charts refreshes the statistics tables displays information only for the selected endpoint Metrics The following metrics are used throughout the statistics section. Accepted / Ack Number of packets successfully accepted by the external system. A higher value indicates more successful data transmission. Send Number of packet transmission attempts. Represents the total volume of outgoing traffic. Errors Number of transmission or connection errors. An increase in this metric usually indicates connectivity issues, incorrect endpoint settings, or external system availability problems. Spooled Number of packets placed into the transmission queue. These packets will be sent later when transmission becomes possible. Dropped Number of packets discarded by the service and not transmitted. Queue Current endpoint queue size. If this value continuously increases, it may indicate problems delivering data to the external system. Statistics tables Several tables are displayed below the charts. Top endpoints Lists the endpoints that transmitted the highest number of packets during the selected period. This helps identify the most active transmission destinations. Recent endpoint errors Displays the most recent relay errors. This table helps identify the cause of transmission issues and determine which endpoint generated the error. Top vehicles for endpoint Lists the objects that generated the highest traffic volume for the selected endpoint. Used for load analysis and identifying the most active objects. Loop suspicion Lists objects that exceeded the configured packet-per-hour threshold. This may indicate: a data transmission loop incorrect device configuration a misconfigured integration If objects appear in this list, it is recommended to review the relay configuration and verify the data source settings."},{"s":"objects-2","t":"Objects","h":["Objects","Object list","Top panel","Table columns","Object actions","Managing relays from the Vehicles section","Relays for a single vehicle","Relays for multiple vehicles","How to add a relay","Resend requests","Resend requests table columns"],"x":"Objects The Objectstab displays all object relay configurations available to the current user. Object list To open the object list, go toRelaying→ Objects. Here you can: find a specific object see which endpoint is used for data transmission enable or disable object relay open relay settings view transmission statistics for an object Top panel The following actions are available on the top panel: Configs — relay configuration settings Export — export the object list Statistics — select the period used for mini-graphs Search — search within the table Clear filters — reset all filters Table columns Actions — available actions for the object relay configuration ID — relay record identifier Agent ID — object identifier Host — IP address or domain name of the external server Port — external server port Format — data transmission protocol Statistics / Err — error mini-graph Statistics / Send — transmission attempts mini-graph for the selected period Statistics / Ack — successfully accepted packets mini-graph for the selected period Last ID — identifier of the last processed packet Uspw — authentication data Time Zone — object relay time zone On/Off — relay status (enabled or disabled) Object actions The following actions are available in the Actions column: turn object relay on or off open the object relay settings You can also open the edit form by double-clicking the object row. The edit form allows you to modify settings that apply only to the selected object. If you need to change the server address, port, transmission protocol, or connection path, edit the corresponding endpoint in the Endpoints tab. Managing relays from the Vehicles section Relay configurations can be managed not only through the Objects tab in the Relaying section but also directly from the object list in the Vehicles section. To do this: 1. Open the Vehicles section 2. Select one or more vehicles 3. Right-click the selection 4. Choose Relaying from the context menu Relays for a single vehicle If a single vehicle is selected, the relay management window for that object opens. The window contains two sections: Displays all relay configurations assigned to the object. From here you can: add a new relay edit an existing relay delete a relay enable or disable data transmission Contains requests for retransmitting historical data for a selected period. Relays for multiple vehicles If multiple vehicles are selected, the menu item appears as Relay (N), where N is the number of selected objects. This allows you to create the same relay configuration for several vehicles at once. The system creates a separate relay record for each selected object using the same endpoint How to add a relay 1. In the Vehiclessection, select one or more objects 2. Open the context menu with a right-click 3. Select Relaying 4. In the Configuration section, click Add The relay creation form will open. Fill in the required parameters: Endpoint — data transmission destination External ID — object identifier in the external system Speed Limit — transmission speed limit Time Zone — object time zone Uspw — authentication data Enabled — enables or disables the relay JSON Config — additional settings in JSON format Schedule — transmission schedule The Host, Port, Format, and Path fields are inherited from the endpoint and cannot be edited here. To modify these settings, edit the corresponding endpoint. After saving, the object will begin transmitting data through the selected endpoint. Resend requests The Resend requests section is used to retransmit previously stored data for a specified period. This feature is useful when: data was not successfully delivered to the external system historical data needs to be retransmitted How to create a resend request 1. In the Resend requests section, click Add 2. Specify the start date and time 3. Specify the end date and time 4. Click Add The request will appear in the table. Resend requests table columns Start Time — beginning of the retransmission period Stop Time — end of the retransmission period Created At — request creation date and time Updated At — date and time of the last update Status — current request status Available statuses: New — request created and waiting for processing In Process — data retransmission is in progress Complete — processing completed After the request is completed, all data for the selected period will be retransmitted through the chosen relay configuration."},{"s":"troubleshooting-1","t":"Troubleshooting","h":["Troubleshooting","No data in the external system","A new object was not connected automatically","Queue keeps growing","Error count (Err) is increasing","Send is increasing, but Ack remains zero","Statistics are not displayed","Best practices"],"x":"Troubleshooting No data in the external system If data is not appearing in the external system, check the following: the endpoint is enabled object relay is enabled the External ID is configured correctly endpoint settings (Host, Port, Format, Path) are correct authentication credentials (Uspw) are valid there are no errors in the Statistics tab the Queue value is not increasing A new object was not connected automatically Check the following: an Auto add rule is enabled for the object's contract the object was created after the Auto add rule was configured the endpoint is enabled the object belongs to the contract associated with the Auto add rule the user has access rights to that contract Queue keeps growing If the Queue value is increasing, packets are accumulating and cannot be delivered. This usually happens when: the external server is unavailable connection settings are incorrect the receiving system rejects the connection Check the Host, Port, and Uspw settings, verify the availability of the external system, and review recent errors in the Statistics tab. Error count (Err) is increasing If the Errmetric continues to grow, check: availability of the external system authentication settings the selected transmission protocol schedule settings any restrictions or requirements on the receiving side Send is increasing, but Ack remains zero If Send increases while Ack remains at zero, the system is transmitting data but is not receiving confirmation from the external system. Check: protocol settings External ID JSON Config authentication credentials error messages in the Statistics tab Statistics are not displayed Possible causes: no data has been transmitted through the endpoint yet the selected time period is too short the endpoint was created recently statistics have not been generated yet Best practices Create a separate endpoint for each external system. Configure Auto add if all new objects within a contract should automatically transmit data to the same external system. Before deleting an endpoint, verify how many objects are connected to it. When troubleshooting, start with the Statistics tab and review the Queue, Err, Send, and Ack metrics."},{"s":"configuring-specific-relay-integrations","t":"Configuring specific relay integrations","h":["Configuring specific relay integrations"],"x":"Configuring specific relay integrations This section describes how to configure individual data relay integrations with external systems. For each supported protocol or service, you will find: required connection parameters endpoint configuration examples transmitted data formats protocol-specific settings and recommendations Select the required system from the list below and follow the corresponding setup guide. The instructions will help you create an endpoint, configure connection settings, connect objects, and verify successful data transmission to the external system."},{"s":"tripia-relay-configuration","t":"Tripia relay configuration","h":["Tripia relay configuration","How to add a relay","Connection parameters","Data transmission interval","Transmitted data","Provider information","Vehicle information","Event registration data","GPS data","Connection data","Vehicle status","GPS events","GPS sensor data","Telemetry capabilities","ADAS events","DMS events","CAN bus data","Driver ID data","Example transmitted packet structure"],"x":"Tripia relay configuration Tripia is a relay protocol used to transmit vehicle telematics data to the Tripia platform in JSON format. The Tripia protocol supports transmission of: vehicle location and speed data trip information vehicle status data sensor readings GPS, ADAS, and DMS events CAN bus data driver identification information Data is transmitted in real time. How to add a relay 1. Log in to the Administration panel 2. Open the Vehiclestab 3. Locate the required vehicle, right-click it, and select Relayingfrom the context menu 4. In the opened window, click Add 5. In the Configurationwindow, enter the relay settings. Connection parameters When creating an endpoint, use the following settings: Format Tripia Host 64.225.12.166 Port 3000 Protocol TCP Data Format JSON Data transmission interval Data is transmitted automatically: every 30 seconds when ignition is ON every 1 minute when ignition is OFF Transmitted data Provider information The following provider information is transmitted: Provider Name Provider Code Vehicle information The following vehicle information is transmitted: Vehicle identifier License plate number Dispatcher information Event registration data The following timestamps are transmitted: Event Timestamp Sent Timestamp GPS data The following navigation parameters are transmitted: Latitude Longitude Speed Heading Altitude Number of satellites HDOP PDOP Connection data The following cellular network parameters are transmitted: Signal strength Mobile operator (MCC/MNC) Connection type Cell ID Vehicle status The following status information is transmitted: Ignition status Movement status Odometer Engine hours Battery voltage GPS events The following GPS-related events are supported: Battery disconnected Signal jamming detected Satellite loss Vehicle towing detected Communication signal lost Communication signal restored Harsh acceleration Harsh cornering Harsh braking Crash detection GPS sensor data The following sensor data can be transmitted: Temperature sensors Door and body status sensors Driver door Passenger door Side cargo door Rear cargo door Engine hood Additional sensors Panic button Telemetry capabilities The following subsystem availability indicators are transmitted: ADAS DMS Reefer CAN Bus Driver ID ADAS events The following Advanced Driver Assistance System (ADAS) events are transmitted: FCW (Forward Collision Warning) LDW (Lane Departure Warning) LDWL LDWR PCW PROX DMS events The following Driver Monitoring System (DMS) events are transmitted: Blocked Face Lost Distraction Frequent Yawning Looking Aside Smoking Phone Calling Drowsiness Seatbelt Violation CAN bus data The following CAN bus parameters are transmitted: Fuel level (%) Fuel volume (liters) Fuel load Fuel consumption Vehicle speed Odometer Engine hours Driver door status Passenger door status Driver ID data The following driver identification information is transmitted: Identifier type Identifier code Driver ID Driver name Example transmitted packet structure When sending data to Tripia, the system generates a JSON packet containing vehicle information, location data, sensor readings, events, and telemetry information. The packet structure includes the following sections: { \"provider\": { \"name\": \"Providers Name\", \"code\": \"1234abc\" }, \"dispatcher\": { \"name\": \"Dispatcher Name\", \"identifier\": \"1234abc\", \"licensePlate\": \"1234abc\" }, \"registry\": { \"eventTimestamp\": \"2016-01-01T00:00:00Z\", \"sentTimestamp\": \"2016-01-01T00:00:00Z\" }, \"gps\": { \"latitude\": 123.456789, \"longitude\": 123.456789, \"speed\": 100, \"heading\": 123, \"altitude\": 123, \"satellites\": 12, \"hdop\": 1.2, \"pdop\": 1.2 }, \"connection\": { \"signal\": 85, \"operatorMCCMNC\": \"Entel\", \"type\": 2, \"cellID\": 123456789 }, \"status\": { \"ignition\": 1, \"movement\": 1, \"odometer\": 12345678.9, \"hourMeter\": 12345678.9, \"battery\": 12.2 }, \"gpsEvents\": { \"batteryDisconnected\": 0, \"jamming\": 1, \"satellitesLost\": 0, \"towedVehicule\": 1, \"signalLost\": 0, \"signalRecovered\": 1, \"driving\": { \"harshAcceleration\": 1, \"harshTurn\": 1, \"harshBraking\": 1, \"crash\": 1 } }, \"gpsSensors\": { \"temperature1\": 12.3, \"temperature2\": 12.3, \"temperature3\": 12.3, \"temperature4\": 12.3, \"temperature5\": 12.3, \"temperature6\": 12.3, \"driverDoor\": 0, \"copilotDoor\": 0, \"sideLoadingDoor\": 0, \"rearLoadingDoor\": 0, \"engineHood\": 1, \"panicButton\": 0 }, \"telemetry\": { \"adas\": 0, \"dms\": 1, \"reefer\": 0, \"canbus\": 1, \"driverid\": 0 }, \"adasEvents\": { \"fcw\": 1, \"ldw\": 1, \"ldwl\": 1, \"ldwr\": 1, \"pcw\": 1, \"prox\": 1 }, \"dmsEvents\": { \"blocked\": 0, \"faceLost\": 0, \"distraction\": 0, \"frequentYawning\": 0, \"lookingAside\": 1, \"smoking\": 0, \"calling\": 1, \"drowsiness\": 0, \"seatbelt\": 1 }, \"canbusData\": { \"fuelPercent\": 12, \"fuelLiters\": 128, \"fuelLoad\": 80, \"odometer\": 12345678.9, \"speed\": 123, \"fuelConsumption\": 11.12, \"driverDoor\": 0, \"copilotDoor\": 1, \"engineHours\": 12345678.9 }, \"driveridData\": { \"type\": 1, \"codeId\": \"123456789\", \"driverName\": \"Nombre conductor\", \"driverId\": \"123456789\" } } The JSON example shown above is provided for reference only. The actual set of transmitted fields depends on: the connected hardware relay configuration settings available vehicle sensors supported telemetry subsystems and integrations"},{"s":"integration-with-riyadh-municipality-","t":"Integration with RIYADH Municipality","h":["Integration with RIYADH Municipality","Set up data relay in the Admin panel","2. Configure required sensors for the vehicle","Enabling the CAN odometer for Teltonika devices"],"x":"Integration with RIYADH Municipality This guide explains how to set up the integration that sends GPS tracker data from the OneGPS system to an external WMS platform, using the RIYADH Municipality protocol. The setup includes two main steps: 1. Relay configuration in the OneGPS Admin panel 2. Sensor configuration for devices Set up data relay in the Admin panel Log in to the Admin panel 2. Go to the \"Vehicles\" tab 3. Find the vehicle you want to configure, right-click on it and select \"Relay\" from the menu 4. In the opened window, click the add relay button 5. Fill in the relay configuration fields: Host Destination server address (IP or domain) admin-dev-wms.iotsquared.io (from protocol) Port Port number for data transmission 443 Format Relay format Select RIYADH municipality External ID Device identifier (usually IMEI) Enter the IMEI of the GPS tracker Path API request path with x-token /api/v1/integrations/http/${httpid} (where ${httpid} is an x-token) For example, /api/v1/integrations/http/32fc9bec-967839-467890-56a0 Time zone Time zone used for the data UTC Speed Limit Speed limit (km/h) 0 Enabled Relay activation status Check the box to enable json config Additional parameters in JSON format Please provide the partner's name Example configuration 2. Configure required sensors for the vehicle To ensure proper data transmission to the WMS platform, you must configure the following mandatory sensors on the vehicle: - GPS antenna sensor** - CAN odometer sensor (for mileage) - Weight sensor Enabling the CAN odometer for Teltonika devices To enable mileage data transmission via the CAN bus, follow these steps: In the Admin Panel, go to the \"Vehicles\" tab 2. Find the vehicle, right-click on it, and select \"Commands\" 3. In the сommands window, select a command from the drop-down list. 5. Specify the command parameters and click the \"Send\" button. 1 — Open сommand list 2 — Specify command parameters setparam 50160:1 — enables CAN odometer data transmission To set an initial mileage value (not starting from zero), you can use the following command: setparam 11807:[value]— sets initial mileage (in km) Where [value] is the desired initial odometer reading in kilometers. Example, if you want to set the mileage to 2729 kilometers, the command would look like this: setparam 11807:2729. These commands configure the Teltonika device to receive mileage data via the vehicle's CAN bus. Once configured, the mileage will be automatically transmitted to the system."},{"s":"additional-relay-json-sonfigurations","t":"Additional relay JSON сonfigurations","h":["Additional relay JSON сonfigurations","Wialon Retranslator","Transmitting processed sensor values","Sensor filtering and processing","Packet filtering","Wialon IPS and Wialon IPS v2","Parameter renaming","Sending IButton in the packet body","inputs block","inputs parameters","Wialon Retranslator, Galileo, and EGTS","Field renaming","Saving relay logs","Temperature data generation","Parameters"],"x":"Additional relay JSON сonfigurations Some relay protocols support advanced configuration through the JSON Config field. JSON configuration allows you to: modify the transmitted data set use processed sensor values rename transmitted fields filter packets before transmission configure special transmission scenarios Supported parameters depend on the selected relay protocol. JSON configuration is specified in the JSON Config field when creating or editing a relay configuration. Wialon Retranslator Transmitting processed sensor values Allows relay transmission of sensor values after they have been processed by the OneGPS system. For example, a fuel level sensor can be transmitted after calibration has been applied. { \"sensors_use\": { \"fuel_1\": 135785, \"NameSensor\": 228 } } sensors_use — list of object sensors whose processed values should be used fuel_1 — parameter name transmitted to the destination system 135785 — sensor ID in the system The sensor ID can be found in the object sensor settings. Fuel level sensors must have calibration configured correctly Values outside the calibration range are transmitted as raw values For example, instead of liters, a raw value such as 65530 may be transmitted Sensor filtering and processing { \"sensors_use\": { \"temp_1\": 1370805, \"temp_2\": 1370806 }, \"tags_rewrite\": { \"temp_2\": \"temp2\", \"temp_01\": \"temp1\" }, \"sensors_filter\": { \"temp_1\": { \"max\": 130, \"min\": 1, \"type\": \"int\" }, \"temp_2\": { \"max\": 100, \"min\": 1, \"type\": \"int\" } }, \"sensors_notuse\": { \"temp_1\": 1, \"temp_2\": 1 } } sensors_use — allows the use of specified object sensor values tags_rewrite — renames parameters before transmission Example: { \"tags_rewrite\": { \"temp_2\": \"temp2\" } } In this case, temp_2 will be transmitted as temp2. sensors_filter — filters sensor values before transmission Supported parameters: type Data type. Currently only int is supported min Minimum allowed value max Maximum allowed value Example: { \"sensors_filter\": { \"temp_1\": { \"min\": 1, \"max\": 130, \"type\": \"int\" } } } Filtering works only for sensors transmitting integer values. sensors_notuse — excludes sensors from transmission Example: { \"sensors_notuse\": { \"temp_1\": 1, \"temp_2\": 1 } } This configuration prevents the specified sensors from being transmitted. Sensor settings configured in OneGPS do not affect relay processing through this mechanism. All transmission parameters must be configured explicitly in JSON config. Packet filtering Allows packets matching specified conditions to be excluded from transmission. { \"packet_filter\": { \"Valid\": \"0\" }, \"keys_to_skip\": [ \"HardVersion\" ] } packet_filter — skips a packet if a specified parameter contains the configured value. Example: { \"packet_filter\": { \"Valid\": \"0\" } } If the Valid parameter equals 0, the packet will not be transmitted. Multiple conditions can be configured. keys_to_skip — skips a packet if it contains any specified field Example: { \"keys_to_skip\": [ \"HardVersion\" ] } If the packet contains the HardVersion field, it will not be transmitted. Multiple fields can be specified. Wialon IPS and Wialon IPS v2 Parameter renaming Allows parameter names to be replaced before transmission. { \"params\": { \"Vsourse\": { \"name\": \"pwr_ext\", \"type\": \"int\" } } } In this example, the Vsourse parameter is transmitted as pwr_ext. Sending IButton in the packet body { \"params\": { \"IButton\": { \"type\": \"tcp_box\" } } } When the IButton parameter is configured with the tcp_box type, the identifier value is transmitted in the packet body. If the parameter is missing, the IButton value is not added to the packet and an empty value is transmitted. This option is commonly used to transmit a driver identifier outside the standard parameter block. inputs block Allows additional digital inputs to be generated based on sensor values. { \"inputs\": { \"DIN1\": { \"max\": 1, \"min\": 1, \"value\": false, \"myval\": false } }, \"params\": { \"DIN1\": { \"name\": \"DIN1\", \"type\": \"int\" } } } inputs parameters Parameter Description min Minimum allowed value max Maximum allowed value value If set to true, the actual sensor value is transmitted. If omitted or set to false, standard packet generation logic is used myval Allows a custom value to be transmitted instead of the sensor value when the condition is met { \"inputs\": { \"DIN1\": { \"max\": 1, \"min\": 1 } }, \"params\": { \"DIN1\": { \"name\": \"DIN1\", \"type\": \"int\" } } } If the sensor value falls within the range from 1 to 1, the value 1 is transmitted. This mechanism is typically used to duplicate sensor states as binary values. For example, it can be used to transmit door status through the inputs block. Wialon Retranslator, Galileo, and EGTS Field renaming { \"tags_rewrite\": { \"temperature\": \"galileo_temp_0\", \"digital\": \"galileo_in_2\" } } Allows field names to be replaced before transmission. Examples: temperature→ galileo_temp_0 digital→ galileo_in_2 Saving relay logs { \"save_data_for_report\": \"1\" } Enables storage of relay data for reporting, diagnostics, and troubleshooting purposes. Temperature data generation { \"temperature_data_generator\": { \"DOS\": \"galileo_in_2\", \"closed_value\": 0, \"temperature_sensors\": [ \"galileo_temp_0\" ] } } This configuration is intended for special integration scenarios. When specified conditions are met, the system can automatically generate temperature sensor values. Parameters DOS Monitored sensor closed_value Value that triggers the condition temperature_sensors List of temperature sensors This functionality is designed for specific integration requirements and should only be used when requested by the receiving system."},{"s":"news","t":"News","h":["News","Select who you want to write news for.","Add text."],"x":"News Select who you want to write news for. Add text. The tab allows you to view existing news and add new ones. ID - unique news number. Headline - news title. Description - news title. Update - date of the last news update. Created - date and time of news creation. Для просмотра новостей в пользовательском интерфейсе, используйте кнопку просмотра новостей."},{"s":"administrator","t":"Administrator","h":["Administrator"],"x":"Administrator The tab is used to view and add new rights in the OneGPS system. • ID - a unique number in the system. • Name - the name of the tabs in the OneGPS system. • Description- additional description. • Partner have - active status."},{"s":"permissions","t":"Permissions","h":["Permissions"],"x":"Permissions The Access Rights section was used to add and change rights to use modules in the system. Add module window. Edit window. If the Partner has permission to use the module, the checkbox should be checked."},{"s":"audit-2","t":"Audit","h":["Audit"],"x":"Audit Time - the latest changes in the Database. Login - unique user login. Contract ID - unique contract number where the changes were made. Table name - table name in the Database where the changes were made. Row ID - unique row number where the changes were made. Agent id - unique object number in the OneGPS system. Admin: masterID = administrator ID, Login = administrator login, partner ID - empty Partner: masterID = partner ID, Login = partner login, Partner ID - partner ID User: masterID = user ID, Login = user login, Partner ID - partner ID"},{"s":"audit-history-2","t":"Audit history","h":["Audit history"],"x":"Audit history The Audit History section is designed to display history by users with time and actions with objects. Audit with detailed information on operations."},{"s":"account-mapping","t":"Account mapping","h":["Account mapping"],"x":"Account mapping Id - unique account number. User name - user name. E-mail - user e-mail. Contract name - contract name in the system. Contract Id - unique number in the system. In the “Account mapping” section, select a user by name, id, or contract number. This is the contract where the object will be mirrored. Double-click on this user, go to settings. Select the account from which you want to mirror the object. Then save the settings. Log in as the selected user to the account where you are mirroring the object. Next, in the user settings, select the necessary objects for display. Important! After deleting the mappig, user rights must be updated (log in to the account as a master). Watch the video to learn how to use Account Mapping."},{"s":"modules-3","t":"Modules","h":["Modules"],"x":"Modules id - unique module number in the system. Name - module name. Description - module description. Paid - column for viewing the payment status for the module. Only for administrators - column for the status of the rights to use the module."},{"s":"templates-editor","t":"Templates Editor","h":["Templates Editor"],"x":"Templates Editor The Template Editor tab is intended for creating and displaying templates. The text and template type can be selected from the pop-up list in the Type field. Add new templates"},{"s":"settings-5","t":"Settings","h":["Settings"],"x":"Settings The tab allows you to customize the display of reports and change the brand name of the partner (rebranding). Reports-Name of the report in the OneGPS system. Alternate title - the name of the report, which can be assigned by partners and sub-partners. Check the box to enable (disable) access to the report. Id - unique identifier of the recast entry. Account ID - partner's agreement number in the system. Org name - the title displayed on the authorization page. Header -header. Url- alternative link to the OneGPS. Url PTM - alternative link to PTM. Logo - a link to the logo image. Second logo - a link to the image of the second logo. Favicon - wei site icon. Displayed in the address bar before the page title. •iOS- a link to a mobile application in the AppleStore. Android- a link to a mobile application on PlayMarket. Theme - selected color theme for the OneGPS. Search field on the page TOTp authorization setting parameter."},{"s":"rebranding","t":"Rebranding","h":["Rebranding"],"x":"Rebranding Rebranding is a marketing strategy that includes a set of activities to change the brand. This may include changing the name, logo, slogan, visual design. The purpose of rebranding is to strengthen the brand, make it more unique, increase the target audience and attract new customers. Header– Partner name in system. Partner - main partner Url - alternative link to the OneGPS. Theme – the selected color theme for the OneGPS. You can choose from a list of a large number of different color schemes. Logo - link to the logo image. iOS- link to the mobile application in the AppleStore. Android – link to the mobile application in PlayMarket."},{"s":"add","t":"Add","h":["Add"],"x":"Add 1. Need to find a partner. 2. Then you need to check if there is a rebrand on it. If not, add a new one. 3. Window for adding a new rebranding or favicon. Increasing the rebranding logo in the settings of the Admin panel. You need to open the rebranding settings, open the css editor write code  .logodiv { background-size: 160px height: 171px !important; top: 90px !important; } Save. Dimensions can be changed in the code, for the size you need. 4. You can check the logo by url. Code for a transparent password entry window  #logintbl { height: 650px; width: 525px; vertical-align: middle; display: table-cell; background: url(../images/login.png) top center no-repeat; background-image: linear-gradient(rgba(0, 0, 0, 0), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5), rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)); } • Enlargement of the rebranding logo You need to open the rebranding settings, open the css editor write down the code .x-body { background-image: url(\"https://adm.OneGPS-gps.africa/backend/files/rebranding/gps.naviafri.com/64affeccdb3f9.jpg\"); background-size: cover; background-position: center; height: 100%; } .logodiv { height: 115px !important; } Next save. The dimensions can be changed in code to suit the size you need In order to increase or decrease the transparency color, you need to specify the following settings."},{"s":"delete-2","t":"Delete","h":["Delete"],"x":"Delete Delete button."},{"s":"security","t":"Security","h":["Security"],"x":"Security This function is intended only for partners. Security settings are in the admin panel in the settings section. You can set up two types of passing: TOTR (using qr) Pass verification via mail.  https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.google.android.apps.authenticator2&hl=ru https://apps.apple.com/ru/app/google -authenticator/id388497605 https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.azure.authenticator&hl=ru https://apps.apple.com/us/app/microsoft -authenticator/id983156458 https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.authy.authy&hl=ru https://apps.apple.com/us/app/twilio-authy/id494168017 Use the authentication app to scan the QR code provided by the service. This links your account to the app and allows you to generate unique codes. Enter the current code displayed in the app into the confirmation field on the website or in the app to complete the process. - Enter your username and password. Start by entering your details. - Open the authentication app. On your mobile device, open the app you used to set up TOTP. - Enter a one-time password. Look at the current one-time password in the app and enter it to complete the login process. Security Even if someone knows your password, they will not be able to access your account without a one-time password. Convenience The authentication app does not require an internet connection to generate the code. Versatility Many services support TOTP, which allows you to use one app to access different accounts. In order to authenticate via email, you need to: Configure SMTP in the partner settings. Add the 2FA_EMAIL configuration. After the settings are entered into the partner configuration, on the main login screen, after entering the login and password from the admin panel, a code entry window will appear. Configuration settings for entering the user interface by code received from the application. Login and code entry screen. Use mail and input window. Multi-factor authentication by default In the admin area, you need to add a key for a partner or account. All users of this account will then be asked to add TOTP authorization to their accounts."},{"s":"faq","t":"FAQ","h":["FAQ"],"x":"FAQ FAQ is a collection of questions and answers on a specific topic that users or clients regularly ask. An FAQ helps users find answers to common questions without having to contact support, saving the company time and resources."},{"s":"mobile-applications","t":"Mobile applications","h":["Mobile applications"],"x":"Mobile applications This section will help you get started and connect with the mobile tools of the OneGPS system: OneGPS Mobile App — an app for vehicle monitoring and managing objects Telegram Bot — a helper bot that allows you to manage your vehicle list, get information about your objects, and use your phone as a virtual GPS tracker PTM (OneGPS Task Manager) — an app for drivers and couriers, where tasks and routes are displayed OneGPS Guarda is a vehicle security and monitoring application. It allows you to monitor vehicle status, manage access, and track movements. OneGPS Tracker is an app that turns your smartphone into a GPS tracker. It allows you to track the device's location in real-time and send the data to the server In this section, we will explain how to use the features of the mobile applications."},{"s":"mobile-pilot","t":"Mobile OneGPS","h":["Mobile OneGPS"],"x":"Mobile OneGPS OneGPS Mobile App is an application for vehicle monitoring and managing objects directly from your smartphone. It provides access to all the core features of the OneGPS monitoring system. With the app, you can: track the real-time location of your objects on the map view the movement history and statistics for vehicles generate reports for a selected period view event notifications monitor the device operational parameters"},{"s":"what-s-new","t":"What’s new","h":["What’s new","Fleet analytics","Balance top-ups via Stripe","AI Assistant in the mobile app","Additional protection when signing in to the mobile app","Viewing events and notifications","Address representation","Updated Info tab","Current track","Raw data points","Arm / Disarm object"],"x":"What’s new Here we’ve collected the latest updates — learn what’s changed and what new features are now available. Fleet analytics The mobile app includes a new Analytics tab for quick access to key fleet performance metrics. It displays an overview for the past 24 hours, including idle and operating time, mileage, and the number of vehicles. Vehicle and connectivity statuses, mileage, violations, geofences, and other metrics are presented in clear charts, making it easier to assess fleet performance and spot issues from your phone. Balance top-ups via Stripe You can top up your organization’s balance through Stripe. In the Finance tab, enter the amount, proceed to payment, and select an available payment method on the Stripe page. This feature is available for accounts with Stripe payments enabled. After completing the payment, you can check the updated account balance directly in the app. AI Assistant in the mobile app The OneGPS AI Assistant is available in the mobile app. It helps users find features and explains how to complete common tasks, such as generating a report, configuring an object, creating a geofence, or connecting a device. Responses may include links to relevant documentation. Chats are saved in the history, so you can return to a previous conversation to review an answer or continue the discussion. Additional protection when signing in to the mobile app The OneGPS mobile app supports two-factor authentication. After entering your login and password, confirm the sign-in with a one-time code from an authenticator app or email. This additional verification protects your account even if someone else obtains your password. Viewing events and notifications The Events section includes separate Notifications and Events tabs, making it easier to find the information you need. The first tab shows triggered notification rules, while the second displays changes in object and sensor states. For each tab, you can select objects, data types, and a viewing period separately. You can also view the location where an event or notification was recorded on the map. Address representation You can now choose which parts of the address are shown in the app — for example, in object cards, reports, history, and other sections. A new Address representation tab has been added to the app menu. Open it to enable or disable specific address components. Updated Info tab When opening an object card, the Infotab now immediately shows a map with the current vehicle location. Current track A new Current tracktab has been added to the object menu. Here you can view the current location of the object and its recent movement directly on the map. Raw data points Raw data viewing is now available in the mobile app. You can see unprocessed points that the device sends to the system. Arm / Disarm object You can now arm and disarm an object directly from the mobile app. If a security sensor is configured, a Arming option appears in the object menu, and corresponding security status icons are shown next to the object in the list."},{"s":"how-to-install-and-log-into-the-app-1","t":"How to install and log into the app","h":["How to install and log into the app","Installing the app","Method 1. Via the App Store","Method 2. Using a direct link","Logging into the app","Steps to log in","Logging in as an administrator","Sign in with two-factor authentication","Signing in with a code from an Authenticator app (TOTP)","Signing in with a code sent by email (Email 2FA)","Login settings","Registration with device","QR code",":","Change server:","Go to system settings:"],"x":"How to install and log into the app In this article, we will explain how to start working with the OneGPS mobile app. You will learn: how to install the app on your smartphone how regular users can log in how administrators can log in and manage user accounts how to sign in with two-factor authentication available login settings Installing the app To start using the mobile version of OneGPS, install the app on your smartphone. Method 1. Via the App Store 1. Open the app store on your phone: Google Play for Android App Store for iPhone 2. In the search bar, type: OneGPS 3. Find the app with the official brand icon and click Install Method 2. Using a direct link You can go directly to the app store via a link on your smartphone: After you open the link, install the OneGPS app. Once installed, the OneGPS icon will appear on your smartphone screen. Tap it to open the app. Logging into the app To log into the app, make sure you have: A smartphone with the OneGPS mobile app installed An internet connection Your login credentials for your OneGPS account Steps to log in 1. Откройте приложение OneGPS 2. На стартовом экране введите логин и пароль от своего веб-аккаунта 1. Open the OneGPS app 2. On the welcome screen, enter your login and password from your web account 3. Tap Sign in 4. If you want to save your login details and avoid entering them every time, check the Remember me box 5. Choose the server to log into After successful login, you will be taken to the main screen of the app, where the different sections will be available. Logging in as an administrator Administrators can log in using their credentials and immediately see the profiles of all users under the contract. They can quickly switch between accounts, help colleagues, and manage settings in the mobile app. 1. Enter the administrator’s login and password 2. Tap Sign in 3. Select the server to log into 4. A list of contracts associated with the administrator will appear in the app 5. Tap the desired account 6. Select a specific user within the contract to access their personal account Sign in with two-factor authentication Two-factor authentication (2FA) provides additional protection for your OneGPS account. To sign in, you need a one-time code in addition to your login and password. Even if someone else obtains your password, they cannot access the account without this code. OneGPS supports two verification methods: Code from an authenticator app (TOTP) — the code is generated by a connected app, such as Google Authenticator or Microsoft Authenticator Code sent by email (Email 2FA) — the system sends a code to your verified email address Before signing in to the mobile app, enable the preferred 2FA method in your user profile in the OneGPS web interface. Both two-factor authentication methods can be enabled for the same account. In this case, you must complete both verification steps in sequence when signing in: enter the code from the authenticator app, and then enter the code sent by email. Signing in with a code from an Authenticator app (TOTP) This verification method uses a six-digit code generated by an authenticator app. For instructions on enabling TOTP authentication in the web interface, see this article. To sign in to the mobile app: 1. Open the OneGPS app 2. Enter your login and password 3. Tap Sign in 4. Open the authenticator app and find the code for your OneGPS account 5. Enter the six-digit code on the sign-in verification screen 6. Tap Sign in After the code is successfully verified, the app opens. The code changes every 30 seconds. If the code expires before you enter it, wait for a new code and try again. Signing in with a code sent by email (Email 2FA) With this method, OneGPS sends a one-time code to the email address specified in your user profile after verifying your login and password. For instructions on enabling email verification in the web interface, see this article. To sign in to the mobile app: 1. Open the OneGPS app 2. Enter your login and password 3. Tap Sign in 4. Check your email and find the message containing the code 5. Enter the code on the sign-in verification screen 6. Tap Sign in After the code is successfully verified, the app opens. If you do not receive the message, check your Spam folder and make sure the correct email address is specified in your profile. Do not share your one-time code with anyone. Login settings On the login screen, in the top-right corner, there is an icon . Tap on it to open the Login settings. Here you can: Registration with device QR code : The app will scan a QR code that already contains the object’s data. 1. Scan the QR code on the device or in the OneGPS system 2. Make sure the QR code fits within the frame on your screen, and the app will automatically scan it 3. Choose the action: Sing in : If you already have an account in the OneGPS system, the device will be automatically linked to your existing account Registration : If you don't have an account, the app will prompt you to create a new one, and the device will be added to the new account New user registration: 1. Fill out the required information: Email Phone number Password Confirm password Tap Continue. 2. Email verification A verification code will be sent to your email address. Enter the code on the confirmation screen. If you don't receive the email, you can request a resend after the timer ends. Tap Confirm After successful verification, you will be logged into the app, and the device scanned via QR code will be linked to your OneGPS account. Change server: Enter the address of the desired server and save the changes by tapping Accept Go to system settings: Open your smartphone’s settings and grant the necessary permissions to the app To exit the Login settings section, tap Cancel."},{"s":"how-to-add-an-object-2","t":"How to add an object","h":["How to add an object","Adding an object using a QR code","Adding an object manually","1. Device","2. Vehicle","3. Additional information"],"x":"How to add an object You can add an object in the mobile app by scanning a QR code or entering its details manually. 1. Open the Objects tab 2. Tap the plus icon in the upper-right corner 3. Select an option: Scan QR code Add manually Adding an object using a QR code 1. Select Scan QR code 2. If prompted, allow the app to access the camera 3. Point the smartphone camera at the QR code on the device or generated in OneGPS. Make sure the entire code is inside the frame Once the QR code is recognized, the object is automatically added to the account currently open in the mobile app. Adding an object manually Manual object creation is available only to users with administrator permissions and consists of three steps. 1. Device Enter the connected device details: Device ID — the unique device identifier Device type — the device model Device tags — optional tags for grouping and search Tap Next. 2. Vehicle Enter the vehicle details: Vehicle number — the vehicle registration number Make Model Modification Vehicle type Color — determines the color of the object icon in the app Fields marked with an asterisk are required. Tap Next. 3. Additional information Enter any additional details: Group Template Time zone Initial mileage Initial engine hours Description The available fields in the vehicle profile depend on the enabled modules. For example, the RFID and Dealer ID fields are displayed when the corresponding modules are enabled for the account. Tap Add object. Once the object has been created successfully, it appears in the main list."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-objects","t":"How to work with objects","h":["How to work with objects","List of objects","Info tab","History tab","Date filter","Action menu","Object menu"],"x":"How to work with objects In this article, we'll explain how to work with the Objects section. You will learn: how to manage the list of objects how to get information about each object how to use the object menu List of objects In the Objects section, you will find a list of all monitoring objects. Here, you can see information about the status of each object and the time of the last connection. To find a specific object, use the Search field and enter the name or number of the object. Click the button to adjust the display settings for the list of objects. You can choose which objects to display and how to group them (e.g., by groups, geofences, or tags). The list provides a brief summary of each object, so you can quickly find out its current location, whether it's moving, and the amount of fuel and mileage. For example, for a vehicle, the summary includes: Object name Date and time of last bessage — when the last data from the vehicle's device was received Number of satellites — how reliably the location is determined Current fuel volume — in liters (e.g., 359.14 L) Total mileage — total distance traveled (e.g., 1,918,896.25 km) Engine hours/Time in operation — total engine working time (e.g., 34,526 hours, 16 minutes) Address of last position — country, region, city, and exact address of the vehicle's current location Current speed — current speed (e.g., 74 km/h) Time in current state — how long the object has been moving or stationary (e.g., \"Updated: 00 sec\" — just updated) By clicking on an object in the list, you'll be directed to theInfo and History tabs with detailed information. Info tab The Info tab displays all the basic information about the object: Location on the map Statistics : Data for the selected day — time in motion, distance traveled, max and average speed, fuel consumption, and average fuel usage per 100 km Sensors : Current sensor data and the time of their last update Additional information : Vehicle model, current status, last connection time, speed, and mileage The Show on map button opens the map with the current location of the object. The Route planningsection allows you to build a route: In OneGPS — the route will open directly in the app On another map — it will open an external mapping service History tab The History tab shows the exact path the object has taken during the selected period. At the top of the screen, a map is displayed showing the object’s location and its track for the selected day. Below the map, there is brief statistics for the selected day: total distance, max and average speed. Below that, you can select a date to see the corresponding map, statistics, and event list for that date. The detailed event list is displayed chronologically. An event includes: Start time Event type Duration For moving events, it shows the distance traveled and average or max speed Starting or ending address, or parking location If you click on an event, the corresponding route will appear on the map, which you can view in the player. Date filter You can choose specific dates to view the movement history for that period. Action menu Click the three dots to open additional features: Current track — shows the object’s current location and its recent movement. On the map, you can see where the vehicle is and the route it has taken Show on map — go back to displaying the object on the map Follow — track an object on the map Create link — get a token to allow external users to view the object’s location on the map Events — view notifications and events for an object, including the time, location, and event type. In the settings , you can select the objects whose events and notifications you want to view, choose the relevant types, and specify the date and time range. Tap a notification or event to view its location on the map. Tap a notification or event to view its location on the map. Video — opens the Video tab with preview images from connected cameras Locking — allows you to lock or unlock the engine (available when the Engine lock module is enabled) Arming the object Rename object — enter a new name and tap Save Points — view the object’s raw data points Object menu For each object, additional functions are available. Click the three dots next to the object to open the menu: Information — navigate to the Info tab for basic information about the object History — navigate to the History tab to see how the object moved during the selected period Current track — shows the object’s current location and its recent movement. On the map, you can see where the vehicle is and the route it has taken Show on map — display the object on the map in real-time Follow — track an object’s movement in real time. While tracking, the selected object is not grouped into a cluster with other objects and remains visible on the map at any zoom level. Create link — get a token for viewing the object on the map by external users Events — view events and notifications for the object Video — opens the Video tab with preview images from connected cameras Locking — allows you to lock or unlock the engine (available when the Engine lock module is enabled) Arming the object Rename object — enter a new name and click Save Points — view the object’s raw data points The menu may contain additional functions depending on the modules activated for your account."},{"s":"how-to-use-the-map","t":"How to use the map","h":["How to use the map","Zooming the map","Your location","Map settings","Enable traffic display","Choose map type","Choose displayed objects","Working with objects on the map","Information and sensors","Follow the object"],"x":"How to use the map After logging into the app, you will land on the Map section. The map shows all objects linked to your account. Zooming the map To zoom in or out on the map, use the zoom buttons — and . When you press them, the map will scale, and the central point will remain in place. Your location Tap the location icon to show your current location on the map. Map settings In the bottom-right corner, you'll find the map settings button . Tap it to access additional options. Here you can: Enable traffic display Slide the switch to the \"on\" position. The map will show road congestion using colors: Green — clear roads Yellow — moderate traffic Red — rraffic jams and heavy congestion Choose map type You can select from several map types to display spatial data. The map type is the base layer of the map used to display spatial data in the monitoring system. Choose displayed objects Tick the checkboxes for the objects you want to see on the map. Working with objects on the map All your objects and their current locations are displayed on the map. To open the object’s menu, click on its icon. The following actions will be available: Information and sensors Follow the object Information and sensors In the information section, several tabs are available. The Info tab shows all the main details about the object. Statistics block displays the data for the selected day: how much time the vehicle was in motion, how many kilometers it traveled, its maximum and average speed, fuel consumption for the day in liters, and the average fuel consumption per 100 km. Sensors block shows the current sensor readings and the time of their last update. Additional information shows the basic object parameters: model, current status, time of the last connection, speed, and mileage. TheShow on map button opens the map with the object’s current location. Below, the Get directions block allows you to immediately plan a route: In OneGPS — open the route on the app’s map On another map — open an external map service The Historytab shows how the object moved during the selected period. At the top of the screen, a map is displayed showing the object’s location and its track for the selected day. Below the map, there is a brief summary for the selected day: total mileage, maximum and average speed. Just below, you can use the date switcher — choose the required day, and the map, statistics, and event list will update accordingly. A detailed list of events for the selected day is displayed in chronological order. Each event includes: Start time Event type Duration For movement events: distance traveled and average or maximum speed Address of the start or end of the movement or parking location If you tap on an event, the corresponding route will appear on the map, which can be viewed in the player. Choose the desired dates to view the movement history for that period. Tap the three dots to open additional functions: Show on map — go back to displaying the object on the map Follow — track an object on the map Create link — get a token to allow external users to view the object’s location on the map Notifications — view events for the object: time, place, event type. Using notification settings , you can select objects for which events will be displayed, configure event types, and specify the period (dates and times) for which to view the list of occurred events. If you tap a specific notification, the event will appear on the map with a marker. Rename object — enter a new name and tap Save Follow the object When you choose theFollow feature, you will be able to watch the object’s movements in real-time."},{"s":"how-to-create-a-report","t":"How to create a report","h":["How to create a report","Additional parameters"],"x":"How to create a report In the Reports section, you can generate a report based on the selected objects and parameters. 1. Select an object In the Objects field, choose one or more objects for which you want to generate the report. 2. Choose the report type Specify the type of report you want to generate, such as: Mileage Time in motion and parking Fuel consumption Sensors data The available report types depend on the settings of your account. 3. Set the date range Select the start and end dates for the report. If necessary, specify the exact time (hours and minutes). The report will only be generated for the selected period. 4. Configure report breakdown In the Split field, choose how you want to split the report into intervals: By days — data will be broken down by each day By weeks — summary data for each week Do not split — the report will cover the entire period in one block 5. Choose grouping Define how to group the rows in the report: By dates — to analyze changes over time (each day or week as a separate row) By objects — to compare different objects (each object in a separate row) 6. Select sensor groups Choose one or more sensor groups for which you want to generate the report. 7. Download the report After configuring all parameters, click Download report. The application will generate the report, and you will be able to open it. Additional parameters Some reports may include additional settings. For example, in the parking report, you can specify the minimum and maximum parking duration (in seconds). If no additional parameters are set, the report will be generated with the default settings."},{"s":"how-to-view-notifications-and-events","t":"How to view notifications and events","h":["How to view notifications and events","How to view notifications","How to configure the notification list","How to view events","How to configure the event list"],"x":"How to view notifications and events The Events section is divided into two tabs: Notifications — alerts triggered by rules configured in advance in the web interface. A notification is generated when the conditions of a configured rule are met. For example, OneGPS generates a Speed Control notification if an object exceeds the specified speed for longer than the configured duration. Events — changes in the state of objects and sensors registered by the system, such as ignition activation, a door opening, prolonged idling, or a sensor trigger. How to view notifications Open the Events section and go to the Notificationstab. Notifications are grouped by date. Each card displays: the object associated with the notification the trigger time the notification name a description of what happened For example, a Speed control notification may include the configured speed limit, the maximum recorded speed, and the duration of the violation. Tap a card to open the map and view the location where the notification was triggered. If push notifications are enabled for the account, the app notifies you about new alerts on your mobile device. How to configure the notification list 1. Go to the Notifications tab 2. Tap the settings icon in the upper-right corner 3. Under Objects, select groups or individual object 4. Under Notification types, select one or more configured notifications, or leave All types selected. 5. Specify the start and end of the period 6. Tap Apply The list will display only notifications that match all selected conditions. To remove the specified conditions and restore the default settings, tap Clear. How to view events Go to the Events tab. It displays events registered by the system for objects and their equipment. Each card displays: the object the event time the event type a description For example, the list may include ignition activation, idling, a door opening, or a service reminder. Tap an event card to open the map and view the location where it was registered. How to configure the event list 1. Go to the Events tab 2. Tap the settings icon 3. Under Objects, select the objects whose events you want to display 4. Under Event types, select the required categories, such as Route events, Agro, Battery, or Complex events. To display events from every category, leave All types selected 5. Specify the start and end of the period 6. Tap Apply To reset the selected conditions, tap Clear. The available notification and event types depend on the account settings, enabled modules, and user permissions."},{"s":"user-menu-and-settings","t":"User menu and settings","h":["User menu and settings","User profile","Finance","How to add funds via Stripe","Address representation","Settings","Appearance"],"x":"User menu and settings The Menusection contains the user profile, financial information, and the main mobile app settings. Here you can switch between profiles, check the account balance, add funds, configure address display, change app settings, and select an interface theme. This article covers: User profile — switching between saved profiles and signing out Finance — viewing the organization’s account details and adding funds via Stripe Address display — selecting which address components appear in the app Settings — configuring the language, object update interval, map options, and layer visibility Appearance — selecting the light, dark, or system theme The available tabs depend on the enabled modules and the user’s permissions. The menu may also include Geofences, Drivers, Inspections, Dashboard, AI Support, Commands, and Video. These features are described in separate articles. User profile In the User profile block, your name or username is displayed. If you want to switch to another account, tap on your username. A list of profiles you've used before will appear. If you've selected the Remember meoption for these accounts, you can easily switch to them without re-entering your login details. If you wish to log out of the current account, go to the Profilestab and tap the Log out button. Finance The Finance tab displays information about the organization’s account: Organization — the organization name Account Number — the account identifier in the system Deposit — the deposited amount Balance — the current account balance Available Balance — the amount available for use SMS Balance — the number of available SMS messages How to add funds via Stripe Stripe is a payment service that supports secure payments by bank card and other available payment methods. If Stripe payments are enabled for your account, you can add funds through the mobile app: 1. Open the Financetab 2. Enter the amount in the Pay with Stripe section 3. Tap Pay 4. On the Stripe page, select a payment method and confirm the payment 5. After the payment has been processed successfully, return to the app and check the balance Address representation In this tab, you can configure which parts of the address will be displayed in the system — for example, in object cards, reports, or history. Go to the Address representation tab and use the toggles to select the address components you want to see. Once you're done, tap the Save button at the bottom of the screen. You can enable or disable the following elements: House number Street City Postal code Region Country Suburb Info Geofences Hide address if geofence exists — when enabled, the system will display only the geofence name if the object is located inside a geofence, instead of the full address. Reverse address values — when enabled, the address will be displayed in reverse order, starting from the country and ending with the house number. Settings In the Settings tab, you can configure how the app works and appears, including map settings and data refresh intervals. Main settings: Language — сhoose the language of the app interface Update interval for objects — set how often the app should update the location data for objects (e.g., every 20 seconds) High location accuracy — enable this option to use more precise methods for determining the location Show Zoom button — if enabled, a zoom button will appear on the map for easy scaling Group objects on the map — еnable this option to group nearby objects into clusters for easier viewing Show geofence names — еnable this function to display geofence names on the map Layer visibility: Visibility of KML layers — enable or disable the display of KML/KMZ layers that were uploaded in the web version (e.g., additional boundaries or objects) on the map Visibility of tile layers — toggle this option to show or hide additional map layers Account management: At the bottom of the screen, you will find the option to delete your account. However, to fully delete your account, you will need to contact technical support. Appearance In the Appearance section, you can choose how the app interface looks: Light theme — the app will be displayed in light colors Dark theme — the app will use dark colors. This theme is often preferred for night-time use or low-light environments The appearance will match your device’s theme if you select System default."},{"s":"how-to-view-dashboards","t":"How to view dashboards","h":["How to view dashboards","How to customize dashboard","Chart customization"],"x":"How to view dashboards In the Dashboardtab, you can visually review the key performance indicators of your vehicles. Here you can see overall statistics and detailed graphs for various parameters. Statistics are displayed for the last 24 hours. At the top of the screen, there are information blocks with the main indicators, such as: Idle time Mileage Number of vehicles Operating time Below are charts that help you visually assess the situation, for example: “Vehicle status”, “Connection”, “Mileage”, “Geofences”, and others. The set of available graphs depends on the modules you have enabled. If you have the PTM module, “Geofences”, and other modules connected, the corresponding graphs will automatically appear in Dashboard. How to customize dashboard You can independently choose which information blocks and graphs you want to see: 1. Tap the settings button 2. In the Vehicles Infoblocks section, check the indicators you need 3. In the Charts section, select the charts that are important to you All graphs you created in the web version (including those made using templates) are also available in the mobile app Chart customization Some charts allow additional configuration. For example, in the Mileage chart, you can set a limit (e.g., 10 000 km). After setting the limit, the chart will show: How many vehicles have traveled less than 10 000 km How many vehicles have traveled more than 10 000 km You can set any limit value that helps you better understand the statistics."},{"s":"how-to-use-ai-support","t":"How to use AI Support","h":["How to use AI Support","How to create a chat","Chat history"],"x":"How to use AI Support AI Supporthelps you work with OneGPS by showing you where to find a feature and explaining the required steps. For example, you can ask how to create a report, configure an object, create a geofence, or connect a device. Responses are generated automatically and may include links to the official documentation. How to create a chat 1. Go to Menu 2. Open AI Support 3. Tap the plus icon in the upper-right corner 4. Enter your question in the Ask a question field 5. Tap the send button 6. Wait for the AI assistant to respond If the response is long, scroll through the chat to read it in full. You can ask follow-up questions in the same chat. Chat history All your previous chats are displayed in AI Support. A chat title is generated based on its topic or creation date. Tap a chat to return to the conversation, view previous responses, or continue asking questions. To start a conversation on a different topic, tap the plus icon and create a new chat."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-geofences-1","t":"How to work with geofences","h":["How to work with geofences","Geofences on the map","Displaying geofences on the map","Map settings","How to create a geofence"],"x":"How to work with geofences When the Geofences module is enabled in your account, you can manage geofences within the mobile app. A Geofence is a defined area on the map that you create to track the movement of objects inside or outside of it. Geofences on the map Once the Geofences module is enabled, a new Geofences tab will appear in the Menu. Here you can select which geofences to display on the map. Simply check the boxes next to the geofences you want to show. Displaying geofences on the map To view geofences on the map, go to the Map section. You will see all the selected geofences displayed on the map. This will allow you to track the movement of objects within these areas. Map settings You can enable or disable geofence visibility directly in the Map settings, giving you control over which areas are visible during tracking. How to create a geofence 1. Tap Add 2. Specify the parameters: Geofence name Group it belongs to (or create a new group) 3. Set the geofence shape: Circle — tap the map and drag to set the radius (the radius can be adjusted by dragging the boundary) Polygon — mark points on the map one by one to define the boundaries 4. Choose the geofence color If needed, tap Clear to discard changes. 5. Tap Save — the geofence will appear in the list"},{"s":"how-to-work-with-drivers","t":"How to work with drivers","h":["How to work with drivers","Viewing the rivers List","Driver profile information"],"x":"How to work with drivers When the Drivers module is enabled in your account, you can manage information about drivers within the mobile app. Viewing the rivers List 1. Open the Menu in the app 2. You will find a new Drivers tab. Tap on it to see the list of all drivers associated with your account Driver profile information When you select a driver from the list, you will be taken to their profile, which displays the following information: Driver's name and photo (if uploaded) Driver's group License status or license number Email address Additional information Driver's contact number Vehicles assigned to the driver To check which drivers are assigned to a particular object: 1. Go to the Objects section in the app 2. Tap on the object’s menu to access additional actions for the object 3. Select the Driverstab to see the drivers linked to that specific object"},{"s":"how-to-work-with-inspections","t":"How to work with inspections","h":["How to work with inspections","Viewing inspections","How to conduct an inspection"],"x":"How to work with inspections When the Service module is enabled in your account, the mobile app gives you the ability to manage Inspections — a tool for checking the condition of vehicles. With inspections, you can: perform inspections on vehicles record what was checked and the condition of the vehicle track the history of all inspections Viewing inspections 1. In the Menu section, you will find a new Inspections tab. Tap it to see all completed inspections for your vehicles 2. You can use the Search field to find a specific inspection. Additionally, you can apply filters : By period — select the date range of inspections By object — choose the vehicles for which the inspections were conducted By inspection type — select the type of inspection you’re looking for 3. Tap on the inspection in the list to open the inspection form with questions and answers. To view all questions and results, tap the expand icon . To exit the inspection, tap Exit the inspection How to conduct an inspection 1. Tap the icon in the top right corner of the screen 2. Select the vehicle you want to inspect. You can search for the vehicle using the Search field or apply filters based on the vehicle model or year of manufacture 3. Choose the inspection type In the web version, inspection forms with questions are created. These forms can be filled out in the mobile app. 4. Answer the questions Answer all the questions in the inspection form. If needed, attach photos to confirm the condition of the vehicle. 5. Add your signature If required, leave your signature in the provided field. If the signature doesn’t work on the first try, use the eraser icon to clear it and try again. 6. Complete the inspection Once you've answered all the questions and signed, tap Next. The inspection will be completed and saved."},{"s":"how-to-view-video-1","t":"How to view video","h":["How to view video","Viewing live video stream","Video playback control","Viewing video history"],"x":"How to view video If your account has the Video module enabled and video devices are installed on your objects, you can view video footage recorded by these devices. Viewing live video stream 1. In the Objectssection, select the object that has a connected camera 2. Open the object’s menu and go to the Videotab 3. In this tab, you will see previews of all connected cameras 4. Click on the desired camera to open the video player Video playback control In the video player, you can: Switch between cameras using the arrows Turn the camera sound on/off Take screenshots Maximize the video to full screen Viewing video history 1. To view the video history, tap the history icon in the upper right corner of the video player 2. In the History section tap the icon and select a date and time to filter the recordings. Then tap Apply In the video history player, the date and the current time of the frame are displayed. You can quickly navigate to a specific moment in the recording by moving the slider. While viewing video history, you can: Take screenshots Adjust the playback speed Toggle sound on/off Maximize the video to full screen"},{"s":"how-to-work-with-commands","t":"How to work with commands","h":["How to work with commands","Sending a command to an object","Command history"],"x":"How to work with commands With the mobile application, you can send commands to objects to manage them remotely. Sending a command to an object 1. Go to the Objects section and select the object to which you want to send a command 2. Tap on the object’s menu and choose the Commands tab 3. In the Commands tab, you will see a list of available commands for the selected object, such as sending an SMS command to the device, requesting settings, etc. Tap on the desired command to send it to the device. Command history In the Command history block, you can see all previously sent commands, their statuses, and details: Command name Status Command type User who sent the command Date of command sending Last status update time Vehicle to which the command was applied If you need to cancel a command, tap and Cancel command. To view the command history for a specific period: 1. Use the filters by tapping on the filter icon 2. Select the date and time to sort commands by the desired period 3. Tap Apply to filter the history"},{"s":"how-to-view-raw-points","t":"How to view raw points","h":["How to view raw points","How to view raw points","Viewing points on a chart","Customizing point field display"],"x":"How to view raw points Raw points are unprocessed data that the device sends directly to the system in real time. They show sensor values exactly as they are received, without any filtering or processing. How to view raw points 1. Open the Object menu 2. Go to the Points tab In this tab, you will see the data currently being sent by the device. To view the full set of raw data: click Raw data —if needed, you can copy the data by clicking Copy Viewing points on a chart To display the data on a chart: 1. Switch to Chart mode 2. Select one or more parameters to display on the chart by enabling the toggle next to each parameter. The chart will show the values of the selected parameters. When you hover over a specific point, a tooltip will appear with the exact time and value at that moment. Customizing point field display In the Points tab, you can customize how the data is displayed: select which parameters to show for each GPS point filter points by a specific time range"},{"s":"how-to-arm-an-object","t":"How to arm an object","h":["How to arm an object","How to configure command templates","How to create a command template","Examples of commands"],"x":"How to arm an object To arm an object, a security sensor must be configured for it. Once the sensor is configured, the Arming option will appear in the object's menu. Additionally, for this feature to work, command templates named arm and disarm must be created in the system. Configure them in advance in the web interface, and the following commands will become available: Arm — arm the object Disarm — disarm the object When the security status changes, a corresponding icon will appear next to the object in the list: the object is armed the object is disarmed How to configure command templates 1. Open the OneGPS user interface 2. Go to the Main section 3. Click the Commands icon A window will open where you can create command templates. How to create a command template 1. Click Add command 2. Select the vehicle for which the command is being created. 3. Specify the parameters : Command type — GRPS Command method — SMS_VIA_GPRS 4. In the SMS field, enter the command text, for example: setdigout 0 — disarm the vehicle 5. If required, check the HEX box if the command must be sent in HEX format 6. Click Save as template and give it a name Template names must be exactly: arm — set vehicle security disarm — disarm vehicle Names must be lowercase. The app identifies and executes commands based on these exact names. Examples of commands Arm (Set security) — arm Teltonika setdigout 1 Adm 333 BLEOUTPUT 0 Disarm — disarm Teltonika setdigout 0 Adm 333 BLEOUTPUT 1"},{"s":"telegram-bot","t":"Telegram bot","h":["Telegram bot"],"x":"Telegram bot The Telegram bot is designed to let you work with the vehicles in your account, receive information about your objects, view their location, and use your phone as a GPS tracker."},{"s":"how-to-sonnect-and-use-the-chat-bot","t":"How to сonnect and use the chat bot","h":["How to сonnect and use the chat bot","How to connect to the chat bot","How to use the bot menu","Help","Advanced Menu","Change language","Bot version","How to get the list of vehicles"],"x":"How to сonnect and use the chat bot In this guide, you will learn: how to connect and log in to the chat bot how to use the menu and main functions how to view your list of vehicles How to connect to the chat bot 1. Open Telegram and find the bot @PilotGpsBot 2. Press Start 3. Select Start authorization 4. Enter the login and password for your account If you make a mistake, tap Reset login and start again. How to use the bot menu Tap Menu — the main functions will appear: Help When you press Help, the bot will send you a short welcome message. It explains what the bot can do: show a list of your vehicles provide information about each vehicle send you your unique ID, which is required to use the tracker Advanced Menu When you open the Advanced Menu, you will see additional options: Vehicles — shows the full list of your vehicles Get ID — the bot sends your unique ID. ID is the number of your virtual GPS tracker. It works just like a real tracker installed in a vehicle, but the data is sent through Telegram from your phone. Get user — shows the account you are currently logged in with Logout — logs you out of the bot Change language Tap this button to switch the bot interface to another language. Bot version Shows the current bot version. How to get the list of vehicles 1. Make sure you are logged in 2. Open the Advanced Menu 3. Select Vehicles — the list of vehicles in your account will appear If you select a specific vehicle, the bot will show: a summary of the object a map link to view its location"},{"s":"using-telegram-as-a-gps-tracker","t":"Using Telegram as a GPS tracker","h":["Using Telegram as a GPS tracker","How to set up the Telegram tracker","1. Get Your ID","2. Create an object in OneGPS","3. Enable location sharing in Telegram"],"x":"Using Telegram as a GPS tracker The Telegram bot can work as a virtual GPS tracker. The bot generates a special ID, which acts as a tracker number. When you enable location sharing in Telegram, the monitoring system receives your phone’s coordinates using this ID — just like from a regular GPS device. How to set up the Telegram tracker 1. Get Your ID 1) Log in to the chat bot 2) Open Advanced Menu 3) Tap Get ID This ID will be used as the tracker number. 2. Create an object in OneGPS 1. In your web account, create a new object 2. Open the object card → Main tab 3. Enter the object name or vehicle number 4. In the Device ID field, paste the ID you received from the bot 5. In Device type, select any available type (the choice does not affect operation) 3. Enable location sharing in Telegram 1. Open the chat with the bot 2. Tap the paperclip icon 3. Select Location 4. Allow location access on your phone 5. TapShare my live location 6. Select how long you want to share your location 7. Tap Share Now your phone is working as a tracker. You will see a message in the chat with your current position. It will contain a location icon — tap it to open the map in Telegram and view your exact point. In the OneGPS system, the object will move together with you — its coordinates will update based on your phone’s geolocation."},{"s":"how-to-create-your-own-telegram-bot","t":"How to create your own Telegram bot","h":["How to create your own Telegram bot"],"x":"How to create your own Telegram bot To create your own Telegram bot, you will need the official @BotFather service — the main Telegram bot used to create and manage all other bots. 1. Open the link: https://t.me/BotFather 2. Press Start 3. Enter the command: /newbot 4. Create a name for your bot BotFather will ask you to provide a display name — this is the name people will see in Telegram. 5. Create a username for the bot — its unique Telegram address Important: the username must end with _bot. Examples: mycompany_bot, skyelectronics_helper_bot 6. Get the token After BotFather creates your bot, it will send you a message with the bot details. You will see: Token to access the HTTP API — your bot’s token Username — your bot’s Telegram username 7. Send the token and username to the OneGPS technical support team They are required to connect your bot to the OneGPS system. Your account in the system must be active, otherwise the bot will not be able to send or receive data."},{"s":"how-to-send-statuses-and-media-files-via-the-telegram","t":"How to send statuses and media files via the Telegram","h":["How to send statuses and media files via the Telegram","Configuring Telegram statuses","How to send a status","Signing in to the Telegram bot","Sending a status in the bot","How to send a photo or video","How to view submitted media files"],"x":"How to send statuses and media files via the Telegram The Telegram bot allows employees to send information to OneGPS directly from a mobile device. An employee can select their current status and send a photo or video from the work site. For example, an employee can send the statuses Departed and Started work in sequence. After completing the work, they can select Work completed and attach a photo of the result. The submitted media files can then be found and viewed in the OneGPS web interface. Configuring Telegram statuses Statuses are configured separately for each OneGPS account. Employees see only the statuses added by the master user in the settings of the corresponding account. A user with master permissions must configure the statuses: 1. Sign in to the OneGPS web interface 2. Open Account settings 3. Go to the Telegram statusestab 4. Click + Add 5. Enter the status name 6. Click OK The new status will appear in the list. The value in the Order column determines its position among the other statuses. To delete a status, select it in the list and click Delete. Once the account is connected to the Telegram bot, the configured statuses become available to authorized users. How to send a status Signing in to the Telegram bot Before starting, the employee must connect the Telegram bot to their OneGPS account: 1. Open the OneGPS Telegram bot 2. Start the bot using the /start command 3. Enter the OneGPS account login 4. Enter the password 5. Wait for the successful authorization message Sign in using the account for which the master user configured the required statuses. Sending a status in the bot 1. Tap Status in the bot menu 2. Select the required status from the list 3. Wait for the Status sentmessage This message confirms that the bot has accepted the selected status. You can then continue using the bot: select another status when the work stage changes or send a photo or video. How to send a photo or video The Telegram bot can be used to send materials that confirm completed work or show the condition of a site or asset. For example, you can send a photo or video of the work site, equipment before maintenance, completed work, or delivered cargo. To send a file: 1. Open the OneGPS Telegram bot 2. Tap the attachment icon 3. Select a photo or video on your mobile device 4. Send the file 5. Wait for the Media savedmessage This message confirms that the file has been received and saved in OneGPS. How to view submitted media files When a user submits a media file for the first time, OneGPS creates a separate object bearing the user’s Telegram name in the object list. All subsequent photos and videos from that user are saved under the same object. 1. Open the Online tab in the OneGPS web interface 2. Find the object bearing the Telegram user’s name in the object tree 3. Right-click the object 4. Select Media 5. Specify the period for which you want to display the files 6. Find the required entry 7. Click the photo or video icon to open the file To save a file to your computer, click the download icon next to the corresponding entry. The Mediatab displays the photos and videos submitted by the user through the Telegram bot, along with the date and time they were received"},{"s":"pilot-driver","t":"OneGPS. Driver","h":["OneGPS. Driver","How PTM works for drivers"],"x":"OneGPS. Driver OneGPS. Driver is a mobile app for drivers, couriers, and delivery personnel. It works in conjunction with OneGPS Task Manager (PTM) and helps manage tasks and routes. Tasks for drivers are created in the PTM web version and then transferred to the OneGPS. Driver mobile app, where drivers can view and complete them. With OneGPS. Driver, drivers can complete various types of tasks, such as: Cargo delivery: Handling tasks related to the delivery of goods, materials, or construction supplies. For example, delivering products, collecting and removing waste, or delivering concrete Taxi and tourism: For taxi drivers and drivers working with tourists, PTM allows managing transportation orders Waste collection: Drivers can follow garbage collection routes, marking each stop along the way as the task is completed How PTM works for drivers 1. The driver receives a task with specified route points that need to be visited 2. During the task, the driver marks each route point, confirming the completion of deliveries or other tasks 3. After all route points are visited, the driver marks the task as completed and, if necessary, submits a report on its completion"},{"s":"how-to-install-and-log-into-the-pilot-driver-","t":"How to install and log into the OneGPS. Driver","h":["How to install and log into the OneGPS. Driver","How to install the OneGPS. Driver app","How to log into the app"],"x":"How to install and log into the OneGPS. Driver How to install the OneGPS. Driver app 1. Open the Google Play app (for Android) or App Store (for iOS) on your smartphone 2. In the search bar, type OneGPS Driver or use the links below to go directly to the app store: 3. Tap Install and wait for the download and installation to complete 4. Once the installation is finished, tap Open, or find the OneGPS. Driver icon on your phone’s home screen and launch the app How to log into the app After launching the app, on the login screen: 1. Enter your login If needed, leave the Remember me option enabled so the app can remember your credentials. 2. Enter your password 3. Tap Next Once logged in, you will be taken to the main screen, where your new tasks are displayed."},{"s":"new-tasks","t":"New tasks","h":["New tasks","Sorting tasks","Viewing a task","Task information","Waybill"],"x":"New tasks The New tab displays tasks that have been assigned to you and are waiting to be accepted. Until you tap Accept task, the task will remain in this tab. Each task is displayed as a card showing the following information: task name task number scheduled start date and time scheduled end date and time estimated duration To open a task, tap its card. Sorting tasks If the list contains many tasks, you can change the sorting order. To do this: 1. Open the New tab 2. Tap the list settings icon in the upper-right corner 3. In the List settings window, select a sorting option: Newest first — newer tasks are displayed at the top of the list Oldest first — older tasks are displayed at the top of the list 4. Tap Apply The list will be automatically updated according to the selected sorting option. Viewing a task To open a task, tap its card in the list. The task card will open and display detailed information. The top section shows: start date and time; end date and time. Below is the Accept task button, which moves the task into active work. Task information The Task information section displays the main details of the trip. The available fields depend on the task type, but typically include: task name customer or contractor information vehicle, cargo, and other related information Waybill Below is the Waybill section, which displays the route and all scheduled points. For each point, the following information is shown: address point number To view detailed information about a point, tap it in the Waybill. The information panel displays: full address current point status Actions button The following options are available through the Actions button: Copy address — copy the address to the clipboard for use in a navigation app or message Show on map — open a map showing the selected point location To open the route map, tap the map icon in the upper-right corner of the task card. For more information about working with maps, see the Route map article."},{"s":"how-to-accept-a-new-task","t":"How to accept a new task","h":["How to accept a new task"],"x":"How to accept a new task When a new task is assigned to you, it appears in the New tab. To accept a task: 1. Open the Newtab from the bottom navigation menu 2. Find the required task in the list and tap its card 3. Review the task details, including the start and end time, route, point addresses, and other relevant information. 4. If you are ready to begin, tap Accept task. Once accepted, the task is automatically moved to the Active tab, where you can track its progress and work with route points."},{"s":"active-tasks","t":"Active tasks","h":["Active tasks","Sorting tasks","Viewing a task","Working with route points","Task progress","Task menu","Completing a task"],"x":"Active tasks The Active tab displays tasks that you have already accepted and are currently working on. Here, you can track task progress, work with route points, and complete tasks. Each task is displayed as a card containing the following information: task name and number completion progress — the number of completed points out of the total number of points scheduled start and end time a progress indicator showing how much of the route has been completed Sorting tasks If the list contains many tasks, you can change the sorting order. To do this: 1. Open the Active tab 2. Tap the list settings icon in the upper-right corner 3. In the List settings window, select a sorting option: Newest first — newer tasks are displayed at the top of the list Oldest first — older tasks are displayed at the top of the list 4. Tap Apply The list will be automatically updated according to the selected sorting option. Viewing a task To open a task, tap its card. The task card displays: scheduled start and end time task type additional information (contact details, cargo information, and other data) the route with the list of points If the task has been accepted, tap Start task to begin working on it. Working with route points After starting a task, the Next point section appears on the screen, showing the address of the next location to visit. The following actions are available for the selected point: Route — build a route in the navigation application installed on your device Arrived — mark your arrival at the point After tapping Arrived, the point becomes active. The following additional actions become available: add a comment complete the point open the More actions menu Comments can be used to record additional information about completed work, deliveries, or other events. The following options are available for the current point: Copy address — copy the address to the clipboard Show on map — view the point location on a map Postpone point — temporarily postpone the point The postpone feature may be useful in the following situations: the recipient is temporarily unavailable; the point cannot be completed due to external circumstances; another point needs to be visited first. After postponing a point, its status changes to Postponed, and the application suggests proceeding to the next point on the route. You can return to the postponed point later. Task progress The current task progress is displayed: in the list of active tasks in the task card under the Progress section Task menu The task menu button is located in the upper-right corner of the task card. The following actions are available: Complete task — finish the task Show full route — open a map displaying all route points For more information about working with maps, see the Route map article. Completing a task After all required work has been completed and all route points have been processed, tap Complete task. Once completed, the task is automatically moved to the Completed tab, where it remains available for review."},{"s":"how-to-somplete-a-report","t":"How to сomplete a report","h":["How to сomplete a report","Opening a report","Filling out a report","Adding photos","Completing a report"],"x":"How to сomplete a report If a report has been configured for a task or route point, the Fill Report button will be available in the application. A report can be assigned to: a specific route point the entire task This depends on the settings configured by the operator in the web version of the system. The number and types of fields in a report may vary. The information that must be provided is determined by the report form configuration. Opening a report 1. Open an active task 2. If the report is assigned to a route point, navigate to the required point and tap Arrived 3. Tap Fill report The report form will open. Fields marked with an asterisk (*) are required. You will not be able to complete the report until all required fields have been filled in. Filling out a report Depending on the report configuration, the form may contain different types of fields, such as: text fields; numeric values; selection lists; switches and checkboxes; comment and signature fields; photos and file attachments. Fill in all required information according to your organization's requirements. Adding photos If the form includes photo documentation, tap Add photo. In the window that opens, select one of the following options: Open camera — take a photo using your device's camera Choose from gallery — attach an existing image from your device You can add multiple photos if required. Completing a report 1. Make sure all required fields have been completed 2. Verify that all entered information and attachments are correct 3. Tap Complete at the bottom of the screen After the report is successfully completed, it will be saved and submitted to the system."},{"s":"completed-tasks","t":"Completed tasks","h":["Completed tasks","Sorting tasks","Viewing a completed task","Task information","Waybill","Point actions","Route map"],"x":"Completed tasks The Completed tab displays all tasks that have been finished. Here, you can review previously completed tasks and their details. Each task is displayed as a card showing the following information: task name task number start date and time end date and time actual completion time Sorting tasks To change the order in which completed tasks are displayed: 1. Open the Completed tab 2. Tap the list settings icon in the upper-right corner 3. In the List settings window, select a sorting option: Newest First — recently completed tasks are displayed at the top of the list. Oldest First — tasks completed earlier are displayed at the top of the list. 4. Tap Apply The list will be updated automatically. Viewing a completed task To view task details, tap the required task card in the list. The task card opens and displays information about the route, visited points, and completion results. Task information The top section of the task card displays: start date and time end date and time Below is the Task Information section. Depending on the system configuration, it may contain: task name company or customer name contact phone number office address task description cargo information other additional details Waybill The Waybillsection displays all points that were included in the task. For each point, the following information is shown: point name and address point number completion status date and time of the visit To view details about a point , tap it in the list. The information panel displays: full address point status start time at the point completion time at the point Point actions The following actions are available for a point: Copy address — copy the address to the clipboard Show on map — open the point location on the map Route map To view the route, tap the map icon in the upper-right corner of the task card. The map displays the route and all task points. For more information about working with maps, map settings, and available controls, see the Route map article."},{"s":"route-map","t":"Route map","h":["Route map","Map settings","Map layer","Show point","Map controls"],"x":"Route map The route map is available from the task card of any new, active, or completed task. To open the map, tap the map icon in the upper-right corner of the task card. The map displays: a purple line representing the route numbered markers representing route points A Route sheet is also displayed at the bottom of the screen, allowing you to view the route on the map and the list of points at the same time. Map settings To open the map settings, tap the settings icon in the upper-right corner of the map screen. Map layer This section allows you to select the map provider and map type. Available options may include: OpenStreetMap 2GIS Google Map Google Satellite Yandex Map satellite maps and other available map layers Select the map view that is most convenient for you. Show point The Show pointsswitch controls whether route points are displayed on the map. Enabled — route point markers are displayed on the map Disabled — route point markers are hidden, and only the route line remains visible Map controls The following controls are available on the map: Zoom in / Zoom out — adjust the map scale Current location — display your current location on the map"},{"s":"account-settings-2","t":"Account settings","h":["Account settings","Opening settings","Signing out","Changing the application language","Changing the appearance","Deleting an account"],"x":"Account settings The Settings tab allows you to view your account information, change the application language and appearance, sign out of your account, or delete it. Opening settings 1. Open the application 2. Tap the Settings icon in the bottom navigation menu The following information and options are available on the screen: User name Email address Language section Appearance section Delete account option Sign-out button in the upper-right corner of the screen Signing out To sign out of your account: 1. Open the Settings section 2. Tap the sign-out button in the upper-right corner of the screen 3. Confirm the action After signing out, the login screen will be displayed. To continue using the application, sign in again. Changing the application language To change the interface language: 1. Open the Settings section 2. Tap Language 3. Select the desired language from the list 4. Tap Change language The interface language will be updated after confirmation. Changing the appearance To change the application appearance: 1. Open theSettingssection 2. Tap Theme 3. Select one of the available options: Light Dark Follow system — the application will follow the appearance settings of your device The selected appearance is applied immediately. Deleting an account To delete your account: 1. Open the Settings section 2. Tap Delete account 3. In the confirmation dialog, select: Cancel — to keep your account Delete — to confirm account deletion"},{"s":"pilot-guarda","t":"OneGPS Guarda","h":["OneGPS Guarda","Control methods"],"x":"OneGPS Guarda OneGPS Guarda is a vehicle security and monitoring application. It helps you track vehicle status, manage access, and monitor its movements. The app is suitable for both personal use and fleet management. With the application, you can: Monitor vehicle status View the current status, battery level, events, and other parameters Manage access Lock and unlock the vehicle without using physical keys Track movements View the current location and trip history Control methods Access to the vehicle is available via: GSM (mobile network) — at any distance Bluetooth — when you are near the vehicle"},{"s":"how-to-install-and-log-into-the-app-2","t":"How to install and log into the app","h":["How to install and log into the app","How to install the app","How to sign in","Login settings","Registration via QR code","New user registration"],"x":"How to install and log into the app How to install the app 1. Open Google Play or the App Store on your smartphone 2. In the search bar, enter OneGPS Guarda or follow the link to the app store: 3. Install the application 4. After installation, tap Open or find the OneGPS Guarda icon on your screen and launch the app How to sign in After launching the app, on the login screen: 1. Enter your account login and password 2. If needed, enable Remember me so the app saves your credentials 3. Tap Sign in After signing in, the My cars tab will open. There you can select a vehicle and view its summary status. Login settings On the login screen, tap the icon in the top-right corner to open Login settings. Here you can change the server: Enter the required server address Tap Accept to save changes Registration via QR code The app allows registration using a device QR code that already contains object data. 1. On the login screen, tap Scan QR code 2. Point your smartphone camera at the QR code (on the device or in the OneGPS system). Make sure the code fits within the frame — it will be scanned automatically 3. Choose an action: Sign in — if you already have a OneGPS account. The device will be automatically linked to your existing account Sign up — if you don’t have an account. The app will prompt you to create one, and the device will be added to the new account New user registration 1. Fill in the required details: Email Phone number Password Confirm password Tap Continue 2. Confirm your email A verification code will be sent to your email. Enter the code on the confirmation screen. If you don’t receive the email, you can request a new code after the timer expires. Tap Confirm. After successful confirmation, you will be automatically signed in, and the device scanned via QR code will be linked to your account."},{"s":"app-menu","t":"App menu","h":["App menu","Car condition","My cars","My beacons","History","Video","Geofences","Reports","Alerts","FAQ","Settings","Theme","Log out"],"x":"App menu To open the app menu, tap the menu icon in the top left corner on any tab. After opening the menu, you will see: your username the selected vehicle (its current status is displayed in the app) The menu provides access to the main sections of the app: Car condition My cars My beacons History Video Geofences Reports Alerts FAQ Settings Below is a detailed description of each section and its features. Car condition The Car condition tab helps you quickly understand the current condition of your vehicle. The screen displays: vehicle name or number current location address status — moving or parked time in current state security status — armed or disarmed lock control button — to remotely lock or unlock the vehicle Bluetooth icon — indicates availability of Bluetooth control To view the vehicle on the map, tap the map icon in the Car condition tab. A map screen with the current location will open. To change the map view, tap the map layer icon . In the Map layer window, select the preferred display option. You can also enable traffic display to see road conditions near the vehicle. My cars The My cars tab shows all vehicles available to you. To view a vehicle tap it in the list — the status screen will open. How to add a vehicle 1. Tap Add in the top right corner 2. Scan the QR code: from the device or generated in the OneGPS system 3. The car will be added to your account automatically My beacons In the My beacons tab, you can view and manage Bluetooth beacons connected to the device. Available for Teltonika devices. Beacons are used for: driver identification access control automation scenarios (for example, automatic disarm when approaching) Each beacon is a separate device with a unique ID. What you see in the list: beacon name ID status (active / inactive) Tap a beacon to open actions: Enable beacon broadcast — when the beacon is detected, the system can automatically perform an action (e.g. disarm the vehicle) Rename beacon — opens a window to edit the name The Update button updates the list from the device. Beacons are configured on the device side (e.g. Teltonika). To appear in the app: they must be configured and registered on the device the device must send the data to the OneGPS system Setup guide for Teltonika devices: History The History tab shows vehicle movement history. You can: view routes analyze trips check events for a selected period Video In the Video tab, you can view recordings from installed video devices. Geofences In the Geofences tab you can: create geofences edit them set notifications for entry and exit Reports In the Reportstab, you can generate reports based on selected vehicles and parameters. Alerts This section displays events and alerts. Each item shows: vehicle or object event type or message date and time event location To view an event tap it — the map with event details will open. To filter notifications: 1. Tap the filter icon 2. Select a period 3. Tap Apply Only matching notifications will remain in the list. FAQ Link to the support website with: frequently asked questions feedback form Settings In the Settings section, you can configure the app and manage access. Language — tap the dropdown and select your preferred language. Notifications Available options: Show address — includes the event address in notifications Sound notifications — enables sound alerts Users — manage access by adding users and setting permissions. How to add a user: 1. Go to Users 2. Tap Add 3. Enter: Email phone number name password 4. Select: accessible objects role (Administrator or User) 5. Tap Save Beacon type Select the Bluetooth beacon type used for vehicle communication: iBeacon — Apple technology using Bluetooth Low Energy, transmits UUID, Major, Minor Eddystone — open BLE format transmitting ID, URL, and telemetry PIN Code — you can set a PIN code for login: 1. Enter a new code 2. Confirm it 3. Tap Save Vehicle settings Select a vehicle to configure: change vehicle image set notifications for events: — no connection with vehicle — movement with ignition off — arm/disarm events — low battery For each event, choose: Push — mobile notification Email — email notification Theme Use the switch at the bottom to change the app theme. Log out Tap Log outto sign out of your account."},{"s":"how-to-view-history-2","t":"How to view history","h":["How to view history","Viewing a trip route","Events in history","History filters"],"x":"How to view history The History tab shows information about your vehicle’s movements and events over a selected period. Here you can see where your vehicle has been, how it moved, and what events occurred. Viewing a trip route To open a trip route: 1. Select the desired movement track from the list 2. Tap the route display icon The route will then appear on the map with detailed information. Use the route player to view the movement — it shows how the vehicle traveled along the route. Events in history In addition to routes, the history shows events. Tap an event in the list to see details — a point with information will appear on the map. Examples of events: Parking — the vehicle was stationary Alarm — a sensor or alert was triggered Arming / Disarming — the vehicle was armed or disarmed History filters Filters help you quickly find the events you need. To set a filter: 1. Open the filter settings 2. Select the desired parameters: Event type — for example, route, parking, alarm Time period 3. After configuring the filter, tap Apply Only the events matching the selected parameters will remain in the list."},{"s":"how-to-view-video-2","t":"How to view video","h":["How to view video","Viewing live video","Viewing video history","Downloading video"],"x":"How to view video If video devices are installed in the vehicle, you can view the footage they record. Viewing live video Go to the Video tab in the app menu — the video players for the connected cameras will be displayed. In the video player, you can: Pause and play the video Turn camera audio on or off Take screenshots Switch to full-screen mode Viewing video history 1. In the player, tap the icon in the top right corner 2. A list of recordings will appear in chronological order 3. To open a recording, tap the desired entry or the play button next to it While viewing a recording, the following functions are available: Play / Pause — start or pause playback Stop — end playback Seek — move along the recording Screenshot — capture a frame from the video Audio — adjust the volume (if the recording has sound) Playback speed — slow down or speed up the video Full-screen mode — expand the player to full screen Downloading video Tap the Download button next to the desired recording in the history list. Filtering recordings 1. Tap the Filter icon 2. Select the date and time 3. Tap Apply Only the recordings that match the selected filter will be displayed."},{"s":"how-to-work-with-geofences-2","t":"How to work with geofences","h":["How to work with geofences","How to create a geofence","Geofence actions","Configuring notifications"],"x":"How to work with geofences The Geofences tab displays a list of all available geofences. In this tab, you can: View existing geofences Create new geofences Edit existing geofences Configure notifications for geofence-related events How to create a geofence 1. Tap Add 2. Specify the parameters: Geofence name Group it belongs to (or create a new group) 3. Set the geofence shape: Circle — tap the map and drag to set the radius (the radius can be adjusted by dragging the boundary) Polygon — mark points on the map one by one to define the boundaries 4. Choose the geofence color If needed, tap Clear to discard changes. 5. Tap Save — the geofence will appear in the list. Geofence actions Tap a geofence in the list, then tap the menu () to see available actions: Delete — remove the geofence Configure notifications — set up alerts Edit — open the geofence for editing Collapse the geofence menu to return to the list Configuring notifications 1. Select the vehicles for which notifications should be received 2. Specify the notification parameters: Notification type Message text 3. Choose how notifications will be delivered: Email SMS Alarm Push notifications in the app 4. To add an email or phone number, tap Add and enter the information. 5. Specify the trigger conditions: Input — entering the geofence Output— exiting the geofence 6. Save the settings"},{"s":"how-to-generate-a-report-2","t":"How to generate a report","h":["How to generate a report"],"x":"How to generate a report In the Reports tab, you can create a report for selected vehicles and parameters. 1. Select vehicles In the Cars field, choose one or more vehicles for which you want to generate the report. 2. Select Report type Choose the type of report you need, for example: Distance traveled Time spent driving and parked Fuel consumption Sensor data 3. Set the period Select the start and end date. If needed, specify the exact time (hours and minutes) — the report will be generated only for the selected period. 4. Choose grouping Decide how the data should be grouped: By date — to track changes over time By vehicle — to compare different vehicles 5. Select sensor tags If required for the report, choose one or more sensor groups. 6. Download the report Tap Download report. The file will be generated automatically and can be opened or saved."},{"s":"how-to-configure-sensors-and-beacons","t":"How to configure sensors and beacons","h":["How to configure sensors and beacons","Supported sensors","How to configure sensors"],"x":"How to configure sensors and beacons The OneGPS Guarda app can display detailed information about your vehicle’s status, such as battery charge, fuel level, temperature, and other parameters. To make this information appear in the app, you must first: Configure the sensors in the OneGPS system Assign them beacons (tags) A beacon (tag) is a unique name assigned to a sensor in the monitoring system. Tags allow the app to understand what each sensor represents and correctly display the data on the screen. Supported sensors garm_main_battery Vehicle battery charge (usually the monitoring device’s power voltage) garm_engine_lock_status Engine lock status garm_fuel_level Fuel level garm_engine_temperature Engine temperature garm_flat_cable_fault_status Cable fault status garm_on_board_temperature Cabin temperature garm_on_board_humidity Cabin humidity garm_output_1_status Output 1 status garm_output_2_status Output 2 status garm_output_3_status Output 3 status garm_engine_status Ignition status How to configure sensors Sensor configuration is performed in the OneGPS system: 1. Open your user interface 2. Select the desired vehicle. Right-click to open the vehicle menu 3. Go to the Sensors tab 4. Create a new sensor or open an existing one 5. Assign the appropriate tag from the table above The app uses the tag to determine which data to display on the Car condition screen. For example: If a temperature sensor is assigned the tag garm_on_board_temperature, the app will display the cabin temperature. If a main power sensor is assigned the tag garm_main_battery, the app will display the vehicle’s battery charge. If an ignition sensor is assigned the tag garm_engine_status, the app will display the engine status — whether it is on or off. If the required tag is not in the list, create it first, then assign it to the sensor. 6. Save your changes After this, the sensor data will appear in the Car condition screen in the mobile app. 1 — Vehicle battery voltage 2 — Current fuel level 3 — Cabin temperature 4 — Ignition status — engine on or off Other parameters that may appear in the vehicle summary: Balance — SIM card status of the monitoring device Engine temperature Door status — open or closed Headlight status — on or off Security status — armed or disarmed"},{"s":"how-to-configure-control-commands","t":"How to configure control commands","h":["How to configure control commands","How to create a command template","Examples of commands"],"x":"How to configure control commands To make the Lock and Unlock buttons work in the app, you need to set up arm and disarm command templates in the OneGPS system. Command templates are configured in the OneGPS system: 1. Open the user interface 2. Go to the Main section 3. Click the Commands icon A window will open where you can create command templates. How to create a command template 1. Click Add command 2. Select the vehicle for which the command is being created. 3. Specify the parameters : Command type — GRPS Command method — SMS_VIA_GPRS 4. In the SMS field, enter the command text, for example: setdigout 0 — disarm the vehicle 5. If required, check the HEX box if the command must be sent in HEX format 6. Click Save as template and give it a name Template names must be exactly: arm — set vehicle security disarm — disarm vehicle get_beacon_ids — retrieve the list of beacons Names must be lowercase. The app identifies and executes commands based on these exact names. Examples of commands Arm (Set security) — arm Teltonika setdigout 1 Adm 333 BLEOUTPUT 0 Disarm — disarm Teltonika setdigout 0 Adm 333 BLEOUTPUT 1 Retrieve beacon list — get_beacon_ids This command retrieves the list of Bluetooth beacons registered on the device. Teltonika getparam 1600;1601;1602;1603;1604;1605;1606;1607;1608;1609;1610 For the commands to work correctly: • The device must be online • A security sensor must be configured in the system — it is required for the app to display the current security status After setting up the command templates, the Lock and Unlock buttons will become active, and you can control the vehicle from the Car condition screen."},{"s":"pilot-tracker","t":"OneGPS Tracker","h":["OneGPS Tracker","How to Install OneGPS Tracker","How to activate the tracker","How to set tracker parameters","Service status","Device ID","Server URL","Location accuracy","Frequency","Distance","Angle","Offline buffering","Wake lock","Status tab","Information tab"],"x":"OneGPS Tracker OneGPS Tracker is an app that turns your smartphone into a GPS tracker. It allows you to track the device's location in real-time and send the data to the server. How to Install OneGPS Tracker 1. Open the Google Play app on your smartphone 2. In the search bar, type OneGPS Tracker or click this link 3. Tap Install and wait for the app to install How to activate the tracker 1. Open the app and turn on the Service status toggle. When the service is on, your location data will start being sent 2. Make sure your phone is connected to the internet (via mobile data or Wi-Fi) 3. To link the device to your account in the system, send the Device ID to customer support How to set tracker parameters On the main screen of the app, the following settings are available: Service status If the toggle is on, the tracker is active, and location data is being sent If the toggle is off, the service is stopped, and data is not sent Device ID A unique identifier for your device (e.g., 705943). You need to provide this ID to customer support so your device can appear in the system. Server URL The server address to which location data is sent. You can change this address if needed. Location accuracy The higher the accuracy, the more precise the track will be, but this will use more battery and data. Choose the optimal setting for your needs. Frequency Set how often the tracker will send data to the server (e.g., every 5 or 10 seconds). Shorter intervals mean more detailed tracking but higher battery and data consumption. Distance Set how far the device needs to travel before sending a new point (e.g., every 50 meters). This helps avoid sending data when the object is nearly stationary. Angle Choose the angle at which a new point will be sent (e.g., if the turning angle exceeds a certain threshold). Offline buffering If this option is enabled, the tracker will save location data when there's no internet connection. Once the connection is restored, all accumulated data will automatically be sent to the server. Wake lock If enabled, this option ensures the tracker runs in the background, even when the phone is not in use. Status tab The Statustab shows the tracker’s operation log, where you can see the current service status and the activity history. At the top of the screen, there's a Clearbutton to remove the current list of messages. Information tab In the Information tab, you’ll find recommendations on how to configure your phone for the tracker to work smoothly. 1. Open the Information tab in the app 2. Scroll down to see step-by-step instructions and screenshots that will help you configure your phone (depending on the model) 3. Follow the recommended steps in your device’s settings to ensure the tracker continues to work without interruptions"},{"s":"pilot-extensions","t":"OneGPS Extensions","h":["OneGPS Extensions","Who is it for","Where to find focumentation and examples","How to get started","How OneGPS extensions appear in the interface","Extensions list","How to enable or disable an extension","Extension details","Unpublished extensions","Example of an extension"],"x":"OneGPS Extensions OneGPS Extensions is a tool that allows developers to extend the functionality of the OneGPS monitoring system. With it, you can add new features, integrate external services, and adapt the platform to meet specific needs. Who is it for OneGPS Extensions is ideal for: developers and integrators who want to customize the system for specific business requirements and connect external services companies that need more functionality than the standard version offers, such as creating custom reports, integrating with other systems, or adjusting interfaces and visualizations teams who want to quickly develop and test new ideas by creating prototypes and minimal viable extensions (MVP) for further iteration organizations that value flexibility and need a platform that can adapt to changes and grow alongside the business Where to find focumentation and examples All materials for getting started with OneGPS Extensions are available in the official GitHub repository: OneGPS Extensions repository Quick start guide Examples and instructions for using ChatGPT to generate code API Documentation for working with maps and markers How to get started 1. Go to the OneGPS Extensions repository on GitHub: OneGPS Extensions repository 2. Review the installation and setup guide in the README file 3. Choose a suitable template or example for your extension — you’ll find examples and instructions in the docs folder 4. Implement your logic 5. Integrate the extension into the OneGPS interface as described in the repository documentation To clearly explain the process of creating extensions using OneGPS Extensions, we’ve prepared a video tutorial titled How OneGPS extensions appear in the interface If extensions are available for your account, you can open the list from the additional actions menu: 1. Click the menu icon in the top-right corner 2. In the drop-down list, select Extensions After that, a window with the list of available extensions will open. Extensions list The Extensions window shows all extensions available for your account. The list is displayed as cards. Each extension usually shows: Extension icon Name Author Short description Toggle switch for enabling or disabling button with extension details If there are many extensions, you can use the search bar at the top of the window to quickly find the one you need. How to enable or disable an extension There is a toggle on the left side of each extension. If the toggle is on, the extension is active for the user. If the toggle is off, the extension is not available in the interface. After you enable an extension, it may: appear as a new menu item open as a separate module add new interface elements or actions to the system Extension details To the right of each extension there is an button. If you click it, a page with information about the extension will open. Unpublished extensions In the top-right corner of the Extensions window, there is a Show drafts option. If you enable it, the list will show not only published extensions, but also draft versions — extensions that have not yet been released. A draft is a preliminary version of an extension that is still being developed, tested, or refined. This option is used, for example, when you need to check an extension before publication or test a new feature. The Show drafts option is available only to administrators. Example of an extension One example of an extension isAirports List. This is a module that shows a list of airports and their locations on the map. After the extension is enabled: a new section appears in the interface a screen with the list of airports opens a map is displayed рядом with it when you select a row in the list, you can immediately see that airport on the map This type of extension adds a new working tool to the system, not just a separate setting."},{"s":"connecting-bi-analytics-to-bi-platforms","t":"Connecting BI Analytics to BI platforms","h":["Connecting BI Analytics to BI platforms","API documentation (Swagger)"],"x":"Connecting BI Analytics to BI platforms BI Analytics allows you to connect monitoring system data to external BI platforms for building custom reports, charts, dashboards, and analytical views. Using the BI Analytics API, you can retrieve data about vehicles, drivers, trips, fuel consumption, Eco Driving events, and other operational metrics, then visualize and analyze this data in any BI platform that supports REST APIs. BI Analytics can be used for both simple reporting and enterprise-level analytics with custom metrics, filters, and visualizations. You can connect BI Analytics to platforms such as: Microsoft Power BI Tableau Qlik Sense Apache Superset Grafana any other BI platform that supports REST APIs Before connecting to BI Analytics, make sure that: the Analytics module is enabled for the partner account the Analytics module is enabled for the user account the bi_analytics = 1 configuration parameter is set for the partner account you have the partner account username and password you are using the correct server URL Once these requirements are met, you can connect to the BI Analytics API from any supported BI platform. API documentation (Swagger) To explore the BI Analytics API, use the Swagger documentation: BI API v1 — Swagger The Swagger documentation allows you to: browse all available API endpoints view request and response schemas download the API specification in JSON format"},{"s":"how-to-enable-bi-analytics-access","t":"How to enable BI Analytics access","h":["How to enable BI Analytics access","1. Enable the Analytics module on the partner account","2. Enable the Analytics module on the user's account"],"x":"How to enable BI Analytics access Access to the BI Analytics API is configured at two levels: 1. Partner account level 2. User account level To enable access, you need to: Enable the Analytics Module for the partner Enable the Analytics Module for the user's account Add the bi_analytics = 1 configuration to the partner card If you don't have access to the module settings or configuration on the partner account, please contact our support team. 1. Enable the Analytics module on the partner account 1. In the admin panel, go to the Partners section 2. Double-click on the selected partner — the partner card will open 3. Go to the Modules tab and enable the Analytics module 4. Next, go to the Configuration tab 5. Click the Add button 6. In the list, select the bi_analytics configuration and set its value to 1 7. Save your changes 2. Enable the Analytics module on the user's account After enabling the module on the partner account, you also need to enable it for the specific user's account. 1. In the admin panel, find the required account 2. Right-click on it and select Edit 3. In the account card, go to the Modules tab 4. Enable the Analytics module 5. Save your changes Once the Analytics module is enabled on both the partner account and the user's account, and the bi_analytics configuration is set — access to the BI Analytics API will be enabled."},{"s":"connecting-to-the-bi-analytics-api","t":"Connecting to the BI Analytics API","h":["Connecting to the BI Analytics API","Where to get your login and password","How to choose the server address"],"x":"Connecting to the BI Analytics API To connect to the BI Analytics API, you need to obtain your authentication credentials and configure the connection settings. Before connecting, make sure you have the following information: the username and password for your partner account the server URL where your account is hosted Once you have this information, you can connect to the BI Analytics API from Power BI or any other BI platform that supports REST APIs. Where to get your login and password To connect to the BI Analytics API, you need the partner account login and password. Use the same credentials that were provided when the partner account was registered. These are the same login details used to sign in to the admin panel. If you have main administrator access, you can find these credentials in the partner account card under the Main settings tab: Login — use as username Password — use as password How to choose the server address To connect to BI Analytics API, you need to specify the server address in the BaseUrl parameter in the query. Use the server where your account is hosted. Production https://OneGPS-gps.com Main server Saudi Arabia https://ksa.OneGPS-gps.com Regional server for Saudi Arabia Africa https://OneGPS-gps.africa Regional server for Africa Blade https://blade.OneGPS-gps.com Blade server Iglit https://gps.iglit.tech Iglit server In the query, specify the required URL, for example: BaseUrl = \"https://ksa.OneGPS-gps.com\","},{"s":"connecting-bi-analytics-in-power-bi","t":"Connecting BI Analytics in Power BI","h":["Connecting BI Analytics in Power BI","How to add a query in Power BI","How to build a chart","If the query doesn't update or you get login errors","How to clear permissions"],"x":"Connecting BI Analytics in Power BI This article demonstrates how to connect BI Analytics to Microsoft Power BI. The same approach can be used with any BI platform that supports REST APIs. How to add a query in Power BI 1. Open Power BI Desktop Launch Power BI Desktop on your computer. If it is not installed, download it for free from the official Microsoft website. 2. On the main toolbar at the top, click the Get Data button. A list of available data sources will appear 3. From the list of data sources, select Blank query 4. The Power query editor will open. This is the window where queries are created and configured. In this window, find the Advanced editor button on the top toolbar and click it. 5. The Advanced editor window will open with an empty query template. 6. Paste the query code into the editor. See the example querybelow In the query, you need to specify: Server address — the URL of your server Login — your login from the partner account Password — your password from the partner account Dates — the start and end dates of the period List of metrics — which data you want to retrieve (see the full list of metrics below) 7. After pasting the code, click Done Power BI will start connecting to the server and loading data. 8. After that, Power BI may display the warning “Information is required about data privacy”. Click Continue. 9. The Privacy levels window will open. In this window, Power BI checks whether it can safely combine and process data from the sources used in the query. In our case, the query uses one trusted data source, and authorization is already handled inside the query itself. To prevent Power BI from blocking the load or interfering with query execution, check: Ignore privacy levels checks for this file. Then click Save. 10. After saving, Power BI will execute the query and open a data preview in Power query editor. You will see a table with rows and columns — this is the data returned by your query. 11. Click Close & Apply. After that, the data will be loaded into Power BI, and you can start working with it. Now you can use the loaded data to create charts, tables, and other visual elements. In the main Power BI Desktop window, the following panels are usually displayed on the right: Visualizations — contains chart types, diagrams, cards, tables, and other visual elements. Choose the type you need, then fill it with your fields Data — shows the list of tables and fields loaded from your query, such as date, object ID, mileage, fuel consumption, and average speed. You can use these fields in visualizations by selecting them with checkboxes, dragging them with the mouse, or rearranging them Filters — lets you limit data using the conditions you need How to build a chart 1. In the Visualizations panel, click the chart type you need. A blank chart template will appear on the report canvas 2. In the Datapanel, select the fields you want to display 3. Power BI will automatically place the selected fields into the chart. In Power BI, you can combine data in different ways: add the same field to multiple charts compare several indicators build multiple visualizations on one page replace fields without recreating the report from scratch If the query doesn't update or you get login errors Sometimes Power BI saves the previous connection settings for a data source. Even if you change the login, password, or server address, the query may keep returning an authorization error because Power BI is still using the old credentials. In this case, you need to clear the saved permissions so that Power BI asks for the credentials again. How to clear permissions 1. In the Power Query Editor, click Data source settingson the toolbar 2. In the window that opens, select the data source you need — for example, your server address 1 — Button Data source settings 2 — Select your data source 3 — Button Clear Permissions 3. Click Clear Permissions 4. In the confirmation window, click Delete 5. Run the query again. Power BI will reapply the connection settings with the new credentials."},{"s":"bi-analytics-api-request-examples","t":"BI Analytics API request examples","h":["BI Analytics API request examples","Basic query example","Advanced query example"],"x":"BI Analytics API request examples This article provides example requests for working with the BI Analytics API. You can use them as a starting point for connecting Power BI or any other BI platform that supports REST APIs. Depending on your requirements, you can choose one of the following examples: Basic example — suitable for learning the API, testing the connection, and building simple integrations. It demonstrates the minimum steps required to authenticate, send a request, and retrieve data. Advanced example — recommended for production environments and scheduled data refreshes. In addition to authentication and data retrieval, it includes API error handling, response validation, empty result handling, and automatic data transformation into a format suitable for Power BI. For production deployments, we recommend using the Advanced example, as it provides more robust error handling and makes the integration easier to maintain. Basic query example let BaseUrl = \"https://<server_address>\", LoginJson = Json.Document( Web.Contents( BaseUrl, [ RelativePath = \"api/bi/login.php\", Headers = [#\"Content-Type\" = \"application/json\"], Content = Json.FromValue([ username = \"YOUR_LOGIN\", password = \"YOUR_PASSWORD\" ]), ManualStatusHandling = {400, 401, 403, 404, 500} ] ) ), Token = LoginJson[access_token], NodeId = LoginJson[user][node_id], AnalyticsJson = Json.Document( Web.Contents( BaseUrl, [ RelativePath = \"api/bi/analytics.php\", Query = [node = Text.From(NodeId)], Headers = [ #\"Authorization\" = \"Bearer \" & Token, #\"Content-Type\" = \"application/json\" ], Content = Json.FromValue([ date_from = \"2026-01-01\", date_to = \"2026-12-31\", metrics = { \"veh_driving_dist\", \"veh_fuel_cons\", \"average_speed\", \"veh_cars_counter\" }, source = \"vehicle\", group_by = \"source_day\", format = \"flat\" ]), ManualStatusHandling = {400, 401, 403, 404, 500}, ExcludedFromCacheKey = {\"Authorization\"} ] ) ), DataList = AnalyticsJson[data], Tbl = Table.FromList(DataList, Splitter.SplitByNothing(), {\"Column1\"}), Expanded = Table.ExpandRecordColumn( Tbl, \"Column1\", {\"source_id\", \"date\", \"veh_driving_dist\", \"veh_fuel_cons\", \"average_speed\", \"veh_cars_counter\"} ) in Expanded Advanced query example It includes API error handling, response validation, and automatic conversion of the returned data into a Power BI table. let BaseUrl = \"https://OneGPS-gps.com\", // --- API ERROR HANDLING --- ApiJson = (Response as binary, Context as text) as record => let Status = try Value.Metadata(Response)[Response.Status] otherwise 200, ParsedJson = try Json.Document(Response) otherwise error Error.Record( Context, \"API response is not valid JSON\", [ status = Status, body = Text.FromBinary(Response) ] ), ErrorMessage = if Record.HasFields(ParsedJson, \"error_description\") then ParsedJson[error_description] else if Record.HasFields(ParsedJson, \"msg\") then ParsedJson[msg] else if Record.HasFields(ParsedJson, \"errors\") then Text.Combine(List.Transform(ParsedJson[errors], each Text.From(_)), \"; \") else if Record.HasFields(ParsedJson, \"error\") then Text.From(ParsedJson[error]) else \"API request failed\", CheckedJson = if Status >= 400 then error Error.Record( Context, ErrorMessage, ParsedJson ) else if Record.HasFields(ParsedJson, \"success\") and ParsedJson[success] = false then error Error.Record( Context, ErrorMessage, ParsedJson ) else ParsedJson in CheckedJson, // --- LOGIN --- LoginResponse = Web.Contents( BaseUrl, [ RelativePath = \"api/bi/login.php\", Headers = [#\"Content-Type\" = \"application/json\"], Content = Json.FromValue([ username = \"LOGIN\", password = \"PASSWORD\" ]), ManualStatusHandling = {400, 422, 429, 500} ] ), LoginJson = ApiJson(LoginResponse, \"BI Analytics login\"), Token = LoginJson[access_token], NodeId = LoginJson[user][node_id], NodeQuery = if NodeId = null then [] else [node = Text.From(NodeId)], // --- ANALYTICS REQUEST --- AnalyticsResponse = Web.Contents( BaseUrl, [ RelativePath = \"api/bi/analytics.php\", Query = NodeQuery, Headers = [ #\"Authorization\" = \"Bearer \" & Token, #\"Content-Type\" = \"application/json\" ], Content = Json.FromValue([ date_from = \"2026-01-01\", date_to = \"2026-12-31\", metrics = { \"veh_driving_dist\", \"veh_fuel_cons\", \"average_speed\", \"veh_cars_counter\" }, source = \"vehicle\", group_by = \"source_day\", format = \"flat\", page = 1, per_page = 100 ]), ManualStatusHandling = {400, 422, 429, 500}, ExcludedFromCacheKey = {\"Authorization\"} ] ), AnalyticsJson = ApiJson( AnalyticsResponse, \"BI Analytics request\" ), // --- VALIDATE DATA --- DataList = if Record.HasFields(AnalyticsJson, \"data\") then AnalyticsJson[data] else error Error.Record( \"BI Analytics response\", \"Response does not contain data field\", AnalyticsJson ), // --- CONVERT TO TABLE --- Tbl = if List.IsEmpty(DataList) then #table( { \"source_id\", \"date\", \"veh_driving_dist\", \"veh_fuel_cons\", \"average_speed\", \"veh_cars_counter\" }, {} ) else Table.FromList( DataList, Splitter.SplitByNothing(), {\"Column1\"} ), Expanded = if List.IsEmpty(DataList) then Tbl else Table.ExpandRecordColumn( Tbl, \"Column1\", { \"source_id\", \"date\", \"veh_driving_dist\", \"veh_fuel_cons\", \"average_speed\", \"veh_cars_counter\" } ), #\"Changed Types\" = Table.TransformColumnTypes( Expanded, { {\"source_id\", Int64.Type}, {\"date\", type date}, {\"veh_driving_dist\", type number}, {\"veh_fuel_cons\", type number}, {\"average_speed\", type number}, {\"veh_cars_counter\", Int64.Type} } ), #\"Sorted Rows\" = Table.Sort( #\"Changed Types\", { {\"source_id\", Order.Ascending}, {\"date\", Order.Ascending} } ) in #\"Sorted Rows\""},{"s":"query-parameters-and-available-metrics","t":"Query parameters and available metrics","h":["Query parameters and available metrics","Query parameters","source","group_by","ACCOUNT_ID","Available metrics","Vehicle — general","Vehicle — fuel","Vehicle — violations","Vehicle — costs","Drivers","Metrics for specific Eco Driving event types","Examples"],"x":"Query parameters and available metrics When sending requests to the BI Analytics API, you specify query parameters to define what data to retrieve and how it should be returned. In addition to the query parameters, you must specify the metrics to include in the response. Metrics determine which values are returned by the API, such as distance traveled, fuel consumption, trip count, or Eco Driving events. Query parameters Query parameters define the data source, how the data is grouped, and any additional filtering options for the request. source The source field defines which data the query should use. Available values: vehicle — vehicle data driver — driver data group_by The group_by field defines how the result is grouped. total Total for all vehicles or drivers for the selected period { \"metric\": 123 } day Total for all vehicles or drivers for each day { \"20260301\": { \"metric\": 123 } } rating Each source, total for the selected period { source_id: { \"metric\": 123 } } source_day Each source, each day { source_id: { \"20260301\": { \"metric\": 123 } } } ACCOUNT_ID The ACCOUNT_ID parameter allows you to limit the data export to a single account. If the query uses the partner account login and password without ACCOUNT_ID, the system will export all vehicles across all accounts available to that partner. If you specify ACCOUNT_ID in the query, only the objects from the selected account will be loaded. Available metrics Metrics define which values are returned by the API. Specify them as a list in the metrics parameter. For example: metrics = [ \"veh_driving_dist\", \"veh_fuel_cons\", \"average_speed\" ] The following sections describe all supported metrics. Vehicle — general veh_trips_cnt Number of trips veh_driving_dist Mileage (km) veh_driving_time Driving time veh_parking_time Parking time veh_ignition_time Ignition on time veh_idle_time Idle time veh_motor_hours Engine hours Vehicle — fuel veh_fuel_cons Fuel consumption veh_fuel_filled Fuel filled veh_fuel_drained Fuel drained Vehicle — violations veh_eco_fine Eco-driving fine veh_eco_cnt Number of eco-driving violations veh_acc_fine Harsh acceleration fine veh_acc_cnt Number of harsh accelerations veh_braking_fine Harsh braking fine veh_braking_cnt Number of harsh braking events veh_turn_fine Harsh turn fine veh_turn_cnt Number of harsh turns veh_speed_fine Speeding fine veh_speed_cnt Number of speeding events veh_belt_fine Seat belt violation fine veh_belt_cnt Number of seat belt violations veh_idle_fine Idle fine veh_idle_cnt Number of idle violations Vehicle — costs veh_fuel_cost_per_kilometer Fuel cost per km veh_service_cost_per_kilometer Service cost per km veh_amount_exp Total expenses cost_of_using Cost of use average_speed Average speed veh_cars_counter Number of vehicles Drivers drv_trips_cnt Number of trips drv_driving_dist Mileage (km) drv_driving_time Driving time drv_parking_time Parking time drv_fuel_cons Fuel consumption drv_fuel_filled Fuel filled drv_fuel_drained Fuel drained drv_eco_fine Eco-driving fine drv_eco_cnt Number of eco-driving violations drv_acc_fine Harsh acceleration fine drv_acc_cnt Number of harsh accelerations drv_braking_fine Harsh braking fine drv_braking_cnt Number of harsh braking events drv_turn_fine Harsh turn fine drv_turn_cnt Number of harsh turns drv_speed_fine Speeding fine drv_speed_cnt Number of speeding events drv_belt_fine Seat belt violation fine drv_belt_cnt Number of seat belt violations Metrics for specific Eco Driving event types To retrieve data for specific Eco Driving event types, use metrics in the following format: eco_{N}_fine— Fine points for the specified event type. eco_{N}_cnt— Number of recorded events of the specified type. N represents the unique identifier (ID) of a driving event type. Each driving event type is automatically assigned a unique ID when it is created. The API uses this ID to generate the corresponding metric names. For example, the predefined driving event types may have the following IDs: 1 Acceleration 2 Braking 3 Turn 4 Speed 5 Seat Belt Custom driving event types are also assigned unique IDs automatically. When building a request, replace N with the ID of the required event type. Examples eco_1_fine / eco_1_cnt Fine points and event count for Acceleration eco_2_fine / eco_2_cnt Fine points and event count for Braking eco_4_fine / eco_4_cnt Fine points and event count for Speed eco_5_fine / eco_5_cnt Fine points and event count for Seat Belt You can find event type IDs in the driving event types directory. The ID is displayed in the ID column. When you create a new event type, the system automatically assigns it a unique ID, which can then be used in BI Analytics API metrics."},{"s":"working-with-api-requests","t":"Working with API requests","h":["Working with API requests","How API works in OneGPS","API request methods","GET","POST","PUT","DELETE","Data formats","Data types"],"x":"Working with API requests API (Application Programming Interface) is a set of rules and tools for software to interact with each other. In OneGPS, the API is used to exchange data between systems, services, applications, and the platform. An API request consists of: URL — the address of the API server and the command Request parameters — the data sent to the server Request method — the way the server is accessed How API works in OneGPS OneGPS uses a distributed infrastructure that includes multiple servers, for example, BLADE, KSA, AFRICA, USA. Each server can have several nodes, each with its own number. When you send an API request to the system, specify the server (for example, BLADE) and the specific node within that server — the node. This ensures the request is processed directly on the selected server and node, minimizing extra routing and response time. Example request: https://blade.OneGPS-gps.com/api/api.php?cmd=list&node=14 blade.OneGPS-gps.com — server name node=14 — specifies the node on the server where the request should be processed For more details on how to authorize and send requests to the OneGPS server, see the next article. API request methods GET Used to retrieve data, for example, requesting a list of objects or account information. In a GET request, the command and parameters are passed directly in the URL. Parameters are added after the ? and separated by &. Example https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=bindControl&uid=123&imei=456&lineuid=789 cmd=bindControl — command uid=123, imei=456, lineuid=789 — parameters POST Used to send data, for example, creating a new object or generating a report. In a POST request, the command and parameters can be sent in the request body, or the command can be specified in the URL while parameters are in the body. Example 1 URL: https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=bindControl Request body { \"uid\": \"123\", \"imei\": \"456\", \"lineuid\": \"789\" } Example 2 URL: https://<server_address>/api/api.php Request body { \"cmd\": \"bindControl\", \"uid\": \"123\", \"imei\": \"456\", \"lineuid\": \"789\" } PUT Used to update data, for example, changing parameters of an object or account. PUT is typically used to update a specific resource, so the identifier is usually included in the URL, and the updated data in the body. Example URL: https://<server_address>/api/api.php/123 Request body { \"cmd\": \"updateControl\", \"uid\": \"123\", \"imei\": \"456\", \"lineuid\": \"789\", \"status\": \"active\" } DELETE Used to delete data, for example, to remove an object or account. In a DELETE request, the command and parameters are usually passed in the URL. Example https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=bindControl&uid=123&imei=456&lineuid=789 Data formats The API supports different formats for both requests and responses. JSON: { \"cmd\": \"bindControl\", \"uid\": \"123\", \"imei\": \"456\", \"lineuid\": \"789\" } Form-encoded: cmd=bindControl&uid=123&imei=456&lineuid=789 XML: <request> <cmd>bindControl</cmd> <uid>123</uid> <imei>456</imei> <lineuid>789</lineuid> </request> Data types The API uses different data types to transfer information between the client and the server. Here are the main types you may encounter in API requests and responses. Data type Description Example in JSON Purpose String Text in quotes \"name\": \"Route #9\" For textual data: names, descriptions, identifiers Number Numeric value, integer or decimal \"price\": 15.35 For numeric data: prices, identifiers, coordinates, time Array Ordered list of elements of any type \"points\": [{\"lat\": \"53.352142\", \"lon\": \"83.758749\"}, {\"lat\": \"52.534012\", \"lon\": \"85.178777\"}] For lists of data: stops, items, users Object Set of key-value pairs \"uid\": \"1\", \"lat\": \"53.352142\", \"lon\":\"83.758749\"} For structured data: stop info, user info, order info Boolean Logical value true/false or 1/0 \"bidirectional\": 1 For flags or states: active/inactive, available/unavailable"},{"s":"how-to-connect-to-the-api","t":"How to connect to the API","h":["How to connect to the API","API address","Authorization type","What a request looks like","Example response"],"x":"How to connect to the API In this article, we will explain how to authorize and make a request to the API. After this, you will be able to send commands to the server and receive data from it. API address All requests are sent to: https://<server_address>/api/api.php <server_address>— this is the address of the server or host where OneGPS is running. For example: For users: https://OneGPS-gps.com/api/api.php For administrators: https://admin.OneGPS-gps.com/ax/api.php The API can be located in different \"folders\" of the project, and this is reflected in the request address. For example: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php https://<server_address>/ax/api.php The folders (api, backend, ax,etc.) are simply the project structure. They indicate which set of commands you're accessing. Always use the full folder path provided in the specific API command, otherwise, the request will not work. Authorization type The connection uses Basic Auth. For regular API access (user), use your account login and password. For administrator API access, use the administrator login and password. What a request looks like The general form of a request is: https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=<command>&node=<node_num> cmd — the command you want to execute node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example response The server will return a response in JSON format: { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", ...requested data... } code = 0 — everything was successful code = 1 — an error occurred msg — a message from the server (for example, \"OK\" or an error description) If you prefer working with XML, add the parameter out=xml to the request — the result will be returned in XML format. Example request: https://<address>/api/api.php?cmd=<command>&node=<node_num>&out=xml Response in XML format: <response> <code>0</code> <msg>OK</msg> ...requested data... </response> If you are just starting to work with the API, we recommend first reading the article Working with API requests to understand commands, parameters, and data formats."},{"s":"administrator-api","t":"Administrator API","h":["Administrator API"],"x":"Administrator API The Administrator API is a special set of commands that provides extended capabilities not available to regular users. It is designed for system management and performing administrative tasks. With the Administrator API, you can: create, modify, or delete user accounts configure access rights and roles retrieve information about the status of objects configure system and module settings Use your administrator account username and password to authenticate."},{"s":"creating-an-account","t":"Creating an account","h":["Creating an account","How to get data","Request address","Method","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Creating an account This API is used to create a new account, including account information and user data (such as the administrator’s login and password). After the request is executed, an account with a unique ID is created, and the first user (administrator) is added with the specified login and password. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/ax/api.php? Method POST Request parameters Required parameters: cmd=accountadd — command to add a new contract bill_price — ID of the pricing list (must be retrieved via the pricelist command) inn — taxpayer identification number (TIN) of the organization node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Optional parameters: acc_deposit — deposit amount (default is 0) acc_type — billing type: 1 — postpaid (default) 2 — prepaid 3 — postpaid light (with disconnection threshold) org_type — organization type: 1 — legal entity (default) 2 — individual 3 — sole proprietor bill_phone — organization’s phone number comment — comment for the contract code — 1C code kpp — KPP (Tax Registration Reason Code) block_limit — disconnection threshold for postpaid light billing type modules — IDs of connected modules (comma-separated, retrieved from modlist) bill_email — email address for the contract org_name — organization name Example request https://<server_address>/ax/api.php?cmd=accountadd&bill_price=143&bill_email=demo1@skyelectronics.ru&org_name=PUP&comment=lalala&inn=123456789012&acc_type=1&org_type=1&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": { \"id\": \"4018\", \"login\": \"demo1@skyelectronics.ru\", \"password\": \"blPs0S\" }, \"msg\": \"OK\" } The response to the API request will include: code — response status 0 — operation successful data — information about the new contract id — unique contract ID login — administrator login created for the contract password — administrator password msg — message indicating the result of the operation"},{"s":"accounts-list","t":"Accounts list","h":["Accounts list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Accounts list This API retrieves the list of accounts with basic information: contract ID, name, balance, and the number of vehicles. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters cmd=accountslist— command to retrieve the list of accounts node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=accountslist&node=1 Response { \"id\": 10, \"org_name\": \"Test relaying\", \"org_type\": 2, \"balance\": \"0.00\", \"acc_deposit\": \"40000.00\", \"comment\": null, \"inn\": null, \"kpp\": null, \"bill_email\": \"relay@mail.com\", \"bill_phone\": \"43542554\", \"hidden\": 1, \"veh_count\": 0 } In response to the API request, the following is returned: id — unique account identifier org_name — organization or contract name org_type — type of organization balance — current contract balance acc_deposit — deposit amount on the contract comment — additional comment for the contract inn — tax identification number (TIN) of the organization kpp — tax registration reason code (if applicable) bill_email — email address for billing or invoicing notifications bill_phone — contact phone number for billing inquiries hidden— deleted account flag: 0 means the account is not deleted, 1 means the account is deleted veh_count — number of vehicles linked to the account"},{"s":"create-user-1","t":"Create user","h":["Create user","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Request example","Response"],"x":"Create user This API creates a new OneGPS user and assigns the main user details: login, password, name, role, phone number, email, and available accounts. Method GET Request address To execute the request, use the following URL: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters cmd=create_user — command for creating a user login — login of the new user password — password of the new user name — user name role_id — user role ID phone — user phone number email — user email address accounts — list of account IDs the user should have access to. If there are several accounts, separate them with commas account_id — ID of the account where the user is created Request example https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=create_user&login=TestAPI13&password=Qwerty123&name=test%20Api%2013&role_id=1& phone=789995&email=testapi13@mailinator.com&accounts=1106,1131&account_id=1003 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success create user: 729\", \"user_id\": 729 } The API response returns: code — command execution result code. The value 0 means successful execution msg — text message with the command execution result user_id — ID of the created user"},{"s":"account-users-list","t":"Account users list","h":["Account users list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Account users list This API allows you to retrieve a list of users for a specific account with basic information for each user. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters cmd=users — command to get the list of users accountid — ID of the account whose users are being requested node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=users&accountid=56832&node=1 Response { \"data\": [ { \"id\": 88977, \"name\": \"valera_test\", \"login\": \"valera17totsenko@gmail.com\", \"passwd\": \"123456\", \"info\": null, \"email\": \"valera17totsenko@gmail.com\", \"lang\": \"en\", \"roleid\": 0 } ], \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: id — user ID name — display name login — username/login passwd — password info — additional user information email — email address lang — user interface language roleid — role ID"},{"s":"update-user-access-to-accounts","t":"Update user access to accounts","h":["Update user access to accounts","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Request example","Response"],"x":"Update user access to accounts This API updates the list of accounts that a OneGPS user has access to. Method GET Request address To execute the request, use the following URL: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters cmd=update_user_accounts_map — command for updating user access to accounts accounts — list of account IDs the user should have access to. If there are several accounts, separate them with commas user_id — ID of the user whose access is being updated Request example https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=update_user_accounts_map&accounts=1087&user_id=535 If you need to specify several accounts: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=update_user_accounts_map&accounts=1087,1131&user_id=535 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success\" } The API response returns: code — command execution result code. The value 0 means successful execution msg — text message with the command execution result"},{"s":"tariff-plans-list","t":"Tariff plans list","h":["Tariff plans list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Tariff plans list This API allows you to retrieve a list of tariffs available for an account, including information about the currency, price, and description. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters cmd=pricelist— command to get the list of tariffs node — the number of the node where the command should be executed coden (optional) — currency code: — numeric (e.g., 840 for USD, 810 for RUB) — alphabetic (e.g., USD or RUR) Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=pricelist&coden=840&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"id\": 143, \"code\": \"840\", \"currency\": 2, \"descr\": \"3 dollars per property per month\", \"name\": \"US dollar\", \"price\": \"3.00\" } ], \"message\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — request result code (0 = successful) data — array of tariff objects: id — unique tariff ID code — currency code (numeric) currency — internal currency ID descr — tariff description name — tariff name (e.g., \"US dollar\") price — tariff price in the specified currency message — text message with the result of the request"},{"s":"adding-payments","t":"Adding payments","h":["Adding payments","How to get data","Method","Request address","Example request"],"x":"Adding payments This API is used to add payment information for an account. You can specify the amount, date, external payment ID, and description. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/ax/api.php? Request parameters cmd=payment — command to add a payment inn — account’s INN (unique identifier) sum — payment amount (format dd.dd) id — payment ID in the external system date — payment date (format DDMMYYYY) descr — payment description (string), e.g., “payment for connection” node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/ax/api.php?cmd=payment&inn=123123123&sum=10.00&id=124&date=04042016&descr=test%20payment&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Payment inserted successfully\", \"data\": { \"payment_id\": 124, \"inn\": \"123123123\", \"sum\": 10.00, \"date\": \"04042016\", \"descr\": \"test payment\" } }"},{"s":"adding-a-vehicle-to-an-account","t":"Adding a vehicle to an account","h":["Adding a vehicle to an account","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Adding a vehicle to an account To add a vehicle to your account, use this API. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters To obtain the required data, append parameters to the request address. Parameters are specified after the question mark ? and are separated by the ampersand &. Required parameters cmd=vehadd — command to add a vehicle account_id — contract number in the system vehmodel — model of the monitoring device. The model ID is obtained from the response of the vehmodellist command. imei — unique number of the monitoring device type — type of vehicle. The vehicle type ID is obtained from the response of the vehtypelist command. If the required type is not in the list, a new ID can be specified. name — state registration number or name of the vehicle msisdn — number of the primary SIM card of the device msisdn2 — number of the secondary SIM card of the device mac — MAC address, identifier of the vehicle's device. If the mac parameter is specified, the system will automatically create a record for the vehicle in the \"Buses\" tab in the admin panel. node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Optional parameters: folder — name of the folder for adding the vehicle. If the specified folder does not exist, it will be created. If no folder is specified, the vehicle will be added to the general list (root folder). info — additional information about the vehicle summer_fuel — fuel consumption in the summer period winter_fuel — fuel consumption in the winter period fuel_type — type of fuel used (50-diesel, 80-92-95-98-gasoline, 100-gas). If the parameter is not specified, the system will automatically set it to diesel. lost_connection_time — time after which a loss of connection is recorded (in seconds) time_zone — time zone of the vehicle. Default is set to Europe/Moscow. initial_motor_hours — initial engine hour readings driver_id — driver identifier initial_mileage — initial mileage of the vehicle. If not specified, it is considered to start from zero. speed_limit — maximum allowable speed. If not specified, speed control is disabled. minsat — minimum number of satellites. Default is set to 4. maxhdop — maximum HDOP. Default is set to 21. mintime — minimum parking time for track splitting. Default is set to 3600 seconds. maxspeed — maximum possible speed. Default is set to 250. minspeed — minimum speed for detecting the start of movement. Default is set to 10 km/h. minparking — minimum parking time on the route. Default is set to 300 seconds. model — make and model of the vehicle year — year of manufacture of the vehicle. If the year is not specified, it defaults to 1900. vin — vehicle identification number engine_horse — engine power organization — name of the owning organization column — column number subdivision — subdivision Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=vehadd&account_id=4019&type=1&name=testveh&imei=80987898789987&vehmodel=13&msisdn=7903&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"agent_id\": \"20518\", \"veh_id\": \"18994\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — response code indicating how the request was processed msg — message with a textual description of the operation result agent_id — identifier of the agent that processed the request veh_id — identifier of the vehicle to which this response pertain"},{"s":"editing-vehicle-information","t":"Editing vehicle information","h":["Editing vehicle information","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Editing vehicle information This API is used to edit a vehicle’s data. You can update information such as type, model, license plate, VIN, year of manufacture, engine power, initial mileage, motor hours, and assign the vehicle to a folder, driver, or subdivision. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters Required: cmd=vehedit — command to edit vehicle information agentid — vehicle identifier or imei — unique device ID node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Optional: type — vehicle type (ID from vehtypelist command) veh_number — displayed name or license plate of the vehicle veh_type — custom vehicle type (string) folder_id — folder to assign the vehicle; if the folder does not exist, it will be created automatically. Default — root folder model — vehicle model initial_motor_hours — initial motor hours driver_id — driver ID initial_mileage — initial mileage (default 0) year — year of manufacture (default 1900) vin — vehicle VIN engine_horse — engine power organization — organization column — column subdivision — subdivision Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=vehedit&agentid=144&node=1 Response { \"Success\": \"The vehicle data has been changed\" }"},{"s":"moving-a-vehicle-between-accounts","t":"Moving a vehicle between accounts","h":["Moving a vehicle between accounts","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Moving a vehicle between accounts This API is used to move a vehicle between accounts. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters To obtain the required data, append parameters to the request address. Parameters are specified after the question mark ? and are separated by the ampersand &. cmd=veh_move_account — command to move the vehicle to another account vehicle_id — identifier of the vehicle that needs to be moved to the account account_id — number of the new account node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=veh_move_account&vehicle_id=23&account_id=1005&node=1 Response {\"code\": 1, \"msg\": \"Success move vehicle 23 to account 1005\"} In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code: 1 — operation completed successfully 0 — error occurred during the operation msg — message with a textual description of the operation result"},{"s":"vehicle-information","t":"Vehicle information","h":["Vehicle information","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request:"],"x":"Vehicle information This API is used to retrieve detailed information about a vehicle. You can request data either by agent_id or by the device’s IMEI number. The response returns full details, including license plate, fuel consumption, speed limits, VIN, model, configuration settings, assigned driver, and more. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters Required: cmd=vehinfo — command to retrieve vehicle information node — the number of the node where the command should be executed One of these parameters must be specified: agentid — vehicle identifier (agent_id) imei — unique IMEI number of the device Example request: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=vehinfo&agentid=81181&node=1 Response [ { \"ownerid\": 5374, \"vehiclenumber\": \"Howen C\", \"typeid\": 1, \"summer_fuel\": \"10\", \"winter_fuel\": \"10\", \"idle_fuel\": \"2\", \"time_zone\": \"Europe/Moscow\", \"speed_limit\": 0, \"agentid\": 81181, \"account_id\": 3925, \"model\": \"\", \"year\": \"1900\", \"vin\": \"\", \"fuel_type\": \"50\", \"driver_name\": \"Test\", \"driver_phone\": \"12121212121\" } ] In response to the API request, the following is returned: ownerid — ID of the owner vehiclenumber — license plate number or vehicle name typeid — vehicle type summer_fuel / winter_fuel / idle_fuel — fuel consumption norms (summer, winter, idle) time_zone — time zone where the vehicle is used speed_limit — speed limit account_id — account ID to which the vehicle is linked model / year / vin — model, year of manufacture, and VIN of the vehicle fuel_type — type of fuel driver_name / driver_phone — name and phone number of the assigned driver config — JSON with additional configuration (fuel settings, engine hours, restrictions, etc.)"},{"s":"account-objects-status","t":"Account objects status","h":["Account objects status","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Account objects status This API provides a list of all vehicles linked to a specific account, along with their current status. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters Add the following parameters to your request URL: cmd=vehicles — command to get the list of vehicles account_id — the account ID for which you want to get the vehicle list node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Optional parameters: is_show_deleted — show deleted vehicles (0 — only active vehicles, 1 — all vehicles, including deleted ones) Response format: The response is a JSON array with vehicle data. Example request With deleted vehicles included: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=vehicles&account_id=2238&is_show_deleted=1&node=1 Response { \"data\": [ { \"active\": 1, \"deleted\": 0, \"agentid\": 2914, \"configuration\": \"Teltonika FM1100\", \"current_mileage\": 69332, \"dir\": 0, \"driver_name\": \"SSS\", \"driver_phone\": \"\", \"folder\": \"ЮФО\", \"lat\": \"47.200989\", \"lon\": \"39.634436\", \"model\": \"HYUNDAI IX35\", \"msisdn\": \"79818775160\", \"on\": 1, \"partner_price\": \"0.00\", \"price\": \"0.00\", \"sats\": 7, \"speed\": 0, \"ts\": \"1457709039\", \"type\": \"Static\", \"uniqid\": \"356307045913463\", \"vehiclenumber\": \"IX35\" }, { \"active\": 0, \"deleted\": 1, \"agentid\": 8026, \"configuration\": \"Teltonika FM4200\", \"current_mileage\": 17441, \"dir\": 0, \"driver_name\": \"\", \"driver_phone\": \"\", \"folder\": \"ЮФО\", \"lat\": \"47.318118\", \"lon\": \"39.995648\", \"model\": \"\", \"msisdn\": \"79110181596\", \"on\": 1, \"partner_price\": \"100.00\", \"price\": \"350.00\", \"sats\": 9, \"speed\": 0, \"ts\": \"1457726726\", \"type\": \"Car\", \"uniqid\": \"354330031297057\", \"vehiclenumber\": \"nex\" } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: active — vehicle status: 1 = active, 0 = inactive deleted — whether the vehicle is deleted: 0 = not deleted, 1 = deleted agentid — unique device ID configuration — GPS device configuration name (e.g., Teltonika FM1100) current_mileage — vehicle mileage, km dir — direction of movement driver_name — driver’s name (if assigned) driver_phone — driver’s phone number folder — folder/category name in the system lat — latitude of the last known position lon — longitude of the last known position model — vehicle model msisdn — SIM card number of the tracker on — ignition status (1 = on, 0 = off) partner_price — partner price price — retail price sats — number of satellites in view speed — current speed, km/h ts — timestamp (UNIX time) of the last position update type — object type uniqid — unique device identifier (usually IMEI) vehiclenumber — vehicle number or label"},{"s":"vehicle-type-list","t":"Vehicle type list","h":["Vehicle type list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Vehicle type list This API is used to retrieve a list of all vehicle types in an account. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters cmd=vehtypelist — command to get the list of vehicle types node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=vehtypelist&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"identifier\": 1, \"name\": \"Car\" }, { \"identifier\": 2, \"name\": \"Truck\" }, { \"identifier\": 3, \"name\": \"Passenger\" }, { \"identifier\": 4, \"name\": \"Agrimotor\" } ], \"message\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — request result code (0 = successful) data — array of vehicle types: identifier — unique vehicle type ID name — name of the vehicle type message — text message with the result of the request"},{"s":"blocking-or-unblocking-vehicles","t":"Blocking or unblocking vehicles","h":["Blocking or unblocking vehicles","How to get data","Request address","Method","Request parameters","Example requests"],"x":"Blocking or unblocking vehicles This API allows you to lock or unlock vehicles based on the parameters agent_id, imei, or account_id. Depending on the status provided, the vehicle will either be locked or activated. Locking or unlocking can be performed using identifiers such as the agent, IMEI, or contract number. How to get data Request address To get data, use the following address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Method POST Request parameters Required: cmd=setvehblock — command to lock or unlock the vehicle node — the number of the node where the command should be executed status — lock status. Values: 0 — locked 1 — active (unlocked) One of these parameters must be specified: agentid — agent identifier imei — IMEI number of the device accountid — contract number Example requests Lock vehicle by IMEI: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=setvehblock&status=0&imei=1231231231&node=1 Unlock vehicle by agentid: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=setvehblock&status=1&agentid=2914&node=1 Response If the vehicle is successfully locked or unlocked, you will receive the following response: { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"agentid\": 2914, \"status\": 1 } ], \"msg\": \"OK\" } The response to the API request will include: code — response code. Value: 0 — operation successful other possible values: errors like 400 or 500 if issues occurred. data — data about the lock/unlock operation agentid — agent identifier for whom the status was changed status — vehicle status: 0 — locked 1 — active (unlocked) msg — message indicating the result of the operation"},{"s":"raw-vehicle-data","t":"Raw vehicle data","h":["Raw vehicle data","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Raw vehicle data This API returns raw GPS track data for a vehicle over a specified period. Requests can only be made for periods of up to 10 days. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters cmd=rawpoints — command to get raw points ts — start date in UNIX timestamp format te — end date in UNIX timestamp format agentid — vehicle identifier node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=rawpoints&ts=1760216400&te=1760302800&agentid=157773 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"agentid\": 2914, \"height\": 32, \"direction\": 1, \"latitude\": \"47.215545\", \"longitude\": \"39.706906\", \"satsinview\": 15, \"servertime\": 1458044666, \"speed\": 10, \"triggerdata\": \"Priority=0;EventSource=0;DIS1=1;GSMVal=4;IN1=67;Vsourse=14547;\", \"unixtimestamp\": 1458022428 }, { \"agentid\": 2914, \"height\": 32, \"direction\": 339, \"latitude\": \"47.215938\", \"longitude\": \"39.706751\", \"satsinview\": 12, \"servertime\": 1458044666, \"speed\": 12, \"triggerdata\": \"Priority=0;EventSource=0;DIS1=1;GSMVal=4;IN1=67;Vsourse=14538;\", \"unixtimestamp\": 1458022437 } ], \"deb\": [ 2914, 1458022428, 1458043980 ], \"message\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — request result code (0 = successful) data — array of GPS points: agentid — vehicle identifier height/ altitude — altitude in meters direction — movement direction in degrees latitude/ longitude — coordinates of the point satsinview — number of visible satellites servertime — server time in UNIX timestamp speed — speed in km/h triggerdata — device trigger information unixtimestamp — event time in UNIX timestamp deb — array of debug information (agentid, start, and end of the period) message — text message describing the result of the request"},{"s":"fuel-sonsumption","t":"Fuel сonsumption","h":["Fuel сonsumption","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Fuel сonsumption This API retrieves fuel consumption data for a specific vehicle over a selected period. It returns information about refuels, fuel used, fuel drains, and fuel sensor readings at the beginning and end of the period. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters cmd=fuel — command to retrieve fuel data ts — start date of the period (Unix timestamp) te — end date of the period (Unix timestamp) agentid — vehicle ID (agent_id) node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=fuel&ts=1708981200&te=1709154000&agentid=6477&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": { \"fill\": 100, \"fuel\": 84, \"stale\": 0, \"start\": 27, \"stop\": 43 }, \"msg\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code of the request (0 = successful) fill — amount of fuel refilled during the period fuel — amount of fuel consumed stale — amount of fuel drained or lost start — fuel sensor reading at the start of the period stop — fuel sensor reading at the end of the period msg — textual message with the request result"},{"s":"create-a-folder","t":"Create a folder","h":["Create a folder","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example requests"],"x":"Create a folder This API is used to create a new folder in the system. You can specify a parent folder, the name of the new folder, and user access rights. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters Required: cmd=addfolder — command to create a folder account_id — account ID parent_id — ID of the parent folder folder_name — name of the new folder node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Optional: users — comma-separated list of user IDs to grant access to the folder users=admins — grants access to all administrators of the account If the parameter is missing or empty, the folder inherits access rights from the parent folder Example requests Grant access to all administrators: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=addfolder&account_id=5654&parent_id=654&folder_name=my_folder&users=admins&node=1 Grant access to specific users: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=addfolder&account_id=5654&parent_id=654&folder_name=my_folder&users=123,212,432&node=1 Create a folder with default parent folder rights: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=addfolder&account_id=5654&parent_id=654&folder_name=my_folder&node=1 Response { \"success\": \"New folder my_folder has been created with ID 27330\" }"},{"s":"renaming-a-folder","t":"Renaming a folder","h":["Renaming a folder","How to get data","Request address","Method","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Renaming a folder This API allows you to change the name of an existing folder on the platform. To do this, you need to send a request with parameters indicating the folder whose name needs to be changed and the new name for it. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php Method POST Request parameters cmd=editfoldername — command to change the folder name node — the number of the node where the command should be executed folder_id — ID of the folder whose name needs to be changed new_folder_name — new name for the folder Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=editfoldername&folder_id=454545&new_folder_name=testname&node=1 Response { \"success\": \"Folder name successfully updated\" }"},{"s":"list-of-folders","t":"List of folders","h":["List of folders","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"List of folders This API is used to get the list of all folders available in the system. The response returns a list with unique folder IDs, their names, and their parent folders. You can get either all folders or only those related to a specific account. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters Required: cmd=getfolders— command to retrieve the list of folders node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Optional: account_id— account ID for which you want to retrieve the list of folders Example request Getting all folders: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=getfolders&node=1 Getting folders for a specific account: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=getfolders&account_id=3925 Response { \"9793\": {\"name\": \"АС-Капитал\", \"parent\": \"Колонна №1\"}, \"11462\": {\"name\": \"Ленэнерго\", \"parent\": \"Колонна №1\"}, \"12551\": {\"name\": \"Вектор\", \"parent\": \"test123\"}, \"16453\": {\"name\": \"test_alex\", \"parent\": \"1april\"}, \"18492\": {\"name\": \"31Novfolder\", \"parent\": \"Колонна №1\"} } In response to the API request, the following is returned: key (e.g., \"9793\") — unique folder ID name — folder name parent — parent folder to which the current folder belongs"},{"s":"deleting-a-folder","t":"Deleting a folder","h":["Deleting a folder","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Deleting a folder This API deletes a specified folder and moves all vehicles from it to the root folder. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address:https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters cmd=deletefolder — command to delete the folder folder_id — identifier of the folder to be deleted node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=deletefolder&folder_id=545&node=1 Response { \"success\": \"The folder has been successfully deleted\" }"},{"s":"module-list","t":"Module list","h":["Module list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Module list This API allows you to get the full list of available modules for an account, including their identifiers and descriptions. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/ax/api.php? Request parameters cmd=modlist— command to retrieve the list of modules node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/ax/api.php?cmd=modlist&node=1 Response \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"descr\": \"Fuel Card Module\", \"identifier\": 7, \"name\": \"fuel\" }, { \"descr\": \"Engine Lock Module\", \"identifier\": 8, \"name\": \"lock\" }, { \"descr\": \"Notification Module\", \"identifier\": 27, \"name\": \"notification\" } ], \"msg\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code— request result code (0 = successful) data— array of module objects: descr— module description identifier— unique module ID name— system name of the module msg— text message describing the result of the request"},{"s":"equipment-list","t":"Equipment list","h":["Equipment list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Equipment list This API is used to retrieve a list of all equipment types (device models) used in an account, including the manufacturer information. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php? Request parameters cmd=vehmodellist — command to get the list of equipment models node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=vehmodellist&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"identifier\": 1, \"name\": \"ASC-6 GPS/GLONASS\", \"supplier\": \"APK COM\" }, { \"identifier\": 2, \"name\": \"ASC-6 Lite GPS/GLONASS\", \"supplier\": \"APK COM\" } ], \"message\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — request result code (0 = successful) data — array of equipment models: identifier — unique model ID name — model name supplier — equipment manufacturer message — text message with the result of the request"},{"s":"working-with-atom-linux-commands","t":"Working with Atom Linux commands","h":["Working with Atom Linux commands","How to get data","Request address","List of commands","Get the list of sent Linux commands (command history)","Request parameters","Request example","Get the list of Linux command templates","Request parameters","Request example","Send a Linux command","Request parameters","Request example","Delete a Linux command","Request parameters","Request example","API error codes","Interaction with commands in the interface"],"x":"Working with Atom Linux commands This article describes the main API commands that allow you to interact with Linux systems through the admin panel. With these commands, you can send, receive, delete, and view the history of commands for Atoms. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/backend/api.php List of commands cmd=atom_get_all — get a list of sent commands cmd=atom_get_commands — get a list of command templates cmd=atom_create_command — send a Linux command cmd=atom_delete_command — delete a Linux command node — the number of the node where the command should be executed Get the list of sent Linux commands (command history) This command allows you to retrieve a list of all commands that have been sent to the Atom. The history includes the Atom identifier, the command that was sent, and its execution status. Method: GET Request parameters cmd=atom_get_all — command for retrieving history ident — unique Atom identifier (for example, b0:44:6t:0d:a9:04) Request example https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=atom_get_all&ident=b0:44:6t:0d:a9:04&node=1 Response example [ { \"id\": 27899, \"agent_id\": null, \"ident\": \"8v:f5:8c:01:13:70\", \"script\": \"ls /root/\", \"processed\": null, \"ts\": 1712138610, \"result\": null } ] Get the list of Linux command templates This command allows you to retrieve a list of Linux command templates that can be used to send to an Atom. Templates contain a description and the command that will be executed on the device. Method: GET Request parameters cmd=atom_get_commands — command for retrieving command templates ident — unique Atom identifier (for example, 80:c5:80:01:68:08) Request example https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=atom_get_commands&ident=80:c5:80:01:68:08 Response example [ { \"id\": 36, \"command_name\": \"Video List\", \"description\": null, \"command\": \"ls -lsh /video/*.avi\" } ] Send a Linux command This command allows you to send a Linux command to the Atom. The command will be added to the queue and executed on the device. Method: POST Request parameters cmd=atom_create_command — command for creating a new command ident — unique Atom identifier script — script command (optional) Request example https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=atom_create_command&ident=b1:42:67:00:00:fd Response example 27917 (ID of the command added to the queue) Delete a Linux command If a command has not yet been executed, it can be removed from the queue using this method. Deletion is possible only before the command has been processed. Method: GET Request parameters cmd=atom_delete_command — command for deletion ident— unique Atom identifier command_id — ID of the command to delete Request example https://<server_address>/backend/api.php?cmd=atom_delete_command&ident=b0:44:6f:0d:av:88&command_id=27921 Response example \"Success delete\" API error codes 400 Bad request — invalid data format or a required parameter is missing. Possible messages: {\"error\": \"Missing ident param\"} — Atom identifier is missing {\"error\": \"Command already processed\"} — the command has already been executed {\"error\": \"Unknown macro: {Unknown macro}\"} — unknown macro command 500 Internal server error — internal server error Interaction with commands in the interface In the OneGPS interface, interaction with Atoms takes place through the admin panel tab \"Autoconductor → Buses\", where the \"Interact\" command is available. Through this function you can: Send commands (Linux commands) to the Atom View the list of available commands (templates) Display the history of all sent commands Access the result of each executed command"},{"s":"managing-rental-contracts","t":"Managing rental contracts","h":["Managing rental contracts","Authorization","Getting an authorization token","Authorization example","Main commands","Request parameters","Example requests","Start rental","Update rental","Stop rental","Response and error codes"],"x":"Managing rental contracts This API provides functionality for managing vehicle rental contracts. With this API, you can start, update, stop rentals, as well as authenticate and generate tokens for authorization. Authorization To use the API, authentication is required with a Bearer token. Getting an authorization token Before sending requests, you need to obtain a token by generating it using the login and password for the admin panel. Authorization example Example request to generate a token: curl --location --request GET 'https://adm.OneGPS-gps.africa/backend/app/contracts.php' \\ --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --data-raw '{ \"cmd\": \"generate_token\", \"login\": \"YOUR_LOGIN_FOR_ADMIN_PANEL\", \"password\": \"YOUR_PASSWORD_FOR_ADMIN_PANEL\" }' Example response: { \"code\": 0, \"odo\": \"0\", \"message\": \"007898667a1b5711682399ea38a84f61\" } You will receive a token in the response. Use it in the Authorization header for further requests. Example header: Authorization: Bearer 007898667a1b5711682399ea38a84f61 Main commands To perform operations, specify the cmd parameter, which defines which operation you want to perform: generate_token — get token for authorization start— start vehicle rental update — update rental details stop — stop vehicle rental Request parameters All parameters are sent in JSON format. Parameter Type Required Description mva int Yes Numeric ID of the rented vehicle ra int Yes Rental agreement number plate_number string Yes Vehicle plate number driver_name string Yes Name of the driver renting the vehicle driver_phone string Yes Driver's phone number driver_passport string No Driver's passport driver_address string No Driver's address driver_license string No Driver's license number driver_license_exp string No Driver's license expiry date driver_email string No Driver's email address driver_birth_date string No Driver's birth date driver_sex string No Gender (m or f) driver_country string No Driver's country driver_rating int No Driver's rating ts_expected int Yes Expected rental start time (Unix timestamp) te_expected int Yes Expected rental end time (Unix timestamp) ts_real int No Actual rental start time (Unix timestamp) te_real int No Actual rental end time (Unix timestamp) start_location object Yes Rental start location (with latitude, longitude or address) end_location object Yes Rental end location (latitude, longitude or address) rate_code string No Rental rate code car_group_charged string No Vehicle group for charging checkout_branch string No Checkout branch mileage_limit int No Mileage limit cid string No Client ID (driver renting the vehicle) Example requests Start rental curl --location --request POST 'https://adm.OneGPS-gps.africa/backend/app/contracts.php' \\ --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --header 'Authorization: Bearer 007898667a1b5711682399ea38a84f61' \\ --data-raw '{ \"cmd\": \"start\", \"mva\": 111, \"ra\": 67890, \"plate_number\": \"ABC123\", \"driver_name\": \"John Doe\", \"driver_phone\": \"123-456-7890\", \"ts_expected\": 1644927600, \"te_expected\": 1644981600, \"start_location\": { \"lat\": 40.712776, \"lon\": -74.005974 }, \"car_group_charged\": \"Economy\" }' Response { \"code\": 0, \"odo\": \"1233\", \"message\": \"Success\" } The response includes: code— response code odo— odometer value message— message with the request result Update rental curl --location --request POST 'https://adm.OneGPS-gps.africa/backend/app/contracts.php' \\ --header 'Authorization: Bearer 007898667a1b5711682399ea38a84f61' \\ --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --data-raw '{ \"cmd\": \"update\", \"ra\": 67890, \"plate_number\": \"ABC123\", \"driver_name\": \"John Doe\", \"driver_phone\": \"123-456-7890\", \"mileage_limit\": 2000 }' Response { \"code\": 0, \"odo\": \"1208607\", \"message\": \"Success\" } Stop rental curl --location --request POST 'https://adm.OneGPS-gps.africa/backend/app/contracts.php' \\ --header 'Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN' \\ --header 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ --data-raw '{ \"cmd\": \"stop\", \"ra\": 67890, \"driver_rating\": 4, \"end_location\": {\"address\": \"Ilino 2c\"} }' Response { \"code\": 0, \"odo\": \"1208607\", \"message\": \"Success\" } Response and error codes 0— Operation successful 1— Vehicle not found 2— No changes to update 3— Rental agreement not found 4— Authentication error 5— Unknown error 6— Insufficient rights to perform operation 7— Contracts module not active 8— This rental agreement number is already in use by another account 9— Vehicle rented by another account 10— Vehicle not rented 11— Incorrect request 12— Invalid location parameter 13— This rental agreement number is used by another agent 14— Passport was provided, but driver's name not specified 15— Driver's name provided, but passport not specified 16— Driver not found with provided details 17— Driver details do not match previously saved data 18— Rental agreement with this number not found 19— This rental agreement has already ended 20— Vehicle already rented 21— Incorrect driver specified 22— Existing contract closed, new one created 500— Internal server error"},{"s":"user-api-v-2","t":"User API v.2","h":["User API v.2"],"x":"User API v.2 The User API provides access to OneGPS functions and data that are allowed for a regular user. With this API, you can: view your own reports and statistics for trips, vehicles, and fuel consumption access information about your personal settings and account details request data related only to your account and your activity in the system This API does not allow access to data of other users or administrators. You can only see and work with information belonging to your own account. Use your user account username and password to authenticate."},{"s":"vehicle-list","t":"Vehicle list","h":["Vehicle list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Vehicle list This API provides comprehensive information about vehicles, including their current location, sensor data, technical specifications, and extra parameters. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters To obtain the required data, append the command to the request address. cmd=list — command to retrieve the list of vehicles. node — the number of the node where the command should be executed The format of the requested data is an array of vehicle data. Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=list&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"list\": [ { \"agentid\": 9003, \"imei\": \"356307046873054\", \"typeid\": 1, \"type\": \"Car\", \"vehiclenumber\": \"Х444МР123\", \"vin\": \"TRDFG65876\", \"folder\": \"Geo-Center OOO\", \"created_time\": 1414224633, \"current_mileage\": 881, \"status\": { \"unixtimestamp\": \"1418744535\", \"lat\": \"43.5562\", \"lon\": \"39.9084\", \"speed\": 0, \"dir\": 268, \"alt\": 246, \"satsinview\": 16 }, \"extra_params\": { \"kmlimit\": \"2\", \"mvanumber\": \"12312\", \"make_model\": \"BMW\" }, \"sensors\": [ { \"name\": \"Parking\", \"description\": \"TEST SPEED\" }, { \"name\": \"Fuel sensor\", \"description\": \"Fuel sensor\" }, { \"name\": \"External power supply\", \"description\": \"External power supply\" }, { \"name\": \"Ignition sensor\", \"description\": \"Ignition sensor\" } ], \"sensors_status\": [ { \"name\": \"Ignition sensor\", \"id\": \"1187852\", \"hum_value\": \"off\", \"dig_value\": \"0\", \"raw_value\": \"0\", \"change_ts\": \"1627467122\", \"group\": \"\" }, { \"name\": \"External power supply\", \"id\": \"1187851\", \"hum_value\": \"26.27 V\", \"dig_value\": \"26.27\", \"raw_value\": \"26272\", \"change_ts\": \"1627467122\", \"group\": \"\" }, { \"name\": \"Fuel sensor\", \"id\": \"1187850\", \"hum_value\": \"1 l\", \"dig_value\": \"1\", \"raw_value\": \"1\", \"change_ts\": \"1626336197\", \"group\": \"\" } ] } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — status code (0 indicates success) msg — status message list — an array of vehicle objects, where each object contains: agentid — unique identifier of the tracking device imei — international Mobile Equipment Identity typeid — numeric identifier of the vehicle type type — type of vehicle vehiclenumber — vehicle registration number vin — vehicle Identification Number folder — organizational group created_time — time the vehicle was added to the system (in Unix timestamp format) current_mileage — current mileage status — current status information, including: unixtimestamp — time of the latest update lat — latitude lon — longitude speed — speed dir — direction of movement (in degrees) alt — altitude above sea level satsinview — number of visible GPS satellites extra_params — additional vehicle parameters. These may vary depending on configuration and object type, and may include: kmlimit — mileage limit mvanumber — vehicle number make_model — make and model sensors — array of available sensors sensors_status — detailed status for each sensor, including: name — sensor name id — sensor identifier hum_value — calibrated human-readable value dig_value — calibrated digital value raw_value — raw, uncalibrated value change_ts — timestamp of the last value change group — sensor group"},{"s":"list-of-objects","t":"List of objects","h":["List of objects","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"List of objects This API provides information about vehicles, including their current location. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/CRIT/api.php?node=<node_id> Request parameters To receive the data, you must send an authorized request and specify the function: Username — user login Password — user password functionName=api_objectlist — command to get the list of objects node — the node number where the command should be executed Response format: an array of vehicle objects. Example request https://<server_address>/api/CRIT/api.php?node=6 Content-Type: multipart/form-data Username=TestName Password= 123456 functionName=api_objectlist Response [ { \"IMEI\": \"352016704092097\", \"PLATE\": \"5209\", \"NAME\": \"\", \"LATITUDE\": 29.7682433, \"LONGITUDE\": 39.9979749 }, { \"IMEI\": \"352016703961657\", \"PLATE\": \"6561\", \"NAME\": \"\", \"LATITUDE\": 25.9131066, \"LONGITUDE\": 46.4645949 } ] The API response returns an array of vehicle objects. Each object contains: IMEI — identifier of the tracker installed on the vehicle PLATE — vehicle license plate number NAME — vehicle name LATITUDE — current latitude of the vehicle location LONGITUDE — current longitude of the vehicle location"},{"s":"finding-the-nearest-vehicles","t":"Finding the nearest vehicles","h":["Finding the nearest vehicles","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Finding the nearest vehicles This API allows you to search for vehicles within a specified radius from a given point, sorted by distance. It is useful for rapid response scenarios (e.g., police, emergency services), where operators need to quickly determine which vehicles are closest to an incident location. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters To get the required data, add the following parameters to the URL: cmd=get_nearest_vehicles— command to get nearest vehicles group_id— ID of the vehicle group (orgdep.id) lat— latitude of the target point (from −90 to 90) lon— longitude of the target point (from −180 to 180) radius— search radius. Supports values like 500M, 1KM, 10KM, 20KM, or a numeric value in meters Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_nearest_vehicles&group_id=228&lat=43.28191&lon=76.90723&radius=50KM Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"group_id\": 123, \"group_name\": \"Police Fleet\", \"location\": { \"latitude\": 24.4539, \"longitude\": 54.3773 }, \"radius_meters\": 10000, \"vehicles_found\": 2, \"vehicles\": [ { \"vehiclenumber\": \"POL-001\", \"agentid\": 456, \"veh_id\": 789, \"imei\": \"123456789\", \"model\": \"Toyota Land Cruiser\", \"vehicle_type\": \"Car\", \"location\": { \"address\": \"Sheikh Zayed Road, Dubai, UAE\", \"latitude\": 24.4550, \"longitude\": 54.3780, \"altitude\": 10 }, \"status\": { \"active\": 1, \"speed\": 0, \"direction\": 0, \"altitude\": 10, \"satsinview\": 12, \"ignition\": 1, \"unixtimestamp\": 1706964000, \"zone\": [] }, \"distance\": { \"meters\": 1234, \"kilometers\": 1.23, \"straight_line_meters\": 1200 }, \"travel_time\": { \"seconds\": 180, \"minutes\": 3.0, \"formatted\": \"3 min\" } } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: group_id — ID of the vehicle group group_name— name of the vehicle group location— coordinates of the target point latitude — latitude longitude — longitude radius_meters — search radius in meters vehicles_found — number of vehicles found vehicles — array of vehicles with details: vehiclenumber — vehicle number agentid — agent/device ID veh_id — vehicle ID imei — device IMEI model — vehicle model vehicle_type — vehicle type (Car, Truck, etc.) location — vehicle location address — address (from geocoder) latitude, longitude, altitude status — current vehicle status active — active status (1 = active) speed — speed in km/h direction — movement direction altitude — altitude satsinview — number of satellites in view ignition — ignition status (1 = on) unixtimestamp — event timestamp in UNIX format zone — array of active geofences distance — distance to target meters — distance in meters kilometers — distance in kilometers straight_line_meters — straight-line distance in meters travel_time — estimated travel time to target seconds — in seconds minutes — in minutes formatted — formatted string, e.g., \"3 min\""},{"s":"object-information","t":"Object information","h":["Object information","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Object information This API provides detailed information about a specific vehicle. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/CRIT/api.php?node=<node_id> Request parameters To receive the data, you must send an authorized request and specify the function and the object: Username — user login Password — user password functionName=api_objectdetails — command to get object details parameters — IMEI of the object for which details are requested node — the node number where the command should be executed Response format: a single vehicle object. Example request POST https://<server_address>/api/CRIT/api.php?node=6 Content-Type: multipart/form-data Username=TestName Password= 123456 functionName=api_objectdetails parameters=352217706092097 Response { \"IMEI\": \"352016704092097\", \"PLATE\": \"5209 \", \"INSTALLATIONDATE\": \"Dec 06, 2023\", \"NAME\": \"\", \"LATITUDE\": 29.7682433, \"LONGITUDE\": 39.9979749 } The API response returns an object with vehicle information. Each field contains: IMEI — identifier of the tracker installed in the vehicle PLATE — vehicle license plate number INSTALLATIONDATE — date when the tracker was installed in the vehicle NAME — vehicle name LATITUDE — current latitude of the vehicle location LONGITUDE — current longitude of the vehicle location"},{"s":"object-history","t":"Object history","h":["Object history","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Object history This API returns the movement history of a vehicle for a specified period. It provides tracks with information about mileage, operating time, stops, start and end coordinates, and more. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/CRIT/api.php?node=<node_id> Request parameters To retrieve the data, you must send an authorized request and specify the function and parameters: Username — user login Password — user password functionName=api_objecthistory — command to get the object history parameters — a parameter string in the following format: IMEI,FROM_DATETIME,TO_DATETIME where: IMEI — object IMEI FROM_DATETIME — start date and time of the period TO_DATETIME — end date and time of the period Response format: an array of object tracks for the specified period. Example request POST https://<server_address>/api/CRIT/api.php?node=6 Content-Type: multipart/form-data Username=TestName Password= 123456 functionName=api_objecthistory parameters=352016704092097,2025-11-21 04:07:03,2025-11-23 04:07:03 Response [ { \"FLCNSMD\": 0, \"ENDTM\": \"2025-11-21 19:05:30\", \"MILEGND\": 106286.11, \"ENDLCTN\": \"Sakaka, Al Jawf Region, 3628, Saudi Arabia\", \"IDLDRTN\": \"00:00:00\", \"CNT\": 1, \"WRKNGDRTN\": \"07:47:22\", \"MILEGN\": 826.37, \"DRVR\": \"\", \"startlatitude\": 24.399892, \"OBJTID\": 1344487, \"WRKNGSTRTTM\": \"2025-11-21 11:18:08\", \"endlongitude\": 48.20568, \"startlongitude\": 39.644265, \"STPDRTN\": \"00:12:28\", \"DY\": \"2025-11-21\", \"endlatitude\": 29.960238, \"OBJCTNME\": \"5209 ب د م\", \"WRKNGSTRTLCTN\": \"Sakaka, Al Jawf Region, 3628, Saudi Arabia\", \"MXSPDP\": 136 } ] The API response returns an array of tracks for the specified period. Each track object contains the following fields: FLCNSMD — fuel consumption within this track ENDTM — track end time MILEGND — total mileage at the end of the track ENDLCTN — address of the track end point IDLDRTN — total idle time within this track CNT — track sequence number (index) for the day WRKNGDRTN — total engine operating time / ignition ON time MILEGN — mileage covered within this track DRVR — driver name startlatitude — latitude of the track start point startlongitude — longitude of the track start point endlatitude — latitude of the track end point endlongitude — longitude of the track end point OBJTID — internal object identifier WRKNGSTRTTM — time of the first ignition ON within this track STPDRTN — total stop duration within the track DY — track date OBJCTNME — object name WRKNGSTRTLCTN — address of the track start point MXSPDP — maximum speed reached in this track"},{"s":"get-the-latest-vehicle-event","t":"Get the latest vehicle event","h":["Get the latest vehicle event","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request","Response"],"x":"Get the latest vehicle event The API returns the latest event for a vehicle from the preceding 24 hours. The method supports account mapping: a user can retrieve an event for a vehicle from a mapped account if the user has access to that vehicle. How to get data Method GET Request address Use the following address to retrieve the data: https://<server_address>/api/v3/events/live Replace <server_address> with the OneGPS server address. Request parameters Add the following parameter to the request address: agent_id — the vehicle identifier in OneGPS. The vehicle must be available to the authenticated user. Example request GET https://<server_address>/api/v3/events/live?agent_id=2000 Authorization: Bearer <token> X-Node: 0 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"events\": [ { \"Cotract Number\": 1102, \"Company Name\": \"Boooch Test\", \"AgentId\": 2000, \"VehicleID\": \"10-14262 Sunny\", \"Road Type\": \"Blacktop\", \"EventID\": 4531206, \"Event Type\": 103, \"Event Message\": \"SpeedN|110|>|112|10|ss|17\", \"Event Start Time\": 1785746496, \"Event Start Speed\": null, \"Event Start location\": { \"lat\": \"25.164997\", \"lon\": \"55.235535\" }, \"Event End Time\": null, \"Event duration\": null } ], \"process_time\": 0.0222 } The API response contains: code — the request result code. A value of 0 indicates that the request was successful msg — the request result events — an array of the events found process_time — the request processing time in seconds The events array contains: Cotract Number — the identifier of the account to which the vehicle belongs Company Name — the account name AgentId — the vehicle identifier in OneGPS VehicleID — the vehicle name Road Type — the road surface type. The current version returns Blacktop EventID — the event identifier Event Type — the event type Event Message — the event message Event Start Time — the event start time as a Unix timestamp Event Start Speed — the speed at the beginning of the event. If the data is unavailable, the method returns null Event Start location — the vehicle coordinates at the beginning of the event: lat — latitude lon — longitude Event End Time — the event end time as a Unix timestamp. If the data is unavailable, the method returns null Event duration — the event duration in seconds. It is calculated as the event end time minus the event start time. If either value is unavailable, the method returns null"},{"s":"current-vehicle-status","t":"Current vehicle status","h":["Current vehicle status","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Examples","Request by IMEI","Request by multiple agent IDs"],"x":"Current vehicle status This API is used to obtain the current status information of one or more vehicles, including data on location, speed, sensor readings, and technical condition. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters To obtain the required data, append parameters to the request address. Parameters are specified after the question mark ? and are separated by the ampersand &. cmd=status — command to retrieve status information imei — unique identifier of the GPS tracker agents — list of agent IDs, specified as a comma-separated list to request statuses of multiple vehicles veh_numbers — registration number of the vehicle, exactly as it appears in the system, with strict adherence to letter and number case node — node number where the command is executed At least one of the following parameters must be present in the request: imei, agents, or veh_numbers. Examples Request by IMEI https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=status&imei=357073296325265&node=1 Request by multiple agent IDs https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=status&agents=51594,44841&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": [ { \"agentid\": 44841, \"imei\": \"865473034076573\", \"configuration\": \"Teltonika FM2200\", \"typeid\": 27, \"type\": \"Car\", \"vehiclenumber\": \"NURKA\", \"folder\": \"REAL\", \"created_time\": 1518167398, \"uniqid\": \"865473034076573\", \"current_mileage\": 65345, \"initial_mileage\": 0, \"driver_name\": null, \"driver_phone\": null, \"model\": \"ВАЗ (Lada) Kalina\", \"info\": \" test\", \"vin\": \"XTA219470K0259832\", \"status\": { \"active\": 1, \"speed\": 0, \"direction\": 27, \"lat\": \"47.119755\", \"lon\": \"39.667492\", \"alt\": 0, \"satsinview\": 16, \"unixtimestamp\": \"1621432378\", \"moving\": 0, \"parking\": 9267, \"firing\": 0 }, \"sensors_status\": [ { \"name\": \"Ignition sensor\", \"id\": \"150549\", \"hum_value\": \"выкл.\", \"dig_value\": \"0\", \"raw_value\": \"12494\", \"change_ts\": \"1621423461\", \"group\": \"GROUP!\" }, { \"name\": \"Датчик топлива\", \"id\": \"368013\", \"hum_value\": \"1249.4 -\", \"dig_value\": \"1249.4\", \"raw_value\": \"12494\", \"change_ts\": \"1621432378\", \"group\": \"myGroup\" } ] } ], \"reqest_time\": 0.0642 } The response is returned in JSON format and contains: imei — unique number of the tracker distance — distance traveled during the specified period agent_id — identifier of the tracking device account_id — account number in the system code — status code (0 indicates success) msg — message about the status of the request data — array of objects containing information about the vehicles: agentid — contract identifier imei — International Mobile Equipment Identity vin — vehicle identification number configuration — model of the tracker typeid — numerical identifier of the vehicle type type — type of vehicle vehiclenumber — registration number of the vehicle folder — organizational group or branch created_time — creation time in Unix timestamp format uniqid — unique identifier current_mileage — current mileage initial_mileage — initial mileage driver_name — name of the driver driver_phone — phone number of the driver model — model of the vehicle info — additional information status — information about the current status: active — whether the object is active (1 for yes, 0 for no) speed — current speed direction — current direction in degrees lat — current latitude lon — current longitude alt — current altitude satsinview — number of visible satellites unixtimestamp — time of the last update moving — time spent moving in seconds parking — time spent parked in seconds firing — ignition status (1 for on, 0 for off) sensors_status — array containing information about the sensors: name — name of the sensor id — identifier of the sensor hum_value — actual value after calibration dig_value — digital value after calibration raw_value — raw value before calibration change_ts — time of the last value change in Unix timestamp format group — sensor group request_time — time taken to process the request in seconds"},{"s":"daily-vehicle-summary","t":"Daily vehicle summary","h":["Daily vehicle summary","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Daily vehicle summary This API retrieves data about a vehicle over the past 24 hours, including mileage, fuel level, refueling, and fuel consumption. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=vehinfo_24_hours — command to get 24-hour vehicle info agent_id — vehicle ID (can specify multiple IDs separated by commas) imei — device IMEI (can specify multiple IMEIs separated by commas) ts — timestamp (Unix timestamp) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=vehinfo_24_hours&imei=10518,165182&ts=1617710825&node=1 Response { \"10518\": { \"total_mileage\": 745851.44, \"fuel_start\": 229.37, \"fuel_stop\": 229.37, \"fuel_fill\": 409.6, \"fuel_drain\": 153.15, \"fuel_consumption_l\": 256.45, \"fuel_consumption_l_per_100km\": 35, \"fuel_consumption_norm_l\": 161.5658, \"fuel_consumption_norm_l_per_100km\": 22, \"engine_hours\": 0 }, \"165182\": { \"total_mileage\": 100946.68, \"fuel_start\": 0, \"fuel_stop\": 0, \"fuel_fill\": 0, \"fuel_drain\": 0, \"fuel_consumption_l\": 0, \"fuel_consumption_l_per_100km\": 0, \"fuel_consumption_norm_l\": 22.313, \"fuel_consumption_norm_l_per_100km\": 10, \"engine_hours\": 0 } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: total_mileage — total mileage fuel_start — fuel level at the start of the period fuel_stop — fuel level at the end of the period fuel_fill — fuel added (refueling) fuel_drain — fuel removed (draining) fuel_consumption_l — actual fuel consumed (liters) fuel_consumption_l_per_100km — fuel consumption per 100 km fuel_consumption_norm_l — normative fuel consumption (liters) fuel_consumption_norm_l_per_100km — normative fuel consumption per 100 km engine_hours — engine hours"},{"s":"link-contract-to-vehicle-","t":"Link contract to vehicle","h":["Link contract to vehicle","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Link contract to vehicle This API links a contract to a vehicle, specifying its license plate, contract start date, contract name, and distance. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=link_contract — command to link the contract PlateNum — vehicle license plate number Date — contract start date (Unix timestamp) Name — contract name Distance — distance (e.g., mileage under the contract) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=link_contract&PlateNum=test12345&Distance=1111&Date=1623927270&Name=1212&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"The linking is successful\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code— result code (0 = success) msg— server message confirming the action"},{"s":"unlink-contract-from-vehicle","t":"Unlink contract from vehicle","h":["Unlink contract from vehicle","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Unlink contract from vehicle This API unlinks a contract from a vehicle. How to get data Method DELETE Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=unlink_contract — command to unlink the contract PlateNum — vehicle license plate number node — node number where the command should be executed (optional) Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=unlink_contract&PlateNum=test12345&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Deletion is successful\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message about the result of the action"},{"s":"update-vehicle-custom-fields","t":"Update vehicle custom fields","h":["Update vehicle custom fields","Request address","Headers","Request parameters","Example requests","Example response"],"x":"Update vehicle custom fields This API updates a vehicle’s custom fields in a single request by agent_id or imei. You can update all fields or just a subset; the number of fields is not limited. Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php Headers Content-Type: application/json Authorization: Basic <your_credentials_b64> Request parameters cmd=agent_update_custom_fields — API command agent_id — vehicle identifier (use if you don’t send imei) imei — device IMEI (use if you don’t send agent_id) custom_fields — object with key–value pairs for updating custom fields In custom_fields you can include any number of fields, e.g., extra_field1, extra_field2, … extra_fieldN (as long as these fields exist in the account). Values may be numbers, strings, or booleans. Example requests Update multiple fields by agent_id: curl -X POST 'https://<server_address>/api/api.php' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H 'Authorization: Basic <your_credentials_b64>' -d '{ \"cmd\": \"agent_update_custom_fields\", \"agent_id\": 30547, \"custom_fields\": { \"extra_field1\": \"Waybill #123\", \"extra_field2\": \"2025-11-01\", \"extra_field7\": \"+79000000000\" } }' Update by imei: curl -X POST 'https://<server_address>/api/api.php' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H 'Authorization: Basic <your_credentials_b64>' -d '{ \"cmd\": \"agent_update_custom_fields\", \"imei\": \"359339099999999\", \"custom_fields\": { \"extra_field3\": \"Pass A12345\", \"extra_field4\": \"2025-12-31\" } }' Example response { \"code\": 1, \"msg\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — execution code: 1 = success, 0 = error msg — textual result (e.g., \"OK\" or an error message)"},{"s":"mileage-and-fuel-level-","t":"Mileage and fuel level","h":["Mileage and fuel level","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Mileage and fuel level This API gets information about a vehicle’s mileage and fuel level at a specific moment in time. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=istatus — API command to get the status imei — unique vehicle identifier in OneGPS unixtimestamp — timestamp for the requested moment (Unix timestamp) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=istatus&unixtimestamp=1408840780&imei=356495042533101&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"total_run\": 0, \"total_run_by_can\": 0, \"engine_hours\": 0, \"fuel_sensors\": [ { \"info\": \"Fuel Sensor 1\", \"id\": 150325, \"fieldname\": \"t_53lls1\", \"fuel\": 0 }, { \"info\": \"Fuel Sensor 2\", \"id\": 150326, \"fieldname\": \"lls2\", \"fuel\": 0 } ], \"fuel\": 0, \"unixtimestamp\": 1408840780 } In response to the API request, the following is returned: total_run — total mileage of the vehicle at the requested time (km) total_run_by_can — mileage according to CAN bus data (km) engine_hours — engine hours in seconds fuel — fuel level in the tank at the requested time (liters) fuel_sensors — array of fuel sensors (if multiple sensors exist), each containing: info — sensor description id — unique sensor ID fieldname — system field name of the sensor fuel — fuel level recorded by this sensor (liters) unixtimestamp — timestamp for which the data is returned (Unix timestamp)"},{"s":"mileage-for-selected-period","t":"Mileage for selected period","h":["Mileage for selected period","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Mileage for selected period This API returns the mileage and engine hours of a vehicle within the selected time range. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET Request parameters cmd=istatus — command to get mileage imei — object identifier in the system start — start of the interval (Unix timestamp) stop — end of the interval (Unix timestamp) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=istatus&imei=6794i&start=1664917200&stop=1665003600&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"run\": 126.79500000000007, \"run_by_can\": 0, \"motorhours\": 9897, \"unixtimestamp_start\": 1664917200, \"unixtimestamp_stop\": 1665003600 } In response to the API request, the following is returned: run — mileage over the period, km run_by_can — mileage from CAN data, km motorhours — engine hours over the period, seconds unixtimestamp_start — start of the interval (Unix timestamp) unixtimestamp_stop — end of the interval (Unix timestamp)"},{"s":"trip-statistics-and-distance","t":"Trip statistics and distance","h":["Trip statistics and distance","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Trip statistics and distance This API provides information about mileage, trips, and parkings of a vehicle for a specified time period. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=distance — API command to get mileage data imei — unique identifier of the vehicle/device start — start time of data collection (Unix timestamp) stop — end time of data collection (Unix timestamp) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=distance&imei=7157153151020&start=1676926800&stop=1677013200&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"distance\": { \"duration\": 23740, \"length\": 104.54, \"start\": 1761944400, \"stop\": 1762030800, \"can\": 0, \"usecan\": false, \"gps\": 104.54, \"parking_time\": 62237, \"parking_count\": 14, \"max_speed\": 92, \"avg_speed\": 18, \"trips_cnt\": 6, \"trips_ts\": 1761947653, \"trips_te\": 1762027933, \"trips_time\": 80280, \"trips_driving\": 18043, \"parkings\": { \"1761948742\": { \"ts\": 1761948742, \"te\": 1761949164, \"lat\": \"25.404495\", \"lon\": \"55.443546\", \"track_id\": 341967622 }, \"1761950225\": { \"ts\": 1761950225, \"te\": 1761978656, \"lat\": \"25.380997\", \"lon\": \"55.434662\" }, \"1761978976\": { \"ts\": 1761978976, \"te\": 1761979445, \"lat\": \"25.386534\", \"lon\": \"55.439198\", \"track_id\": 342008717 }, \"1761983958\": { \"ts\": 1761983958, \"te\": 1761984933, \"lat\": \"25.386375\", \"lon\": \"55.413776\", \"track_id\": 342008717 }, \"1761986309\": { \"ts\": 1761986309, \"te\": 1761988132, \"lat\": \"25.341892\", \"lon\": \"55.397186\", \"track_id\": 342008717 }, \"1761988857\": { \"ts\": 1761988857, \"te\": 1761989297, \"lat\": \"25.368885\", \"lon\": \"55.42438\", \"track_id\": 342008717 }, \"1761989685\": { \"ts\": 1761989685, \"te\": 1762002192, \"lat\": \"25.380968\", \"lon\": \"55.434662\" }, \"1762002484\": { \"ts\": 1762002484, \"te\": 1762002826, \"lat\": \"25.378464\", \"lon\": \"55.442036\", \"track_id\": 342062148 }, \"1762003344\": { \"ts\": 1762003344, \"te\": 1762003645, \"lat\": \"25.381042\", \"lon\": \"55.434467\", \"track_id\": 342062148 }, \"1762005988\": { \"ts\": 1762005988, \"te\": 1762006366, \"lat\": \"25.316278\", \"lon\": \"55.382927\", \"track_id\": 342062148 }, \"1762007656\": { \"ts\": 1762007656, \"te\": 1762008203, \"lat\": \"25.354523\", \"lon\": \"55.382133\", \"track_id\": 342062148 }, \"1762010014\": { \"ts\": 1762010014, \"te\": 1762012025, \"lat\": \"25.381067\", \"lon\": \"55.434717\" }, \"1762012439\": { \"ts\": 1762012439, \"te\": 1762021038, \"lat\": \"25.369600\", \"lon\": \"55.435860\" }, \"1762022306\": { \"ts\": 1762022306, \"te\": 1762027298, \"lat\": \"25.380917\", \"lon\": \"55.434715\" } }, \"tracks\": { \"341967622\": { \"start\": 1761947653, \"stop\": 1761950225, \"ts\": 1761947653, \"te\": 1761950225, \"length\": 22.83, \"can\": null, \"gps\": 22.83, \"max_speed\": 92, \"avg_speed\": 26, \"parkings\": [1761948742], \"parking_count\": 1, \"parking_time\": 422, \"startlat\": \"25.381645\", \"stoplat\": \"25.380997\", \"startlon\": \"55.528587\", \"stoplon\": \"55.434662\" }, \"342008717\": { \"start\": 1761978656, \"stop\": 1761989685, \"ts\": 1761978656, \"te\": 1761989685, \"length\": 43.99, \"can\": null, \"gps\": 43.99, \"max_speed\": 72, \"avg_speed\": 18, \"parkings\": [1761978976, 1761983958, 1761986309, 1761988857], \"parking_count\": 4, \"parking_time\": 3707, \"startlat\": \"25.380960\", \"stoplat\": \"25.380968\", \"startlon\": \"55.434743\", \"stoplon\": \"55.434662\" }, \"342062148\": { \"start\": 1762002192, \"stop\": 1762010014, \"ts\": 1762002192, \"te\": 1762010014, \"length\": 28.78, \"can\": null, \"gps\": 28.78, \"max_speed\": 61, \"avg_speed\": 14, \"parkings\": [1762002484, 1762003344, 1762005988, 1762007656], \"parking_count\": 4, \"parking_time\": 1568, \"startlat\": \"25.381003\", \"stoplat\": \"25.381067\", \"startlon\": \"55.434752\", \"stoplon\": \"55.434717\" }, \"342078382\": { \"start\": 1762012025, \"stop\": 1762012439, \"ts\": 1762012025, \"te\": 1762012439, \"length\": 2.32, \"can\": null, \"gps\": 2.32, \"max_speed\": 34, \"avg_speed\": 19, \"parkings\": [], \"parking_count\": 0, \"parking_time\": 0, \"startlat\": \"25.381067\", \"stoplat\": \"25.369600\", \"startlon\": \"55.434717\", \"stoplon\": \"55.435860\" }, \"342089059\": { \"start\": 1762021038, \"stop\": 1762022306, \"ts\": 1762021038, \"te\": 1762022306, \"length\": 3.97, \"can\": null, \"gps\": 3.97, \"max_speed\": 49, \"avg_speed\": 11, \"parkings\": [], \"parking_count\": 0, \"parking_time\": 0, \"startlat\": \"25.369557\", \"stoplat\": \"25.380917\", \"startlon\": \"55.435802\", \"stoplon\": \"55.434715\" }, \"342095630\": { \"start\": 1762027298, \"stop\": 1762027933, \"ts\": 1762027298, \"te\": 1762027933, \"length\": 2.65, \"can\": null, \"gps\": 2.65, \"max_speed\": 32, \"avg_speed\": 13, \"parkings\": [], \"parking_count\": 0, \"parking_time\": 0, \"startlat\": \"25.380903\", \"stoplat\": \"25.380952\", \"startlon\": \"55.434485\", \"stoplon\": \"55.434668\" } }, \"startlat\": \"25.381645\", \"startlon\": \"55.528587\", \"stoplat\": \"25.380952\", \"stoplon\": \"55.434668\", \"active_days\": 1, \"range_dates\": { \"1761944400\": 1 } } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: duration — total trip duration in seconds length — actual distance traveled in kilometers max_speed — maximum speed in km/h avg_speed — average speed in km/h trips_cnt — number of trips trips_time — total trip time in seconds trips_driving — time spent driving without stops in seconds parking_count — number of parking events parking_time — total parking time in seconds parkings — an array of parking events, each parking event includes: ts — parking start time te — parking end time lat — latitude lon — longitude length — parking duration in kilometers track_id — track identifier associated with the parking event. If the parking is linked to a track, this field will contain a unique track ID. tracks — an array of trip data, each trip includes: length — trip distance max_speed — maximum speed avg_speed — average speed startlat, startlon, stoplat, stoplon — coordinates of the start and end points of the trip"},{"s":"odometer-and-fuel-for-selected-period","t":"Odometer and fuel for selected period","h":["Odometer and fuel for selected period","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters"],"x":"Odometer and fuel for selected period This API retrieves information about vehicle mileage (odometer readings) and fuel consumption over a selected time interval. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET Request parameters cmd=odo_and_fuel — command to fetch odometer and fuel data imei — device identifier (object ID) start — start time of the period (Unix timestamp) stop — end time of the period (Unix timestamp) node — node number on which the command is executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=odo_and_fuel&start=1767214800&stop=1767560400&imei=861076084529922&node=6 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"ok\", \"data\": { \"fuel\": 7.6, \"start_odo\": 407121.6, \"stop_odo\": 407229.4 } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message fuel — amount of fuel consumed during the period start_odo — odometer reading at the beginning of the period stop_odo — odometer reading at the end of the period"},{"s":"fuel-consumption-and-idle-statistics","t":"Fuel consumption and idle statistics","h":["Fuel consumption and idle statistics","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Fuel consumption and idle statistics This API provides data on how much fuel was consumed during the selected time period, as well as how long the vehicle was idling. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=fuelconsumtion — API command to get the report imei — unique vehicle identifier start — start time of the data range (Unix timestamp) stop — end time of the data range (Unix timestamp) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=fuelconsumtion&imei=7157153151020&start=1644741475&stop=1644914275&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": { \"7157153151020\": [ { \"idle\": 98872, \"fuel consumed\": 457.92 } ] }, \"message\": \"success\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success, other values = error) data — object data for the selected vehicle: idle — engine idling time (in seconds) fuel consumed — amount of fuel consumed during the selected period (liters) message — response status message"},{"s":"vehicle-status-and-distance-to-point-or-geofence","t":"Vehicle status and distance to point or geofence","h":["Vehicle status and distance to point or geofence","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example requests","Response"],"x":"Vehicle status and distance to point or geofence This API provides the current status of a vehicle and the time and distance to a specified point or geofence. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=get_status_and_distance — command to get the vehicle status and distance vehnum — garage number or license plate of the vehicle; multiple values can be specified separated by commas zone — geofence name (if you want to calculate distance to a geofence) point — coordinates of a point in the format latitude,longitude (if you want distance to a specific point) node — node number where the command should be executed Example requests https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_status_and_distance&vehnum=M897ET777&zone=Hospital%20Lapino&node=1 https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_status_and_distance&vehnum=M897ET777&point=55.923032,37.659668&node=1 Response { \"cstatus\": 6, \"cstatus_timestamp\": 1554985159, \"destination_name\": \"Zero coordinates\", \"distance\": { \"code\": 0, \"length\": 27613, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"point\": \"55.923032,37.659668\", \"time\": 2002, \"type\": \"point\" }, \"ignition\": 1, \"rbu\": \"08\", \"sensors\": { \"Unloading Sensor\": \"on|1554985138\", \"Loading Sensor\": \"on|1554985138\", \"Ignition Sensor\": \"on|1554985138\", \"FUEL SENSORS OPERATION SENSORS\": \"Operating|1554985138\", \"Fuel ... fuel\": \"202.96 Liter|1554995669\" }, \"source_name\": \"Loading_Chertanovo\", \"status\": 4, \"status_timestamp\": 1554985072, \"unixtimestamp\": 1554995864, \"vehiclenumber\": \"M897ET777\", \"zone\": [] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: cstatus — current status of the vehicle cstatus_timestamp — timestamp of the current status (Unix timestamp) destination_name — name of the destination point distance — object containing information about distance and time to a point or geofence: length — distance in meters time — time in seconds type — type of object (point or zone) point — coordinates of the point (if type=point) ignition — ignition state (1 = on) rbu — RBU identifier sensors — status of various sensors (e.g., loading/unloading, fuel) source_name — name of the data source status — current status of the vehicle status_timestamp — timestamp of the last status change unixtimestamp — timestamp when the data was retrieved vehiclenumber — vehicle license plate or garage number zone — array of current geofences (if any)"},{"s":"vehicle-idle-time","t":"Vehicle idle time","h":["Vehicle idle time","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example API request","Example response"],"x":"Vehicle idle time This API allows you to get information about vehicle idle periods. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET Request parameters Parameters can be sent via the query string or in the POST request body: cmd=get_idle — command to retrieve idle periods node — the number of the node where the command should be executed time_start — start of the period (timestamp, unixtime) time_end — end of the period (timestamp, unixtime) imei — IMEI of the monitoring device. If there are multiple devices, separate them with commas: imei=887556,io9987,76573 Example API request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_idle&time_start=16878956&time_end=16878755&imei=887556,io9987,76573&node=1 Example response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": { \"2914\": [ {\"idle_start\": \"1641814740\", \"idle_end\": 1641814984}, {\"idle_start\": 1641815547, \"idle_end\": 1641825619}, {\"idle_start\": 1641827105, \"idle_end\": 1641893307}, {\"idle_start\": 1641893623, \"idle_end\": 1641898037} // ... other entries ... ] }, \"message\": \"success\" } The API response contains: code — result code: 0 — success, 1 — error data — a dictionary where keys are vehicle identifiers and values are lists of idle periods: idle_start — start time of the idle period (timestamp) idle_end — end time of the idle period (timestamp) message — text message with the result of the request"},{"s":"tracking-link-for-object-","t":"Tracking link for object","h":["Tracking link for object","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Tracking link for object This API is used for getting a temporary link to track an object on the map, with activation and deactivation based on specified geofences. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=get_temp_link — command to get a temporary link imei — unique identifier of the object (vehicle) in the system startgeo — geofence for link activation stopgeo — geofence for link deactivation max_duration — maximum duration of the link in minutes (default: 1440 minutes / 24 hours) Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_temp_link&imei=356307045913463&startgeo=Rostov test API&stopgeo=Rostov exit test API&max_duration=800 Response https://<server_address>/monitor_token.php?token=1f87a19c068ad47f3f769dc151527794&lang=en&zoom=10&startgeo=Rostov test API&stopgeo=Rostov exit test API The API returns a direct link to the map with a temporary token. The link respects the specified geofences for activation (startgeo) and deactivation (stopgeo)."},{"s":"send-command-to-vehicle","t":"Send command to vehicle","h":["Send command to vehicle","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Send command to vehicle This API sends a command to a vehicle using a pre-created command template in the Commandsmodule. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=send_command — API command to send a command to a vehicle agent_id — vehicle (agent) identifier template_name — name of the pre-created command template node — node number where the command should be executed (optional) Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=send_command&agent_id=139849&template_name=test123&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": null, \"command_id\": 3989 } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message about the result of the operation command_id — identifier of the sent command"},{"s":"sent-command-status","t":"Sent command status","h":["Sent command status","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Sent command status This API retrieves the status of a complex command, including the overall status and the status/responses from all objects and sub-commands included in the complex command. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=get_command_status — API command to request the status of a command command_id — identifier of the complex command node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_command_status&command_id=3989&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"message\": \"\", \"detailed_status\": { \"43207\": [ { \"type\": \"GPRS\", \"status\": \"accepted\", \"response\": \"Ver: 03.25.15_88 GPS: AXN_3.80_3333 Hw: FMB010 Mod: 3 IMEI: 352094083043221 Init: 2024-3-30 12:29 Uptime: 505289 MAC: 001E428490AF SPC: 0 (0) AXL: 0 OBD :0 BL:0,2 BT:3\" }, { \"type\": \"GPRS\", \"status\": \"accepted\", \"response\": \"Data channel: 1 GPRS: 1 Phone: 0 SIM: 0 OP: 25001 Signal: 4 New SMS: 0 Roaming: 0 SMSFull: 0 LAC: 10 Cell ID: 11522 NetType: 1 FwUpd: -65536\" } ] }, \"general_status\": \"completed\", \"version\": \"7.2.0\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) message — server message about the result detailed_status — associative array with detailed information for each agent: key (e.g., 43207) — agent identifier type — type of communication channel status — command execution status (accepted, executed, etc.) response — text response from the device general_status — overall status of the complex command (pending, completed, failed) version — system version"},{"s":"fuel-card-transactionss","t":"Fuel card transactionss","h":["Fuel card transactionss","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Fuel card transactionss Use this API to retrieve data about vehicle refueling over a selected period. With it, you can: view fuel card transactions (fuel purchases, card balance, fuel type, gas station) get data on fuel refills and drains from the vehicle’s sensor (coordinates, fuel amount, event location) monitor fuel levels at the start and end of each day for every vehicle How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=fuel — command to get fuel data imei — vehicle identifier in the system start — start time of the period (Unix timestamp) stop — end time of the period (Unix timestamp) addr — адрес заправок node — node number on which to execute the command Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=fuel&imei=863591021267806&start=1404158400&stop=1406836800&addr =1&node=1 The JSON response contains three main sections: 1. Fuel Cards (cards) Array of cards with information about each card and its transactions: \"cards\": [ { \"id\": 601, \"card_num\": \"257464691\", \"balance\": \"0\", \"type_name\": \"Magistral\", \"transactions\": { \"1404206488\": { \"operation\": \"Purchase\", \"ts\": 1404206488, \"azs\": \"Gas Station Name\", \"liters\": \"20.0000\", \"sum\": \"636.00\", \"sumg\": \"658.26\", \"fuel\": \"Diesel\", \"price\": \"31.80\", \"priceg\": \"32.91\", \"saldo\": \"0.00\" } } } ] id — card ID in OneGPS system card_num — card number balance — card balance at the time of the query type_name — card type transactions — fuel card transactions: operation — type of operation (Purchase/Return, etc.) ts — transaction timestamp azs — gas station name liters — fuel volume sum — discounted amount sumg — full amount fuel — fuel type price — discounted price per liter priceg — full price per liter saldo — card balance at the transaction 2. Sensor Fillings/Drains (fillings) Object where keys are event timestamps and values contain data about fuel refills or drains: \"fillings\": { \"1404205495\": { \"ts\": 1404205495, \"type_id\": 1, // 1 = refill, 2 = drain \"liters\": \"23.2862\", \"lat\": \"51.9899\", \"lon\": \"47.8203\", \"address\": \"RU, Saratov Region, Balakovo\", \"fuel_start\": \"223.68\" // fuel level at start of event } } ts — event time (Unix timestamp) type_id — event type (1 = refill, 2 = drain) liters — fuel amount lat/ lon — coordinates address — location description fuel_start — fuel level at the start of the event 3. Daily Fuel Balances (fuel) Object where keys are day start timestamps and values contain fuel balances: \"fuel\": { \"1404072000\": { \"ts\": 1404072000, // day start timestamp \"te\": 1404158400, // day end timestamp \"start\": \"4.76\", // fuel at start of day (liters) \"stop\": \"18.93\" // fuel at end of day (liters) } } ts — day start timestamp te — day end timestamp start — fuel at the start of the day stop — fuel at the end of the day"},{"s":"add-fuel-card-transaction","t":"Add fuel card transaction","h":["Add fuel card transaction","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Add fuel card transaction This API adds a fuel transaction record for a fuel card, including information about the amount of fuel, cost, location, and driver. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters Required: cmd=add_fuel_transaction — API command to add a transaction card_num — fuel card number operation — type of operation (e.g., refill) unixtimestamp — transaction time in Unix timestamp format place — location of the operation azs — name of the fuel station liters — amount of fuel in liters fuel — fuel type price — price per liter without discount sum — total amount without discount node — node number where the command should be executed Optional: priceg — price per liter with discount sumg — total amount with discount driver — driver’s full name lat — latitude of the transaction location lon — longitude of the transaction location info — additional information internal_id — internal transaction identifier Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=add_fuel_transaction&node=1 Example request body (JSON): { \"card_num\": \"1234567890123456\", \"operation\": \"refill\", \"unixtimestamp\": 1695000000, \"place\": \"Main street 12\", \"azs\": \"WOG\", \"liters\": 50, \"fuel\": \"Diesel\", \"price\": 55.0, \"sum\": 2750.0, \"driver\": \"Ivan Ivanov\" } Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message about the operation result"},{"s":"fuel-station-list-","t":"Fuel station list","h":["Fuel station list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Fuel station list This API gets a list of fuel stations (gas stations) including coordinates, type, and description. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=azs — API command to get the list of fuel stations node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=azs&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"list\": [ { \"lat\": \"50.5\", \"lon\": \"30.3643\", \"type_id\": 8, \"type_name\": \"RosBerlio\", \"descr\": \"<b>WOG Gas Station</b><br>Phone: (+38033)2787914<br>Address: Okr. Road, Berkovetskaya St, 6, between Varshavska and Zhytomyr Tr., near Auchan and Epicenter\" }, { \"lat\": \"50.6586\", \"lon\": \"29.8808\", \"type_id\": 8, \"type_name\": \"RosBerlio\", \"descr\": \"<b>AviaCom Gas Station</b><br>Phone: (+38045)7752175<br>Address: 56 km of M-07 (E-373) Kyiv-Kovel-Yagodyn-Lublin\" }, { \"lat\": \"55.551\", \"lon\": \"53.4085\", \"type_id\": 8, \"type_name\": \"RosBerlio\", \"descr\": \"<b>Proton-A LLC Gas Station</b><br>Phone: 8-919-680-07-77<br>Address: 1130 km of M7 highway, Tatarstan, Aktanyshsky District (left side)\" } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message (e.g., \"OK\") list — array of fuel station objects, each containing: lat — latitude of the station lon — longitude of the station type_id — fuel station type ID type_name — fuel station type name descr — station description including address and contact phone"},{"s":"trip-report-2","t":"Trip report","h":["Trip report","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example requests"],"x":"Trip report This API is used for getting information about a vehicle’s trips over a specified period. When the Geofences module is enabled, each trip can be split into segments based on entry and exit from geofences. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=run — command to get the trip report imei — unique identifier of the object (vehicle) start — start time of the period (Unix timestamp) stop — end time of the period (Unix timestamp) zones — include geozone information: — 0 — tracks are returned as whole trips, without splitting by zones — 1— trips are split into segments based on entry/exit from geozones eco— include eco-driving data in tracks node — node number on which to execute the command Example requests https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=run&imei=359772039289781&start=1406318400&stop=1406404800&zones=1&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"zones\": { \"1406341994\": { \"ts\": 1406341994, \"stopuid\": \"213854753412306\", \"stopname\": \"Garage\", \"lat\": \"53.3861\", \"lon\": \"83.7127\", \"msgtype\": 2 }, \"1406353422\": { \"ts\": 1406353422, \"stopuid\": \"966971618589014\", \"stopname\": \"Barnaul Bus Station\", \"lat\": \"53.3508\", \"lon\": \"83.7593\", \"msgtype\": 1 } }, \"tracks\": { \"1406341955\": { \"ts\": 1406341955, \"te\": 1406341994, \"length\": 0.26, \"maxspeed\": \"17\", \"avgspeed\": 17 }, \"1406341994\": { \"ts\": 1406341994, \"te\": 1406353389, \"length\": 119.83, \"maxspeed\": \"96\", \"avgspeed\": 58 } } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: Zones array (zones) — geofence entry and exit points: ts — event time (Unix timestamp) stopuid — unique geofence identifier stopname — geofence name lat — latitude of the point lon — longitude of the point msgtype — event type (1 = entry, 2 = exit) Tracks array (tracks) — trip information: ts — trip start time (Unix timestamp) te— trip end time (Unix timestamp) length — trip distance in kilometers maxspeed— maximum speed during the trip (km/h) avgspeed — average speed during the trip (km/h) lats — latitude of trip start lons — longitude of trip start late — latitude of trip end lone — longitude of trip end eco — eco-driving data (if eco parameter is enabled): Acceleration, Braking, Speed — metrics for each parameter"},{"s":"parking-and-stops-report","t":"Parking and stops report","h":["Parking and stops report","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Parking and stops report This API is used for getting information about vehicle stops and parking events over a specified period. You can get coordinates, duration, and the address of the stop or parking location. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=runstops — API command for getting stop and parking report imei— vehicle identifier in the system start— start of the period (Unix timestamp) stop— end of the period (Unix timestamp) node— node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=runstops&start=1676840400&stop=1676926800&imei=16518&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"stops\": [ { \"ts\": 1676840400, \"te\": 1676873376, \"dur\": 32976, \"lat\": \"56.733388\", \"lon\": \"61.051853\", \"address\": { \"road\": \"R-351\", \"county\": \"Beloyarsky Urban District\", \"state\": \"Sverdlovsk Oblast\", \"region\": \"Ural Federal District\", \"postcode\": \"624053\", \"country\": \"Russia\", \"country_code\": \"ru\" } }, { \"ts\": 1676875342, \"te\": 1676880065, \"dur\": 4723, \"lat\": \"56.893278\", \"lon\": \"60.770452\", \"address\": { \"road\": \"Yekaterinburg Ring Road\", \"county\": \"Beryozovsky Urban District\", \"state\": \"Sverdlovsk Oblast\", \"region\": \"Ural Federal District\", \"postcode\": \"623703\", \"country\": \"Russia\", \"country_code\": \"ru\" } } ], \"parkings\": [ { \"ts\": 1676887881, \"te\": 1676889637, \"dur\": 1756, \"lat\": \"56.781017\", \"lon\": \"58.40189\", \"address\": { \"road\": \"Access to Zobnina from km 216+740 of Perm – Yekaterinburg highway\", \"county\": \"Achitsky District\", \"state\": \"Sverdlovsk Oblast\", \"region\": \"Ural Federal District\", \"postcode\": \"623235\", \"country\": \"Russia\", \"country_code\": \"ru\" } } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message (e.g., \"OK\") stops — array of stop data: ts— stop start time (Unix timestamp) te— stop end time (Unix timestamp) dur— duration of the stop in seconds lat— latitude of the stop lon— longitude of the stop address— address details of the stop (street, district/county, state, region, postal code, country, country code) parkings— array of parking data, structured similarly to stops"},{"s":"trip-report-with-geolocation","t":"Trip report with geolocation","h":["Trip report with geolocation","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Trip report with geolocation This API provides a detailed report on a vehicle’s movements during a selected period, including coordinates and geographic information (address, district, city, etc.). The data includes start and end points of each trip, average and maximum speed, trip length, and location details based on geo-coordinates. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=rungeo — command to retrieve the trip report imei — unique identifier of the vehicle in the system start — start time of the report period (Unix timestamp) stop — end time of the report period (Unix timestamp) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=rungeo&imei=356307045913463&start=1439413200&stop=1439586000&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"tracks\": { \"1439443509\": { \"avgspeed\": 35.3889, \"length\": 12.7304, \"maxspeed\": 59, \"ts\": 1439443509, \"te\": 1439445837, \"slat\": \"47.2037\", \"slon\": \"39.6325\", \"elat\": \"47.2037\", \"elon\": \"39.6325\", \"sgeo\": { \"city\": \"Rostov-on-Don\", \"road\": \"2-ya Krasnodarskaya ulitsa\", \"house_number\": \"80 к13\", \"state\": \"Rostov Oblast\", \"postcode\": \"344091\" }, \"egeo\": { \"city\": \"Rostov-on-Don\", \"road\": \"2-ya Krasnodarskaya Street\", \"house_number\": \"80 к13\", \"state\": \"Rostov Oblast\", \"postcode\": \"344091\" } } } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — execution status (0 = success) msg — status message tracks — object, where keys are trip start timestamps (Unix) and values contain trip data For each trip: avgspeed — average speed (km/h) maxspeed — maximum speed (km/h) length — route length (km) ts — trip start time (Unix timestamp) te — trip end time (Unix timestamp) slat, slon — start coordinates (latitude, longitude) elat, elon — end coordinates (latitude, longitude) sgeo — start location address (city, street, house, postal code) egeo — end location address (city, street, house, postal code)"},{"s":"track-data-","t":"Track data","h":["Track data","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Track data This API loads detailed route points (track data) for a trip previously obtained via the rungeo method. Each track point contains coordinates, speed, and its absolute recording time as a Unix timestamp. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=gettrack — command to retrieve the track id — track ID obtained from the rungeo API response node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=gettrack&id=70685695&node=1 Response { \"track_data\": \"47.2037;39.6325;10;1439443509:47.2037;39.6324;13;1439443510:47.2037;39.6323;15;1439443512:47.2036;39.6304;22;1439443568\", \"track_id\": 70685695, \"unixstarttimestamp\": 1439443509 } The API response contains: track_id— the unique track identifier; unixstarttimestamp— the track start date and time as a Unix timestamp; track_data— a sequence of track points separated by colons (:). Each track point contains four values separated by semicolons (;): 1. latitude — latitude; 2. longitude — longitude; 3. speed_kmh — speed in km/h; 4. point_unix_timestamp — the absolute time when the point was recorded, as a Unix timestamp in UTC. Track point format: latitude;longitude;speed_kmh;point_unix_timestamp Example: 47.2037;39.6325;10;1439443509 The value 1439443509 is the absolute time when the point was recorded, not the number of seconds from the beginning of the track. To calculate the time elapsed since the beginning of the track, use the following formula: elapsed_seconds = point_unix_timestamp - unixstarttimestamp. Example: 1439443568 - 1439443509 = 59"},{"s":"approved-track-management","t":"Approved track management","h":["Approved track management","How to get data","Request address","Request parameters","Set approved periods","Get approved tracks","Track player"],"x":"Approved track management This API helps work with approved vehicle tracks: set periods, retrieve tracks, and use the track player. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters Parameters are passed through the query string using either POST or GET, depending on the command. To get the required data, add parameters to the request URL. Parameters are added after the question mark ? and separated by &. cmd=set_approved_tracks — command to set approved periods cmd=get_approved_tracks — command to get approved tracks node — the number of the node where the command should be executed veh — vehicle registration number or identifier ts — start of the period (timestamp) te — end of the period (timestamp) Set approved periods Example API request: https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=set_approved_tracks&veh=Р852ОВ77&ts=1537945200&te=1537952400&node=1 API response: {\"code\":0,\"msg\":\"OK updated\"} Get approved tracks Example API request: http://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_approved_tracks&veh=Р852ОВ77&ts=1537945200&te=153795 2400 API response: { \"code\":0, \"data\":{ \"approved\":[ {\"length\":2.8051348330013,\"ts\":1537948809,\"te\":1537949431,\"max_speed\":\"59\",\"avg_speed\":26} ], \"not_approved\":[ {\"length\":1.8650025635569,\"ts\":1537945206,\"te\":1537946030,\"max_speed\":\"40\",\"avg_speed\":12}, {\"length\":4.1987356239849,\"ts\":1537948298,\"te\":1537948798,\"max_speed\":\"65\",\"avg_speed\":37} ] } } In the API response: approved — list of approved tracks not_approved — list of tracks that were not approved length — track length in kilometers ts — track start time (timestamp) te — track end time (timestamp) max_speed — maximum speed on the track avg_speed — average speed Track player The track player is a web service that allows visual viewing of vehicle tracks. Unlike the regular API, it immediately returns a ready-made page with the map and track, not JSON or XML. Link to view the track: http://<server_address>/api/track_player.php?login=demo&pass=demo&ts=1536933600&te=1536940800&veh=в848ае199&lang=ru You can use this URL as the iframe src to display the track on a webpage."},{"s":"get-report","t":"Get report","h":["Get report","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example GET request","Example POST request"],"x":"Get report This API generates a report for vehicles, drivers, zones, clients, or tags over a selected time period, with detailed options for customizing the report content. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET or POST Request parameters Required: cmd=get_report — API command to retrieve a report report_type — report type (numeric identifier) start — start of the period (Unix timestamp) stop — end of the period (Unix timestamp) veh_id — vehicle IDs (comma-separated) explode — data separation: 1 — by days, 2 — by weeks, 3 — no separation group — data grouping: 1 — by days, 2 — by objects, 3 — by zones, 4 — by drivers, 5 — by clients, 6 — by groups, 7 — by tags format— report format: json, pdf, xls tz — the time zone used to generate dates and times in the report. Specify a valid IANA time zone identifier, for example, Africa/Johannesburg. If an unknown or invalid time zone is provided, the API returns an error. Optional: drivers_id — list of driver IDs zones_id — list of geofence IDs fillings — include refueling data stales — include idle periods speed — include speed data rashod — include fuel consumption stops — include stops run — include engine run data contr_time/ type — control time tags — list of tag IDs template — report template ID outside_zones — include external zones work_hours — include only working hours fuel — include fuel data vehicle_not_moving_time — include idle time for the report vehicles_has_covered_km — include vehicle mileage notifications — list of notification IDs Example GET request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_report&report_type=1&start=1724623200&stop=1724709600&veh_id=1234,5678&explode=1&group=2&format=json &tz=Africa/Johannesburg&node=1 Example POST request { \"cmd\": \"get_report\", \"report_type\": 1, \"start\": 1724623200, \"stop\": 1724709600, \"veh_id\": \"1234,5678\", \"explode\": 1, \"group\": 2, \"format\": \"json\", \"tz\": \"Africa/Johannesburg\" } Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": [ { \"veh_id\": \"1234\", \"date\": \"2025-09-22\", \"distance\": 125.5, \"fuel_consumption\": 15.2, \"stops\": 5, \"speed_avg\": 60 }, { \"veh_id\": \"5678\", \"date\": \"2025-09-22\", \"distance\": 98.3, \"fuel_consumption\": 12.7, \"stops\": 4, \"speed_avg\": 55 } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = successful operation) msg — server message data — array of report data veh_id — vehicle ID date — report date distance — distance traveled (km) fuel_consumption — fuel consumed stops — number of stops speed_avg — average speed"},{"s":"report-list","t":"Report list","h":["Report list","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example GET request"],"x":"Report list This API retrieves a list of all available reports in the account, including their IDs, names, and the groups they belong to. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET or POST Request parameters cmd=reports_list — command to get the list of reports node — node number where the command should be executed Example GET request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=reports_list&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"id\": 266, \"name\": \"Administrative Actions\", \"group\": \"Administrator\" }, { \"id\": 258, \"name\": \"Blocked Users\", \"group\": \"Administrator\" }, { \"id\": 263, \"name\": \"Created Users\", \"group\": \"Administrator\" }, { \"id\": 261, \"name\": \"Disabled User Account\", \"group\": \"Administrator\" }, { \"id\": 265, \"name\": \"Inactive Users\", \"group\": \"Administrator\" }, { \"id\": 267, \"name\": \"Failed Login Attempts\", \"group\": \"Administrator\" } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 means the operation was successful) data — array of available reports: id — report ID name — report name group — group the report belongs to"},{"s":"add-geofence","t":"Add geofence","h":["Add geofence","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example requests"],"x":"Add geofence This API creates a new geofence, specifying its name, type, color, group, and geometry. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=geofenceadd — command to add a geofence zonename — geofence name zonetype — type of the geofence (1 — polygon, 2 — circle) color — geofence color in HEX format (e.g., #FFFFFF) info — text information about the geofence group_name — name of the geofence group (created if it doesn’t exist, optional) group_id — ID of an existing group (if specified, group_name is ignored, optional) geometry — GEOJson describing the polygon or circle external_id — external identifier for the geofence (optional) node — node number where the command should be executed Example requests Polygon: http://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=geofenceadd&zonetype=1&zonename=test_api_polygon&external_id=12354&info=blabla&color=%23FF3300&group_name=TEST_API&geometry={\"type\":\"Polygon\",\"coordinates\":[[[55.184334,25.112866],[55.183512,25.113472],[55.182154,25.112516],[55.183511,25.111016],[55.184923,25.112133]]]}&node=1 Circle: http://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=geofenceadd&zonetype=2&zonename=test_api_circle&external_id=12345&info=circle_info&color=%23FF3300&group_name=TEST_API&geometry={\"type\":\"Circle\",\"coordinates\":[55.184334,25.112866],\"radius\":150}&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": { \"id\": 158412, \"zonename\": \"test_api_polygon\", \"zonetype\": 1, \"external_id\": \"12354\", \"created_at\": 1610536445, \"updated_at\": 1610536445, \"color\": \"#FF3300\", \"info\": \"blabla\", \"group_id\": 6984 } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message data — object with information about the created geofence: id — geofence identifier zonename — geofence name zonetype — geofence type (1 — polygon, 2 — circle) external_id — external identifier created_at — creation time (Unix timestamp) updated_at — last update time (Unix timestamp) color — geofence color info — text information group_id — geofence group identifier"},{"s":"geofence-list","t":"Geofence list","h":["Geofence list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Geofence list This API returns a list of all geofences with their characteristics, including coordinates, type, color, and group. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=geofencelist — API command to get the list of geofences node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=geofencelist&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": [ { \"id\": 157828, \"zonename\": \"Test Magnit\", \"zonetype\": 2, \"external_id\": null, \"created_at\": 1609780839, \"updated_at\": 1609780839, \"color\": \"#00CC00\", \"info\": \"geozone text info\", \"group_name\": \"TEST\", \"group_id\": 6531, \"geometry\": { \"type\": \"Circle\", \"coordinates\": [40.036497, 46.981936], \"radius\": 305 } }, { \"id\": 153244, \"zonename\": \"school\", \"zonetype\": 1, \"external_id\": 1234, \"created_at\": 1609780839, \"updated_at\": 1609780839, \"color\": \"#FF9900\", \"info\": \"kml upload\", \"group_name\": \"TEST\", \"group_id\": 6531, \"geometry\": { \"type\": \"Polygon\", \"coordinates\": [ [ [55.184334, 25.112866], [55.183512, 25.113472], [55.182154, 25.112516], [55.183511, 25.111016], [55.184923, 25.112133] ] ] } } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message data — array of geofence objects, each containing: id — geofence identifier zonename — geofence name zonetype — geofence type (1 — polygon, 2 — circle) external_id — external identifier (if any) created_at — creation time (Unix timestamp) updated_at — last update time (Unix timestamp) color — color of the geofence in the interface info — additional information group_name — name of the geofence group group_id — group identifier geometry — geofence geometry: type — type (Circle or Polygon) coordinates — coordinates (for circle: [longitude, latitude]; for polygon: array of arrays of coordinates) radius — radius for circle geofence in meters"},{"s":"link-geofence-to-object","t":"Link geofence to object","h":["Link geofence to object","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Link geofence to object This API links an existing geofence to a vehicle, allowing you to set control parameters such as speed limits, idle time, and alerts. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=geofencelink — command to link a geofence zone_id — geofence ID (required if external_id is not specified) external_id — external identifier of the geofence (optional, ignored if zone_id is provided) agent_id — identifier of the agent (vehicle) idle — idle time control in the geofence in minutes (optional) speedlimit — speed limit in the geofence in km/h (optional) alerts — alerts configuration: 0 — no alerts 1 — idle alert 2 — border crossing alert 3 — border and checkpoint alert node — node number where the command should be executed Example request http://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=geofencelink&zone_id=158414&agent_id=66497&idle=10&speedlimit=80&alerts=0&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message indicating the result of the operation"},{"s":"unlink-geofence-from-object","t":"Unlink geofence from object","h":["Unlink geofence from object","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Unlink geofence from object This API removes the link between an existing geofence and a vehicle. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=geofenceunlink — command to unlink a geofence zone_id — geofence ID (required if external_id is not specified) external_id — external identifier of the geofence (optional, ignored if zone_id is provided) agent_id — identifier of the agent (vehicle) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request http://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=geofenceunlink&zone_id=158414&agent_id=66497&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message indicating the result of the operation"},{"s":"geofence-visit-history","t":"Geofence visit history","h":["Geofence visit history","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request for a single vehicle","Example request for multiple vehicles"],"x":"Geofence visit history This API retrieves information about when objects (vehicles) entered and exited specific geozones over a specified period. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=ag_geozones — API command to get geozone visit history start — start of the period (Unix timestamp) stop — end of the period (Unix timestamp) imei — vehicle identifier(s). Multiple IMEIs can be provided, separated by commas node — node number where the command is executed Example request for a single vehicle https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=ag_geozones&start=1606770000&stop=1608584399&imei=865291046424635&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": [ { \"group\": \"General\", \"zonename\": \"Golitsyno Base\", \"ts\": 1606805707, \"te\": 1606808500 }, { \"group\": \"General\", \"zonename\": \"Golitsyno Base\", \"ts\": 1606865643, \"te\": 1606868103 } ] } Example request for multiple vehicles https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=ag_geozones&start=1606770000&stop=1608584399&imei=865291046424635,867481037184458 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": { \"865291046424635\": [ { \"group\": \"General\", \"zonename\": \"Golitsyno Base\", \"ts\": 1606805707, \"te\": 1606808500 }, { \"group\": \"General\", \"zonename\": \"Golitsyno Base\", \"ts\": 1606865643, \"te\": 1606868103 } ], \"867481037184458\": [ { \"group\": \"General\", \"zonename\": \"Golitsyno Base\", \"ts\": 1607949101, \"te\": 1607956478 }, { \"group\": \"General\", \"zonename\": \"Golitsyno Base\", \"ts\": 1608012176, \"te\": 1608041334 } ] } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message (e.g., \"OK\") data — geozone visit information: If single object, data is an array of visits If multiple objects, data is an object where keys are vehicle IMEIs, and values are arrays of visits group — geozone group zonename — name of the geozone ts — entry time into the geozone (Unix timestamp) te — exit time from the geozone (Unix timestamp)"},{"s":"mileage-inside-geofences","t":"Mileage inside geofences","h":["Mileage inside geofences","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Mileage inside geofences This API gets information about the mileage of vehicles within specified geofences for a selected period of time. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=zones_mileage — command for getting mileage in geofences ts — start time of the period (Unix timestamp) te — end time of the period (Unix timestamp) imei — list of device IMEIs, separated by commas zones — list of geofence names, separated by commas (optional; if not specified, mileage for all zones is returned) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=zones_mileage& ts=1596229200&te=1597006800&imei=867857035056105,123123123&zones=Simferopol,Belogorsk,Sudak&node=1 Response { \"code\":0, \"ts\":\"1596229200\", \"te\":\"1597006800\", \"data\":{ \"867857035056105\":[ { \"zone\":\"Simferopol\", \"duration\":88308, \"mileage\":259.84 }, { \"zone\":\"Outside city\", \"duration\":21564, \"mileage\":283.47 }, { \"zone\":\"Belogorsk\", \"duration\":704, \"mileage\":10.77 }, { \"zone\":\"Sudak\", \"duration\":1332, \"mileage\":11.45 } ] } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: zone — geofence name duration — time spent in the zone in seconds mileage — distance traveled in the zone in kilometers"},{"s":"delete-geofence","t":"Delete geofence","h":["Delete geofence","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Delete geofence This API deletes a geofence from the system. When a geofence is deleted, all links between this geofence and vehicles are automatically removed. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=geofencedel — command to delete a geofence zone_id — internal geofence identifier external_id — external geofence identifier (optional) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=geofencedel&zone_id=473989&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": { \"zones_deleted\": [ 473989 ] } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) message — server message indicating the result of the operation data.zones_deleted — an array of IDs of deleted geofences (zone_id) for which the operation was completed successfully"},{"s":"create-driver","t":"Create driver","h":["Create driver","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Create driver This API allows you to add a new driver to the system or update an existing driver’s information. You can specify the full name, iButton code, contact information, driver’s license number, and a list of objects the driver has access to. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=drivers_add — command to add or update a driver name — driver’s full name code — driver’s iButton code (required) description — driver description (optional) phone — driver’s phone number (optional) license — driver’s license number (optional) email — driver’s email address access — comma-separated list of object IDs (agent_id) the driver is allowed to access node — node number where the command should be executed If a driver with the specified parameters already exists, the API will update their data. If not, a new driver will be created. Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=drivers_add&name=NewDriverName&code=123456&description=DESCR&phone=122212343&license=rrr3335552&email=new@email.com&access=1122,3344&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": { \"id\": 16990, \"name\": \"NewDriverName\", \"code\": \"123456\", \"description\": \"DESCR\", \"phone\": \"122212343\", \"license\": \"rrr3335552\", \"email\": \"new@email.com\", \"access\": [1122, 3344] } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: id — unique driver ID name — full name of the driver code — iButton code of the driver description — driver description phone — phone number license — driver’s license number email — email address access — list of object IDs the driver has access to"},{"s":"drivers-list","t":"Drivers list","h":["Drivers list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Drivers list This API is used to get information about drivers registered in the account. It allows retrieving data for each driver, including full name, contact information, description, access rights, and identifiers. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=drivers_list — command to get the list of drivers node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=drivers_list&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"id\": 317, \"name\": \"Ivan Ivanov\", \"code\": \"\", \"description\": \"Driver #3\", \"phone\": \"798345546\", \"license\": \"\", \"email\": \"\", \"access\": [] }, { \"id\": 1122, \"name\": \"Andrey Petrov\", \"code\": \"\", \"description\": \"Driver #1\", \"phone\": \"+525531696255\", \"license\": \"\", \"email\": null, \"access\": [1122] } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: id — driver identifier name — full name of the driver code — driver code (if available) description — driver description phone — phone number license — driver’s license data email — email address access — list of resource or object IDs the driver has access to"},{"s":"assign-driver-to-vehicle","t":"Assign driver to vehicle","h":["Assign driver to vehicle","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Assign driver to vehicle This API links a driver to a specific vehicle using its unique identifier (IMEI) and the driver’s key. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=linkdriver — API command for driver linking imei — unique vehicle identifier in the system driver — driver’s full name driverkey — driver’s key number node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=linkdriver&imei=548545218454&driver=ADAS+driver&driverkey=89185717259&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [], \"message\": \"success\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success, other values = error) data — additional data (empty array in this case) message — server response message (e.g., \"success\")"},{"s":"driver-activity-report","t":"Driver activity report","h":["Driver activity report","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Driver activity report This API retrieves information about driver activities for vehicles over a selected period. The response includes different work modes (e.g., driving, working, resting). How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=gettachodata — command to retrieve tachograph data agent_id — vehicle ID (or multiple IDs, separated by commas) imei — device ID (alternative to agent_id) ts — start time (Unix timestamp) te — end time (Unix timestamp) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=gettachodata&ts=1624568400&te=1624586400&agent_id=120407,120646&node=1 Response { \"С 220 СК 750\": { \"First driver\": { \"Work\": 4997, \"Drive\": 248, \"Rest\": 9117 }, \"Second driver\": { \"Driver available\": 1722, \"Rest\": 9117 } }, \"А 152 АК 790\": { \"First driver\": { \"Drive\": 5436, \"Work\": 1706, \"Rest\": 9084 }, \"Second driver\": { \"Driver available\": 4995, \"Rest\": 9084 } } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: vehicle number — license plate of the vehicle First driver / Second driver — data for each driver Drive — driving time (in seconds) Work — working time (in seconds) Rest — resting time (in seconds) Driver available — time when the driver was available for work (in seconds)"},{"s":"notifications-configuration-","t":"Notifications configuration","h":["Notifications configuration","Supported HTTP methods","Request address","POST","Request body example (JSON)","Response example","GET","Request example","Response example","PUT","Request body example","Response example","DELETE","Request examples","Response example","Notification configuration fields description"],"x":"Notifications configuration This API is used to create, retrieve, update, and delete notification configurations in the OneGPS system. This API is not intended for sending notifications. Instead, it defines the conditions under which notifications will be triggered in the future. Supported HTTP methods POST — create a new notification configuration GET — retrieve a notification configuration by ID PUT — update an existing notification configuration DELETE— remove one or more notification configurations Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/ptm/notification.php POST Creates a new notification configuration. The request body is required. Request URL https://<server_address>/api/ptm/notification.php Request body example (JSON) { \"id\": 0, \"agent_id\": 123, \"type\": \"speed\", \"geozones\": [ { \"name\": \"Warehouse\", \"zonetype\": 1, \"points\": [ [55.7558, 37.6173], [55.7560, 37.6180], [55.7565, 37.6170] ] } ], \"name\": \"Speeding in warehouse zone\", \"email\": true, \"emails_list\": [ \"dispatcher@example.com\", \"manager@example.com\" ], \"sms\": true, \"phones_list\": [ \"+79001234567\", \"+79007654321\" ], \"message_text\": \"Alert! Speed limit exceeded in warehouse zone.\", \"webhook\": true, \"webhook_url\": \"https://yourdomain.com/webhook-handler\", \"ts\": 1680000000, \"te\": 1710000000, \"timezone\": 3, \"zone_rule\": \"in\", \"speed_rule\": \">\", \"speed_value\": 80, \"speed_time\": 15, \"speed_time_units\": \"ss\", \"ignition_rule\": \"on\", \"geozones_rule\": \"in\", \"break_duration\": 0, \"break_time_units\": \"ss\", \"tag_name\": \"truck\", \"temperature_rule\": \">\", \"temperature_value\": 30, \"temperature_time\": 0, \"disconnect_time\": 0, \"disconnect_time_units\": \"ss\", \"odometer_rule\": \">\", \"odometer_value\": 100000, \"odometer_period\": \"abs\" } Response example { \"success\": true, \"msg\": \"Notification successfully created\", \"method\": \"POST\", \"notification\": { \"id\": 123, \"agent_id\": 123, \"type\": \"speed\", \"geozones\": [ { \"name\": \"Warehouse\", \"zonetype\": 1, \"points\": [ [55.7558, 37.6173], [55.7560, 37.6180], [55.7565, 37.6170] ] } ], \"name\": \"Speeding in warehouse zone\", \"email\": true, \"emails_list\": [ \"dispatcher@example.com\", \"manager@example.com\" ], \"sms\": true, \"phones_list\": [ \"+79001234567\", \"+79007654321\" ], \"message_text\": \"Alert! Speed limit exceeded in warehouse zone.\", \"webhook\": true, \"webhook_url\": \"https://yourdomain.com/webhook-handler\", \"ts\": 1680000000, \"te\": 1710000000, \"timezone\": 3, \"zone_rule\": \"in\", \"speed_rule\": \">\", \"speed_value\": 80, \"speed_time\": 15, \"speed_time_units\": \"ss\", \"ignition_rule\": \"on\", \"geozones_rule\": \"in\", \"break_duration\": 0, \"break_time_units\": \"ss\", \"tag_name\": \"truck\", \"temperature_rule\": \">\", \"temperature_value\": 30, \"temperature_time\": 0, \"disconnect_time\": 0, \"disconnect_time_units\": \"ss\", \"odometer_rule\": \">\", \"odometer_value\": 100000, \"odometer_period\": \"abs\" } } GET Fetches a notification configuration by its unique ID. Required query parameter id — ID of the notification Request example https://<server_address>/api/ptm/notification.php?id=123 Response example { \"success\": true, \"msg\": \"Notification retrieved\", \"method\": \"GET\", \"notification\": { \"id\": 123, \"agent_id\": 123, \"type\": \"speed\", \"geozones\": [ { \"name\": \"Warehouse\", \"zonetype\": 1, \"points\": [ [55.7558, 37.6173], [55.7560, 37.6180], [55.7565, 37.6170] ] } ], \"name\": \"Speeding in warehouse zone\", \"email\": true, \"emails_list\": [ \"dispatcher@example.com\", \"manager@example.com\" ], \"sms\": true, \"phones_list\": [ \"+79001234567\", \"+79007654321\" ], \"message_text\": \"Alert! Speed limit exceeded in warehouse zone.\", \"webhook\": true, \"webhook_url\": \"https://yourdomain.com/webhook-handler\", \"ts\": 1680000000, \"te\": 1710000000, \"timezone\": 3, \"zone_rule\": \"in\", \"speed_rule\": \">\", \"speed_value\": 80, \"speed_time\": 15, \"speed_time_units\": \"ss\", \"ignition_rule\": \"on\", \"geozones_rule\": \"in\", \"break_duration\": 0, \"break_time_units\": \"ss\", \"tag_name\": \"truck\", \"temperature_rule\": \">\", \"temperature_value\": 30, \"temperature_time\": 0, \"disconnect_time\": 0, \"disconnect_time_units\": \"ss\", \"odometer_rule\": \">\", \"odometer_value\": 100000, \"odometer_period\": \"abs\" } } PUT Updates an existing notification configuration. Use the same format as in POST, but with the actual id of the notification to be updated. Request address https://<server_address>/api/ptm/notification.php Request body example { \"id\": 123, \"agent_id\": 123, \"type\": \"speed\", \"geozones\": [ { \"name\": \"Warehouse\", \"zonetype\": 1, \"points\": [ [55.7558, 37.6173], [55.7560, 37.6180], [55.7565, 37.6170] ] } ], \"name\": \"Updated speed alert\", \"email\": true, \"emails_list\": [\"alerts@example.com\"], \"sms\": false, \"phones_list\": [], \"message_text\": \"Updated speed alert message\", \"webhook\": false, \"webhook_url\": \"\", \"ts\": 1680000000, \"te\": 1720000000, \"timezone\": 3, \"zone_rule\": \"in\", \"speed_rule\": \">\", \"speed_value\": 90, \"speed_time\": 10, \"speed_time_units\": \"ss\", \"ignition_rule\": \"off\", \"geozones_rule\": \"in\", \"break_duration\": 0, \"break_time_units\": \"ss\", \"tag_name\": \"van\", \"temperature_rule\": \">\", \"temperature_value\": 35, \"temperature_time\": 0, \"disconnect_time\": 0, \"disconnect_time_units\": \"ss\", \"odometer_rule\": \">\", \"odometer_value\": 120000, \"odometer_period\": \"abs\" } Response example { \"success\": true, \"msg\": \"Notification successfully updated\", \"method\": \"PUT\", \"notification\": { \"id\": 123, \"agent_id\": 123, \"type\": \"speed\", \"geozones\": [ { \"name\": \"Warehouse\", \"zonetype\": 1, \"points\": [ [55.7558, 37.6173], [55.7560, 37.6180], [55.7565, 37.6170] ] } ], \"name\": \"Updated speed alert\", \"email\": true, \"emails_list\": [\"alerts@example.com\"], \"sms\": false, \"phones_list\": [], \"message_text\": \"Updated speed alert message\", \"webhook\": false, \"webhook_url\": \"\", \"ts\": 1680000000, \"te\": 1720000000, \"timezone\": 3, \"zone_rule\": \"in\", \"speed_rule\": \">\", \"speed_value\": 90, \"speed_time\": 10, \"speed_time_units\": \"ss\", \"ignition_rule\": \"off\", \"geozones_rule\": \"in\", \"break_duration\": 0, \"break_time_units\": \"ss\", \"tag_name\": \"van\", \"temperature_rule\": \">\", \"temperature_value\": 35, \"temperature_time\": 0, \"disconnect_time\": 0, \"disconnect_time_units\": \"ss\", \"odometer_rule\": \">\", \"odometer_value\": 120000, \"odometer_period\": \"abs\" } } DELETE Deletes one or more notification configurations. Query parameters: id (optional) — ID of the specific notification to delete agent_id (optional) — Deletes all notifications for the specified agent Request examples Delete a single notification: https://<server_address>/api/ptm/notification.php?id=123 Delete all notifications for an agent: https://<server_address>/api/ptm/notification.php?agent_id=456 Response example { \"count\": 1 // Number of deleted notifications } Notification configuration fields description Field Description id Notification ID agent_id Agent ID (device or vehicle identifier) type Notification type (speed, temperature, ignition, etc.) geozones Array of geofences applicable to this notification name Notification name email Enable email alerts emails_list List of email addresses sms Enable SMS alerts phones_list List of phone numbers message_text Text message of the alert webhook_url Webhook endpoint URL webhook Enable webhook ts, te Start and end time of notification (Unix timestamp) timezone Time zone offset (e.g. +3) zone_rule Zone condition (in, out) speed_rule Speed condition (>, <, =) speed_value Speed threshold speed_time Duration of speed violation speed_time_units Time units: ss (seconds), mm (minutes), hh (hours) ignition_rule Ignition state (on, off) geozones_rule Zone behavior (in, out) break_duration Break duration break_time_units Time units for break: ss, mm, hh temperature_rule Temperature condition (>, <) temperature_value Temperature threshold temperature_time Duration of temperature violation (in units) disconnect_time Device disconnection duration disconnect_time_units Time units for disconnection odometer_rule Odometer condition (>, <, =) odometer_value Odometer value odometer_period Odometer mode (abs, rel) tag_name Object tag (e.g. \"truck\")"},{"s":"notification-events","t":"Notification events","h":["Notification events","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Notification events This API retrieves notification events for a selected period, such as speeding, leaving a geofence, or other pre-configured alerts. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=notifications — command to get notification events ts — start time of the period (Unix timestamp) te — end time of the period (Unix timestamp) notification — name of the notification to retrieve events for node— node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=notifications&ts=1606942800&te=1607115600&notification=test&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": [ { \"vehiclenumber\": \"PS2\", \"agentid\": 109792, \"message\": \"Speed > 10 km/h longer than 10 sec max speed=52km/h Duration: 42 sec\", \"ts\": 1606988609 }, { \"vehiclenumber\": \"PS2\", \"agentid\": 109792, \"message\": \"Speed > 10 km/h longer than 10 sec max speed=29km/h Duration: 12 sec\", \"ts\": 1606988438 }, { \"vehiclenumber\": \"PS2\", \"agentid\": 109792, \"message\": \"Speed > 10 km/h longer than 10 sec max speed=17km/h Duration: 13 sec\", \"ts\": 1606988106 } ], \"id\": 3701 } In response to the API request, the following is returned: vehiclenumber — vehicle registration number or internal number agentid — object ID in the system message — notification message text ts — event time (Unix timestamp) id— request/session identifier"},{"s":"event-media-files","t":"Event media files","h":["Event media files","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Event media files This API retrieves media files for selected objects over a specified period. It provides links to photos and videos associated with events, including ADAS and DMS events. How to get data Method GET Request address Use the following URL to retrieve data: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=get_media— command used to retrieve media files agent_id— ID of an object or a comma-separated list of object IDs start— start of the requested period as a Unix timestamp stop— end of the requested period as a Unix timestamp Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_media&agent_id=1234,5678&start=1724623200&stop=1724709600 A successful API response returns information about media files for the selected objects and period, along with links for viewing them. Follow a link to open the image or video associated with the event in your browser."},{"s":"object-live-events","t":"Object live events","h":["Object live events","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Object live events This API returns real-time events (live events) for vehicles, including contract info, company, driver, speed, and event start location. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=live_events — command to get live events node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=live_events&node=1 Response { \"1264\": { \"Contract Number\": \"CONTRACT ID\", \"Company Name\": \"Al Turki Enterprises\", \"Base Location\": \"LEK\", \"Vehicle Owner\": \"SUB-CONTRACTOR\", \"ID\": 1680881937, \"EventID, EventType\": 0, \"VehicleID\": \"5174 WA\", \"Asset Category\": \"Light\", \"Road Type\": \"Blacktop\", \"DriverID\": 18262972, \"Event Start Time\": \"1680881932\", \"Event Start Speed\": 0, \"Event Start Location\": { \"lat\": \"18.185841\", \"lon\": \"55.213230\" } }, \"1123\": { \"Contract Number\": \"\", \"Company Name\": \"Al Turki Enterprises\", \"Base Location\": \"\", \"Vehicle Owner\": \"\", \"ID\": 1680881937, \"EventID, EventType\": 0, \"VehicleID\": \"6076MB\", \"Asset Category\": \"Light\", \"Road Type\": \"Blacktop\", \"DriverID\": 0, \"Event Start Time\": \"1680881413\", \"Event Start Speed\": 0, \"Event Start Location\": { \"lat\": \"18.188658\", \"lon\": \"55.206314\" } } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: Object Key (e.g., 1264, 1123) — unique event identifier Contract Number — contract number Company Name — company name Base Location — vehicle base location Vehicle Owner — vehicle owner ID — internal event ID EventID, EventType — event identifier and type VehicleID — vehicle number or ID Asset Category — vehicle category Road Type — type of road DriverID — driver ID Event Start Time — event start time (Unix timestamp) Event Start Speed — speed at event start Event Start Location — coordinates of event start (lat, lon)"},{"s":"create-waybill","t":"Create waybill","h":["Create waybill","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Create waybill This API creates a new waybill for a specified time period. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=add_waybill — command to add a waybill ts — start time (Unix timestamp) te — end time (Unix timestamp) agent_id — agent identifier name — name of the waybill node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=add_waybill&ts=1596022528&te=1596488400&agent_id=44841&name=test+bill1&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": { \"ts\": 1596022528, \"te\": 1596488400, \"agent_id\": 44841, \"account_id\": 3934, \"waybill\": { \"name\": \"test bill1\" } } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) data — object with the created waybill data: ts — waybill start time (Unix timestamp) te — waybill end time (Unix timestamp) agent_id — agent identifier account_id — account identifier waybill.name — name of the waybill"},{"s":"waybill-information","t":"Waybill information","h":["Waybill information","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Waybill information This API allows you to get waybill data for a specified period. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=get_waybill — command to get waybills ts — start time of the selection period (Unix timestamp) agent_id — agent identifier node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_waybill&ts=1596022528&agent_id=44841&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"ts\": 1596022528, \"te\": 1596488400, \"agent_id\": 44841, \"account_id\": 3934, \"waybill\": { \"name\": \"test bill1\" } } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) data — array with waybill information: ts — waybill start time (Unix timestamp) te — waybill end time (Unix timestamp) agent_id — agent identifier account_id — account identifier waybill — object containing waybill details: name — name of the waybill"},{"s":"delete-waybill","t":"Delete waybill","h":["Delete waybill","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Delete waybill This API deletes an existing waybill for a specified time period. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=remove_waybill — command to remove a waybill ts — start time (Unix timestamp) te — end time (Unix timestamp) agent_id — agent identifier node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=remove_waybill&ts=1596022528&te=1596488400&agent_id=44841&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": 1 } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) data — deletion result (1 = successfully deleted)"},{"s":"create-user-2","t":"Create user","h":["Create user","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Create user This API allows you to create a new user in OneGPS with specified parameters: login, password, role, and contact information. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET Request parameters cmd=create_user — command to create a user login — login of the new user password — user password name — user name phone — user phone number email— user email role_id— user role: 0 — Admin, 1 — User vehicles— list of accessible vehicles (IDs or quantity) folders— list of accessible folders (IDs or quantity) node— node number where the command should be executed The request must include the login and password of the administrator creating the user. Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=create_user&login=TestAPI3&password=123456&name=Test API&role_id=1&phone=789995&email=testapi5@mailinator.com&vehicles=15&folders=5&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success create user: 48\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 — successful operation) msg — server message with the ID of the created user"},{"s":"update-user-login-and-password","t":"Update user login and password","h":["Update user login and password","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Update user login and password This API allows you to change the login and password of an existing user in the OneGPS system. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET Request parameters cmd=update_user_auth — command to update authentication data login — new user login password — new user password user_id — ID of the user whose data should be changed node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=update_user_auth&login=TestAPI&password=qwerty&user_id=48&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 — successful operation) msg — server message about the result of the action"},{"s":"delete-user","t":"Delete user","h":["Delete user","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Delete user This API deletes an existing user in the OneGPS system by their user ID. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method DELETE Request parameters cmd=remove_user — command to delete a user user_id — ID of the user to be removed node — node number where the command should be executed (optional) Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=remove_user&user_id=48&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success remove\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 — successful operation) msg — server message about the result of the action"},{"s":"user-information","t":"User information","h":["User information","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example GET request","Example POST request"],"x":"User information This API retrieves detailed information about a user by their user_id. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET or POST Request parameters cmd=get_user — API command to get user information user_id — identifier of the user whose information should be retrieved node — node number where the command should be executed Example GET request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_user&user_id=19&node=1 Example POST request Request body (JSON): { \"cmd\": \"get_user\", \"user_id\": 19 } Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success\", \"data\": { \"id\": 19, \"roleid\": 1, \"info\": \"\", \"msisdn\": \"123123\", \"email\": \"testTOTP@testTOTP.com\", \"name\": \"dasd\", \"account_id\": 1000, \"lang\": \"en\", \"parent_id\": 2 } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = successful operation) msg — server message data — user information object: id — user ID roleid — user role (0 = Admin, 1 = User) info — additional user information msisdn — phone number email— user’s email address name — user’s name account_id — account ID the user belongs to lang — user interface language parent_id— parent user ID (if any)"},{"s":"user-list-of-own-account","t":"User list of own account","h":["User list of own account","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example GET request","Example POST request"],"x":"User list of own account This API is used to retrieve a list of all users registered under your account. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET or POST Request parameters cmd=get_account_user_list — API command to get the list of account users node — node number where the command should be executed Example GET request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_account_user_list&node=1 Example POST request Request body (JSON): { \"cmd\": \"get_account_user_list\" } Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"id\": 19, \"roleid\": 1, \"info\": \"\", \"msisdn\": \"123123\", \"email\": \"testTOTP@testTOTP.com\", \"name\": \"dasd\", \"account_id\": 1000, \"lang\": \"en\", \"parent_id\": 2 } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = successful operation) data — array of account users: id — user identifier roleid — user role (0 = Admin, 1 = User, 2 = Logistic, etc.) info — additional information msisdn — phone number email — user’s email address name — user’s name account_id — account ID the user belongs to lang — user interface language parent_id — parent user ID (if any)"},{"s":"accessible-accounts-list","t":"Accessible accounts list","h":["Accessible accounts list","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example GET request","Example POST request"],"x":"Accessible accounts list This API is used to retrieve the list of accounts that the currently authorized user has access to. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET or POST Request parameters cmd=get_account_map — API command to retrieve the list of accessible accounts node — node number where the command should be executed Example GET request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_account_map&node=1 Example POST request Request body (JSON): { \"cmd\": \"get_account_map\" } Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ {\"account_id\": 1005}, {\"account_id\": 1000} ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — execution result (0 indicates a successful operation) data — list of accounts accessible to the user account_id — unique identifier of the account"},{"s":"user-folders-list","t":"User folders list","h":["User folders list","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example GET request"],"x":"User folders list This API returns a list of folders that the user has access to in the account. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET or POST Request parameters cmd=get_folders_user — command to get the list of folders For POST requests, parameters can be sent in the request body as JSON node — node number where the command should be executed Example GET request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_folders_user&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success\", \"data\": [ {\"id\":1,\"name\":\"DEMO\"}, {\"id\":6,\"name\":\"IP Emelyanov\"}, {\"id\":11,\"name\":\"FOLDER1\"}, {\"id\":12,\"name\":\"FOLDER2\"} ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 — successful operation) msg — server message data — array of user folders: id — folder ID name — folder name"},{"s":"other-user-s-folders-list","t":"Other user’s folders list","h":["Other user’s folders list","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example GET request","Example POST request"],"x":"Other user’s folders list This API returns the folders accessible to a specified user. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET or POST Request parameters cmd=get_folders_user — command to get the list of folders user_id — user ID (required when requesting folders for another user) node — node number where the command should be executed (optional) For POST requests, parameters can be sent in the request body as JSON Example GET request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_folders_user&user_id=19&node=1 Example POST request { \"cmd\": \"get_folders_user\", \"user_id\": 19 } Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success\", \"data\": [ {\"id\":1,\"name\":\"DEMO\"}, {\"id\":6,\"name\":\"IP Emelyanov\"}, {\"id\":11,\"name\":\"FOLDER1\"}, {\"id\":12,\"name\":\"FOLDER2\"} ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 — successful operation) msg — server message data — array of folders accessible to the user: id — folder ID name — folder name"},{"s":"update-folder-list","t":"Update folder list","h":["Update folder list","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Update folder list This API allows updating the set of folders accessible to a specific user. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method POST Request parameters cmd=update_user_folders — command to update the folder list folders — comma-separated list of folder IDs to assign to the user user_id — ID of the user whose folder access is being updated Example request { \"cmd\": \"update_user_folders\", \"folders\": \"1,11\", \"user_id\": 19 } Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 — successful operation) msg — server message describing the result of the action"},{"s":"update-user-s-accessible-accounts","t":"Update user’s accessible accounts","h":["Update user’s accessible accounts","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Update user’s accessible accounts This API allows assigning a user access to specific accounts. If a partner in the system has multiple accounts, an administrator can choose which accounts a particular user can access. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method POST Request parameters cmd=update_user_accounts_map — command to update the list of accessible accounts accounts — comma-separated list of contract IDs (accounts) to assign to the user Accounts must belong to the same partner as the user. user_id — ID of the user to whom the contracts are assigned node — node number where the command should be executed If the user’s partner ID in the system is 0, assigning contracts is not possible (mapping will not work). Example request { \"cmd\": \"update_user_accounts_map\", \"accounts\": \"1005\", \"user_id\": 19 } Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code— result code (0 indicates a successful operation) msg— server message confirming the result"},{"s":"update-user-vehicles","t":"Update user vehicles","h":["Update user vehicles","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Update user vehicles This API assigns a user access only to the selected vehicles. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method POST Request parameters cmd=update_user_vehicles — command to update the list of vehicles vehicles — comma-separated list of vehicle IDs user_id — ID of the user whose access is being updated node— node number where the command should be executed Example request { \"cmd\": \"update_user_vehicles\", \"vehicles\": \"2,18\", \"user_id\": 19 } Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"Success\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = successful operation) msg — server message"},{"s":"equipment-management","t":"Equipment management","h":["Equipment management","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Equipment management This API allows you to retrieve sensor activity data for a vehicle over a selected period. Using it, you can: track periods when sensors were active (e.g., ignition, equipment turned on) obtain location data when a sensor was triggered analyze vehicle behavior and equipment usage How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=sensors — command to get sensor data imei — vehicle identifier in the system start — start time of the period (Unix timestamp) stop — end time of the period (Unix timestamp) node — node number where the command is executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=sensors&imei=356307046690011&start=1404637430&stop=1404645198&node=1 Response Each sensor is represented as an object where the key is the sensor ID in the system: \"sensors\": { \"9175\": { \"fieldname\": \"DIS1\", \"info\": \"Ignition sensor\", \"id\": 9175, \"work\": { \"1404637430\": { \"ts\": 1404637430, \"te\": 1404637834, \"lat\": \"46.3825\", \"lon\": \"48.0031\", \"place\": \"Russia, Astrakhan Region, Narimanovsky District, Nikolaevskoye Highway, 1b\" }, \"1404638577\": { \"ts\": 1404638577, \"te\": 1404639841, \"lat\": \"46.3625\", \"lon\": \"48.0478\", \"place\": \"RU, Astrakhan, Savushkina Street\" } // ... more sensor activity periods } } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: fieldname — sensor field name in the system (e.g., DIS1) info — sensor description in the system (e.g., \"Ignition sensor\") id — sensor ID in the system work — object containing sensor activity periods; keys are start times (ts) ts — sensor activation time (Unix timestamp) te — sensor deactivation time (Unix timestamp) lat — latitude when the sensor was activated lon — longitude when the sensor was activated place — address or location when the sensor was triggered"},{"s":"sensor-values","t":"Sensor values","h":["Sensor values","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Sensor values This API retrieves values of discrete sensors (fuel level, temperature, weight, etc.) for a specified period of time. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET Request parameters cmd=get_discrete_sensor_data — command to get sensor data imei — object identifier start — start of the period (Unix timestamp) stop — end of the period (Unix timestamp) tag_id — sensor identifier agent_id — agent identifier (optional) node— node number where the command should be executed Sensor data can only be retrieved for up to 24 hours. Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_discrete_sensor_data&imei=350424062310402&start=1677682645&stop=1677761845&tag_id=1158&node=1 Response { \"sensor_data\": { \"966960\": { \"1677682772\": { \"sensor_id\": 966960, \"unixtimestamp\": 1677682772, \"calibrated_value\": \"95.09\", \"speed\": \"0\" }, \"1677682950\": { \"sensor_id\": 966960, \"unixtimestamp\": 1677682950, \"calibrated_value\": \"94.9\", \"speed\": \"12\" } } } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: sensor_id — sensor identifier unixtimestamp — time of the recorded value (Unix timestamp) calibrated_value — calibrated sensor value (e.g., liters of fuel or degrees of temperature) speed — speed of the object at the moment of measurement"},{"s":"battery-charge-by-vin","t":"Battery charge by VIN","h":["Battery charge by VIN","How to get data","Request address","Request method","1. Authorization","2. Retrieving battery sensor data"],"x":"Battery charge by VIN This API retrieves battery charge information for a vehicle based on its VIN. It works only for vehicles that meet the following conditions: the VIN field is filled in the vehicle card a battery charge sensor is connected You can specify multiple VINs in a single request. The response will include battery data for all specified vehicles. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/VTB/api_vtb.php Request method POST The API requires two steps: 1. Authorization — obtain a token to access data 2. Data Request — use the token to retrieve battery information by VIN 1. Authorization Before requesting data, you must authenticate. Request Body: { \"login\": \"your_login\", \"password\": \"your_password\", \"cmd\": \"auth\" } Response { \"token\": \"xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\" } The response contains a token, which is a long unique string. This token is required for all subsequent API requests. Without it, the API will not return battery data. 2. Retrieving battery sensor data After obtaining the token, you can request battery information. Request parameters: Number — list of VINs for vehicles you want to query DateFrom — start date and time (ISO 8601 format) DateTo — end date and time (ISO 8601 format) cmd=vehicle — API command to request vehicle data Request headers: Authorization: Bearer <token>— include the token obtained during authorization Content-Type: application/json— specify that the request body is JSON Request body example: { \"Number\": [\"VIN_of_vehicle\"], \"DateFrom\": \"2023-12-03T00:00:00\", \"DateTo\": \"2023-12-12T00:00:00\", \"cmd\": \"vehicle\" } Response [ { \"Number\": \"456789123\", \"ApplicationNumber\": null, \"CurrentStatus\": \"ACTIVE\", \"CloseDateTime\": \"\", \"LastSyncDate\": \"2024-08-28 15:42:45\", \"AlarmDateTime\": \"2024-08-28 15:42:45\", \"Latitude\": \"55.578352\", \"Longitude\": \"38.125267\", \"BatteryCharge\": \"full charge\", \"LocationLinesID\": \"\", \"FullAddress\": \"ulitsa Garnaeva, Market, Zhukovsky, Moscow Region, Russia\", \"Country\": \"Russia\", \"Region\": \"Moscow Region\", \"Town\": \"Zhukovsky\", \"Street\": \"ulitsa Garnaeva\", \"House\": null, \"numberRegion\": 10 } ] In response to the API request, the following is returned: Number — VIN of the vehicle CurrentStatus — current status of the vehicle LastSyncDate — date of last synchronization BatteryCharge — battery charge level FullAddress, Country, Region, Town, Street — location of the vehicle Latitude, Longitude — coordinates of the vehicle"},{"s":"imei-by-vehicle-number","t":"IMEI by vehicle number","h":["IMEI by vehicle number","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"IMEI by vehicle number This API allows you to determine the IMEI of the device installed in a vehicle by its number. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=get_imei— API command vehnum — vehicle number node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_imei&vehnum=IX35&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"imei\": \"356307045913463\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success, other values = error) msg — server message imei — IMEI of the device installed in the vehicle"},{"s":"raw-data-by-imei","t":"Raw data by IMEI","h":["Raw data by IMEI","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Raw data by IMEI This API allows you to get raw device data for a specified time period. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=ag_events — API command to get raw points imei — unique device identifier ts — start time of the data (Unix timestamp) te — end time of the data (Unix timestamp) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=ag_events&ts=1498201000&te=1498201200&imei=356307045913463&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": { \"1498201016\": {\"alt\": 33, \"lat\": \"47.2124\", \"lon\": \"39.6922\", \"sat\": 15, \"speed\": 0, \"ts\": 1498201016}, \"1498201037\": {\"alt\": 29, \"lat\": \"47.2124\", \"lon\": \"39.6924\", \"sat\": 15, \"speed\": 6, \"ts\": 1498201037}, \"1498201097\": {\"alt\": 31, \"lat\": \"47.2128\", \"lon\": \"39.6933\", \"sat\": 15, \"speed\": 0, \"ts\": 1498201097} }, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"vehicle\": { \"agentid\": 2914, \"created_time\": 1359797592, \"current_mileage\": 90224, \"folder\": \"Sky Electronics\", \"imei\": \"356307045913463\", \"type\": \"Cow\", \"typeid\": 31, \"vehiclenumber\": \"IX35\" } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success, other values = error) msg — server message data — object containing raw points, where keys are timestamps and values: lat — latitude lon — longitude alt — altitude sat — number of satellites speed — speed ts — event timestamp (Unix) vehicle — information about the object: agentid — agent identifier created_time — object creation time current_mileage — current mileage folder — folder/group name imei — device identifier type — object type typeid — object type ID vehiclenumber — vehicle number"},{"s":"ibutton-list","t":"iButton list","h":["iButton list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"iButton list This API retrieves a list of iButtons assigned to a vehicle for a specified period. An iButton is a small electronic device (tag) used to identify drivers or objects. It is typically applied for access control, work time tracking, or recording the start/end of vehicle usage. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=take_ibutton_list — command for the API to get the iButton list ts — start date of the period (Unix timestamp) te — end date of the period (Unix timestamp) agentId— vehicle identifier node — node number where the command is executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=take_ibutton_list&ts=1643662800&te=1645045200&AgentID=79673&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": [ { \"ibutton\": \"ae00001000000000\", \"ts\": 1643680128 }, { \"ibutton\": \"ae00001000000001\", \"ts\": 1643680128 } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) msg — server message (e.g., \"OK\") data — array with information about iButtons: ibutton — unique iButton identifier ts— timestamp when the iButton was registered (Unix timestamp)"},{"s":"passenger-flow-2","t":"Passenger flow","h":["Passenger flow","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Passenger flow This API is used to retrieve data on passenger flow in vehicles equipped with the Autoconductor system. With it, you can: see how many passengers boarded and exited at each stop get stop coordinates, event timestamps, and a video identifier (if video recording is supported by the system) How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=pass_counter — command to call the API ts — start time of the selection period (Unix timestamp) te — end time of the selection period (Unix timestamp) node — node number where the command is executed imei (optional) — IMEI of the vehicle equipped with Autoconductor. If not specified, the selection will include all vehicles with the system Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=pass_counter&ts=1554670800&te=1554757200&imei=963852741159753&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": { \"36571\": { \"agent_id\": 36571, \"stops\": [ { \"busline_id\": 1371, \"door_id\": 0, \"lat\": \"54.9346\", \"lon\": \"51.8981\", \"p_in\": 1, \"p_out\": 0, \"ts\": 1554696995, \"video_id\": 13 }, { \"busline_id\": 1371, \"door_id\": 0, \"lat\": \"54.9238\", \"lon\": \"51.9098\", \"p_in\": 4, \"p_out\": 0, \"ts\": 1554697232, \"video_id\": 14 } ], \"vehiclenumber\": \"autoconductor\" } }, \"msg\": \"OK\" } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — execution code (0 = success) msg — status message (e.g., OK) data — main object containing passenger flow data agent_id — vehicle ID in the system vehiclenumber — internal vehicle number or equipment name (e.g., autoconductor) stops — array of stops with passenger flow data: busline_id — route ID door_id — door number where passenger movement was recorded lat — stop latitude lon — stop longitude p_in — number of passengers boarded p_out — number of passengers exited ts — event time (Unix timestamp) video_id — video recording ID linked to the event (if available)"},{"s":"bus-route-management-","t":"Bus route management","h":["Bus route management","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Creating a bus route (setLine)","Binding a vehicle to a route (bindControl)","Simplified binding by route ID (bindLineToVeh)","Unbinding a vehicle (unbindControl)","Getting the list of routes (listLines)","Combined route creation and vehicle assignment"],"x":"Bus route management This API is used for managing bus routes and assigning vehicles to routes. It allows you to create routes, assign vehicles to trips, and manage route bindings. How to get data Method POST Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters Request parameters: cmd — the command to execute data — JSON object with parameters specific to the command Available commands: setLine — create a new bus route bindControl — bind a vehicle to a route bindLineToVeh — bind a route to a vehicle by route ID unbindControl — remove a vehicle binding listLines — get the list of routes setControl — assign a bus to a trip unsetControl — remove the assignment Creating a bus route (setLine) Request address https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=setLine Request body example { \"account_id\": \"3008\", \"bidirectional\": 1, \"description\": \"Route #9\", \"label\": \"123\", \"name\": \"Route #9\", \"number\": \"12304\", \"type_id\": 0, \"uid\": \"dhqwdh\", \"points\": [ { \"lat\": \"45.0207\", \"lon\": \"33.9993\", \"name\": \"Airport\", \"price\": 15.35, \"radius\": \"91\", \"silent\": 0, \"stop_type\": 0, \"ts\": 600, \"te\": 700, \"uid\": \"819897e2-843b-11e4-bf52-002354656fea\" }, { \"lat\": \"44.9078\", \"lon\": \"34.0784\", \"name\": \"7th City Hospital\", \"price\": 15.35, \"radius\": \"90\", \"silent\": 0, \"stop_type\": 0, \"ts\": 900, \"te\": 1000, \"uid\": \"819897e8-843b-11e4-bf52-002354656fea\" } ] } Route parameters: Parameter Type Description account_id string Account ID bidirectional number 1 = round-trip, 0 = one-way description string Route description label string Optional route label name string Route name number string Route number type_id number Vehicle type (0=bus, 1=trolleybus, 2=tram, 3=minibus) uid string Unique route ID points array Stops on the route Stop parameters: Parameter Type Description lat string Stop latitude lon string Stop longitude name string Stop name price number Fare from previous stop radius string Stop radius in meters silent number 1 = no notifications, 0 = notifications allowed stop_type number 0 = regular, 1 = technical, 2 = terminal ts number Arrival time relative to previous stop (seconds) te number Stop duration (seconds) uid string Unique stop ID Response { \"status\": \"success\", \"message\": \"Route created successfully\", \"data\": { \"uid\": \"dhqwdh\", \"lineuid\": \"2567\" } } Binding a vehicle to a route (bindControl) Request address https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=bindControl Request body example { \"uid\": \"121e1e1e12\", \"imei\": \"12304\", \"lineuid\": \"2e23e23e2e\", \"ts\": \"1392294840\", \"te\": \"1392321600\", \"message_url\": \"http://example.com/message.php\", \"prognoze_url\": \"http://example.com/prognoze.php\" } Parameters: Parameter Type Description uid string Unique binding ID imei string Vehicle IMEI lineuid string Route UID ts string Start timestamp te string End timestamp message_url string Event notification URL prognoze_url string Stop arrival forecast URL Response { \"status\": \"success\", \"message\": \"Binding created successfully\", \"data\": { \"uid\": \"121e1e1e12\", \"imei\": \"12304\", \"lineuid\": \"2e23e23e2e\" } } Simplified binding by route ID (bindLineToVeh) Request address https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=bindLineToVeh Request body example { \"data\": { \"imei\": \"12304\", \"lineid\": \"12345\", \"ts\": \"1392294840\" } } Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": { \"imei\": \"12304\", \"lineid\": \"12345\" } } Unbinding a vehicle (unbindControl) Request address https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=unbindControl Request body example { \"uid\": \"qiwduhqiwdqkdqkn\" } uid — unique identifier of the binding Response { \"status\": \"success\", \"message\": \"Binding removed\", \"data\": { \"uid\": \"qiwduhqiwdqkdqkn\" } } Getting the list of routes (listLines) Request address https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=listLines Response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"data\": [ { \"id\": 2955, \"name\": \"125K\", \"number\": \"125K\", \"bidirectional\": 1, \"description\": \"125K\", \"account_id\": 18306, \"active\": 1, \"type_id\": 0 } ] } Combined route creation and vehicle assignment Request body example { \"uid\": \"qiwduhqiwdqkdqkn\", \"ts\": \"1392294840\", \"te\": \"1392321600\", \"name\": \"test\", \"imei\": \"359772039289781\", \"number\": \"861A\", \"message_url\": \"http://example.com/message.php\", \"prognoze_url\": \"http://example.com/prognoze.php\", \"points\": [ { \"uid\": \"1\", \"lat\": \"53.352142\", \"lon\": \"83.758749\", \"name\": \"Barnaul Bus Station\", \"radius\": \"50\", \"stop_type\": 0, \"price\": 15.35 }, { \"uid\": \"2\", \"lat\": \"52.534012\", \"lon\": \"85.178777\", \"name\": \"Biysk Bus Station\", \"radius\": \"100\", \"stop_type\": 0, \"price\": 15.35 } ] } Response { \"status\": \"success\", \"message\": \"Route and binding created successfully\", \"data\": { \"uid\": \"qiwduhqiwdqkdqkn\", \"imei\": \"359772039289781\", \"lineuid\": \"2e23e23e2e\" } }"},{"s":"bus-status","t":"Bus status","h":["Bus status","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Bus status This API returns the current status of a bus: its route, the current and next stops, as well as route information (number, fare, interval, etc.). How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=currentBusState — command to get the bus status vehnum — bus number node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=currentBusState&vehnum=А%20086%20КХ%20116%20RUS&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"lines\": [ { \"id\": 1086, \"name\": \"1A\", \"number\": \"1A\", \"bidirectional\": 1, \"description\": \"1A\", \"active\": 1, \"price\": \"25.00\", \"interval\": 10, \"folder\": null, \"currentstop_id\": 0, \"currentstop_order\": 0, \"nextstop_id\": 5217, \"nextstop_order\": 48 } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: id — route ID name — route name number — route number bidirectional — route direction flag (1 = bidirectional, 0 = one-way) active — route activity flag (1 = active) price — fare price interval — interval between buses in minutes currentstop_id — ID of the stop where the bus is currently located currentstop_order — sequence number of the current stop nextstop_id — ID of the next stop nextstop_order — sequence number of the next stop"},{"s":"vehicle-arrival-forecast-at-stop","t":"Vehicle arrival forecast at stop","h":["Vehicle arrival forecast at stop","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Vehicle arrival forecast at stop This API provides the predicted arrival time of buses, trolleybuses, and minibuses at a specified stop. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=stationForecast — command to get the arrival forecast stopid — identifier of the stop (taken from the OneGPS interface) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=stationForecast&stopid=7400&node=1 Response [ { \"curent_stop\": 1, \"line_id\": 1371, \"line_name\": \"№ 17 S. KALFA - PL. SUVOROVA - UL. GENERAL ZHIDILOVA\", \"line_num\": \"17\", \"line_type\": \"A\", \"this_stop\": 32, \"time\": 4065, \"vehicle\": \"BUS FLIGHT\", \"vehid\": 48854 } ] The API returns an array of objects with forecasts for each route at the specified stop: curent_stop — sequential number of the stop on the route where the vehicle is currently located line_id — route ID line_name — full name of the route line_num — route number line_type — type of route: 'A' — bus, 'T' — trolleybus, 'M' — minibus this_stop — sequential number of this stop on the route time — predicted arrival time at this stop (seconds) vehicle — vehicle registration number vehid — vehicle ID"},{"s":"route-stop-list","t":"Route stop list","h":["Route stop list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Route stop list This API returns all stops for a given route, including coordinates, stop type, order, and fare information. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=stopList — command to get stop list line_num — route number node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=stopList&line_num=1Б&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"line\": { \"id\": 1083, \"name\": \"1Б\", \"number\": \"1Б\", \"bidirectional\": 1, \"description\": \"1Б\", \"active\": 1, \"price\": \"25.00\", \"interval\": 25, \"folder\": null, \"stops\": [ { \"id\": 5085, \"name\": \"Центр занятости\", \"lat\": \"54.9036\", \"lon\": \"52.3315\", \"radius\": 60, \"stop_type\": 0, \"descr\": null, \"stop_order\": 1, \"price\": \"0.00\", \"ts\": 0, \"te\": 0, \"dir\": 0, \"silent\": 0 }, { \"id\": 5086, \"name\": \"Автовокзал\", \"lat\": \"54.9039\", \"lon\": \"52.3236\", \"radius\": 60, \"stop_type\": 2, \"descr\": null, \"stop_order\": 2, \"price\": \"0.00\", \"ts\": 0, \"te\": 0, \"dir\": 0, \"silent\": 0 } ] } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: line.id — unique route ID line.name — route name line.number — route number bidirectional — 1 if the route is bidirectional active — 1 if the route is active price — fare for the route interval — interval between vehicles in minutes stops — array of route stops: id — unique stop ID name — stop name lat, lon — stop coordinates radius — stop zone radius in meters stop_type — stop type: 0 — normal, 1 — technical, 2 — terminal stop_order — order of the stop in the route silent — 1 to not generate notifications for this stop"},{"s":"route-calculation-and-estimated-arrival-time","t":"Route calculation and estimated arrival time","h":["Route calculation and estimated arrival time","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Route calculation and estimated arrival time This API builds a route between two points and provides the estimated arrival time depending on the selected transport mode. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters cmd=route — command to build the route mode — transport type, one of: bicycle — bicycle bus — bus car — car pedestrian — pedestrian scooter — scooter taxi — taxi truck — truck origin — starting point coordinates (lat,long) destination — destination point coordinates (lat,long) node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=route&mode=bus&origin=47.211908,39.613767&destination=47.201699,39.668183&node=1 Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": { \"routes\": [ { \"id\": \"79de74af-5040-441a-ab7a-62d3d9f7a4b4\", \"sections\": [ { \"id\": \"2cc5fff7-fd4f-43ac-bcda-00d662207c63\", \"type\": \"vehicle\", \"departure\": { \"time\": \"2023-02-22T09:40:07+03:00\", \"place\": { \"type\": \"place\", \"location\": { \"lat\": 47.2119, \"long\": 39.61372 }, \"originalLocation\": { \"lat\": 47.211908, \"long\": 39.613767 } } }, \"arrival\": { \"time\": \"2023-02-22T09:52:32+03:00\", \"place\": { \"type\": \"place\", \"location\": { \"lat\": 47.2017113, \"long\": 39.668221 }, \"originalLocation\": { \"lat\": 47.201699, \"long\": 39.668183 } } }, \"summary\": { \"duration\": 745, \"length\": 5452, \"baseDuration\": 530 }, \"transport\": { \"mode\": \"bus\" } } ] } ] } } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = success) routes — list of calculated routes sections — route segments containing: departure / arrival — departure and arrival times and coordinates summary — trip duration and distance transport.mode — selected transport type"},{"s":"rental-contracts-list","t":"Rental contracts list","h":["Rental contracts list","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Rental contracts list This API provides information about rental contracts from the Rent Car module, including open, booked, and closed contracts. For each contract, the response includes details about the renter, pickup and drop-off locations, planned start and end dates, as well as contract and vehicle numbers. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters To retrieve the required data, add the following parameters to the request URL: cmd=rentcar_contracts — command to get the list of rental contracts status — contract status(es) to retrieve. You can pass one or multiple values separated by commas: open — active contracts (rental is currently ongoing) booked — reserved contracts (booking created, rental not started yet) closed — completed contracts (rental finished) Response format: array of rental contracts Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=rentcar_contracts&status=open,booked,closed Response example { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"list\": [ { \"driver_name\": \"Test renter\", \"driver_phone\": \"905074521698\", \"end_location\": { \"title\": \"Dubai\" }, \"start_location\": { \"title\": \"Dubai\" }, \"mva\": \"12312\", \"ra\": \"16\", \"te_expected\": 1775901540, \"ts_expected\": 1775812680, \"plate_number\": \"Private house\" }, { \"driver_name\": \"Test renter\", \"driver_phone\": \"905074521698\", \"end_location\": { \"title\": \"rgergrr\" }, \"start_location\": { \"title\": \"erterter\" }, \"mva\": \"2222\", \"ra\": \"17\", \"te_expected\": 1775901540, \"ts_expected\": 1775812680, \"plate_number\": \"ABC-123\" } ] } The API response includes: code — status code (0 = success) msg — status message (\"OK\" = request successful) list — array of rental contracts, where each contract contains: driver_name — name of the renter (driver) driver_phone — renter’s contact phone number start_location — rental start location (vehicle pickup): title — name/address of the pickup location end_location — rental end location (vehicle return): title — name/address of the return location mva — Master Vehicle Agreement number ra — Rental Agreement / booking number ts_expected — planned rental start time (Unix timestamp) te_expected — planned rental end time (Unix timestamp) plate_number — vehicle license plate number associated with the contract"},{"s":"eco-driving-data","t":"Eco-driving data","h":["Eco-driving data","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Eco-driving data This API allows you to obtain detailed information about the eco-friendliness of vehicle driving over a specified period. How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request parameters To get the required data, add parameters to the request address. Parameters are specified after the question mark ? and are separated by the ampersand &. cmd = mileco_drive — command to obtain trip data start — date and time of the start of the period for which you want to obtain data stop — date and time of the end of the period Time is specified in Unix timestamp format — the number of seconds that have elapsed since January 1, 1970. vehid — the identifier of the vehicle. If you need data for one vehicle — enter its ID, if for several vehicles — list their IDs separated by commas. f you do not specify the vehid parameter, the system will automatically provide information for all vehicles assigned to your account. imei — a unique number of the vehicle's GPS tracker. Use this parameter to obtain data for a specific tracker. When specifying imei, it is not necessary to indicate the vehid parameter. Example API request with imei: https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=milecodrive&start=1738113401&stop=1738631801&timediff_type=1&imei=8664566839031889725 time_diff_type — grouping data by time. By default, data is collected for each day. time_diff_type=1 — data will be collected for every 7 days time_diff_type=2 — data will be collected for each month node — node number where the command should be executed Example request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=mileco_drive&start=1738113401&stop=1738631801&time_diff_type=1&vehid=32,53&node=1 Response { \"32\": { \"29.01.2025_01:16|04.02.2025_01:16\": { \"date\": \"Wednesday, 29.01.2025 01:16 - Tuesday, 04.02.2025 01:16\", \"veh_id\": 32, \"distance\": 842.75354055, \"agent_id\": 545, \"account_id\": 10025, \"account_name\": \"IP Ivanov\", \"veh_name\": \"О 511 ГР 123\", \"veh_type\": \"Car\", \"eco_data\": { \"speed\": { \"msg\": \"Speed\", \"cnt\": 0, \"val\": 1290, \"fee\": 12 } } } } \"53\": { \"29.01.2025_01:16|04.02.2025_01:16\": { \"date\": \"Wednesday, 29.01.2025 01:16 - Tuesday, 04.02.2025 01:16\", \"veh_id\": 53, \"distance\": 8.24949075, \"agent_id\": 545, \"account_id\": 10025, \"account_name\": \"IP Ivanov\", \"veh_name\": \"О 619АК 67\", \"veh_type\": \"Bus\", \"eco_data\": [] } } In response to the API request, data about vehicle is returned: veh_id — vehicle identifier imei — a unique number of the tracker distance — distance traveled during the specified period agent_id — contract identifier account_id — account number in the system account_name — account name veh_name — name of the vehicle veh_type — type of vehicle eco_data — driving data"},{"s":"timezone-list","t":"Timezone list","h":["Timezone list","How to get data","Request address","Request method","Request parameters","Example GET request","Example POST request"],"x":"Timezone list This API returns a list of available time zones that can be used when configuring a user or an object. How to get data Request address To get the data, use the address: https://<server_address>/api/api.php? Request method GET or POST Request parameters cmd=get_time_zones — API command to retrieve the list of time zones node — node number where the command should be executed Example GET request https://<server_address>/api/api.php?cmd=get_time_zones&node=1 Example POST request Request body (JSON): { \"cmd\": \"get_time_zones\" } Response { \"code\": 0, \"data\": [ { \"text\": \"Africa/Abidjan\" }, { \"text\": \"Africa/Accra\" }, { \"text\": \"Africa/Addis Ababa\" }, { \"text\": \"Africa/Algeria\" }, { \"text\": \"Africa/Asmara\" }, { \"text\": \"Africa/Bamako\" }, { \"text\": \"Africa/Bangui\" } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: code — result code (0 = successful operation) data — list of available time zones text— time zone name in the format Region/City"},{"s":"user-api-v-3","t":"User API v.3","h":[],"x":"To work with User API v3, use the Swagger interface: https://OneGPS-swagger.OneGPS-gps.com/ This version of the API provides updated and structured methods for working with the system. In Swagger, you can: view the list of available methods explore request parameters and responses test the API directly in your browser"},{"s":"get-the-current-vehicle-position-by-name","t":"Get the current vehicle position by name","h":["Get the current vehicle position by name","How to get data","Method","Request address","Authorization","Request headers","Request parameters","Vehicle lookup","Example request","Response","Example successful response","Time formats","Time zone","Time of the latest position","Address determination"],"x":"Get the current vehicle position by name The method returns the vehicle’s latest stored position. The vehicle is identified by its exact name in OneGPS. The method is intended for integration with shipment tracking systems, including WIMT. Unlike methods that use an IMEI, this method accepts the vehicle name in the truckNo parameter. An interactive description of the method is available in Swagger — Track and Trace. How to get data Method POST Request address https://<server_address>/api/v3/vehicles/track-and-trace Replace <server_address> with the OneGPS server address. Authorization The request uses standard API V3 authorization with a Bearer token. Pass the token in the Authorization header: Authorization: Bearer <access_token> Replace <access_token> with a valid API V3 access token. Request headers Authorization Yes Bearer token used to authorize the API V3 user X-Node Yes The user’s node number. It is used to route the request to the correct server Content-Type Yes The request body format. Specify application/json Accept No The response format. The recommended value is application/json Example headers: Authorization: Bearer <access_token> X-Node: 3 Content-Type: application/json Accept: application/json Request parameters The parameters are passed in the request body in JSON format. shipmentNo String Yes The shipment or consignment number. The method accepts the value but does not store or return it truckNo String Yes The vehicle name in OneGPS. It must exactly match the vehiclenumber value countryID String Yes The country code, for example ZA. It is returned unchanged in the response trackingFlag String Yes The tracking status, for example START or STOP. In the current version, the value is accepted but is not stored and does not affect request processing Vehicle lookup The method searches for an exact truckNo match among the vehicles available to the authenticated user. The value must exactly match the vehicle name—the vehiclenumber value—in OneGPS. The lookup takes letter case, spaces, and special characters into account. For example, if the vehicle name is KS51YPGP_F, pass the following value: \"truckNo\": \"KS51YPGP_F\" If the name does not match or the user does not have access to the vehicle, the method does not return its position. Example request { \"shipmentNo\": \"SHIPMENT-123\", \"truckNo\": \"KS51YPGP_F\", \"countryID\": \"ZA\", \"trackingFlag\": \"START\" } Example cURL request: curl --request POST \\ --url \"https://<server_address>/api/v3/vehicles/track-and-trace\" \\ --header \"Authorization: Bearer <access_token>\" \\ --header \"X-Node: 3\" \\ --header \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\ --header \"Accept: application/json\" \\ --data '{ \"shipmentNo\": \"SHIPMENT-123\", \"truckNo\": \"KS51YPGP_F\", \"countryID\": \"ZA\", \"trackingFlag\": \"START\" }' Response If the request is successful, the method returns the vehicle’s latest stored GPS position, address, time zone, and account name. code Integer The request result code. A value of 0 indicates that the request was successful msg String The request result. A successful request returns OK process_time Number The request processing time in seconds countryID String The country code received in the request. It is returned unchanged timeZone String The IANA time zone identifier, for example Africa/Johannesburg truckNo String The name of the vehicle found in OneGPS latitude Number The latitude of the latest stored GPS position longitude Number The longitude of the latest stored GPS position source String The name of the account to which the vehicle belongs streetName String The address of the latest position obtained through reverse geocoding lastRecordedOn Integer The time when the latest GPS position was recorded, as a Unix timestamp The response does not include shipmentNo, trackingFlag, or trackLink. Example successful response { \"code\": 0, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"process_time\": 0.001, \"countryID\": \"ZA\", \"timeZone\": \"Africa/Johannesburg\", \"truckNo\": \"KS51YPGP_F\", \"latitude\": -26.2041, \"longitude\": 28.0473, \"source\": \"Example Fleet\", \"streetName\": \"Main Road, Johannesburg\", \"lastRecordedOn\": 1785142800 } Time formats Time zone The timeZone field contains an IANA time zone identifier: \"timeZone\": \"Africa/Johannesburg\" The IANA identifier defines a regional time zone and allows its applicable rules to be handled correctly. The method does not return a fixed UTC offset such as UTC+02:00. Time of the latest position The lastRecordedOn field contains the time of the latest GPS position as a Unix timestamp: \"lastRecordedOn\": 1785142800 A Unix timestamp is the number of seconds elapsed since January 1, 1970, at 00:00:00 UTC. It does not depend on a time zone. To display the date and time to the user, convert lastRecordedOn using the time zone specified in timeZone. Address determination The streetName field is generated using reverse geocoding: OneGPS converts the coordinates of the latest position into a text address. \"streetName\": \"Main Road, Johannesburg\" The completeness and accuracy of the address depend on the available mapping data."},{"s":"api-for-working-with-pilot-task-manager","t":"API for working with OneGPS Task manager","h":["API for working with OneGPS Task manager"],"x":"API for working with OneGPS Task manager OneGPS Task Manager (PTM) is a module of the OneGPS system for task management. Using this module’s API, you can: create, edit, and delete tasks assign tasks to performers get task status and deadlines retrieve information about current and completed tasks"},{"s":"creating-a-task","t":"Creating a task","h":["Creating a task","Request address","API Access","Example API request","API Response format","Status codes and error codes","How to create a task","Parameters for a Universal template task","Route address parameters","Example request","Example response"],"x":"Creating a task This API is designed for creating tasks related to contracts where the PTM module is enabled. Base API Path: /backend/public/api/task Request address To make a request, use the following URL format: https://<server_address>/backend/public/api/task API Access API access is authenticated using HTTP Basic authentication. The username and password should be formatted as: username_from_PILOT:password_from_PILOT Example API request https://demo:demo@tasks.OneGPS-gps.com/backend/public/api/task/create_universal API Response format The API response is standardized and has the following format: { \"success\": true, // TRUE - successful request, FALSE - error \"msg\": \"OK\", // Error message, if any \"httpCode\": 200, // Error code \"data\": [], // Response data, if available \"et\": 0, // Request execution time \"exceptionMsg\": \"Exception\" // Exception message, if any } Status codes and error codes 200 — Successful request 400 — Missing required parameters 401 — Authorization error: Incorrect username or password 404 — API route not found 500 — Server error How to create a task Request method: POST To create a task using the Universal template, you need to send parameters. The Universal template is automatically created in the system, while additional templates should be pre-configured in the PTM interface. Parameters for a Universal template task For a task with the Universal template, the following parameters are used: 1. external_id: External task identifier (string, default is NULL) 2. vehicle_plate: Vehicle license plate number (default is NULL). If the number does not exist in the system, it will be created as a rental vehicle 3. driver_name: Driver's full name (default is NULL). If not specified, the name from the vehicle_plate will be used. If the driver doesn't exist, they will be created as a rental driver 4. type_name: Task type (default is \"Delivery\"). If the type doesn't exist, it will be created 5. template_name: Task template name (default is \"Universal\"). If the template doesn't exist, it will be created 6. time_zone: Time zone offset from UTC (number, in minutes from UTC, default is NULL) 7. time_zone_name: Time zone name (string, default is NULL) 8. c_begin_time: Scheduled task start time (number — timestamp in seconds, default is current time) 9. c_return_time: Scheduled task end time (number, default is NULL) 10. route_name: Route name (string, default is NULL) 11. cargo: Cargo description (string, default is NULL) 12. temp_min: Minimum temperature (number, default is 2°C) 13. temp_max: Maximum temperature (number, default is 8°C) 14. description: Task description (string, default is NULL) 15. addresses_list (required): List of addresses or route points (strings with addresses or coordinates) Route address parameters Each route address may include additional parameters with suffixes that describe the route characteristics and geographical data: 1. addresses_list: List of addresses or route points (strings with addresses or coordinates) 2. addresses_list_lat: Array of latitudes for the route points 3. addresses_list_lng: Array of longitudes for the route points 4. addresses_list_date_point: Array of timestamps (in seconds) for the planned arrival times 5. addresses_list_date_point_to: Array of timestamps (in seconds) for the planned departure times (automatically calculated if not specified) 6. addresses_list_time_point: Array of loading/unloading times at the route points (in minutes) 7. addresses_list_redirect: Flag for redirecting the current address to the next 8. addresses_list_geofence_id: Geofence IDs for each route point 9. addresses_list_warehouse: Warehouse or object names for each route point 10. addresses_list_ticket: Array of unique order identifiers for each route point 11. addresses_list_phones: Contact phone numbers for each route point 12. addresses_list_schedule: Working schedule for each route point 13. addresses_list_vehicle_volume: Cargo volume in the vehicle 14. addresses_list_trailer_volume: Cargo volume in the trailer 15. addresses_list_action: Description of actions to perform at each route point 16. addresses_list_agent_id: Agent ID from the OneGPS system, used for trash containers Example request fetch(\"https://tasks.OneGPS-gps.com/backend/public/api/task/create_universal\", { \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Basic ZGVtbzpkZW1v\", // base64 encoding \"demo:demo\" \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }, \"method\": \"POST\", \"body\": JSON.stringify({ \"external_id\": \"T-5677786754\", \"vehicle_plate\": \"PT 125 AA 66\", \"driver_name\": null, \"type_name\": \"Delivery\", \"template_name\": \"Universal\", \"time_zone\": -180, \"time_zone_name\": \"Europe/Moscow\", \"c_begin_time\": 1723233360, \"c_return_time\": 1723238700, \"route_name\": \"My route\", \"cargo\": \"Milk\", \"temp_min\": 2, \"temp_max\": 6, \"description\": \"Test\", \"addresses_list\": [ \"215, ul. Narodnogo Opolcheniya, Rostov-on-Don\", \"47.227196,39.705228\", \"Client 2\" ], \"addresses_list_lat\": [ 47.234256, 47.227196, 47.216077 ], \"addresses_list_lng\": [ 39.706467, 39.705228, 39.706928 ], \"addresses_list_date_point\": [ 1723233360, 1723234800, 1723237800 ], \"addresses_list_time_point\": [ 10, 20, 15 ], \"addresses_list_rad_geo\": [ 50, 40, 30 ] }) }); Example response { \"success\": true, \"msg\": \"OK\", \"httpCode\": 200, \"data\": { \"id\": 12563 }, \"et\": 0.5612 }"},{"s":"waybill","t":"Waybill","h":["Waybill","How to get data","Method","Request address","Request parameters","Example request"],"x":"Waybill This API is used to retrieve information about agents’ waybills (tasks) for a specified period. With it, you can: get a list of tasks assigned to each agent view planned and actual execution times check task statuses and details for route points How to get data Method GET Request address To get the data, use the address: https://tasks.<server_address>/backend/public/get_waybill_for_pilot Request parameters from_ts— start time of the selection period (Unix timestamp, e.g., 1536181200) to_ts— end time of the selection period (Unix timestamp, e.g., 1538773200) agents— list of agent IDs separated by commas (e.g., 13128,13130,21055) Example request https://tasks.<server_address>/backend/public/get_waybill_for_pilot?from_ts=1536181200&to_ts=1538773200&agents=13128,13130,21055 Response { \"success\": true, \"msg\": \"\", \"items\": [ { \"agent_id\": 13128, \"c_begin_time\": \"1536200000\", \"c_end_time\": \"1536210000\", \"c_information\": \"{\\\"route\\\":\\\"Route №54\\\",\\\"driver\\\":\\\"Ivanov I.I.\\\"}\", \"c_initiator\": \"Dispatcher\", \"c_progress_time\": \"1536200500\", \"c_running_time\": \"1536200300\", \"c_status_id\": 2, \"c_status_name\": \"Completed\", \"id\": 98765, \"points_info\": [ { \"point_id\": 1, \"lat\": \"55.7558\", \"lon\": \"37.6173\", \"visited_at\": \"1536200600\" } ] } ] } In response to the API request, the following is returned: success — execution result (true = success, false = error) msg — error message (if any) items — array of task data For each task: agent_id — agent identifier c_begin_time — planned task start time c_end_time — actual task end time c_information — string with additional task info (JSON, depends on the template) c_initiator — initiator (who created the task) c_progress_time — actual time of the first point visit c_running_time — actual time of departure to start the task c_status_id — task status ID c_status_name — task status description id — task identifier in the PTM system points_info — array with details of visited route points: point_id — route point ID lat — latitude of the point lon — longitude of the point visited_at — timestamp when the point was visited"},{"s":"equipment-2","t":"Equipment","h":["Equipment"],"x":"Equipment This section contains information about the equipment that can be connected and used with the OneGPS platform. It includes a list of supported devices with details such as brand, model, device type, and connection method — ranging from GPS trackers and IoT devices to mobile DVRs, security panels, and tachographs. The section also provides instructions on how to connect and set up the equipment: how to get started, what to pay attention to during installation, and important features to consider during operation. These materials will help you quickly understand your device and prepare it for proper use."},{"s":"supported-equipment","t":"Supported equipment","h":["Supported equipment"],"x":"Supported equipment Megastek 20003 TCP GPS tracker A-Telematics 20004 TCP GPS tracker Enfora теперь Novatel Wireless / Inseego 20005 TCP GPS tracker Think Power 20006 TCP GPS tracker Think Power Laipac 20007 TCP GPS tracker Systems & Technology Corp 20008 TCP GPS tracker Systech 20009 TCP MVDR 33000 TCP MVDR Digital Matter 20010 TCP Sinocastel 20013 TCP GPS tracker minifinder IMEI 20200 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GT301 IMEI 20201 TCP GPS tracker Wialon Retranslator 20204 TCP Protocol iStartek V202 20205 TCP GPS tracker Cradlepoint 20206 TCP GPS tracker Omni 20207 TCP IoT for scooters Fitcoo 20210 TCP IoT for scooters Guoxiatech 20213 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GB100 IMEI 20214 TCP GPS tracker Tzone RD06 20215 TCP IoT device Tzone Tzone Rd06 WiFi 20236 TCP IoT device Tzone 20216 TCP GPS tracker Suntech ST-300 20217 TCP GPS tracker iStartek VT600 20218 TCP GPS tracker iStartek VT900 20218 TCP GPS tracker SAT-LITE 4 IMEI 20220 TCP GPS tracker Positron 20221 TCP GPS tracker SETRACKER 20224 TCP GPS tracker Setracker Picasau 20225 TCP IoT for scooters Suntech ST-500 20226 TCP GPS tracker Neoway 20227 TCP GPS tracker Neoway GoSafe G1C 20228 TCP GPS tracker MellaTechnology 20229 TCP GPS tracker Alien reader 20230 TCP GPS tracker GP2000/GP4000 20231 TCP GPS tracker Teltonika DashCam 20233 TCP GPS tracker TopFlyTech 20234 TCP GPS tracker Topflytech TopTen 20237 TCP GPS tracker Guangzhou Topten Electronics 20238 TCP GPS tracker Albatros 20240 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GV56 IMEI 20242 TCP GPS tracker Digital Systems 20243 TCP GPS tracker Digital Systems Jointech JT701D 20245 TCP GPS tracker Jointech Jointech JT707, JT709 20246 TCP GPS tracker Jointech PULSE 20248 TCP Security panel Garm M25 OneGPS 20249 TCP Autonomous GPS tracker Garm Future Digital 20250 TCP IoT устройство Dingtek iStartek PT66 20251 TCP GPS tracker Teltonika MQTT IMEI 20252 TCP Modem GPS tracker Queclink GL521 IMEI 20253 TCP GPS tracker GoSafe 30012 TCP GPS tracker StarLine 30019 TCP GPS tracker Teltonika (all models) IMEI 20012 TCP GPS tracker Teltonika (all models) IMEI 20112 TCP GPS tracker Teltonika (all models) IMEI 20032 TCP GPS tracker Teltonika Dash camera IMEI 20233 TCP GPS tracker Sinocastle IMEI 20013 TCP GPS tracker Meitrack (VT300, VT60) Device ID 20016 TCP GPS tracker GPS Storozh Device ID 20017 TCP GPS tracker IMEI 20018 TCP GPS tracker AutoGRAPH Device ID 20019 TCP GPS tracker Suntech ST-270 20020 TCP GPS tracker Voyager Device ID 20021 TCP GPS tracker ASC 20022 TCP GPS tracker Ruptela(all models) IMEI 20023 TCP GPS tracker GlobalSat TR-103 IMEI 20024 TCP GPS tracker Granit Navigator 3 Device ID 20025 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GV500 IMEI 20026 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GL100 IMEI 20027 TCP GPS tracker Format NMEA0183 20028 TCP GPS tracker Agent Silver IMEI 20029 TCP GPS tracker Agent Granit Navigator 6 Device ID 20025 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GL100M IMEI 20029 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GB100 IMEI 20029 TCP GPS tracker Xexun IMEI 20034 TCP GPS tracker GoSafe IMEI 20035 TCP GPS tracker ARNAVI IMEI 20037 TCP GPS tracker Azimut GSM IMEI 20037 TCP GPS tracker GoSafe IMEI 20039 TCP GPS tracker AutoFon Mayak 20040 TCP GPS tracker KASBI Tachograph 20043 TCP Tachograph Xexun IMEI 20044 TCP GPS tracker Mercury Tachograph 20045 TCP Tachograph BOX Tracker 20046 TCP GPS tracker Fort Telecom (FORT 300) 20047 TCP GPS tracker Fort Telecom (FORT 11) 20048 TCP GPS tracker ARNAVI 20049 TCP GPS tracker Navtelecom Signal series 20052 TCP GPS tracker Meitrack (MT90) 20054 TCP GPS tracker ARNAVI 20055 TCP GPS tracker AutoLink 20055 TCP GPS tracker iConcox 20056 TCP GPS tracker JIMI 20056 TCP GPS tracker Cellocator 20057 TCP GPS tracker Amwel 20058 TCP GPS tracker Wialon IPS 20099 TCP Protocol Wialon Combine 20099 TCP Protocol ADM all series 20060 TCP GPS tracker Navtelecom Smart series 20062 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GV320 IMEI 20063 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GV300 IMEI 20063 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GV75 IMEI 20063 TCP GPS tracker Meitrack IMEI 20064 TCP GPS tracker Meiligao IMEI 20064 TCP GPS tracker Meiligao Queclink GL500 IMEI 20065 TCP GPS tracker Shtrikh Tachograph 20066 TCP Tachograph Xirgo 20067 TCP GPS tracker iON 20071 TCP GPS tracker Aplicom 20072 TCP GPS tracker Omnicomm Device ID 20073 TCP GPS tracker SAT-LITE 2 IMEI 20074 TCP GPS tracker Sattelite Solutions cGuard 20075 TCP GPS tracker SAT-LITE IMEI 20076 TCP GPS tracker Sattelite Solutions SpaceTeam IMEI 20077 TCP GPS tracker Atrack IMEI 20078 TCP GPS tracker Jointech GP4000 20079 TCP GPS tracker Queclink GL300 IMEI 20063 TCP GPS tracker Skif-navigator 20081 TCP GPS tracker GT02A 20082 TCP GPS tracker Xirgo MQTT 20084 TCP GPS tracker Coban 20085 TCP GPS tracker SinoTrack 20086 TCP GPS tracker KingNeed 20087 TCP GPS tracker H02 protocol (TK909,TK907,TKSTAR, TK-STARC8,TK808,GT06) 20088 TCP Protocol CIPIA 1883 TCP ADAS EGTS 20089 TCP Protocol Cerber 20090 TCP Fire and security panel Queclink GV65 IMEI 20091 TCP GPS tracker EeLink TK116 20096 TCP GPS tracker EeLink 20202 TCP GPS tracker D-teg 20097 TCP GPS tracker AT05 25098 TCP GPS tracker Wialon Combine 20099 TCP Protocol Wialon IPS 20099 TCP Protocol Navtelecom Start series 20100 UDP GPS tracker Level 20102 UDP GPS tracker Noran 20103 UDP GPS tracker CalAmp 20104 UDP GPS tracker Gator 20105 UDP GPS tracker Gator (M528, M558) 20254 TCP GPS tracker AIS Marine 20106 UDP Protocol International Standard (IMO/ITU) Wialon Combine 20107 UDP Protocol Wialon IPS 20107 UDP Protocol Ge-AM ECO 20255 JТ808 Standard 20227 Fifotrack 20257 RC.MINI 20092 Mokosmart 20256 iStartek (VT100, VT200, VT300, PT60) 20218 TCP GPS tracker 20265 Safseer 20244 Tzone 2g lora 20215 Mictrack 20261 TCP GPS tracker AutoFon Mayak 5, 6 20262 TCP GPS tracker AutoFon Mayak 7 20263 TCP GPS tracker AutoFon Mayak 8 20264 TCP GPS tracker Tefco EH03T TCP JT301X 20246 GPS tracker Jointech BSJ GPS tracker 6608 GPS tracker BSJ Video 6608 MDVR Kingwo 20269 EBQ 8883 20270 AVT110 JLINKIOT TL10 20271"},{"s":"connecting-equipment","t":"Connecting equipment","h":["Connecting equipment"],"x":"Connecting equipment This section provides information on how to connect and set up different types of equipment. It covers the main steps for connection, key features of the devices, and examples of specific configurations."},{"s":"setting-up-jimi-dash-cameras","t":"Setting up Jimi dash cameras","h":["Setting up Jimi dash cameras","Main сonfiguration сommands","1. Allow server connection","2. Telemetry server setup","3. Video upload server setup","4. Live streaming server setup","5. File list server setup","6. Automatic event video upload","Event codes"],"x":"Setting up Jimi dash cameras Jimi DVRs are configured using simple text commands. You just send the device the required command, and it starts working according to your settings. With these commands, you can: connect the DVR to a server specify where to send video and trip data enable live streaming set up automatic video uploads for specific events In this guide, we’ll go through the commands that help you start and properly configure your device. Main сonfiguration сommands 1. Allow server connection COREKITSW,0 This command allows the DVR to connect to the OneGPS VIDEO server. It activates the device’s ability to work with the platform and is usually the first step in the setup process. 2. Telemetry server setup SERVER,1,<domain name>,20056 or SERVER,0,<IP address>,20056 This sets the server where telemetry data (movement, speed, events) will be sent. 1 — use a domain name (e.g., server.example.com) 0 — use an IP address (e.g., 192.168.1.100) 20056 — communication port 3. Video upload server setup UPLOAD,<https://server_address/upload> The address where video files will be uploaded, for playback or event recordings. 4. Live streaming server setup RSERVICE,<rtmp://server_address/live> Sets the RTMP server for real-time video streaming. 5. File list server setup FILELIST,<https://server_address/filelist> The address where the DVR sends the list of available recordings. 6. Automatic event video upload UPLOADSW,<Event>,<Mode> This command sets which events trigger automatic video uploads and which cameras to use. Event — the event that triggers the upload (e.g., CRASH, SOS, OVERSPEED). Mode — how videos are uploaded: — OFF — disable automatic upload — ON — upload from both front and inward cameras — 1— upload only from the front camera — 2 — upload only from the inward camera Example: UPLOADSW,CRASH,ON — when a crash occurs, the DVR will automatically upload video from both cameras. Event codes Below is a list of all events the DVR can detect. These codes can be used with the UPLOADSW command. 1 SOS SOS button pressed 2 CRASH Accident 3 VIBRATE Vibration / impact 4 OVERSPEED Over-speeding 5 RAPIDACC Rapid acceleration 6 RAPIDDEC Rapid braking 7 RAPIDTURN Sharp turn 8 DRIVE Driving mode 9 POWER Power change 10 VOLTAGELOW Low voltage 11 CLOSEEYES Driver’s eyes closed / fatigue 12 YAWN Yawning, sign of fatigue 13 DISTRACTION Driver distracted 14 SMOKING Smoking while driving 15 PHONECALLING Using phone 16 RELAYOFF Relay turned off 17 RELAYRECOVERY Relay restored 18 MISSINGFACE Face not detected 19 NOSDCARD No SD card 20 SDCARDMOUNT SD card inserted 21 OIL Oil sensor event 22 OILTO Oil error 23 TEMPTO Engine overheating 24 UART UART error 25 LANEDEPARTURE Lane departure 26 VEHICLETOOCLOSE Vehicle too close 27 FORWARDCOLLISION Forward collision risk 28 UART_DOOR Door sensor 29 AFIS Face detected (AI) 30 AFIF Face recognition error 31 AWSB Seatbelt detected 32 ANWSB Seatbelt not fastened"},{"s":"setting-up-jimi-jc181-jc182-jc450-jc451-jc371","t":"Setting up Jimi JC181/JC182/JC450/JC451/JC371","h":["Setting up Jimi JC181/JC182/JC450/JC451/JC371","1. Server setup","2. APN setup","3. Device ID setup","4. Time zone setup","5. Reboot the device"],"x":"Setting up Jimi JC181/JC182/JC450/JC451/JC371 To connect Jimi dash cameras (JC181, JC182, JC450, JC451, JC371) to the OneGPS server and start sending data and video, you need to send a few commands. Commands can be sent to the device via SMS or through a remote management server (depending on the device settings). Below are example commands for connecting to the OneGPS server. 1. Server setup SERVER,video.OneGPS-gps.com,6608,NA,NA,NA,NA# This command tells the device where to send the data: video.OneGPS-gps.com — OneGPS server 6608 — server port NA — write NA if the field has no value (empty fields must be filled with NA). 2. APN setup APN,internet,,,,,,,,,mtn,,mtn,,,,,,, This command configures the SIM card access point so the device can connect to the internet: internet — APN name mtn — login mtn — password 3. Device ID setup BCD,1# This command defines which identifier the device will use: 1 = last 12 digits of the IMEI 4. Time zone setup TIMEZONE,±hh:mm This command sets the device time zone so that event times match local time on the server. Example for Moscow time UTC+03:00: TIMEZONE,+03:00 5. Reboot the device REBOOT After entering all commands, reboot the device to apply the new settings."},{"s":"system-requirements-for-a-stand-alone-server","t":"System requirements for a stand-alone server","h":["System requirements for a stand-alone server","Base server requirements","Data retention","Deployment at higher loads","Dedicated servers by role","Database Server","PTM and Logistics server","System architecture","Main components","Deployment options","Backup","External network resources","OS updates","Client access (Map tiles)","Installation conditions","Technical support conditions","Update procedure"],"x":"System requirements for a stand-alone server This article provides the system requirements and recommendations for deploying the OneGPS platform in a stand-alone server configuration. Here you will find: minimum and recommended hardware parameters requirements for the operating system and software components rules for organizing network connectivity specifics of backup, updates, and technical support The information is intended for system administrators, engineers, and integrators who prepare server infrastructure and ensure the stable operation of the system. Base server requirements To process data from up to 10,000 devices, the minimum configuration is: Processor (CPU) 8 server-grade threads/cores Load increases if devices send data every 1-5 seconds Memory (RAM) 64 GB Required for stable database and cache operation Database storage 2 TB SSD (RAID10 if using physical server) SSD is strongly recommended when >1000 devices are connected File storage (XML) 1 TB HDD Used for storing raw device data If devices send messages every 1–5 seconds, disk and CPU load increases sharply — reserve additional resources in such scenarios. Data retention Raw data – up to 3–4 months (can be restored on request; depends on disk space). Aggregated data – stored without limitation. Deployment at higher loads For more than 5,000 devices, it is recommended to use a dedicated database server on SSD. The standard stand-alone installation does not include PTM, Logistics, or Video modules. To use PTM and Logistics, a separate server on Oracle Linux is recommended. To use the Video module, a dedicated server on Windows is required. Dedicated servers by role Database Server OS: Oracle Linux 8.x Processor: from 8 threads Memory: from 64 GB Storage: from 2 TB SSD Network: direct local connection to the application server is required. PTM and Logistics server Requirements similar to the base server Additional +30% capacity Configurations above 3000 devices require individual planning. Video server OS: Windows Server 2016 or later Processor: from 16 threads Memory: from 128 GB Storage: from 3 TB (depends on the required storage volume for video files) Network: broadband connection required, since the server processes video streams. System architecture The OneGPS platform runs on Oracle Linux 8 (64-bit). Deployment in a virtualized environment is supported (with network port forwarding). Main components Nginx PostgreSQL 16 PHP v8 + php-fpm Redis Memcache SocketProxy server + Device Communicator Additionally, required libraries, dependencies, and standard OS administration/monitoring tools are installed. Deployment options Production system — full Linux installation with all requirements. Test system (up to 100 devices): CPU: minimum 4 threads RAM: minimum 4 GB Disk: 200 GB Backup Database backups are performed using DBMS tools. It is recommended to allocate 1 TB of slow disk space (HDD) on separate physical hardware. To archive raw XML data, at least 500 GB of HDD space is required. Backup storage needs may grow depending on the number of devices and the data reporting rate. External network resources If local services are not configured, the server must have access to: Geocoding: global.OneGPS-gps.com (208.76.249.186), HTTP ports 80/443 Routing: global.OneGPS-gps.com (208.76.249.186), HTTP port 5000 Logistics: vroom.OneGPS-gps.com (208.76.249.186), HTTP port 3000 Updates: gitlab.OneGPS-gps. com , SSH port 22122 OS updates Oracle Linux updates are delivered from the nearest mirror via HTTPS. Alternatively, an internal repository or proxy may be configured. Client access (Map tiles) If there is no local tile server, client workstations must have HTTP/HTTPS access to the following resources: Installation conditions During installation, the server must have full unrestricted internet access. After installation and updating of all components, access may be restricted according to security policy. Technical support conditions For technical support, SSH access is mandatory. If SSH access is unavailable, remote support cannot be provided. Update procedure Updates for OneGPS are downloaded from: http://gitlab.OneGPS-gps.com/. During the update process, access to system repositories is also required. If internet access is unavailable at the moment of update, the update process will fail."}]
